# making `$REG/<id>.hold` a DIRECTORY, because chmod does not constrain root
# and a directory does. Dropping `-T` turns that guard's refusal into a false
# success, which is the one outcome it exists to prevent.
#
# `-L` BEFORE `-d`, and that order is the whole correctness of the Darwin arm:
# `-T` treats a symlink-to-directory as the NAME and replaces it, where
# `[ -d ]` follows the link and would refuse a rename GNU performs.
#
# A SYMLINK-TO-DIRECTORY DEST IS THE ONE SHAPE GNU'S `-T` REPLACES THAT BARE
# `mv -f` DOES NOT (D-2187): measured on GNU coreutils 9.4, `mv -f -- src dest`
# with `dest` a symlink to a directory returns 0 with `dest` still the same
# symlink and `src` moved INSIDE the linked directory — the postcondition
# (`0 iff src is now at dest`) is false while the exit code says otherwise.
# Every other destination shape (symlink-to-file, dangling symlink, plain
# file) already replaces correctly; only this one needs help, so the
# `rm -f -- "$2"` below is scoped to exactly that shape — guarded on BOTH
# `-L` and `-d`, never unconditional — not a general unlink-before-rename.
#
# NOT ATOMIC THE WAY `-T` IS, and written down rather than hidden: between the
# test and the rename another process could create a directory at the
# destination. Measured repo-wide (D-2189): this function has EIGHT
# executable call sites across ccd and ccrc — the paragraph this replaces
# claimed it was the destination's ONLY WRITER, which undercounted the call
# sites themselves, not a cardinal it ever stated. The eight, each named WITH
# ITS FILE since this comment is one shared text mirrored into both: (in
# EACH file) `_svc_write_session_plist`, to a launchd plist under
# `$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents` — outside `$REG` entirely; (`ccd/ccd` only)
