# on it.
#
# THE STATE IS PASSED IN, not read here: callers obtain it ONCE per decision
# (`pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")`) and pass the word, so a loop over
# five candidates costs one `cat`, not five.
#
# WAVE 1 TASK 2 — the account arm now reads `_acct_pool_state`, not
# `_acct_pool`. The old `ap=$(_acct_pool "$1")` treated every account this
# side could not see the same way it treated a genuinely untagged one — a
# bare name or empty, with no way to tell "the roster says nothing" from "the
# control plane's projection has never synced". `_acct_pool_state` answers
# with STATE (spec §5.7): `unreadable`/`stale`/`malformed` now reach rc 2
# (nobody decides) instead of silently folding into "unconstrained, serve".
#
# THE PROJECT SIDE DECIDES FIRST, and the order is the whole blast-radius
# argument (spec §5.8). An untagged project is unconstrained, so it must
# serve WITHOUT consulting the account side at all — otherwise a control
# plane that has been down longer than the lease would stop placement into
# every project on the box, instead of only the ones somebody deliberately
# tagged. Moving this below the account read is the difference between an
# outage nobody notices and a fleet-wide stop.
#
# TWO FACTS OUT ALONGSIDE THE EXIT CODE (fix round 1, I1/I2) — `POOL_OK_SIDE`
# and `POOL_OK_STATE`, set on EVERY call before anything returns.
#
# WHY THEY ARE NEEDED. The exit code is three-valued and the WORLD behind it is
# not: rc 2 now stands for FIVE distinguishable conditions — the project tag
# `unreadable` or `malformed`, and the account projection `unreadable`, `stale`
# or `malformed` — and every renderer in this file used to spell exactly one of
# them ("pool tag for <p> is <pps> … fix or clear it"). On a cold node with a
# perfectly readable project tag that sentence blamed a healthy file and told
# the operator to clear it. rc 1 had the twin defect one carrier over: the
# refusal text read the DECLARED roster (`_acct_pool`) while the PROJECTION is
# what decided, so a roster/projection skew printed "claude is in pool 'pool-a'
# and project 'demo' is in pool 'pool-a'" and refused it as a mismatch. An
# adapter may not narrow a distinction it received; these two variables are how
# the distinction leaves this function.
#
#   POOL_OK_SIDE   project | account — WHICH SIDE produced the answer.
#   POOL_OK_STATE  that side's own state word, verbatim: `untagged`,
#                  `named <n>`, `unreadable`, `malformed` or (account only)
#                  `stale`. For rc 1 it is the account's PROJECTED
#                  `named <n>` — the name that actually decided.
#
# GLOBALS, NOT STDOUT, and the precedent is `_session_probe` below: `$( )`
# would fork a subshell and eat them. MEASURED rather than assumed, because
# this only works if no call site captures the call — all 12 invoke it
# rc-style (`; prc=$?`, `|| continue`, `if ! …`), so the writes land in the
# caller's own scope. Two consequences a future caller must respect: a caller
# that runs OTHER functions between the call and the render must SNAPSHOT them
# first (`cmd_swap` does, because `_crosspool_valid` and `_strand_mark` run in
# between), and a caller must never read them without having called first —
# hence the unconditional reset on entry, so a rc-0 return can never leave the
# previous call's words standing.
#
# FOUR MORE FUNCTIONS CLOBBER THESE GLOBALS WHEN CALLED DIRECTLY, beside
# `cmd_swap` above: `_strand_why`, `_ws_least_loaded`, `_swap_target` and
# `_place_for_class` each call `_pool_ok` themselves. They are safe today only
# because every one of their eight call sites (`_place_for_class` x2,
# `_ws_least_loaded` x4, `_swap_target` x1, `_strand_why` x1) is `$( )`
# -substituted — a subshell, so the clobber dies with the subshell and never
# reaches the caller's own `POOL_OK_SIDE`/`POOL_OK_STATE`. That is fragile,
# not structural: this file already has precedent for de-substituting such a
# call (`_place_for_class`'s `PLACE_DEGRADED_TO`, printed on a second line to
# survive the subshell) — the day one of these four is called bare instead of
# captured, the next `_pool_ok` render silently reads the wrong call's answer.
_pool_ok() {   # account-id pool-state -> 0 serve | 1 mismatch | 2 undecidable
  local ap pp
  POOL_OK_SIDE=''; POOL_OK_STATE=''
  case "$2" in
    untagged)  POOL_OK_SIDE=project; POOL_OK_STATE=untagged; return 0 ;;
    "named "*) pp=${2#named } ;;
    *)         POOL_OK_SIDE=project; POOL_OK_STATE="$2"; return 2 ;;   # unreadable | malformed: nobody decides
  esac
  ap=$(_acct_pool_state "$1")
  # FROM HERE THE ANSWER IS THE ACCOUNT SIDE'S, whatever it turns out to be —
  # the project side already answered `named <n>`, which is a readable tag and
  # never a reason to refuse on its own.
  POOL_OK_SIDE=account; POOL_OK_STATE="$ap"
  case "$ap" in
    untagged)  return 0 ;;
    "named "*) ap=${ap#named } ;;
    *)         return 2 ;;                     # unreadable | stale | malformed: nobody decides
  esac
  [[ "$ap" == "$pp" ]]
}
# ── THE TWO NON-SERVE SENTENCES, EACH SPELLED ONCE ──────────────────────────
# Both take their facts as ARGUMENTS rather than reading `POOL_OK_SIDE` /
# `POOL_OK_STATE` themselves, for the reason the variables' own header gives:
# a caller that runs something else in between must pass its SNAPSHOT, and a
# renderer that silently read whatever the globals hold now would be the same
# stale-fact defect one level up.
_pool_undecidable_why() {   # side state project account-id -> the rc-2 sentence
  # FIVE CONDITIONS, FIVE REMEDIES, and saying which is the whole obligation:
  # "both mean nobody decides, but one's remedy is file permissions and the
  # other's is the control-plane link, and the die message and the chip each
  # need to say which" (spec §5.7). The account arms each state POSITIVELY that
  # the project tag is fine, because the sentence they replace said the
  # opposite and an operator acted on it.
  local side="$1" state="$2" project="$3" acct="$4"
  if [[ "$side" == project ]]; then
    echo "pool tag for $project is $state: $POOLS_DIR/$project — fix or clear it"
    return 0
  fi
  case "$state" in
    stale)     echo "the pool projection for $acct is stale: $REG/pool-epoch is past its lease — the remedy is the control-plane link (the pool sync), not this file; project '$project' is tagged and its tag reads fine" ;;
    malformed) echo "the pool projection for $acct is malformed: $REG/pool-epoch is off the grammar — re-sync or remove it; project '$project' is tagged and its tag reads fine" ;;
    *)         echo "the pool projection for $acct is $state: $REG/pool-epoch is absent or unreadable — this node may never have synced; check that and the file's permissions; project '$project' is tagged and its tag reads fine" ;;
  esac
}
_pool_mismatch_why() {   # account-id account-state project project-pool-state -> the rc-1 clause
  # NAMES THE CARRIER THAT DECIDED. `_acct_pool` — the DECLARED roster read —
  # is what stood here, and it is spec §5.6's LOWEST precedence: the
  # projection outranks it, so the two disagree exactly when this sentence
  # matters most, and the old text then named two identical pools and called
  # them a mismatch.
  echo "$1 is in pool '${2#named }' (measured from the projection, $REG/pool-epoch — not the declared roster) and project '$3' is in pool '${4#named }'"
}
_id()    { echo "$1-$2"; }                                  # wrapper project -> id
_tmux()  { echo "cc-$1"; }                                  # id -> tmux name
# `tmux has-session` answers THREE questions with one exit status, and every
# failure is rc=1. Measured 2026-08-19 on an isolated socket:
#
#   session gone           can't find session: cc-demo
#   no server / no socket  error connecting to <path> (No such file or directory)
#   socket, but no server  no server running on <path>
#   tmux absent            rc 127, message from the shell
#
# Only the FIRST is evidence a session died; the rest mean "I could not ask",
# and the callers want opposite answers for the two. THE POLARITY IS THE DESIGN:
# this recognises the one message that means death and calls everything else
# unknown. It must never be rewritten as a list of failure messages — an
# unrecognised future tmux error has to land in `unknown`, which makes callers
# refuse, rather than in `gone`, which makes them destroy. A tmux upgrade that
# rewords its errors therefore degrades this to "refuses too often", never to
# "reaps a live session".
#
# The probe form (D-310 (was D-B8-14)) exists because the supervisor needs BOTH the verdict
# and tmux's own words for the substrate marker, and a `$( )` caller would eat
# the second fact with the subshell. Same classifier, same polarity — the probe
# IS the one classifier now; `_session_verdict` merely re-derives from it.
_session_probe() {   # id -> PROBE_VERDICT=live|gone|unknown, PROBE_DETAIL (unknown only),
                     #       PROBE_SUBSTRATE=present|absent|unmeasured; rc 0
  # PROBE_SUBSTRATE IS NOT A FOURTH VERDICT, AND MUST NEVER BECOME ONE. It answers a
  # DIFFERENT question from PROBE_VERDICT — "is there a tmux server at all", not "is this
  # session alive" — and exactly one caller reads it: `cmd_supervise`'s pre-flight, which
  # needs to tell a server that is definitively ABSENT (nothing to block on; `new-session`
  # will create one) from a server that is merely SILENT (the wedge, where
  # `_tmux_new_session` blocks forever with no deadline). Every other caller — `_alive`,
  # `_ws_status`, `cmd_forget`, `ws-archive`, `ws-reap` — reads PROBE_VERDICT only, and
  # for them `no server running` must stay `unknown`: `unknown` refuses, `gone` DESTROYS
  # (D-308/D-309, and `sessionVerdictFixture.ts`'s own "the mistake this table exists to
  # make loud"). Mapping absent->gone would present every live session as reapable during
  # a cold start. The distinction is declared here and spent in one place.
  # Direct call, never $( ) — the whole point is returning TWO facts without a subshell
  # eating them. The deadline applies ONLY when tmux is a real executable: `timeout` execs
  # its argv, so a test's `tmux() { … }` function stub would be invisible behind it — and a
  # stubbed substrate needs no deadline, it cannot wedge.
  local out rc
  if [[ $(type -t tmux) == function ]]; then
    out=$(tmux has-session -t "$(_tmux "$1")" 2>&1); rc=$?
  else
    out=$(_plat_timeout "$SUBSTRATE_PROBE_DEADLINE_S" tmux has-session -t "$(_tmux "$1")" 2>&1); rc=$?
  fi
  if (( rc == 0 )); then PROBE_VERDICT=live; PROBE_DETAIL=""; PROBE_SUBSTRATE=present; return 0; fi
  # rc 124 is timeout's own kill: tmux printed nothing, so the reason is SYNTHESIZED —
  # an empty marker reason is the one shape a maintainer can do nothing with (spec §1).
  if (( rc == 124 )) && [[ -z "$out" ]]; then
    PROBE_VERDICT=unknown; PROBE_DETAIL="tmux did not answer within ${SUBSTRATE_PROBE_DEADLINE_S}s"
    # SILENCE IS THE WEDGE — the one shape `cmd_supervise` must not walk into.
    PROBE_SUBSTRATE=unmeasured; return 0
  fi
  case "$out" in
    *"can't find session"*) PROBE_VERDICT=gone;    PROBE_DETAIL=""; PROBE_SUBSTRATE=present ;;
    *)                      PROBE_VERDICT=unknown; PROBE_DETAIL="${out:-tmux exited $rc with no message}" ;;
  esac
  # SECOND, INDEPENDENT CLASSIFICATION — deliberately a separate `case`, so that
  # broadening one can never silently broaden the other. The verdict case above
  # recognises the ONE message that means death; this one recognises the two that mean
  # "there is no server", and BOTH were measured on tmux 3.4:
  #
  #   no server running on /tmp/tmux-1000/default            socket present, nobody serving
  #   error connecting to /tmp/tmux-1000/x (No such file …)  no socket at all (cold boot)
  #
  # MEASURED on Darwin too, independently (the macOS port, PR #11): on a fresh macOS box
  # every session deadlocked at the supervisor pre-flight — the supervisor would not
  # spawn because tmux was unreachable, and tmux stayed unreachable because nothing
  # spawned it. A Linux fleet host effectively never sits with no server at all, which
  # is why the shape had not been seen before that port. The port fixed it by matching
  # the bare `No such file or directory`/`no server running` strings in PROBE_DETAIL
  # (`_probe_cold_substrate`, deleted when the two fixes merged — this classification
  # is the one mechanism now). tmux's stderr wording is tmux's own, not the platform's,
  # so the two patterns below are expected to match BSD tmux verbatim — but that
  # expectation is unmeasured on a cold Darwin box with THIS errno-qualified spelling;
  # the port's looser match was. If a cold macOS box ever wedges at spawn again, look
  # here first: an unmatched absence degrades to `unmeasured`, which refuses.
  #
  # The errno is part of the second pattern and is NOT optional. `error connecting to …
  # (Permission denied)` and `(Connection refused)` are the same prefix and are NOT
  # evidence of absence — a server may well be there — so they must fall through to
  # `unmeasured` and refuse, exactly as an unrecognised message does. Matching the bare
  # prefix would turn a permissions fault into "no server, go create one".
  # GATED ON THE VERDICT, never on `PROBE_SUBSTRATE` being unset: these are GLOBALS that
  # outlive the call, so a "only if empty" guard would carry the PREVIOUS probe's answer
  # into this one. Every path out of this function assigns exactly once — rc 0 and the
  # rc-124 branch above both return having done so, the `gone` arm sets `present`, and
  # this covers the remaining one.
  if [[ "$PROBE_VERDICT" == unknown ]]; then
    case "$out" in
      *"no server running"*)                                PROBE_SUBSTRATE=absent ;;
      *"error connecting to"*"No such file or directory"*)  PROBE_SUBSTRATE=absent ;;
      *)                                                    PROBE_SUBSTRATE=unmeasured ;;
    esac
  fi
}
_session_verdict() {   # id -> live|gone|unknown on stdout; always rc 0 (derived; one classifier)
  _session_probe "$1"; echo "$PROBE_VERDICT"
}
# Unchanged in meaning for its eleven other callers: true for `live` only, so
# `unknown` still reads as "not alive" everywhere that has not been taught the
# difference. The two callers where that collapse was destructive — `_ws_status`
# and `cmd_forget` — ask `_session_verdict` directly (D-308 (was D-B8-12)).
_alive() { [[ "$(_session_verdict "$1")" == live ]]; }
# ATOMIC BY RENAME (registry-durability wave 2). The old body was
# `printf '%s' "$3" > "$REG/$1.$2"` — truncate-then-write IN PLACE, so any
# reader that opened the file inside the window read ZERO BYTES or a prefix of
# the new value. The registry's read side treats a field's bytes as a fact, and
# a torn field is a fact that was never true. Write to a tmp in THE SAME
# DIRECTORY (hence the same filesystem, hence a real rename(2) rather than a
# copy) and rename it into place: every reader sees the whole old value or the
# whole new one.
#
# D-112'S INVARIANT SURVIVES AND IS STRONGER HERE. `buildRecord` drops a row on
# a single measured-`absent` identity field with no second listing, which is
# only sound if the NAME never disappears. Truncation never unlinked; rename
# never unlinks either — POSIX requires the destination path stay resolvable to
# the old or the new inode throughout. There is NO ENOENT WINDOW at any point,
# before or after this change. Nothing in this function may `rm` the
# destination.
#
# `-T` (--no-target-directory) IS NOT OPTIONAL. Measured, GNU coreutils 9.4: a
# plain `mv -f tmp "$REG/$id.hold"` where `$REG/$id.hold` is a DIRECTORY exits
# 0 having moved the tmp INSIDE it — a false success on the one field whose
# silent absence is destructive (`cmd_ws_hold` reads this exit status and dies
# on it, and `ccd-hold.test.ts` uses exactly that directory as its any-uid
# stand-in for a failed write). With `-T` it exits 1, as `printf > dir` did.
#
# `-T` IS GNU COREUTILS (`--no-target-directory`), and it is DOCUMENTED HERE
# RATHER THAN PROBED. ccd does gate `flock` with a named `die` at three sites
# (`flock (util-linux) is unavailable — refusing to …`), but a probe on
# `_reg_set` would sit on the hottest path in the file — the `supervised`
# heartbeat, ~11 live sessions each re-stamping every 30s — to re-answer, fork
# by fork, a question that is settled per box; and this is not a new class of
# assumption, since ccd already depends on GNU `stat -c`, `readlink -f` and
# `df -P`. Both fleet boxes are GNU/Linux; measured here, GNU coreutils 9.4.
#
# WHAT IT LOOKS LIKE IF THAT EVER STOPS BEING TRUE, which is the part worth
# writing down: a `mv` that does not know `-T` rejects the option and exits
# non-zero, so EVERY `_reg_set` in the process returns 1. Exactly one caller
# reads that status — `cmd_ws_hold`'s `|| die "could not record the hold …"` —
# and every other call site drops it, so nothing announces the fault. The
# visible symptom is the `supervised` heartbeat quietly ceasing to land, and
# `_session_state`'s 120s freshness window then ages EVERY row to `orphan`
# inside two minutes: a fleet that reads as entirely dead, from one flag.
#
# THE TMP NAME: leading dot, trailing `.tmp`, field never last. The dot keeps
# it out of every bash glob in the tree (`$REG/*.uuid`, `$REG/*.workspace`,
# `$REG/$id.*` — none match a dotfile without `dotglob`, which nothing sets),
# and the `.tmp` suffix keeps it out of node's `readdir`, which DOES return
# dotfiles: `readRegistryMeasured` mints session ids from
# `names.filter(n => n.endsWith('.uuid'))`, so a tmp ending in the field name
# would mint a phantom session. `$BASHPID` separates concurrent supervisors
# (and background subshells of one shell, where `$$` would not). THE SEQUENCE
# IS BELT AND BRACES: nothing currently depends on it. Two writes by one shell
# are strictly sequential, and the first tmp is renamed (or `rm -f`'d) away
# before the second is created; two background subshells of one shell inherit
# the SAME counter value, which is precisely the case `$BASHPID` is there for;
# and no `trap` in this file calls `_reg_set`, so there is no re-entrancy path
# that would make it load-bearing either. It is kept because it costs nothing
# and it keeps the name unique for a future caller that holds neither of those
# properties. Same shape and same reason as `session-hook.sh`'s own
# `$REG/.$id.$$.hookstate.tmp`.
#
# A SIGKILL between the write and the rename leaks one tmp file, forever:
# `_reg_purge`'s glob cannot see a dotfile, and giving it a second glob that
# could would let one session's purge delete another session's IN-FLIGHT tmp
# (ids may nest — see `_reg_purge`'s own note) and turn that write into a false
# refusal. Disclosed rather than swept: an inert few bytes, the same price
# `$REG/.reap-$id.lock` already pays for a lock whose identity is an inode.
_REG_SET_SEQ=0
