  # thing to a caller: THIS acquire measured a state of `$REG` that no amount of waiting
  # changes, so "re-run once the compaction settles" is a fabricated remedy
  # there. An EMPTY `WHY` is STILL NOT a proof of ordinary contention: the
  # `{ exec {fd}<>"$al"; }` open further below can fail on an out-of-contract
  # MODE this user cannot open (not only the `flock -w` timeout this empty
  # default is actually written for), and that residue is KNOWN to contain a
  # durable state — a lock inode this user has no permission to open never
  # becomes openable by waiting — not merely folded in as an unlikely race
  # alongside the timeout. Callers key on the VALUE, and an unrecognised one
  # is not contention either.
  COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=""
  command -v flock  >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
  command -v mktemp >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
  command -v link   >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
  if [ -e "$lock" ] || [ -L "$lock" ]; then
    # OCCUPIED, NOT CONTENDED. Something that is not a regular file holds the
    # lock pathname: a symlink (dangling or not, which is why `-L` is tested
    # separately above), a directory, a FIFO. ccd never writes through it and
    # never replaces it, so no compaction settles and no wait ends this — the
    # remedy is a human removing the object, which is why it carries a token.
    [[ -f "$lock" && ! -L "$lock" ]] || { COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=lock-path-occupied; return 1; }
  elif _compact_lock_vanished "$id"; then
    # REFUSE BEFORE THE MINT: nothing has been created yet, so the refusal owns
    # nothing to clean up and the live holder's inode is never displaced.
    COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=canonical-vanished
    return 1
  else
    # A RUNTIME FAILURE, NOT MECHANISM ABSENCE. `command -v mktemp` succeeded
    # above, so status 2 was already ruled out. `mktemp` answering non-zero
    # HERE means it could not create the private source it mints the lock
    # from in `$REG` — the USUAL cause is `$REG` being full, read-only or not
    # writable by this user, but this reports ANY failure of that one
    # command, not only that cause: a failed fork (`RLIMIT_NPROC`, pid
    # exhaustion on a box that runs ~20 live sessions) or fd exhaustion
    # answers the identical non-zero and is not a property of `$REG` at all —
    # folded in here because the acquire cannot tell them apart, not because
    # they share a remedy (r6 R5-M2). A wait frees neither, so this one
    # carries a token too.
    src=$( umask 077; mktemp "$REG/.$id.compactions.lock-init.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null ) \
      || { COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=lock-source-refused; return 1; }
    [[ -f "$src" && ! -L "$src" ]] || { rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
    link "$src" "$lock" 2>/dev/null || true      # EEXIST: validate, never recreate
    rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null || true
    # THE SAME CONDITION ONE INSTANT LATER — OR A PUBLISH THAT NEVER HAPPENED
    # (r6 R5-M1, split r7 R6-I1). This re-test is a DISJUNCTION, and its two
    # halves are different states owed different remedies, so each now gets its
    # own token instead of collapsing into one:
    #   - SOMETHING IS THERE. A stranger can win the lock pathname between the
    #     `[ -e "$lock" ]` test above and here — mkdir a directory over it, say
    #     — and this re-test is what catches that: without the token, an EMPTY
    #     `WHY` sent all four durable callers to the contention arm for a
    #     pathname nothing here will ever unoccupy, which is the same
    #     false-twice remedy `lock-path-occupied`'s own arm exists to give
    #     instead.
    #   - NOTHING IS THERE. The `link` above swallows EVERY errno behind
    #     `|| true`, and `command -v link` at the top of this function already
    #     ruled out the binary being absent — so a publish that failed because
    #     `$REG` is full, over quota, read-only, or was renamed between the
    #     `mktemp` and the `link` arrives here with the pathname EMPTY.
    #     MEASURED on the pre-split form with a `link` shim answering non-zero:
    #     it answered `lock-path-occupied` on exactly that state, and every
    #     clause of the remedy that token renders was false — no symlink, no
    #     directory, no object at all to remove by hand. That state is a
    #     sibling of `lock-source-refused` three lines up rather than of the
    #     occupied one, and `lock-publish-failed` is its own token.
    #
    #     AND THE CAUSE LIST ABOVE IS NOT THE WHOLE ROUTE (r8 R8-M1). An EMPTY
    #     pathname is also what a `link` that SUCCEEDED looks like after a
    #     stranger unlinks the lock in the instant between the `link` and this
    #     re-test — MEASURED with a shim that publishes for real and then
    #     removes what it published: the same `lock-publish-failed`, with a
    #     healthy `$REG` and no failure of anything. This guard measures a
    #     STATE, never an act, so the token's remedy is worded to the state and
    #     enumerates the `$REG` causes as the usual ones alone.
    if [[ ! -f "$lock" || -L "$lock" ]]; then
      if [ -e "$lock" ] || [ -L "$lock" ]; then COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=lock-path-occupied
      else COMPACT_LOCK_WHY=lock-publish-failed
      fi
      return 1
    fi
  fi
  while (( tries < 8 )); do
    al="$REG/.$id.compactions.lock-open.$$.$RANDOM.$RANDOM"
    [ -e "$al" ] || [ -L "$al" ] || break
    al=""; tries=$(( tries + 1 ))
  done
  [[ -n "$al" ]] || return 1
  link "$lock" "$al" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [[ -f "$al" && ! -L "$al" ]] || { rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  # `{ exec …; } 2>/dev/null`, NEVER `exec … 2>/dev/null`. MEASURED: `exec`
  # with redirections and NO COMMAND applies them to THE SHELL, permanently, so
  # the bare form silences ccd's stderr for the rest of the process — which is
  # how `_spawn_start`'s operator-facing "--resume … left no session" warning
  # vanished under this function's first draft, caught by
  # `ccd-spawn-split.test.ts`. A `{ …; }` group is not a subshell, so the
  # `{fd}` assignment still lands here and a failed open still returns 1.
  { exec {fd}<>"$al"; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null || true
  _compact_lock_same "$fd" "$lock" || { { exec {fd}>&-; } 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  flock -w "$wait" "$fd" 2>/dev/null || { { exec {fd}>&-; } 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  _compact_lock_same "$fd" "$lock" || { { exec {fd}>&-; } 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  COMPACT_LOCK_FD="$fd"
  return 0
}

_compact_lock_release() {   # <fd>
  local fd="${1-}"
  [[ -n "$fd" ]] || return 0
  { exec {fd}>&-; } 2>/dev/null || true
  return 0
}

_compact_lock_why_remedy() {   # <id> <verb> -> the ONE sentence a durable
                                # purge caller journals for the CURRENT
                                # COMPACT_LOCK_WHY (r6 R5-M3). ONE renderer
                                # consulted by all four durable callers
                                # (`ws-rm`, `ws-reap`, `ws-gc --prune`,
                                # `forget`) instead of four copies, so a
                                # single caller's text drifting from the
                                # others is impossible rather than merely
                                # unpinned — the reviewer's own named
                                # alternative to a fifth `LEGS` fixture.
                                #
                                # `<id>` IS THE ROW ID, spelled however the
                                # caller already spells it: `ws-gc --prune`
                                # passes `$project-$slug`, the other three
                                # pass the literal `$id`. `<verb>` selects only
                                # the SHAPE of the two clauses that are
                                # caller-specific rather than token-specific:
                                #   - the RE-RUN clause, empty `WHY` only:
                                #     `ws-gc --prune` sweeps many rows and
                                #     names none in a re-run command; `ws-reap`
                                #     names none either (nothing about a
                                #     completed reap is re-run); `ws-rm` and
                                #     `forget` each name themselves and `$id`.
                                #   - the SURVIVING-STATE clause, every token:
                                #     `ws-rm`/`forget`/`ws-reap` have already
                                #     torn the workspace down, so "$REG/$id.*
                                #     and $REG/$id.generation still stand" is
                                #     the true residue; `ws-gc --prune`
                                #     DECLINES before anything irreversible
                                #     (`_lc_refuse_return`, never `_lc_fail`),
                                #     so "the registry row is untouched" is
                                #     the true fact there instead — a
                                #     different claim, not a style choice.
  local id="$1" verb="$2" tail sentence=""
  if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
    tail="the registry row is untouched"
  else
    tail="$REG/$id.* and $REG/$id.generation still stand"
  fi
  case "${COMPACT_LOCK_WHY:-}" in
    '')
      case "$verb" in
        'ws-gc --prune') sentence="$REG/.$id.compactions.lock was unavailable; $tail" ;;
        ws-reap) sentence="$REG/.$id.compactions.lock was unavailable. $tail; re-run once the compaction settles" ;;
        *) sentence="$REG/.$id.compactions.lock was unavailable. $tail; re-run ccd $verb $id once the compaction settles" ;;
      esac ;;
    canonical-vanished)
      if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock has been unlinked while a live holder still owns its inode, and ccd will not mint a second one over it, so no compaction can settle and a re-run cannot help; $tail, and if no holder remains, restore $REG/.$id.compactions.lock by hand"
      else
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock has been unlinked while a live holder still owns its inode, and ccd will not mint a second one over it, so no compaction can settle and a re-run cannot help. $tail; if no holder remains, restore $REG/.$id.compactions.lock by hand and re-run"
      fi ;;
    lock-path-occupied)
      if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock is not a regular file: a symlink, directory or other object holds the lock pathname, and ccd neither writes through it nor replaces it, so no compaction can settle and a re-run cannot help; $tail, and the remedy is to confirm no holder remains and then remove that object by hand"
      else
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock is not a regular file: a symlink, directory or other object holds the lock pathname, and ccd neither writes through it nor replaces it, so no compaction can settle and a re-run cannot help. $tail; confirm no holder remains, then remove that object by hand and re-run"
      fi ;;
    lock-source-refused)
      # r6 R5-M2: the guard cannot distinguish `$REG` itself refusing the
      # write from a failed fork or fd exhaustion elsewhere on the box, so
      # this reports the MEASUREMENT and offers the cause as the usual one,
      # never the only one, and never claims a re-run cannot help "until" a
      # repair — only "while" whatever actually failed still holds.
      if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock does not exist and ccd could not create the private source it mints the lock from in $REG; the usual cause is $REG being full, read-only or not writable by this user, but any failure of the mint is reported here, and a re-run cannot help while that holds; $tail — repair $REG"
      else
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — $REG/.$id.compactions.lock does not exist and ccd could not create the private source it mints the lock from in $REG; the usual cause is $REG being full, read-only or not writable by this user, but any failure of the mint is reported here, and a re-run cannot help while that holds. $tail; repair $REG, then re-run"
      fi ;;
    lock-publish-failed)
      # r7 R6-I1: the `link` that publishes the private source AS the lock
      # swallows every errno, so this is the one refusal that leaves NOTHING at
      # the lock pathname — which is why it cannot wear `lock-path-occupied`'s
      # sentence, every clause of which asserts an object is there and asks a
      # human to remove it.
      #
      # AND IT NAMES THE STATE, NOT AN ACT (r8 R8-M1, finishing what r7 began).
      # The wording this replaces said the publish "could not be published",
      # which is an ACT this guard never observes: the re-test reaches this
      # token whenever the pathname is EMPTY, and a `link` that SUCCEEDED and
      # was unlinked by a stranger one instant later arrives here too —
      # MEASURED through the real harness with a `link` shim that publishes for
      # real and then removes what it published, which answers exactly this
      # token. Every clause of the act-wording is false in that state: the
      # publish happened, `$REG` is healthy, and "repair $REG" is unperformable.
      # So this says what the guard saw — nothing at the pathname — offers the
      # `$REG` causes as the usual ones rather than the only ones, and names the
      # second route explicitly, for the same reason `lock-source-refused`
      # above reports its measurement instead of its likeliest cause.
      if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — the private lock source was minted but nothing stands at $REG/.$id.compactions.lock; the usual cause is $REG being full, over quota, read-only or moved, but any state that leaves the lock unpublished is reported here — including one removed the instant after it was created — and a re-run cannot help while that holds; $tail — repair $REG"
      else
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — the private lock source was minted but nothing stands at $REG/.$id.compactions.lock; the usual cause is $REG being full, over quota, read-only or moved, but any state that leaves the lock unpublished is reported here — including one removed the instant after it was created — and a re-run cannot help while that holds. $tail; repair $REG, then re-run"
      fi ;;
    *)
      if [[ "$verb" == 'ws-gc --prune' ]]; then
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — the acquire named a condition this caller carries no remedy for, so it is not ordinary contention and a wait is not the answer; $tail — inspect $REG/.$id.compactions.lock by hand"
      else
        sentence="$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — the acquire named a condition this caller carries no remedy for, so it is not ordinary contention and a wait is not the answer. $tail; inspect $REG/.$id.compactions.lock by hand"
      fi ;;
  esac
  # FAIL CLOSED, NEVER EMPTY (r7 R6-I3). Every arm above assigns `sentence`, so
  # on a tree where each token has an arm this line is unreachable — which is
  # exactly why it is here. MEASURED before it existed: delete the catch-all
  # arm, hand the helper an out-of-vocabulary token, and `sentence` stayed
  # UNSET; ccd runs `set -uo pipefail` with no `-e`, so the production capture
  # form (`_rm_why=$(_compact_lock_why_remedy …)`, stdout only) swallowed the
  # unbound-variable death in the subshell and the durable journal line ended
  # "could not be purged: " with nothing after the colon. A remedy that says
  # nothing is worse than one that says it has nothing to say, so this names
  # the token and the pathname a human can look at instead.
  #
  # AND IT CARRIES THE RESIDUE, because the residue is not a remedy (r8 R8-M2).
  # This line said what ccd does not know and stopped there, dropping the one
  # clause every other arm carries — what SURVIVED the act this sentence
  # follows. `$tail` is computed above the `case`, before any token is read, so
  # it is true for every token there will ever be and for this line by
  # construction: `ws-gc --prune` declined before anything irreversible and its
  # row is untouched; the other three have already torn the workspace down and
  # own the registry residue instead. Having no remedy to give is no reason to
  # withhold a fact the caller measured. A SINGLE form is still right here
  # because this line prescribes no re-run — that is the clause the verb
  # selects, and it is the one thing this condition genuinely cannot supply.
  [[ -n "$sentence" ]] || sentence="${COMPACT_LOCK_WHY:-(the acquire named no condition)} — ccd carries no remedy text for this condition; $tail — inspect $REG/.$id.compactions.lock by hand"
  printf '%s' "$sentence"
}

_compact_lock_why_short() {   # -> the SHORT clause the `ws-gc --prune` sweep's
                              # STDOUT TALLY names for the CURRENT
                              # COMPACT_LOCK_WHY (r8 R8-M3).
                              #
                              # THE SWEEP REPORT AND THE DURABLE RECORD DID NOT
                              # AGREE. One line after `_pr_why` renders the
                              # token-specific remedy into the journal, the
                              # status-1 arm told the human reading the report
                              # "the compaction lock was unavailable" — the
                              # CONTENTION story — unconditionally, i.e. for
                              # `canonical-vanished`, `lock-path-occupied`,
                              # `lock-source-refused` and `lock-publish-failed`
                              # alike: the four conditions the durable record
                              # beside it explicitly refuses to call contention.
                              # The shape was already available — the status-2
                              # sibling distinguishes its own cause in the same
                              # report — and the sweep is the surface an
                              # operator actually watches.
                              #
                              # KEYED ON THE SAME TOKENS AS THE REMEDY HELPER,
                              # and `(d1e)` derives the vocabulary from the
                              # acquire and requires an arm here for every one
                              # of it, exactly as it does there — so the two
                              # renderers cannot drift and a fifth condition
                              # cannot gain a remedy while keeping the
                              # contention tally.
                              #
                              # NO ARGUMENTS AND NO INTERMEDIATE VARIABLE. The
                              # short form carries nothing caller-specific: the
                              # row id is already in the tally line the caller
                              # builds, and the re-run and surviving-state
                              # clauses the verb selects belong to the DURABLE
                              # sentence, not to a one-line report row. And
                              # every arm is its own `printf`, so there is no
                              # `sentence` to leave unset and no fail-closed
                              # line to add: the `*)` arm IS the guard the
                              # remedy helper needs one for.
                              #
                              # AND EVERY ARM NAMES A STATE, NOT AN ACT (wb
                              # R9-M1). `lock-publish-failed`'s short form said
                              # the lock "was never published" — the act-wording
                              # its own durable twin deliberately dropped one
                              # round earlier, because the post-`link` re-test
                              # reaches this token whenever the pathname is
                              # EMPTY, which includes a publish that SUCCEEDED
                              # and was unlinked one instant later. Two
                              # renderers keyed on one vocabulary were telling
                              # an operator two incompatible stories about one
                              # state, and this is the one on the surface an
                              # operator watches.
  case "${COMPACT_LOCK_WHY:-}" in
    '')                  printf '%s' "the compaction lock was unavailable" ;;
    canonical-vanished)  printf '%s' "$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — the compaction lock was unlinked under a live holder" ;;
    lock-path-occupied)  printf '%s' "$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — something that is not a regular file holds the compaction lock pathname" ;;
    lock-source-refused) printf '%s' "$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — ccd could not create the private source it mints the compaction lock from" ;;
    lock-publish-failed) printf '%s' "$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — nothing stands at the compaction lock pathname" ;;
    *)                   printf '%s' "$COMPACT_LOCK_WHY — a condition this sweep carries no short form for" ;;
  esac
}

# ── ROW GENERATION: immutable authorization (D-2605, spec §3.4) ──────────
# `$REG/<id>.generation` is AUTHORIZATION and nothing else — not `.uuid`, not a
# payload `session_id`, not journal data, and it never occurs in the sixteen-key
# record. Exactly 36 lowercase ASCII UUID bytes and NO terminal LF.
#
# WHY THE BYTE COUNT IS A CONTRACT rather than a convention: an invalid PRESENT
# generation is never repaired, replaced or removed — so a writer that shipped
# 37 bytes (the trailing LF `_plat_uuid` emits on both its branches) would fail
# the grammar on every later read, nothing could mint over it, and no compliant
# cleanup would unlink it. The row would be wedged for ever. Measured on this
# box: `cat /proc/sys/kernel/random/uuid` is 37 bytes on disk, `$( )` capture is
# 36 characters, and a 37-byte file is rejected by the grammar below because
# bash's `$` anchor refuses a trailing LF.
_reg_generation_valid() {   # <value> -> 0 iff exactly the 36-byte lowercase UUID grammar
  [[ "${1-}" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{8}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{4}-[0-9a-f]{12}$ ]] || return 1
  (( ${#1} == 36 )) || return 1
  return 0
}

# CLASSIFY THE PATHNAME BEFORE READING IT. Genuine absence is the ONLY condition
# that may mint, so it has to be distinguishable from every present-invalid one
# — and a DANGLING SYMLINK is present-and-invalid, never absent, which is why
# `-L` is asked beside `-e` — the same one-expression pairing `_reg_read`
# (`grep -n '^_reg_read()' ccd/ccd`) uses to classify a field before it reads
# it, and the concrete form `_compact_lock_acquire` spells for canonical. (This
# sentence used to credit `_reg_field_state`, a name that has never existed on
# either tree, so the argument rested on a precedent a reader could not check;
# the argument itself is unchanged and stands on its own.) Folding `-L` into
# absence would let a mint clobber a name somebody else owns.
#
# The value is read through an OWNED HARD-LINK ALIAS on a retained FD, never
# `_reg_get`, `_reg_read` or a bare `cat`: those name the canonical path twice
# (once to test, once to read) and a replacement between the two is invisible
# to them. The same-inode recheck after the read is what closes that.
_reg_generation_read() {   # <id> -> 0 + REG_GENERATION; 1 present-but-invalid; 2 genuinely absent
  # `"$REG/$1.generation"`, NOT `"$REG/$id.generation"`, and the distinction is
  # MEASURED rather than stylistic: `local` DECLARES every name in the statement
  # before it performs any assignment, so under ccd's `set -u` an initialiser
  # that references an earlier name in the SAME `local` dies with `id: unbound
  # variable`. (Measured on bash 5.2.21; it is also why `_compact_lock_acquire`
  # above spells its own path off `$1`.)
  local id="$1" p="$REG/$1.generation" al="" fd="" tries=0 v=""
  REG_GENERATION=""
  if [ ! -e "$p" ] && [ ! -L "$p" ]; then return 2; fi
  [[ -f "$p" && ! -L "$p" && -r "$p" ]] || return 1
  command -v link >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  while (( tries < 8 )); do
    al="$REG/.$id.generation-read.$$.$RANDOM.$RANDOM"
    [ -e "$al" ] || [ -L "$al" ] || break
    al=""; tries=$(( tries + 1 ))
  done
  [[ -n "$al" ]] || return 1
  link "$p" "$al" 2>/dev/null || return 1
  [[ -f "$al" && ! -L "$al" ]] || { rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  { exec {fd}<"$al"; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  rm -f "$al" 2>/dev/null || true
  # 37, NOT 36: a 37-character read is what SEES a trailing LF or a longer file,
  # where a 36-character one would silently accept the first 36 bytes of either.
  IFS= read -r -N 37 v <&"$fd" 2>/dev/null || true
  # STILL THE SAME INODE, before the value is trusted: a canonical replaced
  # since the `link` is a different row's authorization wearing this name.
  if ! _compact_lock_same "$fd" "$p"; then { exec {fd}<&-; } 2>/dev/null; return 1; fi
  { exec {fd}<&-; } 2>/dev/null || true
  _reg_generation_valid "$v" || return 1
  REG_GENERATION="$v"
  return 0
}

# GENUINE ABSENCE ONLY. Mints from a validated `_plat_uuid`, writes the no-LF
# bytes to a private `mktemp` source under `umask 077`, and publishes by
# NO-CLOBBER `link` — so two racers cannot both win, and EEXIST means
# reclassify the incumbent rather than repair it. The source goes on every
# handled result.
_reg_generation_mint() {   # <id> -> 0 published or an incumbent stands; 1 refused
  local id="$1" p="$REG/$1.generation" src="" v=""
  command -v mktemp >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  command -v link   >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 1
  v=$(_plat_uuid 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  # THE STRIP IS THE WRITER'S JOB, not the generator's: `_plat_uuid` emits a
  # trailing LF on BOTH branches. `$( )` already removes it; the byte check
  # below is what turns that from an accident into a mechanism.
  _reg_generation_valid "$v" || return 1
  src=$( umask 077; mktemp "$REG/.$id.generation-init.XXXXXX" 2>/dev/null ) || return 1
  [[ -f "$src" && ! -L "$src" ]] || { rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  { printf '%s' "$v" > "$src"; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  [[ "$(wc -c < "$src" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')" == 36 ]] || { rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null; return 1; }
  link "$src" "$p" 2>/dev/null || true
  rm -f "$src" 2>/dev/null || true
  return 0
}

# THE ONE MINTING SITE'S ENTRY POINT, called by row creation and by nobody else.
# `_spawn_start` READS AND VALIDATES; it never mints. A row with no generation
# is answered by the standing fail-closed rule downstream, never by a mint at
# spawn time — minting there would hand a fresh authorization to a row that was
# purged or reused in the gap.
_reg_generation_init() {   # <id> -> 0 the row carries a valid generation; 1 refused
  local rc=0
  _reg_generation_read "$1"; rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) return 0 ;;
    1) return 1 ;;     # PRESENT-INVALID is never repaired, replaced or removed
    *) ;;              # genuine absence: the only mint condition
  esac
  _reg_generation_mint "$1" || return 1
  _reg_generation_read "$1"      # EEXIST reclassifies: read back whoever won
}

# ONE RECORDER for every tracked unlink in `_reg_purge` below, so its STATUS
# and the pathname it blames can never disagree. A site that set `_pg_urc`
# without appending the path — or appended without setting it — would hand the
# caller either a failure it cannot describe or a description of a failure the
# caller never hears about, and describing a failure the caller cannot see is
# the fabricated-cause defect this whole seam exists to remove.
#
# `_pg_urc` is `_reg_purge`'s own `local`, reached here through bash's dynamic scope. TWO sites reach it —
# `_reg_purge`'s own unlinks and `_usage_purge`, whose only call site is inside `_reg_purge` — so it is still
# ONE dynamic extent, and why this is defined immediately beside it: a caller outside that extent would be
# assigning into whatever `_pg_urc` happened to be in scope, which is not a mechanism.
#
# THE SEPARATOR IS PROSE, NOT A GRAMMAR. `$REG/<id>.<suffix>`, `$REG/.<id>.<suffix>` and — since
# the sidecar removals became tracked — `$REG/usage/<id>.json`, `$REG/usage/.<id>.*.tmp` (the GLOB
# PATTERN, never an expansion) and `$REG/usage/<id>.agents` are the shapes that reach here. Ids are
# `<project>-<slug>` and every suffix is dot-free or a private family, so no pathname carries a
# comma today — but nothing DEPENDS on that: the value is a sentence for a human and for the
# journal's `detail`, and nothing parses it back into a list.
_reg_purge_unremoved() {   # path — record one refused unlink AND the status it implies
  _pg_urc=3
  REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED="${REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED:+$REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED, }$1"
}

_reg_purge() {   # id -> rm -f every "$REG/$id.<field>" registry file for id
  # final-review-destructive finding F3. The THREE call sites that used to spell
  # this `rm -f "$REG/$id".*` directly were an unanchored glob: `$id.*`
  # matches `$id.uuid` (this session's own field, one dot, no more) but ALSO
  # `$id.x-y.uuid` — ANOTHER session's field, whose own id happens to be
  # `$id.x-y`. Measured: `$REG/demo-quiet-basin.uuid`,
  # `$REG/demo-quiet-basin.x-y.uuid` and `$REG/demo-quiet-basin.x-y.workdir`
  # side by side, `rm -f "$REG/demo-quiet-basin".*` — zero survivors. Ids are
  # `<project>-<slug>` and slugs are dot-free (`_ws_slug_valid`), so the dot
  # has to come from a PROJECT DIRECTORY name (`demo-quiet-basin.x` beside
  # `demo`) — contrived, but dots in project directory names are legal and
  # ordinary, and the consequence is not local: the victim loses its `.uuid`,
  # `_ws_gc_row` then classifies it `orphan`, and `ws-gc --prune`'s orphan
  # arm runs `git worktree remove` and `git branch -d` on it.
  #
  # Fixed by filtering on the SUFFIX rather than matching every known field
  # name — a field name never contains a dot, so `$id.<field>` has exactly one
  # dot after the id and `$id.x-y.uuid` has two. This is future-proof against a
  # field this file adds tomorrow, which a hardcoded allowlist would not be —
  # the two are the same shape either way.
  #
  # The dot-free claim, measured against every `$REG/<id>.<field>` file a
  # session has today — and that SHAPE is the scope of the claim, corrected
  # (#69 review round 3): one per-session registry file sits outside it and is
  # neither listed below nor purged. `$REG/.<id>.starts`, the start-limiter
  # window (`grep -n '_sl_file=' ccd/ccd`), leads with a DOT, so the loop's
  # `"$REG/$id".*` glob cannot match it and neither explicit `rm -f` names it.
  # Disclosed rather than fixed here: it is a handful of bytes per reaped row,
  # it belongs to the start limiter rather than to this wave, and widening the
  # glob is a change to what `_reg_purge` MEANS — the nested-id guard below
  # turns on that glob's shape. What is not acceptable is the sentence that
  # stood here, which claimed a completeness it had not measured, in the one
  # paragraph whose own moral is that "an inventory that omits files is an
  # inventory a future reader trusts and a future writer copies".
  # THE COUNT MOVED IN THE MERGE, and that is the half a textual union loses:
  # #70 added `compactnote` and `compactskip` while this round rewrote the
  # paragraph around them, so both sides were right and the cardinal belonged
  # to neither. 34 + 2 = 36, re-measured on the merged file rather than added
  # up — the same discipline this paragraph is about. The drawer wave then added
  # `stalenarrownote` and `swapnarrownote` (`_pane_narrow_note`'s per-site log
  # floors): 38; CCR-15's `child` made 39 — by addition, NOT a fresh census of
  # every per-session field, which this list still is not. `ROUTE_FIELDS` (`class`,
  # `effort`, `subagent`, `workflow`, `compact`, `degraded`, `inert`) and
  # `$REG/<id>.stalepress`/`.tscan` are all real per-session fields this list
  # omits; the gap is pre-existing, not new, and this paragraph's count tracks
  # only the additions named above against the last stated total.
  # The 39: `archived`, `archivedreason`, `archivemanifest`, `base`, `branch`,
  # `child`, `compactnote`, `compactskip`, `crosspool`, `hold`, `home`, `hookstate`, `lastcompact`, `lastswap`,
  # `pool`, `prcheckedat`, `prhistory`, `prnumber`, `prphase`, `project`, `rc`,
  # `reaping`, `setup`, `spawn`, `stalenarrownote`, `started`, `stopped`, `stranded`,
  # `strandnotify`, `substrate`, `supervised`, `svcfailed`, `swapblocked`,
  # `swapnarrownote`, `tickstuck`, `uuid`, `workdir`, `workspace`, `wrapper`. Note that `pool` here is the per-session
  # CANDIDATE LIST `_pool_for` reads, NOT an account pool name — two meanings
  # of one word in one file, disclosed rather than renamed. That is a WIDER
  # set than `_reg_set`/`_reg_get`: the inventory
  # that stood here named only the shell-written fields and so silently dropped
  # `prcheckedat`, `prphase` and `prhistory` (written by `_pr_py`'s own
  # `put`/append) and `hookstate` (written by the hooks). The purge itself was
  # always right — it
  # matches the SUFFIX shape, not a list — but an inventory that omits files is
  # an inventory a future reader trusts and a future writer copies.
  # AND THE ORDER IS PART OF THE CONTRACT — final-round verification P4. This
  # loop unlinks one file at a time in GLOB order, i.e. alphabetically, so
  # `archived` went first and `reaping` (r) several files later. A SIGKILL or an
  # OOM between them — the failure model the reap lock exists for, and this
  # project's speciality — left `$REG/<id>.reaping=clips` standing beside a
  # MISSING `$REG/<id>.archived`. Pre-fix that state was resumable: the next
  # `ws-reap` took the resume fork, re-ran (h) and re-ran this purge. Since the
  # F5 archived re-read went into `_ws_reap_tail` it is not: the tail refuses
  # `not-archived` for ever, and `ws-archive` cannot be re-run on a half-purged
  # registry.
  #
  # The invariant that closes it is one line long — `reaping` must never outlive
  # `archived` — so the two STATE markers are removed last and in that order,
  # after every ordinary field. Every intermediate is then either still
  # resumable (both markers present; the tail's clips arm needs only `$id`) or
  # already past resuming. The residue of the very last window is a single empty
  # `$id.archived` file with nothing else beside it: invisible to `ws-gc`'s
  # dead-reg arm (which keys on `.workspace` and `.uuid`) and to `ws-reap`
  # (which keys on `.uuid`), so it leaks — a zero-byte file, disclosed here,
  # against a permanent wedge on the destructive path.
  #
  # THE COST OF THAT ORDERING IS PAID AT `_ws_slug_free`, verification round 3
  # P1. Removing the two markers last makes them OUTLIVE `.uuid` by
  # construction, and `_ws_slug_free` used to ask only `[[ ! -f $id.uuid ]]`.
  # So the residue above is not merely leaked, it is INHERITED: the slug reads
  # free, `cmd_ws_add` re-uses it (it purges nothing first), and the brand-new
  # workspace is born wearing a stranger's `.archived`/`.reaping`. Measured on
  # that state: `ws-archive` answers `already archived` at exit 0 having
  # stopped no session and written no manifest (ccd:5109 below), `_ws_reap_eval`
  # Phase A and `_ws_reap_tail` both PASS the archived gate on a workspace that
  # was never archived, and with `.reaping` present `_ws_reap_locked` takes the
  # resume fork — where the token is not checked and Phase C, the merged-PR
  # proof, is deliberately not re-run. `_ws_slug_free` is therefore an
  # ANY-FIELD check now; see the note there.
  # ── THE BACKSTOP (D3) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # HERE, AND NOT ONE LINE LOWER. Every destruction path on this box terminates
  # in this function — ws-rm (ccd:3942), ws-reap (ccd:9876), ws-gc's dead-reg
  # arm (ccd:10370), forget (ccd:11540) — and a destructive verb added LATER that
  # forgets to journal itself still leaves a record. A silent destruction has to
  # defeat two independent emit sites.
  #
  # BEFORE the loop below, because the loop is what makes `meas` unreadable:
  # the loop unlinks every dot-free `$REG/<id>.<suffix>` except `archived` and
  # `reaping`, so an emit placed after it can only report an id. Moving this
  # line down is the mutant `ccd-lifecycle-purge.test.ts` measures.
  #
  # UNCONDITIONAL, and with NO `tx`: this is not half of a pair. It is the
  # terminal fact — the registry entry ceased to exist — and it is worth writing
  # even for a row that has nothing left, where the id alone is the record. No
  # `verb` either: this function cannot see its caller, and guessing one would
  # be the fabricated fact the design exists to refuse.
  # ── D-2605: THE STABLE LOCK, and it is the ONE new statement allowed to
  # stand between this function's opening brace and the unconditional
  # unconditional purge-done emit below. Nothing else may go here: the landed
  # `ccd-lifecycle-purge.test.ts` "is unconditional" pin checks only
  # emit-before-unlink-loop and the absence of a trailing `||`/`&&`, and both
  # stay GREEN under a silently inserted conditional guard — which is why that
  # test is strengthened with a source scan in the same commit rather than left
  # as the sole protection.
  #
  # MECHANISM ABSENCE IS GATED ON THE GENERATION, not blanket. `<id>.generation`
  # is the witness that this row's hooks were ever handed one: it is minted ONLY
  # by a flock-capable row creation, deleted LAST by this function, and replaced
  # on safe reuse. So a PRESENT generation proves the row's hooks may hold this
  # lock now, and destroying the row under them would be the race the lock
  # exists to exclude — refuse. An ABSENT generation proves no hook on this row
  # ever received one, so no hook arm ever ran the lifecycle and there is
  # nothing to race — proceed. The permanent lock is NOT the witness: it spans
  # generations and is minted even by a purge against a never-existed id, so it
  # proves history rather than a live regime, and gating on it would wedge a
  # genuinely flock-less box fleet-wide.
  # ── THREE NONZERO STATUSES, BECAUSE THERE ARE THREE CONDITIONS ───────────
  # A caller that cannot tell them apart has to guess, and every caller guessed
  # the same wrong one: that a nonzero meant the lock. Measured on this tree
  # before the split — a dead-reg row plus ONE un-removable field made
  # `_ws_gc_prune_row` journal `outcome:"refused"`, `refusal:"purge-refused"`,
  # `detail:"…compactions.lock was unavailable; the registry row is untouched"`
  # while the lock had been HELD, the row was destroyed, `uuid`/`project`/
  # `workspace`/`branch`/`workdir`/`generation` were all gone and a `purge`
  # done-fact was already on disk. Every clause of that record was false.
  #
  #   1  PRE-EMIT LOCK REFUSAL.        Nothing deleted, no purge fact.
  #   2  MECHANISM ABSENT + a live generation. Nothing deleted, no purge fact.
  #   3  POST-EMIT REMOVAL FAILURE.    The row IS destroyed, the purge fact IS
  #      journaled, and `REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED` names what still stands.
  #
  # 1 and 2 are the two halves of "nothing happened"; 3 is the opposite of
  # both, and it is the one a caller must not report as a refusal — `_lc_fail`'s
  # own rule is that a refusal precedes anything irreversible and a failure
  # follows it, and by the time any `rm -f` can fail the emit has already gone.
  local _pg_fd="" _pg_rc=0 _pg_urc=0
  # CLEARED ON ENTRY, unconditionally. `ccd` sources itself into its tests and
  # a shell may purge twice; a stale pathname from the previous call read as
  # this one's would be exactly the fabricated fact the split exists to end.
  REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED=""
  _compact_lock_acquire "$1" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; _pg_rc=$?
  if (( _pg_rc == 2 )); then
    # MECHANISM ABSENT. Fail open only on a generation-ABSENT row.
    if [ -e "$REG/$1.generation" ] || [ -L "$REG/$1.generation" ]; then return 2; fi
  elif (( _pg_rc != 0 )); then
    return 1
  else
    _pg_fd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
  fi
  # `_lc_done` returns 0 on every path, and this emit still carries no condition
  # of its own and still precedes the unlink loop — so nothing HERE gates the
  # purge. What gates it now sits one level up: since D-2605 the whole function
  # runs under the row's stable compaction lock, so a lock miss yields no purge,
  # no purge-done fact, and an `_lc_fail` from the post-action callers. A
  # REMOVAL failure is a different condition and reaches nothing above: the
  # unlink loop runs strictly after this emit, so by the time any `rm -f` can
  # fail the fact is already journaled.
  _lc_done purge "$1" "" \
    meas.project        "$(_reg_get "$1" project)" \
    meas.workspace      "$(_reg_get "$1" workspace)" \
    meas.branch         "$(_reg_get "$1" branch)" \
    meas.uuid           "$(_reg_get "$1" uuid)" \
    meas.wrapper        "$(_reg_get "$1" wrapper)" \
    meas.workdir        "$(_reg_get "$1" workdir)" \
    meas.archivedAt     "$(_reg_get "$1" archived)" \
    meas.archivedReason "$(_reg_get "$1" archivedreason)" \
    meas.held           "$(_reg_get "$1" hold)"
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  local id="$1" f suffix
  for f in "$REG/$id".*; do
    # `[[ -e "$f" ]]` for the unmatched-glob case: this file does not set
    # `nullglob`, so a glob with no matches stays the literal string
    # `$REG/$id.*`, and `-e` on that is false — the loop body never runs.
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    suffix="${f#"$REG/$id."}"
    [[ "$suffix" == *.* ]] && continue
    # `generation` IS A MECHANISM, NOT AN OUTCOME (D-2605): it is a dot-free
    # suffix like any other field, so without this arm the loop deletes it in
    # ordinary glob order and "generation last" is a no-op comment.
    [[ "$suffix" == archived || "$suffix" == reaping || "$suffix" == generation ]] && continue
    # A REMOVAL FAILURE IS ITS OWN CONDITION (spec §3.4), AND ITS OWN STATUS.
    # Every unlink from here down is tracked and the function returns 3 — never
    # the 1 a pre-emit lock refusal returns — if any of them failed. The EMIT
    # above stays unconditional and unmoved, so the purge-done fact is already
    # on disk by the time any of this can fail, and such a run reports the fact
    # WITH a failure status and the un-removable pathname in
    # `REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED`: the row may be destroyed and something beside it
    # may still be standing, which is neither of the two "nothing happened"
    # conditions. The four callers branch on the value, not on the boolean.
    rm -f "$f" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$f"
  done
  # `hookstate.json` (session-hook.sh's `$REG/$id.hookstate.json`, the
  # session-status file the Claude Code hooks write on every tool call) is an
  # ordinary field with no ordering invariant of its own — it belongs here,
  # with the fields above, not in the archived/reaping tail below. But its
  # OWN name holds a dot, so the loop's `[[ "$suffix" == *.* ]]` skip (the
  # nested-id guard the long note above measures) reads
  # `$REG/$id.hookstate.json` as a two-dot stranger and leaves it standing
  # rather than unlinking it — the same shape as `$id.x-y.uuid`, just
  # produced by a field name instead of a project directory name. Removed
  # explicitly here rather than exempted from the loop's filter, so the
  # nested-id guard stays a single, simple rule.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.hookstate.json" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$REG/$id.hookstate.json"
  _usage_purge "$id"   # its three sidecar removals are tracked too — see its own header
  rm -f "$REG/$id.reaping" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$REG/$id.reaping"
  # CONDITIONAL, so the invariant is a mechanism rather than a hope: an `rm`
  # that did not take the breadcrumb must not be followed by one that takes the
  # marker, because `reaping` standing beside a missing `archived` is precisely
  # the wedge this ordering exists to prevent. It is also what makes the order
  # observable to a test — nothing else about two adjacent unlinks is.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.reaping" ]] || rm -f "$REG/$id.archived" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$REG/$id.archived"
  # THE DOT-LEADING PRIVATE COMPACTION FAMILIES, which the loop above cannot
  # see (its glob is `"$REG/$id".*` — id, then a dot) and which would otherwise
  # hold the slug for ever under the widened `_ws_slug_free`. The permanent
  # lock is skipped BY NAME: it spans row generations and safe reuse by design.
  local pf psuffix
  for pf in "$REG/.$id".*; do
    [[ -e "$pf" ]] || continue
    psuffix="${pf#"$REG/.$id."}"
    _ws_private_family "$psuffix" || continue
    rm -f "$pf" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$pf"
  done
  # GENERATION LAST, after the archived/reaping tail and after every private
  # family — and OUTSIDE the loop above, which skips it. It is the row's
  # authorization: while it stands, a hook holding it can still take the lock,
  # so it is the last thing to go.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.generation" || _reg_purge_unremoved "$REG/$id.generation"
  _compact_lock_release "$_pg_fd"
  return "$_pg_urc"
}
_substrate_mark() {   # id — record "this supervisor could not reach tmux" ($REG/<id>.substrate)
  # ONE writer per file: this row's supervisor — and the FIRST WRITE WINS. The epoch is
  # the fault's ONSET ("could not reach tmux SINCE <epoch>", spec §2) and the skew
  # comparison rides the same first write; the first version of this function rewrote the
  # file on every unknown tick, which fabricated the duration (the epoch was never more
  # than one tick old) and destroyed the skew diagnosis one tick after it was recorded
  # (branch review, confirmed major). Nothing needs a rewrite: freshness is `supervised`'s
  # job, and the marker's life is bounded by _substrate_clear on the first live tick.
  # The fleet-wide statement is DERIVED by the reader, never written. Text is never empty.
  local id="$1" why="${PROBE_DETAIL:-tmux gave no reason}" f="$REG/$1.substrate"
  [[ -e "$f" ]] && return 0
  local client server
  client=$(tmux -V 2>&1 || true)
  # Same function-vs-binary split as _session_probe, same reason: `timeout` execs.
  if [[ $(type -t tmux) == function ]]; then
    server=$(tmux display-message -p '#{version}' 2>/dev/null) || server="unreachable"
  else
    server=$(_plat_timeout "$SUBSTRATE_PROBE_DEADLINE_S" tmux display-message -p '#{version}' 2>/dev/null) || server="unreachable"
  fi
  # Through `_reg_set` (wave 2), so the marker cannot be read half-written like
  # any other field. FIRST-WRITE-WINS is untouched: the `[[ -e "$f" ]]` guard
  # above asks about the DESTINATION name, which rename replaces atomically and
  # never unlinks — there is no instant at which a second supervisor's guard
  # could see the marker missing and re-record a later onset.
  _reg_set "$id" substrate "$(date +%s) $why (client ${client:-unknown}; server ${server:-unreachable})"
}
_substrate_clear() { rm -f -- "$REG/$1.substrate"; }
_ws_seed_home() {   # id wrapper — set home once; never clobber a deliberate choice
  [[ -f "$REG/$1.home" ]] || _reg_set "$1" home "$2"
}

# The systemd calls live in their own functions (rather than inline in cmd_ws_add)
# so the test harness can stub them — sourcing ccd and calling cmd_ws_add under
# vitest would otherwise enable a REAL claude-session@<id> unit on the host.
_ws_supervise()   { rm -f "$REG/$1.stopped"                                   # supervision supersedes an earlier stop
                    # POSITIONAL, and there is no `$id` in this function: it is
                    # a one-liner with no `local`, so the emit must say `$1`.
                    _lc_done supervise "$1" "" meas.wrapper "$(_reg_get "$1" wrapper)"
                    _svc_enable_now "claude-session@$1" 2>/dev/null \
                      || echo "warn: could not enable unit claude-session@$1" >&2; }

_ws_unarchive() {   # id — leave the archived state, and leave ALL of it.
  # THE SINGLE CHOKE POINT for leaving an archive, for the same reason
  # `_ws_unsupervise` is one for entering a stop: two call sites must not be
  # able to disagree about WHAT an archive consists of. They did disagree —
  # `cmd_ws_restore` unlinked all three files while every spawn path unlinked
  # none — and the gap is not cosmetic: a workspace that is RUNNING while
  # `$REG/<id>.archived` still stands is a workspace every cleanup selector
  # calls dormant. Measured 2026-09-16: two live sessions carried the stamp for
  # weeks, one of them mid-deploy on an irreversible step, and a sweep that
  # filtered on the stamp selected both.
  #
  # `.archived` LAST, and that order is the crash argument: it is the field
  # every other reader tests, so a SIGKILL between the unlinks leaves the
  # workspace archived-with-less-detail — a state `ws-restore` still finishes
  # and `ws-reap` still refuses — rather than running-with-archive-residue,
  # which nothing collects and `_ws_slug_free` would still call taken.
  rm -f "$REG/$1.archivedreason" "$REG/$1.archivemanifest"
  rm -f "$REG/$1.archived"
}

_ws_unsupervise() {   # id [surface] — disable the unit AND RECORD THAT SOMEBODY MEANT TO.
  # THE SINGLE CHOKE POINT, which is why the stamp lives here and not in
  # cmd_stop: ws-rm, ws-archive, ws-reap and forget all reach systemd through
  # this function, and an archived workspace with no stamp would classify
  # `orphan` for ever (§4.1). The surface defaults to `ccd` because those four
  # call sites ARE ccd acting on its own account; `cmd_stop` passes its own.
  #
  # The word is text from the wire, so it is validated against the closed set
  # on arrival and anything else becomes `unknown` — it is a DECLARATION, not
  # an authentication, and `--surface pwa` means only that the caller said so.
  #
  # Epoch and surface share ONE field: the registry is read per-field per
  # session on a 2s tick, and packing them keeps `stopped` one read instead of
  # two. The stamp is written BEFORE the disable, because the intent is a fact
  # whether or not systemd cooperates — the disable is already swallowed.
  # `${2-ccd}`, NOT `${2:-ccd}`: the colon form defaults on EMPTY too, so a
  # caller that explicitly declares `--surface ''` (an empty word off the
  # wire) would default straight past the closed-set check to `ccd`,
  # misattributing a PWA-sourced stop to ccd acting on its own account. The
  # bare `-` form defaults only when $2 is truly absent (the four internal
  # call sites, which pass no second argument at all) and leaves an explicit
  # empty string to fall into the `case`'s `*` arm below, same as any other
  # non-member word.
  # `$3` IS THE DECLARATION, `$2` IS THE STAMP. The stamp defaults to `ccd`
  # because the four internal callers ARE ccd acting on its own account, and L0
  # forbids ccd's own defaults from reaching `dec.surface`: a default laundered
  # into a declaration is exactly what `none` exists to prevent. `cmd_stop` is
  # the one caller with something declared to pass, and it passes it here.
  local id="$1" surface="${2-ccd}" declared="${3-}"
  case "$surface" in cli|pwa|agent|ccd) ;; *) surface=unknown ;; esac
  # Through `_reg_set` (wave 2): the two words are one value, so the join moves
  # to the call site and the bytes on disk are unchanged. `_session_state` reads
  # this field to tell a deliberate stop from an orphan — a half-written stamp
  # is a session reclassified.
  _reg_set "$id" stopped "$(date +%s) $surface"
  # THE CHOKE POINT'S ERASURE OF THE VERB IS REPAIRED UPWARD, HERE. The stamp
  # keeps one field for two words and names a software PATH, never a human;
  # this line carries the same act into a record `_reg_purge` cannot reach.
  # `_lc_surface_norm` answers EMPTY when nothing was declared, and the encoder
  # omits an empty value, so an internal call reads `dec.surface:"none"`.
  _lc_done unsupervise "$id" "" dec.surface "$(_lc_surface_norm "$declared")"
  rm -f "$REG/$id.supervised"
  # A silent failure here used to be observable at cmd_stop's own inlined call
  # (the message this replaced); routing all five call sites through one
  # choke point must not mean the warning stops firing for any of them. Mirrors
  # _ws_supervise's own warning on the same failure, one function up.
  _svc_disable_now "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null \
    || echo "ccd: warn: could not disable unit claude-session@$id (it may resurrect if it was enabled)" >&2
}

_session_state() {   # id -> running|unsupervised|unclaimed|stopped|restarting|orphan|never-started
  # The bash twin of shared/'s `sessionLifecycle` (spec §4.3). A pure function
  # of four inputs — pane alive, heartbeat freshness, stop stamp, `started` —
  # evaluated in this exact order; both implementations are driven from one
  # fixture, so the order and the 120-second window are the contract, not an
  # implementation detail.
  #
  # `unmeasurable` is the ONE row of the table this side cannot reach: ccd
  # reads $REG off local disk, where a read either works or the file is
  # genuinely absent. That state exists only on the server's side of the seam,
  # where remote `readFile` collapses missing/forbidden/disconnected into one
  # null and an unreadable registry must never print `orphan`.
  local id="$1" sup fresh=0 now
  sup=$(_reg_get "$id" supervised)
  now=$(date +%s)
  # A garbage stamp is NO stamp: this field is ccd's own, but it is a file on
  # disk, and a truncated one must degrade to "nobody is watching" rather than
  # to a silently-fresh `running`. `>= 0` matters as much as `< 120`: without
  # it a FUTURE-dated stamp (clock skew, or a hand-edited registry) reads as
  # fresh forever — `now - sup` goes negative, which satisfies `< 120`
  # unconditionally and never ages out.
  [[ "$sup" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && (( now - sup >= 0 && now - sup < 120 )) && fresh=1
  if _alive "$id"; then
    # §1.6, and the FIRST rung inside this branch on purpose — the same position
    # the TS ladder puts it in, because both are driven from one fixture. A live
    # pane with no `started` stamp is a session nobody wrote a claim for: the
    # repair is a CLAIM (`ccd ensure`), not a process, which is the exact
    # opposite of `orphan`'s. Checked before the freshness split because the
    # specimen that motivated it was alive AND supervised AND unclaimed.
    [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] || { echo unclaimed; return 0; }
    (( fresh )) && { echo running; return 0; }
    # A pane with no supervisor: no auto-swap, no auto-compact, no uuid-sync,
    # and nothing to record its death. What a pre-fix `ccd start` minted.
    echo unsupervised; return 0
  fi
  # The stop stamp is checked BEFORE the heartbeat so that a stop taken inside
  # the 120s freshness window reads `stopped` immediately, not `restarting`.
  # `-e` not `-f`: present-but-unreadable still means somebody stopped this.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.stopped" ]] && { echo stopped; return 0; }
  (( fresh )) && { echo restarting; return 0; }
  [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && { echo orphan; return 0; }
  echo never-started
}

# ── PR / archive lifecycle helpers ─────────────────────────────────
# `local x=$(cmd)` returns local's status, never cmd's — so anything whose exit
# code is read below declares first and assigns second, deliberately.

_json_str() {   # value -> a JSON string literal (quote/backslash/control safe)
  # THE ENCODING POLICY IS PINNED HERE, because the value may be RAW BYTES and a
  # filename is not text. `_ws_collect_ignored` reads paths with `-z`
  # (deliberately — see there), so `ignored[].path`, `sensitive[]` and the
  # `sensitive-ignored` detail carry whatever the filesystem holds, and a Linux
  # path is any byte string without NUL or `/`.
  #
  # The previous form read stdin through python's LOCALE codec. On this box
  # (`LANG=en_US.UTF-8`) that is utf-8 STRICT, so one 0xff byte raised
  # UnicodeDecodeError, stdout was EMPTY, `$(…)` swallowed the status and
  # `cmd_ws_audit` printed `"ignored":[{"path":,"bytes":626,…}]` — a document
  # JSON.parse rejects, at exit 0, over a workspace whose verdict was
  # `sensitive-ignored` and whose `sensitive` array had silently degraded to
  # `[]`. Under `LC_ALL=C` the same input was fine (python falls back to
  # surrogateescape), so it was locale-dependent as well as wrong.
  #
  # So the bytes are read as BYTES — `sys.stdin.buffer`, which no locale can
  # reach — and decoded utf-8 with errors='replace'. LOSSY, deliberately, and it
  # cannot weaken a guard: every decision over these bytes has already been taken
  # in bash on the RAW bytes (`_ws_sensitive_match`'s `case` globs, `du`'s size,
  # `_ws_ignored_digest`'s hash) before anything is serialised. What is left is
  # the HUMAN's copy, and U+FFFD renders as a visible "this is not text" where a
  # `\udcff` surrogate escape — the round-tripping alternative — is valid JSON
  # that most consumers mangle or refuse to re-encode. `json.dumps` defaults to
  # ensure_ascii, so stdout is pure ASCII whatever the writer's locale; `LC_ALL`
  # is pinned anyway rather than inherited, so nothing here depends on the box.
  #
  # NON-ZERO now means one thing only: python3 could not be RUN. Callers must
  # check it — a bare `$(_json_str …)` inside a printf argument list swallows the
  # status by construction, which is how the hole above got printed — and both
  # callers that build a whole record (`_ws_archive_manifest`, `cmd_ws_audit`) probe once
  # up front and refuse rather than checking nineteen substitutions.
  #
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed and measured: `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8` can be deleted
  # with the suite green, and always will be on a box whose LANG is already a
  # UTF-8 one — `sys.stdin.buffer` bypasses the stdin codec and `ensure_ascii`
  # makes stdout pure ASCII, so between them the call is already locale-proof and
  # no fixture on THIS box can tell the two apart. It is written because the
  # defect it guards against is precisely a difference between boxes: the old
  # form was correct under `LC_ALL=C` and broken under `en_US.UTF-8`, so "it
  # works here" was exactly the evidence that was worthless. Pinning it means a
  # future rewrite that goes back to a text-mode read fails on every box rather
  # than on someone else's.
  printf '%s' "${1-}" | LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c 'import json,sys
sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(sys.stdin.buffer.read().decode("utf-8", "replace")))'
}

# ── lifecycle journal ──────────────────────────────────────────── LC-BEGIN ──
# THE APPEND-ONLY RECORD `_reg_purge` CANNOT REACH.
#
# `$REG/.lifecycle/` is a dot-prefixed DIRECTORY, and that is the whole reason
# the feature is possible: `_reg_purge`'s suffix filter globs `$REG/<id>.*` and
# ids never begin with a dot, so no per-session field can hold a destruction
# record but a dotted directory is untouchable. Precedent already load-bearing:
# `$REG/.reaped/` has had zero deleters in this file since Aug 6.
#
# THE INVENTORY IS `_reg_purge`'S R-3 BOUNDARY PARAGRAPH, and it was amended
# in the same commit as this block — it counted seven dot-prefixed artifacts
# and this block added an eighth. THE LIVE COUNT IS NOT REPEATED HERE, and
# that is the correction: a second copy of a cardinal is how the first one
# goes stale unnoticed, which is exactly the defect `_reg_purge`'s own header
# records having shipped once. That paragraph counts DIRECTORIES, not their
# contents (`.reaped/` is one entry, its tombstones are not counted), so
# `.lifecycle/`'s generations, `errors` and `.rotate.lock` are counted WITH
# it — and `ccd-account-auth.test.ts` now derives its cardinal from the
# shipped bash, so the number lives in one place and a measurement keeps it
# honest.
#
# EVERY FUNCTION HERE RETURNS 0 ON EVERY PATH except `_lc_refuse`, which emits
# and then `die`s so refusal and death cannot drift. The journal is best-effort
# and NEVER gates an act: the disk condition that makes an append fail is
# exactly the condition in which `ws-rm`/`ws-gc --prune`/`ws-reap` are the
# recovery tools. One unrecorded destruction is a gap in a record; a fleet that
# cannot clean up is an outage. A failed append bumps `.lifecycle/errors`.
#
# THE WRAPPERS BIND THEIR POSITIONALS WITH `${1-}`, NOT `$1`. ccd runs
# `set -uo pipefail`: a function reading an unset positional EXITS THE SHELL, it
# does not return. With 21 hand-written call sites, one dropped `""` would turn
# a destructive verb into a mid-verb abort — the precise failure "the journal
# never gates an act" exists to prevent, arriving through the journal itself.
#
# `_lc_refuse` therefore enters `ccd-die-containment.test.ts`'s can-die set:
# it must never be wrapped in `$( )`. `_lc_emit`/`_lc_obs`/`_lc_live`/
# `_lc_rotate`/`_lc_tx`/`_lc_json`/`_lc_err`/`_lc_surface_norm`/`_lc_dec_ok`
# are die-free and safe to capture.
#
# NO CARRY GLOBALS. `tip`, `attic` and `tx` are ARGUMENTS. The `LC_TIP=` idiom
# dies under `set -u` on the first call in a process and appends a blank line
# nobody notices. `_LC_SEQ` and `_LC_OBS` are the two exceptions and both are
# initialised HERE at file scope — the `_REG_SET_SEQ` precedent (ccd:460) — so
# `set -u` can never reach an unset read. They carry no event payload.
_LC_DIR="$REG/.lifecycle"
_LC_LINE_MAX=2048                # twin of L0's LC_LINE_MAX      (BYTES)
_LC_GEN_MAX_BYTES=4194304        # twin of L0's LC_GEN_MAX_BYTES — 4 MiB
_LC_GEN_KEEP=4                   # twin of L0's LC_GEN_KEEP — ~a year at ~35 KB/day
_LC_DEC_MAX=512                  # twin of L0's LC_REASON_MAX_BYTES — BYTES, not characters;
                                 # `_lc_dec_ok` below measures under `LC_ALL=C`
_LC_OBS_TMUX_DEADLINE_S=2        # bound on `_lc_obs`'s ONE `tmux list-panes -a`. Runs ONCE PER
                                 # CCD PROCESS, fleet-wide (the memo guard sees to that) — a STALL
                                 # BUDGET, not a generous per-lookup allowance like
                                 # `SUBSTRATE_PROBE_DEADLINE_S`'s 8s. A healthy answer is
                                 # milliseconds; 2s tolerates scheduler contention on a busy box
                                 # without turning a wedged tmux server into a fleet-wide hang once
                                 # wave 3/4 wires this into the hot path.
_LC_SEQ=0
_LC_OBS=""

# THE CLOSED VOCABULARY, and its twin lives in `shared/api.ts` as
# `LIFECYCLE_ACTS`. `server/test/ccd-lifecycle-emit.test.ts` reads THIS array by
# running it and asserts set equality against L0 in both directions. `unknown`
# is DELIBERATELY ABSENT here and present in L0: it is the READER's degrade, not
# a call site's choice. `_lc_emit` maps an unrecognised act to `unknown` plus a
# `badact` field, which is what makes the equality hold by construction rather
# than by everyone remembering.
_LC_ACTS=(archive attic-drop claim create destroy enable ensure forget gc
          hold purge reap rehome release rename restore route spawn start stop
          supervise swap unarchive unsupervise)
# The vocabulary is the set of ACCEPTABLE INPUTS; the degrade word is the
# OUTPUT `_lc_emit` produces when an input is not in that set — the same rule
# the acts array above follows. `unknown` stays OUT of this array for the same
# reason it stays out of that one: if a caller could pass `unknown` and have it
# accepted as a legitimate outcome, "the outcome genuinely was unknown" and
# "the caller passed a token we could not recognise" collapse into one value,
# and `badoutcome` — which exists to preserve the raw token — becomes
# unreachable for that input.
_LC_OUTCOMES=(intent done refused failed)

_lc_now_ns() {   # -> exactly 19 ASCII digits of epoch nanoseconds, on stdout
  # THREE RUNGS, because `%N` is not portable and its failure is silent and
  # poisonous: busybox `date` emits the literal `N`, which makes a generation
  # FILENAME that sorts wrong for ever (the greatest name is the live one) and
  # a `uid` that collides. The only sub-second precedent in this file is
  # ccd:4287's `+%s%3N`, milliseconds, error-suppressed for the same reason.
  local ns
  ns=$(date +%s%N 2>/dev/null)
  [[ "$ns" =~ ^[0-9]{19}$ ]] || ns="$(date +%s 2>/dev/null)000000000"
  [[ "$ns" =~ ^[0-9]{19}$ ]] || ns=0000000000000000000
  printf '%s' "$ns"
}

_lc_tx() {   # -> a correlation id for ONE intent/outcome pair, on stdout
  # Callers hold it in a `local` and pass it to both halves. It is minted here
  # rather than returned by `_lc_intent` so that the emit itself never runs in a
  # subshell — `_lc_obs`'s memo and `_LC_SEQ` are both lost across `$( )`, and
  # `_lc_obs` reads /proc and shells `tmux list-panes`.
  #
  # UNIQUENESS, honestly: called as `$(_lc_tx)` this runs in a SUBSHELL, so
  # `_LC_SEQ` is 1 every time and the guarantee rests on `$BASHPID` (a subshell
  # pid, distinct from the parent's, so a tx can never collide with an event
  # uid) plus a nanosecond clock read one fork apart.
  _LC_SEQ=$(( _LC_SEQ + 1 ))
  printf '%s.%s.%s' "$(_lc_now_ns)" "$BASHPID" "$_LC_SEQ"
}

_lc_cgroup_read() {   # -> this process's `0::` cgroup path, verbatim, on stdout
  # Its own function so a test can plant a cgroup without a container. The
  # `0::` line is the unified-hierarchy one; on this box it is the only line.
  #
  # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15) — THE GENERAL RULE, APPLIED TO PIPELINES: a
  # redirect on ONE stage of a pipe protects only that stage. `sed … | head`
  # gave `sed` its own `2>/dev/null` and left `head` with none, so a PATH
  # missing `head` leaked `ccd: line …: head: command not found` straight to
  # the real stderr. Measured with a PATH built from every OTHER binary this
  # block needs but deliberately missing `head`:
  # `ccd: line …: head: command not found` on real stderr, rc still 0. The
  # fix wraps the WHOLE pipeline in one compound `{ …; } 2>/dev/null`, the
  # same idiom `_lc_rotate`'s flock `exec` established for a builtin with
  # multiple redirects — here for a different reason (a per-stage redirect
  # does not cover its neighbour), same fix shape.
  _plat_cgroup
}

_lc_ppid_of() {   # pid -> its PPid, on stdout, or nothing. ALWAYS rc 0.
  # `/proc/<pid>/status`'s `PPid:` line, NEVER `stat` field 4: `comm` is field 2
  # and can contain spaces AND parentheses, so every positional read of `stat`
  # is wrong for a process someone named `foo) 1 2 3 (bar`.
  #
  # THE TRAILING `return 0` IS LOAD-BEARING, not decoration: under this file's
  # `pipefail`, a gone/unreadable pid makes `sed` fail (rc 2) while `head`
  # still succeeds on its empty input, and pipefail keeps the RIGHTMOST
  # non-zero — `sed`'s 2, not `head`'s 0 — as the pipeline's own status.
  # Measured: `_lc_ppid_of 999999999` answered rc 2 without this line, which
  # is not what this function documents or what its own callers assume;
  # `_lc_obs`'s ancestor walk at ccd:1093 calls this once per hop and would be
  # the first production caller to meet a stale or reaped pid.
  # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15) — same pipeline-redirect gap as `_lc_cgroup_read`
  # above: `sed` had its own `2>/dev/null`, `head` had none, and a PATH
  # missing `head` leaked `ccd: line …: head: command not found` to the real
  # stderr. The compound wrap covers both stages with ONE redirect; the
  # trailing `return 0` below is unaffected — `pipefail`'s rightmost-nonzero
  # rule still applies INSIDE the braces exactly as it did on the bare pipe,
  # and this function always answers 0 regardless either way.
  _plat_ppid "${1-}"
  return 0
}

_lc_obs_json() {   # cg cgraw pid ppid pane paneWhy tty ssh -> one JSON object
  # THE SECOND AND LAST python3 ENCODER IN THIS BLOCK. `_lc_json` builds an
  # EVENT, once per line; this builds the obs FRAGMENT, once per process. A
  # THIRD encoder in this block is a defect — it is how `"pane":,` gets written
  # by a printf argument list that swallowed `_json_str`'s status (ccd:737-739).
  LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import json, sys
k = ["cg", "cgraw", "pid", "ppid", "pane", "paneWhy", "tty", "ssh"]
o = dict(zip(k, sys.argv[1:9]))
for n in ("pid", "ppid"):
    o[n] = int(o[n]) if o[n].isdigit() else None
o["pane"] = o["pane"] or None
o["tty"] = o["tty"] == "true"
# `ssh` IS THE CONNECTION STRING, not a boolean: L0 types it `string | null`,
# and folding it to true/false throws away the only address the record sees.
o["ssh"] = o["ssh"] or None
sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(o, ensure_ascii=True, allow_nan=False, separators=(",", ":")))
' "$@"
}

_lc_obs() {   # populate _LC_OBS once per process. Prints NOTHING. Always 0.
  # THE UNFORGEABLE FAMILY. `dec` is what a caller SAID; this is what the kernel
  # says. Nothing here computes a single "who" — `corroboration()` in L0 relates
  # the two and a disagreement is a fact the operator sees, never a silently
  # picked winner. A double fork makes a caller ANONYMOUS (ppid 1), never
  # someone else.
  [[ -z "$_LC_OBS" ]] || return 0

  local cgraw cg pid ppid pane panewhy tty ssh panes p depth sname spid rc
  pid=$$
  cgraw=$(_lc_cgroup_read)
  # ORDER IS PRECEDENCE, and the innermost fact wins. A pane started by the
  # supervisor sits inside BOTH cgroups; the scope it is RUNNING in is the
  # observed fact, the unit that started it is provenance the path still
  # carries verbatim in `cgraw`.
  case "$cgraw" in
    */ccrc-agent.service*)        cg=agent ;;
    */tmux-spawn-*.scope*)        cg=pane ;;
    */claude-session@*.service*)  cg=supervisor ;;
    */session-*.scope*)           cg=login ;;
    *)                            cg=unknown ;;
  esac
  ppid=$(_lc_ppid_of "$pid"); [[ "$ppid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || ppid=""

  # THE PANE IS AN INTERSECTION, and its absence gets a REASON. A bare null
  # would fold "no tmux on this box", "tmux did not answer", "no ancestor is
  # a pane" AND "tmux did not answer IN TIME" into one value a reader cannot
  # act on — the overloaded null at a seam this repo bans by name. A stall is
  # its own fourth condition, not a slower `not-listed`: an operator facing a
  # wedged tmux server (restart it) needs a different action than one facing a
  # tmux that answered "no" cleanly (nothing to fix).
  pane=""; panewhy=no-ancestor
  if ! command -v tmux >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    panewhy=no-tmux
  else
    # BASH-LEVEL SUBPROCESS, not a bare `timeout tmux …`: `timeout` EXECS its
    # argv, which is invisible to a shell FUNCTION named `tmux` — the same
    # function-vs-binary split `_session_probe` documents (ccd:340-343) — so a
    # stub `tmux() { sleep 30; }` would sail straight past a bare
    # `timeout tmux …` and defeat the guard this exists to add. `declare -f
    # tmux` serializes any function definition into the child `bash -c`
    # script, so the SAME thing runs there — function stub or the real
    # binary either way — as a genuine, killable subprocess. A blank first
    # line rather than `; tmux …`: a leading bare `;` is itself a syntax
    # error when the substitution is empty (the ordinary case: a real tmux on
    # PATH, no stub).
    #
    # UNBOUNDED WAS THE BUG: a wedged tmux socket, contention, or a D-state
    # process previously blocked this call FOREVER — measured: a `sleep 30`
    # stub left `_lc_obs` unreturned past an outer `timeout -s KILL 3`, i.e.
    # SIGKILLed rather than answering — which breaks "never gate the act it
    # observes" the moment wave 3/4 wires this into the hot path.
    panes=$(_plat_timeout "$_LC_OBS_TMUX_DEADLINE_S" bash -c "$(declare -f tmux 2>/dev/null)
tmux list-panes -a -F '#{session_name} #{pane_pid}'" 2>/dev/null)
    rc=$?
    # rc 124 is `timeout`'s own SIGTERM; 137 is what a caller sees if an OUTER
    # kill reached this whole invocation before `timeout` itself could report
    # — both mean "tmux did not answer IN TIME", so both get the SAME distinct
    # reason, never folded into `not-listed`'s "tmux answered no".
    if (( rc == 124 )) || (( rc == 137 )); then
      panewhy=timed-out
    elif (( rc != 0 )); then
      panewhy=not-listed
    else
      p="$pid"; depth=0
      # The regex runs FIRST and every arithmetic test is downstream of it: `p`
      # comes off /proc, and bash evaluates a variable's CONTENTS as arithmetic
      # (ccd:10480-10483 is the paid lesson). `depth` bounds a /proc that lies.
      while [[ "$p" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && (( p > 1 )) && (( depth < 16 )); do
        while IFS=' ' read -r sname spid; do
          [[ "$spid" == "$p" ]] && { pane="$sname"; break; }
        done <<< "$panes"
        [[ -n "$pane" ]] && { panewhy=ok; break; }
        p=$(_lc_ppid_of "$p")
        depth=$(( depth + 1 ))
      done
    fi
  fi

  tty=false; [[ -t 0 ]] && tty=true
  ssh="${SSH_CONNECTION-}"

  # ONE fork for the whole fragment, MEMOISED — not eight `_json_str`
  # substitutions, each of which swallows its own status inside a printf
  # argument list (ccd:737-739) and prints `"pane":,` when the interpreter is
  # gone. NO python3 means the four bytes `null`: not measured is a different
  # fact from measured-as-empty, and `null` is also non-empty, so the memo holds
  # and this whole function does not re-run on every event.
  _LC_OBS=$(_lc_obs_json "$cg" "$cgraw" "$pid" "$ppid" "$pane" "$panewhy" "$tty" "$ssh" 2>/dev/null) \
    || _LC_OBS=null
  [[ -n "$_LC_OBS" ]] || _LC_OBS=null
  return 0
}

_lc_live() {   # -> the generation to append to, on stdout. Mints one if none.
  # THE GREATEST NAME IS THE LIVE ONE, and "greatest" is LENGTH-FIRST, the
  # same rule L0's `compareGenerations` (shared/api.ts) makes a single reader
  # for: comparing LENGTH before content, so a 20-digit name is greater than
  # ANY 19-digit name regardless of what its digits say. `_lc_now_ns` mints
  # fixed-width 19-digit names, so this never bites production — but a bare
  # `[[ "$a" > "$b" ]]` is lexicographic, and lexicographically
  # "9999999999999999999" (19 nines) sorts AFTER "10000000000000000000" (20
  # digits) at the very first byte, exactly backwards from L0. `${#f}` is
  # compared first for that reason, string `>` only as the equal-length
  # tiebreak — which for equal-length digit strings IS numeric order.
  local f newest="" LC_ALL=C
  mkdir -p -- "$_LC_DIR" 2>/dev/null || { printf '%s' ''; return 0; }
  for f in "$_LC_DIR"/journal-*.ndjson; do
    [[ -f "$f" ]] || continue                      # unmatched glob stays literal: no nullglob here
    if [[ -z "$newest" ]] \
      || (( ${#f} > ${#newest} )) \
      || { (( ${#f} == ${#newest} )) && [[ "$f" > "$newest" ]]; }; then
      newest="$f"
    fi
  done
  if [[ -z "$newest" ]]; then
    newest="$_LC_DIR/journal-$(_lc_now_ns).ndjson"
    # `>>`, never `>`: the create form that CANNOT truncate, even if two
    # processes reach it at once.
    #
    # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15): a bare `: >> "$newest" 2>/dev/null` does NOT
    # suppress bash's own redirect-setup diagnostic — the `>>` open is
    # attempted and fails BEFORE the trailing `2>/dev/null` is installed,
    # the same class `_lc_rotate`'s flock `exec` already fixed two FIX
    # ROUNDs ago (see below) but this sibling site was missed. Measured: an
    # existing `.lifecycle` at chmod 555 leaked
    # `ccd: line …: …/journal-….ndjson: Permission denied` to the caller's
    # stderr even though `_lc_emit`'s own rc stayed 0 — a Critical against
    # `_lc_emit`'s "prints nothing on stdout or stderr" contract, since this
    # is the path a caller with an unwritable-but-present `.lifecycle`
    # reaches on every event. The COMPOUND, not the bare `:`, is what the
    # redirect must wrap.
    { : >> "$newest"; } 2>/dev/null || { printf '%s' ''; return 0; }
  fi
  printf '%s' "$newest"
}

_lc_rotate() {   # live-path — mint a fresh generation when the live one is full
  # MINT-A-GREATER-NAME, NEVER TRUNCATE: the full generation is neither renamed
  # nor rewritten, and a new, greater name appears beside it. A truncate would
  # shrink a file the mirror is reading, and `agent/src/tail.ts:53-58` hands its
  # reader a RESET it must model; the whole point of putting the generation in
  # the NAME is that a rotation is a new name appearing rather than an old file
  # getting smaller. Retention is a CEILING, not a schedule: 4 x 4 MiB at the
  # measured ~35 KB/day is about a year.
  #
  # DIE-FREE AND REFUSAL-FREE, unlike every other flock site in this file
  # (all of which `die` when `flock` is unavailable or contested). D7 forbids
  # the journal from gating an act, so a missing or contested lock SKIPS the
  # rotation and lets the generation grow past its cap. `.rotate.lock` is
  # NEVER unlinked — unlinking a lock file while another process holds it is
  # exactly how two processes come to hold "the lock" on two different inodes.
  local live="${1-}" sz lock lfd f n live_now minted="" gens=()
  [[ -n "$live" && -f "$live" ]] || return 0
  sz=$(_plat_size -- "$live" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ "$sz" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 0              # regex BEFORE the arithmetic, always
  (( sz >= _LC_GEN_MAX_BYTES )) || return 0

  command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 0
  lock="$_LC_DIR/.rotate.lock"
  # FIX ROUND 1 (a): a BARE `exec {lfd}>>"$lock" 2>/dev/null` does not
  # suppress bash's own diagnostic for a failed exec-redirection — the
  # message is emitted while the shell is SETTING UP the redirection, before
  # the redirect it names is installed, so it reaches the real stderr rather
  # than the one about to be redirected. Measured: a `.lifecycle` at chmod
  # 555 leaked `ccd: line …: …/.rotate.lock: Permission denied` to stderr
  # even though rc stayed 0. The COMPOUND, not the bare `exec`, is what the
  # redirect must wrap — `2>/dev/null` on `{ …; }` applies to everything
  # inside it, `exec` included, the same way it would to any other command.
  #
  # THE GENERAL RULE (record it here ONCE, canonically, so the next person
  # finds it rather than re-discovering it — this has now cost this plan two
  # separate FIX ROUNDs): a trailing `2>/dev/null` on a simple command with
  # MULTIPLE redirections does NOT suppress bash's own diagnostic for an
  # earlier redirection that failed to open. Bash installs a simple command's
  # redirections IN ORDER, left to right, as it encounters them — so when the
  # FIRST one (`>>`, `>`, or `exec`'s own target) fails to open, bash reports
  # the failure to whatever stderr is CURRENTLY active, which is the real one,
  # because the LATER `2>/dev/null` redirect has not been installed yet. This
  # is true of every BUILTIN that can appear with a second redirect after a
  # failing first one — measured here for `exec`, and for the plain `:` and
  # `printf` builtins at every other write site in this block (`_lc_live`'s
  # mint, `_lc_rotate`'s mint, `_lc_err`'s counter write, `_lc_emit`'s event
  # append) — and is NOT true of an EXTERNAL command's OWN stderr (`mkdir`,
  # `mv`, `rm`, `cat`, `stat`), which a trailing `2>/dev/null` captures
  # normally, because there bash's redirection setup is not itself what fails.
  # The fix is always the same: wrap the WHOLE command in a compound
  # `{ …; } 2>/dev/null`, so the redirect is installed and active BEFORE the
  # inner command — and its own attempt to open ITS redirect — ever runs.
  { exec {lfd}>>"$lock"; } 2>/dev/null || return 0
  flock -n "$lfd" 2>/dev/null || { exec {lfd}>&-; return 0; }

  # FIX ROUND 1 (b): re-measuring `$live`'s OWN size here, as this used to,
  # is a structural no-op — rotation never truncates `$live` (mint-a-
  # greater-name, never truncate), so its size answers "over cap" FOREVER
  # after the very first rotation, and can never tell a SECOND rotator that
  # a first one already acted. Measured with two real concurrent processes
  # racing this function against one over-cap `$live`: 8 of 10 runs (a
  # 15ms stagger between launches) minted TWO generations from one over-cap
  # event, not one. The question under the lock is "has someone ELSE
  # already rotated `$live` away", and `_lc_live` answers it directly: if
  # its CURRENT greatest name is still `$live` itself, nobody has, and this
  # call should mint. `_lc_live` only mints when NO generation exists at
  # all — `$live` is itself already a generation on disk at this point (the
  # `-f "$live"` check at the top of this function), so that branch can
  # never trigger from here.
  if [[ "$(_lc_live)" == "$live" ]]; then
    minted="$_LC_DIR/journal-$(_lc_now_ns).ndjson"
    # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15): same sibling bug as `_lc_live`'s mint above —
    # the COMPOUND, not the bare `:`, must wrap the redirect for
    # `2>/dev/null` to suppress bash's own diagnostic rather than the
    # external command's.
    { : >> "$minted"; } 2>/dev/null || minted=""
  fi

  for f in "$_LC_DIR"/journal-*.ndjson; do [[ -f "$f" ]] && gens+=("$f"); done
  n=${#gens[@]}                                    # bash 5.2: an empty array under set -u is fine
  if (( n > _LC_GEN_KEEP )); then
    # THE LIVE ONE IS NEVER PRUNED, and the guard is not decoration. TWO
    # NAMES are protected, not one: `minted`, THIS CALL's own fresh name,
    # captured directly rather than re-derived — a future-dated name already
    # on disk (impossible today; `_lc_now_ns` is monotonic, but not enforced)
    # would make `minted` the SECOND-smallest by content, and re-deriving via
    # `_lc_live` would answer the OLD future-dated name instead, so the prune
    # would eat the mint it just made and rotation would never converge:
    # `_lc_live` picks the full generation again on the very next event. And
    # `live_now`, re-read rather than assumed: another ccd may have minted a
    # greater one than this call's `minted` between the two.
    live_now=$(_lc_live)
    # LENGTH-FIRST, NOT A BARE `LC_ALL=C sort`: sorting the filenames plain
    # is lexicographic and disagrees with L0's `compareGenerations` across a
    # digit-length boundary. Each name is prefixed with its own zero-padded
    # LENGTH before sorting, so the sort compares length first and content
    # only as the tiebreak — one ordering claim, expressed once, agreeing
    # with `_lc_live` and with L0 by construction rather than by two authors
    # remembering the same rule. `LC_ALL=C` on the `sort` invocation itself,
    # this file's own idiom (ccd:5213, 4033, 1152, 6801) — a `local` variable
    # is never enough for an EXTERNAL command's collation, only for bash's
    # own builtin `[[ ]]` comparisons.
    # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15) — TWO gaps closed with one wrap. (1) Neither
    # `sort` nor `head` in the generator pipe below carried a redirect at
    # all, so a PATH missing either leaked `command not found` straight to
    # the real stderr — the same per-stage-redirect gap as `_lc_cgroup_read`/
    # `_lc_ppid_of` above, found here by this task's own sweep of the whole
    # LC block rather than in the original review. (2) `rm -f -- "$f"`
    # inside the loop body carried NO redirect either — `-f` only silences
    # "no such file", not a genuine permission error, and a chmod-555
    # `.lifecycle` mid-rotation (an ordinary operational state, not a
    # contrived one) makes `rm` print its OWN `Permission denied` to real
    # stderr. Measured directly: `rm -f -- file` inside a directory whose
    # write bit was stripped printed `rm: cannot remove 'file': Permission
    # denied` even with `-f`. Wrapping the WHOLE loop — generator, sort,
    # head, and every `rm` the loop body can run — in one compound
    # `{ …; } 2>/dev/null` closes both at once, rather than four separate
    # per-command redirects that would only repeat the gap under a fifth
    # command added here later.
    {
      while IFS= read -r f; do
        f="${f#*|}"
        [[ -n "$f" && "$f" != "$live_now" && "$f" != "$minted" ]] && rm -f -- "$f"
      done < <(
        for f in "${gens[@]}"; do printf '%05d|%s\n' "${#f}" "$f"; done \
          | LC_ALL=C sort | head -n $(( n - _LC_GEN_KEEP ))
      )
    } 2>/dev/null
  fi
  # A CONTRACT, NOT HYGIENE: flock treats two open()s of one path in one
  # process as two strangers, and ccd is SOURCED, so a descriptor left open
  # here refuses the NEXT rotation in the same shell.
  exec {lfd}>&-
  return 0
}

_lc_err() {   # bump the counted errors file. Prints nothing. Always 0.
  # IT MINTS THE DIRECTORY ITSELF, and that is the one place besides `_lc_live`
  # that does: this counter exists for exactly the states in which the journal
  # could not be written, and half of those are "the directory was not there".
  # A counter that needs the journal's own directory to already exist records
  # nothing on the path it was written for.
  #
  # Temp+rename, `_reg_set`'s idiom (ccd:461-467), because a half-written
  # counter is a counter. Surfaced by the server as `lifecycle.writeErrors`,
  # which together with `lifecycle.newestAt` is what makes a silently-stopped
  # journal visible rather than indistinguishable from a quiet fleet.
  #
  # A KNOWN, ACCEPTED LOSS UNDER CONCURRENCY — RECORDED, NOT MERELY MEASURED
  # ONCE (task 15 FIX ROUND 2). Temp+rename is READ-MODIFY-WRITE, not atomic
  # increment: two concurrent callers can both read the same "$n", both
  # compute the same "n + 1", and the SECOND rename simply overwrites the
  # first — last-writer-wins, not last-writer-adds. Measured directly, 30
  # truly-concurrent `_lc_err` calls (real separate processes, launched
  # together, not staggered): the counter settled at 14-16 across repeated
  # runs, roughly half the true count. This is ACCEPTED: `errors` is a
  # diagnostics-only counter surfaced as `lifecycle.writeErrors`, never the
  # journal itself, and the operator ruling is that temp+rename cannot do
  # meaningfully better here without a lock this best-effort path is not
  # allowed to take (D7: the journal, and everything in this block, must
  # never gate or serialise the act it is recording). The consequence a
  # reader MUST carry: `errors` is a FLOOR, a lower bound on how many
  # appends failed, never an exact count. Treating it as exact is the
  # mistake this comment exists to stop before it is made.
  local n tmp
  mkdir -p -- "$_LC_DIR" 2>/dev/null || return 0
  # `-f` BEFORE THE OPEN — the thirteenth read on the tick closure, and the one
  # a census that had already cleared this function missed (#69 review round 3,
  # post-merge sweep). `cat` opens whatever is here with no regard for TYPE, and
  # this function is reachable from the 5-second tick: `_auto_swap_check`'s
  # `_lc_done rehome` -> `_lc_emit` -> five arms that call `_lc_err`, one of them
  # the failure arm of the journal append itself. MEASURED under `timeout`: a
  # FIFO at `$_LC_DIR/errors` returns rc 124 from `_lc_err` alone, and rc 124
  # through `_lc_emit` with `$_LC_DIR` unwritable — a wedged supervisor, from the
  # counter that exists to report that the journal could not be written.
  #
  # THE FOLD IS ALREADY HERE AND IS THE ONE THIS FUNCTION WANTS. The regex below
  # answers 0 for anything that is not digits, so an unreadable counter already
  # restarts the count — and `errors` is documented above as a FLOOR, never an
  # exact count. A non-regular file answering `""` lands in exactly that arm,
  # byte-identical to a garbage one. Nothing is narrowed.
  n=""; [[ -f "$_LC_DIR/errors" ]] && n=$(cat "$_LC_DIR/errors" 2>/dev/null)
  # THE REGEX IS THE GUARD, not a tidiness check: the next line is an
  # ARITHMETIC context over bytes off disk, and bash evaluates a variable's
  # contents there — a command substitution inside an array subscript EXECUTES.
  [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || n=0
  n=$(( n + 1 ))
  tmp="$_LC_DIR/.errors.$BASHPID.tmp"
  # FIX ROUND 1 (task 15, found by this task's own operator-mandated
  # unwritable-$_LC_DIR proof): a bare `printf … > "$tmp" 2>/dev/null` does
  # NOT suppress bash's own redirect-setup diagnostic for `printf`, a
  # BUILTIN — the `>` open is attempted and fails before the trailing
  # `2>/dev/null` is installed, the exact class `_lc_rotate`'s flock `exec`
  # fix already records (`_lc_rotate`'s FIX ROUND 1 (a) block, by NAME — the
  # range this cited was wrong at base and an insertion has since landed inside
  # it, which is the class D-2186 rules must be re-expressed) and two sibling `_lc_live`/
  # `_lc_rotate` mint sites needed the same fix for (FIX ROUND 2, above).
  # Measured: a chmod-555 `.lifecycle` leaked
  # `ccd: line …: …/.errors.<pid>.tmp: Permission denied` to the caller's
  # stderr even with rc 0 — a Critical against `_lc_err`'s own "prints
  # nothing" contract. `mv`/`rm` below are EXTERNAL commands, so their own
  # `2>/dev/null` already captures normally; only the builtin `printf`
  # needed the compound wrap.
  { printf '%s\n' "$n" > "$tmp"; } 2>/dev/null && _plat_mv_notdir "$tmp" "$_LC_DIR/errors" 2>/dev/null
  rm -f -- "$tmp" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

_lc_json() {   # cap uid ns act outcome id tx badact badoutcome obsjson [k v]...
  # THE SINGLE-FORK EVENT ENCODER, taking fields as argv. The alternative —
  # eight `$(_json_str …)` substitutions inside a printf argument list — is the
  # defect ccd:737-739 records: a bare `$(_json_str …)` swallows its own status
  # by construction, so a missing interpreter prints `"reason":,` and an
  # unparseable line lands in the record. Here a failed interpreter is a
  # NON-ZERO return with empty stdout and the caller writes nothing at all,
  # which is a gap the errors counter records rather than a corrupt line the
  # mirror has to model.
  #
  # The only OTHER python3 encoder in this block is `_lc_obs_json`, which builds
  # the obs FRAGMENT once per process. A third is a defect.
  #
  # NO `_json_str probe … || die` up front, unlike `_ws_archive_manifest` and
  # `cmd_ws_audit`: D7 forbids this path from dying, and the caller's own
  # non-zero branch already covers the same condition.
  LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import json, sys
a = sys.argv[1:]
cap = int(a[0])
uid, ns, act, outcome, sid, tx, badact, badoutcome, obs = a[1:10]
rest = a[10:]

# `at` IS MILLISECONDS — L0 types LifecycleEvent.at that way and the mirror
# stores it that way. Both it and `atNs` come from ONE clock read, so the two
# can never disagree about one event.
ev = {"v": 1, "uid": uid, "atNs": ns, "at": int(ns[:13]), "act": act,
      "outcome": outcome, "id": sid}
if tx: ev["tx"] = tx
try:
    ev["obs"] = json.loads(obs) if obs else None
except ValueError:
    ev["obs"] = None

# THE KEY NAMESPACE. Dotted keys name their family; FOUR names are top-level;
# anything else is DROPPED AND NAMED. That last rule is `badact`s own idiom
# applied to keys — a key we saw and could not model is a different fact from a
# key that was never passed — and it is what lets this encoder be TOTAL without
# quietly inventing a home for a typo.
#
# AN EMPTY VALUE OMITS ITS KEY. Disclosed, and it is not an overloaded null:
# every meas value ccd passes is a `_reg_get` (which cats a missing file to
# empty) or a git ref read, so "measured as empty" and "not measured" are ONE
# condition at this seam, not two a caller handles differently.
#
# FIX ROUND 2 (task 15): `badact` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT from TOP, even
# though it is a genuine top-level field on the wire — it is set ONLY below,
# by the ENCODER itself, never by a callers key/value pair. `badact` exists
# to say that L0 never heard of this act, and that is a fact only this
# function can know; a caller passing a literal `badact` key is meaningless
# by construction, indistinguishable on the wire from the genuine degrade if
# it were allowed through here. Measured: with `badact` in TOP,
# `_lc_emit stop done sess badact forged-not-real` — a perfectly VALID act —
# wrote `badact:forged-not-real`, forging the exact signal a reader uses to
# decide that ccds own vocabulary is stale. `badoutcome` was NEVER in TOP and
# the identical probe against it correctly fell through to
# `badkey:badoutcome` — proof this was a TOP-membership bug, not a design
# question. A stray `badact` key now takes the same path every other
# unmodelled key does: `badkey`.
TOP = ("detail", "refusal", "verb", "branchDeleted")
dec, meas = {}, {}
for i in range(0, len(rest) - 1, 2):
    k, v = rest[i], rest[i + 1]
    if v == "":
        continue
    if k.startswith("dec."):
        dec[k[4:]] = v
    elif k.startswith("meas."):
        meas[k[5:]] = v
    elif k in TOP:
        ev[k] = v
    elif "badkey" not in ev:
        ev["badkey"] = k
dec.setdefault("surface", "none")
ev["dec"] = dec
ev["meas"] = meas or None
# The ENCODER computes these two, after the loop, so they always win over a
# caller that passed `badact` as a key.
if badact:     ev["badact"] = badact
if badoutcome: ev["badoutcome"] = badoutcome

def line(o):
    return json.dumps(o, ensure_ascii=True, allow_nan=False, separators=(",", ":"))

# THE CAP IS ENFORCED BY DROPPING IN A FIXED ORDER, AND BY SAYING SO. A line
# silently cut at 2048 bytes is a line that does not parse; `truncated` is what
# makes the loss a row rather than a silence. `refusal` and `detail` are NOT in
# the ladder: an over-cap refusal that dropped its own token would be an
# untyped `outcome:"refused"`, which is the one thing the record exists to say.
for drop in (None, "reason", "cgraw", "meas"):
    if drop == "reason":
        ev["dec"].pop("reason", None); ev["truncated"] = True
    elif drop == "cgraw" and isinstance(ev.get("obs"), dict):
        ev["obs"].pop("cgraw", None); ev["truncated"] = True
    elif drop == "meas":
        ev["meas"] = None; ev["truncated"] = True
    s = line(ev)
    # FIX ROUND 2 (task 15): THE BYTE MEASUREMENT IS CURRENTLY UNDETECTABLE
    # AS A DISTINCT CHOICE, and that is worth writing down rather than
    # leaving implicit. `line()` calls `json.dumps(…, ensure_ascii=True)`,
    # which guarantees pure-ASCII output for every possible input — every
    # non-ASCII character comes out as a `\uXXXX` escape, all ASCII bytes —
    # so `len(s.encode("utf-8")) == len(s)` always, for any event this
    # encoder can build. A mutant that swapped the byte measurement for a
    # bare `len(s)` would NOT be caught by any test today; verified: 2048
    # bytes untruncated, 2049 truncated, both figures identical whether
    # measured in bytes or characters at `ensure_ascii=True`. The byte
    # measurement is still the CORRECT choice — `LC_LINE_MAX` is a wire
    # BYTE cap, matching L0s and the append writes bytes, not characters —
    # and it becomes LOAD-BEARING, not merely correct, the moment
    # `ensure_ascii` is ever changed to `False`: a non-ASCII `dec.reason` or
    # `detail` would then encode to MORE bytes than characters, and a bare
    # `len(s)` would silently pass lines over the real 2048-byte cap.
    if len(s.encode("utf-8")) <= cap:
        sys.stdout.write(s); raise SystemExit(0)

# THE LAST RESORT CARRIES THE JOINABLE MINIMUM. `tx` and `dec.surface` and the
# refusal token are what a reader needs to pair an intent with its outcome and
# to know what was refused; dropping them here loses the record at exactly the
# moment its payload was interesting.
fb = {"v": 1, "uid": uid, "atNs": ns, "at": int(ns[:13]), "act": act,
      "outcome": outcome, "id": sid,
      "dec": {"surface": ev["dec"].get("surface", "none")}, "truncated": True}
if tx: fb["tx"] = tx
if ev.get("refusal"): fb["refusal"] = ev["refusal"]
sys.stdout.write(line(fb))
' "$@"
}

_lc_emit() {   # act outcome id tx [key value]... — THE ONE WRITER. Always 0.
  # NOTHING BUT THIS FUNCTION WRITES INTO `.lifecycle/` (and `_lc_err`/
  # `_lc_live`/`_lc_rotate`, which only it calls). That is scanned by
  # `ccd-lifecycle-contain.test.ts`, and it is the one defect of `swap.log` this
  # design does not copy: ccd:9264 and ccd:11119 redirect a CHILD'S stdout and
  # stderr into that log, which is why ~30% of its lines are untimestamped and
  # unstructured. No `bash -c` or `systemd-run` string in this file may name
  # `.lifecycle`.
  #
  # PRINTS NOTHING on stdout or stderr. Several call sites sit inside functions
  # whose stdout IS the record (`cmd_ws_rename`'s printf, `_ws_gc_prune_row`'s
  # rows, `_ws_reap_tail`'s receipt); one stray byte corrupts a document a
  # consumer parses.
  local act="${1-}" outcome="${2-}" id="${3-}" tx="${4-}"
  shift 4 2>/dev/null || { _lc_err; return 0; }

  local a badact="" badoutcome="" found=0
  for a in "${_LC_ACTS[@]}"; do [[ "$a" == "$act" ]] && { found=1; break; }; done
  (( found )) || { badact="$act"; act=unknown; }
  found=0
  for a in "${_LC_OUTCOMES[@]}"; do [[ "$a" == "$outcome" ]] && { found=1; break; }; done
  (( found )) || { badoutcome="$outcome"; outcome=unknown; }

  _lc_obs
  # The seq increments in THIS frame — a function is not a subshell — so it is
  # genuinely monotonic per process. Only the `date` read forks.
  _LC_SEQ=$(( _LC_SEQ + 1 ))
  local ns; ns=$(_lc_now_ns)
  local uid="$ns.$BASHPID.$_LC_SEQ"

  local live; live=$(_lc_live)
  [[ -n "$live" ]] || { _lc_err; return 0; }

  local line
  line=$(_lc_json "$_LC_LINE_MAX" "$uid" "$ns" "$act" "$outcome" "$id" "$tx" \
                  "$badact" "$badoutcome" "$_LC_OBS" "$@" 2>/dev/null) \
    || { _lc_err; return 0; }
  [[ -n "$line" ]] || { _lc_err; return 0; }

  # ONE `printf … >>` per event. On Linux an O_APPEND write to a regular file is
  # serialised under the inode lock, so concurrent writers cannot interleave —
  # measured, not assumed: `$REG/swap.log` is 13 concurrent write sites over 49
  # days with zero corruption.
  #
  # FIX ROUND 3 (task 15, same proof as FIX ROUNDs 1-2 above): `printf` is a
  # BUILTIN, so a bare `>> "$live" 2>/dev/null` leaks bash's own
  # redirect-setup diagnostic to the caller's stderr when `$live` exists but
  # is unwritable (its generation file present, its permissions stripped —
  # a live-not-a-mint failure `_lc_live`'s own return-empty guard cannot
  # catch, because `_lc_live` never attempts to WRITE an existing
  # generation). Measured: `ccd: line …: …/journal-….ndjson: Permission
  # denied` on the caller's real stderr with rc 0 — a Critical against
  # `_lc_emit`'s own contract. The COMPOUND wraps the redirect, not the bare
  # builtin.
  { printf '%s\n' "$line" >> "$live"; } 2>/dev/null || { _lc_err; return 0; }
  _lc_rotate "$live"
  return 0
}

# THE FOUR CALLER-FACING SHAPES. `tx` is an ARGUMENT on all of them: a caller
# that pairs an intent with an outcome mints one with `_lc_tx` into a `local`
# and passes it twice. There is no global to carry it, deliberately.
#
# tx IS FOR ONE PAIR, NEVER FOR A CALL TREE. `cmd_enable` runs `cmd_start`
# in-process (ccd:12057) and `_swap_refuse`'s `|| cmd_ensure`
# fallback fires at ccd:12544; each writes its OWN independent
# event with its own tx, because two acts happened and the record says so
# rather than folding one into the other.
#
# EVERY POSITIONAL IS BOUND `${N-}`, NOT `$N`. ccd runs `set -uo pipefail`, and
# a function that reads an unset positional EXITS THE SHELL rather than
# returning. With 21 hand-written call sites one dropped `""` would abort a
# destructive verb mid-teardown — through the journal, which exists precisely to
# gate nothing. `shift N 2>/dev/null || :` for the same reason.
_lc_intent() { local a="${1-}" i="${2-}" t="${3-}"; shift 3 2>/dev/null || :
               _lc_emit "$a" intent "$i" "$t" "$@"; }
_lc_done()   { local a="${1-}" i="${2-}" t="${3-}"; shift 3 2>/dev/null || :
               _lc_emit "$a" done   "$i" "$t" "$@"; }

_lc_fail() {   # act id tx token detail [k v]... — an act that STARTED and broke
  # Distinct from `_lc_refuse` by WHEN, not by severity: a refusal happens
  # before anything irreversible, a failure after. `cmd_ws_rm`'s
  # `git worktree remove` die at ccd:3906 is a FAILURE — the session
  # is already stopped when it fires — and calling it a refusal would say
  # nothing was touched, which is false.
  local a="${1-}" i="${2-}" t="${3-}" tok="${4-}" msg="${5-}"; shift 5 2>/dev/null || :
  _lc_emit "$a" failed "$i" "$t" refusal "$tok" detail "$msg" "$@"
}

_lc_refuse() {   # act id token detail [k v]... — EMITS, THEN DIES. Never returns.
  # Refusal and death cannot drift because they are one call. Call sites live
  # ONLY inside the destructive verbs, never inside `die` itself — putting it
  # there would fabricate a refusal record for every usage error on every verb
  # in the file.
  #
  # THIS FUNCTION IS IN `ccd-die-containment.test.ts`'s CAN-DIE SET (that suite
  # derives the set by call-graph reachability, so there is no list to edit). It
  # must never be wrapped in `$( )`: the capture demotes a fatal to rc 1 and the
  # verb carries on destroying.
  #
  # The field is `refusal`, never `refused`, and the token is an ARGUMENT
  # rather than a format-string literal — both so `ccd-wsaudit-nonpoison.test.ts`'s
  # four-shape scan over this whole block's text cannot see it. That guard's
  # own docstring is the precedent for why the spelling matters, not a
  # specific ccd line.
  local a="${1-}" i="${2-}" tok="${3-}" msg="${4-}"; shift 4 2>/dev/null || :
  _lc_emit "$a" refused "$i" "" refusal "$tok" detail "$msg" "$@"
  die "$msg"
}

_lc_refuse_return() {   # act id tx token detail [k v]... — EMITS, THEN RETURNS
  # THE NON-FATAL SIBLING, and it exists because `_lc_refuse` cannot be reused
  # where the caller must keep going: that one "EMITS, THEN DIES. Never
  # returns." `ws-gc --prune` sweeps a list of rows, and a refusal on one row
  # must not kill the sweep at the first declining row.
  #
  # THE NAME CARRIES ITS DISTINGUISHING PROPERTY, deliberately. The earlier
  # spelling, `_lc_refused`, sat ONE CHARACTER from `_lc_refuse` while differing
  # in ARITY — five positionals against four — and in FATALITY. A mistyped call
  # would shift every argument by one AND kill the sweep at the first declining
  # row: the exact outcome this emitter exists to prevent.
  #
  # IT CARRIES A `tx`, where `_lc_refuse` passes a literal empty one. That is
  # what lets it close a minted transaction: `_ws_gc_prune_row`'s dead-reg arm
  # has already emitted `_lc_intent destroy … "$lctx"` by the time it learns
  # `_reg_purge` refused, and a decline reported only through `_gc_declined` —
  # a printf report row, not a lifecycle emit — would leave that intent with
  # ZERO terminal facts, permanently, on every sweep.
  #
  # Same `refusal`/token spelling discipline as `_lc_refuse` above, and for the
  # same reason: `ccd-wsaudit-nonpoison.test.ts` harvests four token shapes over
  # THIS WHOLE BLOCK'S TEXT, comments included, so neither the emitter's name
  # nor anything written about it may spell one of them. (That suite's own
  # source is where the four are enumerated; quoting them here would be the
  # very poisoning it exists to refuse — measured, an earlier draft of this
  # comment did exactly that and reddened it.)
  local a="${1-}" i="${2-}" t="${3-}" tok="${4-}" msg="${5-}"; shift 5 2>/dev/null || :
  _lc_emit "$a" refused "$i" "$t" refusal "$tok" detail "$msg" "$@"
  return 0
}

_lc_surface_norm() {   # word -> the DECLARED surface, or NOTHING when none was
  # THREE STATES, THREE ANSWERS, and the empty one is the whole point: "no flag
  # was passed" is a different fact from "a word this build does not model".
  # The encoder omits an empty value, so an undeclared surface reads `none`;
  # `unknown` is reserved for a word that arrived and was not recognised.
  #
  # ccd's OWN DEFAULTS MUST NEVER REACH THIS FIELD — `cmd_stop`'s `cli`,
  # `_ws_unsupervise`'s `ccd`. They are ccd acting on its own account, not a
  # caller's declaration, and laundering one into `dec.surface` is exactly the
  # default-as-declaration defect `none` exists to prevent. The closed set is
  # ccd:2407's, so the journal and the `.stopped` stamp agree by construction.
  [[ -n "${1-}" ]] || { printf '%s' ''; return 0; }
  case "$1" in cli|pwa|agent|ccd) printf '%s' "$1" ;; *) printf '%s' unknown ;; esac
  return 0
}

_lc_dec_ok() {   # text -> 0 iff it fits _LC_DEC_MAX *BYTES*. Prints nothing.
  # BYTES, NOT CHARACTERS, and `local LC_ALL=C` is what makes `${#1}` count
  # them — restored on return, because `local` shadows the caller's value for
  # this frame only. A 200-emoji reason is 200 characters and 800 bytes; with a
  # character cap it would pass one surface and be refused by the next, which
  # is one value with two meanings at a provenance seam.
  #
  # THE POLICY IS REFUSE, NEVER TRUNCATE. A 900-byte reason silently recorded
  # as 512 reads as the operator's own words, and the record is the one
  # artefact that outlives the workspace.
  local LC_ALL=C
  (( ${#1} <= _LC_DEC_MAX ))
}
# ── end lifecycle journal ────────────────────────────────────────── LC-END ──
_ws_status() {   # id -> busy|idle on stdout; NON-ZERO when it cannot be read.
  # Fails closed twice over, and both halves are load-bearing.
  #
  # UNREADABLE -> non-zero. A socket hiccup or an unreadable status file must
  # not read as "not working" and let an archive kill a live turn.
  #
  # UNRECOGNISED -> busy. `idle` is an ALLOWLIST; every other value is busy.
  # Claude Code writes at least `idle`, `busy` (a model turn is in flight) and
  # `shell` (a Bash tool command is running) — server/src/livestate.ts:14-30 is
  # this repo's own record of that vocabulary, and of what matching `busy` and
  # calling the rest idle cost when the server side did it. Here the price is
  # steeper than a wrong badge: `ws-reap` gates `git worktree remove` on this
  # answer, so `shell` reading idle deletes the tree out from under a running
  # `npm test`. A status we do not recognise is far likelier to be new work than
  # new rest, and an allowlist is the only polarity that survives the wrapper
  # growing a fourth word. Same polarity as `_auto_swap_check`'s gate (NAMED
  # BY FUNCTION, NOT LINE — a stale "ccd:1913" stood here, pointing at
  # unrelated prose; `_pr_py`'s own comment states the reason: a line number
  # goes stale the moment anything above it grows), which is likewise
  # `[[ "$st" == "idle" ]]` and stops on anything else.
  local id="$1" wrapper cfg pid sf st
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper); [[ -n "$wrapper" ]] || return 1
  cfg=$(_cfg_dir "$wrapper");        [[ -n "$cfg" ]]     || return 1
  # `idle` here is a claim about a session this function LOOKED AT. When the
  # tmux server does not answer there is nothing to look at, and the honest
  # answer is the non-zero this function's own contract reserves for it —
  # `ws-archive` turns it into `status-unknown` and `ws-reap` into a refusal.
  # Answering `idle` instead presented every live session as reapable for as
  # long as the substrate was unreachable (D-308).
  case "$(_session_verdict "$id")" in
    gone)    echo idle; return 0 ;;   # no pane at all: nothing is running
    unknown) return 1 ;;              # could not ask — say so, do not guess
  esac
  pid=$(tmux list-panes -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  [[ "$pid" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
  sf="$cfg/sessions/$pid.json"
  # D-2379: the one unguarded reader of this path family — `_sync_uuid`
  # (`-f`), `_auto_swap_check` (`-f`) and `_auto_compact_check` (a three-rung
  # ladder) each type-test `$sf` before opening it; this one did not, and
  # `grep FILE` opens BY NAME — a FIFO with no writer, or a symlink to an
  # unbounded character device, blocks forever, holding open the destructive
  # verbs that gate on this function (`cmd_ws_archive`, `_ws_reap_eval`,
  # `cmd_ws_reap`). `-f` alone, matching the PATH siblings above rather than
  # the SHAPE siblings (`_project_pool_state`, `_transcript_stalled_pair`)
  # that also pair `-r`: this function's own contract already collapses
  # every unreadable/unrecognised shape to the same non-zero `[[ -n "$st" ]]
  # || return 1` lands on, so a mode-000 `$sf` is unaffected either way —
  # measured, not assumed.
  [[ -f "$sf" ]] || return 1
  st=$(grep -oE '"status":"[a-z_-]+"' "$sf" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d'"' -f4)
  [[ -n "$st" ]] || return 1
  [[ "$st" == idle ]] || { echo busy; return 0; }
  echo idle
}

_transcript_path() {   # id -> the Claude Code transcript for its CURRENT uuid
  # <cfg>/projects/<workdir with / . _ all mapped to ->/<uuid>.jsonl — the same
  # munge server/src/munge.ts applies. Recorded so a reap never has to be
  # reconstructed from an escaped path after the registry is gone.
  #
  # The workdir is RESOLVED first (`_ws_realpath`, longest existing prefix):
  # Claude Code munges its physical cwd — node's process.cwd() resolves
  # symlinks — while the registry keeps the path ccd wrote, and on this box
  # `~/projects -> /data/projects -> /mnt/...`, so the raw munge named a
  # projects dir Claude never writes. cmd_swap's transcript copy already
  # resolves for exactly this reason (its `readlink -f`), and the server's
  # `resolveTranscriptFile` resolves the same way; this was the last raw one.
  #
  # EXISTENCE-FIRST (spec §2.5). Resolving is necessary and was never
  # sufficient: a session that moves relocates its transcript, and this
  # function's answer is written into archive manifests and reap TOMBSTONES,
  # which outlive the registry row they describe. An unchecked guess there is
  # a permanent wrong address. So: the resolved munge if that file exists, then
  # the raw munge if that one does, then the uuid itself — newest match under
  # THIS account's config dir — and only then today's unchecked answer, so a
  # session that has written nothing yet still records the canonical address
  # rather than an empty string.
  #
  # Deliberately no live-cwd rung and no cross-account rung. These callers run
  # at archive, audit and reap time, when the session is stopped or idle and the
  # registry workdir is the durable fact; and a foreign account's copy needs a
  # banner naming it (D4 rung 6), which a tombstone has nowhere to put.
  local id="$1" wrapper cfg workdir uuid munged raw newest
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir); uuid=$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)
  cfg=$(_cfg_dir "$wrapper")
  [[ -n "$cfg" && -n "$workdir" && -n "$uuid" ]] || return 1
  raw=$(printf '%s' "$workdir" | tr './_' '---')
  workdir=$(_ws_realpath "$workdir")
  munged=$(printf '%s' "$workdir" | tr './_' '---')
  if [[ -f "$cfg/projects/$munged/$uuid.jsonl" ]]; then
    printf '%s/projects/%s/%s.jsonl\n' "$cfg" "$munged" "$uuid"; return 0
  fi
  if [[ -f "$cfg/projects/$raw/$uuid.jsonl" ]]; then
    printf '%s/projects/%s/%s.jsonl\n' "$cfg" "$raw" "$uuid"; return 0
  fi
  # Newest by mtime among every project dir holding this uuid. `stat -c %Y` per
  # candidate rather than `ls -t`: the paths are ccd-controlled but a project
  # dir name is a munged workdir, and parsing `ls` output is how a space in one
  # becomes a silently wrong answer.
  newest=$(_transcript_matches "$cfg" "$uuid" \
    | while IFS= read -r f; do printf '%s\t%s\n' "$(_plat_mtime "$f" 2>/dev/null || printf 0)" "$f"; done \
    | LC_ALL=C sort -rn -k1,1 | head -1 | cut -f2-)
  if [[ -n "$newest" ]]; then printf '%s\n' "$newest"; return 0; fi
  printf '%s/projects/%s/%s.jsonl\n' "$cfg" "$munged" "$uuid"
}

_ws_stash_count() {   # main branch -> stash entries belonging to that branch
  # -F on both patterns: a branch name carrying a '.' would otherwise be a
  # wildcard and over-count, which here means refusing a legitimate reap.
  #
  # BOTH forms are needed and `grep -F` is case-sensitive: git writes
  # `On <branch>: <message>` for `git stash push -m` and
  # `WIP on <branch>: <sha> <subject>` for a bare `git stash`, and the bare form
  # is the common one. (Measured, and re-measured by the round-1 verifier after
  # the integration review adjudicated the two as equivalent — they are not.)
  local main="$1" branch="$2" n sha gs base
  n=$(git -C "$main" stash list 2>/dev/null | grep -cF -e "On $branch:" -e "WIP on $branch:" || true)

  # ── the OFF-BRANCH arm — final-round integration docket 6 ────────
  # `refs/stash` is SHARED across every worktree of a repository (measured on
  # git 2.43: a stash pushed from a linked worktree is listed by `git -C $main
  # stash list`), which is what makes the whole branch-scoped count possible.
  # But a stash pushed from a worktree on a DETACHED HEAD does not carry a
  # branch name at all — git writes the literal `(no branch)` — so the two
  # patterns above match nothing and the count came back an honest-looking 0.
  # A workspace holding unsaved work then read CLEAN: §7's `stashes-present`
  # guard, the one with no override, could not fire, and the reap went on to
  # CAS-delete the branch those stashes were taken from. The work itself
  # survives while `refs/stash` does — what is lost is the association, and the
  # sheet said there was nothing to associate.
  #
  # ATTRIBUTED BY THE BASE COMMIT, not by the message, because the message has
  # no name in it to match. A stash commit's FIRST PARENT is the commit HEAD was
  # on when it was taken, so an off-branch stash belongs to this branch exactly
  # when that commit is reachable from it.
  #
  # AND IT DOES REINTRODUCE THE FLEET-WIDE COST FOR ONE SHAPE OF INPUT —
  # round-3 verification P3, which flagged that this arm and the "what the guard
  # must NOT do" paragraph in `_ws_reap_eval` (search: `refs/stash exists`)
  # could not both be read as written. The earlier text here cited that
  # paragraph as though this arm avoided the cost, and then admitted three lines
  # later that it does not. One statement, in both places, instead:
  #
  #   • What `_ws_reap_eval` rejects, with a measurement, is attribution by
  #     EXISTENCE — "refs/stash resolves ⇒ refuse". That fires on a NAMED stash
  #     on another branch (main's own parked work, the ordinary case), which the
  #     operator has a standing reason to keep, so the wedge has no end date.
  #   • What this arm does is attribution by BASE COMMIT, and it fires only on
  #     an ANONYMOUS entry: one that names no branch, that nothing else in this
  #     design can associate with anything, and that nobody has a reason to keep
  #     indefinitely.
  #
  # Different rules — and they SHARE the property the verifier objected to,
  # which is not talked away. An off-branch stash taken at a commit on SHARED
  # history (typically: detached in a workspace before that workspace committed
  # anything) is reachable from every branch in the repository, so it counts
  # against all of them and every workspace of that project answers
  # `stashes-present` until it is dealt with. Measured on a two-branch fixture:
  # one `(no branch)` stash at the root commit, and both `ws/x` and `master`
  # count 1. Pinned by a test, so it is a chosen behaviour and not a discovery.
  #
  # Accepted, because in this file the cost of a no-override guard is measured
  # in whether the operator can get OUT of it — the same standard
  # `stash-unreadable` is held to, and the same conclusion: the remedy is local
  # and exact, and it TRAVELS IN THE REFUSAL DETAIL rather than in this comment,
  # because the operator the message reaches is the only one who can act.
  #
  # The alternative narrowing considered and REJECTED: also require the base
  # commit not to be an ancestor of the merge-base with `origin/HEAD`. It
  # removes the fleet-wide case — and most of docket 6 with it. A reap only runs
  # behind a MERGED PR, so after a true merge every commit of the branch is
  # already an ancestor of `origin/HEAD` and this arm would count nothing at
  # all; it would survive only for squash merges. A guard whose coverage depends
  # on which merge button a reviewer pressed is an argument about PR shape, not
  # a fact about stashes.
  #
  # An unreadable entry counts too: `rev-parse` failing is not evidence of
  # absence.
  #
  # `--format='%H%x09%gs'` and a TWO-field read: the stash commit first, then
  # the reflog subject, which is free-form and goes LAST so that a message
  # containing a tab lands in `$gs` entire rather than shifting the sha.
  # `stash list` is `git log -g refs/stash`, so it takes log's formats.
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r sha gs; do
    case "$gs" in
      'On (no branch):'*|'WIP on (no branch):'*) ;;
      *) continue ;;
    esac
    base=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "$sha^1" 2>/dev/null) \
      || { n=$(( n + 1 )); continue; }
    git -C "$main" merge-base --is-ancestor "$base" "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null \
      && n=$(( n + 1 ))
  done < <(git -C "$main" stash list --format='%H%x09%gs' 2>/dev/null)
  printf '%s\n' "$n"
}

_ws_ignored_digest() {   # workdir -> sha256 of the ignored-entry set
  # NON-ZERO when the set could not be read at all.
  #
  # --ignored=matching, NEVER `ls-files --others --ignored`: the latter
  # enumerates per file (measured 210,070 entries / 24.6 MB in custom-tools,
  # against an 8 MB agent EXEC_MAX_BUFFER), while this collapses each ignored
  # directory to one entry.
  #
  # A failed read must not be indistinguishable from an empty one, and by
  # construction the digests are: a nonexistent directory, a corrupt repo and an
  # index.lock contention all hash sha256("") = e3b0c442…b855, exactly like a
  # workspace with nothing ignored. Harmless while the digest is only recorded;
  # a forgery the moment ws-reap compares a stored digest against a live one to
  # prove gitignored content is unchanged, because a failure on EITHER side then
  # manufactures the match that authorises the delete. So the difference is
  # carried by the exit code.
  #
  # It has to come from PIPESTATUS[0] — git's own status — and not from the
  # pipeline: `grep '^!! '` exits 1 on a legitimately empty ignored set, so the
  # pipeline already returned non-zero for the HEALTHY case (measured: rc=1 for a
  # clean workspace, rc=1 for /no/such/dir). Two statements, because
  # `local d=$(...)` would return local's status.
  #
  # AND STDERR IS THE SECOND RUNG — final-round integration review, item 5 /
  # new finding 3. `PIPESTATUS[0]` alone is not enough: `git status` over a
  # PARTIALLY unreadable tree exits ZERO and writes the diagnostic to stderr
  # only (measured on git 2.43: rc 0, empty stdout, `warning: could not open
  # directory 'tracked/'`), so the digest hashed a SHORT set and answered
  # success. `_ws_collect_ignored` has required an empty stderr since Task 6 for
  # exactly this reason; this function keeps its own copy of that rung because
  # the ARCHIVE manifest still calls it, and a manifest that records a complete
  # digest over an incomplete set is the same forgery one layer down. An
  # incomplete read is not a smaller answer, it is no answer.
  local d errf err
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1
  d=$(git -C "$1" status --porcelain --ignored=matching 2>"$errf" \
        | grep '^!! ' | sort | _plat_sha256 | cut -d' ' -f1; exit "${PIPESTATUS[0]}") \
    || { rm -f "$errf"; return 1; }
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  [[ -z "$err" ]] || return 1
  [[ "$d" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$d"
}

PR_GH_TIMEOUT=8           # gh pr list has no --timeout of its own; a blocking
                          # DNS hang is 30 s, so this is the only bound there is
# THE OPTIONAL HALF GETS HALF THE BUDGET, and the number is the argument rather
# than a round figure. `_gh_pr_checks` is the second network call on a path that
# had one, so the two budgets now add up, and what they have to stay inside is
# the server's outer bound on this verb — the one that decides whether a slow
# GitHub reaches the phone as an honest `unavailable` or as the child being
# killed, which the server reads as `agent-down` and backs the whole project
# off on.
#
# THE OUTER BOUND IS 20 s, NOT 90, AND THIS PAIR HAS TO FIT INSIDE IT.
# An earlier version of this comment asserted the opposite — that
# `remote/runner.ts`'s `CCD_VERB_TIMEOUT_MS` had no `pr-state` key and the live
# bound was therefore the flat 90 s default. It ships `'pr-state': 20_000` as
# the FIRST key in that map (`server/src/remote/runner.ts:28`, and on
# `origin/main` since 27946f31), so the spec's number IS the code's number, and
# `sweepPr`'s every `ccd pr-state --project` is killed at 20 s in remote mode —
# which is this fleet's standing config.
#
# That matters because THIS verb now makes TWO network calls, not one. The old
# 12 + 6 spent 18 of the 20 before `_pr_state_one` had run for a single
# workspace, and each workspace then costs a dozen-odd `git` spawns plus a
# python3 start. The pair is now 8 + 5 = 13, leaving 7 s — 35 % — for the local
# loop, and `pr-timeout-budget.test.ts` PINS that relationship across the two
# languages so this comment can never drift from the map again. A comment is a
# request; that test is the mechanism.
#
# Both numbers stay generous against what they cover, because the expensive
# query is gone: the row window without `statusCheckRollup` answered in 0.89 s
# and the head-filtered form in 0.78 s (vs 11.2 s and a persistent 504 with the
# rollup in-band), and `--state open --limit 100` answered a 19-open-PR repo's
# rollups in 3.09 s and a several-thousand-PR repo's in 3.6 s. A repo whose open
# PRs cannot be read inside the bound loses its check glyphs for that sweep and
# nothing else — which is the whole point of the split.
PR_GH_CHECKS_TIMEOUT=5
PR_JSON_FIELDS=number,state,headRefName,headRefOid,baseRefName,isCrossRepository,mergedAt,mergeCommit,url,title,isDraft
# NAMED ONCE, spent in two places — the head-filtered row window that carries it
# in-band, and the open-PR window `_gh_pr_checks` asks for on its own. A second
# spelling of the token is a second thing to keep in step with `checksFor`'s
# reader, which is what `single-definition` exists to forbid.
PR_CHECK_FIELD=statusCheckRollup

_gh_repo_slug() {   # main-checkout path -> OWNER/NAME, or non-zero
  # Three guards, and the mutation sweep says the last one subsumes the first
  # two: with no origin `git config --get` exits 1 AND prints nothing, so both
  # `|| return 1` and `[[ -n "$url" ]]` can be deleted with the suite still
  # green — the regex rejects "" on its own. They stay because the fall-through
  # only holds while the regex is anchored at both ends, and this function's
  # answer is spent as `--repo` on a WRITE-scoped token: each guard states the
  # case it is about rather than relying on a later line to catch it.
  local url
  url=$(git -C "$1" config --get remote.origin.url 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [[ -n "$url" ]] || return 1
  url="${url%.git}"; url="${url#git@github.com:}"
  url="${url#ssh://git@github.com/}"; url="${url#https://github.com/}"
  [[ "$url" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$url"
}

_gh_pr_list() {   # repo [head-branch] -> rows JSON on stdout; on failure prints
                  # the answer object instead and returns non-zero. NEVER
                  # partially prints.
  # The head filter is a NAMED second parameter and the flag is built here — it
  # is never a forwarded "$@". pflag is last-wins, so a passthrough on the one
  # gh call two verbs share would let a caller override --repo or --state.
  # A caller passes the filter when it asks a question about ONE branch, and
  # two of the three do: `pr-open`, on both its probe and its read-back, and
  # `pr-state --session`, whose unfiltered form answered `HTTP 504` in 11.2 s on
  # a repo with real PR history and 0.78 s with the flag (measured 2026-08-26;
  # the reasoning is at that call site). `pr-state --project` and `ws-reap` ask
  # about every branch at once and pass nothing.
  #
  # ── THE ONE FIELD THAT IS NOT THE SAME FOR BOTH, AND WHY ──────────────
  # This used to end "every reader gets the same fields, the same 100-PR window
  # and the same failure classification, which is the point of there being one
  # reader". Two of those three still hold exactly. The FIELDS no longer do, and
  # the reason is not a preference — it is that GitHub cannot answer the
  # question the wide form was asking. Measured 2026-08-26 against the same live
  # fleet repo the head filter was measured on, and re-measured the day this
  # split shipped:
  #
  #   --state all --limit 100, the twelve fields incl. the rollup  11.2 s, HTTP 504 (3/3)
  #   --state all --limit 100, the same eleven WITHOUT the rollup    0.83 s, rc 0
  #   --state all --limit 100, ONLY `number,statusCheckRollup`     11.2 s, HTTP 504
  #   --state open --limit 100, `number,statusCheckRollup`          3.09 s, rc 0 (19 open)
  #
  # The third line is the load-bearing one. Narrowing the FIELD list does not
  # help at all — `statusCheckRollup` resolves the check runs of every PR
  # returned, so the cost is the WINDOW's size and nothing else. A wide window
  # therefore cannot carry a rollup at any price, and the only remaining choices
  # are to narrow the rows or to go without.
  #
  # So the rollup rides with a question about ONE branch and is asked
  # separately for a question about every branch. That is a mechanism rather
  # than a convention: there is no argument a caller can pass that puts a rollup
  # back into the wide window, so no future call site can re-create the 504 by
  # forgetting. `pr-state --project` gets its rollups from `_gh_pr_checks` and
  # joins them by number; `ws-reap`, the other wide caller, never read a rollup
  # at all (`_pr_py` mode `pick` reads number, url, mergeCommit, headRefOid) and
  # so pays for nothing.
  #
  # The window and the classification are still shared, and they are the halves
  # that made one reader worth having: one place decides what "100 PRs, all
  # states" means and one `case` decides what a failure IS. A caller still
  # cannot invent a fifth failure vocabulary or a different window by calling
  # this differently.
  local repo="$1" head="${2:-}" errf out rc err fields="$PR_JSON_FIELDS"
  local -a filter=()
  [[ -n "$head" ]] && { filter=(--head "$head"); fields="$PR_JSON_FIELDS,$PR_CHECK_FIELD"; }
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp)
  out=$(_plat_timeout "$PR_GH_TIMEOUT" gh pr list --repo "$repo" "${filter[@]}" --state all --limit 100 \
          --json "$fields" 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  if (( rc == 124 )); then printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"timeout"}\n'; return 1; fi
  if (( rc != 0 )); then
    # `gh auth status` is NOT probed first: it is another network call on the
    # unhappy path. The stderr we already have is the cheaper signal.
    case "$err" in
      *"gh auth login"*|*"not logged in"*|*"HTTP 401"*) printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"unauthenticated"}\n' ;;
      *"rate limit"*|*"HTTP 403"*)                      printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"rate-limit"}\n' ;;
      *"dial tcp"*|*"no such host"*|*"proxyconnect"*)   printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"offline"}\n' ;;
      # GITHUB'S OWN 5xx. Measured 2026-08-26 against a live fleet repo with
      # several thousand PRs of history — the slug is operator residue and
      # stays out of this tree. The 100-PR `statusCheckRollup` window this
      # function sends answers
      # `HTTP 504: We couldn't respond to your request in time. …
      # (https://api.github.com/graphql)` after 11.2 s, on 3/3 attempts — a
      # standing property of that repo's PR history, not a blip. It went out as
      # `error`, which the cap renders "GitHub could not be read." and which
      # reached a coordinator as `pr-unmeasurable` with nothing in it to act on.
      #
      # It is a different FACT from every arm above and from the default: the
      # token is fine, DNS resolved, the request arrived — the far side could
      # not finish it. That distinction is the whole of what a reader needs,
      # because it says the same query will fail the same way and the remedy is
      # to ask GitHub for less, not to go and check `gh auth` on the box.
      #
      # Matched on the CODE and never on the prose, which is GitHub's to reword
      # whenever it likes. All four of 500/502/503/504 are here because an
      # outage emits them interchangeably and an arm that knows only the one
      # code we happened to catch is an arm that lapses at the next one.
      *"HTTP 500"*|*"HTTP 502"*|*"HTTP 503"*|*"HTTP 504"*) printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"unavailable"}\n' ;;
      # A BODY THAT STARTED AND STOPPED. Same repo, same day, the same query at
      # `--limit 50`: `unexpected end of JSON input` — Go's decoder meeting EOF
      # mid-value, so gh was being answered and the answer ran out. rc 1 and an
      # empty stdout, indistinguishable from the 504 at the exit code, and a
      # different thing entirely.
      #
      # Not `unavailable`: nothing here says the far side is unwell, and unlike
      # a 504 an identical retry may simply succeed. Not `error`: "the answer
      # was cut off" IS a shape, and it is the one shape in this case statement
      # that says the read was interrupted rather than refused — which is the
      # difference between waiting and narrowing the question.
      *"unexpected end of JSON input"*|*"unexpected EOF"*) printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"truncated"}\n' ;;
      # Still the default, and still `error` — the honest name for a fault with
      # no known shape. Every arm above it earned its token by being MEASURED
      # against a live repo; inventing a sixth classification from a string
      # nobody has seen would put a confident sentence on the phone about a
      # condition nobody diagnosed, which is the failure `error` exists to avoid.
      *)                                                printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"error"}\n' ;;
    esac
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$out"
}

_gh_pr_checks() {   # repo -> `[{number, statusCheckRollup}, …]` on stdout for the
                    # PRs that are still OPEN, or NOTHING and non-zero.
  # The rollup half of the wide read, split off `_gh_pr_list` because a wide
  # window cannot carry it (the measurement is up there). Two things about this
  # function are deliberate and neither is style.
  #
  # IT PRINTS NOTHING ON FAILURE — no answer object, unlike `_gh_pr_list`, which
  # prints one BECAUSE its answer object IS the verb's output for a read that
  # failed. Nothing here is anybody's answer: `checks` is an annotation on a row
  # somebody else already read, so the only two outcomes are "here are some
  # rollups" and "there are none to join", and printing a `{"phase":"unknown"}`
  # object would put a shape into the caller's hands that could be mistaken for
  # rows. The exit code carries the difference for a future caller that needs
  # it; today's one caller treats both the same way on purpose, because both
  # mean exactly `checks: null`.
  #
  # `--state open`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE NARROWING. A rollup is a fact about a
  # pull request that is still being decided, and it is the phases where that is
  # true that the product puts into words: `prSentence` renders the checks clause
  # for `open` and `draft` alone, `PrSheet`'s `.pr-checkline` in that same
  # branch, and the sweep's own faster cadence conjoins `phase === 'open'`.
  #
  # DRAFTS ARE INSIDE `--state open`, and that is measured rather than assumed,
  # because `draft` is one of the two phases whose checks are rendered and a
  # window that quietly dropped them would take the CI glyph off exactly the PRs
  # a worker is still pushing to. GitHub's PR state is OPEN/CLOSED/MERGED and
  # draft is the separate `isDraft` field: `--state open --limit 100` on a large
  # public repo returned 23 rows with `isDraft: true`, all of them `state: OPEN`
  # (68 rows total, 2026-08-26).
  # DISCLOSED, because it is a real narrowing and not a free one: a bound row
  # that is MERGED or CLOSED now carries no rollup, so its keycap loses the
  # `data-checks` dot colour it used to get. No sentence changes, and that
  # colour was overriding `[data-phase='merged']`'s own by source order, so what
  # it took away was the phase's colour rather than a fact — but it IS a
  # difference, and a reader here should not have to discover it downstream.
  #
  # The alternative that would have kept merged and closed rows exact — one
  # head-filtered rollup call per session — is rejected for a measured reason:
  # it makes ONE exec's wall clock scale with the number of workspaces on the
  # project, so a slow GitHub stops being an honest `unavailable` for that repo
  # and becomes the outer bound killing the child, which the server reads as
  # `agent-down` and backs the whole project off on. Two calls is two calls
  # whatever the fleet grows to.
  local repo="$1" out rc
  out=$(_plat_timeout "$PR_GH_CHECKS_TIMEOUT" gh pr list --repo "$repo" --state open --limit 100 \
          --json "number,$PR_CHECK_FIELD" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
  # STDERR IS DISCARDED HERE, which it is nowhere else in this file, and the
  # reason is that there is nothing this function could do with a classification.
  # `_gh_pr_list` keeps its stderr because the reason it derives from it is the
  # verb's answer; this one's failure has exactly one consequence — no rollups to
  # join — and a `reason` token computed here would have no field to travel in
  # and no reader at the far end.
  (( rc == 0 )) || return 1
  [[ -n "$out" ]] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$out"
}

_gh_rows_ok() {   # answer -> 0 if it is a JSON list, non-zero for anything else
  # rc 0 is not the same as an answer. `gh` writes its own diagnostics to
  # STDOUT and still exits 0 ("gh: could not determine base repository"), so a
  # caller that only asks `-n` prints that sentence as its own result: measured,
  # `cmd_pr_open` returned it at exit 0, and the route (Task 13) answers
  # {ok:true} on rc 0 without parsing stdout — the phone is told a PR opened.
  # `_pr_py`'s `rows_in` already refuses the same body on the pr-state side
  # (an rc-0 unparseable read once cleared a persisted `merged:#42`); this is
  # that check for the caller that never reaches python.
  # The answer arrives on STDIN, never in an argv — spec §7, it is
  # GitHub-sourced. The program is argv, which is the safe half.
  printf '%s' "$1" | python3 -c 'import json, sys
try:
    sys.exit(0 if isinstance(json.load(sys.stdin), list) else 1)
except Exception:
    sys.exit(1)'
}

_pr_py() {   # mode [args…]; stdin = the gh rows JSON. The ONE embedded helper.
  # Embedded rather than a sibling file on purpose: ~/.local/bin/ccd is a COPY,
  # so a second file is a second deployment step and a new failure mode in
  # which `ccd caps` advertises a verb whose helper is missing.
  #
  # The program arrives on fd 3, NOT on stdin: `python3 - <<'PY'` reads the
  # program FROM stdin, so the heredoc and the piped rows are the same channel
  # and python consumes the whole of it as source — `sys.stdin.read()` then
  # returns '' at EOF, `rows_in()` answers [], and every PR in the fixture
  # vanishes into `phase:"none"` at exit 0. Measured both forms side by side:
  # `python3 - "$@" <<PY` gives stdin='', `python3 /dev/fd/3 "$@" 3<<PY` gives
  # the rows, with sys.argv identical. The rows cannot move to argv instead —
  # spec §7 is that no GitHub-sourced string is ever placed in an argv.
  python3 /dev/fd/3 "$@" 3<<'PY'
import fcntl, json, os, re, stat, subprocess, sys, time

mode = sys.argv[1]
# `headRefOid` and `mergeCommit.oid` are GitHub-SOURCED, and `is_ours` puts the
# first of them in a git argv. Spec §7: no GitHub-sourced string is ever placed
# in a prompt, an argv, or a shell — so this is the boundary, not a formality.
# Both ends are anchored and the floor is 7, and both are pinned: a row saying
# `headRefOid: "main"` otherwise resolves as a REVISION and binds (main really
# is an ancestor of our tip), and `{1,40}` would let a three-character hex ref
# like `abc` do the same. `is_merged` reads `mergeCommit.oid` through it as a
# SHAPE check only — a merge commit is never an identity, two PRs can share one.
OID = re.compile(r'^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$')

def rows_in():
    """The rows, or None for A BODY WE COULD NOT READ — which is NOT `[]`.

    `[]` is an affirmative answer: this repository has no pull request for you,
    which is `phase:"none"`, which is the one answer meaning "ready to open a
    PR" — and which CLEARS the persisted number. Collapsing an unintelligible
    body into it therefore turns "I could not understand GitHub" into "GitHub
    says the merge you had is gone". `gh` exiting 0 is not the same as `gh`
    having answered: an empty stdout, a JSON object, a proxy's HTML error page
    and a truncated array all arrive here at rc 0. Empty counts as unreadable
    too — `gh pr list --json` prints `[]` for a repo with no PRs, never
    nothing, and an empty stdin is exactly what deviation 8's program-on-stdin
    defect produced."""
    raw = sys.stdin.read().strip()
    if not raw:
        return None
    try:
        v = json.loads(raw)
    except Exception:
        return None
    return v if isinstance(v, list) else None

def git(main, *args):
    return subprocess.run(['git', '-C', main, *args], capture_output=True, text=True)

def is_ours(main, tip, oid):
    """Proof 0 — the PR's head commit exists locally AND, where we have a tip to
    reach from, is reachable from it. This is what a recycled slug and a
    stranger's fork both fail.

    The cat-file rung survives mutation and is kept deliberately: `merge-base
    --is-ancestor` on an object this repository does not have exits 128 and so
    already answers False. Kept because 128 is git's *"I could not do this"*
    and 1 is its *"no"*, and this function must not start reading a fatal as an
    answer if the ancestry call is ever changed. (`OID`'s `{7,40}` survives too:
    `{1,40}` accepts the same strings any real headRefOid is — it bounds a
    GitHub-sourced string rather than deciding anything, and the cat-file rung
    is what actually rejects a value.)

    `tip is None` is the registry's branch not resolving in $main — a hand
    rename or a hand delete, which deviation 7 measured and calls ORDINARY.
    There is then no anchor to reach FROM, and answering False demotes a bound
    MERGED row to `no-commits` and clears the persisted number: a LOCAL fact
    erasing GitHub's answer about a PR. So the ancestry rung is SKIPPED rather
    than failed, and what remains is still three of four conjuncts plus "this
    repository can still see that commit". The recycled slug — the case the
    ancestry rung exists for — cannot be in this state: ws-add creates the
    branch, so a recycled name always resolves and always gets the full proof.
    The wire says which rung answered: `tip` is null beside `ours`."""
    if not isinstance(oid, str) or not OID.match(oid):
        return False
    if git(main, 'cat-file', '-e', oid + '^{commit}').returncode != 0:
        return False
    if tip is None:
        return True
    return git(main, 'merge-base', '--is-ancestor', '--', oid, tip).returncode == 0

def annotate(rows, main, tip, branch, base_short):
    out = []
    for r in rows:
        if not isinstance(r, dict):
            continue
        r['ours'] = is_ours(main, tip, r.get('headRefOid'))
        out.append(r)
    return out

def bound(r, branch, base_short):
    """The workspace<->PR binding. Branch name alone is not it."""
    return (r.get('isCrossRepository') is False
            and r.get('baseRefName') == base_short
            and r.get('headRefName') == branch
            and r.get('ours') is True)

def is_merged(r, branch, base_short):
    # The trailing `bound(...)` conjunct survives mutation: every caller today
    # passes a row `pick` already filtered by `bound`. It stays so the predicate
    # is true of ANY row rather than only of a pre-filtered one — Task 5's
    # `pick` mode calls it too, and "merged" is the answer that fires the
    # archive trigger, which is not a fact to infer from the caller's habits.
    oid = (r.get('mergeCommit') or {}).get('oid')
    return (r.get('state') == 'MERGED'
            and isinstance(r.get('mergedAt'), str)
            and isinstance(oid, str) and OID.match(oid) is not None
            and bound(r, branch, base_short))

def pick(rows, branch, base_short):
    """Highest number AFTER the binding check, never before."""
    cands = [r for r in rows if bound(r, branch, base_short)]
    cands.sort(key=lambda r: r.get('number') if isinstance(r.get('number'), int) else 0)
    return cands[-1] if cands else None

if mode == 'state':
    (id_, project, main, repo, branch, base, base_short, tip, ahead, dirty, template, reg,
     answered_at) = sys.argv[2:15]
    # An argv can only carry a string, so "" is how the shell says UNMEASURED
    # for all three. None here and JSON null on the wire, never 0 and never "":
    # a number is a measurement, and none of these was measured. Deviation 7's
    # `tip: null` rule for the archive manifest, applied to the same fact.
    tip = tip or None
    ahead = int(ahead) if ahead != '' else None
    dirty = int(dirty) if dirty != '' else None

    # THE STAMP ARRIVES WITH THE ANSWER — re-review of the fix wave. This is the
    # value the compare-and-set below ranks runs by, so it has to describe WHEN
    # GITHUB ANSWERED, not when this process finished writing. It used to be
    # `int(time.time() * 1000)` taken further down, after `rows_in()` and after
    # this session's `git log`, while `cmd_pr_state --project` makes ONE gh call
    # and then loops `_pr_state_one` over every session of the repo — so the
    # k-th session's stamp was SECONDS newer than the answer it was carrying and
    # the CAS ranked completion times. `cmd_pr_state` now stamps the moment
    # `_gh_pr_list` returns and hands the same value to every session of the
    # sweep; the failure it closes is written out there.
    #
    # `''` IS NO STAMP AT ALL, and the fallback is this process's own clock —
    # exactly the pre-fix behaviour. A caller that has no millisecond clock (or
    # `_pr_state_one` invoked with four arguments, which the pr-state tests do)
    # must lose the RANKING, never the update: this guard may only remove races.
    checked_at = int(answered_at) if answered_at != '' else int(time.time() * 1000)
    raw_rows = rows_in()
    if raw_rows is None:
        # THE ID-LESS SHAPE, on purpose: the body is one gh call's answer for the
        # whole repository, so it is unreadable for every session of it at once —
        # which is what the id-less object means and what the sweep's per-project
        # backoff keys off. Nothing is persisted: the last good phase, number and
        # checkedAt all stay, which is what makes "last checked 6m ago" the honest
        # reading rather than a fresh-looking lie.
        sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({'phase': 'unknown', 'reason': 'error'}) + '\n')
        sys.exit(0)
    rows = annotate(raw_rows, main, tip, branch, base_short)
    chosen = pick(rows, branch, base_short)
    # `reason` accompanies `phase` and stays None for every phase but one, so
    # that prstate.ts's phaseFor can be pinned equal to this on BOTH fields.
    reason = None
    if chosen is None:
        # `ahead is None` is UNMEASURED, and `None == 0` is False in python, so an
        # unresolvable ref falls through to `none` — "no PR bound to this
        # workspace", which is what was actually observed — instead of
        # `no-commits`, which asserts that the branch is level with base.
        phase, number = ('no-commits' if ahead == 0 else 'none'), None
    elif is_merged(chosen, branch, base_short):
        phase, number = 'merged', chosen.get('number')
    elif chosen.get('state') == 'MERGED':
        # gh says MERGED but a conjunct failed. Never claim merged on a partial
        # match: the archive trigger and the cleanup offer both hang off it.
        # `merge-unproven`, NOT `error`: the gh read succeeded, and calling this
        # `error` puts "GitHub could not be read" on a phone about a read that
        # worked. phaseFor answers the same token for the same row.
        phase, number, reason = 'unknown', chosen.get('number'), 'merge-unproven'
    elif chosen.get('state') == 'CLOSED':
        phase, number = 'closed', chosen.get('number')
    elif chosen.get('isDraft') is True:
        phase, number = 'draft', chosen.get('number')
    else:
        phase, number = 'open', chosen.get('number')

    commits = []
    if tip and base:
        # `--` AFTER the revisions, per Global Constraints. SURVIVES mutation by
        # construction, exactly as the `rev-list` one does: `$base` comes from
        # the registry and always yields a `..`-bearing operand, so git can only
        # read it as a range and no fixture can distinguish the two forms. The
        # rule exists because git's own disambiguation is positional, and
        # `git diff --quiet -- A B` silently treats both shas as PATHSPECS
        # (verified: exit 0, where the same call with `--` last exits 1).
        r = git(main, 'log', '--format=%H%x1f%s%x1f%b%x1e', base + '..refs/heads/' + branch, '--')
        if r.returncode == 0:
            for rec in r.stdout.split('\x1e'):
                rec = rec.strip('\n')
                if not rec:
                    continue
                parts = (rec.split('\x1f') + ['', '', ''])[:3]
                commits.append({'sha': parts[0], 'subject': parts[1], 'body': parts[2].strip()})

    def put(field, value):
        # ATOMIC, the python half of `_reg_set`. `open(…,'w')`
        # truncates in place, so a reader landing inside the window read an
        # EMPTY `prphase`/`prnumber` — and `prphase` is read by `ws-archive`,
        # which files `archivedreason merged:#N` off it. Same tmp shape and same
        # invisibility argument as `_reg_set`: leading dot (out of every bash
        # glob) and a `.tmp` suffix (out of `readRegistryMeasured`'s
        # `endsWith('.uuid')` id minting, which DOES see dotfiles).
        dst = os.path.join(reg, id_ + '.' + field)
        tmp = os.path.join(reg, '.' + id_ + '.' + field + '.' + str(os.getpid()) + '.tmp')
        # D-2380: this tmp open is one of FOUR by-name opens in this mode,
        # alongside its three siblings (`get()` below, the compare-and-set
        # lock, the prhistory append) — not counted alongside them until this
        # pass, undercounted by the same D-2475 staleness their own count had
        # already gone through once this wave. `open(tmp,'w')` blocks on a
        # FIFO exactly as the siblings' `'a'` opens do (measured standalone:
        # `mkfifo f.tmp; timeout 3 python3 -c "open('f.tmp','w')"` -> rc 124),
        # and it is reachable: this path's name embeds `os.getpid()`, and pid
        # reuse on a fleet host running ~20 supervised sessions is ordinary,
        # so a stale non-regular file left by an EARLIER holder of this same
        # pid can sit here when a new process claims it. Unlike the siblings,
        # `'w'` is a CREATE, so `os.path.isfile` alone is the wrong test:
        # the ordinary path never sees this tmp exist at all (fresh per-pid
        # name), a STALE REGULAR tmp left by a prior crash must still be
        # truncated and reused, and only a PRESENT NON-REGULAR tmp must
        # refuse rather than block inside the open.
        # `os.path.lexists(...) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(...).st_mode)`
        # draws exactly that line — absent:
        # proceed and create; regular: proceed and truncate; anything else:
        # raise before the open, the same shape the lock guard below uses —
        # and the raise is caught by the `except OSError` immediately below,
        # so it becomes the same FAULT that block already turns any other
        # write failure into. `lexists`, NOT `exists` (final-round fix,
        # measured end to end): `exists` FOLLOWS a symlink, so a DANGLING
        # symlink at `tmp` answered False — guard skipped, `open(tmp,'w')`
        # created the link's TARGET, and `os.replace(tmp, dst)` renamed the
        # SYMLINK onto `dst`, so `$REG/<id>.<field>` ended up a symlink and
        # the field's bytes landed outside `$REG` entirely. `lexists` does
        # not follow, so a dangling symlink now raises here same as any
        # other non-regular shape. The cleanup arm's `os.remove` DOES clear a
        # FIFO, socket or device at the tmp path — the same self-heal
        # `get()`'s FIFO case gets — but NOT a directory: `os.remove` is
        # `unlink(2)`, which raises an `OSError` — `IsADirectoryError` on
        # Linux (errno 21, measured here), `PermissionError` on BSD/macOS
        # (`unlink(2)` there answers EPERM, and this repo's own server suite
        # runs on `macos-latest` too, unmeasured here) — swallowed either way
        # by the inner `except OSError: pass` below, so a directory refuses
        # correctly but is never self-healed.
        #
        # AND `os.path.isfile` WAS ALSO WRONG HERE, for the same reason
        # `exists` was (F1, review run 69; reproduced end to end before the
        # fix): `isfile` FOLLOWS symlinks, so a LIVE symlink pointing at an
        # EXISTING REGULAR file answered True, the guard did not fire, and
        # the `exists`-era escape reproduced IDENTICALLY — `open(tmp,'w')`
        # wrote THROUGH the link, `os.replace(tmp, dst)` renamed the SYMLINK
        # onto `dst`, and the field's bytes landed outside `$REG`. The
        # `lexists` fix closed the DANGLING half only; BOTH conjuncts had to
        # stop following the link, so the type test is now
        # `stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(...).st_mode)`, which answers about the link
        # itself rather than its target. `lexists` still guards it, so an
        # absent path short-circuits before `lstat` can raise — and a race
        # that removes the path between the two raises `FileNotFoundError`,
        # an `OSError`, which this block already turns into the same FAULT.
        # The guard's population is therefore `lexists ∧ ¬S_ISREG(lstat)`:
        # every PRESENT shape that is not itself a regular file, symlinks to
        # regular files INCLUDED. A directory is the one remaining gap in
        # self-heal, not in the refusal.
        try:
            if os.path.lexists(tmp) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(tmp).st_mode):
                raise OSError('refusing to write a non-regular-file pr-state tmp: ' + tmp)
            with open(tmp, 'w') as f:
                f.write(str(value))
            os.replace(tmp, dst)
        except OSError:
            try:
                os.remove(tmp)
            except OSError:
                pass
            raise

    def get(field):
        # put/clear's own path arithmetic, read instead of written. Needed by
        # the prhistory chokepoint below to see the OUTGOING prnumber/prphase
        # before `clear`/the next `put` overwrite them — nothing upstream of
        # this `state` mode has read $REG for anything but rows_in()'s stdin,
        # so there was no read helper here to reuse.
        #
        # D-2380: `open()` opens BY NAME with no regard for TYPE — a FIFO with
        # no writer, or an unbounded character device, at `$REG/<id>.<field>`
        # blocks in open(2)/read(2) forever, and this is reached from
        # `cmd_pr_state`'s per-id loop, so one poisoned field hangs the whole
        # sweep rather than one row. `except FileNotFoundError` alone cannot
        # catch this — `open()` itself blocks, the handler is never reached.
        # THE TYPE TEST IS `lstat`-BASED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT
        # (D-2989, review 71 CRITICAL 1). `os.path.isfile` FOLLOWS the chain,
        # so a symlink at `$REG/<id>.<field>` resolving to any readable
        # regular file passed it and `open()` returned THAT FILE'S bytes at
        # rc 0 — a fabricated value from outside `$REG`, which `cmd_pr_state`
        # then persisted into `.prhistory`. The sentence this replaces
        # promised "never a fabricated value"; it was false, and it is what
        # defeated two separate audits of this very function.
        # `os.path.lexists(...) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(...).st_mode)`
        # is asked FIRST and does not follow, so every non-regular shape —
        # symlink resolving or dangling, FIFO, socket, device, directory —
        # answers `None` on a type check alone.
        # WHAT THE GUARD NOW GUARANTEES, stated as what it does and not as
        # what it was hoped to do: any bytes this returns came from a REGULAR
        # FILE AT THIS PATH, never through a link to one elsewhere. It does
        # NOT guarantee the field is present — absent and present-but-not-a-
        # regular-file still share the one `None`, which is the overload
        # disclosed immediately below and deliberately still open.
        # `FileNotFoundError` stays caught for the ordinary TOCTOU race
        # (removed between this check and the open).
        #
        # DISCLOSED, NOT CHANGED (final-round item 8): "present but not a
        # regular file" and "absent" collapse to the identical `None` here —
        # an overloaded null at a seam, the shape this repo's own
        # conventions ban, and `_check_models` (`ccd/ccrc-doctor-checks`)
        # takes the OPPOSITE position on the same class (a distinct verdict
        # for a read it cannot trust). Not reopened this late, but NARROWED
        # to what measures true (final-round correction): only a DIRECTORY at
        # this same path still faults at the next `put`/`clear` — `put`'s own
        # guard sits on the TMP path, not on `dst`, so `os.replace(tmp, dst)`
        # renames straight over a FIFO, socket, device or symlink at `dst`
        # with no raise, and `clear`'s `os.remove` unlinks any of those four
        # just as silently. A directory at `dst` is the one shape that still
        # faults, because `os.replace`/`os.remove` themselves refuse it
        # (`IsADirectoryError`) with nothing downstream to swallow that. For
        # every other non-regular shape the overloaded `None` above is NOT
        # contained: the next write silently replaces or unlinks it instead
        # of raising, exactly as though the field had simply never existed.
        # AND THE READ SIDE IS A SEPARATE QUESTION THIS PARAGRAPH USED TO
        # ANSWER BY OMISSION (D-2989). Everything above reasons about what the
        # next WRITE does, and concluded from it that the collapse is
        # equivalent to absence. For a RESOLVING SYMLINK that was never true:
        # the write was indeed silent, but the READ returned another file's
        # bytes at rc 0, so the field was not absent-equivalent, it was
        # fabricated. The guard above now refuses it before the open. The
        # write-side reasoning stands as written; it simply never covered the
        # read, which is the half that bit.
        p = os.path.join(reg, id_ + '.' + field)
        if os.path.lexists(p) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(p).st_mode):
            return None
        if not os.path.isfile(p):
            return None
        try:
            with open(p) as f:
                return f.read()
        except FileNotFoundError:
            return None

    def clear(field):
        try:
            os.remove(os.path.join(reg, id_ + '.' + field))
        except FileNotFoundError:
            pass

    # NARROW FIRST, WIDEN LAST — final-round integration New Finding 9. These
    # are two files and there is no way to write two files atomically, so the
    # question is not "can a kill land between them" (it can) but "which pairs
    # can a kill leave on disk". The shipped order was `put('prphase')` first
    # and the number nineteen lines later, whose intermediate state is
    # {new phase, OLD number} — and for the commonest transition of all, a
    # merged PR going away, that is precisely {'none', 42}: the one pair
    # docket 1 proved otherwise unconstructible, the one `fleet.ts` renders as
    # `#42` under copy that says "no pull request yet", and the one
    # `ws-archive` files as `merged:#42` for a PR that no longer binds here.
    #
    # Three steps in this order instead, so every intermediate is a pair that
    # was ACTUALLY answered together, or a phase with no number at all:
    #     clear number  ->  {old phase, absent}
    #     put phase     ->  {new phase, absent}
    #     put number    ->  {new phase, new number}
    # `{X, absent}` is not a new state to teach anyone: it is what a workspace
    # that never had a PR looks like, what a `merged` row with no `number`
    # field looks like, and `_reg_get`'s readers already treat a missing or
    # non-numeric `prnumber` as null (ccd:1391, ccd:5376). The window is a
    # DEGRADED reading, never a false one, and the old window's was false.
    #
    # The unconditional `clear` also costs one unlink on the path where the
    # number is unchanged, which is the price of not having to reason about
    # which transitions are safe.
    #
    # THE prhistory CHOKEPOINT. This `clear` is the only line in ccd that ever
    # replaces a persisted `prnumber`, so it is the only place PR lineage can
    # be recorded without depending on which sweep or verb caused the
    # replacement. The OUTGOING record — the number about to stop being
    # true — is appended here, immediately BEFORE the clear, because after it
    # `get('prnumber')` can no longer see what is leaving.
    #
    # Appended only when a REAL prior number is about to become a DIFFERENT
    # answer: `old_num != number` fires for another number and for `None`
    # alike (a real number, incoming `None` counts as different, `old_num is
    # not None` counts as int) — the between-waves gap where a program resets
    # the branch and the old PR is not yet superseded by a new one must not
    # lose the record either. Steady state — the sweep re-running every 120 s
    # against an unchanged number — takes `old_num != number` to False and
    # appends nothing, or every sweep for the life of a PR would duplicate it.
    # A non-numeric `old_raw` (corrupt or hand-edited registry) leaves
    # `old_num` None and also appends nothing: there is no number to retire.
    # ONE WRITER AT A TIME, AND ONLY THE NEWEST ANSWER WRITES — fix-wave
    # finding 4. Everything from here to `put('prcheckedat', …)` is one
    # read-modify-write of a single session's entry, and TWO CONCURRENT
    # `ccd pr-state` RUNS AGAINST ONE SESSION ARE ORDINARY: the PR sheet's
    # `GET /api/sessions/:id/pr` fires one on every open while the watcher's
    # 120 s sweep fires another, and nothing upstream serialises them (the send
    # queue wraps the send routes, not these). The comment above reasons only
    # about SERIAL re-runs; these are the two in-flight cases it does not cover.
    #
    #   (a) DUPLICATE. Both read `prnumber=577`, both compute 583, both take
    #       `old_num != number`, and both append `{"pr":603,…}` before either
    #       reaches `clear`. The ledger is append-only and `_ws_archive_manifest`
    #       folds it in verbatim, so the archive card reads
    #       `merged:#583 (waves: #577 #577)` for ever.
    #   (b) FALSE RECORD. The sheet's run reads gh BEFORE the merge (rows bind
    #       #577) while the sweep's reads AFTER (rows bind #583); the sweep
    #       lands first and writes 583; the sheet's run then reads
    #       `old_raw='583'`, takes `583 != 577`, and retires the NEWER, still-live
    #       PR into history — then writes `prnumber` back to 577.
    #
    # The lock alone fixes (a) — under it the `get('prnumber')` below is fresh,
    # so the loser sees 583, `old_num == number`, and appends nothing. It cannot
    # fix (b), because there the loser's ANSWER is older than the winner's, not
    # its read. That takes the compare-and-set: `prcheckedat` timestamps the gh
    # answer this run is carrying — `checked_at`, which `cmd_pr_state` takes AT
    # THE gh READ and passes in, so that the whole of a `--project` fan-out
    # carries the one timestamp of the one call it made. A run whose answer
    # predates what is already on disk persists NOTHING — no history line, no
    # phase, no number. It still prints its own answer on stdout, which is what
    # the caller asked for and the only thing it can honestly claim: a display
    # one sweep stale, never a persisted lie.
    #
    # RANKING ANSWERS, NOT COMPLETIONS, is the whole of it. While the stamp was
    # taken here instead — after `rows_in()` and after the `git log` above — (b)
    # survived the CAS in the shape it is commonest in: the sweep's gh returns at
    # T0 binding #577, the sheet's own `--session` read returns at T0+1.0s
    # binding the NEW #583 and persists it, and the sweep reaches that session at
    # T0+1.4s with a stamp that OUTRANKS the newer answer. #583 went into the
    # append-only ledger and `prnumber` went back to #577.
    #
    # A DEDICATED lock file, never a registry FIELD — wave 2. `flock` attaches to
    # an open file description (an INODE), and `_reg_set "$id" uuid` is written from
    # three call sites — `cmd_ws_add`, `_sync_uuid`, `cmd_start`. The write on the
    # ORDINARY SUPERVISE PATH is `_sync_uuid`, which `cmd_supervise`'s loop calls
    # every tick; the other two, `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start`, run once each. Every
    # such write RENAMES a new inode over `.uuid`. Two `ccd pr-state` runs straddling
    # that rewrite would take LOCK_EX on two different inodes and both enter the
    # compare-and-set below: the duplicate append and lost update this lock prevents.
    # `$REG/.prstate-<id>.lock` is never written, never renamed and NEVER UNLINKED
    # — the same idiom, for the same reason, as `$REG/.reap-$id.lock`, inside
    # `cmd_ws_reap`, which also explains why unlinking a lock while holding it is how
    # two processes come to hold "the lock" on two inodes. Hidden by its leading dot,
    # so no registry glob or id-minting listing sees it; it leaks one 0-byte file per
    # session that has ever run pr-state — the disclosed price. NAMED BY FUNCTION,
    # NOT LINE: a line number here goes stale the moment anything above it grows —
    # twice now on this branch.
    #
    # WIDENED BY ONE STATE AND NARROWED BY ANOTHER — not "unchanged", and the
    # widened state is narrower than it first looks. Measured both ways under
    # a `$REG` that is readable but NOT WRITABLE (`chmod 0555`):
    #
    #   - An EXISTING `.prstate-<id>.lock` (mode 0664) — `open(path, 'a')`
    #     SUCCEEDS and the flock is taken. Appending to an existing file needs
    #     write permission on the FILE, not on the directory it lives in.
    #   - The FIRST-EVER `.prstate-<id>.lock` for a session — `open(path, 'a')`
    #     raises `PermissionError` (EACCES), because that open must CREATE the
    #     file, which does need write on the directory.
    #
    # So the widening is only the FIRST-EVER `ccd pr-state` for a given
    # session under an unwritable `$REG`; every run after that creates no new
    # file and takes the lock normally, same as the old `$REG/<id>.uuid`
    # READ-ONLY open did. In the other direction, the old condition list also
    # carried "no `.uuid` at all" — a lock that could not be taken because the
    # field had not been written yet — and this one cannot hit that, which is
    # a strict improvement. The widened state is a REAL residual, not a dead
    # one — measured, not assumed. Under a `$REG` at `chmod 0555` (readable,
    # not writable): an EXISTING `.prstate-<id>.lock` opens (`open(path, 'a')`)
    # and its flock is taken, fine; the FIRST-EVER `.prstate-<id>.lock` for a
    # session raises `PermissionError` at that same open, because THIS open
    # must CREATE the file, which needs write on the DIRECTORY; an append to
    # an EXISTING `.prhistory` (`open(path, 'a')`, below) is fine too, because
    # appending needs write on the FILE, not the directory — the unwritable
    # `$REG` never touches it; and `clear`'s `os.remove` and `put`'s tmp-create
    # both raise `PermissionError`, because both need write on the DIRECTORY
    # (D-132). So for a session that has never run `pr-state` under an
    # unwritable `$REG`: the lock cannot be created, the compare-and-set below
    # proceeds UNLOCKED, and two concurrent runs can each reach the prhistory
    # append — the duplicate-append harm this lock exists to prevent — before
    # both die at `clear`/`put`. The result is a duplicated PR lineage record,
    # which `_ws_archive_manifest` later folds into the archive manifest
    # verbatim.
    #
    # DISCLOSED, NOT CLOSED (ruling, D-139). It takes a read-only `$REG` AND
    # concurrent `pr-state` runs AND a session with no lock file yet — all
    # three at once — and the natural closes each trade this rare duplicate
    # for a worse ordinary-case cost: locking `.prhistory` itself adds a new
    # inode-stability assumption to the hot path, and skipping the append when
    # unlocked loses a lineage record any time the lock open fails for the
    # ORDINARY reason — a filesystem whose `flock` refuses, or any other
    # `OSError` on the lock open — not only in the unwritable-`$REG` case
    # above — both worse trades for a state in which the registry is already
    # unwritable.
    #
    # THE GUARANTEE IS UNCHANGED, and it is the part that matters: if the lock
    # cannot be taken AT ALL — the state above, or a filesystem whose `flock`
    # refuses — the write goes ahead UNLOCKED. This guard may only remove
    # races, never add a refusal that stops a phase from ever updating.
    lock_f = None
    try:
        # D-2380: same class as `get()` above — `open(path, 'a')` opens BY
        # NAME and blocks forever on a FIFO with no writer; the `except
        # OSError` below cannot fire for a block, only for an error, so it
        # does NOT cover this. Guarded only when something is already there
        # (`os.path.lexists`, not `exists` — same final-round fix as
        # `put()`'s tmp guard above: `exists` follows a symlink, so a
        # DANGLING one at this path would read as absent and skip the
        # guard): the FIRST-EVER lock file for a session must still be free
        # to be CREATED by this open (the very case the comment above this
        # block argues about), so an ABSENT path is not refused here, only a
        # PRESENT non-regular one — which raises the same `OSError` this
        # `try` already turns into "proceed unlocked", so the guarantee that
        # block states ("this guard may only remove races, never add a
        # refusal that stops a phase from ever updating") holds unchanged.
        # The type test is `stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(...).st_mode)`, NOT
        # `os.path.isfile`, for the reason `put()`'s guard above records at
        # length (F1): `isfile` follows the link, so a symlink to an existing
        # regular file passed this guard too and the `'a'` open appended
        # THROUGH it, to a file outside `$REG`.
        lock_p = os.path.join(reg, '.prstate-' + id_ + '.lock')
        if os.path.lexists(lock_p) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(lock_p).st_mode):
            raise OSError('refusing to open a non-regular-file pr-state lock: ' + lock_p)
        lock_f = open(lock_p, 'a')
        fcntl.flock(lock_f.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)
    except OSError:
        if lock_f is not None:
            lock_f.close()
        lock_f = None
    try:
        prev_at = None
        prev_checked = get('prcheckedat')
        if prev_checked is not None:
            try:
                prev_at = int(prev_checked.strip())
            except ValueError:
                prev_at = None     # unparseable: no newer answer is PROVEN, so this one still writes
        # `<=`, not `<`: two runs landing in the same millisecond are not a
        # staleness case, and refusing there would drop a real update.
        if prev_at is None or prev_at <= checked_at:
            old_raw = get('prnumber')
            old_phase = get('prphase') or ''   # read before `put('prphase', ...)` below overwrites it
            if old_raw is not None:
                try:
                    old_num = int(old_raw)
                except ValueError:
                    old_num = None
                if old_num is not None and old_num != number:
                    rec = {'pr': old_num, 'branch': branch, 'phase': old_phase,
                           'recordedAt': int(time.time())}
                    # D-2380: one of FOUR OPENS in this mode, alongside
                    # `get()`, the lock above and `put()`'s own tmp-file open
                    # (above `get()` in this file) — the plan's own cardinal
                    # named two (`get()` and the lock above), and a first
                    # correction this same wave counted three and still
                    # missed `put()`'s tmp-file open, which is by-name and
                    # blocks on the identical shapes. Corrected in place
                    # again here, still taking no new deviation number: the
                    # coordinator's ruling is that a stale COUNT under this
                    # number is the number going stale (the D-2475 shape),
                    # not a new subject, and this is that ruling applied a
                    # second time to the same subject. Same class, same
                    # guard as `put()`'s: `open(path, 'a')` opens BY NAME
                    # and blocks forever on a FIFO with no writer, and this is
                    # the ONE write in this mode with no `try`/`except` of its
                    # own (it sits inside the outer `try` whose only handler
                    # is the `finally` that releases the lock), so an
                    # uncaught fault here is a FAULT, not a silent skip — a
                    # strict improvement over a hang either way, because it is
                    # bounded and visible on stderr, even though it costs this
                    # one row's update rather than merely the append. Guarded
                    # only when present-and-not-regular, exactly like the
                    # lock above (`lexists`, not `exists` — same final-round
                    # fix: a dangling symlink here must not read as absent;
                    # and `stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(...))`, not
                    # `os.path.isfile`, the F1 fix: `isfile` follows the
                    # link, so a LIVE symlink to a regular file passed and
                    # the rows were appended THROUGH it, outside `$REG`):
                    # the first-ever prhistory file must still be free to be
                    # CREATED.
                    hist_p = os.path.join(reg, id_ + '.prhistory')
                    if os.path.lexists(hist_p) and not stat.S_ISREG(os.lstat(hist_p).st_mode):
                        raise OSError('refusing to append to a non-regular-file prhistory: ' + hist_p)
                    with open(hist_p, 'a') as f:
                        f.write(json.dumps(rec) + '\n')
            clear('prnumber')
            put('prphase', phase)
            # phase and number are ONE answer, so a writer that persists half
            # of it lies. A PR that is deleted, force-pushed out of
            # reachability, or whose slug ws-reap has recycled leaves
            # {phase:'none', number:42} on disk if the number is merely left
            # alone: fleet.ts hands that pair straight to the wire, so the cap
            # renders `#42` under a phase whose own copy is "no pull request
            # yet", and ws-archive reads the same field and files
            # `archivedreason merged:#42` for a PR that no longer binds to this
            # workspace. REMOVED rather than blanked, so the field is absent
            # exactly as it is for a workspace that never had a PR.
            #
            # NO FAILED READ REACHES THIS LINE, and it takes two guards to say
            # that, not one. `_gh_pr_list` returns before `_pr_py` is called for
            # every read that failed by EXIT CODE. An rc-0 read whose body is
            # not a list of rows does reach `_pr_py` — and `rows_in` answering
            # None returns above, before any `put`/`clear` runs. The comment
            # here used to claim the first guard alone made this unreachable; it
            # did not, and a `gh` that exited 0 with an empty or unparseable
            # stdout cleared a persisted `merged:#42`.
            if number is not None:
                put('prnumber', number)
            # LAST, and that ordering is the compare-and-set's other half: this
            # is the stamp the next writer compares against, so it must not
            # advance until the answer it describes is actually on disk.
            put('prcheckedat', checked_at)
    finally:
        if lock_f is not None:
            lock_f.close()      # closing the fd releases the flock

    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps({
        'id': id_, 'project': project, 'repo': repo, 'branch': branch, 'base': base,
        'baseShort': base_short, 'tip': tip, 'ahead': ahead, 'dirty': dirty,
        'commits': commits, 'template': template or None, 'rows': rows,
        'phase': phase, 'number': number, 'checkedAt': checked_at, 'reason': reason,
        # THE ROLLUP'S OWN FAILURE, DECLARED. Emitted only when true, so a line
        # from a build that fetched the rollup in-band is unchanged and an older
        # server reading a newer line simply ignores a key it has no reader for
        # (additive-only; absence permits). Without it the server cannot tell a
        # row GitHub said has no checks from a row whose rollup call never
        # answered — both merely lack the key — and it renders the second as the
        # first: "No checks configured", under a fresh timestamp, on a PR whose
        # build may be red.
        **({'checksUnmeasured': True} if os.environ.get('PR_CHECKS_UNMEASURED') == '1' else {}),
    }) + '\n')

elif mode == 'rollups':
    # THE JOIN, and it is here rather than in bash because both documents are
    # GitHub-sourced JSON and spec §7 says no GitHub-sourced string is ever
    # placed in an argv or a shell word. The rows arrive on STDIN as every other
    # mode's do; the rollups arrive on FD 4, for the same reason the program
    # itself arrives on fd 3 — two documents cannot share one stdin without a
    # framing convention, and inventing one over a body GitHub controls is how
    # a truncated answer becomes a forged one.
    #
    # ROWS UNREADABLE IS A REFUSAL, NOT AN EMPTY JOIN. `rows_in()` answering
    # None is the body we could not read, and printing `[]` here would hand the
    # caller an affirmative "this repository has no pull request for you" — the
    # exact substitution `rows_in`'s docstring exists to prevent, one hop
    # earlier than it used to be possible. Exit non-zero with nothing on stdout
    # and the caller keeps the original body, so `state` mode meets the same
    # unreadable answer it would have met without this pass and emits the
    # id-less failure object it always did.
    #
    # ROLLUPS UNREADABLE IS AN EMPTY JOIN, which is the opposite decision about
    # the opposite document, and the difference is what each one is: the rows
    # are the answer, the rollups are an annotation on it. Absent, unparseable,
    # not a list, fd 4 not open at all — every one of those means the same
    # thing, that there is nothing to attach, and every one of them leaves the
    # rows exactly as they arrived. That is what makes a failed rollup call
    # unable to cost a phase.
    #
    # The field NAME arrives in argv rather than being spelled again here, and
    # that is the same rule the rest of this file follows for `$REG` and
    # `$main`: it is ccd's own constant (`PR_CHECK_FIELD`), not a
    # GitHub-sourced string, and a second spelling of it in this heredoc is a
    # second thing that has to be kept in step with what `_gh_pr_checks` asks
    # gh for. One of the two would eventually be renamed alone, and the join
    # would then silently attach nothing.
    ROLLUP = sys.argv[2]
    rows = rows_in()
    if rows is None:
        sys.exit(1)
    try:
        with open('/dev/fd/4') as f:
            raw = f.read().strip()
    except OSError:
        raw = ''
    by_number = {}
    if raw:
        try:
            v = json.loads(raw)
        except Exception:
            v = None
        if isinstance(v, list):
            for r in v:
                # `isinstance(n, int)` and not a truthiness test: PR numbers are
                # ints from GitHub, and an unhashable `number` (a list, an
                # object) would raise on the lookup below and take the whole
                # sweep down. `bool` is an int in python and cannot appear here,
                # so the narrow check is also the safe one.
                if isinstance(r, dict) and isinstance(r.get('number'), int):
                    by_number[r['number']] = r.get(ROLLUP)
    for r in rows:
        if not isinstance(r, dict):
            continue
        n = r.get('number')
        # WRITTEN ONLY WHERE THE SECOND CALL ANSWERED. A row the rollup window
        # did not cover keeps NO `statusCheckRollup` key, which `checksFor`
        # reads as `checks: null` (`Array.isArray(undefined)` is false) — the
        # same answer it gives for a PR GitHub says has no checks. Writing an
        # explicit null instead would say the same thing in more bytes; writing
        # `[]` would say "this PR has no checks", which is a claim.
        if isinstance(n, int) and n in by_number:
            r[ROLLUP] = by_number[n]
    sys.stdout.write(json.dumps(rows) + '\n')

elif mode == 'pick':
    (main, branch, base_short, tip) = sys.argv[2:6]
    rows = annotate(rows_in(), main, tip, branch, base_short)
    # A row that matches the branch and base but whose head is NOT reachable
    # from our tip gets its own token: "no PR at all" and "a PR that is not
    # ours" are different sentences, and only one of them is about a stranger.
    near = [r for r in rows
            if r.get('headRefName') == branch and r.get('baseRefName') == base_short
            and r.get('isCrossRepository') is False]
    chosen = pick(rows, branch, base_short)
    if chosen is None:
        sys.stdout.write('!pr-head-not-ours\n' if near else '')
    elif not is_merged(chosen, branch, base_short):
        sys.stdout.write('!not-merged\n')
    else:
        sys.stdout.write('\t'.join([
            str(chosen.get('number') or ''), str(chosen.get('url') or ''),
            str((chosen.get('mergeCommit') or {}).get('oid') or ''),
            str(chosen.get('headRefOid') or ''),
        ]) + '\n')
PY
}

# ── workspace slugs ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# No dots: tmux -t parses `session:window.pane`. No slashes: systemd
# instance names escape them. Lowercase only, so a slug is never ambiguous
# on a case-insensitive filesystem.
WS_ADJ=(quiet swift amber still brisk clear plain warm bright calm keen soft)
WS_NOUN=(mesa river ridge harbor meadow canyon summit hollow prairie basin cove delta)

_ws_slug_valid() { [[ "$1" =~ ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9-]{1,30}$ ]]; }

# R-3, wave review. Ids are `<project>-<slug>`, and `_reg_purge` unlinks
# `"$REG/$id".*` for every dot-free suffix — so a PROJECT with a LEADING DOT
# mints an id that ALIASES one of ccd's own hidden, dot-prefixed registry
# files for an unrelated session. THE RULE, not a fixed list: a dot-leading
# project aliases EVERY `$REG/.<name>.<dot-free-suffix>` file ccd owns. Four
# known instances illustrate it, all verified: project `.prstate-demo` +
# slug `quiet-basin` -> id `.prstate-demo-quiet-basin`, whose purge glob
# matches `$REG/.prstate-demo-quiet-basin.lock` — the PR-STATE LOCK of the
# unrelated session `demo-quiet-basin` (new this wave, the lock `_pr_py`'s
# migration off `.uuid` introduced); project `.reap-demo` + slug
# `quiet-basin` aliases that same session's REAP LOCK,
# `$REG/.reap-demo-quiet-basin.lock` (pre-dates this wave); project
# `.ws-add` + slug `demo` -> id `.ws-add-demo`, aliasing the WS-ADD LOCK for
# project `demo`, `$REG/.ws-add-demo.lock` (pre-dates this wave);
# project `.tmux` + slug `server` -> id `.tmux-server`, aliasing the
# TMUX-SERVER LOCK `$REG/.tmux-server.lock` that `_tmux_new_session` takes
# (pre-dates this wave); and project `.account` + slug `placement` -> id
# `.account-placement`, aliasing the ACCOUNT-PLACEMENT LOCK
# `$REG/.account-placement.lock` that all five `ccrc account` writing verbs
# serialise on (new with that verb, and NOT amended here when it landed — see
# the census note below) — `server` and `placement` pass `_ws_slug_valid`
# exactly as the other three slugs do, no more contrived. Unlinking a lock file while
# another process holds it is exactly how two processes come to hold "the
# lock" on two different inodes — reopening the double compare-and-set this
# branch's lock migration closed.
#
# NOT reachable this way, and this set IS the boundary — ELEVEN dot-prefixed
# artifacts live under `$REG`; the five above are reachable, these six are
# not. THE CARDINAL IS MEASURED NOW, and it had to be: this paragraph said
# EIGHT about a tree that already held nine, because `.account-placement.lock`
# landed with `ccrc account` and nothing here reds when prose drifts.
# `ccd-account-auth.test.ts`'s "the dot-prefixed registry inventory is a
# census, not a memory" derives the number from the shipped bash and refuses
# a paragraph that disagrees with it. The six:
# `$REG/.lifecycle/` (the lifecycle journal — a dotted DIRECTORY for
# exactly this reason; see the LC-BEGIN block, and note that its generations,
# `errors` and `.rotate.lock` are counted WITH it, as `.reaped/`'s tombstones
# are counted with `.reaped/`); `$REG/.reaped/` (a directory, not a
# `.reaped.<suffix>` file — no id's
# purge glob matches a bare directory, and `rm -f` cannot take one
# regardless); `$REG/.auth/` (the account-connection status files — a dotted
# DIRECTORY for the same reason `.lifecycle/` is one, and unreachable for
# `.reaped/`'s reason rather than the second-dot one below; its per-id
# `<id>.json`, its `.<id>.<pid>.tmp` staging files and its `<id>.run/` FIFOs
# are counted WITH it); `_reg_set`'s own tmps; `ccd-pool-sync`'s
# `$REG/.pool-epoch.$$.tmp` staging file, written by the same tmp-then-rename
# idiom outside `_reg_set` itself, before it renames into the dot-FREE
# `$REG/pool-epoch` — the projection's published name carries no dot, only
# its transient staging copy does; and session-hook.sh's
# `.$id.$$.hookstate.tmp` — the last three ARE candidate glob matches when
# `<id>` is the aliasing id, but their suffix after the match carries a
# SECOND dot, which is exactly what `_reg_purge`'s own `*.*` skip excludes.
#
# THAT SKIP IS THE LOOP'S RULE ONLY, not a general immunity — `_reg_purge`
# also removes one named two-dot file EXPLICITLY, after the loop:
# `rm -f "$REG/$id.hookstate.json"`, un-skipped on purpose (see the note in
# `_reg_purge` beside that line). "Has a dot in the suffix" is sufficient
# for these two tmp families specifically, because nothing in `_reg_purge`
# names them outside the loop, but it is not a general rule. No dot-prefixed
# artifact under `$REG` is ever named `<id>.hookstate.json` for an aliasing
# id, so no alias follows from this explicit line either.
#
# Enforced at CREATION ONLY — `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start`, the two sites
# that mint a NEW registry row for a project — never at `cmd_pr_state`,
# which applies this same `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$` shape FOUR times (its
# `--session` argv, its `--project` argv, the `*.workspace` loop's id
# check, and the registry-project check), of which two are PROJECT checks.
# The justification is not that those uses are read-only — `cmd_pr_state`
# persists three registry fields (`prnumber`, `prphase`, `prcheckedat`) and
# creates `$REG/.prstate-<id>.lock` — it is that NONE OF THEM MINTS AN ID:
# each validates a project that either already exists in a registry row or
# arrived for a read, and refusing there could STRAND an existing row
# rather than prevent a new one. The creation gate is what stops such a row
# ever existing in the first place, so the read side does not need to
# police it too.
_ws_project_valid() {   # project -> 0 when it is a legal project name to CREATE under
  # SAME SHADOW, SAME REASON AS `_pool_name_valid` (D-2542, and D-2522 is the
  # argument). `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` is TWO collation ranges, so under `en_US.UTF-8`
  # this accepts the same non-ASCII letters that one did. `cmd_project_pool`
  # calls BOTH — this for the project and `_pool_name_valid` for the pool — so
  # shadowing only the pool half would leave the verb half-fixed.
  # THE ASYMMETRY IS WHY IT MATTERS HERE RATHER THAN SOMEDAY. The reader is now
  # locale-independent; this gate was not. A project tagged from an interactive
  # shell (`LANG=en_US.UTF-8`) could then not be CLEARED from the fleet's own
  # `claude-session@*` environment, which carries no `LANG` at all: the tag
  # keeps constraining placement and `ccd project-pool --clear` refuses the very
  # name that created it. A constraint you can create and cannot remove with the
  # supported verb is worse than either locale's behaviour on its own.
  local LC_ALL=C
  [[ "$1" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || return 1
  [[ "$1" != .* ]] || return 1
}

# A slug is free only when the registry holds NOTHING for `<project>-<slug>` —
# verification round 3 P1. The old form was `[[ ! -f "$REG/$1-$2.uuid" ]]`, i.e.
# one field standing in for eighteen, and `_reg_purge` (see the long note there)
# now removes `.archived` and `.reaping` AFTER `.uuid` on purpose. A SIGKILL or
# an OOM in that window — this project's speciality — leaves state markers with
# no `.uuid`, which the old test read as a clean slug and handed to the next
# `ws-add`; `cmd_ws_add` purges nothing for the id it creates, so the markers
# became the new workspace's. The property this establishes is not "the markers
# in particular are seen" but that a PARTIALLY-PURGED ID CANNOT BE REUSED AS IF
# IT WERE CLEAN, wherever the interruption landed and whichever function was
# interrupted — `cmd_ws_add`'s own `_reg_set` run has the same windows, and a
# field added tomorrow is covered without anyone remembering to come back here.
#
# The `*.*` skip is `_reg_purge`'s, for `_reg_purge`'s reason and measured the
# same way: a field name never contains a dot, so `$id.<field>` has exactly one
# dot after the id, while `$REG/demo-quiet-mesa.x-y.uuid` — the id
# `demo-quiet-mesa.x-y`, project `demo-quiet-mesa.x`, slug `y`, legal because
# project DIRECTORY names may hold dots — has two. Without the skip that
# stranger would wedge slug `quiet-mesa` in project `demo` permanently.
#
# The direction of the residual leak changes with this and does not go away: a
# purge interrupted in its last window now costs a SLUG (144 per project,
# recycled by ws-reap) rather than costing the next workspace its archive and
# reap gates. Refusing to build is the safe direction, it is visible — `ws-add`
# names the files below — and `rm` on the named files reclaims it.
# THE PRIVATE COMPACTION FAMILIES (D-2605, spec 3.4's inventory), asked of a
# basename whose literal `.<id>.` prefix has ALREADY been stripped. These are
# dot-LEADING, so the `"$REG/$id".*` glob below — id, then dot — is
# structurally blind to every one of them, and a slug handed back while one is
# still present is a slug re-handed with a stranger's claim on it.
#
# THE PERMANENT LOCK IS THE SOLE EXCLUSION, and by name rather than by shape:
# it deliberately outlives the row, spanning generations and safe reuse, so it
# proves history and never a live regime. Gating reuse on it would wedge every
# ever-purged id forever.
_ws_private_family() {   # <suffix after the literal `.<id>.` strip> -> 0 iff it BLOCKS slug reuse
  case "$1" in
    compactions.lock) return 1 ;;
    compactset.*|compactcard.*|compactpost.*) return 0 ;;
    compactserved.*|compactserved-source.*) return 0 ;;
    compactions.lock-init.*|compactions.lock-open.*) return 0 ;;
    compactions-stage.*|compactions-snapshot.*) return 0 ;;
    generation-init.*|generation-read.*) return 0 ;;
    # ── THE TRANSITION ALLOWANCE, exactly the two legacy grammars ────────
    # `_hook_family_sweepable` (`ccd/session-hook.sh`) already carries these,
    # but that sweep runs only from a LIVE row's PreCompact. Without them here,
    # a row upgraded across the D-2605 deploy that carries legacy residue and is
    # then purged by `ws-rm`, `forget` or `ws-gc --prune` keeps
    # `$REG/.<id>.<pid>.<id>.compactset.tmp` (or the claim) FOR EVER: the purge's
    # private-family loop skips it, and no PreCompact will ever run for that id
    # again. Both begin with the pid, so no target-family arm above can match
    # them — every one of those requires the family word first.
    #
    # BLOCKING SLUG REUSE ON THEM IS CORRECT, not a side effect to be split
    # away. This predicate answers one question — "is this dot-leading name
    # private residue of a row that did not finish?" — and legacy residue is
    # residue: a slug handed back while it stands is handed back with a
    # stranger's claim on it, which is the whole property the dot-leading pass
    # exists to establish. The cost is the same bounded one the header above
    # already discloses (a slug, recycled by ws-reap, with `ws-add` naming the
    # file), and it is paid only inside the upgrade window.
    #
    # ANCHORED ON THE DECIMAL PID, not a bare `*`. MEASURED while writing this:
    # the bare `*.compactcard-claim.tmp` arm — which is what
    # `_hook_family_sweepable` still carries — makes `_reg_purge` of
    # `demo-quiet-basin` delete `.demo-quiet-basin.x-y.999.compactcard-claim.tmp`,
    # a DOTTED NESTED id's legacy residue, because after the literal `.<id>.`
    # strip that basename still ends in the grammar. That is the exact cross-id
    # hazard the `*.*` skip on the dot-free pass exists to prevent, reintroduced
    # through the transition allowance. A legacy name's first component is the
    # WRITER'S PID, and a nested id's first component is the rest of its id, so
    # requiring a pure decimal head separates them.
    #
    # The claim grammar is pinned EXACTLY — decimal head, then the grammar and
    # nothing else. The set grammar carries the id in the middle and cannot be,
    # so one contrived collision remains and is stated rather than hidden: an id
    # whose own trailing component is all digits (project `demo-quiet-basin.99`,
    # slug `9`) presents a decimal head after this id's strip. Legal, and its
    # cost is one reclaimed legacy tmp of a neighbour, inside the upgrade window.
    *.compactcard-claim.tmp)
      # The grammar itself carries a dot, so "exactly two components" is the
      # wrong exactness test here — measured, it rejected the real
      # `999.compactcard-claim.tmp`. What exactness means is that everything
      # after the head IS the grammar.
      [[ "${1#*.}" == "compactcard-claim.tmp" ]] || return 1
      [[ "${1%%.*}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
      return 0 ;;                            # `<pid>.compactcard-claim.tmp`
    *.compactset.tmp)
      [[ "${1%%.*}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
      return 0 ;;                            # `<pid>.<id>.compactset.tmp`
  esac
  return 1
}

_ws_slug_free() {   # project slug -> 0 when no registry file exists for the id
  local id="$1-$2" f suffix
  for f in "$REG/$id".*; do
    # Unmatched-glob case, exactly as in `_reg_purge`: no `nullglob` here, so a
    # glob with no matches stays its own literal text and `-e` is false.
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    suffix="${f#"$REG/$id."}"
    [[ "$suffix" == *.* ]] && continue
    return 1
  done
  # THE DOT-LEADING PRIVATE FAMILIES (D-2605). The literal `.<id>.` prefix is
  # what keeps a NESTED id out: `.demo-quiet.` and `.demo-quiet-mesa.` are
  # different strings, so this glob cannot reach across ids the way a substring
  # match would — the same hazard the dot-free check above was built to avoid.
  for f in "$REG/.$id".*; do
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    suffix="${f#"$REG/.$id."}"
    _ws_private_family "$suffix" || continue
    return 1
  done
  return 0
}

# EVERY REASON `_ws_slug_free` CAN REFUSE IS A REASON THIS CAN NAME. Widening
# only the first breaks `cmd_ws_add`'s shipped contract — its refusal NAMES
# WHAT IT FOUND, and an unwidened residue answers the empty string, so the
# operator is told a slug is in use and handed nothing to reclaim while
# `_ws_slug_new` skips that slug forever.
#
# It emits complete `$REG`-relative BASENAMES — dot-free fields as
# `<id>.<field>`, private families as `.<id>.<suffix>` — rather than bare
# suffixes, because the caller prints them as a list rooted once. The old
# `$REG/<id>.{...}` brace template could not express a dot-LEADING name at all,
# and measured, its `, ` separator already defeated the brace expansion it
# implied even for the dot-free case: `echo prefix.{uuid,workdir}` expands,
# `echo prefix.{uuid, workdir}` prints the braces literally.
_ws_slug_residue() {   # project slug -> the $REG-relative basenames the registry still holds
  local id="$1-$2" f suffix out=""
  for f in "$REG/$id".*; do
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    suffix="${f#"$REG/$id."}"
    [[ "$suffix" == *.* ]] && continue
    out+="${out:+, }$id.$suffix"
  done
  for f in "$REG/.$id".*; do
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    suffix="${f#"$REG/.$id."}"
    _ws_private_family "$suffix" || continue
    out+="${out:+, }.$id.$suffix"
  done
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

_ws_slug_new() {   # project -> echo a free, valid slug (or fail)
  local project="$1" slug i gs
  if [[ -n "${CCD_WS_SLUG:-}" ]]; then             # tests and manual naming
    _ws_slug_valid "$CCD_WS_SLUG" || return 1
    _ws_slug_free "$project" "$CCD_WS_SLUG" || return 1; gs=$(_ws_slug_git_state "$project" "$CCD_WS_SLUG") || { echo "ccd: $gs" >&2; return 1; }
    echo "$CCD_WS_SLUG"; return 0
  fi
  for ((i = 0; i < 60; i++)); do
    slug="${WS_ADJ[RANDOM % ${#WS_ADJ[@]}]}-${WS_NOUN[RANDOM % ${#WS_NOUN[@]}]}"
    _ws_slug_free "$project" "$slug" && { gs=$(_ws_slug_git_state "$project" "$slug"); case $? in 0) echo "$slug"; return 0 ;; 2) echo "ccd: $gs" >&2; return 1 ;; esac; }
  done
  return 1
}

_ws_least_loaded() {   # [project] [class] -> the home-able account with the most headroom, or "" if none is placeable
  # UNKNOWN IS NOT ZERO. This used to read `[[ -z "$sc" ]] && sc=0`, so an
  # account _limit_score knows nothing about — no ~/.cc-limits/<w>.json at all —
  # scored 0 and beat every account that had honestly reported its pressure.
  # The emptiest-looking account was simply the one nobody could see. Skip it
  # instead: unmeasured ranks below measured, never above.
  #
  # UNKNOWN IS NOT UNPLACEABLE either. On a fresh box nothing has reported yet,
  # and refusing to place work there would break first run. THE FALLBACK IS
  # THREE TIERS DEEP, and the account wave added the first of them: if NO
  # account was measured this falls back to the first eligible account THAT
  # COULD REPORT AT ALL (`_account_measured` — the ROSTER's answer, not a
  # file's); then to the first eligible account in iteration order whatever its
  # telemetry, so a roster whose every home-able lane opts out can still take
  # work; and only when the health probe has condemned every one of them, to
  # the first condemned one. The first two tiers are the server's
  # `scorable[0] ?? live[0]!` spelled in bash (projectHome,
  # server/src/limits.ts); the third is argued at the `_authdead` skip below
  # and has no server counterpart.
  #
  # The server's copy (projectHome, server/src/limits.ts) is stricter about
  # "unknown" in exactly ONE way this is not, and it is stated here rather than
  # discovered later:
  #
  #   1. CLOSED, by the account wave (D-2596 records that main's copy of this
  #      paragraph said the opposite, truly of its own parent). It skips an
  #      account whose roster entry
  #      says telemetry:'none' — an account that will never report, so its
  #      permanent unknown must not read as permanent emptiness. This function
  #      now asks the same question, through `_account_measured` above. The
  #      sentence that used to sit here said bash had nothing to consult
  #      because the generated file "carries ids, home-ability and the upstream
  #      id, and no telemetry field at all"; that has been false since stage
  #      2a — `shared/generate.mjs` emits CCRC_MEASURED and
  #      `statusline-command.sh` has been reading it. The FALLBACK moved with
  #      the filter, which is the half that made this a real divergence and not
  #      just a missing skip: before this change bash fell back to the first
  #      PLACEABLE account and projectHome to the first SCORABLE one, so on a
  #      roster whose first home-able lane opts out of telemetry, with nothing
  #      reported yet, the two shipped implementations named DIFFERENT accounts.
  #
  #      THE EQUIVALENCE HAS A HOLE, and it is on this side: `_account_measured`
  #      reads an UNSET `CCRC_MEASURED` as measured-everything, so on a box
  #      still holding an accounts.sh generated before stage 2a the measured
  #      tier collapses onto the tier below it and stops filtering. That is a
  #      state `projectHome` cannot reach at all, because `parseRoster` makes
  #      `telemetry` mandatory and the server has no "the roster did not say"
  #      case to collapse into.
  #
  # THERE USED TO BE A SECOND, AND BRIEFLY A THIRD; BOTH ARE NOW CLOSED, and
  # what closed them is one line — `_limit_score`'s `[[ -z "$five" || -z
  # "$seven" ]]`, which was `&&` with `: "${five:=0}"` filling the gap.
  #   • The old gap 2 was the HALF-MEASURED row: `{"five":null,"seven":0}`
  #     scored 0 in bash and unknown on the server.
  #   • The old gap 3 was the HALF-ROLLED row, created by the provenance fix
  #     itself: `{five: <window ended>, seven: 5}` scored 5 in bash and unknown
  #     on the server. Unlike gap 2 that one was REACHABLE by any Anthropic
  #     account at any 5h reset, and it was the placement magnet this file's
  #     first paragraph describes, surviving through the readable half — which
  #     is why it was closed in the same change that opened it rather than
  #     recorded and deferred.
  # Both were one defect wearing two faces: a score is a MAXIMUM, so one known
  # half bounds the truth only from below, and a bound from below is not a
  # score. `_limit_score` now says so, and `half-rolled-window` in
  # projected-home.test.ts's shared fixtures asserts the two sides agree on it.
  #
  # Gap 1 used to hold by luck, and the paragraph that said so is gone with the
  # luck: gpt was the only telemetry:'none' account, the only one whose file
  # carried a null half, and not home-able — absent from CCRC_HOME_ABLE here
  # and from roster.homeAble there — so neither implementation ever scored it.
  # The account wave's decision 22 ends that: `compatible` is a provider class
  # that is telemetry:'none' BY CONSTRUCTION and that an operator MAY declare
  # `homeAble: true`. So gap 1 is now a RULE on both sides rather than an
  # agreement of circumstance, and `projected-home.test.ts` runs both sides
  # over the same fixtures so it says so the day either changes its mind.
  # (`_limit_five`'s back-compat shim
  # survives as the standing evidence that a limits file CAN be missing a key;
  # `_limit_score`'s `:=0` defaults, which used to be cited beside it, are gone
  # — they were the mechanism of the gap, not a defence against it.)
  #
  # THE FIVE CANDIDATE WALKS DO NOT AGREE, AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS ON PURPOSE
  # (PR #61, 2026-09-07; the count is corrected at the wave-2b merge, which
  # added the fourth, and at routing slice 4, which added the fifth). Measured,
  # so the number stops drifting: `grep -cE 'for
  # (w|cand) in (\$\(_pool_for|"\$\{CCRC_HOME_ABLE\[@\]\}")' ccd/ccd` finds
  # 6 matching LINES, 5 of them walks — the sixth is prose, `_default_pool`'s
  # own comment quoting the pattern. The five are `_ws_least_loaded`,
  # `cmd_ws_add`'s refusal-reason builder, `_swap_target`, `_strand_why` and
  # `_place_for_class`. The fifth walks CCRC_HOME_ABLE for the same reason the
  # builder does — it is COUNTING how many in-pool lanes measured unable to
  # serve a class, which is a question about placement's own candidate set, and
  # an overflow lane is not in it.
  # BOTH NUMBERS ARE PINNED — `ccd-auto-swap-pool.test.ts` re-runs this exact
  # pattern and requires both to still be true, the way `_pool_ok`'s own header
  # count is pinned in `ccd-pool-ok.test.ts`. CORRECTED at the fix round after
  # the merge review, D-1956: the sentence written to stop the number drifting
  # said "finds four" while its own cited command answers five, so it could not
  # be re-measured — the same defect its sibling at the `_pool_ok` header had
  # already been corrected for, 2500 lines away, and it was a bare comment
  # claim until now. A comment is a request; a red suite is a mechanism. The builder is the one this block never
  # named: it walks CCRC_HOME_ABLE exactly as placement does, because it exists
  # to explain a PLACEMENT refusal, and it would be wrong for it to offer an
  # overflow lane as the missing answer.
  # PLACEMENT — this function — iterates CCRC_HOME_ABLE
  # and never scores an overflow lane, so the parity above still holds for it
  # exactly as written. ROTATION — `_swap_target` — walks `_pool_for`, which
  # since #61 includes every installed, non-kill-switched lane that is NOT
  # home-able, and it DOES score such a lane with `_limit_score`: gpt's
  # half-null file reads as 0 there. That reading cannot beat an Anthropic
  # account, because `_swap_target` keeps overflow lanes in a bracket of their
  # own and reaches it only when no home-able candidate passed `_pool_ok`,
  # `_account_ok` and `_avail` — the 0 orders overflow lanes among themselves
  # and nothing else. (`_pool_ok` joined that list in wave 2b and this sentence
  # did not follow it until the merge. It matters because it is the one of the
  # three that can empty the bracket with every account HEALTHY.) `_strand_why` (account pools, wave 2b) mirrors `_swap_target`'s walk,
  # so it names an overflow lane too. A reader who finds this function refusing
  # a lane the rotation accepts is looking at the design, not at a drift:
  # a workspace never STARTS on the Codex lane, but a session whose every
  # home-able account is at the ceiling may end up there until one recovers.
  #
  # THE RULE ACROSS ALL THREE UNKNOWN-HANDLING SITES, stated precisely because
  # the loose form of it ("unmeasured never wins") is contradicted two lines
  # below by this function's own fallback: PREFER MEASURED ACCOUNTS; IF NONE IS
  # MEASURED, FALL BACK TO THE FIRST HOME-ABLE ACCOUNT IN ROSTER ORDER THAT
  # COULD REPORT, THEN TO THE FIRST HOME-ABLE ACCOUNT AT ALL, AND ONLY THEN —
  # when the health probe has condemned every one of them — TO THE FIRST
  # CONDEMNED ONE. "Measured" at this site is `_account_measured`, the ROSTER's
  # answer. `_swap_target`'s rescue loop asks a DIFFERENT question — whether a
  # file exists at all (`_limit_score "$cand"` returning non-empty), never what
  # the roster says about telemetry — so a home-able `telemetry:'none'` lane
  # carrying a stale or hand-written limits file still competes normally there,
  # and a row that happens to read a real zero still wins the rescue. That is a
  # residual gap the account wave did not close, recorded here rather than
  # claimed on `_swap_target`'s behalf. Skipping here and ranking-last in
  # `_swap_target` are the two affordable spellings of the same preference —
  # this function can skip because it HAS that fallback, and the rescue loop
  # cannot because its "no candidate" answer strands a stuck session. `_avail`
  # stays permissive because it answers eligibility, not rank.
  # Full reasoning on `_avail`.
  #
  # CCRC_HOME_ABLE is in roster declaration order (shared/generate.mjs emits
  # roster.homeAble as-is), and that order is load-bearing FOUR times over:
  # each of the THREE fallback tiers below takes the first placeable account IN
  # ITS TIER, and the tie-break is a STRICT `<`, so the first measured
  # home-able account also wins every tie. The server's `projectHome` ranks
  # ties by the same declaration order.
  #
  # THE OPTIONAL POSITIONALS ARE THE PROJECT, THEN THE CLASS (account pools,
  # spec §5.5.2; the class positional is routing slice 3, spec §5.4), and the
  # zero-arg AND one-arg calls both keep today's meaning EXACTLY:
  # `_project_pool_state ""` answers `untagged` by its own first guard,
  # `_pool_ok` returns 0 for `untagged`, and every pre-existing caller — the
  # parity harness included — is byte-identical. The class positional
  # defaults to `""` (`local cls="${2:-}"` below); an absent class or an
  # explicit `default` are the SAME no-op — the serviceability filter later
  # in this function never runs — so every caller in this slice, which never
  # passes a class, walks the identical bytes it walked before this second
  # positional existed.
  #
  # THE STATE IS READ ONCE, before the loop, and the WORD is passed to each
  # candidate: a loop over five accounts costs one `cat`, not five.
  #
  # ONE PREDICATE, NO SECOND EXIT. An UNDECIDABLE tag needs no early return of
  # its own: `_pool_ok` answers 2 for every candidate, every candidate
  # `continue`s, `first` stays empty and this function answers "" — which is
  # the right answer (nobody may decide) reached by the same line that skips a
  # wrong-pool lane. Deleting that line therefore reds BOTH the skip cases and
  # the undecidable cases, which is why there is no second guard here to keep
  # in step with it.
  #
  # THE EMPTY ANSWER IS OVERLOADED and stays that way: "" is both "every
  # in-pool lane is at ceiling or disabled" and "the tag is undecidable". There
  # is no second channel on this seam — stdout carries a wrapper name — so the
  # CALLER re-reads the state and names which it was (`cmd_ws_add`, below).
  # WITH A CLASS it carries a THIRD meaning — "every in-pool lane measured
  # unservable for the class" — which `cmd_ws_add`'s reason builder cannot yet
  # name, because nothing on that path knows the class: slice 4's `ws-add
  # --route` is what hands it one, and the arm lands with it.
  #
  # WHAT "" NEVER MEANS IS "every in-pool lane is condemned", and that is why
  # `cmd_ws_add`'s reason builder needs no auth-dead arm: this function answers
  # a lane in that case (see the tiers at the end of the loop). Both meanings ""
  # does carry are things a human declared or must repair; a health verdict is a
  # measurement that can be wrong.
  local pps; pps=$(_project_pool_state "${1-}")
  local cls="${2:-}"
  local best="" bs=1000 first="" firstm="" condemned="" w sc
  for w in "${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[@]}"; do
    _account_ok "$w" || continue
    # AFTER `_account_ok`, BEFORE EITHER FALLBACK: both must land on an IN-POOL
    # account, never on a cheaper-looking lane in another pool.
    _pool_ok "$w" "$pps" || continue
    # POOL FIRST, THEN SERVICEABILITY (routing spec §5.4), as a FILTER on
    # eligibility like _pool_ok: a lane measured at the class's ceiling drops out
    # here, before the condemned tier and before scoring; an UNMEASURED lane stays
    # — unmeasured never becomes ineligible — and ranks by its own score. `default`
    # (or no class) is today's walk byte for byte. projectHome (server/src/limits.ts)
    # is the twin; projected-home.test.ts drives both over one fixture table.
    _class_gate "$w" "$cls"; [[ $? -eq 1 ]] && continue
    # THE CONDEMNED TIER, AND THE SPLIT IS THE WHOLE GUARD. An auth-dead lane
    # must not be PREFERRED — it is a measurement that a credential no longer
    # authenticates — but it must stay ELIGIBLE, because that measurement can be
    # wrong and no destination at all is worse than a bad one (`_authdead`'s
    # header: it never joins `_account_ok`). Those two are compatible only with
    # a fallback tier BELOW the ordinary ones, which is what this branch builds:
    # `first` collects the first candidate NOBODY CONDEMNED, `condemned` the
    # first condemned one, and the condemned tier is reached below only when
    # BOTH `firstm` and `first` are empty — i.e. only when every in-pool lane is
    # condemned. (`firstm` is the account wave's tier and sits ABOVE `first`;
    # the ordering of the three is at the fallback lines themselves.)
    #
    # BOTH ONE-VARIABLE SPELLINGS ARE WRONG, and this function shipped the first
    # of them (D-1954). Assigning a single `first` ABOVE this skip takes the
    # first candidate in roster order whether or not the probe has condemned it,
    # so on a fleet where nothing is measured — a fresh box, or every window
    # rolled over at once — placement lands on a lane measured DEAD while a
    # healthy lane sits one position behind it. Assigning it BELOW the skip is
    # the other wrong answer: it empties the fallback on an all-condemned fleet,
    # this function answers "", and `cmd_ws_add` dies with no destination for a
    # reason no operator asked for. The pair of variables is what satisfies both.
    if _authdead "$w"; then
      [[ -z "$condemned" ]] && condemned="$w"
      continue
    fi
    [[ -z "$first" ]] && first="$w"
    # AFTER `first`, BEFORE the score: a lane the roster says will never report
    # must not be SKIPPED out of the fallback — it is still placeable — but it
    # must not be preferred to one that could report either. Collecting `first`
    # above this line and `firstm` below it is what buys both, and it is the
    # bash spelling of projectHome's `scorable[0] ?? live[0]!`.
    _account_measured "$w" || continue
    [[ -z "$firstm" ]] && firstm="$w"
    sc=$(_limit_score "$w"); [[ -z "$sc" ]] && continue
    (( sc < bs )) && { bs=$sc; best="$w"; }
  done
  # THREE TIERS, WIDENING, and the widest is a lane rather than "". Prefer the
  # cheapest MEASURED lane nobody condemned; then the first lane that COULD
  # report; then the first eligible lane at all whatever its telemetry; then —
  # only when every in-pool lane is condemned — the first CONDEMNED one, in the
  # same roster declaration order every other answer here uses. The middle two
  # are `${firstm:-$first}`, which is projectHome's `scorable[0] ?? live[0]!`
  # (server/src/limits.ts) spelled in bash.
  #
  # THE LAST TIER IS A LANE, NOT "". That is the deliberate half of the decision
  # and it is not the same call as the all-disabled one: `""` above means
  # ELIGIBILITY IS EXHAUSTED — an operator switched every lane off, or a wrapper
  # is missing, or the pool tag is undecidable — and every one of those is
  # something a human did or must fix before work can land. A health verdict is
  # neither: it is a MEASUREMENT, it can be wrong, and letting it empty this
  # function would let one bad probe run wedge every `ws-add` on the box.
  # THE SERVICEABILITY FILTER ABOVE IS A NARROW, DELIBERATE EXCEPTION TO THAT
  # RULE FOR THREE OF THE FOUR CLASSES, and the paragraph that stood here said
  # the opposite of the code (final review finding 2). What holds for all four:
  # `_serviceable` returning 2 (unmeasured) does not `continue` — an unmeasured
  # lane stays eligible and ranks by its own score, exactly like an unmeasured
  # `_limit_score` — so no ABSENCE of a measurement can empty this function.
  #
  # `fable` IS the case the old paragraph described, and only it: its rc 1 is
  # the ROSTER's backend word or the sweep's share estimate against
  # `FABLE_SHARE_CEILING_PCT`, neither of which is the lane's health, and one
  # rung down reads a different figure against a different ceiling — so
  # `_class_below` really is the remedy and an emptied pool really does mean
  # "not on this rung".
  #
  # `opus`/`sonnet`/`haiku` DO NOT WORK THAT WAY. `_serviceable` reads
  # `_limit_field "$w" seven` against `SWAP_CEILING` for all three — the same
  # figure and the same ceiling `_avail` tests — so with one of those classes
  # this function CAN answer "" for a health reason, on the fleet where every
  # in-pool lane's week is measured at 98 or over. And `_class_below` cannot
  # relieve it: all three rungs ask the identical question of the identical
  # figure, so opus, sonnet and haiku answer unservable together. That is a
  # REFUSAL ON A HEALTH MEASUREMENT, which is exactly what the tiers below
  # exist to prevent — it is admitted here rather than repaired because it is
  # what "this session must run at class X" MEANS: a rung the fleet cannot
  # serve is not a destination, and placing anyway would put the session where
  # its own routing record says it cannot run. Its blast radius is bounded by
  # the caller: only a call that PASSES a class reaches it, `default` and the
  # no-class call skip the filter entirely, and no shipped caller passes a
  # class until slice 4's `ws-add --route` — which is where the operator-facing
  # refusal has to be spelled (S3-R3's named obligation on `cmd_ws_add`'s
  # reason builder). `projected-home.test.ts`'s `sonnet-over-SWAP_CEILING`
  # case is the pin.
  #
  # The ordinary health tiers below (condemned included) are computed exactly
  # as before and are untouched by this filter.
  # `_swap_target`'s rank-101 tier is the same ruling for the rescue lane.
  # The server's `projectHome` (server/src/limits.ts) makes both calls the same
  # way, and `projected-home.test.ts` drives the two over one seeded HOME so the
  # day either side changes its mind is the day the suite says so.
  [[ -z "$best" ]] && best="${firstm:-$first}"
  [[ -z "$best" ]] && best="$condemned"
  echo "$best"
}

# CCD_DISK_FLOOR_GB is the one environment override ccd honours, because unlike
# PROJECTS_ROOT/WORKTREES_ROOT it can only make ccd REFUSE to act — it can never
# aim a destructive command at a different tree.
CCD_DISK_FLOOR_GB="${CCD_DISK_FLOOR_GB:-10}"

_ws_disk_free_gb() {   # dir -> whole GiB available on the filesystem holding it
  local d="$1"
  # WORKTREES_ROOT is created lazily, so walk up to the nearest parent that
  # exists; df on a missing path just fails.
  while [[ ! -d "$d" && "$d" != / && -n "$d" ]]; do d=$(dirname "$d"); done
  # -P guarantees one filesystem per line, so a long device name cannot wrap the
  # row and shift field 4. -k pins the block size to 1K, which -P alone does not
  # (GNU df -P reports 512-byte blocks under POSIXLY_CORRECT).
  # NOT `df --output=avail`: GNU df rejects -P and --output together.
  local kb; kb=$(df -Pk "$d" 2>/dev/null | awk 'NR==2 {print $4}')
  [[ "$kb" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
  echo $(( kb / 1024 / 1024 ))
}

cmd_ws_add() {   # [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] project [slug] — new worktree + session for an existing project
  # PER-WORKER RC and the DECLARED ACTOR, both stripped before any positional
  # binds — `cmd_ws_hold`'s shape (ccd:3891) and its reason: a run like
  # `ws-add --no-rc <project> --surface agent --actor '…'` must not bind
  # `project` from a flag-polluted argv.
  #
  # D-410 IS WHY THIS LOOP EXISTS. `--no-rc` used to be consumed by an
  # exact-string match at position 1 and nothing else was parsed at all, so
  # the composed dispatch argv put `--surface` in the SLUG and the verb died
  # at `_ws_slug_valid` — before the worktree, the registry row and the pane.
  # Every dispatched spawn on a caps-advertising box would have refused, and
  # the `create` row of every spawn that did land read *declared: nothing*.
  # (`actor-flags-v1` never meant "this box takes the flags on every verb":
  # it governs the FIVE WORKSPACE VERBS, and `ws-add` MINTS a workspace
  # rather than acting on one — which is why the parse had to be written
  # here rather than inherited.)
  #
  # PER-WORKER RC (task #37, ruling 2026-08-13): the dispatch path declares a
  # spawn a dispatched worker AT CREATION — the only moment the fact is known
  # (hold is written later and released mid-program). Exact-string match, no
  # value parsing: nothing user-controlled reaches that flag. Folding it into
  # the loop is what makes it POSITIONLESS like the other two; the four
  # pre-existing call forms are unchanged, because `_ws_slug_valid` requires a
  # leading `[a-z0-9]` and no legitimate slug can begin with `-`.
  #
  # TWO DEC FLAGS, NOT THREE. `--reason` is deliberately absent: this verb has
  # no reason of its own to collide with, and the dispatch path's reason rides
  # `--actor` (`run:<id> dispatch`). Same ruling as `cmd_ws_hold`'s at
  # ccd:4801-4807, for the same reason — never put a second string on a verb
  # with nothing to say which one a reader is looking at.
  local norc=0 lc_child='' lc_gc=0 lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_args=() lc_route=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      --no-rc)     norc=1; shift ;;
      # An explicit arity check, never a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv
      # fails, shifts nothing, and a loop that shifts before it reads never
      # terminates. MEASURED, and it revises that sentence for THESE arms:
      # delete the check and `--surface` as the last token does not hang —
      # `lc_surface="$2"` trips `set -u` FIRST and the shell aborts with
      # `ccd: line <n>: $2: unbound variable`, rc 1 (1 failed / 28 passed).
      # So what the check buys HERE is the honest usage line; the
      # never-terminating loop is what it buys the day an arm is reordered
      # to shift before it reads its value.
      --surface)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-add [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--route <field>=<value>]... <project> [slug]"
                   lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-add [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--route <field>=<value>]... <project> [slug]"
                   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      # REPEATABLE, one field per flag — the shape `cmd_route --set` already
      # has, so a dispatcher composing one argv for both verbs writes the same
      # pairs. Validated below, before anything on disk is touched.
      --route)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-add [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--route <field>=<value>]... <project> [slug]"
                   lc_route+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
      --route=*)   lc_route+=("${1#--route=}"); shift ;;
      # The minting run (spec §5.1) — what makes this workspace a CHILD; see `_child_runid_valid`.
      --child)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-add [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--route <field>=<value>]... <project> [slug]"
                   lc_gc=1; lc_child="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --child=*)   lc_gc=1; lc_child="${1#--child=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2, ccd:11089-11090): an empty array here
  # is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop
  # is where that distinction has to be made. `lc_gs` carries givenness and the
  # `${lc_w:-unknown}` fallback turns a declared-but-blank surface into
  # `unknown` rather than an empty string the encoder would backfill to `none`.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (ccd:2411-2422, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''`
  # returns 0, so it cannot be the blank guard on its own. A declared `--actor`
  # is refused when blank the same way `cmd_ws_hold` refuses one, and here the
  # refusal still lands where nothing has been created yet.
  if (( lc_ga )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--actor must be non-blank"
    # THE SAME REFUSAL `cmd_route` MAKES, for the same reason (controller ruling
    # S1-R4, extended to this verb by S4-R2). This verb's `--actor` reaches
    # `$REG/swap.log` VERBATIM through `_route_argv_write`'s `[actor=$actor]`,
    # and that file is writable by every session on the box, so a newline in it
    # writes a second, forged `route <id>: …` audit line — the exact shape
    # `cmd_route` refuses. The journal arm needs no guard (`_lc_json` encodes);
    # the log line is the hole. Refused HERE, above `_route_argv_check` and
    # above every mint, so a forged actor leaves the box as it found it.
    case "$lc_actor" in
      *[[:cntrl:]]*) die "ws-add: --actor must not contain control characters" ;;
    esac
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" || die "--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  # ABOVE THE PROJECT POSITIONAL, AND THAT IS THE POINT — earlier than the disk
  # floor, the placement read, the lock, the worktree and the row. A bad
  # `--route` value is an argv a coordinator composed; this verb refuses it with
  # the box exactly as it found it (spec §5.3).
  (( ${#lc_route[@]} )) && _route_argv_check "${lc_route[@]}"
  (( lc_gc )) && ! _child_runid_valid "$lc_child" && die "--child needs a run id (a positive integer), got: $lc_child"
  # BOUND AFTER THE LOOP, NEVER BEFORE IT — this line sat ABOVE the strip in the
  # first cut of this task and `ws-add --no-rc <project>` bound `project` to
  # `--no-rc`, reproducing D-410 one flag to the left. `ccd-spawn-split.test.ts`
  # caught it, unedited, which is what that gate is for.
  local project="${1:?usage: ccd ws-add [--no-rc] [--child <runId>] [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--route <field>=<value>]... <project> [slug]}" slug="${2:-}"
  _ws_project_valid "$project" \
    || die "invalid project '$project' — allowed chars: A-Za-z0-9 . _ -, and no LEADING dot (a dot-leading id aliases ccd's own hidden registry files — see _ws_project_valid)"
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  [[ -d "$main/.git" ]] || die "not a git repo: $main"

  # Before anything is created: no worktree, no branch, no registry entry, no
  # session. A refusal here must leave the box exactly as it found it.
  local free
  free=$(_ws_disk_free_gb "$WORKTREES_ROOT") \
    || die "could not read free space for $WORKTREES_ROOT"
  (( free >= CCD_DISK_FLOOR_GB )) \
    || die "only ${free}G free on $WORKTREES_ROOT, floor is ${CCD_DISK_FLOOR_GB}G — reclaim space first: ccd ws-gc (archived workspaces are listed there and removed only on confirmation; pinned commits: ccd ws-attic --session <id>)"

  # Same reasoning as the disk floor above: _ws_least_loaded is a pure read
  # (no _account_ok candidate means no side effect), so picking the account
  # here — before the worktree/branch/registry exist — is what lets an
  # all-excluded refusal leave the box exactly as it found it. Pressure alone
  # never refuses (the all-pinned fixture rule stands); only every lane
  # failing _account_ok gets here.
  #
  # PLACEMENT NOW KNOWS THE CLASS (routing spec §5.4). Two callers, told apart by
  # `strict`: an explicit `--route class=` is a session that MUST run at that
  # class, so it never falls back; the coordinator row's class is a default
  # nobody typed, so it does. A DISPATCHED worker (`--no-rc`) that named no class
  # gets neither — it keeps today's class-blind placement exactly.
  #
  # THE CLASS IS DERIVED FROM `ROUTE_COORDINATOR_ROW`, never respelled: the row
  # is the one place spec §3's default is written down, and a second literal
  # class word here is a second place the 2026-09-22 move would have had to find.
  local cls strict=1
  cls=$(_route_argv_class "${lc_route[@]}")
  if [[ -z "$cls" ]] && (( ! norc )); then
    cls="${ROUTE_COORDINATOR_ROW%% *}"; cls="${cls#class=}"; strict=0
  fi
  local hw; hw=$(_place_for_class "$project" "$cls" "$strict")
  if [[ -z "$hw" ]]; then
    # TWO EMPTIES, TOLD APART HERE (spec §6). `_ws_least_loaded` answers "" for
    # "every in-pool lane is unusable" AND for "the tag is undecidable", and
    # the two have completely different remedies — enable a lane versus fix one
    # file's bytes. Re-reading the state costs one `cat` on a path that is
    # already refusing.
    #
    # THIS IS A SECOND READER OF ONE FACT, FREE TO DISAGREE with the first
    # (`_ws_least_loaded`'s own `_project_pool_state` call, above) — the exact
    # shape `cmd_project_pool --clear` deliberately avoids by reading once and
    # deciding from `$oldstate`. Here the race is ACCEPTED rather than closed:
    # if the tag changes between the two reads, this message can name a state
    # that did not cause the empty (a chmod landing between them turns "every
    # in-pool lane is at ceiling" into `tag:unreadable`), and in the reverse
    # race the account loop can fall through with every arm passing and print
    # an EMPTY reason list. Both are harmless because this path is already
    # refusing either way — the second read only picks the WORDING of a
    # refusal that has already happened, never whether one happens.
    local w why="" pps ppn="" prc
    pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
    case "$pps" in "named "*) ppn=${pps#named } ;; esac
    if [[ "$pps" != untagged && -z "$ppn" ]]; then
      why=" tag:$pps $POOLS_DIR/$project"
    else
      for w in "${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[@]}"; do
        # FIRST FAILING PREDICATE IN LOOP ORDER — `_ws_least_loaded`'s own
        # order, so the reason names the check that actually stopped this
        # account rather than the last one that would have.
        if   [[ ! -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/$w" ]]; then why+=" $w:missing"
        elif ! _lane_enabled "$w";       then why+=" $w:disabled"
        # BOTH NON-SERVE CODES REACH THIS ARM NOW, AND IT TELLS THEM APART
        # (fix round 1, I1/I2). What stood here read: "`pps` is `untagged` or
        # `named …` in this branch, so [the rule] can only answer 0 or 1 here,
        # and an account reported with `pool=` is always a TAGGED one — the
        # empty answer never reaches this string." BOTH HALVES ARE FALSE since
        # the account side started reading `_acct_pool_state`, and both were
        # measured false, not reasoned about: rc 2 arrives here with a
        # perfectly readable project tag whenever the projection is absent
        # (a node that never synced), past its lease, or off the grammar; and
        # the name this arm printed came from `_acct_pool`, the DECLARED
        # roster, while the PROJECTION is what decided — a skew printed
        # `claude:pool=pool-a` as the reason `claude` cannot serve a `pool-a`
        # project. The rc test (rather than `!`) is what buys the split, the
        # same idiom and the same reason as the `_class_gate` arm below.
        #
        # THE UNDECIDABLE TOKEN NAMES THE DOCUMENT, NOT THE LANE. All three of
        # the account side's undecidable words are properties of the one shared
        # `pool-epoch` file, so `projection=<word>` is the SAME fact for every
        # lane in this loop and its remedy is that file, never this lane —
        # unlike `pool=`, which really is per-lane. It is still emitted
        # per-lane here, deliberately: this loop's contract is one token per
        # account naming its own first failing predicate, and hoisting a
        # single token out of it would be a second shape for callers to parse.
        # `_strand_why`, whose contract is the opposite, hoists.
        #
        # THE QUOTING HERE IS NO LONGER UNREACHABLE-EQUIVALENT — corrected in
        # the same round, because the paragraph that stood here told a future
        # author not to expect a mutation kill. Unquoting `$pps` word-splits
        # `named pool-b` so that `$2` is the bare word `named`, which is
        # neither `untagged` nor `named `* and so takes the PROJECT arm's
        # `*)` — rc 2, and now a visibly different token
        # (`projection=named`) rather than the same mismatch string the two
        # codes used to share. Measured, not argued: unquoting both arguments
        # at this one call site is 3 failed / 44 passed in
        # `ccd-pool-ok.test.ts`, the three being "names the pool, each
        # accounts own first failing predicate, and the remedies" and the two
        # projection cases beside it.
        elif _pool_ok "$w" "$pps"; prc=$?; [[ "$prc" -ne 0 ]]; then
          if [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]]; then why+=" $w:projection=$POOL_OK_STATE"
          else                        why+=" $w:pool=${POOL_OK_STATE#named }"
          fi
        # RC 1 ONLY, and the `$?` test rather than `!` is what makes that true.
        # `_class_gate` answers 1 for a lane MEASURED unable to serve the class
        # and 2 for one nobody measured — and an unmeasured lane is PLACEABLE,
        # so `! _class_gate` would name it as the reason placement failed while
        # `_ws_least_loaded` was perfectly willing to place there. That is a
        # fabricated fact in a refusal message, which is exactly what spec §5.4
        # forbids. With no class this arm cannot fire at all: `_class_gate`
        # returns 0 for an empty class.
        #
        # THE `$?` TEST IS UNREACHABLE-EQUIVALENT TODAY, NOT UNTESTED — the same
        # standing as the quoting on the `_pool_ok` arm above, and measured the
        # same way rather than assumed. Rewriting it as `! _class_gate …` leaves
        # `ccd-place-class.test.ts` 10/10 GREEN, and DELETING the arm reds its
        # case (e), so the arm is exercised and it is the POLARITY that nothing
        # can red. The reason is this loop's own `elif` order: a lane reaching
        # here has already passed `_account_ok` and `_pool_ok`, and
        # `_ws_least_loaded` applies the class filter after exactly those two —
        # so a lane answering rc 2 here would have been PLACED and this builder
        # would never have run. Keep the rc test anyway: it stops being
        # equivalent the day either walk reorders its predicates, and the
        # obligation it discharges (D-2854) is about the message, not about
        # today's reachability.
        elif _class_gate "$w" "$cls"; [[ $? -eq 1 ]]; then why+=" $w:class=$cls-unservable"
        fi
      done
    fi
    # THE POOL REMEDY NAMES BOTH CARRIERS (fix round 1, I2). It used to name
    # only the DECLARED one — "tag an account into pool <n> in
    # ~/.ccrc/accounts.json and redeploy the agent lane" — which is where an
    # operator edits the fact but is no longer what DECIDES it: the account
    # side of the rule reads the projection, so an account already tagged in
    # `accounts.json` keeps refusing until the projection carries the same
    # edge, and this sentence sent the operator to tag an account that is
    # already tagged. The declared step is kept (it is still the edit that
    # starts the chain) with the step that ends it said after it.
    die "no account available for placement${ppn:+ in pool $ppn} —${why}; enable one (rm $REG/<w>-disabled)${ppn:+, tag an account into pool $ppn in ~/.ccrc/accounts.json and redeploy the agent lane, then let the projection ($REG/pool-epoch) carry the same edge — that is what decides — or untag the project (ccd project-pool --project $project --clear)} and retry — nothing was touched"
  fi

  # PER PROJECT, and it spans slug selection through the last registry write —
  # `_reg_set "$id" branch "$branch"` and `_ws_seed_home "$id" "$hw"`, the two
  # fields a racing second ws-add would most visibly corrupt. Two concurrent
  # adds are not hypothetical: an operator double-tap, a second tab, a second
  # device and the coordinator's own HTTP call all reach here, and the PWA's
  # only guard is React state that does not survive a reload.
  #
  # `-n`, matching ccd's two existing flock sites: a loser REFUSES rather than
  # queueing behind a worktree checkout. It deliberately does NOT cover
  # `$main/.ccrc/workspace.sh` (an arbitrary user script with no bound) nor the
  # settle — so it does NOT close retry-after-502; §1.5's dispatch adoption does.
  command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "flock (util-linux) is unavailable — refusing to create a workspace unserialised"
  local addlock="$REG/.ws-add-$project.lock" lfd
  exec {lfd}>>"$addlock" || die "cannot open the ws-add lock at $addlock"
  flock -n "$lfd" || {
    exec {lfd}>&-
    die "busy: another ws-add for $project is in flight"
  }

  # The refusal NAMES WHAT IT FOUND. `_ws_slug_free` is an any-field check
  # (round-3 P1), so it also refuses a slug whose only trace is the residue of
  # an interrupted purge — and "slug in use" on its own would send the operator
  # looking for a workspace that is not there. The list is the reclaim
  # instruction: those are the files, in $REG, and nothing else holds the slug.
  # A PLAIN LIST ROOTED ONCE (D-2605), not the old `$REG/<id>.{...}` brace
  # template: since `_ws_slug_free` now also refuses on the dot-LEADING private
  # compaction families, the template's dot-AFTER-the-id shape could name no
  # such file, and every path it printed for one would not exist.
  [[ -n "$slug" ]] && { _ws_slug_valid "$slug" || die "invalid slug '$slug'"; local gs; \
                        _ws_slug_free "$project" "$slug" || die "slug in use: $slug — in $REG: $(_ws_slug_residue "$project" "$slug")"; \
                          gs=$(_ws_slug_git_state "$project" "$slug") || { [[ $? -eq 1 ]] && die "slug in use: $slug — ${gs#taken }"; die "cannot judge slug $slug for $project — $gs"; }; }
  [[ -z "$slug" ]] && { slug=$(CCD_WS_SLUG="${CCD_WS_SLUG:-}" _ws_slug_new "$project") \
                        || die "could not find a free slug for $project"; }

  git -C "$main" fetch origin --quiet 2>/dev/null || echo "warn: fetch failed; basing on the last known origin" >&2
  local base
  base=$(git -C "$main" symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD) \
    || die "no origin/HEAD — run: git -C '$main' remote set-head origin -a"
  base="${base#refs/remotes/}"

  local wt="$WORKTREES_ROOT/$project/$slug"
  [[ "$wt" != *\'* ]] || die "path must not contain a single quote: $wt"
  mkdir -p "$WORKTREES_ROOT/$project"
  # ws/ namespaces the branch: it sorts with its siblings, matches the type/slug
  # shape every repo here already uses, and says at a glance that the branch is
  # machine-created. The directory and the session id keep the BARE slug — a
  # slash in either would break tmux -t (session:window.pane) and systemd
  # instance names. --no-track is load-bearing: $base is a remote-tracking ref,
  # so git's branch.autoSetupMerge default would otherwise make origin/main this
  # branch's upstream, and `git pull` in the workspace would merge main into it.
  local branch="ws/$slug"
  git -C "$main" worktree add -b "$branch" --no-track "$wt" "$base" --quiet \
    || die "git worktree add failed for $wt"

  # Shared across every worktree of this repo, local only, never committed.
  # Asked of git rather than resolved with `cd`, for the reason spelled out at
  # _ws_common_dir below: --git-common-dir answers with a bare `.git` here (this
  # IS the repo's own checkout), and a captured `cd` can prepend its own print to
  # the answer. Measured what that costs, since $common is a path this WRITES
  # through: the answer arrives two lines long, so `mkdir -p "$common/info"`
  # creates a junk directory whose name ends in a newline, the `.ccrc/` line is
  # appended inside that, and $main's real info/exclude never gets it — leaving
  # .ccrc/ un-ignored in every worktree of the repo.
  local common; common=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir)
  mkdir -p "$common/info"
  grep -qxF '.ccrc/' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null || echo '.ccrc/' >> "$common/info/exclude"
  # Graph store + the sweep's ephemeral corpus filter (spec §D'/§E): excluded so
  # `git worktree remove` collects the store WITH the tree (un-ignored, it wedges
  # ws-rm/ws-reap/ws-gc — none pass --force), and so a SIGKILL-orphaned
  # .graphifyignore can never make a workspace unremovable.
  grep -qxF 'graphify-out/' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null || echo 'graphify-out/' >> "$common/info/exclude"
  grep -qxF '.graphifyignore' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null || echo '.graphifyignore' >> "$common/info/exclude"
  # Regenerable build output a project's own tooling writes straight into the
  # workspace (`npm install`, `cdk synth`) is W-pattern by location — it dies
  # with the workspace for free — but only once git agrees it is nobody's
  # uncommitted work. A project's committed .gitignore is not that guarantee:
  # it is VERSIONED, so a workspace branched before a pattern was added never
  # sees it, and measured live on this fleet, a `node_modules/` line with a
  # trailing slash does not match a SYMLINK named node_modules even when it
  # points at a directory (git's own documented rule for a directory-only
  # pattern) — exactly the shape used to skip a fresh `npm install` per
  # workspace. Measured 2026-09-20, 10 of this box's 136 live workspaces sat
  # permanently `dirty-tree`-refused by `ws-rm` on precisely that, every one
  # of them reporting `?? tools/data-room/node_modules`.
  #
  # Both properties of the two lines below are load-bearing, and the measured
  # shape is why. NO TRAILING SLASH, so a symlink matches at all — that is the
  # whole defect. UNANCHORED (a bare name, no other slash), so the match does
  # not depend on depth: not one of those ten carried the artifact at the
  # workspace root, and every `cdk.out` on this box stands at `cdk.out`,
  # `infra/cdk.out` or `infrastructure/cdk.out`. A leading-slash or
  # root-relative spelling would have caught none of them.
  grep -qxF 'node_modules' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null || echo 'node_modules' >> "$common/info/exclude"
  grep -qxF 'cdk.out' "$common/info/exclude" 2>/dev/null || echo 'cdk.out' >> "$common/info/exclude"

  local id="$project-$slug"
  # ── D-2605: ROW GENERATION IS MINTED HERE, BEFORE ANY ROW FIELD ─────────
  # The acquire comes BEFORE the first `_reg_set`, and a MISS FAILS CLOSED:
  # this verb dies with a retryable message naming the lock and leaves NOTHING
  # — no `.uuid`, no `.generation`, no partial row — so nothing irreversible
  # has happened and re-running is the whole remedy. That is the opposite
  # disposition from `_spawn_start`'s contended miss, and deliberately: there,
  # failing closed would wedge a rescue swap on a compaction lock; here, there
  # is nothing to wedge and a half-written row is the worse outcome.
  #
  # The lock is CLOSED before the setup hook and before `_spawn_start`, which
  # takes its own — it guards the residue check and the generation mint, and
  # nothing else.
  local gfd=""
  # THE REMEDY CLAUSE IS CONDITIONAL because the remedy is. Ordinary contention
  # clears by itself and `retry` is the whole answer; a SET COMPACT_LOCK_WHY is
  # one of three out-of-contract states of `$REG` — the permanent lock unlinked
  # under a live holder, a non-regular object occupying the lock pathname, or a
  # `$REG` that would not take the mint source — and a retry can succeed in
  # none of them, so saying only "retry" is the same fabricated remedy the
  # purge callers' messages carried (D-2782).
  #
  # IT NAMES THE TOKEN AND STOPS (r5 R4-M3). This is transient stderr, not a
  # durable journal line, so it carries no per-cause remedy the way the four
  # `_reg_purge` callers now do. What it must not do is what it used to: spell
  # out the canonical-vanished cause as though the token could only ever be
  # that one, which stopped being true the moment the acquire gained two more.
  _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT" \
    || die "could not take $REG/.$id.compactions.lock within ${COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT}s — nothing was created; retry${COMPACT_LOCK_WHY:+ — EXCEPT that a retry cannot help here: the acquire measured $COMPACT_LOCK_WHY, a state of $REG that no wait changes. Inspect $REG/.$id.compactions.lock and $REG by hand: ccd will not mint a second lock inode over a live holder, will not write through an object occupying that pathname, and cannot mint at all into a $REG that refuses the write}"
  gfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
  if ! _reg_generation_init "$id"; then
    _compact_lock_release "$gfd"
    die "$REG/$id.generation is present and invalid — refusing to build a workspace on it; inspect it by hand"
  fi
  _compact_lock_release "$gfd"; gfd=""
  local uuid; uuid=$(_plat_uuid)
  # ── THE PICK IS RE-TAKEN HERE, AND NOT MERELY RE-VALIDATED ────────────────
  # `$hw` was chosen in the pre-flight, above — deliberately, so that an
  # all-excluded refusal leaves the box exactly as it found it — and between
  # there and here this verb has run a `git fetch` and a `git worktree add`.
  # That is the widest window any account-valued write in this file has, and
  # unlike `start`, `swap` and `prefer` this placement is AUTOMATIC: nobody
  # named this account, so a fresher measurement is simply the better answer and
  # a refusal would be gratuitous. The pick runs in a subshell over the CURRENT
  # projection — `_ws_least_loaded` is a pure read and reads `CCRC_HOME_ABLE`,
  # so sourcing the file beside it is the whole of the update.
  # THE INTERSECTION, NOT THE FRESH LIST. The re-pick exists to avoid an account
  # that LEFT the roster; it must not adopt one that JOINED it. `_ccrc_cfg_dir`,
  # `_ccrc_label` and `_ccrc_hue` in this process are still the startup
  # snapshot's, and the generated `_ccrc_cfg_dir` has no default arm — an id it
  # has never heard of answers EMPTY at exit 0 — so a lane added AND enabled
  # during the `git fetch` window would be picked, exec'd fine, and then have no
  # config directory for the rest of the run: `_sync_uuid` returns early and the
  # session's uuid is never mirrored. So the snapshot is captured BEFORE the
  # source and the fresh home-able list is filtered through it; this pick may
  # drop a lane, never add one.
  #
  # THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO — `_wrapper_rostered_now`'s rule at the one call site
  # that cannot leave the box as it found it. An unreadable projection, an array
  # it no longer declares and a roster with no lane left are three conditions
  # with two different remedies, and the first cut of this said "the roster
  # changed" for all of them while pointing at `rm $REG/<w>-disabled`.
  local fresh fresh_rc=0
  fresh=$(
    snap=" ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]} "
    unset -v CCRC_ACCOUNTS CCRC_HOME_ABLE
    { . "$CCRC_ACCOUNTS_SH" >/dev/null 2>&1; } || exit 9
    declare -p CCRC_HOME_ABLE 2>/dev/null | grep -q '^declare -a ' || exit 9
    keep=()
    for v in ${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[@]+"${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[@]}"}; do
      [[ "$snap" == *" $v "* ]] && keep+=("$v")
    done
    CCRC_HOME_ABLE=(${keep[@]+"${keep[@]}"})
    # THE PROJECT RIDES ALONG, and it did not until this merge (D-2592) — the
    # defect
    # that costs the most to rediscover, because neither side of the merge was
    # wrong on its own. The account wave added this re-pick against a
    # `_ws_least_loaded` that took NO arguments, so a bare call was the only
    # call there was. Account pools then gave that function an optional
    # `[project]` and made the POOL FILTER depend on it: with no argument
    # `_project_pool_state ""` answers `untagged`, `_pool_ok` admits every
    # candidate, and this re-pick silently overwrites the pool-aware pick eight
    # hundred lines up (`hw=$(_ws_least_loaded "$project")`) with the CHEAPEST
    # lane on the box — out of pool, and with no crossing recorded, which is
    # exactly what `cmd_swap`'s guard exists to refuse. Git merged both changes
    # with no conflict; `ccd-pool-ok.test.ts`'s "still places into a tagged
    # project when the pool has an account" is what said so.
    # THE CLASS RIDES ALONG TOO (routing slice 4), for D-2592's exact reason one
    # argument further out: the pre-flight above placed by class, and a re-pick
    # that dropped it would silently overwrite that answer with a class-blind
    # one — on the very fleet where the class is what made the pre-flight
    # choose. TWO LINES, because the rung taken is a SECOND fact and a
    # subshell's variable is lost: the lane, then `PLACE_DEGRADED_TO`. The
    # parent splits them below.
    _place_for_class "$project" "$cls" "$strict" || true
    printf '\n%s' "$PLACE_DEGRADED_TO"
  ) || fresh_rc=$?
  # `$( )` strips trailing newlines, so a re-pick that took no rung leaves ONE
  # line and this is a no-op; one that did leaves two and both are read.
  local fresh_deg=""
  [[ "$fresh" == *$'\n'* ]] && { fresh_deg="${fresh#*$'\n'}"; fresh="${fresh%%$'\n'*}"; }
  (( fresh_rc != 9 )) \
    || die "$CCRC_ACCOUNTS_SH could not be re-read as an account projection, so this workspace cannot be placed on an account this box can still prove exists — regenerate it: ccrc install. The worktree $wt and the branch $branch EXIST and no registry row names them; adopt them once it is fixed with: ccd start <wrapper> $project $wt"
  # NAMES WHAT IS ALREADY ON DISK. Unlike the pre-flight refusal, this one
  # cannot leave the box as it found it: the worktree and the branch exist.
  [[ -n "$fresh" ]] \
    || die "no account is available for placement any more${cls:+ for class $cls} — every lane this box knew at startup is now disabled, missing or gone from the roster${cls:+, or none of them still measures able to serve $cls and nor does the rung below it}. The worktree $wt and the branch $branch EXIST and no registry row names them: enable an account (rm $REG/<w>-disabled)${cls:+ or re-run without --route class=$cls}, then adopt them with: ccd start <wrapper> $project $wt"
  hw="$fresh"
  _reg_set "$id" wrapper "$hw";   _reg_set "$id" project "$project"
  _reg_set "$id" workdir "$wt";   _reg_set "$id" uuid "$uuid"
  _reg_set "$id" workspace "$slug"; _reg_set "$id" base "$base"
  _reg_set "$id" branch "$branch"
  _ws_seed_home "$id" "$hw"
  # PER-WORKER RC: stamped before the first spawn so _spawn_start's consult
  # already sees it; survives swap and every Restart=always respawn; dies at
  # reap via _reg_purge's shape match. Absent = follow the box (D-99's
  # fail-safe direction, per session).
  (( norc )) && _reg_set "$id" rc off
  (( lc_gc )) && { _reg_set "$id" child "$lc_child" || echo "ccd: warn: could not write $REG/$id.child — $id is NOT a child of run $lc_child and nothing will ever reclaim it as one" >&2; }
  # ── THE ROUTING RECORD (routing spec §5.3 for the argv, §4 for the default) ──
  # Here rather than at the pre-flight because every one of these is a WRITE
  # against `$id`, and `$id` does not exist until the lines above. The argv wins
  # over the default outright: a dispatcher that named fields has said what this
  # worker is for, and the coordinator row is what an OPERATOR spawn gets when
  # nobody said anything. A dispatched worker (`--no-rc`) that named nothing gets
  # NEITHER — its spawn line stays byte-identical to a pre-slice-1 one.
  #
  # THE ACTOR IS THE DECLARED ONE WHEN THERE IS ONE. The dispatch path already
  # passes `--actor 'run:<id> dispatch'`, which is a truer answer than the verb's
  # own name; `ws-add` is the fallback for a call that declared nothing.
  if (( ${#lc_route[@]} )); then
    _route_argv_write "$id" "${lc_actor:-ws-add}" argv "${lc_route[@]}"
  elif (( ! norc )); then
    _route_seed_default "$id" "$hw"
  fi
  # AFTER the record, never before it: `degraded` is a statement about a record
  # that exists, and `_route_degrade` reads the standing stamp to decide whether
  # it has anything to say. The rung is the one the RE-PICK took — the pick that
  # actually placed this workspace.
  [[ -n "$fresh_deg" ]] && _route_degrade "$id" "$cls" "$fresh_deg" "unservable: $cls on every in-pool lane at placement"

  # GIVEN BACK HERE, before the setup hook and long before the settle, for the
  # reason cmd_ws_reap states at its own close: `flock` treats two `open()`s of
  # one path in one process as two strangers, and ccd is SOURCED — by `ccd`
  # itself and by its tests — so a descriptor left open would refuse the next
  # ws-add for this project in the same shell for ever. The `die`s above need no
  # close of their own: `die` exits, and the kernel closes what exits. Anything
  # BELOW this line that leaves by `return` (the spawn's rc 3/4/5 path does)
  # needs none either, because the descriptor is already gone by then.
  exec {lfd}>&-

  # A failed setup hook leaves a usable-but-unscaffolded workspace. Say so;
  # never remove work on the strength of a hook's exit code.
  if [[ -x "$main/.ccrc/workspace.sh" ]]; then
    if MAIN="$main" WT="$wt" "$main/.ccrc/workspace.sh"; then
      _reg_set "$id" setup ok
    else
      _reg_set "$id" setup failed
      echo "warn: .ccrc/workspace.sh failed for $id — workspace kept, not scaffolded" >&2
    fi
  fi

  # THE ORDERING IS THE FIX (F8). The pane is created, THEN the claim, THEN the
  # supervision, and only THEN the blocking wait. Killed anywhere after the
  # first line — the agent's 300s ws-add ceiling, a deploy sweep, a SIGTERM —
  # what is left is an ordinary session: claimed, supervised, restartable. What
  # F8 left was a live pane no registry row claimed and no unit was watching,
  # invisible to `ccd ls`, to the PWA and to ws-gc alike.
  #
  # This is also what makes #50's LOUD non-zero exit on rc 3/4/5 safe. It was
  # not safe before: a non-zero ws-add used to mean "an orphan was created". It
  # now means "the session exists, is claimed, is supervised, and did not
  # confirm its TUI".
  #
  # THE CALL SHAPE IS NOT NEGOTIABLE — plain call, `|| return $?`, then read
  # `$SPAWN_FROMSWAP`. No `$( )`: see the block above `_spawn_start` for the
  # commit where a command substitution there turned its `die` into rc 1, a
  # code this very `if` does not test, and this function printed its success
  # line over a spawn that never happened.
  #
  # Supervision wiring is untouched otherwise — `_ws_supervise` still runs on a
  # failed spawn, since a unit that can retry is strictly better than one that
  # never gets enabled. The rc is captured, not discarded: a silent M6-shaped
  # success on the workspace path was found by Task 4's reviewer (cmd_ws_add
  # rc=0 with the success line printed over a spawn whose tmux session had
  # vanished).
  local rc
  # BEFORE the spawn, because `_spawn_start` can `return` and the fact worth
  # recording is that this workspace came into existence — the registry has no
  # creation timestamp anywhere (§0), and `.spawn` is the LAST respawn.
  #
  # THE NAMES ARE THIS FUNCTION'S OWN, measured against its local set: the slug
  # is `$slug` and the wrapper is `$hw` (`_ws_least_loaded`'s pick, used again
  # at ccd:3595). There is no `$ws` and no `$wrapper` here, and under
  # `set -uo pipefail` naming one would EXIT the shell between the lock and the
  # spawn, with the worktree already on disk.
  #
  # THE DEC PAIRS ARE APPENDED ONLY WHEN THEY WERE DECLARED (D-410). Parsing
  # the flags without threading them here would be ceremony: this row is the
  # journal's whole record of a spawn's origin, and until the loop above
  # existed every dispatched spawn wrote it as *declared: nothing*.
  # `lc_gs`/`lc_ga` — givenness, not content — is what decides, so an
  # undeclared call emits EXACTLY the row it emitted before this task.
  #
  # AND IT IS BELT TO THE ENCODER'S BRACES, NOT A GUARD A TEST DEFENDS ALONE
  # — measured, because the plan predicted otherwise. `_lc_json` drops every
  # pair whose value is `""` (ccd:2221-2222) and backfills
  # `dec.setdefault("surface","none")` (ccd:2231), and `lc_surface` cannot
  # hold anything but `none` while `lc_gs` is 0 — so collapsing all three
  # lines to an unconditional `local lc_dec=(dec.surface "$lc_surface"
  # dec.actor "$lc_actor")` changes no byte of any row: ccd-lifecycle-sites
  # stayed 29/29 and every other journal-reading suite 180/180. No fixture
  # can separate them. What IS pinned is the PAIR: remove this conditional
  # AND the `(( lc_gs ))` guard on the normalisation above and the undeclared
  # row reads `surface: unknown` (1 failed / 28). Kept because it says what
  # it means rather than leaning on a downstream drop.
  local lc_dec=()
  (( lc_gs )) && lc_dec+=(dec.surface "$lc_surface")
  (( lc_ga )) && lc_dec+=(dec.actor "$lc_actor")
  _lc_done create "$id" "" "${lc_dec[@]}" meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" \
    meas.branch "$branch" meas.base "$base" meas.workdir "$wt" meas.wrapper "$hw"
  _spawn_start "$id" new || return $?
  _reg_claim "$id"
  _ws_supervise "$id"
  _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
  # rc 6 JOINS 3/4/5 HERE, and it is the SAME SENTENCE this `if` already carries
  # — "the session exists, is claimed, is supervised, and did not confirm its
  # TUI". rc 6 says the startup gates STOOD DOWN: the session was there —
  # `_accept_first_run_prompts` measures the width only BELOW its liveness
  # probe, so a session that never came up still earns 3 — but its pane measured
  # under READER_MIN_COLS, so no gate was read and no live marker was confirmed.
  # That is not "the spawn failed", and OUTSIDE the set it is the exact hazard the block
  # above `_spawn_start` measures: a code no caller tests, printing a SUCCESS
  # line over a spawn nothing confirmed.
  #
  # THE EVIDENCE SENTENCE THAT STOOD HERE DESCRIBED THE PRE-FIX TREE, and it
  # contradicted the ordering paragraph directly above it. It said that in a
  # fixture whose tmux refuses every verb `ccd ws-add` answers rc 6. It does
  # not, and cannot, now that rc 6 sits BELOW the liveness probe. Re-measured
  # 2026-09-16, by sourcing this file into a fixture HOME and calling
  # `_accept_first_run_prompts cc-demo 0` under `tmux() { return 1; }`: the
  # answer is rc 3 — the `has-session` debounce refuses before the width is
  # ever queried. `ccdargv-dec-parity.test.ts`'s own ws-add probe answers 3 for
  # the same reason (measured through that file's harness, same date). rc 6
  # needs a LIVE `has-session` AND a narrow pane together, which only a steered
  # fixture produces (`ccd-reader-standdown.test.ts`'s rc-6 describe).
  #
  # THE ENROLMENT IS STILL NECESSARY, and it is argued from the code rather
  # than from that fixture: `_accept_first_run_prompts` can return 6, this `if`
  # is the only thing between a non-zero settle and the `workspace <id> …`
  # success line below, and a 6 outside the set would print it.
  # `_spawn_settle`'s own `6)` arm names the remedy; this rung is what keeps
  # the verb from claiming otherwise.
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$rc" -eq 4 || "$rc" -eq 5 || "$rc" -eq 6 ]]; then
    echo "ccd: ws-add spawn failed for $id (spawn rc $rc) — see $REG/$id.spawn" >&2
    return "$rc"
  fi
  echo "workspace $id on $hw — $wt (branch $branch, from $base)"
}

# Every path git prints is fully resolved ($HOME/worktrees -> /data/worktrees ->
# /mnt/...), while the registry keeps the path ccd wrote. Resolving needs the
# path to EXIST, and the whole point of the caller below is the case where it
# does not — so resolve the longest existing prefix and re-attach the rest.
_ws_realpath() {   # path -> same path, existing prefix resolved
  local p="$1" head="$1" tail="" real
  while [[ ! -d "$head" && "$head" != / && "$head" != . && -n "$head" ]]; do
    tail="/$(basename -- "$head")$tail"; head=$(dirname -- "$head")   # -- : a path may start with -
  done
  # `>/dev/null` on the cd, not merely `2>`: `pwd -P` is the answer, so anything
  # cd itself prints would be captured as part of it. bash's own cd prints on a
  # CDPATH hit (not reachable here — $head is only ever an absolute path walked
  # upwards), but a `cd` exported as a shell function prints whatever it likes,
  # and that is reachable: bash imports it into this script through the
  # environment. Redirecting cd alone leaves `pwd -P` free to answer.
  real=$(cd -- "$head" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || { echo "$p"; return 0; }
  # `pwd -P` prints "/" for the root and never a trailing slash anywhere else,
  # so stripping is only ever right when there is a tail to re-attach: for the
  # root with no tail, "${real%/}" strips the only character there is.
  if [[ -n "$tail" ]]; then echo "${real%/}$tail"; else echo "$real"; fi
}

# Which repository a directory belongs to, as one absolute, symlink-collapsed
# path — the only question that distinguishes "$main's worktree" from "some
# other repo that was put at that path".
#   exit: non-zero and NO output when the path is in no repository at all —
#         callers must treat the empty string as "no answer", never as a match.
# `--path-format=absolute` (git >= 2.31; this fleet is on 2.43) is doing the work.
# Plain `--git-common-dir` is not comparable as printed — a bare `.git` from a
# repo's own checkout, `../.git` from a subdirectory of one, absolute only from a
# linked worktree — and resolving it here meant a `cd` inside a command
# substitution, whose stdout ccd does not get to control: bash's own cd prints the
# directory it landed on when a relative operand hits CDPATH, and a `cd` exported
# as a shell function prints whatever it likes. Either print is captured as part
# of the answer (`2>/dev/null` catches neither), and the caller then compares
# multi-line garbage — two wrong answers that happen to match wave a squatter
# through, two that do not refuse a healthy workspace. Asking git leaves no `cd`
# to shadow. Measured on git 2.43.0 against the cd-based resolution it replaces:
# byte-identical for a repo's own checkout, a subdirectory of one, a linked
# worktree, a worktree of a worktree, --separate-git-dir, a `.git` that is itself
# a symlink, a bare repo, and a main and its worktree reached through two
# different symlinks (both sides still compare equal); and failing with no output
# for a directory in no repository at all.
_ws_common_dir() {   # dir -> /abs/.../.git
  local dir="$1" gcd
  gcd=$(git -C "$dir" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  [[ -n "$gcd" ]] || return 1
  printf '%s\n' "$gcd"
}

# THE branch source of truth: git's own record of which branch the worktree at
# $2 has checked out, read from the repo at $1. Not `rev-parse HEAD` inside the
# worktree — that answers from whatever repository owns that directory, which
# after a hand-deletion and a stray `git init` need not be $1 at all, and the
# name it returns is then deleted in $1. This lookup cannot leave $1, survives
# the directory being gone (the record stays, marked `prunable`), and follows a
# branch rename whether ccd or the user did it.
#   stdout: branch name, empty for a detached HEAD
#   exit:   0 = git has a registration for that path, 1 = it has none
# `git worktree list` failing (repo gone, corrupt, not a repo) yields no records
# and therefore exit 1 — "no evidence", which is the reading that deletes
# nothing.
_ws_wt_branch() {   # main path
  local main="$1" path="$2" real cur="" line
  real=$(_ws_realpath "$path")
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    case "$line" in
      "worktree "*) cur="${line#worktree }" ;;
      "branch "*)   [[ "$cur" == "$real" || "$cur" == "$path" ]] && { echo "${line#branch refs/heads/}"; return 0; } ;;
      detached)     [[ "$cur" == "$real" || "$cur" == "$path" ]] && { echo ""; return 0; } ;;
    esac
  done < <(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null)
  return 1
}

_ws_branch_holders() {   # main branch -> every worktree PATH whose record names
                          # that branch, one per line (resolved), rc 0 even when
                          # none do. `_ws_wt_branch` read backwards.
  # THE QUESTION `git branch -d` ASKS AND `update-ref -d` DOES NOT. Measured on
  # git 2.43: `git branch -d develop` refuses with "cannot delete branch
  # 'develop' used by worktree at …", while `git update-ref -d refs/heads/develop
  # <tip>` deletes it, rc 0, leaving that worktree's HEAD pointing at a ref that
  # no longer resolves — `git status` there reads "No commits yet on develop" on
  # a checkout with a full history. `_ws_reap_tail` (g) is an `update-ref -d`
  # deliberately (`branch -d` refuses a squash merge, which is the whole point of
  # the proof ladder), so the check `branch -d` would have made has to be made
  # explicitly, by whoever is about to delete.
  #
  # The per-child ladder already asks it (`child-branch-elsewhere`, "removing it
  # here would strand that other checkout"); until now the PARENT did not, and
  # did not have to, because `registry-branch-drift` then refused every branch except
  # the workspace's own — a name only its own worktree could hold. That refusal
  # is gone, so this rung is what replaces it rather than a new idea.
  #
  # PATHS, not the child rung's COUNT, because the remedy is a place: an
  # operator told "checked out in 2 worktrees" has to go and find the other one.
  # Resolved, the same way `_ws_wt_branch` resolves the path it matches, so a
  # symlinked ancestor cannot make our own worktree read as a stranger.
  # AND THE ENUMERATION'S STATUS IS THE ANSWER'S. A `worktree list` that failed
  # prints nothing, and nothing reads as "no other checkout holds this branch" —
  # a fabricated measurement in front of the only irreversible line in the file,
  # and fail-OPEN, which is the one direction this file never allows. Read into
  # a variable on its own line (`local x=$(cmd)` returns local's status), check
  # it, and only then parse.
  local main="$1" branch="$2" cur="" line listing
  listing=$(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    case "$line" in
      "worktree "*) cur="${line#worktree }" ;;
      "branch refs/heads/$branch") [[ -n "$cur" ]] && _ws_realpath "$cur" ;;
    esac
  done <<< "$listing"
  return 0
}

# `main branch workdir` -> the holders that are NOT this workspace, comma-joined,
# empty when none. One reader for the two call sites (`_ws_reap_eval`'s rung and
# `_ws_reap_tail`'s re-proof immediately before the CAS), so the two cannot come
# to different answers about the same repository.
# Non-zero when the enumeration itself failed — NOT "nobody else holds it".
# Both call sites turn that into a refusal, because a question that could not be
# asked is never an answer of "no".
_ws_branch_elsewhere() {
  local main="$1" branch="$2" workdir="$3" mine other out="" holders
  mine=$(_ws_realpath "$workdir")
  holders=$(_ws_branch_holders "$main" "$branch") || return 1
  while IFS= read -r other; do
    [[ -n "$other" && "$other" != "$mine" ]] || continue
    out+="${out:+, }$other"
  done <<< "$holders"
  printf '%s' "$out"
  return 0
}

cmd_ws_rm() {   # id — tear a workspace down. Refuses anything it might destroy.
  # FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE, and that order is the correctness
  # argument — `cmd_stop`'s own header (ccd:12442-12446) records what the other
  # order costs: with the flag left in argv the verb aims at a session that does
  # not exist while the real one keeps running.
  #
  # A VALIDATED FLAG, NEVER A POSITIONAL. ccd:11606-11610 is the paid lesson: an
  # extra argv word on a prefix-whitelisted verb reached a bash arithmetic
  # context where a command substitution EXECUTES. This value reaches no `(( ))`,
  # no `$(( ))`, no `[[ x -ge y ]]` and no array subscript — it is written
  # verbatim into one journal field and parsed nowhere, `cmd_ws_hold`'s own
  # contract (ccd:4426-4427) applied here.
  #
  # AND IT CLOSES A HOLE ON THE WAY PAST: this verb had NO arity guard at all,
  # so `ccd ws-rm x y z` silently ignored `y z`. The residue is checked below.
  local reason="" args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates (ccd:12460-12462).
      #
      # A BARE `die` HERE, DELIBERATELY, and `ccd-refusal-scan.test.ts` names
      # this line in its SANCTIONED set: no `$id` exists yet, and a refusal
      # record whose subject is empty says less than the usage line does.
      --reason)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-rm [--reason <text>] <id>"
                  reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason=*) reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      # F2 FIX (review round 1): CLOSED FLAG SET, NEVER SWALLOWED. Before this,
      # an unrecognised `--foo` fell into the catch-all below, became a
      # positional, and was refused only as an ARITY side-effect (or, alone,
      # misrecorded as `no-such-session: --foo`) — a record that names the
      # wrong defect. A `--`-prefixed token that is not one of the flags above
      # refuses BY NAME. Ids never start with `--` (single-dash ids still fall
      # through to the catch-all below, unaffected).
      --*)        _lc_refuse destroy "$1" bad-args \
                    "unrecognised flag: $1" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason" ;;
      *)          args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"    # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2): an empty array
                         # here is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  # F1 FIX (review round 1): the arity guard now fires BEFORE `id` is bound,
  # so it can never be bypassed by an unbound-parameter death. `${1-}` never
  # exits under `set -u` (unlike the `${1:?}` this replaces) — zero args and
  # too-many args are now the SAME refused, journaled, `die`-through-`_lc_refuse`
  # path, instead of zero args killing the process before any `_lc_*` runs.
  [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || _lc_refuse destroy "${1-}" bad-args \
    "usage: ccd ws-rm [--reason <text>] <id>" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
  local id="${1-}"
  # REFUSED, NOT TRUNCATED, and measured in BYTES. A 900-byte note silently cut
  # to 512 reads as the operator's own words on the one record that outlives the
  # workspace; `_lc_dec_ok` counts bytes under `LC_ALL=C` so a 200-emoji reason
  # cannot pass here and be refused by the next surface. The refusal itself
  # carries NO `dec.reason` — the value is the thing that did not fit.
  _lc_dec_ok "$reason" || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" bad-args \
    "--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes — nothing was touched"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" no-such-session "no such session: $id" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
  local ws project workdir
  ws=$(_reg_get "$id" workspace); project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  # The absence of a workspace field is what distinguishes a main checkout.
  [[ -n "$ws" ]] || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" not-a-workspace \
    "$id is not a workspace — refusing to remove a main checkout" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
  [[ -n "$project" && -n "$workdir" ]] || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" incomplete-registry \
    "incomplete registry for '$id'" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"

  # A hold is a declaration that a program is mid-flight here. Destroying the
  # workspace should take two deliberate acts — release, then remove — never
  # one. The reason is echoed so the operator knows WHICH program refuses.
  # `-e` not `-f`: present-but-unreadable still refuses — doubt reads as HELD.
  # `die`, because this verb's caller is a terminal and every other refusal in
  # it dies too; `cmd_ws_reap`'s copy of this rung answers in JSON on stdout
  # instead, for the reason recorded there (the PWA reads tokens, not stderr).
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
    # D-2378: the `-e` gate above is correct and deliberate — doubt reads as
    # HELD, and this rung is untouched — but the `cat` that used to follow
    # opened BY NAME: a FIFO with no writer, or a symlink to an unbounded
    # character device, blocks in open(2)/read(2) forever, and this verb's
    # caller is a terminal. `_project_pool_state`'s pattern (NAMED BY
    # FUNCTION, NOT LINE): `-f`+`-r` checked BEFORE any attempt to open, so a
    # non-regular shape is answered the SAME fallback text `cat`'s own
    # `|| echo` already gave a directory — but the population this changes
    # is NOT just "what used to hang": a readable-but-not-regular shape that
    # never hung, such as a symlink to `/dev/null`, used to satisfy `cat`
    # promptly with EMPTY output (`-e` true, `-f` false, zero bytes read) and
    # now answers the same fallback marker instead. The true population is
    # "hangs UNION readable-but-not-regular", and every member of it gets a
    # strictly more honest answer than before.
    local _hold_reason
    if [[ -f "$REG/$id.hold" && -r "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
      _hold_reason=$(cat "$REG/$id.hold" 2>/dev/null || echo '<unreadable — treat as held>')
    else
      _hold_reason='<unreadable — treat as held>'
    fi
    _lc_refuse destroy "$id" held \
      "held: $_hold_reason — release first: ccd ws-release --session $id" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
  fi
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"

  # Gather the evidence BEFORE anything is torn down, and gather it from git.
  # `registered` is what decides whether there is anything for `worktree remove`
  # to clear, so it is read from $? on its own line: with no `set -e`, a
  # `local branch=$(...)` would return local's status, not the function's.
  local branch registered
  branch=$(_ws_wt_branch "$main" "$workdir"); registered=$?

  if [[ -d "$workdir" ]]; then
    # REFUSE FIRST, tear down second. A directory that exists but is not a
    # worktree of $main is not ours to remove and its HEAD is not ours to
    # delete: move or delete it by hand and re-run, and the missing-directory
    # path below will finish the job.
    #
    # `registered` cannot decide this on its own: it comes from git's RECORD,
    # and the record outlives the directory. Delete the directory by hand and
    # put anything else back at that path — a `git init`, another repo's
    # worktree — and the record still names it, so `registered` is 0 while the
    # directory belongs to someone else. Trusting it alone killed the session
    # and disabled the unit, and only THEN did `worktree remove` refuse. So ask
    # the directory too, and require both to say $main.
    local wd_common main_common
    wd_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$workdir"); main_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$main")
    (( registered == 0 )) && [[ -n "$main_common" && "$wd_common" == "$main_common" ]] \
      || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" foreign-worktree \
           "$workdir is not a worktree of $main — nothing was touched; move or delete the directory by hand, then re-run (with it gone, ccd clears the leftover record itself; \`git worktree prune\` will not, while the directory is there)" \
           dec.reason "$reason"
    # --porcelain counts untracked files, which is what `git worktree remove`
    # itself objects to. Doing this after the kill would leave the uncommitted
    # work intact but the session dead and out of supervision — and the refusal
    # would be lying about it. A status we could not read is not a clean one
    # (same rule as _ws_gc_dirty).
    #
    # AND THE EXIT CODE IS ONLY HALF OF "COULD NOT READ" — final-round
    # verification P2. The integration-5 class was closed at `_ws_reap_eval`
    # (ccd:3244) and `_ws_archive_manifest` (ccd:1890) and left open HERE, in
    # the one destructive verb a human types by hand, three lines under the
    # comment that already states the rule. Measured on git 2.43: `chmod 000` on
    # a TRACKED directory holding a modified file gives rc 0, EMPTY stdout and
    # `warning: could not open directory 'tracked/'` on stderr — so `$dirty` was
    # empty, the guard passed, `_ws_unsupervise` and `tmux kill-session` ran,
    # and only `git worktree remove` finally refused ("Directory not empty"),
    # printing a message about unlocking a tree while the uncommitted work it
    # never saw was still in it. Exactly the outcome this block's own comment
    # exists to prevent. The uncommitted file survived only because a directory
    # git cannot read is also one git cannot rmdir; that is luck, not a design.
    #
    # ANY diagnostic refuses, with the polarity `_ws_reap_eval` states out loud
    # for the same read: a benign `warning:` a future git decides to print makes
    # this workspace un-removable until the warning is fixed. That is the right
    # direction for a verb whose whole contract is "refuses anything it might
    # destroy", and the alternative — a wording allowlist — is walked straight
    # through by a translated warning. Two statements and a scratch file rather
    # than a pipeline, because `local x=$(cmd)` returns local's status.
    local dirty derrf derr drc
    derrf=$(_plat_mktemp) || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" scratch-unwritable \
      "could not make a scratch file to read $workdir — nothing was touched" \
      dec.reason "$reason"
    dirty=$(git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain 2>"$derrf"); drc=$?
    derr=$(cat "$derrf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$derrf"
    { (( drc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$derr" ]]; } \
      || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" tree-unreadable \
           "could not read $workdir${derr:+ ($derr)} — nothing was touched" \
           dec.reason "$reason"
    [[ -z "$dirty" ]] || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" dirty-tree \
      "worktree not removed (uncommitted changes?) — nothing was touched: $workdir" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
    local nested
    nested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$workdir") \
      || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" tree-unreadable \
           "could not scan $workdir for nested checkouts${_WS_NESTED_WHY:+ ($_WS_NESTED_WHY)} — nothing was touched" \
           dec.reason "$reason"
    [[ -z "$nested" ]] \
      || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" nested-checkouts-present \
           "worktree not removed — nested checkouts live under it, and ccd deletes no repository it did not create: ${nested//$'\n'/, } — nothing was touched" \
           dec.reason "$reason"
  fi

  # ── R4-1 · THE ATTIC PIN, BEFORE ANYTHING IS TOUCHED ────────────────────
  # `git worktree remove` at ccd:3903 deletes both $workdir and
  # $main/.git/worktrees/<slug>/, and the latter holds the HEAD reflog.
  # `_ws_attic_pin`'s reflog read is guarded by `[[ -d "$workdir" ]]`, so a pin
  # taken after that call sees nothing and contributes only a pre-resolved tip.
  # Placing it above `_ws_unsupervise` rather than merely above 2929 puts it on
  # the near side of EVERY irreversible act, so the intent/done pair below
  # brackets a workspace whose commits are already referenced.
  #
  # UNCONDITIONAL: it runs even when `git branch -d` later refuses at ccd:3920,
  # because that is the case where the commits matter most, and even when
  # `registered != 0` so ccd:3903 never runs at all.
  #
  # `_ws_attic_pin` contains no `die`, which is what makes this `$( )` capture
  # safe under `ccd-die-containment.test.ts`. Nothing added here may die inside
  # a capture.
  local reg_branch; reg_branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  local tip="" atticsrc=none attic=0
  if [[ -d "$workdir" ]]; then
    tip=$(git -C "$workdir" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null) && atticsrc=worktree || tip=""
    # THE ASYMMETRY, and it is a measurement rather than a fabricated zero:
    # a workspace still on disk whose tip cannot be resolved is exactly what
    # "refuses anything it might destroy" means. Directory already gone is the
    # other case and it is handled below — refusing there would wedge cleanup
    # on a workspace with nothing left to protect.
    [[ -n "$tip" ]] \
      || _lc_refuse destroy "$id" tip-unreadable \
           "could not resolve a tip for $workdir — nothing was touched; the workspace is still on disk and its commits would be unreferenced" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
  elif [[ -n "$reg_branch" ]]; then
    tip=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify "refs/heads/$reg_branch" 2>/dev/null) \
      && atticsrc=registry || tip=""
  fi
  if [[ -n "$tip" ]]; then
    attic=$(_ws_attic_pin "$main" "$id" "$workdir" "$tip")
    [[ "$attic" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || attic=0
  fi
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  # THE PAIR. `tx` is a `local`, minted here and passed to both halves — there
  # is no global carrying it, and there must not be: the `LC_TIP=` idiom dies
  # under `set -u` on the first call in a process and appends a blank line
  # nobody notices. Everything above this point is the refuse-first ladder, so
  # the intent sits exactly on the boundary of the irreversible.
  local lctx; lctx=$(_lc_tx)
  _lc_intent destroy "$id" "$lctx" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason" \
    meas.project "$project" \
    meas.workspace "$ws" meas.branch "$branch" meas.workdir "$workdir" \
    meas.registered "$registered" meas.tip "$tip" meas.attic "$attic" \
    meas.atticsrc "$atticsrc"

  _ws_unsupervise "$id"
  tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null || true

  if (( registered == 0 )); then
    # Not gated on the directory existing: `git worktree remove` clears the
    # registration either way, and while that registration stands git refuses to
    # delete the branch it names ("cannot delete branch used by worktree at").
    # Still no --force: the tree can have changed since the check above, and a
    # locked worktree refuses either way. By here the session IS stopped, so say
    # that rather than repeat "nothing touched".
    # The recovery has to work in the state it is printed in. `worktree prune`
    # only drops records whose directory is MISSING, so it is named for exactly
    # that case and not offered as a general remedy — the "directory is there
    # but is not ours" case refuses above, before anything is torn down.
    git -C "$main" worktree remove "$workdir" \
      || { _lc_fail destroy "$id" "$lctx" worktree-remove-failed \
             "git refused to clear the worktree record for $workdir"
           die "worktree record not cleared for $workdir — the session was stopped; git refused: unlock it (git -C $main worktree unlock $workdir) or clean the tree, then re-run — and if the directory is already gone, git -C $main worktree prune clears the record"; }
  fi

  # DECLARED IN THE R4-1 BLOCK ABOVE NOW, not here: the attic pin needs it to
  # resolve a tip from the registry when the worktree is gone, and a SECOND
  # `local` in the same function re-declares and blanks it. One reader, one place.
  if [[ -n "$branch" ]]; then
    # git had a record, so the registry is a witness, never the decider: a
    # divergence means the branch was renamed outside ccd, and the name to
    # delete is the one git actually had checked out there.
    [[ -n "$reg_branch" && "$reg_branch" != "$branch" ]] \
      && echo "note: registry recorded '$reg_branch', git's worktree record says '$branch' — deleting '$branch'; '$reg_branch' left alone" >&2
    # No -D: an unmerged branch must survive — and must SAY so, or it is
    # orphaned in silence.
    git -C "$main" branch -d "$branch" 2>/dev/null \
      || echo "kept branch $branch (unmerged, or still in use) — delete it with: git -C $main branch -D $branch" >&2
  elif (( registered == 0 )); then
    echo "note: $workdir was on a detached HEAD — no branch to delete" >&2
    # "No branch to delete" is only half the truth when the registry named one:
    # the teardown above just took away its worktree and its registration, and
    # the line below takes away its registry entry, leaving it with no ws-gc row
    # either — invisible, which is the state this whole path exists to prevent.
    # Same rung-3 rule as the no-record case: git never corroborated that name
    # for this path (the record it did hold was detached), so name it and hand
    # over the command rather than delete it unverified.
    if [[ -n "$reg_branch" ]] && git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$reg_branch" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "warn: registry says branch '$reg_branch', which still exists and now has no worktree — not deleting it unverified. If it is finished: git -C $main branch -d $reg_branch" >&2
    fi
  elif [[ -n "$reg_branch" ]] && git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$reg_branch" 2>/dev/null; then
    # No git record at all (someone pruned it by hand). The registry name is
    # uncorroborated — the only thing that ever tied it to this path is now
    # gone — and deleting the wrong branch costs incomparably more than leaving
    # the right one behind. Name it and hand over the command.
    echo "warn: no worktree record for $workdir; registry says branch '$reg_branch', which still exists — not deleting it unverified. If it is finished: git -C $main branch -d $reg_branch" >&2
  fi

  # ── D-2605: THIS CALLER READS `_reg_purge`'s STATUS — THE VALUE ─────────
  # It reached here AFTER irreversible action, so both nonzero conditions are a
  # FAILURE, not a refusal — that is `_lc_fail`'s documented distinction from
  # `_lc_refuse`: a refusal precedes anything irreversible, a failure follows
  # it. Reporting either as a suppressed success is the exact false success
  # `_reg_purge`'s own "never claims success" rule forbids.
  #
  # BUT THEY ARE TWO DIFFERENT FAILURES, and a boolean `if !` told the operator
  # the same story for both. 1/2 means the purge never started: the row stands
  # whole and re-running is the entire remedy. 3 means it ran and did not
  # finish: the row is GONE, the purge fact is in this journal, and something
  # `rm -f` would not take is still on disk — a message telling that operator
  # to wait for a compaction to settle prescribes a wait for a compaction that
  # is not running and promises state that no longer exists.
  local _rm_prc=0
  _reg_purge "$id" || _rm_prc=$?                                           # finding F3
  if (( _rm_prc == 3 )); then
    _lc_fail destroy "$id" "$lctx" purge-incomplete \
      "the workspace was torn down and the registry row WAS purged — this journal's own purge fact is the record of it — but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed and still stands; re-running \`ccd ws-rm $id\` will not take it, remove it by hand" \
      verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason" meas.unremoved "$REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED"
    echo "ccd: $id: torn down and purged, but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _rm_prc == 2 )); then
    # STATUS 2 IS NOT STATUS 1 (r3 A-I3). `_reg_purge` has answered the two
    # apart since D-2782 and every caller collapsed them, so a flock-less box
    # was told its lock "was unavailable" and to "re-run once the compaction
    # settles" — both false there, and measured: `_compact_lock_acquire`
    # returns 2 from `command -v` BEFORE it touches the lock file, so the
    # pathname the old sentence named does not exist, and no compaction runs or
    # can run. The remedy spec §3.4 traces for THIS verb is the PATH and
    # nothing else: `ws-rm`'s re-run skips the dirty/foreign gates once
    # `[[ -d "$workdir" ]]` is false, so it has no second precondition to meet.
    _lc_fail destroy "$id" "$lctx" purge-mechanism-absent \
      "the workspace was torn down, but the registry row could not be purged: the compaction lock's MECHANISM is absent on this box — flock, mktemp or link could not be resolved on the PATH ccd ran with, so the lock was never consulted and the lock file may not even exist — while $REG/$id.generation is present, so the purge refused rather than race a hook that may hold it. $REG/$id.* and $REG/$id.generation still stand; waiting changes nothing — re-run \`ccd ws-rm $id\` from a PATH that resolves flock, mktemp and link" \
      verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
    echo "ccd: $id: torn down, registry NOT purged — the compaction lock mechanism is absent; see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _rm_prc != 0 )); then
    # STATUS 1 FOLDS SEVERAL CONDITIONS, AND COMPACT_LOCK_WHY NAMES ONLY THE
    # ONES A RE-RUN CANNOT CURE (r5 R4-M3, correcting r4 A-M3's "STATUS 1 IS
    # TWO CONDITIONS"). `_compact_lock_acquire` returns 1 from many distinct
    # states. ONE of them, the `flock -w` timeout, is ordinary contention, and
    # the default sentence below is written for it. WHICH states carry a token,
    # and what an EMPTY `WHY` does and does not prove, are NOT restated here
    # (r7 R6-M1): that census lives in ONE place, the header comment on
    # _compact_lock_acquire, which is the only writer of the variable. The copy
    # that used to stand here went stale inside a single round — it mis-counted
    # the assignment sites the acquire had just grown, and called the whole
    # untokened residue a race at the mint after the acquire had named a
    # durable member of it — so this caller points instead of repeating, and a
    # numeral about the code is made in exactly one place. What this caller
    # needs from the variable is only that nothing clobbers it between
    # `_reg_purge`'s return and here.
    # `_compact_lock_why_remedy` (r6 R5-M3) keys each token to ITS
    # OWN remedy and gives an unrecognised one the catch-all: a condition
    # named at the acquire must never silently inherit "wait and re-run",
    # which is the defect that let one sentence stand for two conditions.
    # That is the same argument `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start` make at their own
    # acquire sites, one level up; this is it one level down, where the
    # record is DURABLE — and it is the ONE renderer all four durable callers
    # consult, so this caller's text cannot drift from theirs.
    local _rm_why; _rm_why=$(_compact_lock_why_remedy "$id" ws-rm)
    _lc_fail destroy "$id" "$lctx" purge-refused \
      "the workspace was torn down, but the registry row could not be purged: $_rm_why" \
      verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason"
    echo "ccd: $id: torn down, registry NOT purged — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  fi

  # AFTER `_reg_purge`, so the `done` line sits after the terminal act. Note
  # this workspace now has TWO records for one removal — the D3 backstop's
  # `purge` line and this `destroy`/`done` — and that redundancy is the design:
  # a silent destruction has to defeat two independent emit sites.
  #
  # THREE STATES, THREE VALUES. `git branch -d` at ccd:3920 refuses an unmerged
  # branch and that refusal is wanted; an empty `bd` is OMITTED by the encoder,
  # which is how "there was no branch" stays distinct from "the branch is gone".
  # A two-valued expression answers `true` for a detached HEAD — a fabricated
  # fact on the one record that outlives the workspace.
  local bd=""
  if [[ -n "$branch" ]]; then
    if git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null
    then bd=false; else bd=true; fi
  fi
  _lc_done destroy "$id" "$lctx" verb ws-rm dec.reason "$reason" meas.branch "$branch" branchDeleted "$bd"
  echo "removed workspace $id"
}

# ── renaming a workspace branch ────────────────────────────────────
# The subset of `git check-ref-format` that matters, checked explicitly rather
# than trusted to git's exit code: an invalid name must be refused BEFORE any
# git command is run with it as an argument.
_ws_branch_valid() {
  local b="$1"
  [[ -n "$b" ]]                              || return 1
  [[ "$b" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+$ ]]           || return 1   # no space, colon, glob, ~, ^, @{
  [[ "$b" != -* ]]                           || return 1   # git would read it as an option
  [[ "$b" != /* && "$b" != */ ]]             || return 1
  [[ "$b" != *..* ]]                         || return 1
  [[ "$b" != *.lock && "$b" != *.lock/* ]]   || return 1   # any component, not just the last
  return 0
}

cmd_ws_rename() {   # ccd ws-rename --session <id> --branch <name> — rename a
  # workspace branch before it is pushed.
  #
  # FLAGS, not positionals, and every refusal is an ANSWER: one JSON object on
  # stdout at exit 0. Both changes are for the same caller. This verb's first
  # unattended caller is FleetWatcher's naming sweep, on the far side of the
  # agent WS, where a non-zero exit carrying a stderr string is
  # indistinguishable from the agent being down — and "this branch is already
  # pushed, never rename it" must not be retried the way a transport failure is.
  # The flags matter from the other end for the same reason: the agent's exec
  # whitelist matches by PREFIX, so a positional verb can only be granted as the
  # one-token ['ws-rename'], which permits `ccd ws-rename <anything>
  # <anything…>` for a call no human ever reviews.
  #
  # THE TOKENS ARE INLINE LITERALS, not arguments to a helper, and that is
  # load-bearing rather than stylistic. `server/test/wsaudit.test.ts:57` scans
  # THIS FILE with /"refused":"([a-zA-Z0-9-]+)"/ and asserts set equality in
  # both directions against `wsaudit.ts`'s SENTENCES; a helper whose format
  # string reads `"refused":"%s"` contributes no token to that scan, so the nine
  # sentences this verb needs would fail the reverse direction and the only
  # fixes would be deleting copy or weakening an approved mechanism.
  # `cmd_ws_reap` already writes every one of its ~30 refusals this way.
  #
  # `_json_str`'s status, checked ONCE and up front — the same probe, for the
  # same reason, as `cmd_ws_audit` (ccd:10967) and `cmd_ws_reap` (ccd:11919).
  # Every refusal below quotes its detail inside a printf ARGUMENT LIST, where a
  # failure is a swallowed status and an empty argument — `"detail":,` — i.e. a
  # document the server reports as a parse error rather than as the refusal it
  # actually was. BEFORE the arity check, because `bad-args` is itself one of
  # the refusals that needs quoting. python3 missing is a FAULT, not a refusal:
  # the verb cannot answer at all.
  _json_str probe >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "python3 unavailable — cannot quote the rename answer safely"

  # WAVE 5 — THE DEC FLAGS, STRIPPED HERE AND NOT ONE LINE EARLIER. Below the
  # `_json_str` probe, because every refusal in this verb is a QUOTED JSON
  # answer at exit 0 and an unquotable detail is a parse error rather than the
  # refusal it actually was. Above the arity rule, because the rule is exact
  # and a flag left in argv would refuse a correct call. A THIRD ARRANGEMENT
  # from Tasks 45-47's two: `cmd_ws_archive` (Task 45) binds no `id` until
  # AFTER its arity check, so its block could go almost anywhere above that
  # check; `cmd_ws_restore` (Task 46) binds `id="${2-}"` as its very first
  # statement, so its block had to precede that binding specifically. This verb
  # binds neither `id` nor `new` until AFTER the arity check below (`local
  # id=$2 new=$4`, ccd:4022 pre-wave-5) — so, unlike Task 46's case, there is no
  # earlier binding this placement could land on the wrong side of.
  #
  # `break`, NOT a refusal in the loop body: a `return` inside the `while` would
  # leave the remaining argv unread, and the answer this verb owes is one
  # document. The first fault wins and the loop stops; `lc_bad` carries it out.
  #
  # THE TOKEN IS `bad-args`, THE ONE THIS VERB ALREADY SPELLS, and that is not
  # tidiness: `server/test/wsaudit.test.ts:53-63` greps this file's raw text for
  # `"refused":"<token>"` and holds the result set-equal to `wsaudit.ts`'s
  # SENTENCES in both directions, so a `bad-surface` invented here is a red
  # suite whose only fixes are new copy or a weakened mechanism.
  local lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_reason='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_gr=0 lc_args=() lc_bad=''
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      --surface)   if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2
                   else lc_bad='--surface needs a value'; break; fi ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2
                   else lc_bad='--actor needs a value'; break; fi ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      --reason)    if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then lc_gr=1; lc_reason="$2"; shift 2
                   else lc_bad='--reason needs a value'; break; fi ;;
      --reason=*)  lc_gr=1; lc_reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop is
  # where that distinction has to be made, before the encoder ever sees the
  # value. `lc_gs` already carries givenness; the `${lc_w:-unknown}` fallback
  # below is what turns a declared-but-blank surface into `unknown` rather than
  # an empty string that `_lc_json` would backfill to `none` — the exact
  # given/no-flag collapse this verb must not produce.
  #
  # DIVERGES FROM THE BRIEF'S OWN STEP-3 SAMPLE, which read
  # `lc_surface=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface")` with no fallback: measured
  # against D-210, that line leaves a declared-but-blank `--surface ''` as the
  # literal empty string, which `_lc_json` backfills to `none` — "no flag was
  # given at all", exactly the collapse D-210 exists to prevent. Same fix
  # Tasks 45-47 already carry for their own copies of this loop.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (ccd:2411-2422, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''` returns
  # 0, so it is not a blank guard on its own. TWO SEPARATE CHECKS below, blank
  # then length — the same shape `cmd_ws_archive`/`cmd_ws_restore` use.
  #
  # DIVERGES FROM THE BRIEF'S OWN STEP-3 SAMPLE, whose single
  # `! _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor"` test cannot fire on a blank value at all
  # (`_lc_dec_ok ''` measures 0 bytes, which is `<= 512`): that line alone
  # leaves a declared `--actor ''` UNREFUSED — the opposite of this task's own
  # Step-1 test ("refuses a blank --actor as JSON at exit 0"). Same gap the
  # brief's --reason sample repeats.
  if [[ -z "$lc_bad" ]] && (( lc_ga )) && [[ -z "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
    lc_bad='--actor must be non-blank'
  fi
  if [[ -z "$lc_bad" ]] && (( lc_ga )) && ! _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor"; then
    lc_bad="--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  if [[ -z "$lc_bad" ]] && (( lc_gr )) && [[ -z "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
    lc_bad='--reason must be non-blank'
  fi
  if [[ -z "$lc_bad" ]] && (( lc_gr )) && ! _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason"; then
    lc_bad="--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  if [[ -n "$lc_bad" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"bad-args","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' "$(_json_str "$lc_bad")"
    return 0
  fi

  # Exact arity and flag order, the shape `cmd_ws_hold` (ccd:2524) and
  # `cmd_ws_release` (ccd:2570) use. `[[ ]]` short-circuits, so `$1` is never
  # expanded when `$#` is 0 and `set -u` has nothing to complain about. The
  # positional form this replaces was a MINIMUM-arity guard (`${1:?}`/`${2:?}`)
  # whose usage refusal was bash's rather than ccd's, and it ignored extra argv.
  if [[ $# -ne 4 || $1 != --session || $3 != --branch ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"bad-args","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "usage: ccd ws-rename --session <id> --branch <name>")"
    return 0
  fi
  local id=$2 new=$4
  if [[ ! $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"bad-args","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "bad session id: $id")"
    return 0
  fi

  if [[ ! -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"no-such-session","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "ccrc has no registry entry for $id")"
    return 0
  fi

  # A hold is a program's declaration that this workspace is mid-flight. WAVE 3
  # §3.1 REVERSES this verb's Build 2.5 policy, and the reversal is the point
  # rather than a side effect: `ws-rm`/`ws-reap`/`forget` refuse a held
  # workspace because they DELETE, and this verb only moves a ref — but the ref
  # it moves is the one every ccrc surface reads as the session's NAME
  # (`sessionLabel` is `name ?? branch ?? workspace ?? id`), so renaming
  # mid-wave leaves a coordinator's ledger, its brief and the fleet screen
  # disagreeing about which worker is which. `FleetWatcher.sweepNames` skips a
  # claimed row before it ever calls this; this rung is defence in depth,
  # because the sweep is not the only caller and a hold can land inside its
  # read-then-call window.
  #
  # PLACED HERE: after the argv/id guards (a caller whose argv this function
  # would not accept must not receive a well-formed ANSWER) and before every
  # git and network probe (learning a workspace is off-limits must not cost an
  # `ls-remote`) — the same placement rule `cmd_ws_reap`'s copy of this rung
  # states for itself.
  #
  # `-e` not `-f`, matching the four existing hold readers: an unreadable-but-
  # present hold still refuses, because doubt reads as HELD.
  #
  # THE TOKENS ARE INLINE, per this function's own header: `wsaudit.test.ts`
  # harvests them out of this file's bytes. `held` already has a SENTENCES
  # entry (cmd_ws_reap emits it), so nothing is owed on the server side.
  #
  # The verb's deliberate absence of a BUSY guard is UNCHANGED and stays
  # deliberate: the naming moment is by definition a busy moment.
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
    # D-2378: bounded exactly as `cmd_ws_rm`'s copy of this rung above —
    # `-f`+`-r` checked before the open, so a FIFO/chardev at the hold path
    # cannot block this read, never touching the `-e` gate itself.
    local _hold_reason
    if [[ -f "$REG/$id.hold" && -r "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
      _hold_reason=$(cat "$REG/$id.hold" 2>/dev/null || echo '<unreadable — treat as held>')
    else
      _hold_reason='<unreadable — treat as held>'
    fi
    printf '{"refused":"held","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$_hold_reason — release first: ccd ws-release --session $id")"
    return 0
  fi

  local ws project workdir reg_branch
  ws=$(_reg_get "$id" workspace); project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  reg_branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  # The absence of a workspace field is what distinguishes a main checkout.
  if [[ -z "$ws" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"not-a-workspace","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$id is not a workspace — refusing to rename a main checkout's branch")"
    return 0
  fi
  # `branch` joins `project`/`workdir` here — the same three fields
  # cmd_ws_reap's identity phase requires (ccd:4227-4231). Without it, a row
  # missing `branch` fell through to the registry-branch-drift rung below,
  # which reads $reg_branch unconditionally: `"the registry says , git's
  # worktree record for $workdir says $old"`, a detail with a blank clause
  # standing in for a refusal that actually names the gap.
  if [[ -z "$project" || -z "$workdir" || -z "$reg_branch" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"incomplete-registry","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "incomplete registry for '$id'")"
    return 0
  fi
  # KEPT deliberately, now that the branch no longer has to be read out of the
  # directory. The record does survive a hand-deletion, so this rename WOULD
  # work — but ws-rename exists for a workspace still being worked in, and a
  # vanished directory is a broken one. Renaming would print a success line and
  # say nothing about the breakage, hiding exactly what ws-gc exists to surface;
  # refusing names it, and ws-rm already handles a gone directory deliberately.
  if [[ ! -d "$workdir" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"worktree-missing","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "worktree is gone: $workdir")"
    return 0
  fi
  # NOT re-implemented on the server. This rule has one definition, on the box,
  # and the server learns its verdict from the `bad-branch` token — two
  # implementations of one rule drift, which is what they do.
  if ! _ws_branch_valid "$new"; then
    printf '{"refused":"bad-branch","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "invalid branch name: $new")"
    return 0
  fi

  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  # THE branch source of truth, same as cmd_ws_rm: git's own record for that
  # path, read from $main. Never `rev-parse` inside $workdir — that answers from
  # whatever repository owns the DIRECTORY, and a hand-deletion plus a stray
  # `git init` made that a stranger, whose branch was then read AND renamed.
  # Two statements: with no `set -e`, `local old=$(...)` returns local's status.
  local old registered
  old=$(_ws_wt_branch "$main" "$workdir"); registered=$?
  # Three rungs, because "no record at all" and "recorded detached" are different
  # states with different remedies, and only one of them is about a broken record.
  #   3: no registration at all (a botched hand cleanup that deleted
  #      $main/.git/worktrees/<name>). Nothing corroborates the registry's name
  #      any more, so there is no name to rename — same rule as ws-rm's
  #      uncorroborated case, and the registry field is named, never used.
  #      `git worktree repair` is NOT offered: measured on git 2.43.0 it exits 1
  #      here ("unable to locate repository") — it cannot rebuild a record that
  #      was deleted. Re-adding the worktree can, once the orphaned checkout is
  #      out of the way (measured: rc 0, and no prune needed — no record stands).
  #      ($reg_branch itself was already fetched and checked non-empty above,
  #      alongside project/workdir.)
  if (( registered != 0 )); then
    printf '{"refused":"worktree-unregistered","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "no worktree record for $workdir in $main — nothing renamed; with no registration no branch name is corroborated (the registry says '${reg_branch:-?}', which nothing now ties to that path): move the leftover directory aside, then git -C $main worktree add $workdir ${reg_branch:-<branch>}")"
    return 0
  fi
  #   2: registered, and the registration says `detached`. The remedy is to check
  #      a branch out, not to repair anything.
  if [[ -z "$old" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"detached","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$id is on a detached HEAD — nothing to rename")"
    return 0
  fi
  #   1: registered, with a branch — $old is git's own name for it.
  # ...but a registration outlives its directory, so it can be STALE: delete the
  # directory by hand, put anything else at that path, and git still names it —
  # `registered` is still 0 and $old is still ccrc's branch, so nothing above
  # notices that the workspace this rename is *about* has been taken over. Ask
  # the directory too and require both to say $main, exactly as cmd_ws_rm does.
  # (The stranger is never written to either way, now that both the read and the
  # rename are scoped to $main; what this guard refuses is renaming on behalf of
  # a workspace that no longer exists.) Recovery measured on git 2.43.0: the
  # stale record makes `worktree add` refuse until `prune` clears it, and prune
  # only drops records whose directory is missing — hence both, in that order.
  local wd_common main_common
  wd_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$workdir"); main_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$main")
  if [[ -z "$main_common" || "$wd_common" != "$main_common" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"worktree-foreign","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$workdir is not a worktree of $main — nothing renamed; move or delete the directory by hand, then git -C $main worktree prune && git -C $main worktree add $workdir $old")"
    return 0
  fi
  # $old is git's own record, corroborated above against the DIRECTORY. It is
  # not yet corroborated against the REGISTRY, and the naming sweep's own
  # idempotence check reads the registry's `branch` field, never this
  # function's git-derived `$old` — the two are different sources of truth for
  # the same fact, and this repo already models what happens when they drift: a
  # hand `git branch -m` moves git's answer while the registry keeps the old
  # name (`cmd_ws_rm`'s note-and-proceed, ccd:2952-2955; `cmd_ws_reap` resolves
  # it the same way now and no longer refuses, which is exactly why THIS verb
  # still must — a rename ACTS ON the name, so renaming git's branch off a stale
  # registry entry drives the two records further apart, while a delete simply
  # removes the branch git actually has). Without this
  # check, a workspace hand-renamed outside `ccd ws-rename` still reads as
  # "still on its born name" to the sweep (the registry never moved) while this
  # verb renames whatever git says instead — silently renaming a hand-chosen
  # branch. Same rung `cmd_ws_reap` already refuses on, same token, same
  # remedy: the registry only self-heals through `ws-rename`'s own
  # `_reg_set … branch`, so a human who renamed by hand runs `ccd ws-rename`
  # once by hand to re-corroborate the two.
  # ── RECONCILE — the one call that makes the drift refusal's own remedy true ──
  # `ws-reap`'s note, `pr-state`'s `branch-drift` answer and this verb's own
  # refusal all tell the operator to "run `ccd ws-rename` once by hand to
  # re-corroborate the two". Measured: before this rung, EVERY spelling of that
  # command was refused — `--branch <git's name>`, `--branch <the registry's>`
  # and `--branch <anything else>` all hit the drift refusal below, which runs
  # before `$new` has been used for anything but a validity check, so the
  # registry could not be made to agree with git through any invocation of the
  # verb that names it. A remedy nobody can execute is worse than no remedy: it
  # reads as a step the operator got wrong.
  #
  # THE ONE INVOCATION THIS ACCEPTS is `--branch <the branch git already has>`,
  # which is a RECORD CORRECTION and not a rename: no ref moves, `git` is not
  # asked to do anything, and the value written is the one `_ws_wt_branch` just
  # read out of `$main`'s own worktree record — the same authority `ws-rm` and
  # `ws-reap` delete on. The pushed-branch guard below is deliberately NOT
  # consulted for it: that guard exists because renaming a pushed branch strands
  # its name on the remote, and nothing here renames anything.
  #
  # It answers in this verb's success shape rather than a refusal, because it
  # SUCCEEDED at the thing it was asked to do — with `old` and `new` equal,
  # which is exactly what "the two records now agree" looks like on the wire.
  # `unchanged` is the neighbouring refusal and is NOT the same state: that one
  # means the registry already said this, and this one means it did not.
  if [[ "$old" != "$reg_branch" && "$new" == "$old" ]]; then
    _reg_set "$id" branch "$old"
    printf '{"renamed":%s,"old":%s,"new":%s,"reconciled":true}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$reg_branch")" "$(_json_str "$old")"
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ "$old" != "$reg_branch" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"registry-branch-drift","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "the registry says $reg_branch, git's worktree record for $workdir says $old — refusing to rename on a stale registry entry; to make the two agree, run this verb again with --branch $old, which corrects the registry and moves no ref")"
    return 0
  fi
  if [[ "$old" == "$new" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"unchanged","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "already named $new")"
    return 0
  fi

  # Renaming after a push leaves the old name on the remote and creates a second
  # branch there on the next push. An upstream is ONE kind of evidence that
  # happened. Asked in $main and about $old by name: branch config is shared
  # across a repo's worktrees, so this is the same answer $workdir would give —
  # from the repo that actually owns the branch. Measured on git 2.43.0: rc 0
  # printing `origin/<old>` with an upstream, rc 128 without one.
  #
  # THE load-bearing refusal, and the reason automatic naming is safe to run
  # unattended: a branch that has been pushed is never renamed.
  if git -C "$main" rev-parse --abbrev-ref "$old@{u}" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    printf '{"refused":"has-upstream","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$old has an upstream — it is already on the remote; rename before pushing, not after")"
    return 0
  fi
  # An upstream is only tracking CONFIGURATION, not proof of a push: `git push
  # origin ws/foo` with no `-u` puts the branch on origin and configures
  # nothing, and the `@{u}` probe above answers `fatal: no upstream configured`
  # for exactly that branch. So ask origin directly too, the same probe the
  # `$new` collision check below already uses: rc 0 = $old is already on origin
  # (refuse — this IS the "has been pushed" case, just reached a second way);
  # rc 2 = never pushed, proceed; anything else = unreachable, and unreachable
  # is not the same as pushed, so it stays a warning, not a refusal — the same
  # convention the `$new` probe's unreachable case already uses.
  local old_rc; git -C "$main" ls-remote --exit-code --heads origin "$old" >/dev/null 2>&1; old_rc=$?
  case "$old_rc" in
    0) printf '{"refused":"has-upstream","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
         "$(_json_str "$old is already on origin (pushed without a tracking upstream) — rename before pushing, not after")"
       return 0 ;;
    2) : ;;
    *) echo "warn: could not reach origin to check for '$old' — renaming anyway" >&2 ;;
  esac

  if git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$new"; then
    printf '{"refused":"name-taken-local","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "branch already exists locally: $new")"
    return 0
  fi

  # --exit-code: 0 = the head exists, 2 = it does not, anything else = we could
  # not ask. Unreachable is not the same as taken, and it stays a WARNING: a
  # refusal here would make ws-rename unusable offline for a branch that has
  # never been pushed.
  local rc; git -C "$main" ls-remote --exit-code --heads origin "$new" >/dev/null 2>&1; rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) printf '{"refused":"name-taken-origin","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
         "$(_json_str "branch already exists on origin: $new")"
       return 0 ;;
    2) : ;;
    *) echo "warn: could not reach origin to check for '$new' — renaming anyway" >&2 ;;
  esac

  # Scoped to the repo that owns the branch, and named on both sides so it can
  # never resolve against whatever HEAD $workdir happens to have. Measured on git
  # 2.43.0: this works on a branch checked out in a linked worktree and retargets
  # that worktree's HEAD and its registration with it (before `branch
  # refs/heads/feat/a`, after `branch refs/heads/feat/b`, rc 0) — which is what
  # lets ws-rm read the new name back out of the record afterwards.
  #
  # THE ONE *REFUSAL* PATH THAT KEEPS A NON-ZERO EXIT — the other is the
  # `_json_str probe` at the top of this function (ccd:2263-2264), which is a
  # FAULT for the same reason this is: nothing about either failure is a verdict
  # on the request. This one is a fault, not a refusal: nothing about the
  # request was wrong. The caller must not read this as a REFUSAL ANSWER — it
  # carries no token and reaches no refusalSentence — but the pair IS still
  # marked attempted, like every other non-ok CcdResult.
  #
  # NO BUSY GUARD, deliberately, unlike `ws-archive`'s `session-busy`: when the
  # title this verb's automatic caller derives from lands, the session is busy by
  # definition — it is answering the first prompt. A busy guard would refuse the
  # only moment automatic naming ever fires.
  git -C "$main" branch -m "$old" "$new" || die "rename failed: $old -> $new"
  # AFTER ccd:4114's `git branch -m` succeeded, so `branch` is the name the
  # repository actually carries now, and `old` is what it carried before.
  _lc_done rename "$id" "" meas.old "$old" meas.branch "$new" \
    meas.project "$(_reg_get "$id" project)" \
    dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
  _reg_set "$id" branch "$new"
  printf '{"renamed":%s,"old":%s,"new":%s}\n' \
    "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$old")" "$(_json_str "$new")"
}

cmd_caps() {   # one verb name per line — what THIS copy of ccd implements.
  # ~/.local/bin/ccd is a COPY, not a symlink to the repo, so a verb can pass
  # the agent's whitelist and still not exist on the box. The agent reads this
  # at start and reports it in its ready frame; the server refuses to emit a
  # verb the agent did not advertise. NOT whitelisted — the agent runs it
  # directly, and it is a pure read.
  #
  # It takes no argv, and says so rather than ignoring it. This whole verb exists
  # so a caller can find out what this copy of ccd supports; answering `ccd caps
  # --json` with the plain list — which is what silently discarding argv did —
  # hands that caller a shape it did not ask for and no way to tell. Every other
  # verb here asserts its argv exactly; this one is the one whose whole job is
  # honesty about capability.
  [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || die "usage: ccd caps"
  cat <<'EOF'
account-pane
attach
caps
clip
coord-pause
enable
ensure
forget
list
menu
pick
pr-open
pr-state
prefer
project-pool
route
start
stop
supervise
swap
swap-self
ws-add
ws-archive
ws-attic
ws-audit
ws-gc
ws-hold
ws-reap
ws-release
ws-rename
ws-restore
ws-rm
version
win-size
ls
EOF
  # `stop-surface` is a CAPABILITY token, not a dispatchable verb — `ccd
  # stop-surface` is not a command. It rides this exact channel deliberately
  # (fix round 2, task 14): the agent's caps reader, the wire's `ccdVerbs`
  # list and the server's `verbSupported` lookup already exist, are already
  # proven, and do a plain membership check with no assumption that every
  # member is dispatchable — so a second capability channel would duplicate
  # working plumbing for no reason. Shaped like a verb (lowercase + hyphens)
  # on purpose, so it needs no new parsing anywhere: it passes the agent's
  # existing `/^[a-z][a-z0-9-]*$/` filter unchanged. CAREFUL — that class
  # gained its `0-9` on 2026-08-24 precisely because this sentence was read as
  # licence for the two `-v1` tokens below, and it was not: the class was
  # `[a-z][a-z-]*` then, `stop-surface` passed it because it carries no digit,
  # and BOTH versioned tokens were silently dropped by `parseCcdCaps`. A token
  # added here is only verb-SHAPED if the filter in `shared/agent-protocol.ts`
  # actually keeps it; `server/test/caps-token-shape.test.ts` now crosses every
  # token this function prints through the real parser and is the only place
  # that answers the question. Names the FLAG `cmd_stop`
  # has carried since D3 (spec §4.1): `ccd stop [--surface <word>] <id>`.
  # `server/test/ccd-archive.test.ts`'s caps<->dispatcher parity check knows
  # these tokens by name (`KNOWN_CAPABILITY_TOKENS`, that file's line 154) and
  # holds the advertised set EXACTLY equal to it — a token added here without
  # updating that list fails loudly there, in BOTH directions: :184 sees it as
  # a phantom verb, :189 sees the list short one. (Re-measured 2026-09-12; the
  # three numbers this comment carried before — 153, 175, 180 — had all
  # rotted, and nothing reds when they do.)
  echo stop-surface
  # `lifecycle-v1` says THIS copy of ccd writes `$REG/.lifecycle/`. It gates a
  # SERVER DECISION — sweep at all — not a file: absent means the mirror never
  # sweeps and every surface says "this box's ccd does not write the lifecycle
  # journal", because an old ccd's silence must not read as a quiet fleet.
  # Verb-shaped so it needs no new parsing anywhere, exactly the argument that
  # put `stop-surface` on this channel.
  echo lifecycle-v1
  # `actor-flags-v1` decides ONE server-side thing: whether to APPEND
  # `--surface`/`--actor`/`--reason` to the five workspace verbs. The server
  # answers FALSE on no evidence (`capSupported`, server/src/ccdargv.ts) because
  # the wrong guess here is a SILENT SUCCESS — a ccd without this token meets
  # the flags in an exact-arity guard, and where it does not die, `runCcdOr502`
  # renders exit 0 as `200 {ok:true}` for a call that recorded nothing. Same
  # argument, same polarity, as `stop-surface` above.
  echo actor-flags-v1
  # `account-v1` says THIS copy of ccd carries the account surface: the
  # `account-pane` verb above and the `ccd-account-auth` helper it execs. It
  # gates a SERVER DECISION — whether to offer any account mutation at all —
  # so every wave-2 route answers `501 unsupported` against a box that has not
  # taken this deploy, rather than emitting a verb that box does not have.
  # Same channel, same argument, as the three tokens above; and the same
  # obligation, which is the list named in the comment beside `stop-surface`.
  echo account-v1
  # `pools-v1` says THIS copy of ccd honours project pool tags. What that
  # means AS OF THIS COMMIT, stated so later waves cannot read it as more:
  # it reads `$POOLS_DIR/<project>` (Task 1), and `_pool_ok` (Task 2) is now
  # CALLED — wired into fresh placement (Task 5), from `_ws_least_loaded`
  # and from `cmd_ws_add`'s refusal loop, so a mismatched or undecidable tag
  # can refuse a `ws-add`. Wiring it into the auto-swap candidate loop and
  # the 5-second tick (`_swap_target`, `_auto_swap_check`) LANDED in wave
  # 2b, and so did `--cross-pool`, which now parses on `cmd_swap`,
  # `cmd_start`, `cmd_prefer` and `cmd_enable` (its alias). Measured: `grep -c --
  # '--cross-pool)' ccd/ccd` finds exactly 5 now (this sentence's own
  # quoting of the pattern adds one to that): one `case` arm per verb named
  # above, plus this line, with no other false positive anywhere else in
  # the file. Fix round 1 (Finding 2a) once corrected a prior version of
  # this comment for claiming the last two in the present tense before
  # either existed; this is the same present tense, said again now that
  # wave 2b has actually landed it.
  #
  # THE FLAG'S REACH IS NARROWER THAN THE GUARD'S, and this comment claimed
  # otherwise until merge review (M6): it read "every manual verb the
  # cross-pool guard applies to", which is false. `cmd_swap_self` (`grep -n
  # '^cmd_swap_self()' ccd/ccd`) has no flag loop and no `--cross-pool`
  # pass-through — its whole body resolves the calling pane's own id and
  # tails into `cmd_swap "$id" "$target"` — so the pool guard DOES apply to
  # `ccd swap-self` and cannot be waived from it. That is the in-session and
  # mobile path (the one this file's own header sells as "just tell Claude to
  # run ccd swap-self"), so it is the arm an operator is likeliest to meet
  # the refusal on and least able to override. The arm COUNT above is
  # mechanised and honest; the scope was not. Whether `swap-self` should
  # learn the flag is a wave-3 question, not a capability to claim here.
  #
  # It gates exactly ONE server decision — "may I build a `--cross-pool`
  # argv" (wave 3) — and `capSupported` refuses on no evidence, because the
  # wrong guess there is a SILENT SUCCESS IF THE FLAG WERE TRAILING: a
  # trailing flag is just a fourth positional nobody reads, so an old ccd
  # would silently ignore it and `runCcdOr502` would render exit 0 as
  # `200 {ok:true}` for a crossing that never happened. Spec §5.6 rejects
  # that shape for exactly this reason: `--cross-pool` is specified LEADING,
  # before the positionals, so on an old ccd it lands in a slot an existing
  # check refuses instead (measured per verb by the pins in
  # `ccd-project-pool.test.ts`'s "pools-v1 is safe to advertise" describe —
  # e.g. `swap`'s shifted target failing `_is_valid_wrapper`, or `start`'s
  # `wrapper` becoming the literal flag). Fix round 3 (Finding 9): a prior
  # version of this sentence said "trailing", the opposite of what the
  # position mechanism and every one of those pins actually rely on — this
  # comment and that describe block must agree, since both argue the same
  # safety property. LOUD failure either way (an old ccd dies nonzero,
  # `runCcdOr502` renders a real error), never a silent success. The TAG
  # ROUTE needs no token: the verb and every reader ship in one ccd inode,
  # so `project-pool`'s presence in `ccdVerbs` IS the evidence. Same
  # argument, same polarity, as `actor-flags-v1` above.
  echo pools-v1
  # route-v1 — this ccd implements `ccd route --session <id> --set <field>=<value>`
  # and reads the routing record at every settle (routing spec 2026-09-14
  # §5.3). A server that sees this token may write routing fields through the
  # verb; a server that does not sees no route verb and passes nothing.
  echo route-v1
  # route-argv-v1 — this ccd takes `--route <field>=<value>` on the three verbs
  # that MINT a session (`ws-add`, `start`, `enable`), validates it before
  # anything is created, and places by the class it names. A server that sees
  # this token may compose routing fields onto a dispatch argv; one that does
  # not passes none and journals that it could not.
  echo route-argv-v1
  # route-apply-v1 — this ccd takes `--apply` on `route`, and its supervise tick
  # re-applies a pending routing record with the SESSION-ONLY keystrokes
  # (`/effort` slider + `s`, `/model` picker + `s`), reading the acknowledgement
  # back before recording anything as applied. A server that sees this token may
  # ask for a live change; one that does not writes the record and lets the next
  # settle apply it.
  echo route-apply-v1
  # account-pools — this ccd honours account-side pool membership: `_pool_ok`
  # (above) reads `_acct_pool_state`'s five-word projection, not merely the
  # declared roster name, so an account-side `unreadable`/`stale`/`malformed`
  # now refuses into a tagged project instead of silently serving it. Spec
  # §5.9 originally sourced this from "the verb's presence in `ccd caps`", but
  # ruling R2 removed the verb the design assumed — there is no `ccd
  # account-pools` to dispatch, only a decision `_pool_ok` already makes on
  # every call. Same channel, same argument, as `pools-v1` above: a CAPABILITY
  # token, not a dispatchable verb, so `PoolsEnforcement.accountPools` can
  # answer `enforced` only against a box that actually took this deploy.
  echo account-pools
  # The capability token for wave 2 of the terminal-drawer program, and it is
  # the proof of BOTH halves of that wave rather than of the verb alone: the
  # `win-size` verb and `_pane_measurable`'s stand-down at every typing site
  # ship in ONE ccd inode, so a server that sees this token knows the readers on
  # this box already yield to a narrow pane. The server gates the un-pin on
  # `capSupported(state,'win-size-v1')` — null → REFUSE — never `verbSupported`,
  # whose null → PERMIT default is right for verbs that have always existed and
  # wrong for one that never did. An un-upgraded agent simply leaves the window
  # pinned: wave 1 behaviour, no fallback path, no second mechanism.
  echo win-size-v1
  # child-argv-v1 — `ws-add --child` and a child's own TMPDIR, one inode; argued at `_child_tmpdir`.
  echo child-argv-v1
}

cmd_version() {   # what THIS box runs, per the deploy's own stamp — never a guess.
  # ~/.ccrc/build.json is written by deploy.sh's stamp_build (temp + rename,
  # so no torn reads). No stamp = a dev checkout or a box no deploy touched:
  # "unstamped" at exit 0 is the honest ANSWER there, not an error. Everything
  # else is a genuine fault, and each now gets ITS OWN sentence — the same
  # condition split `ccd/ccrc`'s `_box_build_fields` makes (that function's
  # own header gives the full rationale). A MISSING DEPENDENCY (python3 not
  # on PATH), a WRONG SHAPE (a directory sitting where the stamp belongs), a
  # PARSE FAILURE, a stamp UNREADABLE FOR PERMISSIONS, and a field that does
  # not TYPE-CHECK (`dirty` as a string, say) are conditions an operator acts
  # on completely differently — `apt install python3` vs. redeploy vs.
  # chmod/chown vs. "something wrote garbage" — and used to collapse into one
  # "build stamp unreadable", sending every one of them toward whichever
  # remedy happened to be wrong.
  [[ $# -eq 0 ]] || die "usage: ccd version"
  local stamp="$HOME/.ccrc/build.json"
  if [[ ! -e "$stamp" ]]; then
    echo "ccd unstamped (no ~/.ccrc/build.json — not deployed by deploy.sh)"
    return 0
  fi
  # Exists but is not a plain file (e.g. a directory left by a half-finished
  # rsync/mkdir race) — a SHAPE problem, not an absence, and must not be read
  # as "unstamped" (the identical split `_box_build_fields` makes, ccd/ccrc).
  [[ -f "$stamp" ]] || die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp (not a regular file)"
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "python3 is required by 'ccd version' but is not on PATH"
  local out rc=0
  out="$(python3 - "$stamp" <<'PY'
import json, sys
try:
    data = open(sys.argv[1]).read()
except Exception:
    sys.exit(2)   # exists, is a regular file, still unreadable -> permissions
try:
    o = json.loads(data)
except Exception:
    sys.exit(3)   # not valid JSON
try:
    sha, ref, built, dirty = o["sha"], o["ref"], o["builtAt"], o["dirty"]
except Exception:
    sys.exit(4)   # valid JSON, missing a required field
if not isinstance(dirty, bool):
    sys.exit(5)   # dirty present but not a boolean — a string like "false"
                   # is truthy in Python, and would otherwise print "dirty"
                   # for a stamp that says the opposite (ccrc:240-245).
print(f"ccd {sha} ({ref}, built {built}{', dirty' if dirty else ''})")
PY
)" || rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) ;;
    2) die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp (permission denied)" ;;
    3) die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp (not valid JSON)" ;;
    4) die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp (missing a required field)" ;;
    5) die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp (dirty is not a boolean)" ;;
    *) die "build stamp unreadable: $stamp" ;;
  esac
  echo "$out"
}

cmd_ws_hold() {   # ccd ws-hold --session <id> --reason <text> — claim a
  # workspace for an active program. The ONE input nothing on this box can
  # measure: an idle agent between waves is indistinguishable from a finished
  # one, so the claim is DECLARED, never inferred — and it is what `ws-rm`,
  # `ws-rename`, `ws-reap` and `forget` each refuse on. Non-destructive and
  # idempotent: re-hold updates the reason. The reason string is the display
  # everywhere — write it verbatim, parse it nowhere.
  # WAVE 5 — TWO FLAGS, NOT THREE, AND THAT IS THE DESIGN. `--reason` on this
  # verb is already the HOLD's reason: mandatory, positional (`$4`), and the
  # display on every ccrc surface. Stripping it here would leave the residue at
  # arity 2, refuse every correct call, and make the workspace unholdable; and
  # accepting a SECOND `--reason` would put two different reasons on one verb
  # with nothing to say which one a reader is looking at. Spec D2's `dec.reason`
  # is therefore this same string — one reason, one meaning.
  #
  # Stripped BEFORE the exact-arity rule, `cmd_stop`'s order — and before any
  # positional binding, so a run like `ws-hold --surface pwa --session <id>
  # --reason x` cannot bind `id` from the raw, flag-polluted argv the way
  # Task 46 found `cmd_ws_restore` almost did.
  local lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates.
      --surface)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-hold [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] --session <id> --reason <text>"
                   lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-hold [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] --session <id> --reason <text>"
                   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2, ccd:11089-11090): an empty array here
  # is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop
  # is where that distinction has to be made, before the encoder ever sees the
  # value. `lc_gs` already carries givenness; the `${lc_w:-unknown}` fallback
  # below is what turns a declared-but-blank surface into `unknown` rather
  # than an empty string that `_lc_json` would backfill to `none` — the exact
  # given/no-flag collapse this verb must not produce.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (`ccd:2411-2422`, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''`
  # returns 0, so it cannot be the blank guard on its own. A declared
  # `--actor` is refused when blank the same way this verb's own `--reason`
  # is refused just below (ccd:4758), and the same shape Task 45's
  # `cmd_ws_archive` and Task 46's `cmd_ws_restore` use.
  if (( lc_ga )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--actor must be non-blank"
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" || die "--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  [[ $# -eq 4 && $1 == --session && $3 == --reason ]] \
    || die "usage: ccd ws-hold --session <id> --reason <text>"
  local id=$2 reason=$4
  # `dec.reason` IS the hold reason — see the note above. Named separately so
  # Task 49's emit site reads ONE variable on every verb rather than a special
  # case here, and so a future editor cannot "add the missing --reason".
  local lc_reason=$reason
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]]       || die "no such session: $id"
  # An empty reason is an orphan hold by construction — nobody could ever say
  # why it exists, and "visible on every surface, reason says why" is the whole
  # deal that makes no-expiry acceptable.
  #
  # WHITESPACE COUNTS AS EMPTY — fix-wave finding 9. `[[ -n "$reason" ]]` alone
  # passed `--reason "   "`, and the layers disagreed about what that meant:
  # the route (`body.reason.trim() === ''`) and the PWA (`holdReason.trim()`)
  # both refuse it, `registry.ts`'s `field()` trims what it reads, and `'' ??`
  # does not short-circuit — so the hold landed as `held: ''`, enforced by
  # every consumer and rendered as nothing at all on every surface. The
  # orchestrator path is the primary one here (spec lifecycle step 1 is an
  # agent invoking ccd directly), so ccd is where it has to be caught. The
  # expansion strips every whitespace character rather than trimming the ends:
  # a reason of one tab is as unsayable as a reason of one space, and an
  # ordinary reason (`program:x wave:1/4`) still has plenty left after it.
  [[ -n "${reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "empty reason — say which program holds this"
  [[ -n "$(_reg_get "$id" workspace)" ]] \
    || die "$id is not a workspace — a hold gates workspace verbs, so a hold on a main checkout gates nothing and is a lie waiting to confuse someone"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" ]] \
    && die "$id is archived — restore first: a hold cannot protect a pane that is already gone"
  # CHECKED, unlike every other `_reg_set` in this file, because this is the one
  # field whose silent absence is destructive. ccd runs `set -uo pipefail` with
  # NO `-e`, so an unchecked failed write would fall straight through to the
  # echo below. Left unprotected, the orchestrator protocol reads exit code +
  # stdout, records the workspace as claimed, and dispatches the next wave;
  # every rung that reads `.hold` straight off disk — `ws-rm`, `ws-rename`,
  # `ws-reap`, `forget` — then sees none and treats a mid-wave workspace as
  # free the moment anyone reaches for one of them.
  # So the ONE verb whose failure means UNPROTECTED refuses like every other
  # doubt path in this function rather than announcing a success it did not have.
  #
  # `_reg_set` writes a tmp and renames it over the destination, and on EVERY
  # failure path it removes its own tmp and returns non-zero — so a failed
  # hold leaves no NEW or PARTIAL `.hold`: none exists if none did before,
  # and any PRE-EXISTING `.hold` is left byte-intact (the rename that would
  # have replaced it never happens). A partial or empty `.hold` is not a
  # state this mechanism can produce any more. The `|| die` below is what
  # turns that non-zero into a named refusal instead of a false success on
  # the echo below — it is not a weaker guard than before, it is the same
  # guard over a narrower failure set.
  #
  # RE-MEASURED FOR THE SHIPPED MECHANISM: `chmod 500 "$REG"` now fails at TMP
  # CREATE — bash reports the failed redirection before `_reg_set`'s own
  # `2>/dev/null` ever applies to it, so `Permission denied` still reaches
  # stderr — `_reg_set` returns 1, and `die` fires: no stdout, no `.hold` file,
  # exit 1. (PRE-WAVE, for contrast: `Permission denied` on stderr, `held <id>:
  # <reason>` on stdout, exit 0, no `.hold` file — the false success this
  # guard exists to refuse.) `ccd-hold.test.ts`'s own stand-in for "$REG"
  # unwritable is a DIRECTORY at `$REG/<id>.hold` — `chmod` does not constrain
  # root, a directory does — and it is `mv -fT`'s `-T` that keeps THAT case a
  # refusal too: a bare `mv -f` would move the tmp inside the directory and
  # exit 0.
  # The reason is DECLARED (`dec`), never measured: ccd:4256-4257's contract is
  # "write it verbatim, parse it nowhere", and the journal keeps that promise.
  # `_lc_json` quotes it with `json.dumps`, so it cannot forge a field however
  # it is spelled.
  _lc_done hold "$id" "" dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
  _reg_set "$id" hold "$reason" \
    || die "could not record the hold for $id — it is NOT held"
  echo "held $id: $reason"
}

cmd_ws_release() {   # ccd ws-release --session <id> — end the program's claim.
  # A release ends THIS file's claim and nothing else. It does NOT mean nobody
  # still considers this workspace claimed: since Build 8 Wave 2 the server's
  # coord.db separately records whether an OPEN RUN still names this session,
  # and `POST /api/sessions/:id/archive` refuses on that alone — an absent hold
  # has never been sufficient there. Idempotent.
  # WAVE 5 — THE DEC FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE, and that order
  # is the whole correctness argument, exactly as `cmd_stop` states it in its
  # own header (grep `FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE`): this verb's
  # guard is EXACT arity on `--session <id>`, so a flag left in argv is not a
  # wider call, it is a REFUSED one — `ccd ws-release --session x --surface pwa`
  # would die in the usage check while the server reads the non-zero as "the
  # release failed on the box".
  #
  # THREE `given` FLAGS, not three value tests. `--surface ''` and no
  # `--surface` at all are two different facts: nobody declared a surface
  # (`none`), versus a caller declared a word this ccd does not know
  # (`unknown`). Testing `[[ -n "$lc_surface" ]]` instead would collapse them,
  # which is `ccd:676-689`'s `${2-ccd}` vs `${2:-ccd}` distinction broken in a new
  # place. Same for `--actor ''` and `--reason ''`, which are REFUSED rather
  # than silently read as absent — one policy, everywhere (AUDIT B5).
  local lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_reason='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_gr=0 lc_args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates (`cmd_stop` carries the
      # same three lines over the same hazard).
      --surface)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-release [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-release [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      --reason)    [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-release [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_gr=1; lc_reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason=*)  lc_gr=1; lc_reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2, ccd:11089-11090): an empty array here
  # is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop
  # is where that distinction has to be made, before the encoder ever sees the
  # value. `lc_gs` already carries givenness; the `${w:-unknown}` fallback
  # below is what turns a declared-but-blank surface into `unknown` rather
  # than an empty string that `_lc_json` would backfill to `none` — the exact
  # given/no-flag collapse this verb must not produce.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (`ccd:2411-2422`, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''`
  # returns 0, so it cannot be the blank guard on its own. A declared
  # `--actor`/`--reason` is refused when blank the same way `cmd_ws_hold`
  # refuses a blank `--reason` (ccd:4758): its own non-blank check, separate
  # from the byte cap, whitespace-only counting as blank too.
  if (( lc_ga )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--actor must be non-blank"
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" || die "--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  if (( lc_gr )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--reason must be non-blank"
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason" || die "--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  [[ $# -eq 2 && $1 == --session ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-release --session <id>"
  local id=$2
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]]       || die "no such session: $id"
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
    # CHECKED, for `cmd_ws_hold`'s reason with the polarity reversed — fix-wave
    # findings 2/7. `rm -f` suppresses ENOENT ONLY; EACCES and EISDIR still fail
    # and still exit non-zero, and ccd runs `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so
    # the unchecked form fell straight through to the echo. Measured both:
    # `chmod 500 "$REG"` -> `Permission denied` on stderr, `released <id>` on
    # stdout, exit 0, hold still standing; `mkdir "$REG/$id.hold"` (which `-e`
    # matches, so every consumer already reads the workspace as held) ->
    # `Is a directory`, same false success. Downstream, `runCcdOr502` keys on
    # the exit code, so `POST /release` answered 200 `{ok:true}` and the PWA's
    # fire-and-forget confirm closed with no toast: the operator is told the
    # claim is gone while `ws-rm`/`ws-rename`/`ws-reap`/`forget` keep refusing
    # and the server's merged push keeps naming the hold. Fail-shut in the safe
    # direction, but a lie either way, and the sibling verb's own guard is the
    # standard this file set.
    # INSIDE the `-e` arm above, so a `not held` answer writes nothing: an
    # idempotent no-op is not an act, and recording one would make the record
    # disagree with what happened. Read BEFORE the unlink, which is the only
    # instant the text exists.
    #
    # D-2378: bounded, but NOT like this rung's four siblings — this is the
    # one read with no `|| echo` fallback, because its polarity is REVERSED
    # (this verb DELETES the hold; the four siblings REFUSE on one). A FIFO or
    # a symlink to an unbounded character device here used to block `cat`
    # forever; `-f`+`-r` checked before the open (`_project_pool_state`'s
    # pattern) now decides without opening. The marker text is deliberately
    # its OWN, never `<unreadable — treat as held>`: that sentence is a
    # refusal instruction, and this is a RELEASE record, the verb succeeding
    # at clearing the very hold in question — and never empty either, per
    # this function's own argument in the paragraph IMMEDIATELY ABOVE (one
    # blank-comment-line separator, not "two paragraphs up" as an earlier
    # revision of this comment said) that a record must not disagree with
    # what happened. And empty is not just undesirable here, it is not
    # REPRESENTABLE on this wire at all: `_lc_json`'s own encoder drops any
    # key whose value is `""` before it ever reaches the line (an empty
    # `meas.held` would not write a disagreeing value, it would write NO
    # `held` key whatsoever) — which is why a marker, not emptiness, is the
    # only way this record can carry the distinction. (Measured: nothing in
    # this repo parses `meas.held` by VALUE — `journalparse.ts`'s
    # `reviveMeas` reads it as an opaque string, and `store.ts` only
    # JSON-stringifies the whole `meas` object — so a new token here breaks
    # no reader.)
    local _hold_reason
    if [[ -f "$REG/$id.hold" && -r "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
      _hold_reason=$(cat "$REG/$id.hold" 2>/dev/null)
    else
      _hold_reason='<unreadable — hold present but could not be read at release>'
    fi
    _lc_done release "$id" "" meas.held "$_hold_reason" \
      dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
    rm -f -- "$REG/$id.hold" \
      || die "could not remove the hold for $id — it is STILL held"
    echo "released $id"
  else
    echo "not held $id"
  fi
}

cmd_coord_pause() {   # ccd coord-pause --state on|off — raise or lower the
  # coordinator's pause marker. `$REG/coordinator-paused` is read by the
  # server's dispatch path (`coord/dispatch.ts`) and by nothing else, and it
  # is the ONE file that stops a program mid-flight. It had no writer in this
  # tree at all: the operator was expected to `touch`/`rm` it by hand over
  # ssh, which is not a control a phone can reach.
  #
  # Non-destructive and idempotent, exactly like `ws-hold`/`ws-release`, and
  # granted on the same argument: a registry-file write/unlink that widens
  # nothing that deletes. CHECKED on both arms, for `cmd_ws_hold`'s reason:
  # ccd runs `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so an unchecked write that
  # failed would fall straight through to the echo and report a pause that is
  # not there — the polarity that matters most on this verb, since the caller
  # then believes the fleet is stopped.
  #
  # The echoed word is for a human reading a journal. NOTHING parses it: the
  # server learns the state by listing $REG, the same way `dispatchRun` always
  # has (`dispatch.ts:106-109`), and for the reason that file's own comment
  # gives about ccd's echoed sentences.
  [[ $# -eq 2 && $1 == --state ]] || die "usage: ccd coord-pause --state on|off"
  local state=$2
  [[ $state == on || $state == off ]] || die "bad state: $state (want on|off)"
  [[ -d "$REG" ]] || die "no registry at $REG"
  local marker="$REG/coordinator-paused"
  if [[ $state == on ]]; then
    touch -- "$marker" || die "could not raise the pause marker — the fleet is NOT paused"
    echo "paused"
  else
    if [[ -e $marker ]]; then
      rm -f -- "$marker" || die "could not clear the pause marker — the fleet is STILL paused"
    fi
    echo "running"
  fi
}

cmd_win_size() {   # ccd win-size --session <id> --mode smallest|canonical — pin or un-pin ONE session's window.
  # THE FLEET-CONTROL VERB the terminal drawer un-pins through (spec §6.1). Two
  # modes and nothing else:
  #   • smallest  — `set-option window-size smallest` UN-LATCHES `manual` (F3);
  #     tmux then sizes the window to the NARROWEST attached client (F5):
  #     deterministic, keystroke-stable, and self-healing when that client
  #     leaves. NEVER `latest` — F4 measured a keystroke-driven reflow flip at
  #     +6 MB tmux RSS per flip, never returned, shedding 50 history lines per
  #     flip from about the seventeenth. THAT IS THIS VERB'S RULE, NOT THIS
  #     FILE'S, and the two read as one without this clause: `_spawn_start`
  #     sets `window-size latest` on every session it creates (the only other
  #     `window-size` write in this file — measured), and the server's
  #     `resize-window` pin then overrides it. What is refused here is reaching
  #     back for `latest` when UN-pinning a window that was pinned.
  #   • canonical — `resize-window -x 220 -y 50` restores the 220x50 grid
  #     `_spawn_start` creates with (`_tmux_new_session … -x 220 -y 50`) and
  #     latches `window-size manual` (F3), which IS the pinned state §3 wants.
  #     THAT LATCH IS THE SERVER'S PIN, NOT SPAWN'S STATE, and this comment said
  #     otherwise until fix round 1: `_spawn_start` follows its 220x50 create
  #     with `set-option … window-size latest`, so what this arm restores is the
  #     state `exec.ts`'s `resize-window` leaves, not the state a fresh session
  #     is in. The 220x50 half was always true; the `manual` half was not.
  #     The server does NOT depend on this arm: its own pin is the already-granted
  #     `tmux resize-window`, which works against an agent of any age. Its callers
  #     are an operator typing the verb on the box and the agent's `win-size`
  #     grant — NOT ccd itself, which is what this line claimed until fix round 2.
  #     Measured 2026-09-16: `grep -n 'cmd_win_size' ccd/ccd` finds three lines —
  #     this definition, the dispatcher's own `win-size)` arm, and this
  #     sentence's own quotation of the command — and no fourth. No ccd
  #     function calls it.
  #
  # EXACT-MATCH TARGETS (`=`), NEVER A BARE `cc-<id>`, AND NEVER GLOBAL. Two
  # separate hazards, and the first one convicted the first version of this verb
  # (D-2780). Both measured on tmux 3.4 over a PRIVATE `-L` socket carrying
  # `cc-demo-quiet-basin` and `cc-other-session`:
  #   • `-t` IS AN fnmatch PATTERN resolved to a unique match, not a session
  #     name. `has-session -t cc-demo` -> rc 0; `set-option -t cc-demo
  #     window-size smallest` -> rc 0 and `cc-demo-quiet-basin` takes it;
  #     `resize-window -t cc-demo -x 200 -y 40` -> rc 0 and that real session
  #     becomes 200x40; `-t 'cc-oth*'` -> rc 0 and `cc-other-session` takes it.
  #     THE SHAPE, AND DELIBERATELY NO NAMES: live session ids on this fleet
  #     share long prefixes and differ only in a final syllable, so an id that
  #     names NO session is routinely a unique PREFIX of one that is live —
  #     which is the whole hazard. (An earlier draft gave a concrete pair here
  #     as a live-box measurement. It was fabricated and wrapper-shaped, not
  #     session-shaped, and this file ships publicly: this illustration carries
  #     no session or account name, because a public-bound file should not
  #     embed the fleet's roster — not a claim that no shipped source ever
  #     does (`_gpt_enabled() { _account_ok gpt; }` names one, measured).) So
  #     the liveness refusal below CANNOT FIRE for a well-formed id that is a
  #     unique prefix of a live session, and the verb would mutate a window
  #     nobody named. The id class does not help: the hazard is built from the
  #     characters it already admits.
  #   • `set-option` with no `-t` AT ALL mutates ONE WINDOW NOBODY NAMED —
  #     whichever session tmux calls current. The claim that stood here was that
  #     it "writes the SERVER-WIDE default and re-sizes every live session at
  #     once"; both halves are false. Re-measured 2026-09-16, tmux 3.4, private
  #     `-L` socket, three detached sessions: `tmux set-option window-size
  #     smallest` → rc 0; `show-options -g window-size` STILL reads `latest`, so
  #     the server default is NOT written; the two other sessions keep
  #     `window-size latest`; and NO session is resized. Exactly one window took
  #     the option. That is the same class the `=` anchor above closes — a
  #     mutation of a target nobody named — which is reason enough to always
  #     pass `-t`, and it is the reason this verb states rather than the one it
  #     used to.
  # `=` is tmux's exact-match prefix and closes the first: `=cc-demo` -> rc 1
  # `can't find session`, `=cc-demo-quiet-basin` -> rc 0, `=cc-oth*:` -> rc 1
  # `no such window`.
  #
  # THE TRAILING `:` IS LOAD-BEARING, NOT COSMETIC. The sentence this replaces
  # said `resize-window` "accepts both forms" and that the colon was spelled
  # "anyway", for tidiness. That was measured against an EXACT name, where both
  # spellings are indeed safe — and it is exactly the case D-2780 is not about.
  # Re-measured 2026-09-16 against a PREFIX, tmux 3.4, private `-L` socket
  # holding ONLY `cc-demo-quiet-basin`:
  #
  #     target                      has-session  set-option         resize-window
  #     `=cc-demo`   (prefix)       rc 1         rc 1 no such win   rc 0  <- RESIZED cc-demo-quiet-basin
  #     `=cc-demo:`  (prefix)       rc 1         rc 1 no such win   rc 1 can't find session
  #     `=cc-demo-quiet-basin`      rc 0         rc 1 no such win   rc 0
  #     `=cc-demo-quiet-basin:`     rc 0         rc 0               rc 0
  #
  # For a WINDOW target, `=` only binds when the colon is present. Drop the
  # colon from the `resize-window` arm below and D-2780 is LIVE AGAIN: a
  # validated id that is a unique prefix of a live session resizes THAT session,
  # at rc 0, silently. `set-option` needs the colon against an exact name too
  # (row 3), so the colon is not optional on either mutating arm — it is the
  # anchor, not its spelling. `has-session` takes a SESSION target and gets
  # `=$t` with no colon; it is the one command for which `=` alone binds.
  #
  # `aggressive-resize` is deliberately left alone — measured a no-op for a
  # one-window session, which every cc-* session is.
  [[ $# -eq 4 && $1 == --session && $3 == --mode ]] \
    || die "usage: ccd win-size --session <id> --mode smallest|canonical"
  local id="$2" mode="$4" t
  # BEFORE ANY TARGET IS BUILT FROM IT. `cc-$id` is handed to tmux, which hands
  # the target on to its own parser; `shared/roster.ts`'s ID_RE is where every
  # rostered id comes from, and it is the class `cmd_account_pane` validates
  # against for this same reason. NOT the looser `^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$` most
  # workspace verbs use: this one is reached from a route the PWA hits.
  #
  # WHAT IT EXCLUDES IS A TMUX TARGET ALPHABET, not just a shell one, and no
  # part of this verb passes through a shell. Measured on the same private
  # socket: `*` and `?` are fnmatch (`-t 'cc-oth*'` and `-t 'cc-oth?r-session'`
  # both rc 0), `:` is session:window (`-t cc-other-session:0` rc 0), `.` is
  # window.pane, and `=`/`$`/`@`/`%` are the exact-match and session/window/pane
  # id sigils (`-t '$0'` rc 0). An allow-list refuses them wherever they appear,
  # which is stronger than the positions each is actually special in.
  [[ "$id" =~ ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$ ]] || die "bad id: $id"
  # The vocabulary check runs BEFORE tmux is touched at all, so an unknown mode
  # runs nothing — and it is its OWN sentence, not the usage line: the caller
  # got the shape right and the word wrong.
  case "$mode" in
    smallest|canonical) ;;
    *) die "bad mode: $mode (want smallest|canonical)" ;;
  esac
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  tmux has-session -t "=$t" 2>/dev/null || die "no such session: $t"
  # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`: an unguarded failure would fall straight
  # through to the echo and report a size change that did not happen. Wave 3's
  # `sizer.unpin` keys on this exit code to tell 'ok' from 'failed'.
  if [[ "$mode" == smallest ]]; then
    tmux set-option -t "=$t:" window-size smallest \
      || die "could not un-pin $t — the window is STILL at its pinned size"
    echo "smallest"
  else
    tmux resize-window -t "=$t:" -x 220 -y 50 \
      || die "could not pin $t — the window is NOT at the canonical grid"
    echo "canonical"
  fi
}

cmd_account_pane() {   # ccd account-pane --id <id> [--method setup-token|openai-login] [--cancel]
  # THE ONE DETACHED-PROCESS VEHICLE THE WHITELIST CAN CREATE (spec §6). Only
  # ccd may make a tmux session — `EXEC_WHITELIST.tmux` grants has-session,
  # list-panes, capture-pane, send-keys and resize-window and nothing else
  # (agent/src/whitelist.ts) — so a `claude setup-token` mint, which is an Ink
  # full-screen TUI that produces ZERO BYTES without a tty (measured
  # 2026-09-07, CC 2.1.263), has no home without this verb.
  #
  # `_tmux_new_session`, NEVER `_spawn`. Two separate reasons, and both matter:
  # `_spawn_start` reads wrapper/workdir/uuid out of the registry and dies with
  # `incomplete registry for '<id>'` for an id that names an ACCOUNT rather
  # than a session; and `_spawn_settle` drives `_accept_first_run_prompts`,
  # which presses Down and Enter into the pane. A sign-in a robot answers is a
  # sign-in that was dismissed. Through `_tmux_new_session` the pane also lands
  # in ccrc-tmux-server.scope like every other, which is the whole argument
  # that function's own header makes.
  #
  # FLAG-ANCHORED, exactly as `cmd_coord_pause` above: `--id` is not a
  # confirmation token, it is the verb's whole argument surface, and a
  # one-token grant would permit every positional form it might ever grow.
  # Wave 2 enrols it in the agent's REQUIRED_VERB_FLAG so that dropping the
  # flag from the GRANT is a boot refusal; this line is the half that holds
  # on a box, where there is no whitelist at all (D-1865).
  [[ $# -ge 2 && $1 == --id ]] || die "usage: ccd account-pane --id <id> [--method setup-token|openai-login] [--cancel]"
  local id="$2"; shift 2
  # BEFORE ANY PATH OR PANE NAME IS BUILT FROM IT, and this is the tree's own
  # stated rule rather than a local nicety — the R-3 block in `_ws_common_dir`
  # ("a dot-leading project aliases EVERY `$REG/.<name>.<dot-free-suffix>`
  # file ccd owns") is the same argument about the same class of value. The id
  # is about to be single-quoted into a command string tmux hands to a shell.
  # The class is `shared/roster.ts`'s ID_RE, which is where every rostered id
  # comes from.
  [[ "$id" =~ ^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$ ]] || die "bad id: $id"
  local method='' cancel=0
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates (cmd_ws_archive carries
      # the same three lines over the same hazard).
      --method) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd account-pane --id <id> [--method setup-token|openai-login] [--cancel]"
                method="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --cancel) cancel=1; shift ;;
      *) die "unknown flag: $1" ;;
    esac
  done
  _is_valid_wrapper "$id" || die "unknown-account: $id (no roster entry claims it)"
  local pane; pane="cc-auth-$id"
  if (( cancel )); then
    # `null`, not a refusal: "there is no pane" is the state --cancel exists to
    # reach, so reaching it is success. The caller that wants to know whether it
    # killed anything reads the field.
    if tmux has-session -t "$pane" 2>/dev/null; then
      tmux kill-session -t "$pane" || die "could not kill $pane"
      printf '{"cancelled":%s}\n' "$(_json_str "$pane")"
    else
      printf '{"cancelled":null}\n'
    fi
    return 0
  fi
  case "$method" in
    setup-token|openai-login) ;;
    '') die "usage: ccd account-pane --id <id> [--method setup-token|openai-login] [--cancel]" ;;
    # `login` is deliberately absent: it drives a plain pipe with no pane and
    # no pty (measured), so asking for a pane to run it in is a request this
    # verb should refuse rather than quietly honour.
    *) die "bad-method: $method (want setup-token|openai-login; login needs no pane)" ;;
  esac
  # IDEMPOTENT BY MEASUREMENT, not by hope. `has-session` conflates "gone",
  # "no server" and "no socket" into rc=1 (the four-row measurement beside
  # `_tmux`), so this check can be wrong in the direction of trying again —
  # and tmux itself is the backstop there: `new-session -d -s <name>` on a
  # live name exits non-zero with `duplicate session`, which the `||` below
  # turns into a refusal rather than a second helper.
  ! tmux has-session -t "$pane" 2>/dev/null \
    || die "auth-in-progress: $pane is already running (ccd account-pane --id $id --cancel to stop it)"
  _tmux_new_session -d -s "$pane" -x 120 -y 40 \
    "exec '$HOME/.local/bin/ccd-account-auth' '$id' '$method'" \
    || die "could not create $pane"
  # `_json_str` for both, though both are already constrained — the id by the
  # regex above and the method by the `case` — because the tree's rule is that
  # a JSON string literal is produced by the one encoder, not by a printf whose
  # safety is an argument in a comment.
  printf '{"pane":%s,"method":%s}\n' "$(_json_str "$pane")" "$(_json_str "$method")"
}

cmd_route() {   # ccd route --session <id> --set <field>=<value> [--set ...] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>]
  # Routing spec 2026-09-14 §5.3. WRITES THE RECORD; TYPES ONLY UNDER `--apply`.
  # Without that flag — the COORDINATOR's form, and the default — a session
  # running this against a worker types nothing into that worker's pane and ccd
  # applies the fields at the next settle. With it, the record is written first
  # and then applied to the LIVE pane with the session-only keystrokes, if and
  # only if the pane answers the same idle predicate the compactor uses; a pane
  # that is mid-turn, drafting or sitting out a limit is left alone, the refusal
  # is recorded in `routeskip`, the verb says `queued`, and the supervise tick
  # retries. Every value is
  # validated BEFORE anything is written, so a bad call is a `die` the caller
  # sees and an unchanged record, never a silently-absent field. The PREVIOUS
  # value is read VALIDATED: an unrecognised one is rendered as a byte count in
  # the log and the journal, never its bytes — the file is writable by any
  # session on the box and a multi-line value would forge log lines.
  local usage="usage: ccd route --session <id> --set <field>=<value> [--set <field>=<value>]... [--apply] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>]"
  local id="" actor="" reason="" apply=0 kv f v cls eff old raw rc inert
  local -a sets=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      --session) [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "$usage"; id="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --set)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "$usage"; sets+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
      --apply)   apply=1; shift ;;
      --actor)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "$usage"; actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason)  [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "$usage"; reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      *)         die "$usage" ;;
    esac
  done
  # --actor and --reason are free-form caller text that reaches swap.log
  # verbatim (the journal arm is safe — `_lc_json` JSON-encodes); refuse any
  # control character in either so a newline can never forge a second
  # `route …` line (controller ruling S1-R4). Dies before anything is written.
  case "$actor$reason" in
    *[[:cntrl:]]*) die "route: --actor/--reason must not contain control characters" ;;
  esac
  [[ -n "$id" && ${#sets[@]} -gt 0 ]] || die "$usage"
  [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "no such session: $id"
  for kv in "${sets[@]}"; do
    [[ "$kv" == *=* ]] || die "bad --set '$kv' (want <field>=<value>)"
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    _route_word_in "$f" "${ROUTE_FIELDS[*]}" || die "unknown routing field '$f' (want one of: ${ROUTE_FIELDS[*]})"
    [[ "$f" == degraded || "$f" == inert ]] && die "'$f' is written by ccd only"
    # THREE ANSWERS, THREE SENTENCES (fix round 2, Finding 1). `_route_valid`
    # answers 2 when the field's vocabulary itself could not be read — today
    # only `subagent`, whose list is PROJECTED into `$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh`
    # by `ccrc install` — and that is not the caller's value being wrong, it is
    # this box not being able to say. Both refuse (fail closed); they name
    # different remedies, and folding them into "bad value" told an operator
    # with a perfectly good `haiku` to go and fix `haiku`. The `*)` arm takes
    # rc 1 and any rc a future vocabulary arm invents — unknown is bad value,
    # the fail-closed side.
    _route_valid "$f" "$v"; rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
      0) ;;
      2) die "route: this box's roster projection carries no subagent class list — re-run ccrc install" ;;
      *) die "bad value for $f ($(_route_bytes "$v") bytes) — not in its vocabulary" ;;
    esac
  done
  # Haiku takes no effort level: the PAIR is refused whether it arrives in one
  # call or across two (the record on disk is half of the pair).
  # `_route_peek`, not `_route_get`: this read only DECIDES, and the decision it
  # may reach is a `die` that writes nothing at all. Reading through `_route_get`
  # left a `route-reject` line and a floor marker behind on a refused call that
  # changed nothing — and, worse, burned the field's note floor so the NEXT real
  # spawn stayed silent about the same bad value (fix round 2, minor M2).
  cls=$(_route_peek "$id" class); eff=$(_route_peek "$id" effort)
  for kv in "${sets[@]}"; do
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    [[ "$f" == class ]] && cls="$v"
    [[ "$f" == effort ]] && eff="$v"
  done
  # `auto` types nothing (spec §5.1: it is the ABSENT-equivalent), so
  # haiku+auto is not the refused pair — only haiku+a LEVEL is (controller
  # ruling S1-R5).
  [[ "$cls" == haiku && -n "$eff" && "$eff" != auto ]] && die "class haiku takes no effort level — set effort=auto or pick another class"
  # THE STAMP FIRST, AND IT IS STILL BEFORE EVERY SIDE EFFECT THAT MATTERS: the
  # last `die` is above this line, so a refused call leaves the record — and the
  # stamp — exactly as it found them. What this writes is what the LAST SETTLE
  # composed, so the fields written below are a divergence the applier types
  # rather than one a tick swallows (`_route_apply_seed`'s own header).
  _route_applied_before_write "$id"
  for kv in "${sets[@]}"; do
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    # NO `|| raw=""`: `_reg_get` prints nothing on every failing arm, so the
    # fallback assigned what the capture had already produced while making this
    # line read as though it branched on an exit code it never used
    # (`ccd-crosspool.test.ts` enumerates the two sites that really do).
    raw=$(_reg_get "$id" "$f" 2>/dev/null)
    old=$(_route_get "$id" "$f")
    if [[ -n "$raw" && -z "$old" ]]; then old="<$(_route_bytes "$raw") bytes, unrecognised>"; fi
    _reg_set "$id" "$f" "$v" || die "could not write $f for $id — the record is unchanged from this field on"
    _lc_done route "$id" "" dec.actor "$actor" dec.reason "$reason" detail "$f: ${old:-∅} -> $v"
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') route $id: $f ${old:-∅} -> $v${actor:+ [actor=$actor]}${reason:+ ($reason)}" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    echo "set $id $f=$v"
    # A `degraded` STAMP IS ABOUT THE CLASS IT REPLACED, so a new class may void
    # it — one reader for that decision, `_route_stale_degrade_clear`.
    if [[ "$f" == class ]]; then _route_stale_degrade_clear "$id" "$v"; fi
  done
  # THE OPERATOR ASKED FOR SOMETHING ELSE, SO THE BUDGET RESETS (ruling S4-R7).
  # `routetries` counts attempts at ONE pending field; a write to this session
  # changes what is pending — or re-states it deliberately, which is an operator
  # saying "try again" — so a counter measured against the old record may not
  # keep the new one from ever reaching the pane. Before the `--apply` gate, not
  # after: the coordinator's plain form writes the record the tick will apply,
  # and it must not inherit a spent budget either.
  _route_tries_clear "$id"
  (( apply )) || return 0
  # THE SETTLE-APPLIED FIELDS ARE RECORDED AT WRITE TIME. `workflow`, `subagent`
  # and `compact` have no keystroke — they are an argv `--settings` key, an env
  # var and a field the compactor reads — so recording them here is not a claim
  # that a pane was touched; it is the honest statement that the applier has
  # nothing left to do about them. Without it a `--apply` that wrote only one of
  # the three would leave this session pending for ever against a keystroke that
  # does not exist. Gated on `--apply` because a plain `cmd_route` promises to
  # touch nothing but the record.
  #
  # AND A FIELD THE `inert` STAMP NAMES IS NOT CLAIMED HERE EITHER (controller
  # ruling S6-R3). This is the THIRD `routeapplied` writer — the spawn's
  # composition and `_route_apply_seed` are the other two, and both already read
  # the stamp before they claim a field. Unguarded, a `--apply` naming
  # `subagent` or `workflow` on a session the last settle stamped
  # `inert=subagent`/`inert=workflow` wrote `routeapplied … subagent=haiku`
  # beside that stamp: two records of one session contradicting each other —
  # `inert` saying this lane composed no `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`,
  # `routeapplied` saying it did — until the next settle rewrote both. The
  # sentence above stays true either way ("the applier has nothing left to do"),
  # but silence and "applied" are two different reasons for it and the record may
  # not collapse them.
  #
  # `compact` IS NEVER INERT — `ROUTE_INERTABLE` has no word for it, because it
  # is a threshold ccd's own compactor reads rather than anything a lane composes
  # — so its arm asks nothing and stays as it was.
  #
  # THE STAMP IS READ ONCE, through `_route_peek` (this loop writes no journal
  # line of its own for the read, and the field is being read only to DECIDE) and
  # through `_route_inert_has`, the one reader of the `inert` vocabulary.
  inert=$(_route_peek "$id" inert)
  for kv in "${sets[@]}"; do
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    case "$f" in
      workflow|subagent) _route_inert_has "$inert" "$f" || _route_applied_set "$id" "$f" "$v" ;;
      compact)           _route_applied_set "$id" "$f" "$v" ;;
    esac
  done
  # THE SAME PREDICATE THE COMPACTOR USES, and it is asked BEFORE any keystroke
  # for the same three reasons that function gives: a mid-turn pane would take
  # the slash command as a message, an occupied input box would swallow it, and
  # a session sitting out a usage limit on its own loses that wait to ANY key
  # (D-2229). `queued` is the honest answer in every one of those cases — the
  # record is written, the tick will retry, and nothing was typed.
  #
  # THE COMPACTOR'S KEYSTROKE IS ASKED ABOUT FIRST, and from this process too:
  # a supervisor tick may have sent `/compact` into this very pane a second ago
  # and the idle predicate cannot see it yet (`_route_compact_settling`).
  if _route_compact_settling "$id"; then
    _route_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"
    echo "queued $id"
  elif _pane_for_keystroke "$id" && _idle_for_keystroke "$id" "$KS_PANE" ""; then
    _route_apply_now "$id" || echo "queued $id"
  else
    _route_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"
    echo "queued $id"
  fi
}

# D-2925: `cmd_project_pool`'s `--pool` arm proved a registry-only project
# exists with `grep -qxF -- "$project" "$REG"/*.project 2>/dev/null` — a
# GLOB, not a single named path, so `grep` opens EVERY matched `.project` row
# BY NAME, in glob order, before it can report a match or a miss. A FIFO or a
# symlink-to-an-infinite-character-device among those rows — sorting before
# the row that would have answered — blocks `grep`'s own `open`/`read`
# forever, and `ccd project-pool --pool` never returns. `_project_pool_state`
# above already closes this exact class for the SINGLE-file read; this glob
# is the second, unguarded reader of the same directory.
#
# `_project_pool_state`'s pattern, copied here rather than re-derived: a type
# test (`-f`+`-r`) checked BEFORE any attempt to open, so the shape alone
# decides which rows are even offered to `grep` — never a read of one this
# function has not first typed. Behaviour-identical for every input that
# answers today: an entry `grep` already could not usefully read contributed
# nothing through the original's `2>/dev/null` either, so skipping it here
# changes no verdict, only whether the skip can hang.
_reg_project_glob_has() {   # project -> 0 iff some readable $REG/*.project row's content equals it
  local project="$1" f
  for f in "$REG"/*.project; do
    [[ -f "$f" && -r "$f" ]] || continue
    grep -qxF -- "$project" "$f" 2>/dev/null && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

cmd_project_pool() {   # ccd project-pool --project <p> --pool <name> | --project <p> --clear
  # THE PROJECT SIDE of the pool rule gains a writer here. The tag is
  # `$POOLS_DIR/<project>` — one file, one token — and until this verb
  # existed the only writer was an ssh session and an `echo`, which ruling 2
  # rules out as the only way in.
  #
  # THIS VERB ALONE DOES NOT REACH A PHONE — fix round 1 (Finding 2b): a
  # prior version of this comment said it did. The exec-whitelist grant that
  # lets the agent's PWA-facing path invoke it at all is this same wave's
  # Task 4; the server route that calls the grant is wave 3's; the PWA
  # control the operator actually taps is wave 4's. Until all three land,
  # this verb is reachable the same way the `echo` it replaces was — a
  # session running `ccd` directly, over ssh or a script, not yet the phone.
  #
  # MODELLED LINE FOR LINE ON `cmd_coord_pause` ABOVE: flag-led, EXACT arity
  # (usage die otherwise), validation before anything on disk is touched, and
  # BOTH write arms CHECKED. That last is not decoration: ccd runs
  # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e` (ccd:9), so an unchecked write that failed
  # would fall straight through to the echo and report a tag that is not there.
  # This is the verb where a false success is worst — a route keyed on the exit
  # code then believes a project is constrained while placement is free.
  #
  # NO `_lc_done` LINE. The lifecycle vocabulary is per-SESSION and this verb
  # acts on a project; `cmd_coord_pause` journals nothing for the same reason.
  # The record it does leave is one `swap.log` line, below.
  local project pool="" clear=0
  local usage="usage: ccd project-pool --project <p> --pool <name>|--clear"
  case "$#" in
    4) [[ $1 == --project && $3 == --pool ]] || die "$usage"; project=$2; pool=$4 ;;
    3) [[ $1 == --project && $3 == --clear ]] || die "$usage"; project=$2; clear=1 ;;
    *) die "$usage" ;;
  esac
  # BEFORE THE FILESYSTEM. `_ws_project_valid` refuses `..`, `.`, dot-leading,
  # whitespace and slashes, so the joined path can never leave `$POOLS_DIR` and
  # a dot-leading name can never alias this directory's own tmp namespace.
  _ws_project_valid "$project" \
    || die "invalid project '$project' — allowed chars: A-Za-z0-9 . _ -, and no LEADING dot (the tag is a file in $POOLS_DIR, whose dot-leading names are its tmp namespace)"
  [[ -d "$REG" ]] || die "no registry at $REG"

  local oldstate old="-" new="-"
  oldstate=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  case "$oldstate" in "named "*) old=${oldstate#named } ;; esac

  if (( clear )); then
    # NO EXISTENCE CHECK ON `--clear`, deliberately. The doctor's `pools-stale`
    # remedy IS this command run against a project whose directory and registry
    # rows are both gone; a remedy that refused on the very state it cures
    # would leave the finding unfixable.
    #
    # DECIDE FROM `$oldstate`, NOT ANY FRESH FILESYSTEM TEST — fix round 2
    # (Finding 4, D-1847). Fix round 1 paired `-e` with `-L`, which closes the
    # dangling-symlink/loop shape but not the whole class: a tag under an
    # UNSEARCHABLE $POOLS_DIR (mode 000) answers FALSE to `-e` AND to `-L` —
    # neither predicate can stat OR lstat through a directory it cannot
    # search — so that guard would skip the removal again, a different shape
    # of the identical false success. The defect was never that `-e` was the
    # wrong predicate; it is that the verb asked the filesystem a SECOND
    # time, with different semantics, having already asked through the one
    # reader whose entire job is answering that question (spec §5.1: `ccd` is
    # the authority on the tag's state, and in practice that authority is
    # `_project_pool_state`) — two readers of one fact, free to disagree.
    # `untagged` is the ONLY state with nothing to remove; `named`,
    # `unreadable` and `malformed` all mean something IS there, and the
    # removal must be attempted and SUCCEED. One reader, both shapes closed,
    # and every future fix to the reader inherited for free.
    #
    # THE MESSAGE ON FAILURE says "may still be constrained" rather than "is
    # STILL tagged" — corrected in fix round 3 (Finding 7): a prior version
    # of this comment claimed `_pool_ok` answers non-serve for EVERY
    # non-`untagged` state, which is false for `named`. Measured against
    # `_pool_ok`'s own rule ("account untagged, OR project untagged, OR names
    # equal"): for `unreadable`/`malformed` it answers non-serve (rc 2,
    # undecidable) for EVERY candidate, unconditionally — nobody decides
    # while the tag cannot be read. For `named <pool>` it answers SERVE (rc
    # 0) for any candidate whose own account pool is unset or already equals
    # `<pool>`, and only mismatches (rc 1) a candidate tagged with a
    # DIFFERENT pool — a named tag does not block every account, only some.
    # "May still be constrained" is honest for both shapes: universally true
    # for `unreadable`/`malformed`, and genuinely possible — not certain —
    # for `named`. Claiming a NAMED tag survives outright ("is STILL tagged")
    # would also be wrong for `unreadable`/`malformed`, where there is no
    # genuine, readable tag to name at all.
    # `rm -f`'s TREATMENT OF ENOTDIR IS A USERLAND SPLIT, not a property of
    # `rm` — corrected in fix round 7, after macOS CI failed on the sentence
    # that stood here. When $POOLS_DIR ITSELF is a regular file,
    # `$POOLS_DIR/$project` cannot be resolved (ENOTDIR):
    #   - GNU coreutils classes ENOTDIR with ENOENT as "already gone" and
    #     `rm -f` returns 0. MEASURED, coreutils 9.4, on this branch.
    #   - BSD/macOS does NOT: the guard below fails and the verb refuses HERE
    #     instead of at the final re-measurement. INFERRED, not measured — I
    #     cannot run macOS. The evidence is CI plus this function's own
    #     control flow: `swap.log` came back EMPTY there, and between entry
    #     and the append the only reachable `die` in that fixture is this
    #     one, so the `rm` must have returned non-zero. The usual BSD `rm`
    #     tests `errno != ENOENT` alone, which would produce exactly this,
    #     but I have not read Apple's shipped source.
    # ENOENT itself is swallowed by both, and that half is not a guess: it is
    # the one case POSIX names for `-f` ("Do not write diagnostic messages or
    # modify the exit status in the case of nonexistent operands"). ENOTDIR is
    # not named, which is precisely where the two userlands part.
    #
    # NEITHER BEHAVIOUR IS A BUG HERE, so nothing below is platform-forked:
    # both userlands REFUSE and neither reports a false `untagged`; they
    # differ only in WHERE. Round 2 made the reader authoritative for the
    # DECISION to remove; trusting THIS call's rc for the OUTCOME was the same
    # mistake one level out, and it is a mistake on GNU whatever BSD does.
    # Fixed below, once, as the verb's unconditional LAST ACT (fix round 4,
    # Finding 10) rather than here — see that comment for why inline was not
    # enough either.
    #
    # BEFORE ASSERTING ANY `rm`/`mv`/`stat`/`du` SEMANTIC IN THIS FILE, CHECK
    # BOTH USERLANDS. This wave shipped three distinct kinds of comment that
    # were "true where I measured it" — true of one FILE and false of a
    # sibling, true of one ARM and false of the other, and now true of one
    # PLATFORM and false of the other. `ccd` runs on both and CI tests both,
    # so a coreutils-only fact stated flatly is a red suite one merge later.
    # `_plat_mv_notdir` is the precedent for when the split is real
    # enough to fork the CODE; this one is not, and is documented instead.
    if [[ "$oldstate" != untagged ]]; then
      rm -f -- "$POOLS_DIR/$project" \
        || die "could not clear the pool tag for $project — placement may still be constrained ($POOLS_DIR/$project)"
    fi
  else
    _pool_name_valid "$pool" \
      || die "invalid pool name '$pool' — one token matching $POOL_NAME_RE (lowercase, starts with a letter, max 32 chars)"
    # EXISTENCE, ON THE `--pool` ARM ONLY. Two ways to be a project: a
    # directory under $PROJECTS_ROOT, or a registry row that names one — which
    # is what makes the four non-git projects and every custom-workdir session
    # taggable. The glob is guarded because an empty registry leaves it its own
    # literal text and grep would complain about a file that is not there.
    [[ -d "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project" ]] \
      || _reg_project_glob_has "$project" \
      || die "no such project '$project' — neither $PROJECTS_ROOT/$project nor any registry row names it"
    # A WARNING, NOT A REFUSAL. This pool may be about to gain an account, and
    # the FLEET's roster copy is the authority — the server's can lag it
    # (§3.3).
    #
    # "WHEN IT BITES, IT BITES LOUDLY: AN EMPTY POOL STRANDS" stood here and
    # was falsified by PR #61 (D-1915). Three successive rewrites of the
    # replacement were each false in turn — D-1963, D-1962, and the
    # existence-for-availability substitution that followed it (D-1972) — each
    # one re-derived the rule instead of citing it. NOTHING IS RESTATED HERE
    # NOW: the conditions under which such a pool strands, and the conditions
    # under which it refuses placement, are in **spec §5.7.3**, which is their
    # only home. Do not re-derive them at this call site.
    #
    # THIS CENSUS IS ALSO WIDER THAN PLACEMENT, and that gap is older than the
    # merge: it walks `CCRC_ACCOUNTS` while placement walks `CCRC_HOME_ABLE`,
    # so tagging a project into a pool whose only member is an overflow lane
    # passes this warning silently and then refuses every `ws-add`. Making the
    # warning honest means counting home-able members separately — a behaviour
    # change with its own red-first test, deliberately NOT folded into the
    # merge that found it.
    #
    # THE CARRIER THIS CENSUS READS IS THE DECLARED ONE (item 6, carried
    # Task-2 M5): `_acct_pool` is the roster read, not `_acct_pool_state`'s
    # projection. Defensible — this fires at tag time, an honest claim about
    # the roster — but say so, since every other pool-aware reader in this
    # file now prefers the projection and a future author may assume this
    # one does too.
    #
    # AND ON A TWO-BOX FLEET IT IS THE ONE REMAINING LIVE READER OF THAT
    # DECLARED CARRIER (item 7, pre-existing, outside this wave's diff): it
    # walks THIS (the fleet) box's `accounts.sh`, while placement now follows
    # the SERVER box's roster copy through `$REG/pool-epoch` (spec §5.6 rule
    # 1). A roster edit that has reached the server but not yet synced to
    # this box's `accounts.sh` makes this warning and placement disagree —
    # noted here, not restructured.
    local w hit=0
    for w in "${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"; do
      [[ "$(_acct_pool "$w")" == "$pool" ]] && { hit=1; break; }
    done
    (( hit )) || echo "warn: no rostered account is in pool $pool" >&2

    mkdir -p -- "$POOLS_DIR" \
      || die "could not create $POOLS_DIR — $project is NOT tagged"
    # `_reg_set`'s atomicity, verbatim: tmp then rename. A SIGKILL between the
    # two leaks one dot-leading tmp — the same disclosed price `_reg_set` pays,
    # and every reader skips dot-leading entries, which is exact because no
    # project may lead with a dot.
    local tmp="$POOLS_DIR/.$project.$BASHPID.tmp"
    # `2>/dev/null` BEFORE `> "$tmp"`, and the order is the whole of it — the
    # same rule `_project_pool_state` spells out for its own read: bash applies
    # a command's redirections LEFT TO RIGHT and reports a FAILING one on
    # whatever stderr is current AT THAT POINT. Written the other way round
    # (`> "$tmp" 2>/dev/null`, which is what stood here) the silencer is not
    # yet installed when the OPEN is attempted, so only `printf`'s runtime
    # WRITE errors were caught and the open's own failure escaped — measured
    # with `$POOLS_DIR` at mode 0500: `ccd: line <this one>: …/.demo.<pid>.tmp:
    # Permission denied`, straight from the shell, ahead of the `die` that
    # explains it; with this order, that line is gone and only the `die`
    # remains. It was never a dead redirection, only half of one, and
    # `ccd-project-pool.test.ts` pins the half that was missing.
    #
    # THE `rm -f` ON THE FAILURE PATH STILL REPORTS, deliberately unsilenced —
    # but only where the UNLINK itself is denied, which is a stricter fixture
    # than the one above. Measured at mode 0500 it says nothing (`-f`
    # suppresses the ENOENT of a tmp that was never created); measured at mode
    # 000 it says `rm: cannot remove '…': Permission denied`. That is not the
    # shell talking over its own `die`: it is a SECOND, independent failure —
    # the tmp could not be cleaned up either — and an operator reading the
    # `die` below deserves to know a stray tmp may have been left behind.
    # Silencing it would fold two facts into one.
    printf '%s' "$pool" 2>/dev/null > "$tmp" \
      || { rm -f -- "$tmp"; die "could not write the pool tag for $project — it is NOT tagged"; }
    _plat_mv_notdir "$tmp" "$POOLS_DIR/$project" \
      || { rm -f -- "$tmp"; die "could not write the pool tag for $project — it is NOT tagged"; }
    new="$pool"
  fi

  # THE ONE swap.log LINE WHOSE SUBJECT IS A PROJECT. Every other line in that
  # file names a session id.
  #
  # WHY THAT IS WORTH THE ODDITY IS TRUE NOW, not an argument for a later
  # wave. The retag is the CAUSE of the `rehome` and `auto-pool` lines that
  # follow it within a tick, and a reader who cannot see the cause beside
  # its effects has to correlate two files. Both effects exist now: measured,
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s home re-seed calls `_lc_done rehome …` (which names
  # `rehome` to `_lc_emit`, not merely to the `_LC_ACTS` vocabulary array) and
  # appends `rehome $id: home … -> … [pool=…]` to this same swap.log, and its
  # affinity arm sets `aff_verb=auto-pool` and appends `auto-pool $id: … ->
  # … [home=…]` the same way (`grep -n '_lc_done rehome\|aff_verb=auto-pool'
  # ccd/ccd` finds both). This line's justification is no longer a promise;
  # the line itself remains the only record a project-scoped retag leaves
  # anywhere.
  #
  # THIS LINE MUST LAND BEFORE THE FINAL RE-MEASUREMENT BELOW — fix round 4
  # (Finding 10). Reproduced: `ln -s "$REG/pools/$project" "$REG/swap.log"`
  # makes this verb's OWN audit line alias the tag it just decided to remove
  # (or, on the no-op `--clear` path, the tag it never had to touch at all).
  # Where the append ran AFTER the round-3 re-measurement, the append
  # RE-CREATED the tag — as unparseable, date-stamped content, so the next
  # reader sees `malformed` — while the verb had already certified `untagged`
  # and could only report the answer it already had. Round 1 parked the
  # unchecked append on the reasoning that losing a line does not make any
  # exit code lie; round 3 then moved the last WRITE past the last
  # MEASUREMENT, which silently made that reasoning false — nothing announced
  # the change, the same question just got asked again. Writing this line
  # BEFORE the measurement below, rather than adding a guard AROUND this
  # line, is the fix: the invariant that actually holds is not "this write is
  # harmless" (an argument that keeps expiring as this function grows one
  # more write after the one it was made about) but THE VERB'S LAST ACT IS A
  # MEASUREMENT, so nothing this function does can outrun what it reports —
  # WITH ONE STATED EXCEPTION, and it is stated HERE rather than only where it
  # is introduced, because this is where the invariant is claimed. Fix round 5
  # appends a `pool-tag-void` line on each of the two `die` paths below, AFTER
  # the measurement, so on those two paths the measurement is not the last act.
  # That write can itself alter the very tag that was measured — measured, with
  # `$REG/swap.log` symlinked at `$POOLS_DIR/demo`, a refused `--pool` left
  # that path holding the pool name with a whole audit line concatenated onto
  # it (159 bytes on a fixture box; the length is the path's), reading
  # `malformed`. What the invariant still buys is the only thing it was ever
  # for: those writes run only AFTER a refusal has been decided, so no write in
  # this function can outrun a REPORTED SUCCESS. A `die` is not a report of
  # success. See the round-5 note below.
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') pool-tag $project: $old -> $new" >> "$REG/swap.log"

  # THE FINAL RE-MEASUREMENT, UNCONDITIONAL, ON BOTH BRANCHES — the verb's
  # actual last act (fix round 4, Finding 10; generalises fix round 3's
  # Finding-6 re-measure, which ran only inside the `--clear` arm's `rm`
  # branch and only ahead of the line above). Nothing after this call can
  # falsify what it reports, because nothing runs after it: this reads the
  # same authority every other decision in this file defers to
  # (`_project_pool_state`, spec §5.1) one last time, over `$project`'s OWN
  # tag as it exists after every write this call has made, log line included,
  # and refuses to echo success unless that reader agrees.
  #
  # `$project`'s OWN TAG, AND NOTHING ELSE — the reader takes one project, so
  # this certifies one project. It is NOT a claim that the call left the pools
  # directory as it found it. Measured: with `$REG/swap.log` symlinked at
  # `$POOLS_DIR/quiet-basin`, `--project demo --pool pool-a` exits 0 printing
  # `tagged demo pool-a` (true, and `demo` really does read `named pool-a`)
  # while the append destroys a DIFFERENT project's tag — `quiet-basin` goes
  # from `named pool-b` to `malformed` in the same call. A verb that was not
  # asked about `quiet-basin` cannot certify it, and this one does not try.
  #
  # A CORRECTIVE LINE ON EITHER DIE PATH BELOW — fix round 5 (Finding 13),
  # and NOT a reversion of fix round 4. Moving the append upstream of the
  # measurement (round 4) closed the false-SUCCESS shape, but it left a
  # different artifact behind: every `die` below now runs AFTER the
  # `pool-tag` line above, so a reader scanning swap.log sees a transition
  # recorded for a call that in fact refused (measured: `$POOLS_DIR` a plain
  # file made a failing `--clear` write `pool-tag demo: - -> -` where the
  # pre-round-4 code wrote nothing at all). The fix is NOT to move the
  # append back after the measurement — that is shape 4 again, the exact
  # false success round 4 closed. Instead, a write that happens AFTER a
  # failure decision cannot manufacture a false success, because nothing
  # downstream reads it as one: the function is about to `die`, so this
  # second line's only job is to keep a HUMAN scanning the file from reading
  # the first one at face value.
  #
  # `pool-tag-void` IS A DIFFERENT VERB IN THE VERB COLUMN, and that is the
  # whole of the claim. A reader parsing the third field of a swap.log line
  # gets `pool-tag-void`, never `pool-tag`, so it can never be counted as a
  # second genuine transition. A SUBSTRING GREP CAN be fooled, and `-w` does
  # not save it: `-` is not a word constituent, so `pool-tag` is a whole word
  # inside `pool-tag-void` too. Measured on a swap.log holding one of each
  # line, `grep -c pool-tag` = 2 and `grep -cw pool-tag` = 2, while
  # `grep -c pool-tag-void` = 1. Anything counting real transitions must
  # anchor on the column (or exclude `pool-tag-void` explicitly), not match
  # the token loose.
  local finalstate; finalstate=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  if (( clear )); then
    if [[ "$finalstate" != untagged ]]; then
      echo "$(date '+%F %T') pool-tag-void $project: the pool-tag line above did NOT take effect — this call refused" >> "$REG/swap.log"
      die "could not clear the pool tag for $project — placement may still be constrained ($POOLS_DIR/$project)"
    fi
    echo "untagged $project"
  else
    # FIX ROUND 5 (FINDING 12): this die used to reuse the printf/mv
    # failure's own "it is NOT tagged" — accurate THERE, where nothing was
    # ever written, but reachable HERE with the tag path holding GARBAGE
    # instead. `$finalstate` reads `malformed` under a construction anyone
    # can run (measured: the rename succeeds, then an aliased swap.log
    # append — `ln -s "$POOLS_DIR/$project" "$REG/swap.log"` — concatenates
    # the audit line onto the freshly-written pool name with no separating
    # newline, which `_pool_name_valid` rejects).
    #
    # ONE CONSTRUCTED STATE, THREE RACE-ONLY ONES — corrected in fix round 6.
    # The sentence this replaces also claimed `unreadable` here "by the same
    # construction that reaches `unreadable` on the `--clear` arm", and that
    # is measurably wrong: on THIS arm neither shape survives to the
    # measurement. `$POOLS_DIR` a plain file dies earlier, at `mkdir -p`
    # ("could not create … — demo is NOT tagged"); `$POOLS_DIR` at mode 000
    # dies earlier still, at the `printf` into the tmp ("could not write the
    # pool tag … — it is NOT tagged"). So `unreadable` belongs with
    # `untagged` and `named <other>` — reachable only if a concurrent writer
    # lands between the rename above and this read, which is exactly the
    # class the enumeration already excluded for those two.
    #
    # WHAT THE MESSAGE HAS TO BE HONEST FOR IS STILL ALL FOUR, race included,
    # and it is. For `malformed` and `unreadable`, `_pool_ok`'s `*)` arm
    # answers non-serve (rc 2, undecidable) UNCONDITIONALLY: nobody decides,
    # for every candidate — so "it is NOT tagged" would tell the operator the
    # opposite of the truth (the project is not free, it is UNDECIDABLE) and
    # the cure differs: remove the garbage, not retry the write. For
    # `untagged` and `named <other>` the tag is readable and `_pool_ok`
    # decides normally — Finding 7's point — and there "NOT tagged" would be
    # wrong in the other direction, or simply naming the wrong pool. The
    # message below claims neither NOT-tagged nor STILL-tagged; it says only
    # what is true of every one of the four — the path no longer holds what
    # this call wrote.
    if [[ "$finalstate" != "named $pool" ]]; then
      echo "$(date '+%F %T') pool-tag-void $project: the pool-tag line above did NOT take effect — this call refused" >> "$REG/swap.log"
      die "could not confirm the pool tag for $project as $pool — the tag path no longer holds what this call wrote ($POOLS_DIR/$project)"
    fi
    echo "tagged $project $new"
  fi
}

cmd_ws_archive() {   # ccd ws-archive --session <id> — fold a merged workspace
  # out of the live fleet. DESTROYS NOTHING: no worktree remove, no branch -d,
  # no rm of the registry. Its one cost is the tmux pane (scrollback and any
  # in-flight turn), which is why it demands an affirmative idle.
  # WAVE 5 — THE DEC FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE, and that order
  # is the whole correctness argument, exactly as `cmd_stop` states it in its
  # own header (grep `FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE`): this verb's
  # guard is EXACT arity on `--session <id>`, so a flag left in argv is not a
  # wider call, it is a REFUSED one — `ccd ws-archive --session x --surface pwa`
  # would die in the usage check while the server reads the non-zero as "the
  # archive failed on the box".
  #
  # THREE `given` FLAGS, not three value tests. `--surface ''` and no
  # `--surface` at all are two different facts: nobody declared a surface
  # (`none`), versus a caller declared a word this ccd does not know
  # (`unknown`). Testing `[[ -n "$lc_surface" ]]` instead would collapse them,
  # which is `ccd:676-689`'s `${2-ccd}` vs `${2:-ccd}` distinction broken in a new
  # place. Same for `--actor ''` and `--reason ''`, which are REFUSED rather
  # than silently read as absent — one policy, everywhere (AUDIT B5).
  local lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_reason='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_gr=0 lc_args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates (`cmd_stop` carries the
      # same three lines over the same hazard).
      --surface)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-archive [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-archive [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      --reason)    [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-archive [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>"
                   lc_gr=1; lc_reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason=*)  lc_gr=1; lc_reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2, ccd:11089-11090): an empty array here
  # is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop
  # is where that distinction has to be made, before the encoder ever sees the
  # value. `lc_gs` already carries givenness; the `${w:-unknown}` fallback
  # below is what turns a declared-but-blank surface into `unknown` rather
  # than an empty string that `_lc_json` would backfill to `none` — the exact
  # given/no-flag collapse this verb must not produce.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (`ccd:2411-2422`, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''`
  # returns 0, so it cannot be the blank guard on its own. A declared
  # `--actor`/`--reason` is refused when blank the same way `cmd_ws_hold`
  # refuses a blank `--reason` (ccd:4758): its own non-blank check, separate
  # from the byte cap, whitespace-only counting as blank too.
  if (( lc_ga )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--actor must be non-blank"
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" || die "--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  if (( lc_gr )); then
    [[ -n "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] || die "--reason must be non-blank"
    _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason" || die "--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
  fi
  [[ $# -eq 2 && $1 == --session ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-archive --session <id>"
  local id=$2
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]]       || die "no such session: $id"
  [[ -n "$(_reg_get "$id" workspace)" ]] \
    || die "not a workspace — refusing to archive a main checkout"
  # `return`, not `exit`: ccd is sourceable (the BASH_SOURCE guard below), so an
  # `exit` here would terminate an in-process batch caller at its first
  # already-archived workspace and skip the rest with a success code.
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" ]] && { echo "already archived $id"; return 0; }

  local workdir; workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  [[ -n "$workdir" ]] || die "incomplete registry for '$id'"
  # The same refusal ws-restore makes, for a stronger reason: with the directory
  # gone every tree field in the manifest reads PRISTINE — dirty 0 because the
  # status read failed, worktreeBytes 0 from _ws_gc_bytes's '-' fallback,
  # ignoredDigest = sha256("") — and the output still said "nothing deleted".
  [[ -d "$workdir" ]] \
    || die "worktree is gone: $workdir — cannot describe it for the archive record; see: ccd ws-attic --session $id"

  local st
  st=$(_ws_status "$id") || die "status-unknown"
  [[ "$st" != busy ]] || die "session-busy"

  # Build the manifest BEFORE anything is torn down, and refuse if it cannot be
  # told truthfully. Its exit status used to be discarded and every field inside
  # it 2>/dev/null-swallowed, with no [[ -s ]], no parse and no guard before the
  # `archived` marker went down — so a workspace whose worktree had moved away
  # archived at exit 0 with a manifest asserting a clean, empty tree, and a box
  # without python3 persisted syntactically invalid JSON as the archive record.
  # This is what Task 5's audit ladder and ws-reap compare against: a manifest
  # that lies pristine is a manifest that authorises a deletion. The helper says
  # on stderr which rung refused.
  local manifest
  manifest=$(_ws_archive_manifest "$id") \
    || die "cannot describe $id truthfully — nothing was touched"
  [[ -n "$manifest" ]] || die "empty archive manifest for $id — nothing was touched"
  # Parsed, not merely non-empty: the fields are individually guarded above, and
  # this is what keeps a future unquoted or non-numeric field from becoming a
  # record that only LOOKS like JSON.
  #
  # `json.dumps(..., allow_nan=False)`, not a bare `json.loads` — fix-wave
  # finding 3. `json.loads` alone accepts `NaN`/`Infinity`/`-Infinity`, which
  # RFC 8259 has no literal for and `JSON.parse` in node throws on, so this
  # last gate before the record is persisted agreed with a document its one
  # real consumer (`manifestBytes`, server/src/registry.ts) cannot read. The
  # round trip rejects both the bare literals and any number that floats to
  # infinity; the guard now answers the question it is written to ask, which is
  # "can the reader of this file parse it", not "can python".
  printf '%s' "$manifest" \
    | python3 -c 'import json,sys; json.dumps(json.loads(sys.stdin.read()), allow_nan=False)' 2>/dev/null \
    || die "archive manifest for $id is not valid JSON — nothing was touched"

  # Into the REGISTRY, never into the worktree — the worktree is the thing that
  # may later be deleted, and a manifest inside it would die with it.
  # Through `_reg_set` (wave 2), with the TRAILING NEWLINE carried in the value:
  # `printf '%s\n'` put one there and `_reg_set` adds none, so passing it
  # explicitly keeps the file byte-identical. `manifestBytes` (registry.ts)
  # JSON.parses this and would tolerate either, which is the reason to be
  # deliberate rather than casual — a migration that also moves the bytes is two
  # changes in one commit.
  _reg_set "$id" archivemanifest "$manifest"$'\n'

  _ws_unsupervise "$id"                                   # clears Restart=always
  tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null || true

  # Truthful in three values, not always "merged": a workspace can be archived
  # with a PR the gh-verified prphase machinery has confirmed merged, PR
  # number and all (`merged:#N`), with nothing beyond base to judge at all
  # (`empty`), or with real commits nobody has proven merged (`manual`).
  #
  # DEVIATION FROM THE SPEC'S LETTER, KEPT TO ITS GOAL: the spec named
  # `_ws_gc_merged` (an ancestor check) as the decider. Ancestor checks cannot
  # see a squash merge — the fleet's normal flow — because the squashed
  # commit on main shares no lineage with the branch it replaces, so
  # `_ws_gc_merged` would read `manual` for the common case: a new lie
  # replacing the old one. `prphase`, written by `cmd_pr_state` off a real
  # `gh` read, is what actually answers "was this merged", so it decides
  # instead — the spec's goal (truth) over its letter. `merged:#N` outranks
  # `empty`: a bound PR whose phase reads merged implies commits existed to
  # merge, even when a squash or an ancestor-swallowing rebase leaves this
  # branch's own ahead-count at 0 — losing the PR number there would be a
  # truth this ladder already had, thrown away. A branch with no bound PR, a
  # PR whose phase is anything but merged, or a base this run could not
  # measure ahead against at all, also lands manual — the vocabulary is
  # closed at three values.
  local pr phase reason ahead base
  pr=$(_reg_get "$id" prnumber)
  phase=$(_reg_get "$id" prphase)
  # Matches _pr_state_one / _ws_reap_eval's own idiom (ccd:3458): the
  # registry's recorded base, falling back to the symbolic `origin/HEAD` —
  # not a live `symbolic-ref` read off $main, which asks git a question the
  # registry has already answered and can disagree with it if $main's remote
  # HEAD moved after this workspace was created.
  base=$(_reg_get "$id" base); [[ -n "$base" ]] || base="origin/HEAD"
  ahead=""
  [[ -n "$base" ]] && ahead=$(git -C "$workdir" rev-list --count "$base..HEAD" 2>/dev/null) || ahead=""
  # Reason and its git reads are both settled BEFORE the `archived` marker
  # goes down: a mid-window kill between the two used to leave the marker
  # written with no reason ever recorded, and the `already archived` early
  # return above would hand that workspace back forever without repairing
  # it. Marker and reason are written adjacently, right after, so nothing
  # else can land between them either.
  if [[ "$phase" == merged && "$pr" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then reason="merged:#$pr"
  elif [[ "$ahead" == 0 ]]; then reason="empty"
  else reason="manual"; fi
  _reg_set "$id" archived "$(date +%s)"
  _reg_set "$id" archivedreason "$reason"
  # AFTER both markers, so `reason` is the value that actually landed. It is one
  # of exactly three words (ccd:4702-4704): merged:#N | empty | manual.
  # `.archived` is the one registry field carrying a WHY, and it is measurably
  # false on four live rows right now — which is why the journal records the act
  # rather than trusting the field.
  _lc_done archive "$id" "" meas.archivedReason "$reason" \
    meas.branch "$(_reg_get "$id" branch)" meas.project "$(_reg_get "$id" project)" \
    dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"

  local note=""
  case "$reason" in
    merged:*) note=" (merged in #$pr)" ;;
    empty)    note=" (no commits beyond base)" ;;
  esac
  echo "archived $id$note — worktree kept at $workdir, nothing deleted"
}

_ws_archive_manifest() {   # id -> one JSON object on stdout
  # NON-ZERO, with the reason on stderr, when the workspace cannot be described
  # truthfully. Every field used to be swallowed individually, which made the
  # unreadable cases indistinguishable from a clean, empty, unstashed tree.
  #
  # The line between the two halves of that sentence is the whole design, and it
  # is NOT "anything unexpected refuses":
  #   REFUSED — a question git cannot answer at all: no worktree record for this
  #     path, a directory belonging to another repository, an unreadable tree or
  #     ignored set, no python3 to quote with. There is nothing truthful to write.
  #   RECORDED — a fact about the world that happens not to be the expected one:
  #     the worktree is on another branch, or on a detached HEAD, or the branch
  #     the registry names no longer resolves. Those are describable, so they are
  #     described, and the DESTRUCTIVE verb refuses on them at the instant of
  #     deletion (Task 5/6: `registry-branch-drift`, `detached-head`,
  #     `branch-missing`) where a refusal costs nothing but a deferred cleanup.
  # Archive is lossless and is still fired with no human watching — the
  # server's run-close lane does it (`coord/close.ts`, `{state:'failed',
  # archive:true}`) — so a refusal it cannot converge out of strands the
  # workspace in the live fleet forever with nothing on screen to say why. Same
  # argument Task 2's Context already makes for a dirty tree ("refusing would
  # strand merged-but-dirty workspaces in the live fleet permanently"), and the
  # same division `cmd_ws_rm` draws for this very divergence: the registry is a
  # witness, never the decider (ccd:491-497).
  local id="$1" project workdir branch base main tip dirty ignored stashes bytes pr prhistory transcript
  project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch);   base=$(_reg_get "$id" base)
  [[ -n "$project" && -n "$workdir" && -n "$branch" ]] \
    || { echo "manifest: incomplete registry for '$id'" >&2; return 1; }
  main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"

  # Every string field is quoted by python3, so without it NONE of them are and
  # the record is not JSON. Probed once, up front, rather than six times inside
  # the printf. ccd's other python3 call site (ccd:2331) warns and continues,
  # which is right for a best-effort transcript sanitize and wrong for a record
  # that authorises deletions.
  _json_str probe >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || { echo "manifest: python3 unavailable — cannot quote JSON safely" >&2; return 1; }

  # ccrc asks $main; $workdir is only ever a path git is asked ABOUT. The tree
  # reads below are the one class that must be `-C "$workdir"` — they are
  # questions about that directory's contents — so they run only after git's own
  # record AND the directory itself have both said $main, which is the pair
  # cmd_ws_rm requires before it touches anything (ccd:458-461). `registered` is
  # read from $? on its own line: `local x=$(...)` returns local's status.
  local wthead registered wd_common main_common rc
  wthead=$(_ws_wt_branch "$main" "$workdir"); registered=$?
  (( registered == 0 )) \
    || { echo "manifest: $main has no worktree record for $workdir" >&2; return 1; }
  # DRIFT IS A FIELD, NOT A REFUSAL. `_ws_wt_branch` follows a rename whether ccd
  # or the user did it (ccd:403-409), so `$wthead` is git's own answer for this
  # path and `$branch` is the registry's claim: recorded side by side as `branch`
  # and `worktreeHead`, which is the pair Task 5 fingerprints (`worktreeHead=`)
  # and puts on the wire as `headMatchesRegistry`. "" is git's answer for a
  # recorded detached HEAD, and the same "" Task 5 compares against `$branch`.
  # Three ordinary states used to refuse here, permanently and identically on
  # retry: a `git branch -m` inside the worktree, a `git checkout other-branch`
  # to compare something, a `git bisect`. None of them makes the workspace
  # undescribable, and refusing left the sweep logging the same line every two
  # minutes forever.
  #
  # The record alone cannot decide the DIRECTORY: it outlives the directory, so a
  # hand-deletion plus a stray `git init` at the same path with the same branch
  # name still reads healthy and still answers $branch (measured on git 2.43).
  # That one stays a refusal — every tree field below would describe a stranger's
  # tree and file it under this workspace.
  wd_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$workdir"); main_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$main")
  [[ -n "$main_common" && "$wd_common" == "$main_common" ]] \
    || { echo "manifest: $workdir is not a worktree of $main" >&2; return 1; }

  # `--quiet` turns git's own two answers into two exit codes, which is what lets
  # "that ref does not exist" be a field and "I cannot read that repository" stay
  # a refusal. Measured on git 2.43: rc 1 for an absent ref in a readable repo,
  # rc 128 for a repo git cannot read at all (and without --quiet, 128 for both).
  # `tip` is recorded as JSON null when absent, never as "" or a substitute oid: a
  # renamed branch leaves the registry's name resolving to nothing, and ws-reap
  # refuses that as `branch-missing`. The tip stays the tip of the branch this
  # record NAMES — filing the renamed branch's tip under `branch` would be the
  # forgery this rung exists to prevent.
  tip=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) : ;;
    1) tip="" ;;
    *) echo "manifest: could not ask $main for refs/heads/$branch" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
  # Measured against whatever is checked out, which is what `worktreeHead` above
  # makes legible: on a drifted worktree this counts that branch's uncommitted
  # work, and it is still the honest answer to "what is uncommitted here" — but
  # only once the read below is CHECKED, which is what the next paragraph is
  # about, and which the previous version of this comment claimed without.
  #
  # AND STDERR IS CAPTURED — final-round integration review, item 5 / new
  # finding 3. `git status --porcelain` over a PARTIALLY unreadable tree exits
  # ZERO with EMPTY stdout and the diagnostic on stderr alone. Measured on git
  # 2.43, `chmod 000` on a TRACKED directory holding a modified file:
  #     rc 0, stdout empty, stderr "tracked/deep/code.txt: Permission denied"
  #                                "warning: could not open directory 'tracked/'"
  # so `2>/dev/null` plus an exit-code test recorded `"dirty":0` for a tree with
  # uncommitted work in it. `_ws_reap_eval` (ccd:3109+) and `_ws_collect_ignored`
  # were hardened against exactly this and this read was not; two reads, one
  # rule, and this is the third site.
  #
  # THIS IS A REFUSAL, not a recorded field, and that is the ladder this
  # function's own header draws rather than an exception to it: "a question git
  # cannot answer at all … an unreadable tree or ignored set" is the REFUSED
  # arm, and a tree that cannot be walked is exactly that. The RECORDED arm is
  # for facts that are simply not the expected one — a drifted branch, a
  # detached HEAD — and a partial read is not a fact about the workspace, it is
  # the absence of one. The archive path deliberately records rather than
  # refuses (ccd:2689's comment says so), which is precisely why the honesty of
  # the numbers it does record is the only protection there is.
  local derrf derr drc
  derrf=$(_plat_mktemp) \
    || { echo "manifest: could not make a scratch file to read the tree at $workdir" >&2; return 1; }
  dirty=$(git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain 2>"$derrf"); drc=$?
  derr=$(cat "$derrf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$derrf"
  { (( drc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$derr" ]]; } \
    || { echo "manifest: could not read the tree at $workdir${derr:+ ($derr)}" >&2; return 1; }
  dirty=$(printf '%s' "$dirty" | grep -c . || true)
  # Same rule again, one line down: `_ws_ignored_digest` used to check only
  # `PIPESTATUS[0]`, which is also 0 in the case above, so the recorded
  # `ignoredDigest` silently covered an incomplete set. The guard now lives
  # inside that function, where its own callers inherit it.
  ignored=$(_ws_ignored_digest "$workdir") \
    || { echo "manifest: could not read the ignored set at $workdir" >&2; return 1; }
  # $main answered rev-parse a moment ago — rc 0 or a clean "no such ref", both
  # of which are answers — so its stash list is readable; the count is the only
  # field whose repository has already been proven healthy.
  #
  # Scoped to the `branch` field, NOT to `worktreeHead`: it is the count of
  # entries BELONGING to that branch — those whose subject names it, plus those
  # taken from it on a detached HEAD, which name no branch at all and are
  # attributed by base commit (integration docket 6, see `_ws_stash_count`) —
  # and pairing it with any other name
  # would make the record two things at once. So on a drifted worktree this can
  # read 0 while `worktreeHead`'s branch holds stashes — which is safe in the one
  # direction that matters, and the reason CHANGED with the drift rung and is
  # worth restating rather than leaving as it stood. It used to be "`ws-reap`
  # refuses drift before it ever consults a stash count". `ws-reap` no longer
  # refuses drift: it resolves it, and its stash rung then counts against GIT'S
  # branch, the one it is about to delete. What still holds — and is now the
  # whole argument — is the second half: reap recomputes the count itself, from
  # the branch it resolved, and never reads this field. `worktreeHead` beside it
  # is what tells a reader which question THIS number answered.
  stashes=$(_ws_stash_count "$main" "$branch")
  # Pre-merge fix round, finding 5: `null`, not `0` — the seventh instance of
  # the measurement-forgery class in this plan (deviation 10, "a number is a
  # measurement"). The whole-directory-gone case already refuses earlier (the
  # `[[ -d "$workdir" ]]` guard above `_ws_archive_manifest`, and the
  # `git -C "$workdir" status` read a few lines up, both before this point);
  # this is the narrower window — `du` failing on a workdir that DOES exist
  # and IS otherwise readable. `registry.ts`'s `manifestBytes` already treats
  # anything but a finite JSON number as unmeasured (Task 19), so writing the
  # honest value here is the whole fix: a fabricated 0 would have argued
  # against a cleanup that could free real disk.
  bytes=$(_ws_gc_bytes "$workdir"); [[ "$bytes" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || bytes=null
  pr=$(_reg_get "$id" prnumber); [[ "$pr" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || pr=null
  # prHistory folds in the outgoing-PR ledger the prhistory chokepoint appends
  # to (inside `_pr_py`'s `state` mode, immediately before its one
  # `clear('prnumber')`), so an ARCHIVED workspace's record carries the whole
  # lineage — the spec's `merged:#591 (waves: #577 #583 #591)` card — and not
  # just the single current `pr` beside it.
  #
  # WHAT IT ACTUALLY OUTLIVES, MEASURED. The previous version of this comment
  # asserted two mechanisms this file disproves — that the manifest is "the one
  # record ws-reap later reads", and that the lineage therefore survives the
  # worktree. Neither is true, and the correction is the contract:
  #   - NOTHING IN ccd READS THIS MANIFEST BACK. `grep -n archivemanifest ccd`
  #     is four sites: the field-name list, this write, `ws-restore`'s own
  #     `stat -c %s` of the file (for `meas.manifestBytes`, byte count only,
  #     never the content), and its `rm -f`. `_ws_reap_eval` re-measures every
  #     fact it needs itself. The only reader anywhere is
  #     `server/src/registry.ts`'s `manifestBytes`,
  #     which extracts `worktreeBytes` and nothing else. This field's audience
  #     is a HUMAN reading an archived workspace's record.
  #   - IT DOES NOT SURVIVE A REAP. `_reg_purge` (ccd:100) unlinks every
  #     dot-free `$REG/<id>.<suffix>` except `archived`/`reaping`, so ws-reap
  #     step (i) takes this manifest AND the raw `.prhistory` together. The one
  #     document that outlives a reaped workspace is the tombstone
  #     `$REG/.reaped/<id>.json` (`_ws_tombstone`), which has no `prHistory`
  #     field. Pinned by `the reap purge takes the manifest AND the raw ledger`.
  #   - IT DOES SURVIVE A RESTORE, which is the durability the spec asks for
  #     ("recoverable via `ws-restore`, prhistory intact", spec Failure modes):
  #     `cmd_ws_restore` removes `archived`/`archivedreason`/`archivemanifest`
  #     and leaves `.prhistory` standing, so a workspace that goes back into
  #     the fleet re-archives later with its lineage complete.
  #
  # THREE INPUTS, THREE ANSWERS — and telling the third from the first is why
  # the read below is exit-code-driven instead of one blanket `except`:
  #   ABSENT -> `[]`. A workspace whose PR number was never replaced never grew
  #     a history file; `[]` is the measured answer, not a stand-in for one.
  #     (The scalars above answer `null` when unmeasured. This is an array whose
  #     emptiness is a FACT rather than a gap, and `[]` for the absent file is
  #     what the interface says.)
  #   MALFORMED -> `[]`, raw file untouched on disk. A line the record cannot be
  #     trusted to mean is neither a partial history nor a refusal: the manifest
  #     stays valid JSON and the ledger is still there for a human to read.
  #   PRESENT AND UNREADABLE -> REFUSAL, on this function's own stderr channel,
  #     the REFUSED arm of the header's ladder ("a question git cannot answer at
  #     all … an unreadable tree or ignored set"). A `chmod 000` ledger — or a
  #     directory at that path — used to raise inside the blanket `except` and
  #     write `"prHistory":[]` at exit 0: "this workspace retired no PRs", the
  #     most reassuring answer available, in the record whose own header says a
  #     manifest that lies pristine is a manifest that authorises a deletion.
  #     That is THE SIXTEENTH FORGERY's exact shape (`_ws_clip_manifest`
  #     below: `chmod 000` -> `[]` at rc 0 -> "clips: none"), and it is refused
  #     here for the same reason rather than argued out of. Present-vs-absent is
  #     distinguishable — the python tells them apart by which exception `open`
  #     raises, which is also right when `[[ -r ]]` would lie (root, ACLs).
  local prc
  prhistory=$(python3 -c '
import json, sys
try:
    with open(sys.argv[1], "rb") as f:
        raw = f.read()
except FileNotFoundError:
    sys.stdout.write("[]"); raise SystemExit(0)
except OSError:
    raise SystemExit(3)          # present and unreadable — the caller refuses
try:
    rows = [json.loads(ln) for ln in raw.decode("utf-8").splitlines() if ln.strip()]
    # `allow_nan=False` — fix-wave finding 3. Pythons json module accepts and
    # EMITS `NaN`/`Infinity`/`-Infinity`, which RFC 8259 has no literal for, in
    # both directions. So the malformed arm below had a hole: `{"pr": 1e999}`
    # is VALID JSON by the spec (the class this whole ladder exists for — a
    # corrupt or hand-edited ledger), loads to `inf`, and re-emitted as a bare
    # `Infinity` token. Every "is it JSON" guard downstream is this same python
    # and accepts it too, so the manifest persisted at exit 0 — and `JSON.parse`
    # in node, the ONE real consumer (`manifestBytes`), throws on it, which
    # pins `archivedBytes: null` for ever on the record whose own header says a
    # manifest that lies pristine is a manifest that authorises a deletion.
    # Raising ValueError here puts it in the MALFORMED arm where it belongs:
    # `[]`, raw file untouched on disk, a human can still read the ledger.
    out = json.dumps(rows, allow_nan=False)
except ValueError:               # bad JSON, bytes that are not text at all, or a value JSON cannot express
    sys.stdout.write("[]"); raise SystemExit(0)
sys.stdout.write(out)
' "$REG/$id.prhistory" 2>/dev/null); prc=$?
  case "$prc" in
    0) : ;;
    3) echo "manifest: the PR ledger at $REG/$id.prhistory exists and cannot be read" >&2; return 1 ;;
    # Everything else is the reader dying with the question unanswered — killed
    # mid-run, or an interpreter that broke after `_json_str probe` proved it
    # present. Not knowing is not `[]`.
    *) echo "manifest: could not read the PR ledger at $REG/$id.prhistory (python3 exited $prc)" >&2; return 1 ;;
  esac
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, DISCLOSED — and the disclosure that stood here was
  # FALSE, which is the whole reason finding 3 shipped. It said "rc 0 above can
  # only be `json.dumps(rows)` or the literal `[]`, both valid JSON, so no
  # fixture can reach this refusal". `json.dumps(rows)` was not necessarily
  # valid JSON: it emitted bare `Infinity`/`NaN` for any ledger line carrying a
  # value python floats to infinity or NaN, and this guard — the same python —
  # accepted them, so the invalid document sailed through into the manifest.
  # `allow_nan=False` above closes the producer; this guard is now STRICT in
  # its own right (`json.dumps(json.loads(...), allow_nan=False)`, which
  # rejects both the bare literals on input and any value that cannot be
  # re-emitted) rather than trusting the producer's shape. It stays a real
  # refusal, not a substitution: after the ladder above, a field this function
  # cannot compute is never allowed to read as a workspace that retired no PRs.
  printf '%s' "$prhistory" \
    | python3 -c 'import json,sys; json.dumps(json.loads(sys.stdin.read()), allow_nan=False)' >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || { echo "manifest: the PR ledger read for $id did not produce JSON" >&2; return 1; }
  transcript=$(_transcript_path "$id") || transcript=""
  printf '{"id":%s,"branch":%s,"worktreeHead":%s,"base":%s,"tip":%s,"dirty":%s,"ignoredDigest":%s,"stashes":%s,"worktreeBytes":%s,"pr":%s,"prHistory":%s,"transcript":%s}\n' \
    "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$branch")" "$(_json_str "$wthead")" "$(_json_str "$base")" \
    "$( [[ -n "$tip" ]] && _json_str "$tip" || echo null)" \
    "$dirty" "$(_json_str "$ignored")" "$stashes" "$bytes" "$pr" "$prhistory" "$(_json_str "$transcript")"
}

cmd_ws_restore() {   # ccd ws-restore --session <id> — undo an archive entirely
  # No confirmation: it only creates. The one thing it must not do is leave the
  # session unsupervised, so it calls `_ws_supervise` itself below rather than
  # leaving that to whoever next runs a start verb. (The line-numbered claim
  # this comment used to carry — "`ccd ensure` does NOT re-supervise" — was
  # stale twice over: the numbers had drifted, and `cmd_ensure` now adopts a
  # live-but-unsupervised pane via `_resupervise_live`. It restores nothing on
  # THIS path regardless, because `_spawn` below has not run yet.)
  #
  # WAVE 5 — THE DEC FLAGS, stripped BEFORE `local id="${2-}"` below is ever
  # bound and before the exact-arity rule, for the reason `cmd_ws_rm`'s F1 fix
  # states at ccd:3726: an id bound from raw, flag-polluted argv is bound
  # WRONG, not just early. Unlike `cmd_ws_archive` (Task 45), THIS verb reads
  # `$2` into `id` on the very next line, and reuses that one binding through
  # the arity/bad-session-id/no-such-session checks below — so
  # `ccd ws-restore --surface pwa --session abc123` would otherwise capture
  # `id=pwa` before this loop ever ran and refuse a legitimate restore as
  # `no-such-session: pwa` once the flags were stripped out from under it.
  # This loop's own `set --` is what makes the existing `local id="${2-}"`
  # correct; nothing below this block changes.
  #
  # REFUSED THROUGH `_lc_refuse`, unlike Task 45's `cmd_ws_archive`:
  # `ccd-refusal-scan.test.ts` (wave 3) slices `cmd_ws_restore`'s body and
  # requires every fatal refusal in it to leave a journal line first. A bare
  # `die` here would be a refusal on a destructive-adjacent verb that nothing
  # can read afterwards, which is the hole D4 exists to close.
  #
  # `""` FOR THE ID, deliberately: this loop runs before ANY id is bound, so
  # there is no session to name yet. An empty id is "no subject was
  # measured", which is a different fact from a wrong one.
  #
  # `bad-args` IS THE TOKEN, the one this file already spells (`cmd_ws_rename`'s
  # inline literals, and `wsaudit.ts`'s SENTENCES entry for it). Inventing
  # `bad-surface` would need new copy in `SENTENCES` and would red
  # `wsaudit.test.ts` in both directions.
  local lc_surface=none lc_actor='' lc_reason='' lc_gs=0 lc_ga=0 lc_gr=0 lc_args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates.
      # FIX ROUND 3 (operator escalation on F1's residual) — these three
      # ARITY ("needs a value") refusals fire INSIDE the parsing loop, before
      # the post-loop ladder below has validated anything. `lc_actor`/
      # `lc_reason` can already hold an UNVALIDATED (blank or over-cap) value
      # from an EARLIER `--actor`/`--reason` occurrence when a LATER flag's
      # missing value triggers one of these — measured: `--actor <600B>
      # --reason` (no value) put the full 600-byte actor on this refusal,
      # unexamined, the same leak class F1 closed on the post-loop ladder,
      # one arm over. Each arm below now applies the identical non-blank-
      # and-within-cap test the post-loop ladder uses before adding
      # `dec.actor`/`dec.reason` — a field that is not GIVEN is already ''
      # here (blank, so excluded the same way) and needs no separate case.
      --surface)   if [[ $# -lt 2 ]]; then
                     local lc_ra=(dec.surface "$lc_surface")
                     [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" && lc_ra+=(dec.actor "$lc_actor")
                     [[ -n "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason" && lc_ra+=(dec.reason "$lc_reason")
                     _lc_refuse restore "" bad-args "usage: ccd ws-restore [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>" verb ws-restore "${lc_ra[@]}"
                   fi
                   lc_gs=1; lc_surface="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) lc_gs=1; lc_surface="${1#--surface=}"; shift ;;
      --actor)     if [[ $# -lt 2 ]]; then
                     local lc_ra=(dec.surface "$lc_surface")
                     [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" && lc_ra+=(dec.actor "$lc_actor")
                     [[ -n "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason" && lc_ra+=(dec.reason "$lc_reason")
                     _lc_refuse restore "" bad-args "usage: ccd ws-restore [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>" verb ws-restore "${lc_ra[@]}"
                   fi
                   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --actor=*)   lc_ga=1; lc_actor="${1#--actor=}"; shift ;;
      --reason)    if [[ $# -lt 2 ]]; then
                     local lc_ra=(dec.surface "$lc_surface")
                     [[ -n "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor" && lc_ra+=(dec.actor "$lc_actor")
                     [[ -n "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]] && _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason" && lc_ra+=(dec.reason "$lc_reason")
                     _lc_refuse restore "" bad-args "usage: ccd ws-restore [--surface <word>] [--actor <text>] [--reason <text>] --session <id>" verb ws-restore "${lc_ra[@]}"
                   fi
                   lc_gr=1; lc_reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason=*)  lc_gr=1; lc_reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      *)           lc_args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2, ccd:11089-11090): an empty array here
  # is not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  set -- "${lc_args[@]}"
  # `_lc_surface_norm ''` prints EMPTY, not `unknown` (ccd:2395-2408, D-210) —
  # it cannot tell "no argument" from "an explicit blank" apart, so THIS loop
  # is where that distinction has to be made, before the encoder ever sees the
  # value. `lc_gs` already carries givenness; the `${lc_w:-unknown}` fallback
  # below is what turns a declared-but-blank surface into `unknown` rather
  # than an empty string that `_lc_json` would backfill to `none` — the exact
  # given/no-flag collapse this verb must not produce.
  if (( lc_gs )); then local lc_w; lc_w=$(_lc_surface_norm "$lc_surface"); lc_surface=${lc_w:-unknown}; fi
  # `_lc_dec_ok` is LENGTH-ONLY (ccd:2411-2422, D-210) — `_lc_dec_ok ''`
  # returns 0, so it cannot be the blank guard on its own. A declared
  # `--actor`/`--reason` is refused when blank the same way `cmd_ws_hold`
  # refuses a blank `--reason` (ccd:4758) and Task 45's `cmd_ws_archive`
  # follows: its own non-blank check, separate from the byte cap, whitespace-
  # only counting as blank too.
  # FIX ROUND 2 (final whole-branch review, F1) — VALIDATE BOTH FIELDS BEFORE
  # EMITTING ANY REFUSAL. The prior shape (blank-then-length, actor-then-
  # reason, refusing INSIDE each check) was order-dependent: with BOTH
  # --actor and --reason over cap, the actor-length refusal fired before
  # --reason was ever checked, so it carried `dec.reason` UNCHECKED — an
  # over-cap declared value riding a SIBLING field's refusal line, exactly
  # what the omit-only-the-failed-field exception exists to prevent
  # (measured: a 513-byte `dec.reason`, `truncated` unset). The blank-actor
  # refusal leaked the same unchecked reason. Fix: compute BOTH fields'
  # validity first, into `lc_actor_bad`/`lc_reason_bad`, and emit exactly ONE
  # refusal — actor's message wins ties, the same priority the old two-block
  # shape gave it by running first — omitting `dec.actor`/`dec.reason`
  # independently, keyed on whether THAT field is the one that failed, never
  # on which refusal happened to fire first. `_lc_json` already drops any
  # key whose value is empty (ccd:2169), so a field that was never GIVEN
  # needs no special case here: `lc_dec_args` simply omits the flag, and the
  # encoder's own rule covers a given-but-blank value that slipped through.
  local lc_actor_bad='' lc_reason_bad=''
  if (( lc_ga )); then
    if [[ -z "${lc_actor//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
      lc_actor_bad='--actor must be non-blank'
    elif ! _lc_dec_ok "$lc_actor"; then
      lc_actor_bad="--actor is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
    fi
  fi
  if (( lc_gr )); then
    if [[ -z "${lc_reason//[[:space:]]/}" ]]; then
      lc_reason_bad='--reason must be non-blank'
    elif ! _lc_dec_ok "$lc_reason"; then
      lc_reason_bad="--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes"
    fi
  fi
  if [[ -n "$lc_actor_bad" || -n "$lc_reason_bad" ]]; then
    local lc_dec_args=(verb ws-restore dec.surface "$lc_surface")
    [[ -z "$lc_actor_bad" ]]  && lc_dec_args+=(dec.actor "$lc_actor")
    [[ -z "$lc_reason_bad" ]] && lc_dec_args+=(dec.reason "$lc_reason")
    _lc_refuse restore "" bad-args "${lc_actor_bad:-$lc_reason_bad}" "${lc_dec_args[@]}"
  fi
  local id="${2-}"
  [[ $# -eq 2 && $1 == --session ]] \
    || _lc_refuse restore "$id" bad-args "usage: ccd ws-restore --session <id>"
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || _lc_refuse restore "$id" bad-session-id "bad session id"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]]       || _lc_refuse restore "$id" no-such-session "no such session: $id"

  # SERIALISED WITH `ws-reap`, ON WS-REAP'S OWN LOCK — final-round destructive
  # review, finding F5. These two verbs are the only pair in the design that
  # write the same fact from opposite directions: `ws-reap` requires
  # `$REG/$id.archived` and this removes it. Unserialised, the two orders both
  # end badly, and BOTH were measured:
  #   - restore inside `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase C (the <=12 s `gh pr list` plus
  #     the <=60 s `git fetch`) is a lost update seconds to a minute wide: the
  #     reap's token still matches, because it was computed in that same run and
  #     `archived` is not one of the fingerprint's fourteen inputs, so the tail
  #     destroys the workspace the human just brought back.
  #   - restore between the tail's (e) and its (i) is worse in the other
  #     direction: the reap has already unsupervised the session and killed the
  #     pane, this re-`_spawn`s it and re-`_ws_supervise`s it, and then (f)/(i)
  #     remove the worktree and purge the registry under a supervised unit and a
  #     live pane — a session with `Restart=always` pointed at a directory that
  #     no longer exists and no registry row to resurrect it from.
  # `_ws_reap_tail` also re-reads the marker (see there), which is what covers
  # the RESUME path — a breadcrumb outlives a restore by hours and no lock can
  # span that. The lock is what closes the concurrent shapes.
  #
  # THE SAME LOCK, not a second mechanism: two locks for one invariant is two
  # things to get out of step, and this one is already the kernel's — released
  # on every form of death including SIGKILL, never unlinked, its whole argument
  # written out at `cmd_ws_reap`. `-n`, so a restore declines rather than
  # blocking a route behind a reap that may be sixty seconds inside a fetch;
  # `in-progress` is the shape `ws-reap` itself answers for the mirror case.
  #
  # AFTER the `uuid` check and BEFORE the `archived` one. Before, so no lock
  # file is created for an id with no registry at all; after, so `archived` and
  # `workdir` — the two facts a concurrent reap can change — are read UNDER the
  # lock rather than in front of it.
  command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _lc_refuse restore "$id" flock-unavailable \
         "flock (util-linux) is unavailable — refusing to restore unserialised against ws-reap"
  local lock="$REG/.reap-$id.lock" lfd
  exec {lfd}>>"$lock" || _lc_refuse restore "$id" lock-unopenable "cannot open the reap lock at $lock"
  flock -n "$lfd" || {
    exec {lfd}>&-
    _lc_refuse restore "$id" in-progress \
      "another ccd process is reaping $id and still holds the lock — refusing to restore mid-cleanup"
  }

  [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" ]]   || _lc_refuse restore "$id" not-archived "not archived: $id"
  local workdir; workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  [[ -d "$workdir" ]] || _lc_refuse restore "$id" worktree-missing \
    "worktree is gone: $workdir — see: ccd ws-attic --session $id"
  # ── R4-2 · SUPERSEDE, DO NOT ERASE ──────────────────────────────────────
  # INLINE AND INSIDE THE FLOCK REGION (opened ccd:5278, closed ccd:5361), so
  # the values recorded are the ones being erased: a concurrent `ws-reap` holds
  # the same `$REG/.reap-<id>.lock` and can change `.archived` underneath an
  # unserialised read.
  #
  # `_lc_done` returns 0 on every path, and that is not a nicety here:
  # ccd:5330-5333 records that any new non-zero `return` between the
  # acquisition and `exec {lfd}>&-` leaks this lock in the SOURCING shell for
  # ever, and ccd is sourced by itself and by its tests.
  #
  # NO NEW REGISTRY FIELD. A 25th per-session field costs a `SessionRecord`
  # field, a `reviveFleetSession` literal change and 24 extra agent round-trips
  # per 2 s tick, for a fact the journal already carries and `_reg_purge`
  # deletes anyway.
  #
  # DISCLOSED RESIDUAL: the manifest is preserved as a BYTE TOTAL only. Read
  # fresh with `stat` because nothing in ccd reads that field back (ccd:5129
  # says so verbatim), and left ABSENT rather than 0 when it cannot be measured
  # — a fabricated 0 would argue that nothing was lost.
  _lc_done restore "$id" "" verb ws-restore \
    meas.archivedAt     "$(_reg_get "$id" archived)" \
    meas.archivedReason "$(_reg_get "$id" archivedreason)" \
    meas.manifestBytes  "$(_plat_size -- "$REG/$id.archivemanifest" 2>/dev/null)" \
    meas.workdir        "$workdir" \
    meas.branch         "$(_reg_get "$id" branch)" \
    dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
  # ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  _ws_unarchive "$id"
  # THE SAME F8 SHAPE, converted the same way — and this verb's agent budget is
  # CCD_VERB_TIMEOUT_MS['ws-restore'] = 60s, FIVE TIMES TIGHTER than the ws-add
  # budget whose expiry caused F8. The kill is likelier here, not less.
  # Same call shape, same reason: no `$( )` around `_spawn_start`, or its `die`
  # comes back as an rc 1 the `if` below does not test.
  #
  # Captured, not discarded — same M6-shaped gap as ws-add, folded into this
  # task because it owns the start paths and the spawn-rc plumbing. Supervision
  # wiring stays untouched: `_ws_supervise` still runs on a failed spawn.
  #
  # A BRANCH RATHER THAN `_spawn_start … || return $?`, WHICH IS THE SHAPE
  # EVERY OTHER CALL SITE USES — and the difference is the descriptor, not
  # taste. This is the only caller holding one, and the close is eleven lines
  # below; an early `return` between the two leaves it open. (Shipped that way
  # for one commit: the split's `|| return $?` landed here unchanged from the
  # lockless call sites, and a failed resume left the reap lock held in the
  # sourcing shell for ever.) The spawn's verdict travels in `$rc` instead, so
  # SUCCESS AND FAILURE LEAVE THE LOCKED REGION THROUGH THE SAME CLOSE and the
  # next editor has nothing to remember.
  # THE THIRD RESPAWN PATH, AND THE ONLY ONE THAT NEVER MINTED (D-2994).
  # `cmd_ensure` and `cmd_start` each initialise a missing generation before
  # they spawn; this verb reaches `_spawn_start` directly, so a row archived
  # before D-2605 came back with a pane carrying no CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION and
  # `_hook_generation_ok` failed closed for that pane's whole life. D-2993 fixed
  # the supervised path and left this one — and no respawn sweep can reach an
  # archived row, so this is the arm that heals it.
  #
  # BEST-EFFORT, NEVER FATAL, for a DIFFERENT reason than `cmd_ensure`'s: by
  # this line the restore has already cleared `archived` and put the worktree
  # back, so a `die` would strand a HALF-RESTORED row — and this sits inside the
  # reap flock region whose contract is that success and failure leave through
  # the same close (no `return` between the lock and its release, pinned by
  # `ccd-restore-reap-lock.test.ts`). rc 2 skips and CONTINUES, `cmd_start`'s
  # precedent for not gating pre-existing registry work on a mechanism it never
  # required.
  local _wr_gfd="" _wr_grc=0
  _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; _wr_grc=$?
  if (( _wr_grc == 0 )); then
    _wr_gfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
    _reg_generation_init "$id" \
      || echo "ccd: warn: $id: $REG/$id.generation is present and invalid — restoring without it; inspect it by hand" >&2
    _compact_lock_release "$_wr_gfd"; _wr_gfd=""
  else
    # NOT A SILENT FALL-THROUGH, WHICH IS THE WHOLE LESSON OF D-2993. The
    # absent-generation case took an arm that said nothing, and the fleet ran
    # inert for a day behind that silence. Both remaining codes are named — and
    # neither refuses the restore, so this is a disclosure, not a gate. It also
    # keeps `session-hook.sh`'s acquire-site sentence true: the two FUNCTIONS
    # that fall through an unknown code into a SILENT continue are still
    # `cmd_start` and `_spawn_start`, and this site is deliberately not a third.
    echo "ccd: warn: $id: $REG/$id.generation was not checked on restore —" \
         "_compact_lock_acquire rc $_wr_grc (1 = contended, 2 = no flock mechanism);" \
         "if this row carries none, the restored pane's compaction lifecycle is inert until its next respawn" >&2
  fi
  local rc=0
  if _spawn_start "$id" resume; then
    _reg_claim "$id"
    _ws_supervise "$id"
  else
    rc=$?
  fi
  # GIVEN BACK BEFORE THE SETTLE, not merely before the verb answers. The reason
  # `cmd_ws_reap` gives at its own close applies unchanged (`flock` treats two
  # `open()`s of one path in one process as two strangers, and ccd is SOURCED —
  # by `ccd` itself and by its tests — so a descriptor left open here would
  # refuse the next `ws-reap` in the same shell with `in-progress` for ever),
  # and the settle can now block for SPAWN_SETTLE_S: holding the reap lock
  # across it would answer `in-progress` to every ws-reap on this id for four
  # minutes.
  #
  # THE `die`s ABOVE NEED NO CLOSE OF THEIR OWN — and that is a statement about
  # `die`, not about early exits in general. `die` is `echo …; exit`, and the
  # kernel closes what exits; a `return` does not exit, so any path that leaves
  # this function by returning must close the descriptor EXPLICITLY. That is
  # why the spawn's failure is a captured rc above and a `return` below THIS
  # LINE rather than one above it.
  #
  # Given back regardless of the spawn's fate — the undo (archive stamps gone)
  # is real either way, which is what made the old placement unconditional too.
  exec {lfd}>&-
  # THE UNDO LANDED AND THE SESSION DID NOT COME BACK — `_lc_fail`, never
  # `_lc_refuse`: the archive stamps are already gone at ccd:5316, so calling
  # this a refusal would say nothing was touched, which is false. AFTER
  # ccd:5361's close, so it sits on no path that still holds the descriptor.
  [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || { _lc_fail restore "$id" "" spawn-failed \
      "the archive stamps were removed but $id did not come back up (spawn rc $rc)" \
      dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
    return "$rc"; }
  _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
  # rc 6 too — argued at `cmd_ws_add`'s copy of this `if`.
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$rc" -eq 4 || "$rc" -eq 5 || "$rc" -eq 6 ]]; then
    _lc_fail restore "$id" "" spawn-failed \
      "the archive stamps were removed but $id did not settle (spawn rc $rc)" \
      dec.surface "$lc_surface" dec.actor "$lc_actor" dec.reason "$lc_reason"
    echo "ccd: ws-restore spawn failed for $id (spawn rc $rc) — see $REG/$id.spawn" >&2
    return "$rc"
  fi
  echo "restored $id — $workdir"
}

_attic_project() {   # id -> the project owning its attic refs
  # Registry first, tombstone second: after a reap the registry is gone, and
  # the attic is exactly what the user still needs to reach.
  local id="$1" p
  p=$(_reg_get "$id" project); [[ -n "$p" ]] && { printf '%s\n' "$p"; return 0; }
  p=$(sed -n 's/.*"project"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([^"]*\)".*/\1/p' "$REG/.reaped/$id.json" 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  [[ -n "$p" ]] && { printf '%s\n' "$p"; return 0; }
  return 1
}

cmd_ws_attic() {   # ccd ws-attic --session <id>   |   ccd ws-attic --drop <id>
  # --drop is terminal-only: only ['ws-attic','--session'] is whitelisted, so
  # the PWA cannot reach it. Attic refs are never garbage-collected on a timer.
  [[ $# -eq 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-attic --session <id> | ccd ws-attic --drop <id>"
  local mode=$1 id=$2
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
  local project; project=$(_attic_project "$id") || die "no such session: $id"
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  case "$mode" in
    --session)
      git -C "$main" for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' "refs/ccrc/attic/$id/" 2>/dev/null
      ;;
    --drop)
      local n=0 ref
      while IFS= read -r ref; do
        [[ -n "$ref" ]] || continue
        git -C "$main" update-ref -d "$ref" && n=$(( n + 1 ))
      done < <(git -C "$main" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' "refs/ccrc/attic/$id/" 2>/dev/null)
      # AFTER the loop, and that is not style: `$n` is the destroyed count and
      # it does not exist until ccd:5227 has run. An emit above the loop can
      # only report zero, which on a destructive verb is a fabricated
      # measurement — the class this file names by number.
      _lc_done attic-drop "$id" "" meas.dropped "$n" meas.project "$project"
      echo "dropped $n attic ref$( (( n == 1 )) || echo s ) for $id"
      ;;
    *) die "usage: ccd ws-attic --session <id> | ccd ws-attic --drop <id>" ;;
  esac
}

_pr_state_one() {   # id main repo rows [answered-at-ms] [rows-head-branch] -> one JSON line
  # `$5` is WHEN gh ANSWERED `$4`, in milliseconds, and it belongs to the answer
  # rather than to this session: `cmd_pr_state` takes it once, at the read, and
  # hands the same value to every session of a `--project` sweep. That is what
  # makes `_pr_py`'s compare-and-set rank ANSWERS. It is optional and `""` is the
  # honest default for a caller that has none — `_pr_py` then falls back to its
  # own clock, which is the pre-fix behaviour and not a refusal.
  #
  # `$6` is WHICH BRANCH gh was asked about for `$4`, and it is here for the
  # same reason `$5` is: it is a property of the ANSWER, not of this session, so
  # it travels with the rows rather than being re-derived beside them. Since the
  # `--session` path started sending `--head`, the branch is read once — to
  # build the question — and the binding below must use THAT string and not a
  # second read of the field, because the two reads are now separated by a
  # network call `PR_GH_TIMEOUT` bounds at twelve seconds. A `ws-rename` or a
  # hand-edited registry landing in that window would otherwise have this
  # function bind branch B's rows against branch C: `bound()` cannot match, the
  # phase collapses to `none`, and `none` is the answer that CLEARS the
  # persisted `prnumber` and appends the outgoing PR to the append-only
  # `.prhistory` — a local fact erasing GitHub's answer about a PR, which is
  # exactly what the drift rung below refuses to do. Answering about the branch
  # gh was asked about is coherent and at most one sweep stale; the next sweep
  # asks about the new name.
  #
  # `""` — every `--project` caller, and the four-argument calls the pr-state
  # tests make — means "these rows are the whole repository's, not one
  # branch's", and the registry is then the only source there is.
  local id="$1" main="$2" repo="$3" rows="$4" answeredAt="${5:-}" rowsHead="${6:-}"
  local project branch base baseShort tip ahead workdir dirty tmpl
  project=$(_reg_get "$id" project)
  branch="$rowsHead"; [[ -n "$branch" ]] || branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  base=$(_reg_get "$id" base);       workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  # CARRIES THE ID. A per-session failure and a whole-repo failure are
  # different events: the server backs a PROJECT off on the id-less shape,
  # which three sites emit — `_gh_repo_slug`, `_gh_pr_list`, and `_pr_py`
  # mode `state` for an unparseable rc-0 body — and each of those really is
  # one gh answer for the whole repo, so every session of it is equally
  # unreadable. An id-less object HERE, by contrast, would let one
  # registry-incomplete workspace mark its seven siblings `unknown` and
  # silence the whole project's lane. §6's "Partial sweep" row promises
  # per-session honesty; this is where that is kept.
  [[ -n "$branch" ]] || { printf '{"id":%s,"phase":"unknown","reason":"error"}\n' "$(_json_str "$id")"; return 0; }
  [[ -n "$base" ]] || base="origin/HEAD"
  baseShort="${base#origin/}"
  # `--quiet` turns git's own two answers into two exit codes, and the same
  # three-way case `_ws_archive_manifest` walks (ccd:1138-1143) is walked here for
  # the same reason. Measured on git 2.43: rc 1 for an absent ref in a readable
  # repository, rc 128 when the repository cannot be read at all — and WITHOUT
  # `--quiet`, 128 for both, which collapses "the branch was renamed" into "I
  # cannot see this repository". `tip=""` here means the FIRST of those and is
  # emitted as JSON null; the second is not a fact about a PR at all, so it is
  # this session's failure object and persists nothing.
  #
  # The `tip=""` arm is an EQUIVALENT mutant, disclosed rather than pinned: the
  # command substitution already assigned "" when rev-parse printed nothing, so
  # deleting the assignment changes no value. It is written out because the arm
  # is where a reader learns that rc 1 is a FIELD and not a failure, and an empty
  # arm is indistinguishable from an unhandled one.
  local rc
  tip=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null); rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) : ;;
    1) tip="" ;;
    *) printf '{"id":%s,"phase":"unknown","reason":"error"}\n' "$(_json_str "$id")"; return 0 ;;
  esac
  # "" is UNMEASURED, not zero. `ahead=0` is the claim "this branch is level with
  # base", which is what `no-commits` is built from, and a ref that does not
  # resolve has not been measured against anything. _pr_py reads "" as null.
  ahead=""
  if [[ -n "$tip" ]] && git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "$base" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # `--` after the revisions per Global Constraints, and it SURVIVES mutation
    # by construction: `$base` comes from the registry and always yields a
    # `..`-bearing operand, which git can only read as a range, so no fixture can
    # distinguish the two forms. Followed anyway — git's disambiguation is
    # positional, and the rule is cheaper than re-deciding it per call site.
    ahead=$(git -C "$main" rev-list --count "$base..refs/heads/$branch" -- 2>/dev/null)
    # The GUARD and its FALLBACK VALUE both survive mutation, and for the same
    # reason: both revisions were verified a line ago, so rev-list has no way to
    # fail here today and nothing can observe which value the guard would have
    # written. Kept because `int(ahead)` in _pr_py raises on a non-numeric string
    # and this session's whole line then disappears from the sweep's output; `""`
    # rather than `0` because an unwritten count is UNMEASURED, which is what the
    # rest of this function now says, and `0` would say "level with base".
    [[ "$ahead" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || ahead=""
  fi
  # THE ONE READ IN THIS VERB THAT IS ABOUT A DIRECTORY. ccrc asks $main;
  # $workdir is otherwise only ever a path git is asked ABOUT — but "what is
  # uncommitted here" is a question about that directory's contents, so it is
  # `-C "$workdir"` and therefore has to be ENTITLED first: git's own record AND
  # the directory itself must both say $main, the pair `cmd_ws_rm` requires
  # before it touches anything and `_ws_archive_manifest` walks for the identical
  # read. Neither rung alone is enough — the record outlives the directory, so a
  # hand-deletion plus a stray `git init` still answers $branch while its tree
  # belongs to a stranger. Measured: without the common-dir rung the squatter's
  # two untracked files were counted and reported as this workspace's.
  #
  # UNENTITLED OR UNREADABLE IS `null`, NOT A REFUSAL AND NEVER 0. `dirty` is an
  # advisory — the PR state is entirely knowable when gh answered and the
  # worktree did not — and this verb answers for every workspace of a project
  # from one gh call, so refusing here would cost the siblings their state too.
  # `ws-archive` refuses on these same facts because it writes a record that
  # authorises deletions; this one only reports. What is NOT allowed is `0`: the
  # shipped one-liner piped into `grep -c` and threw git's status away, so a
  # deleted worktree answered `"dirty": 0` — an affirmative "nothing is
  # uncommitted here" about a directory that is not there. That is deviation 6's
  # silent-empty-stream defect, one task later in the same file. `registered` and
  # the status are read from `$?` on their own lines: `local x=$(cmd)` returns
  # local's status, not the command's.
  #
  # Two of the three rungs survive mutation and are kept rather than pinned,
  # because each is redundant only GIVEN the others and the rule names all three:
  #   - `registered != 0` — a path whose common dir equals `$main`'s is inside
  #     `$main`'s repository, and `git status` from anywhere in a worktree
  #     reports that worktree, so the count is the same with or without this
  #     rung today. It is the rung the plan's rule names FIRST, and it is the one
  #     that stops answering if `_ws_common_dir` is ever loosened.
  #   - `-z "$main_common"` — an unreadable `$main` makes both sides empty and
  #     therefore equal, which is the fail-OPEN this guard exists to stop; it is
  #     unreachable only because the tip read above already returned on rc 128.
  # The `wd_common != main_common` rung is the one that catches the squatter, and
  # it is pinned.
  local wthead registered wd_common main_common porcelain prc
  dirty=""
  wthead=$(_ws_wt_branch "$main" "$workdir"); registered=$?
  # ── DRIFT IS NOT A PHASE, AND THIS VERB MAY NOT INVENT ONE ────────
  # The defect: `swift-delta`'s `.prphase` read `no-commits` while its worktree
  # held 27 commits — on a DIFFERENT branch. Everything above measured the
  # REGISTRY's `branch`, which still resolved and was still level with its base,
  # so `ahead=0` was true about a branch nobody was working in, and `no-commits`
  # is exactly the claim built from it. Downstream that is not cosmetic: it
  # hard-disables the PWA's "Open pull request" composer (only `none` renders
  # it), it is in `verifyDone`'s REGRESSED set, and the first poll of the
  # mismatch retires the live `prnumber` into the append-only `.prhistory`.
  #
  # AND THE FIX IS NOT TO SWITCH THE SOURCE. Unlike `ws-reap` — which destroys
  # the worktree and the branch git has checked out, so git's record is the
  # subject of the act — this verb BINDS A REMOTE PR TO A NAME AND WRITES THAT
  # BINDING DOWN. Measuring git's branch instead would silently re-bind every
  # drifted workspace on the fleet at the next 120 s sweep: `bound()` fails on
  # the old name, `prnumber` is cleared and one line is appended to the ledger
  # that `_ws_archive_manifest` folds into archive records — irreversible, and
  # done before anyone looked. Every other verb that meets drift either refuses
  # and names both records (`cmd_pr_open`, `cmd_ws_rename`) or records both as
  # fields (`_ws_archive_manifest`: "DRIFT IS A FIELD, NOT A REFUSAL"); a poller
  # that picked would be the only one in ccd that resolves drift by rewriting
  # lineage.
  #
  # So: say `unknown`, name both, PERSIST NOTHING — `_pr_py state` is what
  # persists, and this returns before it, leaving `prphase`/`prnumber` at their
  # last honest values. `unknown` is the FAILED-READ bucket (`prVerdict` reads
  # it as `unmeasurable`, never `regressed`), which is what this is: nobody
  # measured this workspace's PR, because there are two candidate answers to
  # "this workspace's branch" and choosing between them is the operator's call
  # (`ccd ws-rename` reconciles them).
  #
  # `-n "$tip"` IS PART OF THE CONDITION, not decoration. It narrows this to the
  # shape that lies: the registry's branch still RESOLVES, so the measurement
  # above produced a confident answer about the wrong branch. When it does not
  # resolve — the rename shape — `tip` is already null and `ahead` already
  # unmeasured, so this function is already saying "I did not measure that", and
  # the four pinned cases of that shape keep their behaviour untouched.
  if (( registered == 0 )) && [[ -n "$wthead" && -n "$tip" && "$wthead" != "$branch" ]]; then
    echo "pr-state: the registry says $branch, git's worktree record for $workdir says $wthead — PR state not measured for $id (reconcile with: ccd ws-rename --session $id --branch $wthead)" >&2
    printf '{"id":%s,"phase":"unknown","reason":"branch-drift"}\n' "$(_json_str "$id")"
    return 0
  fi
  wd_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$workdir"); main_common=$(_ws_common_dir "$main")
  if (( registered != 0 )) || [[ -z "$main_common" || "$wd_common" != "$main_common" ]]; then
    echo "pr-state: $workdir is not a corroborated worktree of $main — dirty unknown for $id" >&2
  else
    # THE STDERR RUNG, final-round verification P2. `dirty` already has an
    # honest UNMEASURED value — `""`, which becomes JSON `null` — and this was
    # the branch that failed to use it: a partially unreadable tree exits ZERO
    # with the diagnostic on stderr (measured, git 2.43), so `grep -c .` counted
    # the empty stdout and the wire carried `"dirty":0`, an affirmative "nothing
    # is uncommitted here" about files nobody looked at. Same shape, same round,
    # same fix as `cmd_ws_rm` and `_ws_gc_dirty`; here it lands on `null` rather
    # than on a refusal, because this function REPORTS and those two DESTROY.
    local perrf perr
    perrf=$(_plat_mktemp) || perrf=""
    if [[ -z "$perrf" ]]; then
      echo "pr-state: could not make a scratch file to read $workdir — dirty unknown for $id" >&2
    else
      porcelain=$(git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain 2>"$perrf"); prc=$?
      perr=$(cat "$perrf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$perrf"
      if (( prc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$perr" ]]; then dirty=$(printf '%s' "$porcelain" | grep -c . || true)
      else echo "pr-state: could not read the tree at $workdir — dirty unknown for $id" >&2; fi
    fi
  fi
  tmpl=""
  [[ -f "$main/.github/pull_request_template.md" ]] && tmpl=$(cat "$main/.github/pull_request_template.md")
  printf '%s' "$rows" | _pr_py state "$id" "$project" "$main" "$repo" "$branch" "$base" \
    "$baseShort" "$tip" "$ahead" "$dirty" "$tmpl" "$REG" "$answeredAt"
}

cmd_pr_state() {   # ccd pr-state --session <id> | ccd pr-state --project <project>
  # A CONSTANT NUMBER OF gh CALLS PER REPO, never one per session — one for
  # `--session`, two for `--project`, and neither number moves when a project
  # grows a ninth workspace. That was "ONE gh call per repo, whichever form"
  # until `--project` had to stop asking for a hundred check rollups GitHub
  # answers with `HTTP 504`; the rollups are a second call now, and the budget
  # argument the sentence was making (8 projects x 1 call / 120 s ~ 5 % of the
  # GraphQL allowance) survives doubling and would not survive going per
  # session. Read-only apart from the three registry fields it persists so a
  # ccrc restart degrades to honest stale.
  [[ $# -eq 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd pr-state --session <id> | ccd pr-state --project <project>"
  local mode=$1 arg=$2
  case "$mode" in
    --session) [[ $arg =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id" ;;
    --project) [[ $arg =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad project" ;;
    *)         die "usage: ccd pr-state --session <id> | ccd pr-state --project <project>" ;;
  esac

  local -a ids=()
  if [[ $mode == --session ]]; then
    [[ -f "$REG/$arg.uuid" ]] || die "no such session: $arg"
    [[ -n "$(_reg_get "$arg" workspace)" ]] || die "not a workspace: $arg"
    ids=("$arg")
  else
    local f id
    for f in "$REG"/*.workspace; do
      # An empty registry leaves the glob unexpanded. Survives mutation because
      # the `.uuid` test below then looks for a file literally named `*.uuid`
      # and fails too — an accident of two guards, not one guard doing two jobs.
      [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
      id=$(basename "$f" .workspace)
      # The same regex `--session` applies to its argv, applied to what the
      # directory listing handed back — every `$REG/$id.*` path below is built
      # from it. The plan's rule is "before any path is built from an id", with
      # no exception for ids ccd wrote itself; a filename cannot hold a `/` so
      # nothing traversable is reachable and every read here is read-only, which
      # makes this the rule rather than a hole being closed.
      [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || continue
      [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || continue
      [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" project)" == "$arg" ]] || continue
      ids+=("$id")
    done
  fi
  (( ${#ids[@]} )) || return 0

  local project main repo rows answeredAt head=""
  project=$(_reg_get "${ids[0]}" project)
  # A registry VALUE, and `$main` is built from it. `--session` validated the id
  # it was handed and nothing validated this. `die`, not an answer object,
  # because a project name ccd cannot build a path from is a registry-IDENTITY
  # error — the class this verb already dies on (`no such session`, `not a
  # workspace`) — and it costs the repo's one gh call per sweep to proceed.
  [[ "$project" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad project in registry for ${ids[0]}"
  main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  repo=$(_gh_repo_slug "$main") || { printf '{"phase":"unknown","reason":"no-remote"}\n'; return 0; }
  # ── `--session` ASKS ABOUT ONE BRANCH, SO IT SAYS SO ───────────────
  # Measured 2026-08-26 against a live fleet repo with several thousand PRs of
  # history — the slug is operator residue and stays out of this tree. The
  # unfiltered call this line used to make answered `HTTP 504` after 11.2 s on
  # 3/3 attempts; `--head <branch>` answered the same question, with the same
  # fields, in 0.78 s. The wide window was ninety-nine other branches' PRs
  # fetched, annotated with a `git cat-file` and a `merge-base` apiece, shipped
  # over the wire and then dropped by the first conjunct that reads them.
  #
  # NARROWING IS SAFE BECAUSE EVERY READER OF THESE ROWS ALREADY CONJOINS
  # `headRefName == branch`, so a head-filtered answer is a strict SUPERSET of
  # what anything reads — not a judgement, an enumeration: `_pr_py`'s `bound()`,
  # its `is_merged()` (which conjoins `bound`) and its `pick()` (which filters by
  # it) on this side; `boundRow()` and `isMergedRow()` on the server's, where
  # `line.rows` has exactly ONE reader, `phaseFor`'s `boundRow(line.rows, …)`.
  # Nothing iterates the rows for another branch. `--head` also matches
  # `headRefName` ACROSS fork owners, so it cannot even drop the stranger's-PR
  # rows the `isCrossRepository` conjunct exists to refuse — the filter takes
  # away nothing a conjunct was going to be shown.
  #
  # `--project` KEEPS THE WIDE WINDOW, and that is the whole reason this is a
  # variable rather than an unconditional flag: that form asks about every
  # workspace of the repo from one call, and the only branch in scope here is
  # `ids[0]`'s. A `--head` built from it would filter the sweep on the FIRST
  # session's branch and answer `none` for every sibling — clearing each one's
  # `prnumber` and retiring it into the append-only `.prhistory`.
  #
  # READ BEFORE THE CALL, AND HANDED DOWN. The same string is passed to
  # `_pr_state_one` below as the branch these rows were fetched for, so the
  # question and the binding cannot be two different reads of a field a
  # `ws-rename` can change during a twelve-second network call; the reasoning is
  # at `_pr_state_one`'s own signature. An empty value (a registry with no
  # `branch` for this id) sends no flag and re-reads the field — the pre-fix
  # behaviour, ending in that function's id-carrying `error` object.
  [[ $mode == --session ]] && head=$(_reg_get "$arg" branch)
  # The answer object _gh_pr_list printed IS the output on failure: one repo
  # failing marks that repo's sessions unknown and touches no other project.
  rows=$(_gh_pr_list "$repo" "$head") || { printf '%s\n' "$rows"; return 0; }
  # ONE STAMP FOR THE WHOLE FAN-OUT, TAKEN AT THE ANSWER — re-review of the fix
  # wave. `_pr_py`'s compare-and-set persists only the NEWEST answer, and it can
  # only tell which that is if `prcheckedat` describes WHEN GITHUB ANSWERED. The
  # stamp used to be taken inside python, per session, after `rows_in()` and
  # after that session's `git log` — while this verb reads the rows in ONE gh
  # call and then loops over every session of the repo below (the rollup call
  # added under it is a second call and carries no stamp: it annotates an answer
  # rather than being one). Eight workspaces on one repo, and
  # the k-th session's stamp was seconds newer than the answer it carried: the
  # CAS ranked COMPLETION TIMES, which is not a fact about GitHub at all.
  #
  # What that cost, at the wave boundary this feature exists for: the sweep's gh
  # returns at T0 binding #577; the operator opens the PR sheet at T0+0.4s and
  # its own `pr-state --session` read returns at T0+1.0s binding the NEW #583 (a
  # merge landed between the two reads) and persists it; the sweep reaches that
  # session at T0+1.4s, out-stamps the newer answer, retires still-live #583 into
  # the append-only ledger `_ws_archive_manifest` folds in verbatim, and writes
  # `prnumber` back to #577. A manifest that lies is a manifest that authorises a
  # deletion. With the stamp taken here, every session of this sweep carries T0
  # and the sheet's newer answer stands.
  #
  # HERE rather than inside `_gh_pr_list`: its answer comes back through a
  # command substitution — a SUBSHELL — so a variable it set would not survive to
  # this line, and its stdout is the rows, shared with `cmd_pr_open` and
  # `_ws_reap_eval`. This is the first statement after the answer arrives; the
  # gap is a fork's return, not a sweep's.
  #
  # NOT NUMERIC IS UNMEASURED. `%3N` is GNU; a `date` that prints it literally
  # leaves `answeredAt=""` and `_pr_py` falls back to its own clock, which is
  # precisely the pre-fix behaviour. This guard may only remove races, never add
  # a refusal that stops a phase from ever updating.
  answeredAt=$(_plat_epoch_ms 2>/dev/null)
  [[ "$answeredAt" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || answeredAt=""

  # ── THE ROLLUPS, WHICH THE WIDE WINDOW CANNOT CARRY ────────────────
  # `_gh_pr_list` sends `statusCheckRollup` only with a head filter, because a
  # 100-PR window carrying it is answered `HTTP 504` and no field list makes
  # that cheaper (the measurement is at that function). `--project` is the form
  # that has no head filter and still has a reader for the field: the server
  # calls `checksFor` on the ONE row `boundRow` binds, per line. So the rollups
  # come from their own call and are joined onto the rows by number.
  #
  # AFTER THE STAMP, NEVER BEFORE. `answeredAt` has to describe when GITHUB
  # ANSWERED THE ROWS — it is what `_pr_py`'s compare-and-set ranks answers by,
  # and the paragraph above spells out what a stamp taken later costs. This call
  # is a second network round trip; taking it before the stamp would age every
  # session's answer by however long GitHub took over an annotation.
  #
  # `--project` AND NOT "no head": those are the same set today, and they say
  # different things. `--session` with a registry that has lost its `branch`
  # also sends no filter — and `_pr_state_one` answers that session with its
  # id-carrying `error` object without reading a row, so spending a second gh
  # call on it would buy an annotation for an answer that does not exist.
  #
  # NOTHING HERE MAY COST THE ROWS. Both `_gh_pr_checks` and the join can fail
  # and each failure leaves `$rows` exactly as it arrived: the first because a
  # failed call assigns nothing (the `||` sets the empty string), the second
  # because `rows` is only replaced on the join's own exit 0. A missing rollup
  # is `checks: null` at the far end and nothing else — never a phase, never a
  # reason, never the id-less object that would back the whole project off.
  local rollups="" joined="" jrc=0
  # `local -x`: dynamically scoped AND exported, so `_pr_py state` — a python3
  # CHILD, several frames down inside `_pr_state_one` — reads it from the
  # environment without a fourteenth positional argument threaded through a
  # signature three call sites share. It unwinds when this function returns, so
  # nothing leaks into a later verb.
  #
  # `--project` ONLY. In `--session` the rollup rides the head-filtered row
  # window in-band, so if the rows answered the rollup is there; there is no
  # second call to fail and nothing to declare.
  local -x PR_CHECKS_UNMEASURED=0
  if [[ $mode == --project ]]; then
    rollups=$(_gh_pr_checks "$repo") || rollups=""
    # THE CALL DID NOT ANSWER. Not "this repo has no checks" — a timeout
    # (PR_GH_CHECKS_TIMEOUT), a 5xx, an unparseable body. Say so, because the
    # rows below will carry no rollup key and their bare absence is the same
    # shape a genuinely check-less PR wears.
    [[ -n "$rollups" ]] || PR_CHECKS_UNMEASURED=1
    if [[ -n "$rollups" ]]; then
      # `jrc` on its own line, and the declaration above initialises all three:
      # `local joined=$(...)` returns LOCAL's status rather than the join's — the
      # trap this file has already been bitten by twice — and a bare `local x`
      # leaves x unset, which `set -u` aborts on the moment a stubbed caller
      # reaches a reference to it.
      joined=$(printf '%s' "$rows" | _pr_py rollups "$PR_CHECK_FIELD" 4<<<"$rollups"); jrc=$?
      if (( jrc == 0 )) && [[ -n "$joined" ]]; then
        rows="$joined"
      else
        # The call ANSWERED and the pass that attaches its answer is what broke.
        # Same consequence for the reader as a failed call — no rollup key on any
        # row — so it earns the same declaration. The rows themselves are kept
        # untouched, which is the isolation this split exists for.
        PR_CHECKS_UNMEASURED=1
      fi
    fi
  fi

  local id
  # `$head` again, as the sixth argument: the rows, the moment they were
  # answered and the branch they were answered ABOUT are one answer, and all
  # three are handed down together. `""` on the `--project` fan-out says these
  # rows are the whole repository's, which is what makes the same loop right for
  # both forms.
  for id in "${ids[@]}"; do _pr_state_one "$id" "$main" "$repo" "$rows" "$answeredAt" "$head"; done
}

cmd_pr_open() {   # ccd pr-open --session <id> --title <t> --body-b64 <b64> --draft <true|false>
  # The ONE additive write in this whole design. Fixed arity, no getopt loop,
  # no "$@" passthrough (pflag is last-wins, so a passthrough would let a
  # caller override -R/--head/--base), and no flag anywhere takes a path.
  [[ $# -eq 8 && $1 == --session && $3 == --title && $5 == --body-b64 && $7 == --draft ]] \
    || die "usage: ccd pr-open --session <id> --title <title> --body-b64 <base64> --draft <true|false>"
  local id=$2 title=$4 b64=$6 draft=$8
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]]     || die "bad session id"
  [[ -n $title ]]                    || die "empty title"
  (( ${#title} <= 256 ))             || die "title too long"
  [[ $title != *[[:cntrl:]]* ]]      || die "title has control characters"
  [[ $draft == true || $draft == false ]] || die "bad --draft"

  local body bytes
  body=$(printf '%s' "$b64" | base64 -d 2>/dev/null) || die "bad --body-b64"
  [[ -n $body ]] || die "empty body"   # an empty --body SUPPRESSES the repo template
  bytes=$(printf '%s' "$body" | wc -c)
  (( bytes <= 65536 )) || die "body too large"
  printf '%s' "$body" | iconv -f UTF-8 -t UTF-8 >/dev/null 2>&1 || die "body is not valid UTF-8"

  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "no such session: $id"
  # Workspace-only, ALWAYS. Without this the verb would push --set-upstream
  # origin main on its first step for nine of nine live sessions.
  [[ -n "$(_reg_get "$id" workspace)" ]] \
    || die "not a workspace — refusing to open a PR from a main checkout"

  local project workdir branch base baseShort main repo
  project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch);   base=$(_reg_get "$id" base)
  [[ -n "$project" && -n "$workdir" && -n "$branch" && -n "$base" ]] \
    || die "incomplete registry for '$id'"
  baseShort="${base#origin/}"
  [[ "$branch" != "$baseShort" ]] || die "head equals base"
  main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"

  # The registry NAMES the branch; git's own worktree record is what entitles us
  # to push it. Read from $main and nowhere else (`_ws_wt_branch`, ccd:3671-3681),
  # the same three rungs cmd_ws_rm walks (ccd:406, :416, :428) and
  # _ws_reap_eval re-proves, in refusal form. TWO statements, deliberately:
  # `local wthead=$(...)` returns LOCAL's status, so `registered` would be 0 for
  # every path and rung 3 would be dead code.
  local wthead registered
  wthead=$(_ws_wt_branch "$main" "$workdir"); registered=$?
  (( registered == 0 )) \
    || die "$main has no worktree record for $workdir — refusing to push a branch nothing corroborates"
  [[ -n "$wthead" ]] \
    || die "$main records $workdir on a detached HEAD — there is no branch to open a PR from"
  [[ "$wthead" == "$branch" ]] \
    || die "the registry says $branch, git's worktree record for $workdir says $wthead — refusing to push either"

  # This is why the verb lives in ccd at all: the agent's exec op passes no
  # cwd, its cwd is $HOME which is not a repo, and the server cannot read
  # ~/worktrees/* (checkPath, whitelist.ts:83-90).
  repo=$(_gh_repo_slug "$main") || die "no origin remote for $project"

  # Idempotence, before anything is pushed or created, and it FAILS CLOSED.
  # `_gh_pr_list` (ccd:218) is the read Task 3 already hardened: it captures
  # gh's exit status and classifies its stderr. What stood here open-coded the
  # same call with `2>/dev/null` and captured STDOUT ALONE — and rc 124, an
  # expired token, a rate limit and an offline box all produce empty stdout,
  # which is indistinguishable from "no PR". Measured, with gh exiting 124 on
  # the probe: rc 0, the branch on the origin, one `pr create` issued. A bounded
  # write may not proceed on an unproven "no PR exists"; the concrete loss is a
  # DUPLICATE PR whenever the existing one is cross-repository, because our own
  # create then succeeds.
  #
  # `--head` matches across fork owners, so a stranger's PR on the same branch
  # name reads as "exists" and we decline to create — the fail-SAFE direction.
  # What the UI believes is decided by pr-state's binding, not by this line.
  local existing opened rc reason
  existing=$(_gh_pr_list "$repo" "$branch"); rc=$?
  if (( rc != 0 )); then
    # `_gh_pr_list` prints its answer OBJECT on failure; the classified reason
    # is the part of it this verb has to say out loud. "gh failed" would send
    # the reader to check `gh auth` for a box that is merely offline.
    reason="${existing##*\"reason\":\"}"; reason="${reason%%\"*}"
    die "could not check for an existing PR on $branch ($reason) — nothing was pushed"
  fi
  _gh_rows_ok "$existing" \
    || die "gh answered something that is not a list of PRs — nothing was pushed"
  if [[ "$existing" != "[]" ]]; then printf '%s\n' "$existing"; return 0; fi

  # Run in $main, NEVER in $workdir — the one network write in this design, so
  # the repository it is asked of matters more here than anywhere else. $main
  # owns refs/heads/$branch (a linked worktree's commits are in the shared object
  # store, so this sends exactly the workspace's work — verified) and $main is
  # the repository whose remote.origin.url produced $repo above, so the slug in
  # `gh pr create` and the ref on the wire name ONE repository. Resolved in
  # $workdir they name two: a stranger repo at that path answers
  # refs/heads/$branch with ITS commit, and measured on git 2.43 with an `origin`
  # pointing at ours it lands on OUR ref, after which the create call opens a PR
  # over a stranger's work. Nothing above reads $workdir either — it is a KEY
  # into git's records here, not a repository this verb talks to.
  # Fully qualified both sides, NEVER --force, branch from the registry and
  # corroborated by git, never from the request. The SOURCE half is what a test
  # can tell apart: with a same-named tag in $main — release tooling makes them
  # from branch names — a bare `ws/x` is "src refspec matches more than one" and
  # the one bounded write fails outright. The destination half is EQUIVALENT and
  # stays anyway: measured with `refs/tags/ws/x` present on both sides, a
  # qualified src alone lands on `refs/heads/ws/x` exactly as the pair does, so
  # no fixture can distinguish them — it is written out because the ref this
  # sends to is the one thing a reader of this line most needs stated.
  # Failure aborts before any gh call.
  # --set-upstream writes branch.<b>.remote/merge into the COMMON config,
  # so the workspace sees the upstream ws-reap later requires (verified: byte-
  # identical keys and values to the same push run inside the worktree).
  # STDOUT is dropped because this verb's stdout is its ANSWER: `--set-upstream`
  # prints "branch 'x' set up to track 'origin/x'." there, so the create path
  # used to answer that sentence followed by the rows, which is not JSON. The
  # ref update summary and every failure go to stderr, where the route wants
  # them.
  git -C "$main" push --set-upstream origin "refs/heads/$branch:refs/heads/$branch" >/dev/null \
    || die "push failed for $branch — nothing was opened"

  # `--head "$branch"` and `--head "$wthead"` are EQUIVALENT here and no fixture
  # can tell them apart: the drift rung above dies unless the two strings are
  # equal, so by this line they are the same string. `$branch` is written
  # because the registry's name is what this verb is FOR — `$wthead` would read
  # as "whatever git last saw", which is the reading the corroboration exists to
  # rule out. `>/dev/null` on the call is not equivalent: real `gh pr create`
  # prints the new PR's URL on stdout, and this verb's stdout is its answer.
  local -a args=(pr create --repo "$repo" --head "$branch" --base "$baseShort"
                 --title "$title" --body "$body")
  [[ "$draft" == true ]] && args+=(--draft)
  # The push is NOT rolled back if this fails: the ref on the origin is the
  # workspace's own work, lossless and what a retry needs to find there, and an
  # automatic `push --delete` would be a destructive rollback of a state nobody
  # lost anything to. So the refusal NAMES what it left behind, the way the
  # push's own refusal says "nothing was opened" about a state where truly
  # nothing was — the route surfaces exactly this stderr in its 502.
  _plat_timeout "$PR_GH_TIMEOUT" gh "${args[@]}" >/dev/null \
    || die "gh pr create failed — $branch is on origin with its upstream set, but no PR was opened"

  # The answer, read back through the SAME reader as the probe: same fields,
  # same 100-PR window, same classification. Two hand-written `--json` lists
  # made two success shapes out of one contract — `isDraft` was present on the
  # created answer and absent on the idempotent one — and a caller could tell
  # which path it had taken from a field that is supposed to describe the PR.
  # A failure here is NOT a failed open: the PR exists, and saying so is the
  # difference between "retry" and "look before you retry".
  opened=$(_gh_pr_list "$repo" "$branch"); rc=$?
  if (( rc != 0 )); then
    reason="${opened##*\"reason\":\"}"; reason="${reason%%\"*}"
    die "the PR was opened, but reading it back failed ($reason)"
  fi
  _gh_rows_ok "$opened" \
    || die "the PR was opened, but gh answered something that is not a list of PRs"
  printf '%s\n' "$opened"
}

# ── reap guards ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Evaluated by ws-audit to PRODUCE a verdict, and again by ws-reap to RE-PROVE
# it at the instant of deletion. One implementation, two evaluations at two
# times: the audit's verdict is never trusted, it is recomputed.

# THE secret-shaped name list, and now it has TWO consumers, so it is a list
# rather than a `case` — `_ws_sensitive_match` walks it for one name, and
# `_WS_SENSITIVE_FIND` is the same list as a `find` predicate for the scan
# INSIDE a collapsed ignored directory. Two hand-maintained copies of a §7
# guard's definition is exactly the drift this design cannot afford, so the
# predicate is DERIVED here, once, at source time.
#
# Small, non-rotting and permanent. `.ccrc/` deliberately does NOT match: it is
# listed and destroyed like any other ignored directory, because ccd itself put
# it in info/exclude and exempting it would be a blind spot ccd manufactured.
#
# `.env*` AND `*.env`, because dotenv has two spellings and only the first was
# here for a round: `production.env`, `prod.env` and `My Project.env` all read
# as ordinary rubbish queued for deletion in the one guard §7 forbids
# overriding, while four places claimed the case was closed by the `-z` read. It
# was not; `-z` fixed the QUOTING of `deploy key.pem` and `prod db.sqlite`,
# whose suffixes were already listed, and `My Project.env` had no arm to match
# quoted or unquoted. `env.sample` and `environment` are the negatives that keep
# this a suffix and not a substring.
_WS_SENSITIVE_GLOBS=( '.env*' '*.env' '*.pem' '*.key' '*.p12' 'id_[dre]*' '*.kdbx'
                      'credentials*' '*.sqlite*' '*.db' '*.dump' '*.sql' 'secrets*' )
_WS_SENSITIVE_FIND=()
for _wsg in "${_WS_SENSITIVE_GLOBS[@]}"; do
  (( ${#_WS_SENSITIVE_FIND[@]} )) && _WS_SENSITIVE_FIND+=( -o )
  _WS_SENSITIVE_FIND+=( -name "$_wsg" )
done
unset _wsg

_ws_sensitive_match() {   # path -> 0 when it is secret-shaped
  # `[[ $b == $g ]]` with the pattern UNQUOTED is glob matching, i.e. the same
  # operation each `case` arm used to be — including `id_[dre]*`'s bracket
  # expression. `pins the two matchers to one list` walks a table of positives
  # and negatives through this function AND through the find predicate and
  # requires the same answer from both.
  local b="${1%/}" g; b="${b##*/}"
  for g in "${_WS_SENSITIVE_GLOBS[@]}"; do
    [[ "$b" == $g ]] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# F3 REFINEMENT (pre-merge fix round; the human partner ruled directly:
# apply it). The inside-of-ignored scan enumerates every entry under a
# collapsed directory, and three of the globs above — `credentials*`,
# `secrets*`, `*.pem` — reach one directory into a dependency or build tree
# and catch its ORDINARY contents there: a TypeScript declaration file, a
# generated JS bundle and its sourcemap, a template. Measured over 15 real
# projects: 338 inside-hits, 7 of 15 projects refused on the FIRST tap, and
# essentially all of it was exactly this shape — `credentials.d.ts`,
# `cacert.pem`, 33 x `.env.example`, `secretsmanager.generated.js`,
# `secrets-manager.js.map`. A secret-shaped name that ALSO ends in a source,
# compiled or template extension is NOISE, not a secret. Measured effect:
# 32 hits, 4 of 15 projects — and the survivors still include the real
# `.env`, `.env.local`,
# `infrastructure/dist/config/{production,staging,dev}.env` and
# `dev-data/app.db`, exactly the content this guard exists for.
#
# EXCLUDED IS NOT INVISIBLE — the other half of the ruling. Every name this
# drops is still COUNTED (`REAP_SENSITIVE_FILTERED`, `sensitiveFiltered` on
# the wire), so a wrong filter is something anyone can notice from the
# audit's own output rather than a silent hole. This is a §7 no-override
# guard, so the list is narrow and enumerated rather than inferred from a
# pattern: on any doubt a name stays sensitive.
_WS_SENSITIVE_NOISE_EXT=( ts tsx js jsx mjs cjs map py pyc pyi go rs java rb php cs c h cpp swift kt
                          example sample template dist )

_ws_sensitive_noise() {   # path -> 0 when a secret-shaped name is vendored/template NOISE
  local b="${1%/}" ext; b="${b##*/}"
  for ext in "${_WS_SENSITIVE_NOISE_EXT[@]}"; do
    [[ "$b" == *".$ext" ]] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# THE PACKAGE-MANAGER EXEMPTION, and it is this guard's OWN DEFINITION applied
# rather than a softening of it. The guard refuses on names that are
# secret-shaped, in no commit, and CANNOT BE RECOVERED -- and that third clause
# is simply false inside an installed dependency tree. Every byte of
# `site-packages` comes back from `requirements.txt`, every byte of
# `node_modules` from the lockfile. A reinstallable file is not an
# unrecoverable secret, so refusing on one protects nothing and costs the
# operator the whole workspace.
#
# WHY F3's EXTENSION FILTER STRUCTURALLY CANNOT REACH THESE. `_ws_sensitive_noise`
# drops a match whose basename ends in a source/compiled/template extension,
# which cannot catch either shape a real venv produces: certifi's `cacert.pem`
# -- a PUBLIC CA trust bundle, shipped identically in every install -- ends in
# `.pem`, the very suffix that flagged it, and botocore's `data/secretsmanager`
# is an API-model DIRECTORY with no extension at all. Measured on one 557 MB
# `.venv`: four hits, all four under `site-packages`, refusing a section 7
# no-override guard with nothing an operator could pass to get past it. F3's own
# note records 4 of 15 projects still refusing after the extension pass -- this
# is that residue, and no length of extension list reaches it.
#
# NARROW AND ENUMERATED, the same discipline `_WS_SENSITIVE_NOISE_EXT` states:
# only segments that are unambiguously a package manager's install root.
# `vendor` is DELIBERATELY ABSENT -- Go and composer both use it, but so does
# any project that simply has a folder of that name, and on doubt a name stays
# sensitive.
#
# Matched as a PATH SEGMENT, never a substring: the operand is padded to
# `/a/b/c/` and tested for `*/site-packages/*`, so a directory called
# `my-site-packages-notes` holding a real `deploy.pem` is untouched.
#
# COUNTED, NEVER SILENCED. Both call sites route this through the same
# `REAP_SENSITIVE_FILTERED` arm F3 uses, so an over-broad segment shows up in
# `ccd ws-audit` output instead of becoming a silent hole.
_WS_VENDOR_SEGMENTS=( site-packages dist-packages node_modules )

_ws_sensitive_vendored() {   # path -> 0 when it sits INSIDE a package-manager tree
  # THE DIRECTORY CHAIN, NOT THE WHOLE PATH. `inside a tree` is the claim, so
  # the final component is dropped before the test: a path that merely IS a
  # segment (`node_modules`, `site-packages`) is not inside one, and a bare
  # name has no chain at all. Unreachable via the two live call sites today --
  # neither segment name matches _WS_SENSITIVE_GLOBS, so the filter is never
  # asked about them -- but this helper states a property, and the prune
  # follow-up in the inside-scan would consume it directly.
  local d="${1%/}" seg
  case "$d" in
    */*) d="/${d%/*}/" ;;
    *)   d="/" ;;
  esac
  for seg in "${_WS_VENDOR_SEGMENTS[@]}"; do
    [[ "$d" == *"/$seg/"* ]] && return 0
  done
  return 1
}

# ── D1: the nested-checkout guard ──────────────────────────────────────────
# A registered child, a plain `git init`, a clone, a linked-worktree pointer
# file: every one of them is a `.git` entry at depth ≥ 2. `git worktree list`
# structurally cannot see the independent ones (measured), so this is a
# FILESYSTEM walk. rc 0 = the walk completed (stdout may be empty); rc 1 = it
# did not, with the reason in _WS_NESTED_WHY — same polarity as every Phase B
# read: unreadable refuses, it never guesses.
_WS_NESTED_WHY=''
_ws_nested_checkouts() {   # dir -> resolved checkout roots, one per line
  local dir outf errf err rc f p
  _WS_NESTED_WHY=''
  # Resolve the OPERAND before it ever reaches `find`. `find`'s default `-P`
  # refuses to descend a symlink given as the exact top-level path (measured:
  # `find "$symlinkToDir" -mindepth 1` walks nothing at all — no error, rc 0,
  # empty stdout), so an unresolved symlink here would make the walk answer
  # "no nested checkouts" on a tree it never looked at. That is the one shape
  # of wrong this guard cannot afford: rc 0 with an empty answer reads as
  # certainty, and every caller proceeds straight to `git worktree remove` on
  # the strength of it. Resolving first also means every entry `find` walks,
  # and everything the loop below prints, already hangs off the real root.
  # `>/dev/null` on the cd, not merely `2>` — same reasoning as `_ws_realpath`
  # above: a `cd` exported as a shell function (BASH_FUNC_cd%%, the shape
  # zoxide/direnv/a .bashrc `cd` wrapper take) prints whatever it likes on
  # STDOUT, straight into this capture, and that is reachable from `cmd_ws_rm`
  # in a real shell. `2>/dev/null` alone left `$dir` carrying the hook's own
  # line ahead of `pwd -P`'s, which sent a corrupt path into `find` below and
  # refused a perfectly healthy workspace.
  dir=$(cd "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) \
    || { _WS_NESTED_WHY="could not resolve $1"; return 1; }
  outf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { _WS_NESTED_WHY='could not make a scratch file'; return 1; }
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { rm -f "$outf"; _WS_NESTED_WHY='could not make a scratch file'; return 1; }
  _plat_timeout "$REAP_SCAN_SECONDS" find "$dir" -mindepth 2 -name .git -print0 >"$outf" 2>"$errf"; rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  if (( rc == 124 )); then
    rm -f "$outf"
    _WS_NESTED_WHY="the nested-checkout walk did not finish within ${REAP_SCAN_SECONDS}s"
    return 1
  fi
  (( rc == 0 )) || { rm -f "$outf"; _WS_NESTED_WHY='could not walk the tree'; return 1; }
  [[ -z "$err" ]] || { rm -f "$outf"; _WS_NESTED_WHY="could not read all of the tree ($err)"; return 1; }
  while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
    # `>/dev/null 2>&1`, not `2>/dev/null` alone — same reasoning as the operand
    # resolution above, and the same defect class: a `cd` exported as a shell
    # function prints whatever it likes on STDOUT, straight into this capture,
    # corrupting the entry this loop reports for a real nested checkout.
    p=$(cd "$(dirname "$f")" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || p="${f%/.git}"
    printf '%s\n' "$p"
  done <"$outf" | LC_ALL=C sort
  rm -f "$outf"
}

# Registered children = entries of `git worktree list --porcelain` whose
# RESOLVED path sits strictly inside the parent's resolved dir. Registration is
# FLAT at every depth (measured); nesting is a filesystem accident, so the tree
# is reconstructed here by path prefix — resolved on BOTH sides, or the fleet's
# symlinked roots make every containment test pass vacuously.
_ws_children() {   # main parentdir -> "resolvedpath\tbranch\theadoid" per child
  local main="$1" parent wt="" head="" br="" out=""
  parent=$(cd "$2" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || return 1
  local porc; porc=$(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    case "$line" in
      "worktree "*) wt="${line#worktree }" ;;
      "HEAD "*)     head="${line#HEAD }" ;;
      "branch "*)   br="${line#branch refs/heads/}" ;;
      "")
        if [[ -n "$wt" ]]; then
          local rp; rp=$(cd "$wt" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || rp="$wt"
          [[ "$rp" == "$parent"/* ]] && out+="$rp"$'\t'"$br"$'\t'"$head"$'\n'
        fi
        wt=""; head=""; br="" ;;
    esac
  done <<< "$porc"$'\n'
  printf '%s' "$out" | LC_ALL=C sort
}

# The plumbing probe D2's descent asks of every registered child before it asks
# `git status`: is this checkout mid-operation. Reimplemented from git's own
# on-disk facts rather than Conductor's script, so the answer is ours to reason
# about — a rebase, a merge, a cherry-pick or a revert each leave a marker file
# or directory under the child's OWN gitdir (`--git-path` resolves the linked
# worktree's private `rebase-merge`/`rebase-apply`/`MERGE_HEAD`/etc., not the
# common one `$main` shares with every other worktree), and a conflicted merge
# with no marker of its own still leaves unmerged stages in the index, which
# `ls-files -u` sees. `rc 1` on ANY git read here means "could not tell", not
# "idle" — the caller must refuse `tree-unreadable` rather than wave a child
# through it never actually looked at.
_ws_child_op() {   # childdir -> op name on stdout ('' = idle), rc 1 = unprobeable
  local d="$1" g p op=""
  for p in rebase-merge:rebase rebase-apply:rebase MERGE_HEAD:merge \
           CHERRY_PICK_HEAD:cherry-pick REVERT_HEAD:revert; do
    g=$(git -C "$d" rev-parse --git-path "${p%%:*}" 2>/dev/null) || return 1
    [[ -e "$d/$g" || -e "$g" ]] && { op="${p##*:}"; break; }
  done
  if [[ -z "$op" ]]; then
    local conf; conf=$(git -C "$d" ls-files -u 2>/dev/null | head -1) || return 1
    [[ -n "$conf" ]] && op="conflicted-merge"
  fi
  printf '%s' "$op"
}

# D4 — `cmd_ws_audit`'s OWN child enumeration, deliberately independent of
# `_ws_reap_eval`'s D2 descent above. That descent stops at the FIRST child
# that fails a rung and refuses right there — exactly right for a verdict,
# which needs only one reason. `children[]` is read by a human deciding
# whether to TRUST that refusal, so it cannot inherit the short-circuit: this
# function re-walks `_ws_nested_checkouts` and `_ws_children` itself and
# reports every child's own measured state, whether or not the eval's loop
# ever reached it — the sheet that shows WHY the eval will refuse, not merely
# THAT it did.
#
#   stdout: JSON array of {path,branch,headOid,dirty,busy,stray}
#   exit:   0 = the array is complete; 1 = NOTHING on stdout — the walk, the
#           `worktree list` read, or a per-child probe failed outright. The
#           caller falls back to `null`, never to a partial array: an
#           enumeration a human trusts is either whole or absent.
#
# A registered child that fails the ownership pair `_ws_reap_eval`'s D2 child
# descent proves (`_ws_common_dir` + `--show-toplevel`, copied verbatim from
# that same ownership rung rather than re-derived, so the two can never
# quietly diverge) is reported `"stray":true` rather than aborting the whole
# call: `$main`'s registration for that path is stale — a foreign clone
# dropped there, or the child's own `.git` deleted out from under it — so
# from the human reading this sheet it IS a stray, something at that path
# `$main` cannot vouch for. The one thing this producer must never do over
# ONE bad entry is go silent about every other one.
_ws_child_manifest() {   # main workdir -> JSON array on stdout; non-zero and NOTHING when it could not complete
  local main="$1" workdir="$2" mainreal
  mainreal=$(_ws_common_dir "$main") || return 1

  local nested children
  nested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$workdir") || return 1
  children=$(_ws_children "$main" "$workdir") || return 1

  # The identical in-bash set difference `_ws_reap_eval`'s D2 stray
  # computation uses, for the identical reason stated there: `comm` needs
  # both sides pre-sorted in the SAME collation it compares under, and its
  # own exit status is the only thing a caller can see — a `sort` or
  # `printf` failing inside either `<(...)` is invisible to it. An
  # associative array has no subprocess to fail and no locale to disagree
  # with.
  local -A childset=(); local cline cpath
  while IFS= read -r cline; do
    [[ -n "$cline" ]] || continue
    cpath="${cline%%$'\t'*}"
    childset["$cpath"]=1
  done <<< "$children"$'\n'
  local strays=""
  while IFS= read -r cline; do
    [[ -n "$cline" ]] || continue
    [[ -v childset["$cline"] ]] || strays+="$cline"$'\n'
  done <<< "$nested"$'\n'
  strays="${strays%$'\n'}"

  # Registered children first. `crest`/`cbr`/`chead` split the SAME way
  # `_ws_reap_eval`'s child loop splits them — parameter expansion, never
  # `IFS=$'\t' read` — because bash treats tab as IFS WHITESPACE even when it
  # is the only character in IFS — a detached child's empty branch column
  # (`path<TAB><TAB>headoid`) would collapse and shift `chead` into `cbr`.
  local out="[" first=1 crest cbr chead ccommon ctop cop errf err rc dirt cdirty entry
  while IFS= read -r cline; do
    [[ -n "$cline" ]] || continue
    cpath="${cline%%$'\t'*}"
    crest="${cline#*$'\t'}"
    cbr="${crest%%$'\t'*}"
    chead="${crest#*$'\t'}"
    # I4 (whole-branch review): a registered child whose directory is simply
    # gone used to fall through to the `else` arm below and report
    # `"stray":true` — false, and the opposite of what `stray` means
    # elsewhere on this sheet (`WsAuditChild`'s own docstring: "an
    # unregistered checkout ccd did not create"). `$main` DOES still record
    # this one; there is only nothing left at `$cpath` to read ownership
    # from, so `_ws_common_dir`/`--show-toplevel` never get a chance to run.
    # Reported registered (`stray:false`) with the branch/head the porcelain
    # record itself already gave above, and null for the two measurements
    # this producer genuinely could not take.
    if [[ ! -d "$cpath" ]]; then
      entry="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$cpath"),\"branch\":$( [[ -n "$cbr" ]] && _json_str "$cbr" || echo null),\"headOid\":$( [[ -n "$chead" ]] && _json_str "$chead" || echo null),\"dirty\":null,\"busy\":null,\"stray\":false}"
      (( first )) || out+=","; first=0
      out+="$entry"
      continue
    fi
    ccommon=$(_ws_common_dir "$cpath") || ccommon=""
    ctop=$(git -C "$cpath" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || ctop=""
    if [[ -n "$ccommon" && "$ccommon" == "$mainreal" && -n "$ctop" && "$ctop" == "$cpath" ]]; then
      # `_ws_child_op` failing means "could not tell", never "idle" — the
      # same polarity `_ws_reap_eval`'s D2 rung reads it with — and a `git
      # status` that fails outright or writes a diagnostic on stderr
      # while exiting 0 is the identical partial-read shape Phase B's own
      # `dirty-tree` rung refuses on. Either one here means this ONE child's
      # state could not be measured, and that takes the WHOLE array down
      # with it rather than silently reporting the rest as though nothing
      # were missing.
      cop=$(_ws_child_op "$cpath") || return 1
      errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1
      dirt=$(git -C "$cpath" status --porcelain 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
      err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
      { (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]]; } || return 1
      # Measured even when non-zero — unlike the eval's `child-dirty` rung,
      # THIS producer reports rather than refuses; a non-zero count is data,
      # not a reason to stop.
      cdirty=$(printf '%s' "$dirt" | grep -c . || true)
      entry="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$cpath"),\"branch\":$( [[ -n "$cbr" ]] && _json_str "$cbr" || echo null),\"headOid\":$( [[ -n "$chead" ]] && _json_str "$chead" || echo null),\"dirty\":$cdirty,\"busy\":$( [[ -n "$cop" ]] && _json_str "$cop" || echo null),\"stray\":false}"
    else
      entry="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$cpath"),\"branch\":null,\"headOid\":null,\"dirty\":null,\"busy\":null,\"stray\":true}"
    fi
    (( first )) || out+=","; first=0
    out+="$entry"
  done <<< "$children"$'\n'

  # Filesystem strays — unregistered checkouts `$main` has no record of at
  # all, the set difference computed above.
  while IFS= read -r cline; do
    [[ -n "$cline" ]] || continue
    entry="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$cline"),\"branch\":null,\"headOid\":null,\"dirty\":null,\"busy\":null,\"stray\":true}"
    (( first )) || out+=","; first=0
    out+="$entry"
  done <<< "$strays"$'\n'

  out+="]"
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

REAP_SCAN_SECONDS=30      # the whole-workspace bound on the inside-of-ignored scan

_ws_sensitive_inside() {   # absdir relpath deadline -> 0 with hits in _WS_INSIDE_HITS, 1 = the scan did not complete
  # THE §0 GUARD, ONE DIRECTORY DOWN. `--ignored=matching` collapses a wholly
  # ignored directory to a SINGLE entry, so `build/.env` holding a live key was
  # handed to `_ws_sensitive_match` as `build/`, matched nothing, and was
  # deleted under a sheet reading "not in git · 1 entry, 39 B — build/".
  # Measured end to end before this existed: verdict `reapable`, a token issued,
  # `sensitive: []`, and `build/.env` gone afterwards. Moving one secret one
  # directory deeper defeated the only guard §7 refuses to give an override.
  #
  # `find`, NOT `git ls-files --others --ignored --exclude-standard`, and the
  # difference is not a preference — measured on git 2.43 with a nested
  # repository inside an ignored `build/`:
  #     git ls-files …  ->  build/nested/          (one entry, and that is all)
  #     find …          ->  build/nested/.env      (the file)
  # git collapses a nested repository exactly the way `--ignored=matching`
  # collapses an ignored directory, so it reproduces this very blindness one
  # level deeper — over content `rm -rf` destroys all the same. The question
  # here is "what will be deleted", which is a filesystem question.
  #
  # ALL TYPES, not `-type f`: `_ws_sensitive_match` matches DIRECTORY names too
  # (git hands it `secrets/` as a top-level entry and the `%/` strip is there
  # for exactly that), so restricting the scan to regular files would answer a
  # different question inside than outside.
  #
  # BOUNDED, and the bound is a DEADLINE for the whole workspace rather than a
  # per-entry timeout, so a workspace with 226 ignored entries cannot cost 226
  # timeouts. Measured on this box against the tree `--ignored=matching` was
  # chosen for (custom-tools: 226 collapsed entries, 355,392 entries beneath
  # them): 3.4 s with one `find` per entry, 1.2 s as a single walk. The
  # performance reason for `--ignored=matching` still holds for the DISPLAY —
  # nothing here is printed, counted or sized, only matched — and sensitivity
  # cannot be sampled. Expiry is a read that did not complete, which is a
  # refusal, and the remedy travels in the message.
  #
  # ANY DIAGNOSTIC IS ALSO A READ THAT DID NOT COMPLETE, the same rule and the
  # same polarity as the `git status` beside it: a `chmod 000` subdirectory
  # makes find print `Permission denied` and carry on, and the entries it could
  # not reach are then simply absent — which is the state that says "no secrets
  # here" about files nobody enumerated.
  # `${1%/}` — git names a collapsed directory `build/`, with the trailing
  # slash, and a doubled separator makes `${f#"$abs"/}` match nothing, so every
  # hit came back as `build/` + the ABSOLUTE path. Measured.
  # TWO MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed and measured, and they are the same
  # reason: nothing can spend the budget in a fixture. `left=$(( deadline -
  # SECONDS ))` can be replaced by a constant, and `(( left > 0 ))` by `(( 1 ))`,
  # with the whole suite green (44 files / 712 tests, exit 0, each measured on
  # its own) — because reaching either needs a scan that really takes 30 s, and
  # the largest ignored tree on this box takes 3.4 s. What IS pinned is the
  # `rc == 124` arm (`refuses, with the remedy, when the scan runs out of its
  # budget`, which fails `timeout` for `find` alone), so the EXPIRY behaviour
  # has a fixture and only the clock that produces it does not. They stay
  # because a per-entry timeout is the thing they exist to prevent: without the
  # subtraction each entry gets a fresh 30 s and 226 entries cost 226 of them.
  local abs="${1%/}" rel="${2%/}" ent="$2" deadline="$3" outf errf err rc left f
  _WS_INSIDE_HITS=()
  left=$(( deadline - SECONDS ))
  (( left > 0 )) || {
    REAP_IGNREASON="the scan of the ignored directories did not finish within ${REAP_SCAN_SECONDS}s (stopped at $ent): remove or move the largest ignored directory and reap again"
    return 1
  }
  # TWO MORE MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed and measured, and they are the same
  # pair `_ws_collect_ignored` discloses for the same reason: an empty temp-file
  # name is itself a failing redirect, so `>""` never runs the scan and the
  # `(( rc == 0 ))` rung below refuses anyway. Both stay because each states the
  # case it is about, and because the fall-through only holds while that later
  # line keeps its `rm`. The four `rm -f "$outf"` here ARE pinned individually,
  # by `removes the inside-scan's scratch files too`, which walks all four exits
  # — with any one deleted a fixture for a DIFFERENT failure still cleans up.
  outf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { rm -f "$outf"; return 1; }
  _plat_timeout "$left" find "$abs" -mindepth 1 \( "${_WS_SENSITIVE_FIND[@]}" \) -print0 \
    >"$outf" 2>"$errf"; rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  if (( rc == 124 )); then
    rm -f "$outf"
    REAP_IGNREASON="the scan of the ignored directories did not finish within ${REAP_SCAN_SECONDS}s (stopped at $ent): remove or move the largest ignored directory and reap again"
    return 1
  fi
  (( rc == 0 )) || { rm -f "$outf"; REAP_IGNREASON="could not walk the ignored directory $ent"; return 1; }
  [[ -z "$err" ]] || { rm -f "$outf"; REAP_IGNREASON="could not read all of the ignored directory $ent ($err)"; return 1; }
  # `${f#"$abs"/}` with the prefix QUOTED: an unquoted one is a GLOB, and an
  # ignored directory is free to be called `build[1]`.
  while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
    [[ -n "$f" ]] || continue
    _WS_INSIDE_HITS+=( "$rel/${f#"$abs"/}" )
  done <"$outf"
  rm -f "$outf"
}

_ws_collect_ignored() {   # workdir — fills REAP_IGNORED (`sensitive\tbytes\tpath`) / REAP_SENSITIVE / REAP_IGNDIGEST
  # NON-ZERO when the ignored set could not be READ, and both of its reads are
  # checked. Neither failure can be allowed to look like "there is nothing
  # ignored here", because that is the answer that authorises the delete:
  #
  #   - The DIGEST half forges the token. A failure and an empty set hash
  #     identically (Task 2, deviation 6), so a discarded status puts "" into
  #     REAP_IGNDIGEST on the audit side AND on the reap side, the fingerprints
  #     match, and D2's consent check passes. Measured with only
  #     `status --ignored=matching` failing: both sides produce
  #     `igndigest='' … token=17af9144…`, an exact match, from a read that never
  #     happened. The digest is now built from the RECORDS this function
  #     produces rather than from a second `git status` (see the tail), so this
  #     read is the only one that can fail and its status is checked here.
  #   - The ENUMERATION half is worse, because it needs no token at all: an empty
  #     REAP_SENSITIVE means `sensitive-ignored` cannot fire, and the resume path
  #     (Task 6 Step 4) reads that array directly. Same measurement:
  #     `sensitive=()` for a worktree holding a gitignored `secrets/creds.env`.
  #
  # The read is therefore ONE `git status` whose status is checked, and the loop
  # filters `!! ` itself rather than piping through grep — a pipeline's exit code
  # would be grep's, and grep exits 1 on a legitimately empty ignored set. It is
  # also the ONLY `git status` here now: `_ws_ignored_digest` survives for the
  # ARCHIVE manifest, which records what it cannot describe rather than refusing.
  #
  # AND THE EXIT CODE IS NOT THE WHOLE ANSWER. A PARTIALLY unreadable tree makes
  # git warn and exit 0: measured on git 2.43, `chmod 000` on a subdirectory
  # gives rc 0, no line for anything inside it, and `warning: could not open
  # directory 'sub/'` on stderr — which `2>/dev/null` throws away. The entries
  # git could not reach are then simply absent, so a gitignored `secrets/` under
  # that directory leaves REAP_SENSITIVE empty and `sensitive-ignored`, a guard
  # §7 forbids overriding, cannot fire over files nobody ever enumerated. So
  # stderr is captured and ANY diagnostic is a read that did not complete.
  # Deliberately not a match on git's wording: git translates its warnings and
  # ccd pins no locale, so a text test would pass silently on a box with LANG
  # set — the failure mode this exists to remove.
  #
  # AND THE READ IS `-z`, WHICH IS A CORRECTNESS REQUIREMENT, NOT A STYLE.
  # Without it git C-QUOTES any path holding a space, a non-ASCII byte, a quote,
  # a backslash or a control character: measured on git 2.43, `!! .env` bare
  # beside `!! "deploy key.pem"` and `!! "s\303\251crets/"`. `_ws_sensitive_match`
  # is then handed a string whose first and last characters are `"`, so every
  # SUFFIX pattern dies on the trailing quote and every PREFIX pattern on the
  # leading one — measured end to end, a gitignored private key named
  # `deploy key.pem` produced verdict=reapable, sensitive:false and a TOKEN,
  # against the one guard §7 says has no override (`prod db.sqlite`,
  # `ssh key.pem`, same shape). This comment used to name `My Project.env` in
  # that list and was WRONG to: its suffix had no arm in `_ws_sensitive_match`
  # at all, so it was un-matched quoted and un-matched unquoted, and no amount of
  # `-z` was going to close it. That one is closed where it lives, by the `*.env`
  # arm. The quoted string is also what the size
  # read was given, and it names nothing on disk, so `ignoredBytes`
  # under-reported precisely the entries that matter — and the human, whose
  # reading of a filename this whole design rests on, was shown
  # `"s\303\251crets/"`.
  #
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: `-c core.quotePath=false` is a stated NO-OP
  # here and cannot be pinned. Measured on git 2.43 in both directions: with
  # `-z` the output is verbatim whether that config is false, true or unset, and
  # WITHOUT `-z` the config unquotes the non-ASCII byte and still quotes the
  # space — so it fixes nothing on its own and changes nothing beside `-z`. It
  # is passed so the line says what it is for; `-z` is what does the work, and
  # `reads the path git C-QUOTES` is what kills its removal.
  #
  # `-z` needs a FILE rather than `$(…)`: command substitution drops NUL bytes,
  # so the separator would vanish and every entry run together.
  #
  # What `-z` gives it also takes back, and the record shape is the answer: git
  # is no longer escaping a TAB or a NEWLINE inside a path either, so
  # `path\tbytes\tsensitive` read left to right shifts its fields on
  # `a<TAB>b.log` and `cmd_ws_audit` prints `"bytes":b.log`, which is not JSON.
  # Hence `sensitive\tbytes\tpath`: the two fixed-shape fields first — one of
  # `0|1`, one of `[0-9]+`, neither able to contain a tab — and the free-form
  # one last, so the path keeps whatever bytes it has.
  #
  # `du -sb` rather than `_ws_gc_bytes`, which answers `-` for anything that is
  # not a DIRECTORY (its own contract, and right for the workspace totals it was
  # written for): every ignored FILE was therefore recorded as 0 bytes, so a
  # 400 MB `dump.sql` was listed as free. `$wd` is absolute, so no `--` guard is
  # needed against a leading dash — but `head -n1` IS needed, because du echoes
  # the path back beside the size and a path with a newline in it makes that two
  # lines (measured: `cut -f1` alone returned `1\nd.log`, which fails the
  # numeric test and silently became 0).
  #
  # AND A `du` THAT DID NOT FINISH REFUSES THE WHOLE COLLECTION — round-3 item 3,
  # routed here by the ui-tsx verifier, and the TWELFTH instance of the
  # measurement-forgery class on this branch (after `_ws_gc_bytes`, c140dbd, and
  # this round's own `_ws_gc_dirty`). The rung used to be
  # `[[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || b=0`, which forged in BOTH of du's failure modes:
  #   - `du` stubbed to fail / the entry gone: `b` empty, recorded as 0;
  #   - `du -sb` on a PARTIALLY readable directory: measured, GNU coreutils 9.4,
  #     `chmod 000` on one subdirectory gives rc=1, stderr
  #     `du: cannot read directory '…/sub': Permission denied` and stdout
  #     `5000  top` for a tree that really holds 14000 bytes. The old test was
  #     `^[0-9]+$`, so the PARTIAL TOTAL passed as a measurement — worse than
  #     the 0, because 0 looks like a bug and 5000 looks like an answer.
  # Both land in `ignoredBytes`, which `ReapSheet.tsx` prints as a stated total
  # immediately above the Remove button.
  #
  # The fix is not a `null` in the record: `ignoredBytes` is `number` on the
  # wire (shared/api.ts:269) and `humanBytes(null)` renders `0 B`, so a null
  # would relocate the forgery into the PWA rather than close it. It is the
  # refusal this function already owns — the same `return 1` + `REAP_IGNREASON`
  # the git-status rungs and the inside-walk rungs use, which every caller
  # already handles: `_ws_reap_eval` refuses `tree-unreadable` (ccd:3484),
  # `_ws_reap_tail` refuses rather than finishing "on a guess" (ccd:4418), and
  # `_ws_gc_reclaimable` declines "refusing to remove a tree it cannot describe"
  # (ccd:5313). A size ccd could not take is a tree ccd cannot describe, and the
  # only thing this function's answer is used for is authorising a deletion.
  # `pipefail` is set at the top of this file, so the pipeline's status IS du's.
  #
  # THREE MORE MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed with the reason each is one, and
  # two of them are the same reason — an empty temp-file name is itself a
  # failing redirect, so the read never runs and the NEXT guard refuses:
  #   - `outf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1` — removable with the suite green: `>""` is
  #     an invalid redirect, git never runs, and `(( rc == 0 ))` refuses.
  #   - the `|| { rm -f "$outf"; return 1; }` on the `errf` line — removable for
  #     the same reason PLUS one more: `2>""` fails the read, so control reaches
  #     `(( rc == 0 )) || { rm -f "$outf"; …` one line later, which unlinks the
  #     same file. Measured, not assumed: a test was written for it and the
  #     mutant was re-swept and still SURVIVED. The OTHER THREE `rm -f "$outf"`
  #     — the exit-code one, the stderr one and the one after the loop — are
  #     each pinned individually by `removes its scratch file on EVERY path that
  #     refuses`, which walks all four exits, because with any single one
  #     deleted a fixture for a DIFFERENT failure still cleans up and one case
  #     pins nothing.
