    # THE REGISTRY ENTRY IS REPORTED, NEVER TRUSTED, NEVER SILENTLY DELETED:
    # `REAP_REGBRANCH` keeps it, `REAP_DRIFT` carries the sentence the audit
    # puts on the wire and the sheet renders BEFORE the tap, and step (g)
    # never names it. The two conditions that are not drift — no worktree
    # record, and a recorded detached HEAD — are still refusals, above.
    if [[ "$wthead" != "$branch" ]]; then
      REAP_DRIFT="the registry recorded $regbranch; git's worktree record for $workdir says $wthead — $wthead is the branch this cleanup evaluates and removes, and $regbranch is left alone"
      branch="$wthead"
      REAP_BRANCH="$wthead"
    fi
    # STILL REQUIRED — and now for a different reason, which is why it is worth
    # saying out loud rather than leaving in place by inertia. It used to sit
    # immediately after the branch read in order to CONTAIN a name that might
    # have come from another repository; with `_ws_wt_branch` no name can, so
    # containment is no longer its job and the ordering above no longer turns on
    # it. What is left is the DIRECTORY, and that is not the weaker reason — it
    # is ccd's own (ccd:763-778). Everything below this line reads or destroys
    # `$workdir`: Phase B asks it for `status --porcelain` and for its ignored
    # set, so a stranger's clean tree would certify OUR workspace as safe to
    # delete; and `_ws_reap_tail` hands the same path to `git worktree remove`,
    # which in ws-rm killed the session and disabled the unit BEFORE git got
    # round to refusing. `registered` cannot decide it either — the record
    # outlives the directory, and measured on git 2.43 a `git init` at that path
    # leaves the record reading healthy with no `prunable` marker, because the
    # record's gitdir file now points at the stranger's own `.git`. Hence: both
    # sides must say $main, and this must run BEFORE Phase B rather than merely
    # somewhere after the branch read.
    #
    # `_ws_common_dir` (ccd:713-718), never a `cd` inside a command substitution.
    # An earlier draft of this plan wrote the cd form; `ccrc/ws-rm-fix` deleted
    # exactly that pattern from cmd_ws_add and cmd_ws_rm and documented why at
    # _ws_common_dir, so writing it back here would reintroduce a bug main has
    # already fixed. The reason, restated because it decides an irreversible
    # refusal: ccd does not control the stdout of a captured `cd`. Plain
    # `--git-common-dir` prints a bare `.git` from a repo's own checkout, so the
    # cd form is load-bearing rather than cosmetic — and bash's own cd prints the
    # directory it landed on when a relative operand hits CDPATH, while a `cd`
    # exported as a shell function (bash imports those from the environment)
    # prints whatever it likes. `2>/dev/null` catches neither. Both sides then
    # compare multi-line garbage: two wrong answers that happen to match wave a
    # squatter through, two that differ refuse a healthy workspace.
    # Empty is NO ANSWER, never a match — the helper's own contract — so both
    # sides are required non-empty before the comparison means anything.
    #
    # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: replacing the FIRST call with the banned
    # `cd "$workdir" && git rev-parse --git-common-dir` form leaves the suite
    # green, and the reason is worth knowing rather than papering over. `$workdir`
    # is absolute, so CDPATH cannot fire on it, and from a LINKED worktree
    # `--git-common-dir` already answers absolutely — so on this side the two
    # forms agree. The form is banned for the OTHER side: from a repository's own
    # checkout plain `--git-common-dir` prints a bare `.git`, and `$main` is
    # exactly that. `_ws_common_dir` (ccd:713-718) is where that is pinned, and
    # it is called here rather than reimplemented so there is one answer to
    # break.
    common=$(_ws_common_dir "$workdir") || common=""
    mainreal=$(_ws_common_dir "$main")  || mainreal=""
    [[ -n "$common" && -n "$mainreal" && "$common" == "$mainreal" ]] \
      || { _reap_refuse foreign-worktree "$workdir belongs to ${common:-no repository}, not ${mainreal:-no repository}"; return 1; }
    # Still called WTHEAD, still fingerprinted as `worktreeHead=`, still on the
    # wire as `headMatchesRegistry`, all deliberately unrenamed: `worktree list
    # --porcelain` reports each worktree's HEAD (git reads it out of
    # `$GIT_DIR/worktrees/<slug>/HEAD`), so the VALUE is the same fact it always
    # was. Only the repository the question is put to changed. One thing about
    # the value did change and is worth knowing while reading the fingerprint: a
    # detached worktree now yields "" where `rev-parse` yielded the literal
    # `HEAD` — unobservable, because rung 2 refuses before this line.
    #
    # STILL A MUTATION SURVIVOR, and for a DIFFERENT REASON than the note this
    # replaces — which is the whole point of rewriting it rather than deleting
    # it. It used to read: "writing `REAP_WTHEAD="$branch"` here leaves the
    # suite green, and always will — rung 1 above refuses unless
    # `wthead == branch`, so on this line the two are equal BY CONSTRUCTION …
    # the day that rung moves or softens, the fingerprint must follow git rather
    # than quietly follow the registry." Rung 1 HAS moved: it notes the
    # divergence instead of refusing it. But it resolves that divergence by
    # assigning `branch="$wthead"`, so by this line the two are equal by
    # construction AGAIN — same survivor, new mechanism, and the swap is still
    # unobservable.
    #
    # What the old note asked for did happen, one line at a time and somewhere
    # else: the fingerprint's SECOND input is `$regbranch`, the registry's name,
    # kept under its own name precisely so that git's answer and the registry's
    # are two hashed facts rather than one. THAT swap is a kill (`hashes BOTH
    # names, so rewriting either one invalidates the consent`). This line is
    # written from `wthead` because the field means "what git's record says",
    # and if a later edit ever stops the drift rung equalising the two, this is
    # the line that must follow git.
    REAP_WTHEAD="$branch"

    # OURS TO REMOVE, AND NOBODY ELSE'S. The last identity question, and the one
    # the drift rung above made necessary: while that rung refused, the only
    # branch that could reach the CAS delete was the workspace's own
    # `ws/<slug>`, a name nothing else holds. Now the branch is whatever git has
    # checked out here — and `git switch --ignore-other-worktrees develop`
    # inside a workspace is a thing an operator can do in one command (measured,
    # git 2.43: it succeeds, and `worktree list --porcelain` then reports
    # `refs/heads/develop` for BOTH paths). Reaping that workspace would then
    # delete the branch the project's own checkout is standing on: measured, the
    # ref goes, rc 0, and `git status` in `$main` reads "No commits yet on
    # develop" over a full history.
    #
    # Same rule and same word as the per-child ladder's `child-branch-elsewhere`
    # ("removing it here would strand that other checkout"), asked of the parent.
    # It is deliberately NOT a proof about the work — the ladder below proves
    # that — but about the REF: a branch two checkouts share is not this
    # workspace's to delete, however thoroughly its commits are merged.
    local elsewhere elsrc
    elsewhere=$(_ws_branch_elsewhere "$main" "$branch" "$workdir"); elsrc=$?
    (( elsrc == 0 )) \
      || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not enumerate $main's worktrees, so whether another checkout holds $branch was never asked"; return 1; }
    [[ -z "$elsewhere" ]] \
      || { _reap_refuse branch-elsewhere "$branch is also checked out at $elsewhere — removing it here would strand that checkout; move it off $branch first"; return 1; }

    # ── Phase B: nothing local is lost ─────────────────────────────
    # TWO statements, and the pipeline's status is not the read's. `status
    # --porcelain | grep -c .` counts ZERO lines for a tree nobody could read, so
    # the one-line form passes the `dirty-tree` guard on an unreadable worktree —
    # the same defect the archive manifest shipped and deviation 6 closed there
    # (`_ws_gc_dirty` states the rule for the file: a status we could not read is
    # not a clean one). An index.lock contention or a permission change mid-flight
    # is enough, and this guard is one §5.3 forbids overriding.
    #
    # And the exit code is only HALF of "could not read", which is why stderr is
    # captured rather than discarded. A PARTIALLY unreadable tree exits 0:
    # measured on git 2.43, `chmod 000` on an untracked subdirectory holding
    # uncommitted work gives rc 0, EMPTY stdout, and `warning: could not open
    # directory 'sub/'` on stderr. The count is then 0, `dirty-tree` passes, and
    # the audit hands out a token certifying a clean tree over work it never
    # saw. Same rule as the ignored set in `_ws_collect_ignored`, and for the
    # same reason: these two are the guards §5.3/§7 allow no override for, so
    # "some of the tree" is not an answer either of them may act on. The test is
    # for the DIAGNOSTIC and not its wording — git translates warnings and ccd
    # pins no locale.
    #
    # SAID OUT LOUD, because the polarity is chosen and not free: ANY diagnostic
    # refuses, so one benign `warning:` — a stale index extension, a
    # `core.fsmonitor` complaint, an advice line a future git decides to print —
    # makes this workspace un-reapable, permanently, with no override anywhere.
    # That is the correct direction (the alternative is a wording allowlist that
    # a translated warning walks straight through), and the cost is that the
    # remedy is to fix the warning, not to press through it. `ws-archive`, which
    # runs unattended, is deliberately NOT built this way — it records what it
    # cannot describe rather than refusing forever.
    #
    # `dirt` and `REAP_DIRTY` are two names because they are two types: the raw
    # status text is not the count, and one variable holding both in turn is how
    # a later reader ends up printing porcelain into a sentence that says
    # "file(s)".
    # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: this line's `|| { _reap_refuse … }` is
    # removable with the suite green, for the same reason as the two in
    # `_ws_collect_ignored` — an empty `errf` makes `2>""` a failing redirect, the
    # status read never runs, and the `(( rc == 0 ))` two lines down refuses with
    # the same token. Unlike those two there is no file to leak here, so nothing
    # observable is left to pin. It states the case rather than relying on a
    # later line to catch it.
    errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not read the tree at $workdir"; return 1; }
    dirt=$(git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
    err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
    (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]] \
      || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not read the tree at $workdir"; return 1; }
    REAP_DIRTY=$(printf '%s' "$dirt" | grep -c . || true)
    (( REAP_DIRTY == 0 )) \
      || { _reap_refuse dirty-tree "$REAP_DIRTY uncommitted or untracked file(s)"; return 1; }
    _ws_collect_ignored "$workdir" \
      || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not read the ignored set at $workdir${REAP_IGNREASON:+ — $REAP_IGNREASON}"; return 1; }
    (( ${#REAP_SENSITIVE[@]} == 0 )) \
      || { _reap_refuse sensitive-ignored "${REAP_SENSITIVE[*]}"; return 1; }
    # LAST rung of Phase B, deliberately, and after `sensitive-ignored` for the
    # same reason it sits after every other Phase B guard: the ladder is walked
    # outermost-cheapest first, and a nested checkout is a whole-tree `find`
    # (bounded by `REAP_SCAN_SECONDS`, same as the ignored-set walk this rung
    # sits beside) — no reason to pay for it ahead of the guards a `git status`
    # and a directory listing already answer. `cmd_ws_rm` (by NAME — the range
    # this cited named `_is_valid_wrapper`, not `cmd_ws_rm`, at base too) walks
    # only two rungs — `dirty`, then this one — never `sensitive-ignored`
    # (it has no `_ws_collect_ignored` call at all), but the two rungs they
    # DO share run in the same relative order there, and the audit's verdict
    # must read the same ladder or the sheet and the CLI would refuse two
    # different workspaces for the same `nested`. That symmetry held while
    # both refused on ANY nested checkout; D2 below narrows only the AUDIT's
    # rung to strays. `cmd_ws_rm` deliberately keeps the blanket refusal —
    # untouched by this task. `_ws_reap_tail`'s own tail backstop (the
    # nested-checkout scan it runs immediately before the PARENT's own `git
    # worktree remove`) keeps its EXACT blanket rule too, and stays that way
    # even once Task 8 gives it a set of registered children to reason
    # about: Task 8's own teardown loop tears every consented child down
    # FIRST, in its own `children` phase, so by the time that backstop runs
    # the consented set is already gone — anything the scan still finds is
    # either a stray that appeared after consent or a child whose own
    # teardown failed and already refused before the backstop was ever
    # reached, and both are still refusals. (Cited by function and
    # description here, not by line, because a line number drifts and this
    # one already had — see the fix that first noticed.)
    #
    # D2 — CHILD-AWARE DESCENT. A nested checkout is no longer an automatic
    # refusal: one git registered under `$main` (`_ws_children`, ccd:2988-3006)
    # is a session ccrc itself may have spawned under this workspace, and it
    # gets its own per-child ladder below rather than the blanket
    # `nested-checkouts-present`. An UNREGISTERED one — a stray `git init`, a
    # clone someone dropped in by hand — is still exactly what that token
    # means: `$main` has no record of it, so nothing about it can be reasoned
    # about here, and the ladder below never runs over it. `strays` is the
    # set difference, computed on the same RESOLVED paths both enumerations
    # already produce (`_ws_nested_checkouts` resolves the walk's operand,
    # `_ws_children` resolves both the parent and every `worktree` line), so a
    # symlinked ancestor cannot make a registered child misread as a stray or
    # the reverse.
    nested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$workdir") \
      || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not scan for nested checkouts${_WS_NESTED_WHY:+ — $_WS_NESTED_WHY}"; return 1; }
    children=$(_ws_children "$main" "$workdir") \
      || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not enumerate registered worktrees"; return 1; }
    # An IN-BASH set difference, not `comm` over two `sort`ed process
    # substitutions — fix round 1, two measured defects in that form. First:
    # `comm` requires both inputs pre-sorted in the SAME collation it compares
    # under, and this pipeline sorted with `LC_ALL=C` while `comm` itself read
    # the ambient locale — under a UTF-8 locale a path holding a non-ASCII
    # byte can sort differently under the two, desyncing `comm`'s merge and
    # manufacturing a spurious stray over a perfectly healthy child. Second:
    # `strays=$(comm ...)` captures only `comm`'s own exit status — a `sort`
    # or `printf` failing inside either `<(...)` is invisible to
    # `[[ -z "$strays" ]]`, and an invisible failure here reads as "no
    # strays", which is fail-open on the one rung this file never allows an
    # override for. An associative array has no subprocess to fail and no
    # locale to disagree with.
    local -A _ws_childset=(); local _wcline _wcpath
    while IFS= read -r _wcline; do
      [[ -n "$_wcline" ]] || continue
      _wcpath="${_wcline%%$'\t'*}"
      _ws_childset["$_wcpath"]=1
    done <<< "$children"$'\n'
    strays=""
    while IFS= read -r _wcline; do
      [[ -n "$_wcline" ]] || continue
      [[ -v _ws_childset["$_wcline"] ]] || strays+="$_wcline"$'\n'
    done <<< "$nested"$'\n'
    strays="${strays%$'\n'}"
    [[ -z "$strays" ]] \
      || { _reap_refuse nested-checkouts-present "$(printf '%s\n' "$strays" | grep -c .) unregistered checkout(s): ${strays//$'\n'/, }"; return 1; }
    # The per-child ladder itself, outermost-cheapest first and in the same
    # relative order Phase B above uses for the parent: an in-progress
    # operation (a rebase/merge/cherry-pick/revert marker, or a conflicted
    # merge with none — `_ws_child_op`, ccd:3008-3030) BEFORE the detached-HEAD
    # check, which in turn runs before a `git status`.
    #
    # That op-before-detached order is not cosmetic. Measured on git 2.43: a
    # worktree stopped mid-rebase on a conflict is ITSELF on a detached HEAD —
    # `git rebase` replays each commit with the worktree checked out at the
    # commit being replayed, and a conflict leaves it there, branch ref
    # unmoved. Checking `cbr` first would read every mid-rebase child as
    # merely `child-busy "detached HEAD at …"`, which is TRUE but not the
    # question this rung exists to answer, and the fixture that pins the
    # DETAIL text ("… refuses child-busy with the op in the detail") would
    # read "detached HEAD" instead of "rebase". Probing for the operation
    # first gives the more specific diagnosis; only a child that is detached
    # AND idle — no marker, no unmerged stage — falls through to the plain
    # detached-HEAD refusal below.
    #
    # After that: dirty; then unpushed, proved against the PARENT project's
    # `origin/HEAD` exactly as ccd proves the parent's own branch, because a
    # child's commits are only as safe as the remote the parent itself
    # trusts; then whether the child's branch is ALSO checked out somewhere
    # else, which is not this child's business to answer about itself and so
    # runs last.
    REAP_CHILDLINES=""
    local childline cpath cbr chead crest
    # `IFS= read -r childline` plus manual `%%`/`#` splitting, NOT
    # `IFS=$'\t' read -r cpath cbr chead`. Measured: tab is unconditionally
    # "IFS whitespace" in bash — true even when it is the ONLY character in
    # IFS — so consecutive delimiters collapse and leading/trailing ones are
    # stripped exactly as they are for the default space-separated case.
    # `_ws_children` prints a detached child as `path<TAB><TAB>headoid` (an
    # EMPTY branch field between two tabs), and `IFS=$'\t' read -r cpath cbr
    # chead` against that line silently shifts every field left — `cbr`
    # receives the head oid and `chead` comes back empty — so the very next
    # rung (`[[ -n "$cbr" ]]`) reads a detached child as a branch NAMED after
    # its own commit sha and never refuses `child-busy` at all. Parameter
    # expansion has no such whitespace carve-out: a run of consecutive tabs
    # produces the empty fields between them, which is what an empty branch
    # column actually means.
    while IFS= read -r childline; do
      [[ -n "$childline" ]] || continue
      cpath="${childline%%$'\t'*}"
      crest="${childline#*$'\t'}"
      cbr="${crest%%$'\t'*}"
      chead="${crest#*$'\t'}"
      # I4 (whole-branch review): a directory that is simply gone answers the
      # ownership pair below the SAME "" a foreign clone dropped at this path
      # or a `.git`-less child would answer — but it is a different fact.
      # `git worktree list` (the read `$children` came from) still names this
      # path; nothing has told git the checkout itself is gone. Checked
      # first, before either read below even has a directory to open, so the
      # refusal names what actually happened — a stale registration, prune
      # it — instead of the blanket "not $main's worktree any more" the
      # ownership pair would otherwise report for a fact it cannot see.
      [[ -d "$cpath" ]] \
        || { _reap_refuse child-record-stale "git still records a worktree at $cpath but nothing is there — run: git -C $main worktree prune, then re-audit"; return 1; }
      # OWNERSHIP, reproven live — right after the `-d` check above, before
      # anything else touches `$cpath`, exactly the position `_ws_reap_eval`'s
      # own foreign-worktree rung (Phase B's `$workdir`/`$main` common-dir
      # comparison, above this per-child descent) holds for `$workdir`.
      # `_ws_children` enumerated this path once, from a `worktree list`
      # snapshot; a lot can happen to the filesystem between that read and
      # this line.
      #
      # TWO checks, AND-combined, because fix round 1 measured that neither
      # alone proves ownership. `_ws_common_dir "$cpath" == $mainreal` catches
      # a foreign clone dropped at a registered child's path — its own
      # unrelated `.git` resolves to a different common dir — but NOT the
      # sibling failure the same review measured: delete `$cpath/.git`
      # outright and git's upward discovery walks past the empty spot, finds
      # `$workdir`'s OWN `.git` two directories up (a child always lives
      # nested inside its parent's working tree), and answers with
      # $workdir's common dir — which IS $mainreal, because $workdir is
      # itself main's worktree. Measured on git 2.43: `_ws_common_dir`
      # cannot tell "$cpath is a healthy linked worktree" from "$cpath's own
      # `.git` is gone and every read of it is secretly reading $workdir
      # instead" — both answer $mainreal. `--show-toplevel` is what
      # distinguishes them: for an intact child it answers `$cpath` itself;
      # for the `.git`-less case it answers `$workdir`, because upward
      # discovery roots the repository at whichever ancestor's `.git` it
      # actually found. Neither probe alone proves ownership — a foreign
      # clone's own toplevel IS itself, and a `.git`-less child's common-dir
      # IS $mainreal — so both run.
      local ccommon; ccommon=$(_ws_common_dir "$cpath") || ccommon=""
      local ctop; ctop=$(git -C "$cpath" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || ctop=""
      [[ -n "$ccommon" && "$ccommon" == "$mainreal" && -n "$ctop" && "$ctop" == "$cpath" ]] \
        || { _reap_refuse nested-checkouts-present "the checkout at $cpath is not $main's worktree any more"; return 1; }
      local cop; cop=$(_ws_child_op "$cpath") \
        || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not probe $cpath for an in-progress operation"; return 1; }
      [[ -z "$cop" ]] || { _reap_refuse child-busy "$cop in progress at $cpath"; return 1; }
      [[ -n "$cbr" ]] || { _reap_refuse child-busy "detached HEAD at $cpath"; return 1; }
      errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not read the child at $cpath"; return 1; }
      dirt=$(git -C "$cpath" status --porcelain 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
      err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
      { (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]]; } \
        || { _reap_refuse tree-unreadable "could not read the child at $cpath"; return 1; }
      local cdirty; cdirty=$(printf '%s' "$dirt" | grep -c . || true)
      (( cdirty == 0 )) || { _reap_refuse child-dirty "$cdirty uncommitted file(s) in $cpath"; return 1; }
      local cbase; cbase=$(git -C "$main" symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD) \
        || { _reap_refuse child-unpushed "no origin/HEAD to prove $cpath against"; return 1; }
      local cahead; cahead=$(git -C "$cpath" rev-list --count "$cbase..HEAD" 2>/dev/null) \
        || { _reap_refuse child-unpushed "could not compare $cpath against $cbase"; return 1; }
      (( cahead == 0 )) || { _reap_refuse child-unpushed "$cahead commit(s) in $cpath unreachable from $cbase"; return 1; }
      # `-cxF`, not a `^…\$`-anchored BRE — `$cbr` is a branch name interpolated
      # straight into the pattern, and a BRE gives `.`, `*`, `[`, `\` inside it
      # special meaning; the fixed-string idiom already at `_ws_reap_tail`'s own
      # "STILL REGISTERED?" rung (`grep -qxF "worktree $ccpath"`) is what closes
      # that for a path, and the same fix applies here for a branch.
      local holders; holders=$(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null | grep -cxF "branch refs/heads/$cbr" || true)
      (( holders == 1 )) || { _reap_refuse child-branch-elsewhere "branch $cbr is checked out in $holders worktrees"; return 1; }
      REAP_CHILDLINES+="$cpath"$'\t'"$cbr"$'\t'"$chead"$'\t'"$cdirty"$'\n'
    done <<< "$children"$'\n'
  fi

  # THE STASH READ THAT CANNOT FAIL SILENTLY — corroborated here rather than
  # widened in `_ws_stash_count`, whose `2>/dev/null … || true` is Task 2's
  # deliberate contract and which Task 4 consumes.
  #
  # Every failure of that helper is a 0, and a 0 is what authorises the delete.
  # Neither of the two techniques already in this file can see it: measured on
  # git 2.43, with `.git/logs/refs/stash` unreadable OR deleted while
  # `refs/stash` still resolves, `git stash list` returns rc 0, EMPTY stdout and
  # EMPTY stderr — so the exit code says fine and Phase B's stderr rule has
  # nothing to test. The failure is deterministic, so ws-reap recomputes the
  # same 0 and the `--expect` consent check matches. That is deviation 6's
  # digest forgery in the `stashCount` field: a deletion authorised over stashes
  # nobody counted.
  #
  # What CAN see it is the ref, and the invariant is ONE-WAY — not the
  # biconditional this comment used to claim. Measured across push/pop/drop/clear
  # on git 2.43, one half IS a law git keeps: at least one entry ⇒ `refs/stash`
  # resolves, because git creates the ref on the first push and deletes it when
  # the last entry goes. The CONVERSE is not, and the converse is what this guard
  # rests on: `git reflog expire --all --expire=now --expire-unreachable=now` — a
  # documented maintenance command that does not mention refs/stash — empties the
  # stash reflog and LEAVES THE REF, so it resolves (rc 0) beside a `git stash
  # list` that returns rc 0 and zero entries. Measured on git 2.43.
  # (`git gc --prune=now` does not reach it, nor does gc with
  # `gc.reflogExpire=now` — git protects refs/stash from gc by default, measured
  # too. The trigger is narrow. It is not impossible.)
  #
  # So the guard has a FALSE-POSITIVE STATE and it is DISCLOSED rather than
  # engineered away: from here the two are indistinguishable, and the only safe
  # reading of "I cannot tell" is to refuse. What that costs is said out loud —
  # a repository in that state answers `stash-unreadable` PERMANENTLY, for every
  # workspace in it, with no override, and since this runs before the `exists`
  # branch it blocks the `reap-interrupted` resume path too. Which is why the
  # REMEDY travels in the refusal detail rather than in this comment: the
  # operator the message reaches is the only one who can act, and neither
  # 'git stash clear' nor 'git update-ref -d refs/stash' is something they will
  # guess. Both measured against the reflog-expire fixture, in the test that
  # names it.
  #
  # What the guard must NOT do is read "refs/stash exists" as "this branch has
  # stashes". A stash on another branch is a legitimate, reapable state (also
  # measured: a stash pushed from $main's own checkout leaves this branch's
  # count at an honest 0), and refusing it would make every workspace in a repo
  # whose main holds one stash permanently un-reapable, with no override. So the
  # corroboration is on the WHOLE list and the branch-scoped count stays
  # `_ws_stash_count`'s.
  #
  # THAT SCOPING IS WHAT DOCKET 6 EXPLOITED, and the fix is in the count rather
  # than here: a stash taken from a DETACHED worktree says `(no branch)` and so
  # named nothing for the scoped grep to match, which is a 0 that authorised the
  # delete. `_ws_stash_count` now attributes those by base commit.
  #
  # AND THAT ARM DOES REACH FLEET-WIDE FOR ONE SHAPE OF INPUT — round-3
  # verification P3, reconciled in one place (see `_ws_stash_count`, "AND IT
  # DOES REINTRODUCE THE FLEET-WIDE COST") and cross-referenced here so this
  # paragraph is not read as covering it. The rule rejected above is attribution
  # by EXISTENCE, which fires on a NAMED stash the operator has a standing
  # reason to keep. The arm attributes by BASE COMMIT and fires only on an
  # ANONYMOUS entry; when that entry's base sits on shared history it is
  # reachable from every branch, so it does block every workspace of the project
  # until it is dealt with. That is accepted on the same test this file applies
  # to `stash-unreadable` — can the operator get out of it — which is why the
  # remedy is in the `stashes-present` detail below and not only in a comment.
  if git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet refs/stash >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    stashall=$(git -C "$main" stash list 2>/dev/null | grep -c .)
    (( stashall > 0 )) \
      || { _reap_refuse stash-unreadable "refs/stash resolves in $main but the stash list read back empty — either its reflog cannot be read, or maintenance ('git reflog expire --all') emptied it and left the ref: if this repository truly holds no stashes, 'git stash clear' (or 'git update-ref -d refs/stash') clears the stale ref"; return 1; }
  fi
  REAP_STASHES=$(_ws_stash_count "$main" "$branch")
  (( REAP_STASHES == 0 )) \
    || { _reap_refuse stashes-present "$REAP_STASHES stash entr$( (( REAP_STASHES == 1 )) && echo y || echo ies) belonging to $branch (a stash taken from it on a detached HEAD names no branch and is attributed by its base commit, so an anonymous entry based on shared history counts against every workspace of $project): 'git -C $main stash list' names them, and 'git stash branch <name> stash@{N}' keeps one, 'git -C $main stash drop stash@{N}' discards one"; return 1; }
  # ── Phase C: containment, proven from the EVIDENCE ───────────────
  # THE RULE THIS BLOCK IS ARRANGED AROUND, because getting it wrong is what
  # made three archived workspaces (~3.6 G) permanently un-reapable from the
  # sheet on 2026-08-22 while `ws-rm` removed all three by hand: WHICH PROVER
  # RUNS MAY NEVER DEPEND ON A FACT THAT IS ORTHOGONAL TO WHAT IS BEING PROVEN.
  #
  # This used to be `if <the branch has a remote-tracking ref> then <PR bind and
  # `_ws_merge_proof`> else <strict ancestry, or refuse>`. `@{upstream}`
  # resolving is a fact about a REF IN THIS CLONE, not about whether the work is
  # safely somewhere else — and GitHub deletes the head branch on merge
  # ("Automatically delete head branches", the default), so the tracking ref
  # disappears exactly for the branches that ARE merged. A merged branch
  # therefore took the ancestry-only arm, which a squash can never satisfy
  # (its commits are not ancestors of anything), and answered `no-upstream`:
  # "<branch> was never pushed and holds commits not reachable from
  # origin/HEAD", about a branch that was pushed, reviewed and merged.
  # `_ws_merge_proof` — the ancestor -> tree -> patch-id -> cherry ladder — is
  # precisely the prover for that case, and it was sitting behind the arm split,
  # unreachable. Measured on `clear-cove` (tip e071a3aa, PR #618 squashed as
  # e5ffe56d): ancestor fail, tree fail, patch-id PASS, cherry fail. The prover
  # approved it. It was never called.
  #
  # So the ladder below is walked by every branch, in one order, and the
  # upstream probe keeps exactly ONE job — C1, which is a refusal about local
  # work, not a choice of prover — plus a say in the WORDING of C5's last
  # refusal (which missing evidence to name). Nothing else consults it.

  # ── C0: the tip, once ────────────────────────────────────────────
  # Every rung below needs it, and `_ws_reap_tail`'s branch delete is
  # `update-ref -d refs/heads/$branch "$REAP_TIP"` — an empty old-value degrades
  # that CAS into an unconditional delete (measured, git 2.43 — see the comment
  # at the call site). Reapable with an empty tip is not a state this function
  # may reach by any path, on any rung, which is why this is resolved here
  # rather than inside whichever rung happens to prove.
  REAP_TIP=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null) \
    || { _reap_refuse branch-missing "refs/heads/$branch does not resolve"; return 1; }

  # ── C1: work that is only here ───────────────────────────────────
  # A tracking ref that EXISTS and is behind the branch is direct evidence of
  # commits that are nowhere else — cheap, local, and it refuses before any
  # network cost. It selects no prover: when the tracking ref is gone (the whole
  # defect above) this rung is simply SKIPPED and both provers still run.
  if up=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "$branch@{upstream}" 2>/dev/null); then
    # The DIRECTION is unchanged and the SENTENCE is not — round-3 P2's class,
    # last instance, found by the final-round destructive review (F5b). A failed
    # `rev-list` yields "", which is `!= 0`, so this refuses, which is right; but
    # it then printed " commit(s) are not on the remote" — a count, with the
    # number missing, about a comparison that never ran. `_ws_gc_merged` and
    # `_ws_gc_dirty` both already say the rule for the file: report unprovable as
    # unprovable.
    ahead=$(git -C "$main" rev-list --count "$up..refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null)
    [[ "$ahead" == 0 ]] \
      || { _reap_refuse unpushed-commits "$( [[ "$ahead" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] \
             && echo "$ahead commit(s) are not on the remote" \
             || echo "could not count the commits between $up and refs/heads/$branch — refusing on a comparison that did not run")"; return 1; }
  fi

  # ── C2: there is a remote at all ─────────────────────────────────
  # `git config --get remote.origin.url`, NOT `git remote get-url origin`, and
  # the difference is a measured hole rather than a preference: with
  # `remote.origin.url` unset but a `pushurl` still configured, `get-url` prints
  # the REMOTE'S NAME and exits 0 (measured, git 2.43), so a repository with no
  # fetch URL walked this rung and failed at C3 instead — `fetch-failed`, a
  # transport story, about a repository that has no address at all. `--get` is
  # also `_gh_repo_slug`'s own first line, so C2 and C5's slug parse now agree
  # about what "there is no origin" means. Two statements, and the emptiness is
  # tested rather than inferred: `--get` exits 0 for a key that is set to "".
  ourl=$(git -C "$main" config --get remote.origin.url 2>/dev/null) || ourl=""
  [[ -n "$ourl" ]] \
    || { _reap_refuse no-remote "no origin remote for $project — there is nothing to prove this work is anywhere else"; return 1; }

  # ── C3: the fetch, ONCE, before any proof ────────────────────────
  # Mergedness is NEVER proven against an unfetched ref — the doctrine both old
  # arms already stated for themselves, now said once. Six repos on this box
  # have never been fetched; one is 24 days stale. A fetch that FAILED proves
  # nothing rather than proving against yesterday.
  #
  # ONE fetch, not two: the old arms fetched one each and no path ever ran
  # both, so this is the same cost, not a new one. What DID move is precedence
  # — a repository that cannot be fetched now refuses `fetch-failed` before
  # `gh pr list` is ever spent on it, where the old Phase C called gh first.
  # That is the cheaper order and the more honest one: an unfetched clone
  # cannot answer a mergedness question no matter what gh says about it.
  _plat_timeout 60 git -C "$main" fetch --quiet origin \
    || { _reap_refuse fetch-failed "could not fetch origin — nothing is claimed about your commits"; return 1; }
  REAP_FETCHED=$(date +%s)

  # ── C4 — RUNG 1: strict ancestry against origin/HEAD ─────────────
  # The strongest and cheapest statement available: every commit on this branch
  # is reachable from origin's CURRENT default branch, so nothing unique can be
  # destroyed. It needs no PR, no gh call and no network beyond the fetch just
  # made, which is why it is asked first — and it is the same definition of
  # merged that `_ws_gc_merged` applies to orphans and the per-child ladder
  # applies to nested checkouts.
  #
  # OPERATOR RULING 2026-08-22, recorded because it has two consequences a
  # reader will otherwise think are bugs (D-173, D-174). Ancestry runs BEFORE
  # the PR bind, so a workspace whose PR was merged with a real merge commit
  # ONTO THE DEFAULT BRANCH now proves `contained` and binds no PR, where it
  # used to prove `ancestor` and carry the PR number into the audit and the
  # tombstone; and a workspace whose bound PR is still OPEN, but whose commits
  # have all reached origin/HEAD by some other route, is now reapable where it
  # refused `not-merged`. Both were put to the operator with their costs before
  # this was written. Neither destroys anything unique — that is exactly what
  # the ancestry proof states — and reap deletes only the LOCAL branch, so an
  # open PR and its remote head are untouched.
  #
  # THE SYMREF IS RE-DERIVED FROM THE REMOTE, not read from the clone-time
  # memory — adversarial-review finding on this rung's first draft, and it
  # survives the reorder unchanged. `git fetch` neither prunes a branch the
  # remote deleted nor moves the local origin/HEAD symref, so a remote that
  # switched its default and deleted the old one left the draft proving
  # containment against a FROZEN tracking ref: "origin already holds every
  # commit on it", said about an origin that holds none of them (measured, git
  # 2.43 — deleted default, symref still resolving, is-ancestor still 0).
  #
  # AND A COMPARISON THAT DID NOT RUN IS NEVER A "NO". Each way this rung can
  # fail to ANSWER — the remote advertising no default, a symref that does not
  # resolve, a merge-base that errored — records WHY in `ancwhy` and falls
  # through to rung 2, whose merge proof needs no origin/HEAD at all. `ancwhy`
  # is then spent in C5's last refusal, so the operator is told which half could
  # not be checked instead of being told "not an ancestor" about a question
  # nobody managed to ask. That polarity is `_ws_gc_merged`'s and
  # `_ws_gc_dirty`'s, and it is the one thing that must survive any later edit
  # to this rung.
  ancwhy="its commits are not reachable from origin's default branch"
  if _plat_timeout 60 git -C "$main" remote set-head origin --auto >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    if obase=$(git -C "$main" symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD); then
      git -C "$main" merge-base --is-ancestor "refs/heads/$branch" "$obase" 2>/dev/null
      case $? in
        0) REAP_PROOF=contained; contained=1 ;;
        1) ancwhy="its commits are not reachable from $obase" ;;
        *) ancwhy="comparing it against $obase failed, so containment was never actually compared" ;;
      esac
    else
      ancwhy="origin/HEAD does not resolve, so containment could not be compared against anything"
    fi
  else
    ancwhy="origin did not advertise a default branch (git remote set-head origin --auto failed), so containment could not be compared against anything"
  fi

  # ── C6: the base oid ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  # A REFUSAL, not a default, and it belongs to BOTH rungs — which is why it
  # sits between them rather than inside either. This was the last fingerprint
  # input that answered a failed read with `""`: measured, with only this read
  # failing the verdict stayed `reapable` and a token was issued over a field
  # nobody measured — and because the failure is deterministic, ws-reap
  # recomputes the same `""` and the `--expect` consent check matches. Same
  # class as the ignored digest (deviation 6) and the stash count above.
  # `$base` is the registry's, so this fires for a workspace whose base ref was
  # deleted or renamed under it, which is a state to reconcile rather than one
  # to delete over.
  #
  # BEFORE RUNG 2, NOT AFTER THE LADDER, and the position is the finding rather
  # than the rung: both old arms resolved it before their own proof — the
  # contained arm right after its ancestry, Phase C right before
  # `_ws_merge_proof` — so a workspace whose base ref was deleted answered
  # `base-missing`, the state it is actually in. Resolved after the ladder
  # instead, the same workspace answers `tree-differs` and the operator is
  # handed a diff dump about a proof that failed for an unrelated reason.
  REAP_BASEOID=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify "$base" 2>/dev/null) \
    || { _reap_refuse base-missing "$base does not resolve in $main"; return 1; }

  # ── C5 — RUNG 2: the merged PR, and `_ws_merge_proof` ────────────
  # Skipped in full when rung 1 proved there is no unique work: a PR bind for a
  # branch that has no PR refuses `no-bound-pr`, and a merge proof against an
  # empty merge commit refuses `merge-commit-missing` — both statements about
  # evidence this verdict does not rest on. Rung 1 mints proof `contained` and
  # binds NO PR: REAP_PRNUM, REAP_PRURL, REAP_MERGE and REAP_HEADOID keep their
  # reset "" — the audit prints them as null/"" (the same unbound shape every
  # pre-Phase-C refusal has always had), never a plausible value nobody
  # measured.
  if (( ! contained )); then
  # NOT `no-remote`, AND THE REASON IS THE ONE THIS WHOLE WAVE IS ABOUT. C2
  # proved this project HAS an origin and C3 fetched from it, so refusing
  # `no-remote` here would put "This project has no `origin` remote" — the
  # sentence `wsaudit.ts` renders, and the sheet renders sentences, never
  # details — on a repository ccd read from three rungs earlier. That is the
  # same shape as the old "was never pushed" said of a pushed branch, and the
  # rewrite would have introduced it: on the old ladder a branch with no
  # upstream took the contained arm and never reached `_gh_repo_slug` at all,
  # so a project on a plain path or a non-GitHub host refused `no-upstream`
  # with a true detail. Measured on both trees with the same fixture.
  #
  # An origin that is not a GitHub OWNER/NAME is not a missing origin — it is a
  # repository no PR can bind to, which is exactly the empty-row position
  # below, whose chooser already picks its sentence from remote EVIDENCE rather
  # than from the slug. So it takes that arm. `no-remote` keeps ONE producer in
  # this function (C2), where the sentence is true.
  if repo=$(_gh_repo_slug "$main"); then
    rows=$(_gh_pr_list "$repo")   || { _reap_refuse gh-unreadable "$rows"; return 1; }
    row=$(printf '%s' "$rows" | _pr_py pick "$main" "$branch" "$baseShort" "$REAP_TIP")
  else
    row=''
  fi
  case "$row" in
    '')
      # BOTH RUNGS HAVE NOW FAILED, and the token names the strongest evidence
      # that is MISSING rather than the arm that ran — there is only one arm
      # now. This decides a SENTENCE, never a prover.
      #
      # AND "WAS IT EVER PUSHED" IS ASKED OF THE CONFIG, NOT OF THE TRACKING
      # REF, which is the whole lesson of this block restated one rung lower.
      # `$up` is the tracking ref, and the tracking ref is exactly what
      # disappears when GitHub deletes a merged head — so answering from it
      # would print "was never pushed" about a branch that was pushed and
      # merged, the precise falsehood this ladder exists to stop, just from a
      # different line. `branch.<name>.merge` SURVIVES the delete (measured,
      # git 2.43: after `git push origin --delete`, `@{upstream}` stops
      # resolving with rc 1 while `git config --get branch.ws/x.merge` still
      # answers `refs/heads/ws/x`), so it is the honest witness that this
      # branch once had a home on the remote. `git branch --unset-upstream`
      # removes it, and that IS a branch with no remote evidence left.
      # THREE WITNESSES, because each of the first two misses a real push.
      # `$up` is `@{upstream}`, which needs BOTH the config and the tracking
      # ref. `branch.<name>.merge` survives GitHub's head delete (measured, git
      # 2.43) but is absent for `git push origin ws/x` with no `-u` — a push
      # that leaves the branch on origin and configures nothing, which is
      # exactly the case `cmd_ws_rename` already asks origin directly about.
      # The tracking REF is the witness for that one: it resolves, the commits
      # are on origin, and calling that "never pushed" is the same false
      # sentence one rung further down.
      if [[ -n "$up" ]] || git -C "$main" config --get "branch.$branch.merge" >/dev/null 2>&1 \
         || git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/remotes/origin/$branch" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        _reap_refuse no-bound-pr "no merged PR binds to $branch, and $ancwhy"
      else
        _reap_refuse no-upstream "$branch was never pushed (it has no remote-tracking branch and no tracking configuration), no merged PR binds to it, and $ancwhy"
      fi
      return 1 ;;
    '!pr-head-not-ours')  _reap_refuse pr-head-not-ours "the PR head commit is not reachable from $branch"; return 1 ;;
    '!'*)                 _reap_refuse "${row#!}" "the bound PR is not merged"; return 1 ;;
  esac
  IFS=$'\t' read -r REAP_PRNUM REAP_PRURL REAP_MERGE REAP_HEADOID <<< "$row"
  # §7's argv boundary, restated on THIS side of the split — because the split
  # is what can move a value out of the field that was anchored and into one
  # that was not. `_pr_py pick` checks `mergeCommit.oid` and `headRefOid`
  # against `OID` (ccd:2750) inside python, but `url` and `number` are
  # GitHub-sourced and unanchored, and all four come back joined with tabs. So a
  # PR whose `url` holds a TAB shifts every later field: measured with
  # `url: "https://x/\tinjected"`, the string `injected` became REAP_MERGE and
  # reached `git cat-file -e "injected^{commit}"`, `git merge-base --is-ancestor
  # -- "$tip" "injected"` and `git diff --stat "injected" …`. It failed closed
  # there, but by luck rather than by construction, and one anchored test is
  # what turns the one into the other. Same regex as python's, deliberately:
  # a floor of 7 because a 3-character hex string like `abc` can be a real ref,
  # and both ends anchored because `headRefOid: "main"` otherwise resolves as a
  # REVISION and binds.
  #
  # ALL SIX PIECES ARE PINNED — the two anchors on each oid, the floor and the
  # ceiling — and the route that reaches them is a fixture that shadows
  # `_pr_py pick` and hands this line one crafted row at a time. That is not a
  # contrivance; it is the case the line is FOR. While the two ends are one
  # producer, python's `OID` screens both oids before they are joined and the
  # tab-shift above always leaves a literal TAB in the tail field, so the
  # character class alone answers everything and NOTHING beneath it is
  # observable. Measured, and the reason this comment is a correction: the
  # previous one claimed "the character class and the anchors are what is
  # pinned" and disclosed the floor as the sole survivor. Re-swept, EIGHT
  # mutants of this one line survived — dropping either conjunct, dropping
  # either `^`, dropping either `$`, `{1,40}`, and `{7,}`. With `^` gone,
  # `origin/deadbeef1` matches on its nine-hex tail and reaches
  # `git cat-file -e "origin/deadbeef1^{commit}"`, `merge-base --is-ancestor`
  # and `diff --stat`: the exact "resolves as a REVISION and binds" case the
  # two lines above say the anchors exist to stop. bash's `=~` searches unless
  # anchored, which is why each anchor needs its own fixture rather than
  # inheriting one from its neighbour.
  [[ "$REAP_MERGE" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$ && "$REAP_HEADOID" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{7,40}$ ]] \
    || { _reap_refuse pr-fields-malformed "gh named mergeCommit='$REAP_MERGE' headRefOid='$REAP_HEADOID'"; return 1; }
  git -C "$main" cat-file -e "${REAP_MERGE}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null \
    || { _reap_refuse merge-commit-missing "$REAP_MERGE is not in the object store after a fetch"; return 1; }
  if _ws_merge_proof "$main" "$REAP_MERGE" "$REAP_TIP"; then
    REAP_PROOF="$_WS_PROOF"
  else
    _reap_refuse tree-differs "$(git -C "$main" diff --stat "$REAP_MERGE" "$REAP_TIP" -- | head -5; git -C "$main" log --oneline "$REAP_MERGE".."$REAP_TIP" | head -5)"
    return 1
  fi
  fi   # (( ! contained )) — rung 1 proved it, so rung 2 has nothing to add

  # ── Phase D1: the session ──────────────────────────────────────
  st=$(_ws_status "$id") || { _reap_refuse status-unknown "cannot read the wrapper status file"; return 1; }
  [[ "$st" != busy ]] || { _reap_refuse session-busy "the session is working — a model turn, a Bash tool command, or a status this ccd does not recognise"; return 1; }

  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed and unpinnable BY CONSTRUCTION: the `sort` can
  # be deleted with the suite green, because every state that reaches this line
  # has an EMPTY sensitive set — a non-empty one refuses `sensitive-ignored`
  # above, with no override — so the digest is the constant sha256 of one blank
  # line. It stays because the field is a set digest and a set has no order,
  # and because the ordering `_ws_collect_ignored` produces is git's, which is
  # not promised. The only way to pin it would be a state this design forbids.
  REAP_SENSDIGEST=$(printf '%s\n' "${REAP_SENSITIVE[@]-}" | sort | _plat_sha256 | cut -d' ' -f1)
  # The clips manifest is `_ws_clip_manifest`'s, the same producer the TOMBSTONE
  # records at (a), so the record of what was deleted and the consent to delete
  # it cannot describe different files. Hashed rather than carried, because the
  # fingerprint is a line-per-fact document and a manifest holds newlines.
  #
  # TWO STATEMENTS AND A REFUSAL — the sixteenth forgery (see
  # `_ws_clip_manifest`). This was one line, `… | _plat_sha256 | cut`, which
  # reports the PIPE's status and therefore could not have noticed the
  # producer failing even if it had looked. An unenumerable clips directory
  # hashed as `[]`, deterministically, on both sides: the audit issued a token
  # over it and ws-reap recomputed the same digest, so `--expect` matched and
  # step (h) destroyed a directory nothing had listed. A digest is a claim that
  # the thing digested was read; when it was not, there is no fingerprint to
  # take and the answer is a refusal, exactly as `base-missing` and the ignored
  # digest (deviation 6) are refusals rather than empty strings.
  #
  # `printf '%s\n'`, not `printf '%s'`: `$(…)` strips the trailing newline
  # `_ws_clip_manifest`'s `echo` produced, and the digest of `[]` has to stay
  # the digest of `[]\n` — the value every existing token fixture computes as
  # `printf '[]\n' | sha256sum`. The refusal is the only thing that changes.
  clipsjson=$(_ws_clip_manifest "$id") \
    || { _reap_refuse clips-unreadable "could not list $HOME/.cc-clips/$id — the pasted images this cleanup would delete cannot be enumerated"; return 1; }
  REAP_CLIPDIGEST=$(printf '%s\n' "$clipsjson" | _plat_sha256 | cut -d' ' -f1)
  # `printf '%s'`, not `printf '%s\n'`: unlike the clips manifest above,
  # `REAP_CHILDLINES` is not `$(…)`-captured (it is built in place by the
  # descent loop), so there is no subshell trailing-newline strip to
  # compensate for — an empty child set is the literal empty string, and its
  # digest is `sha256sum` of nothing, exactly what a fixture with no
  # registered children computes by hand.
  REAP_CHILDDIGEST=$(printf '%s' "$REAP_CHILDLINES" | _plat_sha256 | cut -d' ' -f1)
  # `$regbranch`, NOT `$branch`: input 2 is "what the registry says" and input
  # 11 (`REAP_WTHEAD`) is "what git says", and after the drift rung `$branch` IS
  # input 11. Passing it here would hash git's name twice and leave the
  # registry's out of the token altogether — so a registry entry rewritten
  # between the audit and the tap would not move the fingerprint, on the one
  # verb whose whole consent mechanism is that any change to a fact it measured
  # invalidates the token.
  REAP_TOKEN=$(_ws_fingerprint "$id" "$regbranch" "$REAP_TIP" "$REAP_HEADOID" "$REAP_MERGE" "$REAP_PROOF" \
                 "$REAP_DIRTY" "$REAP_IGNDIGEST" "$REAP_SENSDIGEST" "$REAP_STASHES" "$REAP_WTHEAD" \
                 "$REAP_BASEOID" "$REAP_CLIPDIGEST" "$REAP_CHILDDIGEST")
  REAP_VERDICT=reapable
  return 0
}

cmd_ws_audit() {   # ccd ws-audit --session <id> — read-only about the WORKSPACE;
                    # the token's source
  # "READ-ONLY" IS ABOUT THE WORKSPACE, NOT ABOUT THE REPOSITORY, and the
  # distinction is worth stating where the header makes the claim. This verb
  # destroys nothing and creates nothing that outlives it — no worktree, no
  # branch, no registry field, no tombstone — which is the property the sheet
  # depends on. It does, through `_ws_reap_eval`, run `git fetch origin` and
  # `git remote set-head origin --auto`, so it can advance remote-tracking refs
  # and REPOINT `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` when the remote has changed its
  # default branch. Both were already true on the contained arm; the containment
  # ladder made them true on every audit, which is a widening of WHEN rather
  # than of WHAT. Neither is optional: mergedness is never proven against an
  # unfetched ref, and a symref read from clone-time memory proved containment
  # against a default the remote had deleted (measured, git 2.43).
  [[ $# -eq 2 && $1 == --session ]] || die "usage: ccd ws-audit --session <id>"
  local id=$2
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"

  # `_json_str`'s status, checked ONCE and up front — the same probe, for the
  # same reason, as `_ws_archive_manifest` (ccd:8133). Nineteen `_json_str` substitutions
  # below sit inside printf ARGUMENT LISTS, where a failure is a swallowed status
  # and an empty argument, i.e. `"path":,` — a document that authorises deletions
  # and does not parse, at exit 0. Bytes can no longer fail the encode (see
  # `_json_str`), so what is left is python3 not being runnable at all, and the
  # only honest answer to that is to print NOTHING and exit non-zero. Before
  # `_ws_reap_eval`, so a refusal costs no gh call and no fetch.
  _json_str probe >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "python3 unavailable — cannot quote the audit record safely"

  # BEFORE `_ws_reap_eval`, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT. Its Phase-A refusals —
  # `not-archived` first among them — return with every downstream field null,
  # which is exactly the shape that made F8's orphan invisible to the one
  # artifact whose job is answering "what is the state of this workspace".
  # These three are answerable on EVERY verdict, so they are answered on every
  # verdict. (`_alive` appeared nowhere in this verb before.)
  local aliveflag=false startedflag=false unitstate
  _alive "$id" && aliveflag=true
  [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && startedflag=true
  unitstate=$(_ws_unit_state "$id")

  _ws_reap_eval "$id" || true      # a refusal is an ANSWER, not an error
  local project workdir branch base repo transcript reaping
  project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch);   base=$(_reg_get "$id" base)
  repo=$(_gh_repo_slug "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project" 2>/dev/null) || repo=""
  transcript=$(_transcript_path "$id") || transcript=""
  reaping=$(_reg_get "$id" reaping)

  # Sensitive-first, then bytes descending. Counts and total bytes are NEVER
  # truncated — the whole judgement this design rests on is the human reading
  # a filename, and a truncated list makes it structurally unavailable.
  #
  # NUL-terminated end to end, and the record is `sensitive\tbytes\tpath`
  # (`_ws_collect_ignored`): the read that fills it is `-z`, so a path may
  # legitimately contain a TAB or a NEWLINE. `printf '%s\n' | sort` splits such
  # a path across two records and an `IFS=$'\t' read -r p b s` on a
  # path-first record shifts every field — measured, `a<TAB>b.log` printed
  # `"bytes":b.log`, which is not JSON. So: `sort -z`, `read -d ''`, and the
  # two fixed-shape fields peeled off the FRONT with `${…%%}`/`${…#}` rather
  # than by word-splitting, which would collapse a run of tabs.
  #
  # AND THE WHOLE BLOCK IS SKIPPED WHEN NOTHING WAS SCANNED — final-round tests
  # review F3, the fourteenth instance of the measurement-forgery class and the
  # first one to reach the delete-confirmation surface itself. These four
  # fields used to be initialised to `[`/`0`/`0`/`[` and printed
  # UNCONDITIONALLY, on every verdict, so a Phase-A refusal
  # (`registry-branch-drift`, `foreign-worktree`, `no-worktree-record`,
  # `detached-head`, `incomplete-registry` — every one of which leaves the
  # worktree ON DISK, possibly full of work) emitted
  # `"ignored":[],"ignoredCount":0,"ignoredBytes":0,"sensitive":[]` and
  # `ReapSheet` rendered "not in git: 0 entries, 0 B" about a tree
  # `_ws_collect_ignored` had never been asked to look at. `worktreeBytes` on
  # the same document already said `null` (deviation 120); its four siblings
  # did not. `null` is the answer, for the reason the whole class is closed
  # that way: 0 is the claim "there is nothing here", and nobody looked.
  local ign_json=null total=null count=null sens_json=null sfilt=null first=1 rec rest p b s
  if (( REAP_IGNSCANNED )); then
   ign_json="["; total=0; count=0; sfilt="${REAP_SENSITIVE_FILTERED:-0}"
   if (( ${#REAP_IGNORED[@]} )); then
    while IFS= read -r -d '' rec; do
      s="${rec%%$'\t'*}"; rest="${rec#*$'\t'}"
      b="${rest%%$'\t'*}"; p="${rest#*$'\t'}"
      # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: `[[ -n "$p" ]] || continue` is removable
      # with the suite green, and always will be — REAP_IGNORED's records are
      # built by _ws_collect_ignored, whose own `[[ -n "$p" ]]` (disclosed
      # there) already refuses an empty path, and `read -d ''` cannot produce
      # an empty record from `printf '%s\0'` over a non-empty array. Same
      # reasoning as the `$dl` sibling below; kept as defence in depth against
      # a future producer. (Gate finding N8.)
      [[ -n "$p" ]] || continue
      (( first )) || ign_json+=","; first=0
      ign_json+="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$p"),\"bytes\":$b,\"sensitive\":$( ((s)) && echo true || echo false)}"
      total=$(( total + b )); count=$(( count + 1 ))
    done < <(printf '%s\0' "${REAP_IGNORED[@]}" | sort -z -t$'\t' -k1,1nr -k2,2nr)
   fi
   ign_json+="]"

   # Inside the same `REAP_IGNSCANNED` block, and that is the point rather than
   # tidiness: `sensitive` and `sensitiveFiltered` are two answers from the one
   # scan, so a document that reports the scan's ignored set as unmeasured and
   # its secret-shaped names as "none" would be self-contradictory in the
   # direction that reassures.
   local sfirst=1 sp
   sens_json="["
   for sp in "${REAP_SENSITIVE[@]-}"; do
     [[ -n "$sp" ]] || continue
     (( sfirst )) || sens_json+=","; sfirst=0
     sens_json+="$(_json_str "$sp")"
   done
   sens_json+="]"
  fi

  # `-z` HERE TOO, for the reason the ignored list has it: this array is read by
  # the same human, in the same document, before the same irreversible action,
  # and without `-z` git C-QUOTES the same five classes of byte — measured, a
  # dirty `déjà vu.txt` rendered `"?? \"d\\303\\251j\\303\\240 vu.txt\""` beside
  # an `ignored` array already carrying raw paths. No guard reads this array
  # (`dirty-tree` refuses on `REAP_DIRTY`, a count from `_ws_reap_eval`'s own
  # read), so nothing here decides anything; the whole judgement this design
  # rests on is a human reading a filename, and `\303\251` is not one.
  #
  # A pipe rather than a temp file: process substitution is a PIPE, so the NULs
  # survive it — it is `$(…)` that drops them, which is why the collector needs
  # a file and this does not. And `-c core.quotePath=false` is deliberately NOT
  # repeated here: `_ws_collect_ignored` records it as a measured NO-OP beside
  # `-z`, and a second copy would be a second line no fixture can pin.
  #
  # A RENAME OR COPY IS TWO RECORDS, `XY <to>NUL<from>NUL`, where the non-`-z`
  # form printed one `R  <from> -> <to>` line — so the second is consumed here
  # and rejoined into that line. Unpaired it would join the list as an entry
  # with no status letters: a bare filename, in exactly the list a human is
  # asked to read. The letter is in the X column: measured on git 2.43, a
  # worktree-only rename is not detected at all (` D` plus `??`) and only an
  # INDEX rename produces the two-path form. `C` is not dead weight beside `R`:
  # it needs `status.renames=copies` AND a modified source (git's copy detection
  # only looks at sources that changed in the same diff, and status has no
  # `--find-copies-harder`), and both halves are in a fixture.
  #
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed and measured: `[[ -n "$dl" ]] || continue` is
  # removable with the suite green. Under `-z` git's output ends in a NUL and
  # never contains two in a row, so `read -d ''` cannot produce an empty record
  # — it hits EOF and ends the loop instead. Kept for the reason the identical
  # line in `_ws_collect_ignored` is kept: the next statement indexes `$dl`
  # positionally, and an empty record would put `""` into the one list this
  # design asks a human to read filenames out of.
  #
  # AND `null` FOR A READ THAT DID NOT HAPPEN OR DID NOT FINISH — final-round
  # tests review F3, the same rung as the ignored block above. This was
  # `dirty_json="["` … `2>/dev/null`, so BOTH "there is no directory to read"
  # and "git failed, or half-read the tree" produced `[]`, and `ReapSheet`
  # renders `[]` as **uncommitted: none** — the single most reassuring row on a
  # delete-confirmation sheet, stated about a tree nobody looked at.
  #
  # The rc AND the stderr, for the reason `_ws_gc_dirty` and Phase B both give
  # in full: a PARTIALLY unreadable tree exits ZERO with empty stdout and the
  # diagnostic on stderr (measured, git 2.43), so the exit code alone answers
  # "clean" for a tree holding uncommitted work behind a chmod-000 directory.
  # A temp file rather than the process substitution this used to have,
  # because a pipeline's status is not the read's — the same two-statement
  # shape Phase B states the rule for. The NULs survive a file exactly as they
  # survive a pipe.
  local dirty_json=null dfirst=1 dl dsrc dout derrf derr drc
  if [[ -d "$workdir" ]]; then
    dout=$(_plat_mktemp) || dout=""
    derrf=$(_plat_mktemp) || derrf=""
    if [[ -n "$dout" && -n "$derrf" ]]; then
      git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain -z >"$dout" 2>"$derrf"; drc=$?
      derr=$(cat "$derrf" 2>/dev/null)
      if (( drc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$derr" ]]; then
        dirty_json="["
        while IFS= read -r -d '' dl; do
          [[ -n "$dl" ]] || continue
          case "$dl" in
            [RC]*) IFS= read -r -d '' dsrc && dl="${dl:0:3}$dsrc -> ${dl:3}" ;;
          esac
          (( dfirst )) || dirty_json+=","; dfirst=0
          dirty_json+="$(_json_str "$dl")"
        done < "$dout"
        dirty_json+="]"
      fi
    fi
    [[ -n "$dout" ]]  && rm -f "$dout"
    [[ -n "$derrf" ]] && rm -f "$derrf"
  fi

  # `ahead` starts at `null` and BOTH ways of not measuring it keep it there —
  # final-round destructive review F2, the last surviving `|| x=0` in this
  # file. The initialiser and the fallback were each a fabricated 0, and both
  # are reachable: the `if` does not run at all when `$base` does not resolve
  # or `$branch` is empty, and `rev-list` can fail inside it.
  #
  # ccd already litigated this exact value 1,340 lines earlier. `_pr_state_one`
  # (ccd:2539) writes `ahead=""` under the comment: `"" is UNMEASURED, not
  # zero. ahead=0 is the claim "this branch is level with base" … a ref that
  # does not resolve has not been measured against anything.` The file
  # disagreed with itself; it no longer does.
  local exists=false headmatch=false ahead=null bytes=0
  [[ -d "$workdir" ]] && exists=true
  [[ -n "$REAP_WTHEAD" && "$REAP_WTHEAD" == "$branch" ]] && headmatch=true
  # THE BRANCH THIS DOCUMENT IS ABOUT is the one `_ws_reap_eval` just evaluated
  # — git's worktree record when there is one — and `$branch` here is still the
  # REGISTRY's, read at the top of this function. They differ under drift, and
  # every field below that says "branch" has to mean the branch a reap would
  # actually remove, or the sheet describes one branch and the tap deletes
  # another. `$branch` keeps the registry's name under `registryBranch`, so
  # nothing is lost and nobody has to infer either from the other.
  #
  # The fallback is the registry's, for the refusals that never reach the
  # worktree block at all (`no-such-session`, `not-archived`, and every other
  # Phase A rung): `REAP_BRANCH` is "" there, and an empty branch on the wire
  # would be a worse answer than the name the registry does have.
  local evbranch="$REAP_BRANCH"; [[ -n "$evbranch" ]] || evbranch="$branch"
  if [[ -n "$evbranch" ]] && git -C "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project" rev-parse --verify --quiet "$base" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    ahead=$(git -C "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project" rev-list --count "$base..refs/heads/$evbranch" 2>/dev/null)
    [[ "$ahead" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || ahead=null
  fi
  # Pre-merge fix round, finding F: `null`, never a fabricated `0` — the same
  # rule `_ws_archive_manifest` (ccd:1842) already carries, applied here where
  # `_ws_gc_bytes`'s own fix (ccd:4105) actually closes the gap. This is the
  # figure `ReapSheet.tsx`'s primary Remove button prints beside "Remove
  # <slug> ·" — the one confirm the whole design exists to protect — so a
  # `du` that could not fully measure the tree must say so rather than hand
  # the sheet a plausible-looking lie. `WsAudit.worktreeBytes` is `number |
  # null` on the wire for exactly this reason (shared/api.ts).
  bytes=$(_ws_gc_bytes "$workdir"); [[ "$bytes" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || bytes=null

  # The clips manifest, read HERE so its status is visible — the sixteenth
  # forgery (`_ws_clip_manifest`). `null` is "this directory exists and could
  # not be enumerated", the same word and the same rule as `dirty`,
  # `ignored`, `stashes` and `worktreeBytes` on this document: `[]` is the
  # claim that there is nothing here, and nobody looked. `WsAudit.clips` is
  # `… [] | null` on the wire so no consumer can be forced back into
  # fabricating the empty array to compile, and `ReapSheet` renders the null
  # as "could not be read" rather than as **none**.
  local clips_json
  clips_json=$(_ws_clip_manifest "$id") || clips_json=null

  # `children[]` — D4, `_ws_child_manifest`. `null` shares its rule with
  # `dirty`/`ignored`/`clips` above: the workdir is gone, or `_ws_reap_eval`
  # refused before Phase A finished proving identity, or the enumeration
  # itself failed outright. `$REAP_WTHEAD` is the SAME signal
  # `headMatchesRegistry` below already reads for "Phase A passed" — it is
  # assigned at the end of `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase A, only once the eval has
  # walked registry-branch-drift, detached-head, no-worktree-record AND
  # foreign-worktree without refusing, and stays `""` (its `_ws_reap_reset`
  # default) on every refusal at or before those rungs, workdir-missing
  # included. Gated on nothing deeper than that: a Phase-B refusal further
  # down the ladder (dirty-tree, sensitive-ignored, nested-checkouts-present,
  # any per-child rung) must still see a populated `children[]` — that is
  # the whole point of D4, the sheet that shows a human WHY the eval is
  # about to refuse.
  local children_json=null main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  if [[ -n "$REAP_WTHEAD" && -d "$workdir" ]]; then
    children_json=$(_ws_child_manifest "$main" "$workdir") || children_json=null
  fi

  # `branch` IS THE BRANCH A REAP WOULD REMOVE, and `registryBranch` is the
  # witness beside it — additive and absence-permitting, so a server reading an
  # older ccd sees the key missing and renders no note rather than throwing.
  # `drift` is the SENTENCE, built once on the box where both names and the
  # workdir are in hand, because a client that assembled it from two fields
  # would be a second definition of the same rule.
  printf '{"id":%s,"branch":%s,"registryBranch":%s,"drift":%s,"base":%s,"workdir":%s,"project":%s,"repo":%s,' \
    "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$evbranch")" "$(_json_str "$branch")" \
    "$(_json_str "$REAP_DRIFT")" "$(_json_str "$base")" \
    "$(_json_str "$workdir")" "$(_json_str "$project")" "$(_json_str "$repo")"
  printf '"exists":%s,"headMatchesRegistry":%s,"reaping":%s,' \
    "$exists" "$headmatch" "$( [[ -n "$reaping" ]] && _json_str "$reaping" || echo null)"
  # The session behind the workspace, not just the tree. `unit` is null — never
  # a fourth word — when this box has no systemctl, or the manager did not answer.
  printf '"alive":%s,"started":%s,"unit":%s,' \
    "$aliveflag" "$startedflag" "$( [[ -n "$unitstate" ]] && _json_str "$unitstate" || echo null)"
  # Six fields, one source of truth for whether each is a measurement: `null`
  # from the builders above whenever the read did not happen. See the two
  # blocks for the reason; the printf is deliberately dumb so no third rule can
  # appear here.
  printf '"dirty":%s,"ignored":%s,"ignoredCount":%s,"ignoredBytes":%s,"sensitive":%s,"sensitiveFiltered":%s,' \
    "$dirty_json" "$ign_json" "$count" "$total" "$sens_json" "$sfilt"
  # `clips` on the wire, because the sheet cannot ask a human to consent to a
  # deletion it does not list. `_ws_clip_manifest` is the same producer the
  # tombstone and the fingerprint use, and it is read on every verdict — a
  # refusal is the answer a human reads too.
  #
  # `$clips_json` rather than a `$(…)` in this argument list, and that is the
  # whole of the sixteenth forgery's fix on the wire: a command substitution
  # inside a printf ARGUMENT LIST swallows the producer's status, so the field
  # was `[]` for a directory nobody could open and `ReapSheet` rendered
  # **clips: none** — the most reassuring row on the sheet, above a reachable
  # Remove button. It is computed above, where the status is visible, and it is
  # `null` (never `[]`) when the enumeration did not happen.
  # `stashes` is `null` until `_ws_reap_eval` has actually read the stash list
  # (its reset default is now "" rather than 0, see `_ws_reap_reset`): on a
  # Phase-A refusal the read never happens, and `ReapSheet` renders a 0 there
  # as **stashes: none** — a promise that nothing stashed is at stake, made
  # about a list nobody opened.
  printf '"clips":%s,"stashes":%s,"worktreeBytes":%s,"commitsAheadOfBase":%s,' \
    "$clips_json" \
    "$( [[ "$REAP_STASHES" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "$REAP_STASHES" || echo null)" \
    "$bytes" "$ahead"
  printf '"pr":{"number":%s,"url":%s,"mergeCommit":%s,"headRefOid":%s},' \
    "$( [[ "$REAP_PRNUM" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "$REAP_PRNUM" || echo null)" \
    "$(_json_str "$REAP_PRURL")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_MERGE")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_HEADOID")"
  # `fetchedAt` is `null` until Phase C actually fetched — same rung. Its reset
  # default of 0 is a real epoch second, and `ReapSheet` printed it through
  # `days()`: "merged in #? (proof: none), 20669 days ago", a date for a fetch
  # that never ran, on the same line as two fields (`pr.number`, `merge.proof`)
  # that have said `null` for the same state since deviation 10.
  printf '"merge":{"proof":%s,"fetchedAt":%s},"transcript":%s,' \
    "$( [[ -n "$REAP_PROOF" ]] && _json_str "$REAP_PROOF" || echo null)" \
    "$( [[ "$REAP_FETCHED" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "$REAP_FETCHED" || echo null)" \
    "$(_json_str "$transcript")"
  printf '"children":%s,' "$children_json"
  # Three verdicts, not two, and the third one is why: `reapable` MEANS a token.
  #
  # `token` is present ONLY when the verdict is reapable AND the tree was
  # actually read. The second half is not a belt-and-braces restatement of the
  # first: with the worktree gone and a `reaping` breadcrumb explaining why,
  # `_ws_reap_eval` skips the whole of Phase B on purpose — that is the RESUME
  # of a killed reap, and refusing it would strand the workspace half-deleted
  # forever — so it reaches `reapable` with `REAP_DIRTY=0`, `REAP_IGNDIGEST=""`,
  # `REAP_SENSITIVE=()` and `REAP_WTHEAD=""` straight out of `_ws_reap_reset`.
  # Those are the reset defaults, not measurements, and `dirty-tree` and
  # `sensitive-ignored` — the two guards §7 forbids overriding — were never
  # evaluated at all. A fingerprint over them is deviation 6's digest forgery
  # with more fields: ws-reap recomputes the same unmeasured values and the
  # consent check matches, so the token would certify a tree nobody ever looked
  # at. The audit therefore reports the state (`exists:false`, `reaping`) and
  # hands out nothing to confirm against; resuming a killed reap is ws-reap's
  # own path, not a token-carrying delete.
  #
  # AND IT MUST NOT SAY `reapable` WHILE DOING SO. Withholding the token from a
  # verdict that still read `reapable` produced a wire shape the sheet
  # mis-renders: `wsaudit.ts` gives `reapable` an EMPTY sentence, `ReapSheet`
  # renders the primary Remove button on `verdict === 'reapable'`, and that
  # button's `confirm()` early-returns when `token === undefined` — a button
  # that silently does nothing, under no explanation at all. Two different
  # states must not share a verdict: this one is `reap-interrupted`, the
  # worktree is already gone, ccrc's own breadcrumb says ccrc removed it, and
  # what is left is a RESUME (`ws-reap --resume`, Task 6), which is not a
  # token-carrying delete and never asks for a confirmation the human cannot
  # give. `_ws_reap_eval` is deliberately untouched: its `reapable` is what the
  # resume path itself depends on.
  #
  # AND A THIRD STATE THAT MUST NOT SHARE ONE EITHER, for the identical reason
  # — the sixteenth forgery. `_ws_reap_eval` refuses `clips-unreadable` when
  # its own read fails, so this branch covers the WINDOW between that read and
  # this one: the directory can be made unreadable in the microseconds
  # between, and then the token above would certify a manifest while the
  # document beside it says the manifest could not be taken. One state, one
  # verdict: no token, and the same token name the eval uses, so the sheet
  # reads the same sentence whichever read caught it.
  if [[ "$REAP_VERDICT" == reapable && "$clips_json" == null ]]; then
    printf '"verdict":"clips-unreadable","detail":%s}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "could not list $HOME/.cc-clips/$id — the pasted images this cleanup would delete cannot be enumerated")"
  elif [[ "$REAP_VERDICT" == reapable && "$exists" == true ]]; then
    printf '"verdict":"reapable","detail":"","token":%s}\n' "$(_json_str "$REAP_TOKEN")"
  elif [[ "$REAP_VERDICT" == reapable ]]; then
    printf '"verdict":"reap-interrupted","detail":%s}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "a previous removal stopped after the ${reaping:-worktree} step and left no worktree to inspect")"
  else
    printf '"verdict":%s,"detail":%s}\n' "$(_json_str "$REAP_VERDICT")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_DETAIL")"
  fi
}

_ws_attic_pin() {   # main id workdir tip -> echoes how many refs were created
  # Every distinct sha in the worktree's HEAD reflog, deduped and capped, plus
  # the tip. ~50 bytes each, no new objects, and the commits become REFERENCED
  # rather than dangling — so gc.pruneExpire can never reach them.
  #
  # TWO MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed and each measured (the `head -200` that
  # used to be a third is now half of the pinned ORDER below):
  #   - `git cat-file -e "${sha}^{commit}" || continue` is removable with the
  #     suite green: `update-ref` refuses a value it cannot resolve, so a
  #     nonexistent object creates no ref and increments nothing either way.
  #     It stays for the `^{commit}` PEEL rather than for the existence test —
  #     `update-ref` will happily point a ref outside refs/heads at a blob or a
  #     tree, and the attic is a record of COMMITS. No reflog can produce one,
  #     which is why no fixture can pin it.
  #   - `[[ -d "$workdir" ]] &&` is removable for the same reason: this helper
  #     is called only from the tail's `[[ -z "$resumed" ]]` arm, where Phase A
  #     has already proved the directory is $main's worktree. With it gone the
  #     read errors into /dev/null and the tip still comes through. It stays so
  #     the helper is safe to call from anywhere, which is how the resume path
  #     would want to call it if it ever did.
  #
  # THE CAP IS ON THE REFLOG, AND THE TIP IS APPENDED AFTER IT. It used to be
  # applied to `{ reflog; tip } | sort -u`, i.e. to the UNION, which made the tip
  # just another line in hex-sort order — measured end to end on a workspace
  # with 231 commits: 237 distinct reflog shas, the tip at rank 201 of them,
  # `tip pinned in attic? 0`, 200 refs written and not one of them the branch's
  # own commit. That commit is the ONE a squash merge leaves outside main, so it
  # is precisely the commit the attic exists for; after `origin/<branch>` is
  # auto-deleted on merge and the next `fetch --prune` runs, it is dangling with
  # the two-week fuse §5.5(a) exists to remove. Spec §5.5(a) reads "deduped,
  # capped at 200, PLUS `tip`", and now so does this: `sort -u | head -200` over
  # the reflog, then the tip, then a second `sort -u` — which dedupes rather
  # than caps, so a tip already among the 200 is not pinned twice and does not
  # inflate the count the sheet prints. At most 201 refs.
  #
  # What IS pinned, by `pins the tip when the reflog cannot be read, and makes
  # no ref from a non-sha`: the shape check (without it `refs/heads/main` is a
  # string `update-ref` resolves, and the attic grows a ref made out of a NAME)
  # and the unconditional tip append; by `pins the tip even past the 200-ref
  # cap`: the ORDER of the cap and the append; and by `pins the amended-away
  # commit` the first `sort -u`, because `%H` prints one line per reflog ENTRY
  # and the count that reaches the sheet has to be the number of refs that
  # exist. The cap's own MAGNITUDE — 200 rather than 20 — remains unpinned:
  # bounding it costs a fixture with 200 resolvable commits in it, and the
  # fixture above pins where the cap is applied without paying for how big it is.
  local main="$1" id="$2" workdir="$3" tip="$4" sha n=0
  while read -r sha; do
    [[ "$sha" =~ ^[0-9a-f]{40}$ ]] || continue
    git -C "$main" cat-file -e "${sha}^{commit}" 2>/dev/null || continue
    git -C "$main" update-ref "refs/ccrc/attic/$id/$sha" "$sha" 2>/dev/null && n=$(( n + 1 ))
  done < <( { [[ -d "$workdir" ]] && git -C "$workdir" reflog show --all --format=%H 2>/dev/null | sort -u | head -200; \
              printf '%s\n' "$tip"; } | sort -u )
  echo "$n"
}

_ws_clip_manifest() {   # id -> JSON array of {name,bytes}, and nothing else;
                        # NOTHING at all, and non-zero, when the directory
                        # exists and could not be enumerated
  # Pre-merge fix round, finding A — the EIGHTH instance of the
  # measurement-forgery class (deviation 10), and the one where two sides
  # AGREE on a value NEITHER measured: the wire manifest, the fingerprint
  # (`clipsDigest`, `_ws_reap_eval`) and the tombstone (`_ws_tombstone`) all
  # come from this ONE producer, so all three inherited its blind spot.
  #
  # The old enumeration was `for f in "$dir"/*` filtered by `[[ -f "$f" ]]` —
  # depth-1, non-dot, REGULAR FILES ONLY. Step (h) (`_ws_reap_tail`, ccd:4107)
  # is `rm -rf "$cdir"` on the WHOLE DIRECTORY. Measured: with `clip-a.png`
  # (10 B) present, an audit issued a token; adding `.hidden-secret.png`
  # (4 KB) and `archive/{prod.env,big.bin}` (1 MB) left the manifest AND the
  # token byte-identical; the STALE token from before those files existed
  # still validated a reap; the whole directory — dotfile, subdirectory and
  # all — was destroyed; the tombstone still names only `clip-a.png`. Fixed
  # by enumerating with `find -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1`, which unlike a bare
  # glob does not skip dotfiles, and by sizing a DIRECTORY entry with `du -sb`
  # (recursive) rather than `stat`, whose size is the inode's, not its
  # contents' — so the enumeration now matches exactly what (h) destroys.
  # `sort -z` keeps the manifest deterministic across two calls over an
  # unchanged directory, the property the digest and the CAS both depend on
  # — `find`'s own enumeration order is a filesystem implementation detail,
  # not a promise, even though it happens to be stable on THIS box for an
  # unmodified small directory (measured: the token is identical across two
  # back-to-back audits with or without this line, which is why it is a
  # disclosed survivor rather than a pinned one — the fixture that would
  # distinguish them needs a filesystem, or a directory history, this suite
  # cannot arrange). Kept anyway, the same judgement the attic's 200-ref cap
  # gets for the identical reason (CONSTRAINTS.md): a property this design
  # depends on must not rest on an implementation detail merely because
  # today's filesystem happens to make it hold.
  #
  # `[[ -d "$dir" ]] || { echo "[]"; … }` used to be a disclosed mutation
  # survivor: `find` on a nonexistent path printed nothing on stdout and a
  # diagnostic on stderr (discarded), so the loop read no records either way,
  # and the guard stood only to make the empty answer a DECISION rather than an
  # accident of `find`'s own exit code — "which this function does not
  # otherwise check", as this comment said in as many words until the
  # sixteenth forgery below made it check. It is a survivor no longer: with the
  # enumeration status honoured, deleting this line turns a MISSING clips
  # directory (no clips were ever pasted — a real, common, empty state) into
  # the refusal an UNREADABLE one gets, and `refuses to enumerate a clips
  # directory it cannot read, and still answers [] for one that is simply
  # absent` fails on the second half.
  #
  # ── THE THIRTEENTH FORGERY (cross-lane seam round) ────────────────────────
  # Both size rungs below used to answer a FAILED READ with `0`, and the older
  # comment above defended it: the entry's NAME lands either way, and the name
  # is what the digest and the token are built from. That argument is sound
  # about the name and says nothing about the number — and the number is not
  # private to this function. `cmd_ws_audit` puts this manifest on the wire
  # (ccd:3848) and `ReapSheet.tsx:303` SUMS `clips[].bytes` into a stated total
  # printed on the same sheet, above the same Remove button, as the worktree
  # figure `_ws_gc_bytes` already refuses to fabricate. So the fabricated 0 was
  # never merely a display understatement; it was a measurement claim made to a
  # human about to authorise a `rm -rf`.
  #
  # Re-measured here, GNU coreutils 9.4, before the fix: `du -sb` over a tree
  # holding 14000 bytes with one `chmod 000` subdirectory exits **1**, writes
  # `du: cannot read directory …: Permission denied` to stderr, and STILL
  # PRINTS `5000`. A numeric-looking answer is therefore not evidence of a
  # completed measurement, and `[[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]` on its own accepted it.
  # Both rungs now check the exit status FIRST (`pipefail` is set file-wide, so
  # the pipeline's status is du's) and keep the numeric test as the second
  # conjunct for a `du` that succeeds and prints something else.
  #
  # WHY `null` PER ENTRY AND NOT THIS FUNCTION'S OWN REFUSAL, which is what
  # `_ws_collect_ignored` (ccd:3167) got for the same class one round earlier:
  #   - This function has no refusal channel to use. Its two other consumers
  #     are the fingerprint (`clipsDigest`, ccd:3700) and `_ws_tombstone` /
  #     `_ws_tombstone_reclip` — and the tombstone is the record that OUTLIVES
  #     the workspace. A refusal there is either ignored or turns one
  #     unreadable clip into a half-finished reap with the worktree already
  #     gone. `_ws_collect_ignored` could refuse because every one of ITS
  #     callers had somewhere to put the refusal before anything was destroyed.
  #   - A manifest has MANY entries and they are independent. One unreadable
  #     clip is a reason not to state that clip's size; it is not a reason to
  #     refuse to describe the other nine, or to withhold the whole sheet.
  #   - The name still lands, which is the half of the old comment that was
  #     right: the consent boundary and the digest keep working unchanged.
  # `null` is a fact ("nobody measured this"), not a value, and it is
  # deterministic for a given readability state, so `clipsDigest` still
  # discriminates and still refuses `state-changed` on a real change.
  # `shared/api.ts:342` types `clips[].bytes` as `number | null` so that a
  # consumer CANNOT go back to fabricating one to satisfy the compiler, and
  # `ReapSheet.tsx`'s `clipsSizeText` states what was measured and discloses
  # the rest ("1 MB + 1 unmeasured") rather than folding null into the sum.
  #
  # The `null` is also what keeps the old comment's other worry answered: `b`
  # is now always either digits or the four characters `null`, so the record is
  # valid JSON on every path — `"bytes":` with nothing after it, the document
  # that does not parse, is unreachable from either rung.
  #
  # ── THE SIXTEENTH FORGERY (final-round confirmation-surface review) ───────
  # The thirteenth fix above closed the SIZE of an entry and left the LIST
  # itself. A clips directory that EXISTS and cannot be ENUMERATED answered
  # `[]` at exit 0 — a claim of emptiness nobody established — and `[]` is not
  # a degraded answer on this surface, it is the most reassuring one:
  # `ReapSheet.tsx` renders it as **clips: none**.
  #
  # REPRODUCED before the fix, `chmod 000` on `~/.cc-clips/<id>` under an
  # isolated $HOME holding a 4 KB paste and a 1 MB subdirectory:
  # `find` exits 1 printing nothing, this function echoed `[]` at rc 0, and
  # `cmd_ws_audit` answered `"clips":[],…"verdict":"reapable"` WITH A TOKEN.
  # The fingerprint is no defence and could not be: `clipsDigest` is this same
  # `[]`, so ws-reap recomputes the identical digest, `--expect` matches, and
  # step (h) `rm -rf`s a directory whose contents were never listed to the
  # human — two sides agreeing on a value neither measured, which is the
  # eighth forgery's shape (above) reached through a different door.
  #
  # `find`'S EXIT STATUS CANNOT BE THE WHOLE TEST, and this is the measurement
  # that decides the shape of the guard. GNU findutils 4.9.0, same box:
  #   - mode 000 (no `r`): `opendir` fails. rc 1, stdout EMPTY. Nothing was
  #     enumerated — the case above.
  #   - mode 400 (`r`, no `x`): `readdir` succeeds and every per-entry `stat`
  #     fails. rc **1**, and stdout carries the COMPLETE list of names
  #     (measured: `plain.png` and `sub` both printed, with a single
  #     `Permission denied` for `sub` on stderr).
  # Identical status, opposite facts. So the guard is `rc != 0 AND no records`,
  # not `rc != 0`: the mode-400 shape is a complete list of names whose sizes
  # are unmeasured, which is exactly what the per-entry `null` rungs below are
  # for and what `a clip whose size stat cannot take is null, not 0 bytes`
  # already pins. Refusing it would delete a pinned honest answer to close a
  # different hole.
  #
  # A SCRATCH FILE, not the old process substitution, because a pipeline's
  # status is not visible through `< <(…)` at all — the same two-statement rule
  # Phase B and `cmd_ws_audit`'s dirty read both state, and NULs survive a file
  # exactly as they survive a pipe. `pipefail` is set file-wide, so `lrc` is
  # `find`'s (or `sort`'s).
  #
  # AND THE REFUSAL PRINTS NOTHING. `[]` plus a non-zero status was the obvious
  # shape and is the wrong one: it leaves the forgery fully formed on stdout
  # for any consumer that does not check the status — which is precisely how
  # this instance existed, since two of the three consumers could not check a
  # status they were never given (`… | sha256sum` reports the pipe's, and a
  # `$(…)` inside a printf ARGUMENT LIST swallows it). Empty stdout is
  # `cmd_ws_audit`'s own rule for its `_json_str` probe, stated there: when the
  # honest answer cannot be produced, "print NOTHING and exit non-zero". A
  # status-blind consumer then writes `"clips":` and fails to parse — loudly
  # wrong instead of quietly reassuring — and all three consumers here DO check
  # it: the audit refuses `clips-unreadable` and withholds the token, the
  # fingerprint refuses the same rather than hash an emptiness nobody read, and
  # the tombstone records `null` (see `_ws_reap_tail`), because a record that
  # outlives the workspace must not say "none" about a directory nobody opened.
  #
  # RESIDUAL, disclosed rather than papered over: a `readdir` that dies PART
  # WAY through a large directory (EIO, a network filesystem) exits non-zero
  # with a TRUNCATED list, which this guard accepts as the mode-400 shape and
  # publishes as complete. Separating those two needs a second, independent
  # enumeration to disagree with — two reads of the same directory microseconds
  # apart, which is a race of its own — and no fixture on this box can produce
  # a partial `readdir` to pin either behaviour. What is closed here is the
  # whole-directory case, which is reproducible with a `chmod`.
  # TWO `local` STATEMENTS, not one. `local id="$1" dir="$HOME/.cc-clips/$id"`
  # expands EVERY word before performing ANY assignment, so under `set -u` the
  # `$id` in the second word is unbound and the function dies
  # `id: unbound variable` — unless the CALLER happens to have a `local id`,
  # which by dynamic scoping is exactly what all three call sites have today.
  # So the bug was invisible and the function was untestable in isolation at
  # the same time, for the same reason. Disclosed by the ccd lane as residual
  # #3 and left as latent; closed here because the unmeasured-clip fixtures
  # below call this function directly, and a producer that can only be
  # exercised through a 30-second destructive end-to-end reap is a producer
  # whose failure rungs do not get pinned.
  local id="$1"
  local dir="$HOME/.cc-clips/$id" f name b brc out="[" first=1 lf lrc
  [[ -d "$dir" ]] || { echo "[]"; return 0; }
  lf=$(_plat_mktemp) || return 1
  find "$dir" -mindepth 1 -maxdepth 1 -print0 2>/dev/null | sort -z >"$lf"; lrc=$?
  # `-s` IS THE SECOND CONJUNCT, and it is a measurement of this read rather
  # than a guess about why it failed: a non-empty file means `readdir` handed
  # back at least one name, which no unenumerable directory can do, and an
  # EMPTY one at rc 0 is a directory that is genuinely empty.
  { (( lrc == 0 )) || [[ -s "$lf" ]]; } || { rm -f "$lf"; return 1; }
  while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
    name="$(basename "$f")"
    if [[ -d "$f" ]]; then
      # A directory's own size is the directory ENTRY's — `du -sb`, the same
      # recursive read `_ws_gc_bytes` uses, is what makes this figure the
      # bytes `rm -rf` actually reaches rather than one inode.
      #
      # `rc` then numeric, in that order and for that reason: the partial
      # total measured above is numeric AND wrong, so the numeric test cannot
      # be the outer guard. No stderr conjunct, unlike `_ws_gc_bytes` — that
      # needs a scratch file, and this is a LOOP, so it would take a `mktemp`
      # per clip and add a failure mode (mktemp failing mid-manifest) for a
      # conjunct `_ws_gc_bytes`'s own disclosure says never fires first. Same
      # rc+numeric shape `_ws_collect_ignored` took for the same class.
      b=$(_plat_bytes "$f" 2>/dev/null); brc=$?
      { (( brc == 0 )) && [[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; } || b=null
      name="$name/"
    else
      # Reachable only if the entry disappears between the `find` and the
      # `stat`, or its directory becomes unreadable in the same window — no
      # fixture can arrange either, which is why this rung was `|| echo 0` for
      # so long. A race is still a failed read: it answers `null` like its
      # sibling rather than claiming an empty file was deleted.
      b=$(_plat_size "$f" 2>/dev/null); brc=$?
      { (( brc == 0 )) && [[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; } || b=null
    fi
    (( first )) || out+=","; first=0
    out+="{\"name\":$(_json_str "$name"),\"bytes\":$b}"
  done < "$lf"
  rm -f "$lf"
  echo "$out]"
}

_reap_paths_json() {   # -> REAP_SENSITIVE as a JSON array of strings
  # Shared by BOTH refusal sites in cmd_ws_reap (the evaluated one and the
  # resume one), so the two cannot describe the same finding differently — and
  # both sites are now pinned, one per fixture, because a shared helper with one
  # caller under test is a shared helper by intention only.
  #
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: `[[ -n "$p" ]] || continue` is removable with
  # the suite green, exactly as its two siblings in `_ws_collect_ignored` and
  # `cmd_ws_audit` are. REAP_SENSITIVE is filled by the collector, which already
  # refuses an empty path, so no fixture can put one here. Kept as defence in
  # depth against a future producer, which is the same reason given there.
  local out="[" first=1 p
  for p in "${REAP_SENSITIVE[@]-}"; do
    [[ -n "$p" ]] || continue
    (( first )) || out+=","; first=0
    out+="$(_json_str "$p")"
  done
  printf '%s]\n' "$out"
}

_ws_tombstone() {   # id clipsjson -> writes .reaped/<id>.json, echoes its path
  local id="$1" clips="$2" dir="$REG/.reaped" f="$REG/.reaped/$1.json"
  mkdir -p "$dir"
  # `sensitive\tbytes\tpath`, NUL-terminated, read exactly as cmd_ws_audit reads
  # it (Task 5, deviation 18): the collector's `git status … -z` leaves a TAB or
  # a NEWLINE in a path intact, so the two fixed-shape fields come off the FRONT
  # and the path is everything after the second tab. A `read -r p b s` on a
  # path-first record shifts every field, and this record is what the TOMBSTONE
  # says was destroyed — the one document that outlives the workspace. The field
  # ORDER is pinned by `records in the tombstone the ignored manifest it
  # destroyed, field for field`, whose ignored entry is named `a<TAB>b.log`;
  # before that fixture existed every other one here had an EMPTY ignored set,
  # so this whole loop was unreached and the order was an argument rather than a
  # measurement.
  #
  # SEVEN MUTATION SURVIVORS in this function, disclosed together because six of
  # them are one reason — the tombstone is written on the EVALUATED path only,
  # after Phase A proved the registry complete and Phase C proved the PR, so the
  # states these lines are about cannot occur where they run:
  #   - `if (( ${#REAP_IGNORED[@]} ))` — on bash 4.4+ an empty array under
  #     `set -u` expands to nothing and the loop reads one empty record and
  #     skips it. The guard is what stops the empty case depending on that.
  #   - `[[ -n "$p" ]] || continue` and `[[ -n "$aref" ]] || continue` — neither
  #     producer can emit an empty record (gate finding N8's class, and the same
  #     answer: the next statement dereferences the value into a document a
  #     human reads filenames out of).
  #   - `[[ -d "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)" ]] &&` on the reflog — the workdir
  #     exists whenever (b) runs; it stays so an empty `reflog` is a decision.
  #   - `| head -200` on the reflog — a cap, unpinnable for the reason
  #     `_ws_attic_pin`'s MAGNITUDE is (where the cap is APPLIED is pinned there
  #     now, by `pins the tip even past the 200-ref cap`; how big it is still
  #     costs a fixture with 200 resolvable commits in it, and this dump is not
  #     read by any guard at all).
  #   - the `REAP_PRNUM` numeric test — no longer a survivor since the
  #     contained rung landed: a fresh reap can now reach this line with an
  #     EMPTY `REAP_PRNUM` (never pushed, nothing unique, no PR bound), and the
  #     contained end-to-end test pins the null it prints. The sibling in
  #     `_ws_reap_tail`'s printf was always reachable (the resume path prints
  #     an empty one) and is pinned there.
  # The seventh is `|| transcript=""`, which cannot change anything at all: a
  # bare `VAR=$(cmd)` already assigns "" when cmd fails. It is written because
  # `cmd_ws_audit` (ccd:3065) writes it, and these two records name the same
  # transcript — they are kept identical so they cannot drift.
  local ign_json="[" first=1 rec rest p b s
  if (( ${#REAP_IGNORED[@]} )); then
    while IFS= read -r -d '' rec; do
      s="${rec%%$'\t'*}"; rest="${rec#*$'\t'}"
      b="${rest%%$'\t'*}"; p="${rest#*$'\t'}"
      [[ -n "$p" ]] || continue
      (( first )) || ign_json+=","; first=0
      ign_json+="{\"path\":$(_json_str "$p"),\"bytes\":$b,\"sensitive\":$( ((s)) && echo true || echo false)}"
    done < <(printf '%s\0' "${REAP_IGNORED[@]}")
  fi
  ign_json+="]"
  # THE ATTIC REF NAMES, not the count of them — spec §5.5(b) lists "the attic
  # ref names" among what this document contains, and (j) is where the count
  # goes, on the verb's stdout. They are different facts and both are needed:
  # `ccd ws-attic --drop <id>` releases the refs, and after that this document
  # is the only record of which commits were pinned — which is the whole point
  # of a tombstone, since by then the branch, the reflog and the registry are
  # all gone. Read back from git rather than passed in, so the list has ONE
  # producer and cannot disagree with itself; `_ws_attic_pin` has already
  # created them by the time (b) runs, and its own answer is "how many I
  # created", which is not the same question.
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$(_reg_get "$id" project)" attic_json="[" afirst=1 aref
  while IFS= read -r aref; do
    [[ -n "$aref" ]] || continue
    (( afirst )) || attic_json+=","; afirst=0
    attic_json+="$(_json_str "$aref")"
  done < <(git -C "$main" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' "refs/ccrc/attic/$id/" 2>/dev/null)
  attic_json+="]"
  # THE CONSENTED CHILD SET, RECORDED — Task 8. One string per
  # `REAP_CHILDLINES` line (`path<TAB>branch<TAB>head<TAB>dirty`, the exact
  # text the descent in `_ws_reap_eval` built and the fingerprint's
  # fourteenth input hashed), `_json_str`-encoded so the tab survives the
  # round trip. `[]` when the workspace had no registered children — a
  # MEASUREMENT, taken at eval, never `null`: this document is what a
  # resume reads back as "what was consented", and an unmeasured children
  # set is not the same fact as a measured empty one (the same distinction
  # `clips` draws elsewhere in this function). `_ws_tombstone` runs only on
  # the FRESH path (`_ws_reap_tail`'s `if [[ -z "$resumed" ]]` arm), in the
  # same process `_ws_reap_eval` just populated `REAP_CHILDLINES` in, so
  # there is no capture boundary to lose the trailing entries across.
  local children_json="[" cfirst=1 cchild
  while IFS= read -r cchild; do
    [[ -n "$cchild" ]] || continue
    (( cfirst )) || children_json+=","; cfirst=0
    children_json+="$(_json_str "$cchild")"
  done <<< "$REAP_CHILDLINES"$'\n'
  children_json+="]"
  # THE TWO NAMES, READ THROUGH A DEFAULT AND A FALLBACK, and both halves are
  # deliberate. `${REAP_BRANCH-}` because this function is also called directly
  # — `ccd-prhistory.test.ts` drives the WRITER, seeding only the REAP_* globals
  # the document's field list needs — and under `set -u` a bare read there kills
  # the command substitution, which leaves `"branch":,` in a printf ARGUMENT
  # LIST: the exact swallowed-status shape `cmd_ws_audit`'s `_json_str` probe
  # exists to prevent, and a tombstone that does not parse is a tombstone that
  # cannot be read back by the resume path. The registry fallback because an
  # EMPTY name is not a fact this document may state: it is the one record that
  # outlives the workspace, and "the branch this cleanup was about" has to name
  # something. On every real path `_ws_reap_eval` has already set both (and
  # `_ws_reap_reset` at least emptied them), so the fallback is reachable only
  # from a caller that skipped the eval — where the registry IS what such a
  # caller means.
  local tbranch="${REAP_BRANCH-}" tregbranch="${REAP_REGBRANCH-}"
  [[ -n "$tbranch" ]] || tbranch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  [[ -n "$tregbranch" ]] || tregbranch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  local reflog="" transcript
  [[ -d "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)" ]] && reflog=$(git -C "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)" reflog show --all --format='%H %gs' 2>/dev/null | head -200)
  transcript=$(_transcript_path "$id") || transcript=""
  {
    # `branch` IS THE BRANCH THIS CLEANUP IS ABOUT — `REAP_BRANCH`, i.e. git's
    # worktree record — not the registry field it used to read. Under drift the
    # two differ, and this document is the only thing that outlives the
    # workspace: recording the registry's name here would name a branch that
    # still exists while saying nothing about the one that was deleted, and the
    # resume path (which reads its branch back out of this file) would then CAS
    # the wrong ref and wedge every later attempt on `branch-moved`.
    # `registryBranch` records the witness beside it — the name this cleanup
    # LEFT ALONE — so the pair can never be reconstructed as one fact.
    printf '{"id":%s,"project":%s,"workdir":%s,"branch":%s,"registryBranch":%s,"base":%s,"tip":%s,' \
      "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$(_reg_get "$id" project)")" \
      "$(_json_str "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)")" "$(_json_str "$tbranch")" \
      "$(_json_str "$tregbranch")" \
      "$(_json_str "$(_reg_get "$id" base)")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_TIP")"
    printf '"uuid":%s,"wrapper":%s,"mergeCommit":%s,"proof":%s,"pr":%s,"prUrl":%s,' \
      "$(_json_str "$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)")" "$(_json_str "$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)")" \
      "$(_json_str "$REAP_MERGE")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_PROOF")" \
      "$( [[ "$REAP_PRNUM" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "$REAP_PRNUM" || echo null)" "$(_json_str "$REAP_PRURL")"
    printf '"ignored":%s,"clips":%s,"transcript":%s,"attic":%s,"reflog":%s,"children":%s,"reapedAt":%s}\n' \
      "$ign_json" "$clips" "$(_json_str "$transcript")" "$attic_json" "$(_json_str "$reflog")" \
      "$children_json" "$(date +%s)"
  } > "$f"
  echo "$f"
}

_ws_tombstone_reclip() {   # id clipsjson -> rewrites an EXISTING tombstone's
                            # `clips` field in place; non-zero if it could not.
  # Pre-merge fix round, finding E, same underlying gap as finding A: the
  # resume path re-proves Phase B and Phase D1 in full but never re-reads
  # `_ws_clip_manifest` — `tomb` on the resume branch is just the EXISTING
  # file's path (ccd:4053) — so a clip pasted between the original consent
  # and the resume is `rm -rf`'d at (h) and appears in NO record at all.
  # Measured: a 3 MB clip pasted after the tombstone was written was
  # destroyed on resume, and the tombstone still named only the first one.
  # The tokenless resume is disclosed and defensible (the tombstone IS the
  # consent record, ccd:3826-3847); the undisclosed half this closes is that
  # the record has to cover what THIS run destroys, not what the original
  # run saw.
  #
  # Only the `clips` field moves. `ignored`/`attic`/`reflog`/`pr`/`proof` etc.
  # keep whatever the ORIGINAL evaluated run measured — re-deriving them here
  # would need Phase C re-run (deliberately not, ccd:3843-3845) and Phase B's
  # full ignored-set walk (the resume only re-proves dirty/sensitive-ignored
  # as REFUSALS, ccd:3971-3982 — it never rebuilds `ignored[]` as a document).
  # `clips` is the one field this resume both re-measures AND is about to
  # destroy, so it is the one field this rewrites.
  #
  # Read-modify-write through python end to end, not a bash-side field
  # substitution: the existing document's every OTHER field has to survive
  # untouched, including ones this shell was never handed (`ignored`, `pr`,
  # `attic`, `reflog`). `separators=(",", ":")` matches `_json_str`'s own
  # compact style (no spaces) — without it a round-trip through python's
  # default `json.dump` changes every byte in the file even when `clips`
  # itself did not change, which would silently break
  # `_ws_reap_tail`'s own promise (ccd:3969-3975, the ORIGINAL wording this
  # finding narrows rather than removes) that an unrelated resume leaves the
  # journal alone.
  #
  # Failure is REFUSED, not swallowed — measured with a real permission
  # error (`chmod 444` on the tombstone file itself, which is what actually
  # blocks python's `open(…, "w")`; a directory-only chmod does not, since
  # overwriting an EXISTING file needs write on the FILE, not on the
  # directory that holds it). The caller turns a non-zero return here into
  # `tombstone-unwritable` before (h) runs — never destroy clips a document
  # could not be made to name truthfully.
  #
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: `[[ -s "$f" ]] || return 1` is removable
  # with the suite green. By the time either call site reaches this
  # function the tombstone is either the one `_ws_tombstone` just wrote at
  # (b) in THIS process, or the resume path's own `tombtip` read (ccd's
  # `_ws_reap_locked`) already proved a non-empty tombstone exists moments
  # earlier — no fixture can arrange this function running with the file
  # genuinely absent. Kept as the same "refuse rather than guess" instinct
  # every other guard in this file states for itself, not because a test
  # can currently force the branch.
  local id="$1" clips="$2" f="$REG/.reaped/$1.json"
  [[ -s "$f" ]] || return 1
  printf '%s' "$clips" | python3 -c '
import json, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
clips = json.loads(sys.stdin.read())
with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
    doc = json.load(fh)
doc["clips"] = clips
with open(path, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
    json.dump(doc, fh, separators=(",", ":"))
' "$f" 2>/dev/null
}

_ws_tomb_str() {   # tombstone-json-file field -> that STRING field on stdout,
                    # empty (rc 0) when the field is absent, null, or not a
                    # string; NON-ZERO only when the document could not be
                    # opened or parsed at all.
  #
  # PYTHON, AND THE `sed` IT REPLACES IS WHY. The resume path used
  # `sed -n 's/.*"tip"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*"\([0-9a-f]*\)".*/\1/p'` over a
  # one-line document, and `.*` is GREEDY: with two `"tip"` keys on the line it
  # returns the LAST one (measured — a hand-built document with a duplicate key
  # answered `2222`, not `1111`). That was survivable while the only other
  # fields were an oid and a path array, and it stops being survivable the
  # moment this record grows a second field whose NAME is a branch: a `"branch"`
  # pattern would match the last one on the line, and the value it returned
  # would be handed to `update-ref -d` at (g). JSON is not a regular language
  # and the one decoder in this file that already knows that is python's
  # (`_ws_tomb_children`, immediately below, which says the rest of the
  # argument in full: `cmd_ws_reap` makes python3 a hard precondition of every
  # reap path before either fork).
  #
  # ABSENT AND UNREADABLE ARE DIFFERENT ANSWERS, per the file's own rule about
  # not collapsing two conditions a caller handles differently. An absent field
  # is `""` at rc 0 — that is how a tombstone written by an older ccd, which
  # never recorded `registryBranch`, reads as "nothing to say" rather than as a
  # broken journal — while a document that will not parse is non-zero.
  #
  # WHAT EACH CALLER DOES WITH THAT NON-ZERO, stated per site rather than as a
  # blanket claim (an earlier draft of this comment said "every call site turns
  # that into a refusal", which was false of the third): `_ws_reap_locked` and
  # `_ws_reap_tail` are ABOUT TO DESTROY, so an unreadable journal refuses there
  # — the tip is the CAS old-value and an empty one degrades the delete into an
  # unconditional one. `_ws_reap_eval`'s resume-shaped arm is a READ, taken to
  # describe a cleanup that is already interrupted, and it falls back to the
  # registry's name: it destroys nothing, an older tombstone legitimately lacks
  # the field, and the reap that would follow refuses on the same unreadable
  # document one function later — so refusing the DESCRIPTION as well would
  # remove the operator's only view of a state they still have to resolve.
  local f="$1" field="$2"
  [[ -s "$f" ]] || return 1
  LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import json, sys
with open(sys.argv[1], "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
    doc = json.load(fh)
v = doc.get(sys.argv[2])
sys.stdout.write(v if isinstance(v, str) else "")
' "$f" "$field" 2>/dev/null
}

_ws_tomb_children() {   # tombstone-json-file -> one raw
                          # "path<TAB>branch<TAB>head<TAB>dirty" line per
                          # CONSENTED child on stdout, rc 0; empty output at
                          # rc 0 when the field is absent (an old tombstone,
                          # written before Task 8, reads as consented-empty)
                          # or the array itself is empty (a workspace with no
                          # registered children); NON-ZERO — a refusal at
                          # EVERY call site, never a silently-accepted
                          # consented-empty — when the document could not be
                          # opened or parsed at all.
  #
  # PYTHON, not a bash-regex decoder (fix round 1, finding 2 — the decoder
  # this replaced could not close two gaps at once). `_json_str`
  # (`json.dumps`, `ensure_ascii=True`) escapes any non-ASCII byte in a path
  # or branch name as `\uXXXX`; three bash string substitutions have no way
  # to decode that back to the original byte and would leave it literal.
  # Worse, the substitution ORDER was wrong for a path that itself contains
  # a literal backslash-t: `/a\test` encodes as `\\test` (an escaped
  # backslash, then plain text) and unescaping `\t` BEFORE `\\` reads those
  # two characters as the FIELD DELIMITER, injecting a tab nobody consented
  # to — a decode bug that could desynchronise the very field split the
  # caller relies on. `cmd_ws_reap` already makes python3 a hard
  # precondition of every reap path (the `_json_str probe` gate at its own
  # entry, before either the fresh or the resume fork), so this adds no new
  # dependency — only a decoder that is actually closed, mirroring
  # `_ws_tombstone_reclip`'s own idiom: `[[ -s "$f" ]] || return 1` first
  # (a genuinely missing tombstone is impossible by the time either caller
  # reaches this function, since both run only after the SAME file's `tip`
  # was already read successfully — kept anyway for the same "refuse rather
  # than guess" instinct that function states for itself), then hand the
  # read-and-print entirely to python and return ITS status, never masking
  # a failure into empty-but-successful output.
  #
  # `LC_ALL=C.UTF-8` on the invocation, for the reason `_json_str`'s own
  # comment gives in full for the ENCODE side: this function's whole job is
  # printing RAW BYTES to stdout for a bash reader, and Python's text-mode
  # stdout encoding is locale-dependent unless pinned — the read side is
  # already locale-proof (`ensure_ascii=True` makes the FILE pure ASCII, so
  # `open(..., encoding="utf-8")` cannot vary by box), but nothing pins the
  # WRITE side without this.
  local f="$1"
  [[ -s "$f" ]] || return 1
  LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import json, sys
path = sys.argv[1]
with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
    doc = json.load(fh)
children = doc.get("children", [])
if children is None:
    children = []
for line in children:
    sys.stdout.write(line)
    sys.stdout.write("\n")
' "$f" 2>/dev/null
}

cmd_ws_reap() {   # ccd ws-reap --expect <token> --session <id>
  # The ONLY destructive verb. Every guard is re-evaluated HERE, on the box, at
  # the instant of deletion — the audit's verdict is not trusted, it is
  # recomputed. A refusal prints on stdout and exits 0: it is an answer.
  [[ $# -eq 4 && $1 == --expect && $3 == --session ]] \
    || die "usage: ccd ws-reap --expect <token> --session <id>"
  local token=$2 id=$4
  [[ $token =~ ^[0-9a-f]{64}$ ]]    || die "bad token"
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]]    || die "bad session id"

  # `_json_str`'s status, checked ONCE and up front — the same probe, for the
  # same reason, as `cmd_ws_audit` (ccd:10967) and `_ws_archive_manifest` (ccd:8133).
  # This is the THIRD caller that builds a whole record, and the record it
  # builds is the TOMBSTONE: the one document that outlives the workspace, and
  # the only thing left naming the transcript once step (i) has removed the
  # registry. Every `_json_str` here sits inside a printf ARGUMENT LIST, where a
  # failure is a swallowed status and an empty argument — `"path":,` — so
  # without this the resume path, which is the one route to the destructive tail
  # that never calls `_pr_py` and so is not already refused by python3 being
  # missing, would delete and then report it in a document that does not parse.
  # Before the lock, so a refusal leaves no lock behind for the next attempt to
  # trip over, and before `_ws_reap_eval`, so it costs no gh call and no fetch.
  _json_str probe >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || die "python3 unavailable — cannot quote the reap record safely"

  # THE HOLD RUNG, the same declaration `cmd_ws_rm` refuses on: a program has
  # said it is mid-flight in this workspace, so destroying it takes two
  # deliberate acts — release, then reap — never one.
  #
  # ANSWERED IN THIS FUNCTION'S IDIOM, not `cmd_ws_rm`'s. `cmd_ws_rm` is the
  # terminal path and `die`s; here a refusal prints JSON on stdout and exits 0
  # (this function's own header says so, and the flock contest below does
  # exactly this for `in-progress`, a state refusal `_ws_reap_eval` never
  # evaluates either). The reason is mechanical: `parseReap`
  # (server/src/wsaudit.ts) reads a `refused` TOKEN out of stdout and
  # classifies everything else — a `die`'s stderr included — as its generic
  # `error` refusal, whose sentence is the raw shell string: a bash command on
  # a phone screen. So `held` is a named token, and it carries its sentence in
  # that file's `SENTENCES` map, which `wsaudit.test.ts` holds exactly equal to
  # the set of tokens this source can emit — a set it computes by grepping THIS
  # FILE'S TEXT, comments included, so no comment here may spell a refusal
  # object out in full (measured: an earlier draft of this one did, and
  # invented a token called `error`).
  #
  # PLACED HERE, after the argv guards and before both the lock and
  # `_ws_reap_eval`: after, because it reads `$id` and a caller whose argv this
  # function would not accept must not receive a well-formed ANSWER; before,
  # because learning a workspace is off-limits must not cost a gh call, a
  # fetch, or a lock left behind. `-e` not `-f`: an unreadable-but-present hold
  # still refuses — the fail-shut polarity is that doubt reads as HELD.
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
    # D-2378: bounded exactly as `cmd_ws_rm`'s copy of this rung — `-f`+`-r`
    # checked before the open, so a FIFO/chardev at the hold path cannot
    # block this read, never touching the `-e` gate itself.
    local _hold_reason
    if [[ -f "$REG/$id.hold" && -r "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
      _hold_reason=$(cat "$REG/$id.hold" 2>/dev/null || echo '<unreadable — treat as held>')
    else
      _hold_reason='<unreadable — treat as held>'
    fi
    printf '{"refused":"held","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$_hold_reason — release first: ccd ws-release --session $id")"
    return 0
  fi

  # BEFORE either path, because ccd runs under `set -u` and `_ws_reap_tail`
  # reads REAP_PRNUM and REAP_PROOF unconditionally. On the resume path
  # `_ws_reap_eval` is never called, so without this they are UNBOUND, and the
  # final printf loses those two arguments: it emits {"pr":,"proof":} —
  # syntactically invalid JSON, which the caller reports as a parse error rather
  # than as the successful resume it actually was. (Reproduced in bash: two
  # `unbound variable` lines on stderr and a truncated object on stdout, exit 0.)
  # `_ws_reap_eval` calls this again on the normal path; it is idempotent.
  _ws_reap_reset

  # THE LOCK IS THE KERNEL'S, NOT A TRAP'S — and on this box that is the whole
  # argument. `mkdir` plus `trap … EXIT` was the shipped form, and a trap does
  # not run on SIGKILL: an OOM kill (this project's own speciality), a
  # `systemctl` hard stop, a power loss or a reboot each leave the directory
  # behind, every later `ws-reap` for that session answers `in-progress`, and
  # the RESUME PATH — the entire recovery mechanism — sits one refusal behind
  # it with no verb in ccd able to clear it. Measured, same fixture, only the
  # signal changed: SIGTERM left the lock free and the next run resumed;
  # SIGKILL left it HELD and every later run answered
  # `{"refused":"in-progress"}`, for ever. Step (i)'s `_reg_purge "$id"` does
  # not match this name — it purges `$REG/$id.<field>`, and the lock is
  # `$REG/.reap-$id.lock`, a different prefix entirely — and nothing else in
  # the file does either.
  #
  # `flock` attaches an advisory lock to an OPEN FILE DESCRIPTION, and the
  # kernel drops it when the last copy of that description closes — which every
  # form of death does, including the ones no handler can see. Nothing has to
  # run for the lock to come back. Verified on this box (util-linux 2.39.3,
  # `flock from util-linux 2.39.3`): a second acquirer is refused while the
  # holder lives, and a `kill -9` of the holder frees it for the next acquirer.
  #
  # THE DESCRIPTION IS INHERITED BY CHILDREN, and that is the RIGHT semantics
  # here rather than a leak. Measured: a child of the holder keeps the lock
  # after the holder is SIGKILLed, and it is released when the child exits. The
  # case the old comment worried about — "an orphaned `git worktree remove`
  # grandchild keeps running" after the shell died — is exactly the case in
  # which a second reap must NOT start, and under a trap it was exactly the
  # case in which one could. Nothing in the destructive sequence daemonises
  # (`git worktree remove`, `systemctl --user disable --now`,
  # `tmux kill-session`, `rm -rf` are all short-lived clients), so the interval
  # is bounded by the work itself.
  #
  # THE LOCK FILE IS NEVER UNLINKED. Unlinking it while holding it is how two
  # processes each end up holding "the lock" on two different inodes: the
  # second `open()` after the unlink creates a NEW file, whose lock nobody
  # contends. What is left behind is a 0-byte hidden file per session that has
  # ever been offered to `ws-reap` — inert (the registry is read through
  # `$REG/*.workspace` and `$REG/$id.<field>`, and a dotfile matches neither),
  # and the price of a lock whose identity is an inode rather than a promise.
  # The BREADCRUMB remains the recovery record; this only serialises runs.
  local lock="$REG/.reap-$id.lock" lfd rc=0
  # NOT a degraded mode. Without `flock` the serialisation is absent, not
  # weaker, so the destructive verb does not run — the same shape as the
  # `_json_str` probe above, and the same `die`. Pinned by `refuses to run the
  # destructive verb unserialised when flock is missing`, which shims `command`
  # rather than emptying PATH, because every stub in that file spells its own
  # passthrough `command git`.
  command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || _lc_refuse reap "$id" flock-unavailable \
         "flock (util-linux) is unavailable — refusing to run the destructive verb unserialised"
  exec {lfd}>>"$lock" \
    || _lc_refuse reap "$id" lock-unopenable "cannot open the reap lock at $lock"
  flock -n "$lfd" || {
    # Close OUR descriptor and touch nothing else: the winner's lock lives on
    # its own description and no path operation here can reach it. (Under the
    # mkdir form the loser's own trap could `rmdir` the winner's lock, which is
    # why that trap had to be armed after the mkdir rather than before it.)
    #
    # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed and measured: this close is removable with
    # the whole suite green, because a refused caller is a caller that is about
    # to exit and the kernel closes it then. It is not the same line as the one
    # after `_ws_reap_locked`, which IS pinned (`gives the lock back when the
    # FUNCTION returns`): that one is on the path where the shell goes on to do
    # other things. This one stays so that "every path gives the descriptor
    # back" is a property of the function rather than of its callers.
    #
    # THE MEASURED HOLE, CLOSED. Twelve `$REG/.reap-<id>.lock` files exist on
    # the live box and one holds a lock with no tombstone beside it: a reap was
    # attempted, refused, and recorded nowhere at all.
    #
    # `_lc_emit` directly, NOT `_lc_refuse`: this path answers JSON on stdout
    # at exit 0 and must keep doing exactly that (the PWA reads tokens, not
    # stderr), whereas `_lc_refuse` dies. The token is an ARGUMENT, so it
    # contributes nothing to `wsaudit.test.ts`'s scan; the literal one line
    # below still does, which is why that test stays green with no edit.
    _lc_emit reap refused "$id" "" verb ws-reap refusal in-progress \
      detail "another ccd process is already reaping $id and still holds the lock"
    exec {lfd}>&-
    printf '{"refused":"in-progress","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "another ccd process is already reaping $id and still holds the lock")"
    return 0
  }
  # EVERY return path gives the descriptor back, which is why the body is its
  # own function. `flock` treats two `open()`s of one path in one process as
  # two strangers, so a descriptor left open by one call refuses the NEXT call
  # in the same shell — and ccd is sourced (by `ccd` itself and by its tests),
  # so "the process exits eventually" is not the same statement as "the lock
  # went back".
  _ws_reap_locked "$token" "$id"; rc=$?
  exec {lfd}>&-
  return "$rc"
}

_ws_reap_locked() {   # token id — everything the reap lock serialises
  local token="$1" id="$2"
  local resumed; resumed=$(_reg_get "$id" reaping)
  if [[ -n "$resumed" ]]; then
    # Resume: the tombstone written at (b) is what distinguishes "the user
    # deleted the worktree" from "we did".
    #
    # `--expect` IS NOT CHECKED ON THIS PATH, and that is a decision rather than
    # an oversight, so it is written down here where the path forks. Everywhere
    # else the plan and the spec call the token THE consent boundary — "any
    # drift refuses `state-changed`" — and the argv still requires a shape-valid
    # 64-hex string, so `ws-reap` cannot be spelled without one. What it is not
    # is compared: there is nothing to compare it to. The token is
    # `_ws_reap_eval`'s, built from fourteen facts Phase B and Phase C measured,
    # and on a resume the worktree is usually already gone — Phase B has no tree
    # to read and Phase C would need a gh call an outage could withhold for
    # ever, which is the wedge §5.6 exists to end. `cmd_ws_audit` says the same
    # thing from the other side by refusing to ISSUE a token for
    # `reap-interrupted`: a token nobody can obtain cannot be the boundary.
    #
    # What stands in its place is stricter in the ways that still have facts
    # behind them: the TOMBSTONE is the consent record — it exists only because
    # a token-carrying reap was authorised, and its `tip` is re-validated
    # against the live ref per phase below — and Phase B and Phase D1, the two
    # whose facts can still change in the gap, are re-proven in full. What is
    # accepted is that a SECOND human, holding any 64-hex string, can finish a
    # cleanup a first human authorised. Measured: after a kill at (c),
    # `ws-reap --expect ffff…ff` completed the reap.
    # BOTH FACTS COME OUT OF THE JOURNAL, through the one decoder that can read
    # it (`_ws_tomb_str`, which records why the `sed` this replaces was a hazard
    # rather than a style). The tip is the CAS old-value at (g); the BRANCH is
    # the ref that CAS names, and reading it from the registry — which is what
    # this line did — is exactly wrong under drift: the tombstone was written
    # about git's branch, so a registry read here would re-validate the tip of a
    # DIFFERENT ref, refuse `branch-moved`, and wedge the resume permanently on
    # a workspace whose cleanup was already authorised.
    #
    # ABSENCE-PERMITS on the branch, and only on the branch: a tombstone written
    # before this field existed carries the registry's name in `branch` — which,
    # under the rung that then refused every drift, was equal to git's by
    # construction — so falling back to the registry reproduces exactly what
    # that older run would have deleted. The TIP has no fallback: it is the only
    # proof this journal belongs to this branch and its absence is a refusal.
    local tombtip branch
    tombtip=$(_ws_tomb_str "$REG/.reaped/$id.json" tip) || tombtip=""
    branch=$(_ws_tomb_str "$REG/.reaped/$id.json" branch) || branch=""
    [[ -n "$branch" ]] || branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
    # THE TIP IS REQUIRED ON EVERY PHASE. It is the CAS old-value at (g) and the
    # only proof this journal belongs to this branch, since Phase C is
    # deliberately not re-run here. Empty is the ABSENCE of that proof, never a
    # value: with the branch also gone the two empties compare equal, so the
    # emptiness test cannot be folded into the comparison below.
    if [[ -z "$tombtip" ]]; then
      printf '{"refused":"branch-moved","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "no tombstone tip for $id — refusing to finish a cleanup whose journal cannot be read")"
      return 0
    fi
    # AND THE COMPARISON IS PER PHASE, because §5.6's "re-validate … THEN
    # continue from the recorded phase" is a contradiction once the phase is
    # `clips`: (g) has ALREADY CAS-deleted the branch by then, so `live` is
    # empty by construction and an unconditional comparison reads "moved" for
    # the one state in which gone is correct — permanently. Measured before it
    # was per phase: a kill at (h) answered `branch-moved` on every later
    # attempt, the clips directory leaked, the registry row survived with no
    # worktree and no branch, and the detail said "nothing was deleted after
    # the worktree" while everything up to and including the branch had been.
    #
    # `clips` is the ONLY phase that skips it, and the default arm re-validates:
    # a breadcrumb holding anything ccd did not write is not a phase this can
    # reason about, so it gets the stricter reading, not the looser one. What
    # protects (g) in the skipped case is (g)'s own compare-and-swap — a branch
    # recreated at another sha after the delete fails that CAS and refuses
    # there.
    case "$resumed" in
      clips) : ;;
      *)
        local live; live=$(git -C "$PROJECTS_ROOT/$(_reg_get "$id" project)" rev-parse --verify "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null)
        if [[ "$live" != "$tombtip" ]]; then
          printf '{"refused":"branch-moved","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
            "$(_json_str "the branch moved while cleaning up — nothing was deleted after the worktree")"
          return 0
        fi ;;
    esac

    # ── Phase B, AGAIN, on the resume path ────────────────────────────
    # §5.3 is unconditional: "every guard below is evaluated inside ws-reap, on
    # the box, at the instant of deletion". §5.6 authorises resuming a tail; it
    # does not authorise skipping the guards on the way through it. And the
    # breadcrumb is written at (c), BEFORE `git worktree remove` at (f), so
    # `reaping=worktree` WITH the worktree still on disk is the routine outcome
    # of one SIGTERM inside that window — not an exotic case. Without this
    # block the next reap deletes that worktree unaudited, and a .env written
    # between the two runs is gone with it.
    #
    # Phase C is deliberately NOT re-run: mergedness was proven before the
    # breadcrumb existed and the tombstone records the proof, and re-requiring
    # a gh read would let an outage strand a half-reaped workspace forever.
    # What is re-proven here is Phase B and Phase D1 — the guards whose facts
    # can still have changed in the gap, and the only two the resume can cost
    # anything by skipping.
    local workdir; workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
    if [[ -d "$workdir" ]]; then
      # Both reads checked, for the reason spelled out at `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase
      # B — and here it matters more, because this path has no token to fall back
      # on: nothing downstream compares a fingerprint, so an unreadable tree that
      # counted 0 dirty files and an unreadable ignored set that produced an empty
      # REAP_SENSITIVE would take `git worktree remove` straight through both
      # guards. `local x=$(cmd)` returns local's status, so the read is its own
      # statement.
      # AND STDERR IS CAPTURED, exactly as `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase B captures
      # it (ccd:3013-3020) and for the same measured reason: a PARTIALLY
      # unreadable tree exits 0 with EMPTY stdout and `warning: could not open
      # directory 'sub/'` on stderr, so the count is 0, `dirty-tree` passes, and
      # the cleanup finishes over work it never saw. Two reads, one rule.
      #
      # TWO MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed and each measured whole-suite (44
      # files / 712 tests, exit 0): dropping the `&& [[ -z "$rerr" ]]` conjunct,
      # and restoring `2>/dev/null` so there is no `$rerr` to test. Both leave
      # the suite green, because `_ws_collect_ignored` two statements
      # below reads the same tree under the same rule and refuses
      # `tree-unreadable` first — measured with `chmod 000` on an untracked
      # directory holding uncommitted work: the resume DOES refuse and the work
      # DOES survive, but the refusal comes from the ignored read. This is
      # defence in depth, and it is written out because the ordering it relies
      # on is not a property anyone would preserve on purpose: reorder those two
      # statements, or relax the ignored read, and a partially unreadable tree
      # walks into `git worktree remove`.
      local rdirty rerrf rerr rrc
      rerrf=$(_plat_mktemp) || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not read the tree at $workdir — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      rdirty=$(git -C "$workdir" status --porcelain 2>"$rerrf"); rrc=$?
      rerr=$(cat "$rerrf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$rerrf"
      { (( rrc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$rerr" ]]; } || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not read the tree at $workdir — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      rdirty=$(printf '%s' "$rdirty" | grep -c . || true)
      if (( rdirty != 0 )); then
        printf '{"refused":"dirty-tree","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "$rdirty uncommitted or untracked file(s) appeared while the cleanup was interrupted")"
        return 0
      fi
      # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: the `${REAP_IGNREASON:+ …}` here is
      # removable with the whole suite green — the EVALUATED site's identical
      # interpolation is pinned (by the scan-budget and unreadable-directory
      # fixtures, which both refuse before any breadcrumb exists), and no
      # fixture reaches a failing scan on the RESUME path, which needs a
      # breadcrumb AND a surviving worktree AND an unreadable ignored directory
      # at once. It stays because the two sites describe the same failure and
      # the remedy is the whole reason the reason exists.
      _ws_collect_ignored "$workdir" || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not read the ignored set at $workdir${REAP_IGNREASON:+ — $REAP_IGNREASON} — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      if (( ${#REAP_SENSITIVE[@]} != 0 )); then
        printf '{"refused":"sensitive-ignored","detail":%s,"paths":%s}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "${REAP_SENSITIVE[*]}")" "$(_reap_paths_json)"
        return 0
      fi

      # THE CHILD SET, RE-DERIVED — Task 8's own re-proof, same LADDER
      # POSITION as `_ws_reap_eval`'s descent (last in Phase B: a
      # nested-checkout scan is a whole-tree `find`, the priciest read here,
      # so it runs only once the cheaper dirty-tree and ignored-scan guards
      # above have already passed) and for the identical reason nothing
      # above it can substitute: a registered child (and a stray beside one)
      # is gitignored out of the PARENT's own `git status --porcelain` by
      # fixture convention and by the descent's own comment, so neither the
      # `rdirty` read nor the ignored-scan two rungs up can see one appear,
      # vanish, or turn dirty in the gap a resume crosses.
      #
      # `rcmain` is read fresh rather than carried from the caller: nothing
      # upstream of the resume fork computed it, and `_ws_children` needs
      # it resolved (ccd:2988-3006) exactly as `_ws_reap_eval`'s own descent
      # does at its own copy of this read.
      local rcmain; rcmain="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$(_reg_get "$id" project)"
      local rcnested rcchildren rcstrays
      if ! rcnested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$workdir"); then
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not scan for nested checkouts under $workdir${_WS_NESTED_WHY:+ — $_WS_NESTED_WHY} — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      fi
      rcchildren=$(_ws_children "$rcmain" "$workdir") || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not enumerate registered worktrees under $workdir — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      # The SAME in-bash set difference `_ws_reap_eval`'s descent uses
      # (search: "An IN-BASH set difference, not `comm`") and for the
      # identical reason: a locale-sorted `comm` over two process
      # substitutions can desync on a non-ASCII path and manufacture a
      # false stray, or swallow a real one, with no visible failure either
      # way — the one rung this file allows no fail-open on.
      local -A _rc_childset=(); local _rcline _rcpath
      while IFS= read -r _rcline; do
        [[ -n "$_rcline" ]] || continue
        _rcpath="${_rcline%%$'\t'*}"
        _rc_childset["$_rcpath"]=1
      done <<< "$rcchildren"$'\n'
      rcstrays=""
      while IFS= read -r _rcline; do
        [[ -n "$_rcline" ]] || continue
        [[ -v _rc_childset["$_rcline"] ]] || rcstrays+="$_rcline"$'\n'
      done <<< "$rcnested"$'\n'
      rcstrays="${rcstrays%$'\n'}"

      # The CONSENTED set the tombstone recorded — `_ws_tomb_children` reads
      # `[]` and an absent field alike as "nothing", which is exactly right
      # here: an OLD tombstone (written before Task 8) never promised
      # anything about children, so every live one below reads as new. Its
      # FAILURE is a different fact from either — the document could not be
      # read at all — and fix round 1's finding 2 is exactly that a garbage
      # decode must never silently pass as "nothing was consented" (which
      # would UNDER-refuse a phase-`worktree` resume, where empty-live is
      # the PASS condition) so a parse failure is checked here and refused
      # on its own, before it can be mistaken for a measurement.
      local rcconsented; rcconsented=$(_ws_tomb_children "$REG/.reaped/$id.json") || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not read the consented child set from the tombstone for $id — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      local rcchanged=""
      if [[ "$resumed" == children ]]; then
        # Every live child must still BE the line that was consented,
        # matched on `path<TAB>branch<TAB>head` — never on the record's own
        # trailing `dirty` column, which is always the literal 0
        # `_ws_reap_eval` measured before it ever appended a line to
        # `REAP_CHILDLINES` (a STORED 0 proves nothing about NOW). So dirt
        # is re-read here, live, exactly as Phase B re-reads it for the
        # PARENT a few lines up.
        local -A _rc_consentset=(); local _rcconline _rcprefix
        while IFS= read -r _rcconline; do
          [[ -n "$_rcconline" ]] || continue
          _rcprefix="${_rcconline%$'\t'*}"
          _rc_consentset["$_rcprefix"]=1
        done <<< "$rcconsented"$'\n'
        while IFS= read -r _rcline; do
          [[ -n "$_rcline" ]] || continue
          if [[ -v _rc_consentset["$_rcline"] ]]; then
            local _rccpath="${_rcline%%$'\t'*}"
            # REGISTERED BUT ABSENT = SKIP, NOT tree-unreadable — fix round
            # 1, caveat 4. `_ws_children` lists a child straight out of
            # `git worktree list --porcelain`, which keeps reporting a
            # PRUNABLE record (directory gone, admin metadata still there)
            # long after the directory itself is gone — the exact state a
            # hand-deleted child, or a teardown loop killed between its own
            # `worktree remove` succeeding and the next step, leaves behind,
            # and the teardown loop's own skip-already-gone rung treats it
            # as normal, not as damage. `git -C "$_rccpath" status` on a
            # path that does not exist cannot succeed — it was refusing
            # `tree-unreadable` here, permanently, over a state ccd itself
            # produces routinely. There is nothing to read for dirt when
            # there is nothing on disk, so this reads as clean and moves on;
            # the CONSENT match above already proved this is the same child
            # that was promised, which is the fact this branch exists to
            # check.
            if [[ -d "$_rccpath" ]]; then
              local _rcerrf _rcerr _rcrc _rcdirt
              _rcerrf=$(_plat_mktemp) || {
                printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
                  "$(_json_str "could not read the child at $_rccpath — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
                return 0
              }
              _rcdirt=$(git -C "$_rccpath" status --porcelain 2>"$_rcerrf"); _rcrc=$?
              _rcerr=$(cat "$_rcerrf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$_rcerrf"
              { (( _rcrc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$_rcerr" ]]; } || {
                printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
                  "$(_json_str "could not read the child at $_rccpath — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
                return 0
              }
              [[ -z "$_rcdirt" ]] || rcchanged+="$_rccpath is dirty now; "
            fi
          else
            rcchanged+="$_rcline is not among the consented children; "
          fi
        done <<< "$rcchildren"$'\n'
        [[ -z "$rcstrays" ]] || rcchanged+="new stray checkout(s): ${rcstrays//$'\n'/, }; "
      else
        # worktree|branch|clips — and any breadcrumb this dispatch has never
        # written, which `_ws_reap_tail`'s own vocabulary check refuses
        # regardless: by any of these phases every consented child was
        # supposed to be torn down already, so the live set here must be
        # EMPTY, full stop.
        [[ -z "$rcchildren" ]] || rcchanged+="${rcchildren//$'\n'/, } still registered; "
        [[ -z "$rcstrays" ]] || rcchanged+="new stray checkout(s): ${rcstrays//$'\n'/, }; "
      fi
      if [[ -n "$rcchanged" ]]; then
        printf '{"refused":"state-changed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "the child checkouts changed while the cleanup was interrupted — ${rcchanged%; }")"
        return 0
      fi

      # Phase D1 too, and for the same reason: the breadcrumb is written at (c),
      # BEFORE _ws_unsupervise at (d), so a SIGTERM in that window leaves
      # `reaping=worktree` with the session STILL RUNNING. Without this the
      # resume yanks the worktree out from under a live turn OR a running Bash
      # tool command — `idle` is an allowlist in _ws_status, so `shell` refuses
      # here (deviation 6). The tree is clean and pushed, so no bytes are lost,
      # but §5.3 is unconditional and session-busy is one of its guards.
      local rst; rst=$(_ws_status "$id") || {
        printf '{"refused":"status-unknown","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "cannot read the wrapper status file — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
      if [[ "$rst" == busy ]]; then
        printf '{"refused":"session-busy","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "the session is working — a model turn or a Bash tool command — and the cleanup was interrupted before it was stopped")"
        return 0
      fi
    fi

    REAP_TIP="$tombtip"
    _ws_reap_tail "$id" "$resumed"
    return 0
  fi

  if ! _ws_reap_eval "$id"; then
    local paths="[]"
    [[ "$REAP_VERDICT" == sensitive-ignored ]] && paths=$(_reap_paths_json)
    # ONE EMIT FOR ALL 35 `_reap_refuse` TOKENS, at the single point where
    # `REAP_VERDICT` becomes JSON. `_ws_reap_eval` never prints — it sets
    # globals through `_reap_refuse` (ccd:7346) — so this is the only place the
    # verdict exists as a value, and journaling the 35 call sites individually
    # would be 35 chances to miss one.
    _lc_emit reap refused "$id" "" verb ws-reap refusal "$REAP_VERDICT" detail "$REAP_DETAIL"
    printf '{"refused":%s,"detail":%s,"paths":%s}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$REAP_VERDICT")" "$(_json_str "$REAP_DETAIL")" "$paths"
    return 0
  fi

  # D2 — the consent IS the fingerprint, and it is checked HERE, in ccd. A
  # server-side check fails open into deletion; this one cannot.
  if [[ "$token" != "$REAP_TOKEN" ]]; then
    printf '{"refused":"state-changed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "expected $REAP_TOKEN, was given $token — this workspace changed since the list you were shown")"
    return 0
  fi

  _ws_reap_tail "$id" ""
}

_ws_reap_tail() {   # id resumed-phase — the journalled destructive sequence
  local id="$1" resumed="$2" project workdir branch regbranch main attic clips tomb bytes
  project=$(_reg_get "$id" project); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  # THE BRANCH THIS TAIL DESTROYS, and it is not the registry's field any more.
  # Two origins, one per path, exactly as `childlines` has two below and for the
  # same reason:
  #   FRESH — `REAP_BRANCH`, THIS SAME PROCESS's `_ws_reap_eval`, which set it
  #     from git's worktree record moments ago. That is the branch every rung of
  #     Phase B and Phase C just measured, so it is the only branch a proof was
  #     taken about.
  #   RESUME — the tombstone, because the eval never ran on this path and the
  #     worktree is usually already gone, so git has no record left to ask. The
  #     journal is the consent record and it is what (b) wrote the branch into.
  # The registry is the fallback on both, and it is a fallback rather than a
  # source: it answers only for a tombstone written before this field existed
  # (where the two names were equal by construction anyway), and `set -u` makes
  # an unset `REAP_BRANCH` fatal rather than silent.
  if [[ -z "$resumed" ]]; then
    branch="$REAP_BRANCH"; regbranch="$REAP_REGBRANCH"
  else
    branch=$(_ws_tomb_str "$REG/.reaped/$id.json" branch) || branch=""
    # The witness travels with the fact it is a witness to. A resume that read
    # the registry for this would answer with whatever the row says NOW, which
    # is a different question from "what did the run this is finishing say it
    # was leaving alone" — and an older tombstone simply has no answer, which is
    # the empty string, not a guess.
    regbranch=$(_ws_tomb_str "$REG/.reaped/$id.json" registryBranch) || regbranch=""
  fi
  [[ -n "$branch" ]] || branch=$(_reg_get "$id" branch)
  # THE RECEIPT IS A MEASUREMENT TOO — final-round tests review F2. This was
  # `|| bytes=0`, the last of the three identical `_ws_gc_bytes "$workdir"`
  # lines to still fabricate (ccd:5375 archive manifest and ccd:8224 audit were
  # closed in earlier rounds), and it is the one printed AFTER the irreversible
  # delete: `{"reaped":…,"bytes":0}` states "this deletion reclaimed nothing"
  # on a `du` that never completed. On the RESUME path it is 0 EVERY time — the
  # worktree was already removed at (f) of the interrupted run, `_ws_gc_bytes`
  # answers `-` for a path that is not a readable directory, and the run that
  # in fact reclaimed gigabytes reports zero.
  #
  # `ReapResult.bytes` is `number | null | undefined` on the wire and nothing in
  # the PWA reads it today; that is the reason this was harmless, not a reason
  # to leave it. A receipt is the record a human reaches for when they want to
  # know what a delete cost, and it may not answer with a number nobody took.
  bytes=$(_ws_gc_bytes "$workdir"); [[ "$bytes" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || bytes=null

  # THE PAIR, opened after the last measurement and before every destructive
  # step in this function. `resumed` is on the line because an interrupted reap
  # that resumes writes a SECOND intent — two attempts, two records, which is
  # exactly what the 12 orphaned `.reap-<id>.lock` files could not say.
  local lctx; lctx=$(_lc_tx)
  _lc_intent reap "$id" "$lctx" verb ws-reap meas.project "$project" \
    meas.workdir "$workdir" meas.branch "$branch" meas.bytes "$bytes" \
    meas.resumed "$resumed"

  # A breadcrumb holding anything ccd did not write is not a phase this can
  # reason about, so it gets the stricter reading, not the looser one — the
  # same rule `_ws_reap_locked`'s tip re-validation already states for the same
  # uncertainty. The LOOSE reading used to live below, as
  # `[[ "$resumed" == "" || "$resumed" == worktree ]]`: an unrecognised value is
  # neither `""` nor `worktree`, so it fell to the ELSE of that single test —
  # `git worktree remove` silently skipped — and the run then fell straight
  # through to (g)'s branch delete, (h)'s clips delete and (i)'s registry
  # delete, and reported `{"reaped":...}`. Measured: a breadcrumb of
  # `reaping=sometime-else` with the worktree still on disk finished with
  # `wt-exists true`, the branch gone, and the registry gone — an orphaned
  # worktree, still registered with git, standing next to a deleted branch,
  # on a run that claimed success. Checked BEFORE any of (f)/(g)/(h)/(i).
  #
  # `children` joins the vocabulary here (Task 8): it is the phase written
  # BEFORE any registered child falls, exactly as `worktree` is written
  # before the PARENT falls, and it is closed the same way — a value this
  # dispatch has never written is still refused, never guessed at.
  case "$resumed" in
    ""|children|worktree|branch|clips) : ;;
    *)
      printf '{"refused":"reaping-phase-unknown","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "breadcrumb phase '$resumed' for $id is not one ccd ever writes — refusing to guess which steps already ran")"
      return 0 ;;
  esac

  # AND SO IS "IS THIS REF ANYONE ELSE'S?", for the same reason and in the same
  # place: §5.3 is unconditional — every guard is evaluated HERE, on the box, at
  # the instant of deletion — and this one guards the single most irreversible
  # line in the file. `_ws_reap_eval` asks it too (`branch-elsewhere`), but the
  # eval does not run on the RESUME path at all, and between a consent and the
  # tap somebody can `git switch --ignore-other-worktrees` onto this branch from
  # anywhere in the project.
  #
  # `<= 0`, not `== 0` COUNTING OUR OWN: on the fresh path our worktree still
  # holds the branch and is filtered out by path; on a resume it is already gone
  # and holds nothing. Both reduce to "no OTHER checkout has it".
  #
  # PLACED WITH THE ARCHIVED RE-READ, ahead of (a)/(b)/(c), so a refusal writes
  # NOTHING — no attic pin, no tombstone, no breadcrumb. A refusal here on a
  # resume leaves the cleanup exactly as interrupted, which is recoverable (move
  # the other checkout off the branch, re-run) in a way a deleted shared branch
  # is not.
  local tail_elsewhere tail_elsrc
  tail_elsewhere=$(_ws_branch_elsewhere "$main" "$branch" "$workdir"); tail_elsrc=$?
  (( tail_elsrc == 0 )) || {
    printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "could not enumerate $main's worktrees, so whether another checkout holds $branch was never asked — nothing was deleted")"
    return 0
  }
  [[ -z "$tail_elsewhere" ]] || {
    printf '{"refused":"branch-elsewhere","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$branch is also checked out at $tail_elsewhere — refusing to delete a branch another checkout is standing on; move it off $branch first, then re-run")"
    return 0
  }

  # THE ARCHIVED MARKER IS RE-READ HERE, immediately before the first step that
  # writes anything — final-round destructive review, finding F5. `archived` is
  # the ONE precondition of this whole verb that another ccd verb can withdraw:
  # `cmd_ws_restore` (ccd:5651) removes `$REG/$id.archived`, re-`_spawn`s the
  # session and re-`_ws_supervise`s it, turning the workspace back into an
  # ordinary live one. Nothing re-read the marker after `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase
  # A (ccd:7358), and it is not one of `_ws_fingerprint`'s fourteen inputs, so
  # the token cannot notice either.
  #
  # TWO REACHABLE SHAPES, both measured, and the sequential one is the one with
  # the teeth:
  #   - THE RESUME PATH, no race at all. A reap SIGKILLed at (c) leaves a
  #     breadcrumb the human can only clear by restoring; restore then brings
  #     the workspace back to life, and the breadcrumb OUTLIVES it. The next
  #     `ws-reap` takes `_ws_reap_locked`'s resume fork, which skips
  #     `_ws_reap_eval` entirely and therefore never asks the `not-archived`
  #     question at all. Measured before this line existed:
  #     `{"reaped":"demo-quiet-basin",…,"resumed":"worktree"}` — worktree
  #     removed, branch CAS-deleted, registry purged, on a LIVE restored
  #     workspace, hours after the tap that authorised it.
  #   - THE EVALUATED PATH, a real race. Phase A checks the marker, Phase C
  #     then spends up to 12 s in `gh pr list` and up to 60 s in `git fetch`; a
  #     restore inside that window is a lost update seconds to a minute wide,
  #     and D2 waves the token through because the token was computed in this
  #     same run. `cmd_ws_restore` now takes THIS verb's own lock, which closes
  #     the race at its source; this stays because a re-read costs one `stat`
  #     and covers the resume shape too, which no lock can.
  #
  # `not-archived` rather than a new token: the state IS not-archived, it is the
  # word `_ws_reap_eval` already uses for it (ccd:7359), and `wsaudit.ts`'s
  # SENTENCES already carries the sentence. Placed ahead of (a)/(b)/(c) so a
  # refusal writes NOTHING — no attic pin, no tombstone, no breadcrumb.
  #
  # THE DETAIL NAMES THE STATE, NOT A CULPRIT — final-round verification P4.
  # It used to assert "ws-restore brought it back while this cleanup was
  # pending", which is one cause of a missing marker and was, until `_reg_purge`
  # was reordered in the same round, not the only one: a kill inside the purge
  # unlinked `archived` before `reaping` and produced this refusal with a
  # diagnosis naming a verb that had never run. The observable fact is that the
  # marker is gone; who removed it is an inference, and an inference is not what
  # belongs in the one line an operator acts on.
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" ]] || {
    printf '{"refused":"not-archived","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
      "$(_json_str "$id is no longer archived — the archive marker is gone (ws-restore removes it), so this cleanup will not continue; archive it again to finish")"
    return 0
  }

  # `|| clips=null` ON BOTH ARMS — the sixteenth forgery's third consumer, and
  # the one that gets a RECORD rather than a refusal (`_ws_clip_manifest` sets
  # out why in full). The tombstone outlives the workspace: `[]` in it is the
  # permanent, unfalsifiable statement "this cleanup destroyed no pasted
  # images", written about a directory ccd could not open, and it is the only
  # document left once the branch, the reflog and the registry are gone. `null`
  # is the fact — nobody enumerated it — and `WsTombstone.clips` is typed
  # `… [] | null` to keep it representable.
  #
  # NOT a refusal, unlike the other two consumers, and the difference is the
  # same one `_ws_collect_ignored`'s own comment draws: this arm runs on the
  # RESUME, where the worktree may already be gone and a refusal converts one
  # unreadable directory into a workspace stranded half-deleted forever. The
  # record can still be made truthful, so it is made truthful and the cleanup
  # finishes. (The evaluated arm cannot reach it except through the same
  # microsecond window `cmd_ws_audit`'s verdict covers — Phase D2 recomputed
  # the digest moments earlier and would have refused `clips-unreadable` — and
  # it is written the same way there so the two arms cannot drift into
  # describing one directory two ways.)
  if [[ -z "$resumed" ]]; then
    attic=$(_ws_attic_pin "$main" "$id" "$workdir" "$REAP_TIP")            # (a)
    clips=$(_ws_clip_manifest "$id") || clips=null
    tomb=$(_ws_tombstone "$id" "$clips")                                   # (b)
    _reg_set "$id" reaping children                                        # (c)
  else
    # (d) AND (e) ARE NOT RE-RUN ON A RESUME, which is safe only because of
    # what stands in front of this path — moved down beside the block that
    # actually runs them (below the children phase now, not above it), but
    # the reason is unchanged: `_ws_reap_eval` refuses `not-archived` unless
    # `$REG/$id.archived` exists, and `cmd_ws_archive` (ccd:5206) is what
    # writes it — after `_ws_unsupervise` and after `tmux kill-session`. So by
    # the time any breadcrumb can exist the unit is disabled and the pane is
    # gone, and re-running either would be a second call to an idempotent
    # no-op. Measured: a resumed reap completes with `unsup=0`. What it does NOT
    # cover is something re-supervising the session between the archive and the
    # reap; the resume's own Phase D1 re-proof refuses `session-busy` if that
    # something is actually working, which is the part that could cost bytes.
    tomb="$REG/.reaped/$id.json"
    attic=$(git -C "$main" for-each-ref --format='%(refname)' "refs/ccrc/attic/$id/" 2>/dev/null | grep -c . || true)
    # Pre-merge fix round, finding E: re-measure clips NOW, on the resume,
    # not whatever `_ws_clip_manifest` answered at the ORIGINAL (b) — this
    # run's (h), a few lines below, `rm -rf`s whatever is on disk at THIS
    # instant, and a clip pasted in the gap must not be destroyed with no
    # record of it anywhere. Before (h), same as everywhere else in this
    # function guards run immediately before the step they guard.
    clips=$(_ws_clip_manifest "$id") || clips=null
    _ws_tombstone_reclip "$id" "$clips" || {
      printf '{"refused":"tombstone-unwritable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "could not update the tombstone's clips record for $id — refusing to finish the cleanup without an accurate record")"
      return 0
    }
  fi

  # (c2) THE CHILDREN PHASE — Task 8. Runs on the FRESH path, right after the
  # breadcrumb above first names it, and on a RESUME whose breadcrumb is
  # STILL `children` — finishing the SAME consented set a killed run left
  # partly torn down, never starting a new one. The two origins for
  # `childlines` are deliberate: fresh reads `REAP_CHILDLINES`, THIS SAME
  # PROCESS's own descent (`_ws_reap_eval` populated it moments earlier, in
  # the very call that is still running); resume reads the tombstone's
  # `children` array instead, because the resume path never calls
  # `_ws_reap_eval` at all and `REAP_CHILDLINES` stays the empty string
  # `_ws_reap_reset` gave it. `_ws_reap_locked`'s own re-derivation, just
  # before this function was ever called, has already proved that recorded
  # set still matches what is live — this loop trusts that and acts.
  if [[ "$resumed" == "" || "$resumed" == children ]]; then
    local childlines
    if [[ -z "$resumed" ]]; then
      childlines="$REAP_CHILDLINES"
    else
      childlines=$(_ws_tomb_children "$tomb") || {
        printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "could not read the consented child set from the tombstone for $id — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
        return 0
      }
    fi
    # INNERMOST FIRST — `awk`'s injected length key, sorted numerically
    # descending, `cut` drops the key back off. Order is not a nicety here:
    # measured in a scratch fixture, `git worktree remove` on a worktree
    # that still physically contains ANOTHER registered worktree does NOT
    # refuse. With the nested path gitignored — exactly as it must be for
    # the PARENT (and, one level down, for a CHILD with a grandchild of its
    # own) to stay clean at all — git treats the nested checkout as ordinary
    # ignored content and deletes the whole subtree outright, silently, past
    # even a `git worktree lock` on the nested one (a lock is checked only
    # when THAT path is itself the target, never when an ancestor directory
    # happens to contain it). So this order is not git enforcing anything
    # for us; it is the only order in which each child's OWN
    # `worktree remove` is what actually removes it.
    # FIX ROUND 1, FINDING 1a: the pipeline's own status, checked — it was
    # not, despite `set -o pipefail` (ccd:4) already making it MEAN
    # something. `awk`/`sort`/`cut` failing here is unmeasured but not
    # unimaginable (a scratch-space exhaustion mid-sort, say), and a failed
    # sort silently reads as an EMPTY `sorted`, which is indistinguishable
    # from "no consented children" — the loop below would then walk past
    # every one of them, unremoved, straight to the post-loop check. `LC_ALL=C`
    # on the sort itself — house rule consistency with every other
    # LC_ALL=C sort in this file (`_ws_nested_checkouts`, `_ws_children`):
    # an ambient locale must not change which child a numeric tie resolves
    # to first.
    local sorted
    sorted=$(printf '%s' "$childlines" | awk '{print length($1) "\t" $0}' | LC_ALL=C sort -rn | cut -f2-) || {
      printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "could not order the consented child set for $id — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
      return 0
    }
    local mainreal; mainreal=$(_ws_common_dir "$main") || mainreal=""
    # RESOLVED, not the raw registry string — `ccpath` (both origins: fresh
    # `REAP_CHILDLINES` and the tombstone that recorded it) is always a
    # `pwd -P` result (`_ws_children`'s own containment test resolves its
    # `$workdir` argument the identical way before comparing), and
    # `$workdir` itself is not guaranteed to already be one: this file's own
    # header notes `$HOME/projects -> /data/projects` is a SYMLINK in
    # production. Comparing a resolved path against an unresolved one is
    # the exact class of bug a symlinked ancestor manufactures elsewhere in
    # this file (search: "symlinked ancestor cannot make a registered child
    # misread"), and the fallback to the raw value on a failed resolve
    # keeps this a containment CHECK, never a silent pass.
    local workdirreal; workdirreal=$(cd "$workdir" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || workdirreal="$workdir"
    local cline crest crest2 ccpath ccbr cchead
    while IFS= read -r cline; do
      [[ -n "$cline" ]] || continue
      # Parameter expansion, NOT `IFS=$'\t' read`, for the identical measured
      # reason `_ws_reap_eval`'s own descent states in full (search: "IFS=
      # read -r childline"): tab is IFS whitespace even when it is the only
      # character in IFS, so a detached child's empty branch column would
      # silently shift every later field left under the IFS form. The
      # trailing `dirty` column is still not read — Phase B/D2 already
      # proved it 0 at consent time — but `cchead` now is, for the
      # mergeability probe below.
      ccpath="${cline%%$'\t'*}"
      crest="${cline#*$'\t'}"
      ccbr="${crest%%$'\t'*}"
      crest2="${crest#*$'\t'}"
      cchead="${crest2%%$'\t'*}"

      # CONTAINMENT, RE-DERIVED — fix round 1, caveat 8. On a resume,
      # `ccpath` is read off the TOMBSTONE, a file on disk, not measured
      # live off `$workdir` the way the fresh path's `REAP_CHILDLINES` was
      # — so this is the same "re-derive, never trust" instinct every other
      # rung on this ladder states for itself, applied to the one value
      # here that a resume takes from a RECORD rather than a live read.
      [[ "$ccpath" == "$workdirreal"/* ]] || {
        printf '{"refused":"worktree-remove-failed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "$ccpath is not under $workdir — refusing to act on a consented child line outside the workspace")"
        return 0
      }

      # THE EXACT PREDICATE `branch -d` APPLIES, PROVEN BEFORE ANYTHING IS
      # REMOVED — fix round 1, finding 3. Consent measured this child as 0
      # commits ahead of `origin/HEAD` (`_ws_reap_eval`'s own descent,
      # `cahead` against `$cbase=origin/HEAD`), but `branch -d` does not
      # check against `origin/HEAD` at all: these children carry no
      # upstream (`git worktree add -b` configures none), so `branch -d`'s
      # own "fully merged" rule falls back to `$main`'s CURRENT LOCAL HEAD.
      # Phase C's mandatory fetch — run once per reap, to prove the
      # PARENT's own merge — advances the remote-tracking `origin/HEAD`
      # without touching `$main`'s local branch at all, so a child proven
      # merged into an origin that has since moved ahead of `$main`'s local
      # checkout can fail this SAME check `branch -d` is about to make.
      # `merge-base --is-ancestor` is the plumbing `branch -d` itself
      # answers this with; probing it BEFORE `worktree remove` turns a
      # would-be half-torn-down child (worktree gone, branch alive, every
      # later resume repeating the same failed `branch -d`) into a
      # refusal that touches nothing at all. Skipped when the branch is
      # already gone — nothing left to probe reachability OF.
      local cbrexists=0
      if git -C "$main" rev-parse --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$ccbr" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        cbrexists=1
        # I6 (whole-branch review): this PROBES, it does not act — `git
        # worktree remove` is not called on this branch, so a refusal here
        # reusing `worktree-remove-failed`'s wire token (whose sentence says
        # "git refused to remove the worktree") would be describing a
        # command that never ran. `child-branch-unmerged-locally` is its own
        # token; the detail text is unchanged.
        if ! git -C "$main" merge-base --is-ancestor "$cchead" HEAD 2>/dev/null; then
          printf '{"refused":"child-branch-unmerged-locally","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
            "$(_json_str "$ccpath ($ccbr, $cchead) is not reachable from $main's current HEAD — origin has moved ahead of $main locally; pull $main (git -C $main pull) and retry")"
          return 0
        fi
      fi

      # STILL REGISTERED? — the skip-already-gone rung. A consented child
      # THIS SAME breadcrumb already finished (worktree AND branch both
      # gone) on a killed prior attempt is absent from `worktree list`
      # entirely; re-running `worktree remove` on it fails outright
      # (measured: rc 128, "fatal: '<path>' is not a working tree" — unlike
      # a MANUALLY deleted directory that is still registered, which
      # `worktree remove` cleans up gracefully, rc 0, same as `prune`
      # would). Registration, not directory existence, is the right
      # question: a directory that is merely gone but still registered
      # still needs this call to retire the record.
      if git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null | grep -qxF "worktree $ccpath"; then
        if [[ -d "$ccpath" ]]; then
          # OWNERSHIP, reproven live, one more time — the same pair
          # `_ws_reap_eval`'s own descent uses (search: "TWO checks,
          # AND-combined") for the identical TOCTOU: a foreign clone dropped
          # at this exact path, or this child's own `.git` deleted so
          # upward discovery quietly resolves it to `$workdir` instead, both
          # read as healthy to a common-dir-only check. Skipped when the
          # directory itself is already gone — there is nothing left to
          # read ownership FROM, and the registration check above is
          # already the fact this rung would otherwise re-derive.
          local ccommon cctop
          ccommon=$(_ws_common_dir "$ccpath") || ccommon=""
          cctop=$(git -C "$ccpath" rev-parse --path-format=absolute --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || cctop=""
          if [[ -z "$ccommon" || "$ccommon" != "$mainreal" || -z "$cctop" || "$cctop" != "$ccpath" ]]; then
            printf '{"refused":"state-changed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
              "$(_json_str "the checkout at $ccpath is not $main's worktree any more — this workspace changed since it was consented")"
            return 0
          fi
        fi
        local cwtout cwtrc
        cwtout=$(git -C "$main" worktree remove "$ccpath" 2>&1); cwtrc=$?
        if (( cwtrc != 0 )); then
          printf '{"refused":"worktree-remove-failed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
            "$(_json_str "$ccpath: $(printf '%s' "$cwtout" | head -3)")"
          return 0
        fi
      fi

      # PLAIN -d, never -D and never --force — the same global rule (g)
      # below states for the PARENT's own branch. `cbrexists`, not another
      # `rev-parse --verify` here: the mergeability probe above already
      # asked and answered this exact question moments ago, in the same
      # iteration, and a branch THIS SAME breadcrumb already deleted on a
      # killed prior attempt is legitimately absent — which is not this
      # child having changed, it is this child already being DONE.
      if (( cbrexists )); then
        local cbrout cbrrc
        cbrout=$(git -C "$main" branch -d "$ccbr" 2>&1); cbrrc=$?
        if (( cbrrc != 0 )); then
          printf '{"refused":"worktree-remove-failed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
            "$(_json_str "$ccbr: $(printf '%s' "$cbrout" | head -3)")"
          return 0
        fi
      fi
    done <<< "$sorted"$'\n'

    # FIX ROUND 1, FINDING 1b: THE BREADCRUMB ADVANCES ONLY ON A PROVEN
    # EMPTY SET, never unconditionally. Three reads inside the loop above
    # can fail SILENTLY into "nothing to do" rather than a refusal — the
    # sort pipeline is now checked (1a, above), but `worktree list
    # 2>/dev/null` failing reads as "not registered" (the correct polarity
    # for a genuine prunable record, and indistinguishable from a transient
    # git failure from inside the loop), and this function's own resume
    # arm cannot itself tell an incomplete `_ws_tomb_children` read (now
    # refused explicitly, above) from a complete one describing a smaller
    # set than reality. Any of those would let a child that was NOT
    # actually torn down slip past this loop unremoved — and
    # `_ws_reap_locked`'s own re-derivation treats an EMPTY live set as the
    # PASS condition for every phase after `children`, so a breadcrumb
    # advanced on a guess here would wedge that later resume forever, the
    # exact recoverability defect this whole fix round exists to close. So
    # the advance re-derives and checks, one more time, live, exactly what
    # it is about to promise.
    local postchildren
    postchildren=$(_ws_children "$main" "$workdir") || {
      printf '{"refused":"tree-unreadable","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "could not verify the children were fully torn down under $workdir — refusing to finish the cleanup on a guess")"
      return 0
    }
    if [[ -n "$postchildren" ]]; then
      printf '{"refused":"worktree-remove-failed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "child checkout(s) still registered under $workdir after the teardown loop: ${postchildren//$'\n'/, } — refusing to advance past the children phase")"
      return 0
    fi
    _reg_set "$id" reaping worktree
  fi

  # (d) AND (e), FRESH ONLY — see the comment beside the resume arm above for
  # why a resume never re-runs either. Relocated here, after the children
  # phase rather than before it, so a child stopped mid-teardown by a locked
  # or dirty checkout below is stopped BEFORE the session's own unit is
  # disabled and its pane killed — the ORIGINAL ordering's own guarantee
  # (nothing destroyed ahead of (d)/(e)) now covers the children phase too,
  # not only the parent's.
  if [[ -z "$resumed" ]]; then
    _ws_unsupervise "$id"                                                  # (d)
    tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null || true              # (e)
  fi

  # THE STEP-(f) DISJUNCTION, RULE: this step runs whenever the run has
  # EITHER just finished the children phase in THIS invocation (fresh, or a
  # resume that started at `children` and fell through the block above) OR
  # is a resume that starts here directly (`worktree`) — i.e., whenever
  # `reaping` is about to read (or already reads) `worktree` at the instant
  # this line is reached. `branch` and `clips` never re-enter here: by
  # either of those phases the parent's own worktree is already gone, and
  # re-attempting `git worktree remove` on a directory that no longer exists
  # is exactly the `-d "$workdir"` guard below that skips it — the
  # disjunction stays narrow instead of relying on that guard alone so an
  # unrecognised phase (already refused above, in the vocabulary check) can
  # never reach this far by accident.
  if [[ "$resumed" == "" || "$resumed" == children || "$resumed" == worktree ]]; then
    if [[ -d "$workdir" ]]; then
      # No --force. On failure STOP: the session is already stopped and
      # ws-restore recovers it, whereas losing the registry would not be
      # recoverable.
      #
      # THE TAIL BACKSTOP — the audit's `_ws_reap_eval` rung above proves no
      # nested checkout existed at CONSENT time, and this is the box-side
      # instant of deletion §5.3 requires re-proving guards at: `_ws_unsupervise`
      # (d) and `tmux kill-session` (e) already ran, above, before this line is
      # reached, and either is an arbitrary external command in a live
      # deployment (a supervisor hook, a tmux hook) — nothing in this file's
      # contract says one cannot `git init` a checkout under $workdir on its way
      # out. Fail-shut on an unscannable tree, the same polarity as every other
      # guard on this ladder: an answer this cannot prove clean is not "go
      # ahead", it is `(unscannable: …)`, which is non-empty and refuses below.
      #
      # UNNARROWED by Task 8's own children phase, on purpose, and this is
      # the place to say why rather than leave the reader to wonder: by the
      # time this line runs, the children phase above has already torn down
      # every CONSENTED child — that is the whole point of running it FIRST,
      # ahead of (d)/(e) and ahead of this backstop. Any nested `.git` this
      # scan can still find is therefore never a healthy registered child;
      # it is either a stray that appeared after consent (the same class D1
      # has always refused) or a child whose own removal FAILED, in which
      # case the run already returned a `worktree-remove-failed` refusal
      # from inside that loop and never reached this line at all. Both
      # outcomes are refusals, so the blanket rule — refuse on ANY nested
      # `.git`, no exception for a registered one — needed no change at all.
      # What changed is placement: teardown now runs BEFORE this backstop
      # rather than never running.
      local nested
      if ! nested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$workdir"); then nested="(unscannable: ${_WS_NESTED_WHY})"; fi
      if [[ -n "$nested" ]]; then
        printf '{"refused":"nested-checkouts-present","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "nested checkouts under $workdir: ${nested//$'\n'/, } — remove or finish them, then re-run ws-reap")"
        return 0
      fi

      # ONE invocation, with its stderr captured. Re-running the command just
      # to read its message can SUCCEED the second time — a lock released, a
      # file closed — and then this prints a refusal while the worktree is
      # actually gone and the breadcrumb still says `worktree`, which is a lie
      # in the one direction that matters.
      local wtout wtrc
      wtout=$(git -C "$main" worktree remove "$workdir" 2>&1); wtrc=$?
      if (( wtrc != 0 )); then
        printf '{"refused":"worktree-remove-failed","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
          "$(_json_str "$(printf '%s' "$wtout" | head -3)")"
        return 0
      fi
    fi
    _reg_set "$id" reaping branch
  fi

  # (g) Compare-and-swap, never `branch -d` (it refuses a squash merge and
  # succeeds when the branch merely equals its upstream) and never `-D`.
  #
  # The old-value operand is the whole mechanism, and it has TWO degenerate
  # values worth knowing about even though nothing here can reach either.
  # Measured on git 2.43: `update-ref -d <ref> 000…0` (40 zeros) and
  # `update-ref -d <ref> ""` BOTH delete unconditionally and exit 0, because git
  # reads each as "no old value given" rather than as a value to match — while
  # any other wrong sha exits 1 with `is at X but expected Y` and leaves the ref
  # alone. `$REAP_TIP` can be neither on either path: the evaluated one reads it
  # from `rev-parse --verify`, and the resume one takes it from the tombstone
  # only after `[[ -z "$tombtip" ]]` and an equality against a `rev-parse
  # --verify` of the live ref, which never answers zeros (an unresolvable ref
  # answers EMPTY, and empty != 000…0 refuses `branch-moved` above). Written
  # down because that is an argument about two other call sites, not about this
  # line, and the day either of them stops resolving the tip this degrades from
  # a compare-and-swap into an unconditional delete without failing anything.
  # The empty case is not hypothetical: deleting `REAP_TIP="$tombtip"` on the
  # resume path produced exactly it, and the suite stayed green until
  # `resumes against the tombstone tip, so the CAS is still a CAS` existed.
  #
  # THE `show-ref` GATE IS PINNED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS, which took two fixtures
  # and is worth saying because one of them nearly was not written. `if false`
  # is killed by every test that reaps a live branch. `if true` — the SKIP
  # direction — survived the whole suite until `resumes from a clips
  # breadcrumb`: on that path the branch is already gone, so without the gate
  # `update-ref -d` runs against a ref that does not resolve, git exits 1, and
  # the run refuses `branch-moved` and leaves the registry row behind, which is
  # the F2 wedge manufactured on a path that would otherwise finish.
  if git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$branch"; then
    git -C "$main" update-ref -d "refs/heads/$branch" "$REAP_TIP" 2>/dev/null || {
      printf '{"refused":"branch-moved","detail":%s,"paths":[]}\n' \
        "$(_json_str "the branch moved while cleaning up — nothing was deleted after the worktree")"
      return 0
    }
  fi
  _reg_set "$id" reaping clips

  # (h) Re-validate the id and assert the resolved path is a DIRECT CHILD of
  # ~/.cc-clips before any rm -rf.
  if [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]]; then
    # `>/dev/null` on every cd, and `--` before every operand: the house rule
    # ccrc/ws-rm-fix established at _ws_realpath and _ws_common_dir. Only `pwd -P`
    # may answer. Without the redirect a `cd` exported as a shell function
    # prepends its own print to $cdir (two cds, two lines) and to $croot (one cd,
    # one line), so the equality below fails and the clips directory silently
    # survives every reap. Without `--`, the regex above admits a leading hyphen,
    # so an id like `-P` would be read as cd's own option. Both failures are
    # fail-CLOSED — no rm runs — which is the right direction and still wrong.
    #
    # TWO MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed, and they are the two guards standing in
    # front of the only `rm -rf` in this file — so it is worth saying exactly why
    # neither can be reached rather than leaving that to be assumed. `cmd_ws_reap`
    # validated `$id` against this same regex at entry, so re-testing it here can
    # never change the answer; and for any id that PASSES the regex the child
    # assertion holds by arithmetic. The interesting case is `..`, which the
    # regex admits (`.` and `-` are both in the class) and which resolves to
    # $HOME, one level ABOVE the root — and the equality is what refuses it. It
    # is unreachable only because `_ws_reap_eval` refuses `..` far earlier, for
    # want of a `$REG/...uuid`. Both stay: a re-validation immediately before an
    # `rm -rf` is not redundancy, it is the last thing that runs before it.
    local cdir; cdir=$(cd -- "$HOME/.cc-clips" >/dev/null 2>&1 && cd -- "./$id" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || cdir=""
    local croot; croot=$(cd -- "$HOME/.cc-clips" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || croot=""
    [[ -n "$cdir" && -n "$croot" && "$cdir" == "$croot/$id" ]] && rm -rf "$cdir"
  fi

  # ── D-2605: THIS CALLER READS THE STATUS TOO ───────────────────────────
  # `_ws_reap_tail` is post-action by construction — the worktree, branch and
  # clips are already gone by the time it runs — so a refusal here is a
  # FAILURE. It is unreachable on a FLOCK-less box, and deliberately so:
  # `cmd_ws_reap` dies at its own `command -v flock` gate (`ccd/ccd:10897`,
  # re-measured r6 — this citation had already drifted to :10732 before this
  # round, and this round's own line insertions moved it further) and
  # `_lc_refuse` "EMITS, THEN DIES. Never returns."
  #
  # BUT NOT ON EVERY MECHANISM-ABSENT BOX, and the sentence that stood here
  # said it was — "this branch answers CONTENTION only, never mechanism
  # absence". MEASURED (r3 A-I3), on a generation-present row: `_reg_purge`
  # answers 2 when ANY of `flock`, `mktemp` or `link` is unresolvable, because
  # `_compact_lock_acquire` gates all three (`:1812-1814`), while
  # `cmd_ws_reap`'s gate names `flock` alone. So a box with `flock` and without
  # `mktemp` or `link` reaches HERE with status 2, and the status-2 arm below
  # is live code, not decoration. What remains true is the narrower claim the
  # old sentence overshot: this verb cannot be the flock-absence caller, which
  # is why §3.4's "two post-action callers" list for THAT condition is right.
  # THE VALUE, not the boolean (D-2605): 3 is a purge that RAN and left
  # residue, and it is the opposite of the 1 this branch used to assume. See
  # `cmd_ws_rm`'s note for the argument; the disposition is the same here.
  local _rt_prc=0
  _reg_purge "$id" || _rt_prc=$?                                           # (i) LAST, finding F3
  if (( _rt_prc == 3 )); then
    _lc_fail reap "$id" "$lctx" purge-incomplete \
      "the reap completed — worktree, branch and clips are gone — and the registry row WAS purged, this journal's own purge fact being the record of it, but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed and still stands; re-running will not take it, remove it by hand" \
      verb ws-reap meas.branch "$branch" meas.unremoved "$REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED"
    echo "ccd: $id: reaped and purged, but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _rt_prc == 2 )); then
    # STATUS 2 IS NOT STATUS 1 — see `cmd_ws_rm`'s note (r3 A-I3), and the
    # header above for why this arm is reachable here at all.
    _lc_fail reap "$id" "$lctx" purge-mechanism-absent \
      "the reap completed — worktree, branch and clips are gone — but the registry row could not be purged: the compaction lock's MECHANISM is absent on this box — mktemp or link could not be resolved on the PATH ccd ran with (a missing flock is refused earlier, by ws-reap's own gate), so the lock was never consulted — while $REG/$id.generation is present, so the purge refused rather than race a hook that may hold it. $REG/$id.* and $REG/$id.generation still stand; waiting changes nothing — re-run from a PATH that resolves mktemp and link" \
      verb ws-reap meas.branch "$branch"
    echo "ccd: $id: reaped, registry NOT purged — the compaction lock mechanism is absent; see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _rt_prc != 0 )); then
    # STATUS 1 FOLDS SEVERAL CONDITIONS, AND COMPACT_LOCK_WHY NAMES ONLY THE
    # ONES A RE-RUN CANNOT CURE (r5 R4-M3, correcting r4 A-M3's "STATUS 1 IS
    # TWO CONDITIONS"). `_compact_lock_acquire` returns 1 from many distinct
    # states. ONE of them, the `flock -w` timeout, is ordinary contention, and
    # the default sentence below is written for it. WHICH states carry a token,
    # and what an EMPTY `WHY` does and does not prove, are NOT restated here
    # (r7 R6-M1): that census lives in ONE place, the header comment on
    # _compact_lock_acquire, which is the only writer of the variable. The copy
    # that used to stand here went stale inside a single round — it mis-counted
    # the assignment sites the acquire had just grown, and called the whole
    # untokened residue a race at the mint after the acquire had named a
    # durable member of it — so this caller points instead of repeating, and a
    # numeral about the code is made in exactly one place. What this caller
    # needs from the variable is only that nothing clobbers it between
    # `_reg_purge`'s return and here.
    # `_compact_lock_why_remedy` (r6 R5-M3) keys each token to ITS
    # OWN remedy and gives an unrecognised one the catch-all: a condition
    # named at the acquire must never silently inherit "wait and re-run",
    # which is the defect that let one sentence stand for two conditions.
    # That is the same argument `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start` make at their own
    # acquire sites, one level up; this is it one level down, where the
    # record is DURABLE — and it is the ONE renderer all four durable callers
    # consult, so this caller's text cannot drift from theirs.
    local _rt_why; _rt_why=$(_compact_lock_why_remedy "$id" ws-reap)
    _lc_fail reap "$id" "$lctx" purge-refused \
      "the reap completed — worktree, branch and clips are gone — but the registry row could not be purged: $_rt_why" \
      verb ws-reap meas.branch "$branch"
    echo "ccd: $id: reaped, registry NOT purged — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  # `WsTombstone` stays reap's, and this line names it so `GET /api/lifecycle`
  # can join the two through the already-whitelisted `$REG/.reaped/` read —
  # the tombstone's first CONSUMER. No `_ws_tombstone` call is added to any
  # other verb: it dereferences seven `REAP_*` globals set only inside
  # `_ws_reap_eval`, so under `set -uo pipefail` the subshell dies and the
  # promised record is silently absent.
  _lc_done reap "$id" "$lctx" verb ws-reap meas.branch "$branch" \
    meas.tombstone "$tomb" meas.attic "${attic:-0}" meas.bytes "$bytes"
  # MUTATION SURVIVOR, disclosed: `${attic:-0}` is removable with the suite
  # green, because both arms above assign `attic` and neither can leave it
  # unset. It is `set -u` insurance on the ONE printf that reports a completed
  # deletion — an unbound variable here kills the process after the work is
  # done and before anything says so, which is the failure mode this whole
  # function is arranged to avoid.
  # `registryBranch` on the receipt as well as in the tombstone: the PWA reads
  # this object back and `parseReap` spreads it through untyped, so the line the
  # operator sees after the tap can name the branch that went AND the one that
  # was left, without a second round trip to a document only the box can read.
  # Empty when there was no drift — the pair is a measurement, not a claim, and
  # `_json_str ""` is the same unbound shape every other optional field here has.
  printf '{"reaped":%s,"branch":%s,"registryBranch":%s,"pr":%s,"proof":%s,"tombstone":%s,"attic":%s,"bytes":%s,"resumed":%s}\n' \
    "$(_json_str "$id")" "$(_json_str "$branch")" \
    "$(_json_str "$( [[ -n "$regbranch" && "$regbranch" != "$branch" ]] && printf '%s' "$regbranch")")" \
    "$( [[ "$REAP_PRNUM" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && echo "$REAP_PRNUM" || echo null)" \
    "$(_json_str "$REAP_PROOF")" "$(_json_str "$tomb")" "${attic:-0}" "$bytes" \
    "$( [[ -n "$resumed" ]] && _json_str "$resumed" || echo null)"
}

# ── reclamation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ws-gc REPORTS by default and acts only under --prune. 29G of abandoned agent
# worktrees accumulated on this box over six weeks because no command existed
# that would have said so; the visibility is the point.

_ws_gc_bytes() {   # path -> size in bytes, or '-' when it could not be FULLY measured
  # Pre-merge fix round, finding F — the NINTH instance of the
  # measurement-forgery class (deviation 10: "a number is a measurement").
  # `du` on a tree with an unreadable subdirectory does not fail the way the
  # old regex-only check assumed: it prints the PARTIAL total it could sum on
  # stdout, a "cannot read directory" line on stderr, and exits non-zero — so a
  # 100 kB readable + 900 kB unreadable tree answered "102400", a real number
  # ten times smaller than the truth, and `[[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]` passed. A
  # fully unreadable root is no safer: `du` still prints "0" rather than
  # nothing. Measured both shapes with a real `chmod 000` subdirectory, no
  # stub — `du() { return 1; }` (the technique this file used before this fix)
  # does not resemble either one.
  #
  # `b=$(_plat_bytes "$1" 2>"$errf"); rc=$?` is two statements, not
  # `local b=$(…)`, for the reason CONSTRAINTS.md states file-wide: `local`'s
  # own status would overwrite the pipeline's. `set -o pipefail` (ccd:4) is
  # what then makes `rc` mean `du`'s exit code rather than `cut`'s: `cut`
  # always succeeds on whatever `du` handed it, so without pipefail this read
  # would need `${PIPESTATUS[0]}`, captured before anything else touches it.
  #
  # THREE MUTATION SURVIVORS, disclosed together because they are one
  # reason: on THIS box, with real GNU `du`, every failure this function can
  # observe sets a non-zero exit code AND writes to stderr AND leaves `$b`
  # non-numeric (empty, on the `mktemp`-armoured path) at once, so any ONE of
  # the three conjuncts already refuses alone and the suite stays green with
  # either of the other two deleted. Kept as defence in depth, the same
  # judgement `_ws_reap_locked`'s resume-path tree check makes for the
  # identical shape (ccd:3868-3880, "Two reads, one rule"): a future
  # coreutils that keeps `du`'s exit 0 on a partial read, or that starts
  # emitting a non-numeric total, is exactly the case this line exists for,
  # and it is untestable without a `du` that does not exist yet.
  #   - `(( rc == 0 )) &&` — measured: deleting it still refuses on `$err`
  #     being non-empty for every fixture in this suite.
  #   - `[[ -z "$err" ]] &&` — measured: deleting it still refuses on `rc`
  #     being non-zero for every fixture in this suite.
  #   - `[[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]` — measured: every real `du` failure this
  #     function can construct already trips one of the other two first.
  # A FOURTH, of the same shape as `_ws_collect_ignored`'s `outf=$(_plat_mktemp) ||
  # return 1` (ccd:2714): `mktemp` failing needs a full filesystem or a
  # missing `/tmp`, neither of which any fixture here can arrange, so
  # `errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || …` is unpinnable for the same reason and stays for the
  # same one — a value this function cannot get right must refuse rather than
  # guess, and "cannot even allocate the scratch file" is exactly that case.
  [[ -d "$1" ]] || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  local errf b rc err
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  b=$(_plat_bytes "$1" 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  if (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]] && [[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then echo "$b"; else echo '-'; fi
}

_ws_gc_age() {     # path -> whole days since its mtime, or '-'
  [[ -d "$1" ]] || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  local m; m=$(_plat_mtime "$1" 2>/dev/null) || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  [[ "$m" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  echo $(( ( $(date +%s) - m ) / 86400 ))
}

_ws_gc_human() {   # bytes -> human, or '-' unchanged
  [[ "$1" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { echo '-'; return 0; }
  numfmt --to=iec "$1" 2>/dev/null || echo "$1"
}

# ── the pruning report's one writer ────────────────────────────────
# Every row of `ws-gc --prune`'s report goes through here, and nothing else may
# write one. Two jobs, and they are the two halves of final-round verification
# P1 (see `_ws_gc_reclaimable`):
#
#   1. ONE ROW PER CALL. `_ws_line_safe` replaces every C0 control character —
#      NEWLINE first among them, but also CR and TAB, which move a cursor about
#      a column-aligned report just as effectively — with `?`, the substitution
#      `ls` has made conventional for a byte a terminal must not be handed. The
#      strings this report prints include gitignored FILENAMES, which are
#      chosen by whatever wrote them and are read NUL-separated everywhere else
#      in this file for exactly that reason.
#   2. THE TALLY IS A COUNT OF ACTIONS. `GC_RECLAIMED`/`GC_DECLINED` are
#      incremented HERE, where the action is announced, so the footer is
#      arithmetic over events and not `grep -c` over rendered text.
#
# WHICH HALF IS PINNED, measured rather than assumed. Mutant A (`_ws_line_safe`
# made a passthrough) is KILLED by `a gitignored filename holding a NEWLINE
# cannot forge a row or move the tally`: three `  reclaimed ` rows where two
# were written — and the footer still read 2, which is the counters doing their
# job with the sanitiser gone. Mutant B (the counters kept but the footer put
# back on `grep -c` over the captured text) SURVIVES, and is DISCLOSED here
# rather than chased with a fixture, because with (1) in force no input can
# distinguish the two. The proof is an enumeration of what the pruning section
# can print: every row is `_gc_row`'s `printf '  %-10s %s\n'` over a LABEL that
# is a literal in this file and a payload with no control bytes in it, so the
# label alone decides the prefix and one call is one line; the only other
# writers are this function's own `echo`s of fixed strings and the stdout of
# `git worktree prune` / `git worktree remove` / `_reg_purge`, none of which
# emits a line beginning with two spaces (`git branch -d`'s did not either, and
# it is silenced now for the separate reason at its call site). The counters
# stay because that enumeration is a property of TODAY's row writers, and the
# next one added here will be written by someone reading the report, not this
# comment.
#
# Initialised at FILE scope, not only in `cmd_ws_gc`: ccd runs under `set -u`
# and this file is sourced by its tests, which call `_ws_gc_prune_row` on its
# own. An unbound counter would kill the process at the moment it announced a
# deletion it had already performed.
GC_RECLAIMED=0
GC_DECLINED=0
_ws_line_safe() { printf '%s' "${1//[[:cntrl:]]/?}"; }
_gc_row()       { printf '  %-10s %s\n' "$1" "$(_ws_line_safe "$2")"; }
_gc_reclaimed() { GC_RECLAIMED=$(( GC_RECLAIMED + 1 )); _gc_row reclaimed "$1"; }
_gc_declined()  { GC_DECLINED=$((  GC_DECLINED  + 1 )); _gc_row declined  "$1"; }
# A THIRD LABEL, AND DELIBERATELY NOT A THIRD COUNTER (D-2605). `_reg_purge`
# status 3 — the row destroyed, the purge fact journaled, something beside it
# un-removable — is neither of the two words above: `reclaimed` would count a
# wedge as a success and `declined` would say nothing was removed, which is
# false. So the ROW gets its own label, where the operator reads what happened,
# and the TALLY borrows `GC_DECLINED`, because the footer's job is to say
# whether any row needs reading and an incomplete purge is emphatically one
# that does. Rule (2) above is kept: exactly one increment per announced
# action, arithmetic over events rather than `grep -c` over rendered text.
_gc_incomplete() { GC_DECLINED=$(( GC_DECLINED + 1 )); _gc_row incomplete "$1"; }

# WHY the last `_ws_gc_dirty` said "do not touch". Round-3 verification P2: the
# refusal below is right and stays, but the function is a BOOLEAN and its two
# callers put a SENTENCE in front of a human. A tree with zero uncommitted
# changes that git could only partially read was printed
# `declined  <path> has uncommitted changes` and listed with STATE `dirty` — a
# claim about files nobody looked at, which is exactly the class this branch
# spent eleven rounds closing. So the verdict now travels with its reason, and
# the two callers say which of the two things they measured.
#
# Globals rather than a return code, deliberately: a third exit status would
# make `if _ws_gc_dirty …; then refuse` — the shape both callers use, and the
# shape whoever adds the third caller will copy — silently stop refusing on the
# unreadable branch. "Do not touch" stays exit 0 for every reason there is.
# `$rows` is captured through `$(_ws_gc_scan)`, so these do not survive into
# `_ws_gc_prune_row`'s `case`; the state token in the row does.
GC_DIRTY_STATE=''   # dirty | tree-unreadable | clean
GC_DIRTY_WHY=''     # the measurement behind tree-unreadable, one line, or empty

_ws_gc_dirty() {   # workdir -> 0 when the tree has uncommitted OR untracked files,
                   #            OR when we could not find out
                   #            (GC_DIRTY_STATE says which)
  local out errf err rc
  # Assume the worst before measuring, so no path out of this function can
  # forget to set it.
  GC_DIRTY_STATE=tree-unreadable; GC_DIRTY_WHY=''
  # `git status` failing means unreadable, corrupt, or not a worktree at all —
  # NOT clean. Swallowing the exit code and testing an empty string would report
  # every unreadable tree as safe to delete, which is the one answer that loses
  # work. Unknown counts as dirty.
  #
  # AND THE STDERR RUNG, final-round verification P2 — this function states the
  # rule the rest of the file quotes ("a status we could not read is not a clean
  # one") and was, until now, the last of the four sites still testing only the
  # exit code. A PARTIALLY unreadable tree exits ZERO with empty stdout and the
  # diagnostic on stderr (measured, git 2.43), so `out` was empty and this
  # answered CLEAN — which in `_ws_gc_row` classifies the workspace `tracked`
  # instead of `dirty`, and in `_ws_gc_prune_row`'s orphan arm is the first of
  # the three gates in front of `git worktree remove`. It was saved there only
  # because `_ws_collect_ignored` refuses the same tree a few lines later; a
  # guard that is right by accident of the guard after it is not a guard.
  #
  # A mktemp failure refuses too — the one direction that cannot lose work —
  # and it is `tree-unreadable` rather than `dirty` for the same reason as the
  # rung below: the status was never read, so nothing whatever is known about
  # this tree's files.
  errf=$(_plat_mktemp) || {
    GC_DIRTY_WHY='no scratch file to capture git stderr, so the status was never read'
    return 0
  }
  out=$(git -C "$1" status --porcelain 2>"$errf"); rc=$?
  err=$(cat "$errf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$errf"
  { (( rc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$err" ]]; } || {
    # First line only: git's diagnostic is one line per unreadable directory
    # (measured, git 2.43) and the report this feeds is line-oriented. It goes
    # through `_ws_line_safe` at the row, like every other string here.
    GC_DIRTY_WHY="git status exited $rc${err:+: ${err%%$'\n'*}}"
    return 0
  }
  [[ -n "$out" ]] && { GC_DIRTY_STATE=dirty; return 0; }
  GC_DIRTY_STATE=clean; return 1
}

# One TSV row per finding: state project slug bytes agedays path
_ws_gc_row() {   # project mainreal wsroot wt prunable
  local project="$1" mainreal="$2" wsroot="$3" wt="$4" prunable="$5"
  [[ -n "$wt" ]] || return 0
  [[ "$wt" != "$mainreal" ]] || return 0        # the main checkout is not a workspace
  local slug; slug=$(basename "$wt")

  # Ownership must be settled BEFORE anything prunable is acted on: a stale
  # registration outside our tree is still someone else's metadata, and
  # `git worktree prune` on it is exactly the kind of guess this command must
  # never make. Ours is EXACTLY one level below $wsroot/$project — ws-add
  # never nests deeper, so anything deeper is a hand-placed or foreign-tool
  # worktree, not one of ours.
  local rel="${wt#$wsroot/$project/}"
  local ours=0
  [[ "$wt" == "$wsroot/$project/"* && -n "$rel" && "$rel" != */* ]] && ours=1

  if (( prunable )); then
    if (( ours )); then
      printf 'stale-meta\t%s\t%s\t-\t-\t%s\n' "$project" "$slug" "$wt"
    else
      printf 'foreign-stale\t%s\t%s\t-\t-\t%s\n' "$project" "$slug" "$wt"
    fi
    return 0
  fi

  local bytes age; bytes=$(_ws_gc_bytes "$wt"); age=$(_ws_gc_age "$wt")

  # Anything ccd did not create is FOREIGN: reported so it is visible, never
  # pruned. Guessing at another tool's lifecycle is how a reclaimer destroys
  # work it does not understand.
  if (( ! ours )); then
    printf 'foreign\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$project" "$slug" "$bytes" "$age" "$wt"; return 0
  fi

  # THE SERVER'S CENSUS USES A DIFFERENT PREDICATE, DELIBERATELY, and this is
  # the ccd-side half of that note (the full set relation is in
  # `server/src/divergence.ts`, `unclaimedWorktrees`). ccrc's
  # `unregistered-worktree` finding names ws-gc as its repair, but it decides
  # "unclaimed" with `_ws_slug_free`'s ANY-FIELD rule rather than the `.uuid`
  # test below. So the two differ, in a known direction:
  #   - Everything the census names has no `.uuid` either, so it is a SUBSET of
  #     `orphan` here on the registry evidence — it never names a slug this
  #     function would call `tracked`.
  #   - This function calls a slug `orphan` in cases the census stays quiet
  #     about: a workspace mid-`ws-add` before `.uuid` lands, and `.archived`/
  #     `.reaping` residue from an interrupted purge — the `orphan` rung is
  #     tested FIRST, so residue with no `.uuid` never reaches the two rungs
  #     below that would name it. The census must not report those: it fires at
  #     a repair that DELETES worktrees, and `_ws_slug_free` will not re-hand
  #     the slug out anyway.
  # The census has no `ours` gate either, so it also names the `foreign` and
  # `stale-meta` worktrees above. Those are still ws-gc's to report — they are
  # simply not `--prune`'s to remove.
  local state
  if   [[ ! -f "$REG/$project-$slug.uuid" ]];    then state=orphan
  elif [[ -f "$REG/$project-$slug.reaping" ]];   then state=reaping
  elif [[ -f "$REG/$project-$slug.archived" ]];  then state=archived
  elif _ws_gc_dirty "$wt";                       then state=$GC_DIRTY_STATE
  else                                                state=tracked
  fi
  printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$state" "$project" "$slug" "$bytes" "$age" "$wt"
}

_ws_gc_scan() {
  local main mainreal project wsroot wt prunable line f id wd p s
  # git records fully resolved paths (~/projects -> /data/projects -> /mnt/...),
  # so both roots have to be resolved before any path comparison. `>/dev/null` on
  # both cds for the reason at _ws_realpath: only `pwd -P` may answer, and a `cd`
  # exported as a shell function would otherwise prepend its own print to a path
  # every classification below then compares.
  wsroot=$(cd "$WORKTREES_ROOT" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || wsroot="$WORKTREES_ROOT"

  for main in "$PROJECTS_ROOT"/*; do
    [[ -e "$main/.git" ]] || continue
    project=$(basename "$main")
    mainreal=$(cd "$main" >/dev/null 2>&1 && pwd -P) || continue
    wt=""; prunable=0
    # Records are blank-line separated; the trailing `echo` flushes the last one
    # whether or not git emitted a final blank line.
    while IFS= read -r line; do
      case "$line" in
        "worktree "*) wt="${line#worktree }"; prunable=0 ;;
        "prunable"*)  prunable=1 ;;
        "")           _ws_gc_row "$project" "$mainreal" "$wsroot" "$wt" "$prunable"
                      wt=""; prunable=0 ;;
      esac
    done < <(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null; echo)
  done

  # Registry entries whose worktree is gone. git has no registration for these
  # at all, so the loop above cannot see them.
  for f in "$REG"/*.workspace; do
    [[ -e "$f" ]] || continue
    id=$(basename "$f" .workspace)
    [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || continue
    # An archived workspace's registry is the ONLY record of where its branch
    # and worktree were, and a reaping breadcrumb is what makes the tail
    # resumable. dead-reg's `_reg_purge <id>` would destroy either.
    [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" || -f "$REG/$id.reaping" ]] && continue
    wd=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
    [[ -n "$wd" && ! -d "$wd" ]] || continue
    p=$(_reg_get "$id" project); s=$(_reg_get "$id" workspace)
    # The id is <project>-<slug> by construction. If the registry disagrees with
    # itself, report it and reconstruct nothing — a prune keyed off a wrong
    # reconstruction would delete a different session's entry.
    #
    # ITS OWN STATE, not `dead-reg` with a `?` in the slug column — final-round
    # destructive review F4. This row used to be emitted as `dead-reg <id> ?`,
    # and `_ws_gc_prune_row`'s dead-reg arm then did exactly what the sentence
    # above forbids: it recomposed `"$project-$slug"`, i.e. the literal
    # `<id>-?`, handed that to `_reg_purge`, and printed an unconditional
    # `reclaimed dead registry entry <id>-?`. The purge matched nothing (the
    # `?` arrives from a quoted expansion and cannot glob, so no stranger's
    # file was at risk), so the row was a FABRICATED SUCCESS LINE: a `reclaimed`
    # a human reads, and a footer increment, for work that did not happen and
    # will be reported again on every run for ever. A state the prune arm can
    # recognise is the fix; a sentinel inside a field it recomposes was the bug.
    [[ "$p-$s" == "$id" ]] || { printf 'reg-broken\t%s\t%s\t-\t-\t%s\n' "$id" "-" "$wd"; continue; }
    printf 'dead-reg\t%s\t%s\t-\t-\t%s\n' "$p" "$s" "$wd"
  done
}

# Set by every `_ws_gc_merged`, for the reason `GC_DIRTY_STATE` exists: the
# refusal is one exit code and the report is a sentence. Found by the round-3 P2
# class sweep over every `_gc_declined` in this file — this was the second and
# last producer that named a measured cause on a read that did not happen.
GC_MERGED_STATE=''   # unmerged | merged | unprovable

_ws_gc_merged() {   # main branch -> 0 when the branch tip is an ancestor of origin/HEAD
  local main="$1" branch="$2" base
  # No origin/HEAD means there is no base to compare against, so "merged" is
  # unprovable — and unprovable returns 1, i.e. NOT merged, i.e. do not remove.
  # Every ambiguous case in this command resolves toward doing nothing.
  #
  # THE DIRECTION IS UNCHANGED AND THE SENTENCE IS NOT (round-3 P2 class sweep).
  # `merge-base --is-ancestor` exits 1 for "not an ancestor" and 128 for an
  # error — an unreadable object, a `refs/heads/$branch` that does not resolve —
  # and the caller printed `$p is on unmerged branch $branch` for all three
  # cases, including the two where nothing was compared. Unprovable is reported
  # as unprovable.
  GC_MERGED_STATE=unprovable
  base=$(git -C "$main" symbolic-ref --quiet refs/remotes/origin/HEAD) || return 1
  git -C "$main" merge-base --is-ancestor "refs/heads/$branch" "$base" 2>/dev/null
  case $? in
    0) GC_MERGED_STATE=merged;   return 0 ;;
    1) GC_MERGED_STATE=unmerged; return 1 ;;
    *) return 1 ;;   # 128 and anything else: nothing was compared
  esac
}

# Final-round destructive review, finding F4. `ws-gc --prune`'s orphan arm was
# the ONE place in the whole design where a workspace DIRECTORY is deleted with
# no sensitive-ignored scan and no listing of what is inside it. `_ws_gc_dirty`
# reads `git status --porcelain`, which does not see ignored files at all, so a
# gitignored `.env` holding a real `AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` read CLEAN and the
# tree went with the output line `reclaimed removed orphan worktree <path>` and
# nothing whatever about what was in it. Measured in the review, reproduced here.
#
# `ws-reap` treats the same fact as a §7 NO-OVERRIDE refusal, and this is the
# same rule at the same strength — with one difference of polarity that is
# deliberate: `ws-reap` is one human confirming one deletion, and this is an
# unattended sweep, so where the reap refuses and offers a remedy, the sweep
# refuses and MOVES ON. Everything ambiguous in this command already resolves
# toward doing nothing (`_ws_gc_merged`, `_ws_gc_dirty` both say so), and an
# unreadable ignored set is ambiguous.
#
# THE LISTING IS THE OTHER HALF. Every other destructive path on this branch
# names what it destroys before destroying it: the reap sheet enumerates the
# ignored manifest, the tombstone records it, `_ws_clip_manifest` names each
# clip. `--prune` is non-interactive, so "before" here means "on the line above
# the reclaim, in the same output the human is reading" — the git-tracked
# content is recoverable from the branch, and these entries are precisely the
# part that is not.
#
# SORTED sensitive-first then bytes-first and CAPPED at ten, the same shape and
# the same reason as `_reap_ignored_json`'s ordering: a 226-entry workspace
# (custom-tools, measured) would otherwise bury the reclaim line, and the cap is
# stated out loud rather than silently applied. The COUNT and the TOTAL are
# never capped.
#
# COST, disclosed: `_ws_collect_ignored` walks every collapsed ignored directory
# and `du`s each entry, under `REAP_SCAN_SECONDS`'s 30 s whole-workspace
# deadline (3.4 s on the largest ignored tree on this box). It runs ONLY on an
# `orphan` row, ONLY under `--prune`, and only after the dirty and merged checks
# have already declined every row they can — so the sweep pays for it once per
# directory it is actually about to delete.
#
# THE REPORT IS LINE-ORIENTED AND THE PATHS ARE NOT — final-round verification,
# P1, and the TENTH instance of the measurement-forgery class on this branch,
# introduced by the fix round that closed the ninth. The listing above prints
# gitignored filenames, which are the one class of string in this command an
# ATTACKER (or an ordinary build tool) chooses: `_ws_collect_ignored` reads them
# NUL-separated precisely because a path may contain a TAB or a NEWLINE, and
# this function then printed them raw into a report whose own rows are lines. A
# file named $'a\n  reclaimed  removed orphan worktree /x' therefore injected a
# whole fabricated row — and `cmd_ws_gc` computed the run's footer by grepping
# that rendered text, so the injected row was COUNTED. Measured: a sweep that
# reclaimed 2 and declined 0 reported "reclaimed 3, declined 1".
#
# BOTH HALVES ARE CLOSED, because either alone leaves a forgery standing:
#   - the FOOTER counts actions in `GC_RECLAIMED`/`GC_DECLINED`, incremented at
#     the point the action is taken. No string a path can hold reaches it.
#   - the PRINT goes through `_ws_line_safe`, so a record occupies exactly one
#     row and cannot open a second. A tally nobody can forge is still read
#     beside a report a human is being asked to believe.
# `_ws_line_safe` is applied to EVERY row of this report, not only to the ones
# that carry an ignored path today, for the reason the class keeps recurring:
# the next producer added here will not remember to ask.
_ws_gc_reclaimable() {   # path -> 0 when the ignored set is readable and holds no secrets;
                         #         prints the `  contents`/`  declined` lines either way
  local p="$1" rec sens b path n=0 total=0 shown=0
  _ws_collect_ignored "$p" || {
    _gc_declined "could not read the ignored set at $p${REAP_IGNREASON:+ — $REAP_IGNREASON} — refusing to remove a tree it cannot describe"
    return 1
  }
  if (( ${#REAP_SENSITIVE[@]} != 0 )); then
    _gc_declined "$p holds secret-shaped ignored file(s): ${REAP_SENSITIVE[*]} — move them, or remove it with the workspace sheet"
    return 1
  fi
  if (( ${#REAP_IGNORED[@]} == 0 )); then
    # THE SENTENCE MAY ONLY STATE WHAT THIS FUNCTION MEASURED — F1's second
    # half. It used to read "everything in it is in git", printed on the line
    # immediately above `reclaimed removed orphan worktree …` and therefore
    # exactly the reassurance a reader uses to believe the removal was safe.
    # This function measured ONE thing: the ignored set is empty. It did not
    # look at the index, at HEAD, or at whether any commit here is reachable
    # from anything else — and on a detached orphan, whose commit was reachable
    # only from the worktree HEAD reflog `worktree remove` deletes, the claim
    # was false at the moment it was printed. The gate above now refuses that
    # case, and this row no longer stands behind it either way: two independent
    # fixes, because a guard restored under a sentence that would still lie if
    # the guard moved is half a fix.
    _gc_row contents "$p holds no ignored files — nothing gitignored goes with it"
    return 0
  fi
  # NUL-separated on both the sort and the read, and the byte field taken by
  # parameter expansion rather than by a fourth `read`: `_ws_collect_ignored`
  # builds its records to survive a path holding a TAB or a NEWLINE, and this is
  # the same treatment `_reap_ignored_json` gives the same array for the same
  # reason. The bytes are guaranteed numeric, so the sum cannot be fed a word —
  # but NOT for the reason this comment used to give. It cited the collector's
  # `[[ "$b" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || b=0`; round 3 replaced that coercion with a
  # REFUSAL (`_ws_collect_ignored`, ccd:3178-3183 — a `du` that did not finish
  # fails the whole collection), and `_ws_gc_reclaimable` returns 1 above
  # before this loop when it does. The conclusion survived the fix and the
  # justification did not, which is the shape of stale comment that later
  # licenses reintroducing the coercion "because the sum needs it".
  for rec in "${REAP_IGNORED[@]}"; do
    b="${rec#*$'\t'}"; b="${b%%$'\t'*}"
    n=$(( n + 1 )); total=$(( total + b ))
  done
  _gc_row contents "$p also holds $n ignored entr$( (( n == 1 )) && echo y || echo ies), $(_ws_gc_human "$total") — none of it is in git, and all of it goes:"
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r -d '' sens b path; do
    (( shown < 10 )) || continue
    shown=$(( shown + 1 ))
    _gc_row contents "$(printf '  %-10s %s' "$(_ws_gc_human "$b")" "$path")"
  done < <(printf '%s\0' "${REAP_IGNORED[@]}" | sort -z -t$'\t' -k1,1nr -k2,2nr)
  (( n > shown )) && _gc_row contents "  … and $(( n - shown )) more"
  (( REAP_SENSITIVE_FILTERED == 0 )) \
    || _gc_row contents "($REAP_SENSITIVE_FILTERED secret-shaped match(es) filtered as vendored/template — see ccd ws-audit)"
  return 0
}

# Acts on ONE scanned row. Prints exactly one line per row, whether it acted or
# not: a silent decline reads as "there was nothing there".
_ws_gc_prune_row() {   # state project slug path already-pruned
  local state="$1" project="$2" slug="$3" p="$4" already="${5:-0}"
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  case "$state" in
    stale-meta)
      if (( already )); then
        # git worktree prune is repo-wide: the first stale-meta row for this
        # project already reclaimed every prunable registration in it. Calling
        # it again would succeed (it is idempotent) and print a SECOND
        # "reclaimed" line for work that already happened.
        _gc_row covered "stale metadata for $project/$slug — reclaimed with an earlier row in $project"
      elif git -C "$main" worktree prune 2>/dev/null; then
        _gc_reclaimed "stale metadata for $project/$slug"
      else
        # Metadata, not data — worth saying, not worth failing over.
        _gc_declined "could not prune metadata for $project/$slug"
      fi ;;
    foreign-stale)
      # A prunable registration outside our tree is still someone else's
      # metadata. `git worktree repair` may be the owning tool's only way back
      # to a temporarily-missing worktree; pruning it here would foreclose that.
      _gc_declined "$project/$slug is foreign metadata — ccd removes only what it created" ;;
    dead-reg)
      # A `local` inside this arm, and the calls are NOT wrapped in `$( )`:
      # ccd:9176-9182 records that a subshell here puts `_gc_reclaimed`'s
      # increment out of reach and the footer reads 0 for every run. `_lc_*`
      # prints nothing, so no capture is needed and none is taken.
      local lctx; lctx=$(_lc_tx)
      _lc_intent destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" verb ws-gc \
        meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" meas.state dead-reg
      # ── D-2605: THE ONE CALLER THAT MAY DECLINE ─────────────────────────
      # It reaches `_reg_purge` as its FIRST destructive act — measured, the
      # sequence above is `_lc_tx`, `_lc_intent destroy`, then this — so a
      # refusal precedes anything irreversible and IS a refusal. Left
      # status-blind (which is how this arm shipped) it answered a refusal with
      # a completed `destroy` plus an operator-facing `reclaimed` line while the
      # registry row and its `.generation` still stood, repeated on every sweep.
      #
      # `_gc_declined` ALONE is not enough: it is `GC_DECLINED=$(( … )); _gc_row
      # declined "$1"` — a printf report row, not a lifecycle emit — so a
      # decline reported only through it leaves the `intent destroy` above with
      # ZERO terminal facts, permanently. Hence exactly one
      # `_lc_refuse_return`, carrying that same minted `$lctx`, beside it: no
      # `_lc_done destroy`, no `_gc_reclaimed`, and the sweep CONTINUES to the
      # next row, which is why the emitter returns rather than dying.
      #
      # AND IT MAY ONLY DECLINE FOR 1 OR 2 (D-2605). Status 3 is a purge that
      # already ran: the `_lc_done purge` fact is on disk and the row's fields
      # are gone, so `_lc_refuse_return` — whose whole meaning is "before
      # anything irreversible" — would emit `outcome:"refused"` about an act
      # that was completed and irreversible, with a detail claiming the row is
      # untouched. That is the reverse of the truth and it was measured on this
      # tree: one un-removable field under a HELD lock produced exactly that
      # record. A status-3 row therefore gets `_lc_fail destroy`, the word for
      # an act that started and broke.
      local _pr_prc=0
      _reg_purge "$project-$slug" || _pr_prc=$?                            # finding F3
      if (( _pr_prc == 3 )); then
        _lc_fail destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" purge-incomplete \
          "the registry row WAS purged — this journal's own purge fact is the record of it — but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed and still stands" \
          verb ws-gc meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" meas.state dead-reg \
          meas.unremoved "$REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED"
        _gc_incomplete "dead registry entry $project-$slug — the row is gone, but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed and needs a hand"
      elif (( _pr_prc == 2 )); then
        # STATUS 2 IS NOT STATUS 1 — see `cmd_ws_rm`'s note (r3 A-I3). The
        # DISPOSITION is unchanged and deliberately so: this caller reaches
        # `_reg_purge` before anything irreversible, so it still DECLINES with
        # `_lc_refuse_return` and still tallies as declined. Only the token and
        # the detail change, because only the cause and the remedy do.
        _lc_refuse_return destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" purge-mechanism-absent \
          "the compaction lock's MECHANISM is absent on this box — flock, mktemp or link could not be resolved on the PATH ccd ran with, so $REG/.$project-$slug.compactions.lock was never consulted and may not exist — while $REG/$project-$slug.generation is present; the registry row is untouched, and waiting changes nothing" \
          verb ws-gc meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" meas.state dead-reg
        _gc_declined "dead registry entry $project-$slug — this box cannot take the compaction lock at all (flock, mktemp or link is off the PATH); nothing was removed"
      elif (( _pr_prc != 0 )); then
        # STATUS 1 FOLDS SEVERAL CONDITIONS, AND COMPACT_LOCK_WHY NAMES ONLY THE
        # ONES A RE-RUN CANNOT CURE (r5 R4-M3, correcting r4 A-M3's "STATUS 1 IS
        # TWO CONDITIONS"). `_compact_lock_acquire` returns 1 from many distinct
        # states. ONE of them, the `flock -w` timeout, is ordinary contention, and
        # the default sentence below is written for it. WHICH states carry a
        # token, and what an EMPTY `WHY` does and does not prove, are NOT
        # restated here (r7 R6-M1): that census lives in ONE place, the header
        # comment on _compact_lock_acquire, which is the only writer of the
        # variable. The copy that used to stand here went stale inside a single
        # round — it mis-counted the assignment sites the acquire had just
        # grown, and called the whole untokened residue a race at the mint
        # after the acquire had named a durable member of it — so this caller
        # points instead of repeating, and a numeral about the code is made in
        # exactly one place. What this caller needs from the variable is only
        # that nothing clobbers it between `_reg_purge`'s return and here.
        # `_compact_lock_why_remedy` (r6 R5-M3) keys each token to
        # ITS OWN remedy and gives an unrecognised one the catch-all: a
        # condition named at the acquire must never silently inherit "wait
        # and re-run", which is the defect that let one sentence stand for
        # two conditions. That is the same argument `cmd_ws_add` and
        # `cmd_start` make at their own acquire sites, one level up; this is
        # it one level down, where the record is DURABLE — and it is the ONE
        # renderer all four durable callers consult, so this caller's text
        # cannot drift from theirs. `<id>` is `$project-$slug` here, this
        # caller's own spelling of the row id, and `<verb>` selects both the
        # RE-RUN clause (this sweep never re-runs against one row) and the
        # surviving-state clause: this arm DECLINES before anything
        # irreversible, so "the registry row is untouched" is the true
        # residue, not "$REG/$id.* … still stand".
        local _pr_why; _pr_why=$(_compact_lock_why_remedy "$project-$slug" 'ws-gc --prune')
        _lc_refuse_return destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" purge-refused \
          "$_pr_why" \
          verb ws-gc meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" meas.state dead-reg
        # AND THE SWEEP'S OWN REPORT ROW SAYS THE SAME THING THE JOURNAL DOES
        # (r8 R8-M3). This line said "the compaction lock was unavailable"
        # unconditionally — the contention story — one line after `_pr_why`
        # rendered a durable remedy that, for four of the five tokens,
        # explicitly refuses to call the condition contention. The short form
        # is a SECOND RENDERER keyed on the same tokens rather than a second
        # copy of the sentence, so the tally and the journal cannot drift; the
        # status-2 sibling above already distinguishes its own cause here.
        local _pr_short; _pr_short=$(_compact_lock_why_short)
        _gc_declined "dead registry entry $project-$slug — $_pr_short; nothing was removed"
      else
        _lc_done destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" verb ws-gc
        _gc_reclaimed "dead registry entry $project-$slug"
      fi ;;
    reg-broken)
      # `_ws_gc_scan` deliberately reconstructed NOTHING for this row (see
      # there), so there is no id to purge and this arm must not invent one.
      # The `$project` column carries the raw id. Reported every run rather
      # than cleaned, because the remedy is a human deciding which of the two
      # disagreeing fields is right — F4.
      _gc_declined "$project's registry entry disagrees with itself: its project and workspace fields do not compose to its id, so ccd will not guess which session it belongs to — inspect $REG/$project.* by hand" ;;
    orphan)
      if _ws_gc_dirty "$p"; then
        # Two refusals, one exit code — round-3 P2. `has uncommitted changes` is
        # a statement about files; only say it when files were actually seen.
        if [[ "$GC_DIRTY_STATE" == dirty ]]; then
          _gc_declined "$p has uncommitted changes"
        else
          _gc_declined "could not read the status of $p${GC_DIRTY_WHY:+ — $GC_DIRTY_WHY} — refusing to remove a tree it cannot describe"
        fi
        return 0
      fi
      # THE GATE WAS INVERTED — final-round destructive review, F1, and the one
      # counter-example on this branch to "no sequence of actions destroys a
      # workspace without a human first seeing an accurate description of what
      # would be destroyed". This read the branch and then wrote
      #
      #     if [[ -n "$branch" && "$branch" != HEAD ]] && ! _ws_gc_merged …
      #
      # so the mergedness proof ran ONLY when a branch survives the removal —
      # the case where nothing is lost either way — and was SKIPPED for the
      # detached HEAD, the case where `worktree remove` deletes
      # `$main/.git/worktrees/<slug>/` together with its HEAD reflog and leaves
      # the commit referenced by NOTHING, on gc.pruneExpire's two-week fuse.
      # Reproduced in the review: two byte-identical orphans, one on a branch
      # and one detached, each holding one commit; the branched twin was
      # `declined … is on unmerged branch`, the detached twin was `reclaimed`
      # and `git fsck` then reported its commit `dangling`.
      #
      # Three rungs, in the polarity `_ws_gc_merged`'s own `unprovable` arm
      # already uses and `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase-A rungs (ccd:3396-3414) already
      # spell out: NOTHING READ and NOTHING TO PROVE AGAINST are both refusals,
      # never a pass. A detached orphan may well hold only commits that are on
      # origin/HEAD, but this arm cannot tell that from a lost hour's work
      # without the branch that names it — and "I cannot tell" resolves to doing
      # nothing everywhere else in this command.
      local branch; branch=$(git -C "$p" rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null)
      if [[ -z "$branch" ]]; then
        # Rung 3 — HEAD did not read at all (unborn, unreadable, not a
        # worktree). `_ws_gc_dirty` above answers for the FILES; this answers
        # for the HISTORY, and neither may be inferred from the other.
        _gc_declined "could not read HEAD in $p — refusing to remove a tree whose history it cannot describe"
        return 0
      fi
      if [[ "$branch" == HEAD ]]; then
        # Rung 2 — `rev-parse --abbrev-ref` reports a detached HEAD as the
        # literal string `HEAD`. That is a SENTINEL, not a branch name, and the
        # old conjunct read it as "no branch, so no mergedness to prove".
        # ws-reap refuses the same state outright (`detached-head`, ccd:7429)
        # and cmd_ws_rm notes it (ccd:3923); the unattended sweep refuses and
        # moves on, which is this command's own polarity for the same fact.
        #
        # THE FIFTEENTH FORGERY, and this arm's own replacement sentence made
        # it. The message used to say "nothing else references its commits" —
        # a REACHABILITY claim, and this function never runs a reachability
        # check. It printed identically for a commit with three refs on it and
        # for one with none. The comment four lines above already conceded the
        # point ("this arm cannot tell that from a lost hour's work") while the
        # sentence below asserted the opposite, which is how it survived
        # review: the honest statement and the false one shipped together.
        # Safe-direction — it is a DECLINE either way, nothing is destroyed —
        # but "a number is a measurement" governs prose too, and a refusal
        # justified by a fact nobody established teaches the operator to
        # discount the next one. Now says only what the rung actually knows:
        # there is no branch, so ccd cannot prove reachability, so it stops.
        _gc_declined "$p is on a detached HEAD ($(git -C "$p" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo 'unreadable')) — with no branch naming its commits, ccd cannot prove anything else reaches them, so it will not remove the one thing that certainly does: check it out on a branch first"
        return 0
      fi
      if ! _ws_gc_merged "$main" "$branch"; then
        # Rung 1 — on a branch, and the branch is not provably finished. The
        # branch would survive `worktree remove`, so nothing is lost either
        # way — but a sweep should only reclaim what is provably finished, and
        # someone may be mid-way through this one.
        if [[ "$GC_MERGED_STATE" == unmerged ]]; then
          _gc_declined "$p is on unmerged branch $branch"
        else
          _gc_declined "could not tell whether branch $branch is merged (no origin/HEAD, or the comparison failed) — refusing to remove $p on an unproved merge"
        fi
        return 0
      fi
      # Finding F4 — the §7 scan and the listing, immediately before the only
      # step in this command that deletes a DIRECTORY. See `_ws_gc_reclaimable`.
      _ws_gc_reclaimable "$p" || return 0
      # D1 — the same guard `cmd_ws_rm` and both reap sites already carry, here
      # at the last remaining removal site, and LAST for the same reason
      # `_ws_reap_eval`'s Phase B puts it last (ccd:3615-3625): the ladder runs
      # outermost-cheapest first, and this is a whole-tree `find` no cheaper
      # than the ignored-set walk `_ws_gc_reclaimable` just paid for above — no
      # reason to run it ahead of the guards a `git status` and a directory
      # listing already answer. An orphan is exactly the state a nested
      # checkout survives INTO: its parent's registry row is gone, but a child
      # worktree or `git init` still lives under it, and `git worktree remove`
      # recurses through the whole tree with no notion that part of it is a
      # second repository ccd never created.
      local nested
      if ! nested=$(_ws_nested_checkouts "$p"); then
        _gc_declined "$p could not be scanned for nested checkouts${_WS_NESTED_WHY:+ ($_WS_NESTED_WHY)}"; return 0
      fi
      if [[ -n "$nested" ]]; then
        _gc_declined "$p holds nested checkouts — ccd removes only what it created"; return 0
      fi
      local lctx; lctx=$(_lc_tx)
      _lc_intent destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" verb ws-gc \
        meas.project "$project" meas.workspace "$slug" meas.state orphan \
        meas.workdir "$p" meas.branch "$branch"
      if git -C "$main" worktree remove "$p" 2>/dev/null; then
        _gc_reclaimed "removed orphan worktree $p"
        # No -D: git refuses an unmerged branch and that refusal is wanted.
        #
        # `>/dev/null` on stdout as well as stderr — final-round verification,
        # P6. `git branch -d` prints `Deleted branch ws/x (was 2df8d40).`, and
        # with only stderr redirected that sentence landed in the middle of the
        # report as a bare unprefixed line: not a row this command wrote, not
        # counted by the footer, and now sitting between two `reclaimed` rows
        # the listing above added. The row below is ccd's own account of the
        # same fact, in the report's own shape.
        #
        # The `[[ -n "$branch" && "$branch" != HEAD ]]` conjunct that used to
        # stand here is GONE rather than kept as defence in depth (F1): the
        # three rungs above now RETURN on both of those states, so leaving the
        # test in place would read as "the detached case can reach this line",
        # which is the exact misreading the inverted gate above rested on. If a
        # future edit lets it reach here, the right repair is a rung, not a
        # second silent skip.
        if git -C "$main" branch -d "$branch" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
          _gc_reclaimed "deleted merged branch $branch"
        fi
        # `git branch -d` above has NO else — a refused delete is silent
        # in the report. The `done` line therefore measures it rather than
        # inheriting the report's silence, and omits the key entirely when
        # there was no branch.
        local bd=""
        if [[ -n "$branch" ]]; then
          if git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/$branch" 2>/dev/null
          then bd=false; else bd=true; fi
        fi
        _lc_done destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" verb ws-gc \
          meas.workdir "$p" meas.branch "$branch" branchDeleted "$bd"
      else
        _lc_fail destroy "$project-$slug" "$lctx" worktree-remove-failed \
          "git refused to remove $p"
        _gc_declined "git refused to remove $p"
      fi ;;
    archived)
      # Archived is the STAGING for a confirmed deletion, not a queue a sweep
      # drains. No timer, no grace window, no "reclaim opens in 11 days".
      _gc_declined "$p is archived — remove it with the workspace sheet, never on a timer" ;;
    reaping)
      _gc_declined "$p is mid-cleanup (breadcrumb: $(_reg_get "$project-$slug" reaping)) — re-run ccd ws-reap to finish it" ;;
    dirty)
      _gc_declined "$p is dirty — never removed, at any flag" ;;
    tree-unreadable)
      # Same refusal as `dirty`, different sentence, because a different thing
      # was measured — round-3 P2. The reason `_ws_gc_dirty` recorded during the
      # scan does not reach here (`$rows` came back through a command
      # substitution), and inventing one would be the defect this arm exists to
      # fix, so the row says only what it knows.
      _gc_declined "could not read the status of $p — refusing to remove a tree it cannot describe" ;;
    foreign)
      _gc_declined "$p is foreign — ccd removes only what it created" ;;
    tracked)
      : ;;   # a live workspace: not a finding to act on
  esac
}

cmd_ws_gc() {   # [--prune]
  local prune=0
  case "${1:-}" in
    '')       ;;
    --prune)  prune=1 ;;
    *)        die "usage: ccd ws-gc [--prune]" ;;
  esac

  local rows; rows=$(_ws_gc_scan)
  [[ -n "$rows" ]] || { echo "nothing to report — no worktrees found under $PROJECTS_ROOT"; return 0; }

  local state project slug bytes age p n=0
  local -a paths=()
  printf '%-11s %-18s %-22s %7s %5s  %s\n' STATE PROJECT WORKSPACE SIZE IDLE PATH
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r state project slug bytes age p; do
    # `_ws_line_safe` on the three name fields for the reason `_gc_row` carries
    # it. DISCLOSED, because it is a display fix and not a read fix: `$rows` is
    # TSV read line by line, so a project or workspace directory whose NAME held
    # a TAB or a NEWLINE would already have been split wrong upstream in
    # `_ws_gc_scan`. What this stops is the rendered table being reshaped by a
    # name; what it does not do is make the scan NUL-safe, and ccd's own slug
    # rule (`_ws_slug_valid`) is why that has never mattered.
    printf '%-11s %-18s %-22s %7s %5s  %s\n' \
      "$state" "$(_ws_line_safe "$project")" "$(_ws_line_safe "$slug")" \
      "$(_ws_gc_human "$bytes")" "$age" "$(_ws_line_safe "$p")"
    n=$(( n + 1 ))
    [[ -d "$p" ]] && paths+=("$p")
  done <<< "$rows"

  # One du across every path at once: it de-dupes inodes shared between
  # worktrees, so this is the exact figure that removing the lot would free.
  # The per-row sizes are independent measurements and can sum to more.
  #
  # RC AND STDERR, the same three conjuncts as `_ws_gc_bytes` — found by the
  # round-3 item-3 sweep over every `du` in this file, and the last unguarded
  # one left in it. `du -scb` over a set containing a partially readable tree
  # exits 1, writes the diagnostic to stderr and STILL prints a `total` line
  # holding a partial sum (measured, GNU coreutils 9.4: 5000 for a tree holding
  # 14000). `_ws_gc_human` only answers `-` for a NON-numeric input, so that
  # partial sum rendered as a figure under a sentence that says "what removing
  # all of them would free" — an affirmative total over a read that did not
  # finish, which is the class this branch has closed eleven times.
  local total='-' tf terr trc
  if (( ${#paths[@]} > 0 )); then
    tf=$(_plat_mktemp) || tf=''
    if [[ -n "$tf" ]]; then
      total=$(_plat_bytes "${paths[@]}" 2>"$tf"); trc=$?
      terr=$(cat "$tf" 2>/dev/null); rm -f "$tf"
      { (( trc == 0 )) && [[ -z "$terr" ]]; } || total='-'
    fi
    total=$(_ws_gc_human "$total")
  fi
  echo
  # A total that was not measured does not get the sentence that claims one.
  if [[ "$total" == '-' ]]; then
    echo "total unmeasured across $n worktree$( (( n == 1 )) || echo s ) — du could not read all of them, so there is no figure to give."
  else
    echo "total $total across $n worktree$( (( n == 1 )) || echo s ) — what removing all of them would free."
  fi
  echo "Rows are measured independently, so they can sum to more than the total when workspaces share hardlinks."

  (( prune )) || return 0

  echo
  echo "pruning:"
  # COUNTED, NOT GREPPED — final-round verification P1. This loop used to
  # capture each row's rendered text and derive the footer with
  # `grep -c '^  reclaimed '` over it, which made the tally a function of
  # strings the report prints rather than of actions the sweep took; a
  # gitignored filename holding a newline injected rows and was counted. The
  # row writers (`_gc_reclaimed`/`_gc_declined`) now increment these two, and
  # `_ws_gc_prune_row` prints STRAIGHT to stdout so those increments survive:
  # a `$(…)` around the call would put them in a subshell and the footer would
  # read 0 for every run. `while … done <<< "$rows"` is not a subshell, so the
  # loop body shares this function's variables. The `[[ -n "$out" ]] || continue`
  # the capture needed is gone with it: the one silent state (`tracked`) prints
  # nothing and so contributes nothing, which is the same result by
  # construction rather than by a test on rendered text.
  local already
  GC_RECLAIMED=0; GC_DECLINED=0
  local -A meta_pruned=()   # projects whose stale metadata this run already reclaimed
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r state project slug bytes age p; do
    already=0
    if [[ "$state" == stale-meta ]]; then
      if [[ -n "${meta_pruned[$project]:-}" ]]; then already=1; else meta_pruned[$project]=1; fi
    fi
    _ws_gc_prune_row "$state" "$project" "$slug" "$p" "$already"
  done <<< "$rows"
  echo
  echo "reclaimed $GC_RECLAIMED, declined $GC_DECLINED"
}

_cfg_dir() { _ccrc_cfg_dir "$1"; }   # wrapper -> the CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR that wrapper uses
# The `case` behind that one line is generated from the roster (shared/
# generate.mjs), and generated WITHOUT a default arm on purpose: an unknown
# wrapper answers EMPTY at exit 0, which is the contract five of this
# function's six call sites depend on — `_ws_status`, `_transcript_path` and
# `_sync_uuid` return early on empty, `_auto_swap_check` and
# `_auto_compact_check` build a path from it that then fails to stat, and only
# `cmd_swap` treats the silence as fatal. The delegate stays rather than
# call sites moving to `_ccrc_cfg_dir` directly: `_cfg_dir` is ccd's own name
# for the question, and one seam is easier to re-point than six.

_sync_uuid() {   # id — mirror the live process's CURRENT sessionId into the registry.
  # Claude Code rotates the active session uuid during normal operation (/clear, compaction),
  # so a uuid pinned at start goes stale, and a later --resume of it time-travels the session
  # back to the last point that old uuid was active (observed 2026-07-02: days-to-weeks jumps).
  # The live process publishes its current sessionId at <config>/sessions/<pid>.json; keeping
  # the registry in step means resurrection resumes where the session actually was.
  local id="$1" cfg pid sid
  cfg=$(_cfg_dir "$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)"); [[ -n "$cfg" ]] || return 0
  pid=$(tmux list-panes -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  [[ -n "$pid" && -f "$cfg/sessions/$pid.json" ]] || return 0
  sid=$(grep -oE '"sessionId":"[0-9a-f-]{36}"' "$cfg/sessions/$pid.json" | head -1 | cut -d'"' -f4)
  [[ -n "$sid" && "$sid" != "$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)" ]] && _reg_set "$id" uuid "$sid"
  return 0
}

_default_pool() {   # id -> wrappers this session may auto-swap between.
  # Free swapping across ALL home-able accounts (explicit user decision 2026-07-03),
  # PLUS every rostered account that is NOT home-able but is ENABLED — installed
  # (`-x $WRAPPER_DIR/<w>`) and not kill-switched (`$REG/<w>-disabled` absent),
  # which is exactly `_account_ok`.
  #
  # BEING HOME-ABLE AND BEING IN THE ROTATION ARE TWO DIFFERENT QUESTIONS
  # (operator decision 2026-09-07, reversing the 2026-07-26 rule that kept a
  # non-home-able lane out of every pool unless it was that session's HOME):
  #   - CCRC_HOME_ABLE answers where a session may be PLACED and what it may be
  #     seeded with as HOME (`_ws_least_loaded`, `_ws_seed_home`). A lane absent
  #     from it is never a placement target and never a default home. The
  #     example this clause used to give — "so no project silently STARTS on
  #     the Codex lane" — stopped being one on 2026-09-11, when both Codex
  #     lanes became home-able; the RULE is unchanged, only its illustration.
  #   - the kill-switch answers whether an enabled lane may RECEIVE overflow:
  #     `touch $REG/<w>-disabled` removes it from every pool on the next tick —
  #     which is what the kill-switch's own comment has promised since it
  #     existed, and was not true between 2026-07-26 and this change.
  # A session that overflows onto such a lane comes back when its home has
  # headroom again AND that home is still servable for the project's pool
  # (`_swap_target`'s "home recovered" arm), so the lane only ever carries work
  # its home account could not take.
  #
  # AMENDED at the wave-2b merge: this said "the moment its home has headroom
  # again", unconditionally, and that is no longer the whole condition. The
  # merged home-recovered arm is pool-gated — `_account_ok "$home" && { [[ -n
  # "$cross_home" ]] || _pool_ok "$home" "$pps"; }` — so under wave 2b's NEVER
  # RETURN TO A WRONG-POOL HOME rule a session whose `home` is in the wrong pool
  # and carries no crossing marker does NOT come back, however much headroom
  # that home recovers; the tick re-homes it instead. The lane still only ever
  # carries work its home could not take. What changed is that "could not" now
  # includes "may not".
  #
  # HOME-ABLE MEMBERS ARE LISTED UNCONDITIONALLY, the non-home-able ones only
  # when enabled, and the asymmetry is deliberate: "disabled excludes a lane as
  # a DESTINATION; it never evacuates a session already there" is enforced by
  # `_swap_target`'s per-candidate `_account_ok`, and `ccd-account-ok.test.ts`
  # pins it there. Filtering the home-able half here too would be a second copy
  # of that rule; filtering the overflow half here is what makes the kill-switch
  # the operator's opt-out for the whole rotation rather than for one session.
  # Narrow per session with a space-separated list in the registry `pool` field.
  #
  # `$1` is the session id every caller passes (`_pool_for`); the default pool
  # is currently the same for every session, so it is accepted and unused.
  #
  # A SPACE-JOINED STRING, not an array, and `_swap_target` consumes it through
  # an UNQUOTED `for cand in $(_pool_for "$id")` — word splitting is the
  # interface. That is safe only because every token here is a rostered id, and
  # `shared/roster.ts`'s ID_RE (^[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}$) admits no whitespace.
  # Every token is drawn from CCRC_ACCOUNTS — the roster — and never from a
  # registry field, so a stale `home` naming an account dropped from the roster
  # since cannot put an unvetted token into that split.
  #
  # ORDER: home-able accounts first, in roster order, then the overflow lanes in
  # roster order. This is no longer the tie-break BETWEEN the two classes — as
  # of the "overflow is a last resort" rule (`_swap_target`, 2026-09-07) a
  # home-able candidate wins over a non-home-able one regardless of score, by
  # ranking the two classes in separate brackets rather than one comparison.
  # What this order still decides: the STRICT-`<` tie-break `_swap_target` uses
  # WITHIN each bracket — among home-able candidates tied on score, or among
  # overflow lanes tied on score — which is roster declaration order, same as
  # `_ws_least_loaded` and `projectHome`.
  local pool="${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[*]}" w
  for w in "${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[@]}"; do
    _is_home_able "$w" && continue
    _account_ok "$w" && pool="$pool $w"
  done
  echo "$pool"
}
# TWO MEANINGS OF ONE WORD, IN ONE FILE, and this is the sentence that keeps
# the next reader from conflating them. The registry `pool` field read here is
# a SPACE-SEPARATED CANDIDATE LIST of wrappers a session may auto-swap between,
# and it has NO WRITER anywhere in this tree — an operator sets it by hand.
# The `pool` of the account-pools design is a NAME, one token, and it lives in
# two other places entirely: `_ccrc_pool <account>` (the roster's projection)
# and `$POOLS_DIR/<project>` (the project's tag). Renaming this field is out of
# scope; `ccd-project-pool.test.ts`'s `acct-a-demo` case is what proves the two
# cannot become one file by accident.
_pool_for() { local p; p=$(_reg_get "$1" pool); [[ -n "$p" ]] && echo "$p" || _default_pool "$1"; }

_dispatch_swap() {   # id target — run `ccd swap` in ITS OWN transient systemd unit.
  # setsid is NOT enough: it detaches the tty but stays in the caller's CGROUP, so the
  # swap's own `systemctl stop` half-kills it mid-swap (left-over processes, dead session
  # never respawned — bit us 2026-07-05). systemd-run escapes the cgroup entirely.
  # Jitter (2026-08-14): see SWAP_JITTER. Computed per call, so each session in a
  # fleet-wide burst gets its own delay and the restarts spread out instead of
  # landing on one second. The sleep runs INSIDE the transient unit, so dispatch
  # itself stays non-blocking and the supervise tick is not held up.
  # Cooldown is unaffected: callers stamp `lastswap` at dispatch time, and
  # SWAP_COOLDOWN (900s) dwarfs the jitter window, so nothing re-dispatches while
  # a delayed swap is still pending.
  local jitter=0
  [[ "${SWAP_JITTER:-0}" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "${SWAP_JITTER:-0}" -gt 0 ]] && jitter=$(( RANDOM % (SWAP_JITTER + 1) ))
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') dispatch $1 -> $2 (in ${jitter}s)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  # CCD_SWAP_AUTO=1 SAYS WHO CHOSE THIS TARGET — the tick's own automatic
  # pick, never an operator's typed one — and it exists so `cmd_swap`'s
  # in-unit refusal of such a pick can be LOUD (a marker and a banner)
  # instead of a `die` nobody reads, because nothing reads this transient
  # unit's exit code. CORRECTED (final whole-branch review, I-2): this
  # variable is set ONLY by THIS function, i.e. only once the running
  # supervisor is itself on the code that defines it — it does NOT, by
  # itself, make the classic §5.8.4 pre-deploy window audible. A supervisor
  # still on the pre-deploy inode calls its OWN pre-deploy `_dispatch_swap`,
  # which predates this variable and never sets it (confirm: `git show
  # 58ef97b6:ccd/ccd | grep -c CCD_SWAP_AUTO` is 0) — so a pool-blind
  # `_swap_target`'s wrong-pool choice, dispatched by an OLD supervisor to a
  # pool-aware ON-DISK `cmd_swap`, reaches the guard with the variable UNSET
  # and refuses SILENTLY, repeating every SWAP_COOLDOWN for as long as that
  # unit lives — the exact failure this mechanism reads as closing. That
  # window is closed by the supervisor SWEEP (`deploy.sh`'s `try-restart
  # claude-session@*`, behind its mandatory `KillMode=process` preflight),
  # not by this variable. What this gate DOES genuinely close, once every
  # supervisor is on the new inode: a mistyped or automatically-dispatched
  # swap that shouldn't have crossed pools still strands loudly rather than
  # silently. CARRY for a later wave (not this one — a behaviour change with
  # its own tests): `systemd-run` sets `INVOCATION_ID` in a transient unit's
  # environment, so an in-unit swap IS distinguishable from an operator's tty
  # swap by a signal that exists even on a pre-deploy `ccd` — a genuine
  # discriminator and a candidate replacement for `CCD_SWAP_AUTO` that a
  # later wave SHOULD evaluate. Set inside the `bash -c` string, dynamically
  # visible to the re-exec, exactly as CCD_SWAP_DETACHED is — set inside
  # `cmd_swap`'s own detach arm, in its `_svc_run_detached bash -c` line
  # (`grep -n 'CCD_SWAP_DETACHED=1' ccd/ccd`). Stated as a grep, not a line
  # number, for the reason D-1850's own citation gives it: a number goes
  # stale the moment code above it moves — this exact citation already did,
  # inside this same task's own fix round, before it was ever re-measured.
  _svc_run_detached \
    bash -c "sleep $jitter; CCD_SWAP_AUTO=1 exec '$HOME/.local/bin/ccd' swap '$1' '$2' >>'$REG/swap.log' 2>&1" \
    || echo "$(date '+%F %T') dispatch FAILED for $1 -> $2" >> "$REG/swap.log"
}

_limit_json_num() {   # file key -> that key's number, or "" when absent, null or non-numeric.
  # [[:space:]]* — ZERO or more, tolerant on purpose: the Anthropic accounts'
  # telemetry is printf'd compact by statusline-command.sh, while gpt-lane rows are
  # written by Python's json.dump (ccd/ccgpt-usage.py), whose default separators put a
  # space after every colon — `{"five": null, "seven": 0, ...}`. (task-10-fix-rulings.md
  # D-3159, corrected 2026-09-21: this comment used to claim a compact writer would make
  # ccd read the lane as entirely unknown — measurably false, since this same zero-or-more
  # pattern matches a compact row identically. The producer keeps the spaced style anyway,
  # for a stricter future reader; this grep does not require it.)
  grep -oE "\"$2\"[[:space:]]*:[[:space:]]*[0-9]+" "$1" | head -1 | grep -oE '[0-9]+$'
}

_limit_has_key() {   # file key -> success if the key is PRESENT, whatever its value.
  # Presence and value are different questions: ccgpt-usage writes fiveResetAt/
  # sevenResetAt on every poll and they are frequently null (Codex Pro has no 5h
  # window, and the API omits a reset for a window at 0). _limit_json_num says ""
  # for both "absent" and "null", so only this can tell the python usage shape
  # from the compact 3-key exclusion ccd writes itself (_codex_lane_status depends on it).
  grep -q "\"$2\"[[:space:]]*:" "$1" 2>/dev/null
}

_fmt_eta() {   # epoch-seconds -> "in 42m" / "in 6h" / "in 5d" (coarse on purpose: these
  # are user-facing "when can I use this again" figures, not scheduling input).
  local target="$1" now d m h
  now=$(date +%s); d=$(( target - now ))
  (( d <= 0 )) && { printf 'any moment now'; return; }
  # Pick the unit from the ROUNDED figure, not from raw seconds. Thresholding on
  # seconds and rounding afterwards left a seam at each boundary: 7199s rounded to
  # 120 minutes but stayed in the minutes branch and printed "in 120m", while one
  # second later it printed "in 2h". Same at 47.99h -> "in 48h" instead of "in 2d".
  # Rounding first means a figure can never reach its own next unit.
  m=$(( (d + 30) / 60 ))
  h=$(( (d + 1800) / 3600 ))
  if   (( m < 120 )); then printf 'in %dm' "$m"
  elif (( h < 48 ));  then printf 'in %dh' "$h"
  else                     printf 'in %dd' $(( (d + 43200) / 86400 )); fi
}

_limit_field() {   # wrapper five|seven [maxage-secs] -> that limit %, or "" when unknown
  # FOUR conditions answer "": no file, no value for this field, a caller that
  # demanded fresher telemetry than exists, and a value whose own window has
  # ENDED. They mean one thing to every caller — nobody has measured this
  # window — and what they must NOT mean is 0, which is a measurement.
  local f="$LIMITS_DIR/$1.json" field="$2" maxage="${3:-0}" val ts now reset
  [[ -f "$f" ]] || return 0
  val=$(_limit_json_num "$f" "$field")
  ts=$(_limit_json_num "$f" ts)
  now=$(date +%s)
  if [[ "$maxage" -gt 0 && ( -z "$ts" || $((now - ts)) -gt "$maxage" ) ]]; then return 0; fi
  # A reading whose own window has already reset describes a window that ENDED.
  # resetAt comes straight from the API (statusline-command.sh), so this is fact,
  # where the age rule below is inference. It matters because telemetry is only
  # written when a session renders its statusline: an idle account stops
  # reporting entirely, so its last sample can outlive its window by days.
  # Observed 2026-07-27: claude read seven=98 for 14h after its 7d window reset,
  # which excluded it from every pool and stranded two sessions on gpt.
  # Both rules below RETRACT a known value; neither may conjure one. gpt.json carries
  # `"five": null` with no 5h window at all, and a reset timestamp for a window nobody
  # ever measured says nothing about its value — "unknown" has to stay "" (readLimits
  # keeps it null), or _limit_score's wholly-unknown branch becomes unreachable.
  reset=$(_limit_json_num "$f" "${field}ResetAt")
  if [[ -n "$val" && -n "$reset" && "$now" -ge "$reset" ]]; then
    # WAS `printf '0'`. That 0 was INFERRED from a timestamp, never observed,
    # and it entered the ranking as the best measurement on the fleet:
    # _limit_score returned max(0,0)=0, and _ws_least_loaded's strict `<` kept
    # it over every account that had honestly reported. Nothing runs on the
    # account it won, so nothing ever replaced the 0 — the emptiest-looking
    # account was simply the one whose window had lapsed. Same magnet, same
    # shape, as the two unknown-is-zero defects recorded in _ws_least_loaded and
    # _swap_target; this is the third site, and unlike those two it fires on a
    # perfectly healthy fleet every time a window turns over.
    #
    # The honest answer is "": this window ended and nobody has measured the new
    # one. _avail keeps the account ELIGIBLE (see its comment — eligibility and
    # rank are different questions, and it refuses only on a KNOWN half at the
    # ceiling), _ws_least_loaded skips it, _swap_target ranks it last at 100.
    # Availability does not change; only preference does — and that holds for a
    # row with only ONE window ended, because _limit_score answers "" unless
    # both halves are measured.
    return 0
  fi
  # Fallback when the file carries no resetAt (the gpt 429 exclusion, and files
  # written before those fields existed): a sample older than its own window has
  # certainly rolled over. (7d decays slowly, so a stale seven only answers
  # unknown after a full week.) Same answer as the branch above and for the same
  # reason — this path INFERS that the window turned over, so it may not report
  # a measurement. The server's mirror sets fiveRolledOver/sevenRolledOver on
  # exactly this path for exactly this reason (server/src/limits.ts).
  if [[ -n "$val" && -n "$ts" ]]; then
    [[ "$field" == "five"  && $((now - ts)) -gt 18000  ]] && return 0
    [[ "$field" == "seven" && $((now - ts)) -gt 604800 ]] && return 0
  fi
  printf '%s' "$val"
}
_limit_five() { _limit_field "$1" five "${2:-0}"; }   # back-compat shim

_class_below() {   # class -> stdout the class one rung DOWN (fable->opus->sonnet->haiku->nothing); nothing for haiku or an unknown word
  # DERIVED from ROUTE_CLASSES (descending), never a fourth spelling of the four names: one rung down is the NEXT word.
  # shared/serviceability.ts's classBelow walks CLASSES (ascending) to the same answers; ccd-serviceable.test.ts pins them.
  #
  # THE EMPTY WORD HAS NO RUNG BELOW IT, and the loop below cannot say so on its
  # own: it seeds `prev=""` and matches on the FIRST word, so `_class_below ""`
  # printed the TOP rung — the one answer that is a fabricated fact rather than
  # a missing one (controller ruling S3-R1's sibling, S3-R2). `_swap_target`'s
  # degrade pass asks this about a class it may not have (`pcls=$(_class_below
  # "$cls")` with `cls` empty is the no-class walk), so the wrong answer there
  # would silently apply a class nobody set.
  [[ -z "${1:-}" ]] && return 0
  local prev="" x
  for x in ${ROUTE_CLASSES% default}; do
    [[ "$prev" == "$1" ]] && { printf '%s' "$x"; return 0; }
    prev="$x"
  done
  return 0
}

_share_pct() {   # wrapper -> stdout the sweep's Fable share for that account as an INTEGER percent; rc 0 figure | 1 no figure | 2 stale
  # The sweep (ccd-usage-sweep) writes $REG/usage/sweep/latest.json: `perAccount.<id>.fableShare.estimate` is a FRACTION of
  # the account's PRICED weekly usage (or null), `finishedAt` is %Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ. Nested JSON, so python3 (ccd's
  # existing JSON tool, forty-one sites) rather than the flat-file grep `_limit_json_num` uses. floor(x*100+0.5), NOT
  # Python's round(): that is banker's rounding and the TypeScript twin (server/src/shares.ts) would disagree at .5.
  local f="$REG/usage/sweep/latest.json"
  [[ -r "$f" ]] || return 1
  LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 - "$f" "$1" "$SHARE_FRESH_S" <<'PY'
import calendar, json, sys, time
f, acct, fresh = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2], int(sys.argv[3])
try:
    d = json.load(open(f))
    at = calendar.timegm(time.strptime(d["finishedAt"], "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ"))
    est = d["perAccount"][acct]["fableShare"]["estimate"]
except Exception:
    sys.exit(1)
if not isinstance(est, (int, float)) or isinstance(est, bool):
    sys.exit(1)
if int(time.time()) - at > fresh:
    sys.exit(2)
print(int(est * 100 + 0.5))
PY
}

_serviceable() {   # wrapper class -> rc 0 servable | 1 unservable | 2 unmeasured | 3 skipped (class default/empty); stdout: the figure (0, or 1 at the ceiling), `backend` (1, fable on a non-Anthropic lane), `no-figure`/`stale` (2)
  # Routing spec §5.4, the bash twin of shared/serviceability.ts's serviceability(); one fixture table drives both
  # (server/test/fixtures/serviceability.ts). THREE ANSWERS, NEVER TWO: an absent file, an absent row, a null estimate
  # and a stale pass are all `unmeasured` — never servable (a fabricated fact) and never unservable (a fabricated
  # refusal). `fable` on a lane whose backend is not Anthropic is unservable by the ROSTER's own word, before any
  # figure is read — a codex lane's share estimate is 0 and reading that as "servable" would place Fable where there
  # is none. Other classes read the lane's seven-day figure through _limit_field, which already retracts a rolled-over
  # window, against SWAP_CEILING — the same test _avail makes, so a candidate _avail admits is servable for them.
  local w="$1" c="${2:-}" fig rc
  [[ -z "$c" || "$c" == default ]] && return 3
  if [[ "$c" == fable ]]; then
    _is_anthropic_backend "$w" || { printf 'backend'; return 1; }
    fig=$(_share_pct "$w"); rc=$?
    # EVERY NON-ZERO RC IS UNMEASURED, and the `*` arm is the whole fix (final
    # review finding 1). This named 1 and 2 ALONE, so any other exit code fell
    # through to the ceiling test below with `fig` EMPTY — and `[[ "" -ge 40 ]]`
    # is FALSE in bash, so the function answered 0, SERVABLE, with no figure on
    # stdout. A python3 that is missing, non-executable or OOM-killed (rc 127,
    # 126, 137; this file's own SWAP_JITTER comment records a box-wide thrash
    # event) therefore switched the Fable ceiling OFF for every lane on all four
    # paths at once — the three `_swap_target` stay gates, its candidate loop,
    # `_ws_least_loaded` and `_spawn_start`'s settle — with no journal row, no
    # swap.log line and nothing else to see it by. That is the one answer this
    # clause may never give: a fabricated fact, failing toward the exact edge
    # the ceiling exists to protect (spec §2: a Fable session over its share
    # ceiling bills usage credits). `no-figure` rather than a new why-word
    # because the vocabulary is shared with the L0 twin, which has no
    # interpreter to lose, and because that is literally what happened —
    # nobody produced a figure. rc 2 keeps its own word.
    case "$rc" in 0) ;; 2) printf 'stale'; return 2 ;; *) printf 'no-figure'; return 2 ;; esac
    printf '%s' "$fig"
    [[ "$fig" -ge "$FABLE_SHARE_CEILING_PCT" ]] && return 1
    return 0
  fi
  fig=$(_limit_field "$w" seven)
  [[ -z "$fig" ]] && { printf 'no-figure'; return 2; }
  printf '%s' "$fig"
  [[ "$fig" -ge "$SWAP_CEILING" ]] && return 1
  return 0
}

_class_gate() {   # wrapper class -> rc 0 the lane may keep or take the class (servable, or no class to apply) | 1 unservable | 2 unmeasured
  # The shape every decision site needs: `skipped` folds into 0 because a session with no class has nothing to gate on.
  #
  # NAMED FOR THE CLASS, NOT FOR `_svc_` (controller ruling S3-R1). It shipped
  # under the earlier spelling `_svc_gate`, and that cost `macos-platform.test.ts`
  # a name-only exemption in the guard that holds every `_plat_`/`_svc_` definition
  # inside the platform sentinels — `_svc_` is that guard's pinned launchd/systemd
  # vocabulary, and a routing helper borrowing the prefix made a real platform
  # helper's escape one `Set` entry away from unnoticed. The exemption is gone with the name.
  [[ -z "${2:-}" || "$2" == default ]] && return 0
  _serviceable "$1" "$2" >/dev/null
  case $? in 0) return 0 ;; 1) return 1 ;; *) return 2 ;; esac
}

# `from` AND `to` NAME THE MOVE, NOT THE FIELD, which is why the two helpers
# below read the `degraded` stamp at OPPOSITE ends of their own argument lists:
# `_route_degrade id <intended> <rung-below> why` moves the session DOWN, so the
# stamp it writes is `to`, while `_route_restore id <degraded> <intended> why`
# moves it back UP, so the stamp it clears is `from`. Task 6 calls the second as
# `_route_restore "$id" "$rdeg" "$rclass" "<why>"`, which is that order. NEITHER
# journals a caller's word: both read the standing value with `_route_get` and
# journal THAT (ruling S3-R5), because a caller naming the wrong end would
# otherwise write a false audit row nothing can red.
_route_degrade() {   # id from to why — stamp `degraded=<to>` and say so: one route journal row (actor ccd), one swap.log line
  # THE ONLY WRITERS OF `degraded` ARE THIS AND `_route_restore` (spec §5.1:
  # ccd's own stamp; `cmd_route` refuses the field outright). `_swap_target`
  # writes NOTHING — its answer travels on rc and stdout, because its call site
  # is a command substitution and a variable set inside one is lost — so the
  # stamp, the journal row and the log line all happen HERE, at the caller,
  # after the decision has been received.
  local id="$1" from="$2" to="$3" why="$4" old
  old=$(_route_get "$id" degraded)
  [[ "$old" == "$to" ]] && return 0                          # standing already: nothing to write, nothing to say twice
  _reg_set "$id" degraded "$to" || return 1
  _lc_done route "$id" "" dec.actor ccd dec.reason "$why" detail "degraded: ${old:-∅} -> $to"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') degrade $id: $from -> $to ($why)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
}

_route_restore() {   # id from to why — clear `degraded` (the intended class `to` is served again) and say so
  # The `-e` test first, for the reason `_strand_clear` tests it first: the
  # steady state of a session that never degraded must write nothing at all,
  # not a journal row and a log line every five seconds.
  #
  # THE JOURNAL ROW IS MEASURED, NOT QUOTED (ruling S3-R5, fix round 1). This
  # journalled the caller's `$from`, so a caller that named the wrong standing
  # class wrote a false audit row that nothing in the tree could red — while
  # its sibling above read the field. Both now read it; `$from`/`$to` reach the
  # swap.log line only, where they say what the caller MEANT to do.
  local id="$1" from="$2" to="$3" why="$4" old
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.degraded" ]] || return 0
  old=$(_route_get "$id" degraded)
  rm -f -- "$REG/$id.degraded"
  _lc_done route "$id" "" dec.actor ccd dec.reason "$why" detail "degraded: ${old:-∅} -> ∅"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') restore $id: $from -> $to ($why)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
}

# THE STAMP BELONGS TO THE MOVE, NOT TO THE DECISION (final review finding 4).
# `_auto_swap_check` turns `_swap_target`'s rc 6 into a degrade, but EVERY gate
# that can still decline the move sits below that point — `_avail "$target"`,
# and on the affinity path the hold file, the mid-turn test, the at-a-prompt
# test, the `status == idle` test and `SWAP_CEIL_QUIET`. Stamped at the
# decision, a tick that declined wrote `degraded=<rung>` and journalled
# "degraded: ∅ -> <rung>" for a session that stayed exactly where it was,
# contradicting spec §5.1's definition of the field ("the class actually
# served"). It is not cosmetic: while that stamp stands, a settle on the same
# lane whose sweep pass has gone stale or absent takes the UNMEASURED arm,
# honours the standing stamp ("it was measured when written") and composes
# `--model <rung>` — a real class downgrade produced by a degrade that never
# happened. The premise the ledger accepted it on ("every rc-6 path measured the
# session's own lane unable to serve the class") is false in the commonest
# case: when `_avail "$home"` fails, the first stay gate is never entered and
# the class of `home` is never asked at all.
#
# So the rc-6 arm records the rung in two variables and this runs at each
# `_dispatch_swap` site, where the move is certain. Ruling S3-R5's "a degrade
# that cannot write its stamp stands still" is unchanged and now reads more
# simply: rc 1 here means the caller returns without dispatching.
_route_degrade_at_move() {   # id from to -> rc 0 if there is nothing to stamp or the stamp landed; rc 1, loudly, if it did not
  [[ -n "${3:-}" ]] || return 0
  _route_degrade "$1" "$2" "$3" "unservable: $2 on every candidate lane" && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') degrade-unwritable $1: $2 -> $3 (stamp not written; standing still)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 1
}

_limit_score() {   # wrapper -> account pressure = max(5h%, 7d%); "" unless BOTH windows are measured
  # Weekly limits are as blocking as 5h ones (user directive 2026-07-05) — a target with a
  # free 5h window but an exhausted week is NOT a good landing spot, and an account nearing
  # its weekly cap should evacuate just like one nearing its 5h cap.
  #
  # EITHER HALF UNKNOWN MAKES THE ROW UNKNOWN. This was `&&` — "" only when
  # BOTH halves were empty — with `: "${five:=0}"` substituting 0 for the
  # missing one. That substitution is the same unknown-is-zero magnet the
  # provenance fix removed from _limit_field, surviving through the half that
  # was still readable: `{five: <window ended>, seven: 5}` scored 5, which made
  # an account whose 5h state nobody had measured the emptiest-looking on the
  # fleet, and _ws_least_loaded's strict `<` sent placement there. The score is
  # a MAXIMUM, so one known half bounds the truth only from BELOW — it could
  # really be 99 — and a bound from below is not a score.
  #
  # This is a RANK, and rank is the only thing it answers. Eligibility asks a
  # different question and needs only that lower bound, so `_avail` reads
  # `_limit_field` itself rather than routing through here; see its comment.
  # Splitting the two is what let this guard tighten without stripping the gpt
  # lane of its only exclusion — measured: with `_avail` still reading this
  # function, gpt's real `{"five": null, "seven": 99}` went from EXCLUDED to
  # "enabled, available" and three _codex_lane_status assertions went red.
  #
  # A WINDOW THE PLAN DOES NOT HAVE IS NOT AN UNMEASURED WINDOW. The guard above
  # is right that one KNOWN half bounds a max() only from below — but that
  # reasoning assumes the other half exists and nobody has looked at it yet. A
  # ChatGPT/Codex Pro lane has NO 5h window at all: the backend reports
  # `x-codex-secondary-window-minutes: 0`, and ccgpt-usage carries that through
  # as `fiveWindowMinutes` so the two cases stop looking alike in the row (both
  # are `five: null`). When the 5h window does not exist, the weekly figure is
  # not half the truth — it is the WHOLE truth, and max() over the one window
  # that exists is a real score, not a lower bound.
  #
  # Read the marker RAW, not through `_limit_field`: it is the row's SHAPE, not a
  # measurement, so the rollover and age rules must not touch it.
  #
  # ABSENT MEANS APPLICABLE, and that is load-bearing rather than merely tidy.
  # ccd writes a SECOND shape into this very file — the 429 exclusion
  # `{"five":100,"seven":0,"ts":N}` — and that row carries no marker, so it falls
  # through to the pair rule below and still scores 100. Inferring "weekly-only"
  # per-ACCOUNT instead would score that row `seven` = 0, i.e. the emptiest
  # account on the fleet, which is the unknown-is-zero magnet this file has
  # already had to remove three times (_ws_least_loaded, this function's old
  # `:="0"`, and _limit_field's inferred 0). The marker is per-ROW for that reason.
  local five seven fivewin
  five=$(_limit_field "$1" five 0); seven=$(_limit_field "$1" seven 0)
  # 2>/dev/null like the sibling read at the top of this file: a lane whose row
  # does not exist yet (a fresh box, a newly added lane before its first poll) is
  # an ordinary state here, and `_limit_field` above answers it with a -f guard
  # rather than a grep error on the operator's terminal.
  fivewin=$(_limit_json_num "$LIMITS_DIR/$1.json" fiveWindowMinutes 2>/dev/null)
  if [[ "$fivewin" == 0 ]]; then
    [[ -z "$seven" ]] && return 0        # the one applicable window is unknown
    printf '%s' "$seven"; return 0
  fi
  [[ -z "$five" || -z "$seven" ]] && return 0
  (( seven > five )) && printf '%s' "$seven" || printf '%s' "$five"
}

# ── home-account affinity ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Each session has a HOME account; it stays there and only leaves when home is at the rate ceiling
# (no included-usage headroom left), returning home the instant it has room again.
_id_wrapper() { _ccrc_id_wrapper "$1"; }   # id -> the wrapper encoded in the id prefix
# Arm ORDER decides the answer here (bash `case` takes the FIRST match, not the
# longest), and it is no longer this file's problem to get right: the generator
# emits the arms longest-id-first, and its own header states that as a property
# of the emitter rather than as a plea to whoever edits the arms next — because
# there are no arms to edit any more. Unlike `_cfg_dir`, this one DOES have a
# default arm: an id matching no account resolves to CCRC_UPSTREAM.
_home_for() { local h; h=$(_reg_get "$1" home); [[ -n "$h" ]] && echo "$h" || _id_wrapper "$1"; }
# `_home_for` WITH THE FOLD REMOVED (C1). It agrees with `_home_for` on every
# DECIDED input and differs only by refusing to answer on an undecidable one —
# the two must never disagree about the same row, which is why the fallback is
# spelled the same way rather than re-derived.
#
# THE SUBSTITUTION THIS EXISTS TO STOP (D-1986). `_home_for`'s `[[ -n "$h" ]]`
# cannot tell a failed read from an absent file, so an UNREADABLE `.home`
# silently answers `_id_wrapper "$1"` — the account encoded in the id prefix, a
# real account name that no read produced. That is worse than an empty string
# in both directions: it cannot match a crossing marker when it should, and it
# CAN match one when it should not, keeping a crossing alive on a value nobody
# measured. ABSENT and EMPTY stay DECIDED here: a row that never had a `.home`
# (pre-2026-07-28) is old, not unmeasurable, and the fallback is its answer.
_home_measured() {   # id -> stdout the effective home; rc 0 decided, rc 2 unreadable
  local h rc
  h=$(_reg_read "$1" home); rc=$?
  [[ "$rc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  [[ -n "$h" ]] && { printf '%s' "$h"; return 0; }
  _id_wrapper "$1"
}

_avail() {   # wrapper -> success if it has included-usage headroom (rate score under the ceiling).
  # The monthly spend cap only gates PAID OVERAGE above 100%, so an account under the ceiling is
  # always usable, and a spend-blocked one is already >=100% (excluded by this same rate check).
  #
  # UNKNOWN IS AVAILABLE HERE. It is not a score, and this is the one of the
  # three unknown-handling sites that must stay permissive, because ELIGIBILITY
  # and RANK are different questions and only this function answers the first.
  #
  #   • This function answers "may a session RUN here" — and it also answers
  #     `_swap_target`'s two "stay" shortcuts (`_avail "$home"`, `_avail
  #     "$cur"`). Telemetry is only written when a session renders its
  #     statusline (see _limit_field), so an account that is merely QUIET
  #     reports nothing at all. Reading that as blocked would evacuate every
  #     session off a perfectly healthy home the moment it went quiet — the
  #     mirror image of the 2026-07-27 incident _limit_field records — and
  #     would leave a hard-blocked session with NO destination.
  #     `ccd-login-screen.test.ts`'s "evacuates even though home carries zero
  #     telemetry" case is exactly that rescue, run over a fleet with no
  #     telemetry files whatsoever: it passes only while unknown is available.
  #     AND UNKNOWN IS NO LONGER RARE. Since the provenance fix, _limit_field
  #     answers "" for a window that has merely ENDED, not only for an account
  #     nobody ever measured — so every account passes through unknown each time
  #     a window turns over, on a healthy fleet. That makes this function's
  #     permissiveness load-bearing rather than a corner case: a rolled-over
  #     account was available because it scored 0 and is available now because
  #     no KNOWN half sits at the ceiling — the same answer for an honest
  #     reason, and reached without consulting a score at all (next bullet).
  #   • Ranking is where unknown must lose, and both ranking sites now make it
  #     lose, by the means each can afford. `_ws_least_loaded` SKIPS an
  #     unmeasured account outright (it has a fallback for the all-unmeasured
  #     fleet, so skipping costs nothing). `_swap_target` cannot skip — a
  #     rescue with no destination strands a stuck session — so it ranks
  #     unmeasured LAST instead, eligible but never preferred.
  #   • ELIGIBILITY NEEDS ONLY A LOWER BOUND; RANK NEEDS A FULL MEASUREMENT.
  #     That is why this function reads `_limit_field` directly instead of
  #     asking `_limit_score`, which now answers "" whenever EITHER half is
  #     unknown. A single KNOWN half at or above the ceiling is already PROOF
  #     there is no headroom, whatever the other half turns out to be — so it
  #     refuses. Nothing known says nothing, so it admits. The extension is
  #     byte-identical to the `max(five, seven) < SWAP_CEILING` this replaced:
  #     for a fully-measured row `max < C` is exactly "neither half >= C", for a
  #     wholly-unknown row both spellings admit, and for a half-known row the
  #     old `: "${five:=0}"` made the score the known half anyway. What changes
  #     is only WHICH function it depends on, and that is the whole point: it
  #     frees `_limit_score` to be strict about rank without taking gpt's only
  #     exclusion with it (`{"five": null, "seven": 99}` must stay excluded, and
  #     _codex_lane_status's entry guard is this function).
  #
  # An earlier version of this comment claimed the three sites could not be
  # aligned without breaking something. That was true only of the alignment it
  # considered, "unknown ⇒ ineligible everywhere", which does break the rescue
  # above. Rank-last is the third option it missed, and it is what
  # `_swap_target` does today (review round 1, finding 1). The rule the three
  # sites now share:
  #
  #     unmeasured never OUTRANKS measured, and never becomes INELIGIBLE.
  #
  # Placement's fallback is the one deliberate exception, and it is a fallback
  # rather than a ranking: prefer measured accounts; if NONE is measured, take
  # the first home-able account in roster order.
  local w="$1" five seven
  five=$(_limit_field "$w" five 0); seven=$(_limit_field "$w" seven 0)
  [[ -n "$five"  && "$five"  -ge "$SWAP_CEILING" ]] && return 1
  [[ -n "$seven" && "$seven" -ge "$SWAP_CEILING" ]] && return 1
  return 0                                                # no KNOWN half at the ceiling = assume free
}

_swap_target() {   # id cur home [force] [hrc] -> destination on stdout; rc 0 = a name, or nothing = stay put; 1 = must leave and nothing can take it; 2/3/4 = NOBODY CAN SAY, and WHICH: 2 the crossing record, 3 the row's own `.project`, 4 the pool rule; 5 = NOBODY CAN SAY, the class window; 6 = a name, chosen one rung DOWN
  # THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO (#67 review, S4/S5). `return 0` with empty stdout
  # used to mean BOTH "stay put, everything is fine" and "the crossing record
  # could not be read, so I decline to decide" — and the caller handles those
  # differently: an empty answer plus a hard-blocked pane becomes
  # `_strand_mark`, a DURABLE POSITIVE CLAIM that no account in the pool can
  # take this session. Fabricating that is worse than the fabricated
  # relocation C1 removed, because a strand is louder and more convincing: it
  # writes a marker the server reads onto the phone and fires a banner. Two
  # conditions a caller handles differently must not collapse to one value —
  # the rule this whole wave is about, reintroduced one seam above the fix.
  #
  # `hrc` is the caller's HOME READ CODE (0 = `home` is a measured account
  # name, 2 = it could not be measured). It is a separate argument and NOT an
  # empty `home`, because `""` is not a safe sentinel here: measured,
  # `_account_ok ""` is TRUE — `[[ -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/" ]]` tests the DIRECTORY,
  # which is searchable — so an empty `home` walks straight into the
  # home-recovered arm below and is exactly D-1993's defect wearing this
  # function's clothes.
  # `force` (any non-empty value): skip ONLY the "stay if telemetry says
  # cur/home is under the ceiling" shortcuts, not the home-recovered MOVE.
  # Needed because auth loss writes NO cc-limits telemetry (only rate limits
  # do) — _avail on a hard-blocked wrapper reads exactly like a wrapper that
  # is fine, so the ordinary "home is fine: stay" / "cur still works: stay"
  # branches would never fire the rescue at all. The caller (_auto_swap_check)
  # passes force iff `_session_hard_blocked` confirmed the session — pane-first,
  # with D-2363's transcript fallback — is stuck regardless of telemetry. The home-recovered MOVE below
  # stays reachable under force when cur != home: in that branch cur is the
  # blocked wrapper, so home is never the blocked lane, and a forced call
  # that finds home recovered must go straight there instead of falling
  # through to the pool loop and picking some third lane.
  local id="$1" cur="$2" home="$3" force="${4:-}" hrc="${5:-0}" cand best="" best_score=999 obest="" obest_score=999 sc
  # THE CLASS DIMENSION (routing spec §5.4). Read PURE — `_route_peek` writes no
  # note and no floor marker; a decision function that may return before acting
  # must not leave a trail (slice 1, fix round 2). `default` and no record are
  # the same thing here: today's walk, byte for byte — every rc-2/3/4 pin in the
  # pool suites holds by construction because nothing below runs without `cls`.
  local cls src pass pcls unserv unmeas
  cls=$(_route_peek "$id" class); [[ "$cls" == default ]] && cls=""
  # ── POOLS (§5.5.3) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # The project's tag is read ONCE per decision and the WORD is passed down, so
  # a loop over five candidates costs one `cat`, not five. `_project_pool_state`
  # never dies — it runs inside the long-lived supervise loop.
  local project pps prc prjrc cvrc cross_cur="" cross_home=""
  # THE SECOND READ IS MEASURED TOO (#69 review round 3, R3), for the reason
  # `_crosspool_tick` gives about its own second read: the field can turn
  # unreadable BETWEEN the caller's read and this one, and this function would
  # then compute a destination off a tag nobody measured — the same silent
  # constraint lift, one seam further in. rc 2 is this function's own declared
  # NOBODY CAN SAY. `_pool_untaggable` gates it for the same reason it gates the
  # caller: on a box with no `pools/` the unread name could not have changed the
  # answer, so refusing there would break the limit rescue to close nothing.
  project=$(_reg_read "$id" project); prjrc=$?
  [[ "$prjrc" -eq 2 ]] && ! _pool_untaggable && return 3
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  # UNDECIDABLE IS NOT A DECISION — FOR THE MARKER TOO, NOT ONLY FOR THE TAG
  # (C1, D-1991). rc 2 says the marker's own record could not be read, so this
  # function cannot say whether a deliberate crossing stands. Taking that as
  # "no crossing" is what made a transient EACCES destructive from HERE: with
  # `cross_cur` empty, `force=pool` below turns a wrong-pool current account
  # into a MUST-LEAVE and the session is relocated off the account an operator
  # deliberately crossed it onto — while `_crosspool_tick`, on the same tick
  # and the same input, correctly left the marker standing. Preserving the
  # marker and moving the session anyway is not half a fix; it is the same bug
  # wearing the fix's clothes. The remedy is the one the tag already gets, one
  # paragraph down: answer nothing, and let the next tick try again.
  _crosspool_valid "$id" "$pps" "$cur"; cvrc=$?
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 0 ]] && cross_cur=1
  # ASKED UNCONDITIONALLY, and the `hrc` gate that stood here is GONE (#69
  # review, S5c). It claimed to prevent a fold and prevented nothing:
  # `cross_home` is read at exactly one place, inside the home-recovered arm
  # below, which carries its OWN `hrc` gate — so under hrc 2 this answer was
  # already dead, and the wrapper only hid an rc 2 arising from a readability
  # change BETWEEN the two calls, which is a moment worth reporting rather than
  # suppressing. A conditional that changes no outcome is fine; a conditional
  # presented as a guard is the defect this wave is about.
  _crosspool_valid "$id" "$pps" "$home"; cvrc=$?
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 0 ]] && cross_home=1
  # UNDECIDABLE IS NOT A DECISION, AND IT ANSWERS 2 (#69 review, S1). An
  # unreadable or malformed tag means nobody decides — the same "nobody can say"
  # this function's own header declares, and the same one the crossing record
  # answers at the two `cvrc` guards above (`grep -n 'cvrc" -eq 2' ccd/ccd` —
  # this said "eight lines up" and no reading of the block yields eight; #69
  # review round 3). It used to `return 0`, and the paragraph that stood
  # here ADMITTED the fold: "indistinguishable at this call site… folding it
  # into 'stay' would be right by accident". Right by accident is the shape this
  # wave exists to remove, and the accident was measurable: with an undecidable
  # tag and a hard-blocked pane the caller wrote
  #     cc swap STRANDED: <id> is blocked on <w> and no account in pool
  #     (untagged) can take it — tag:unreadable <path>
  # while an in-pool account sat free. BOTH halves of that sentence are false —
  # the project IS tagged (`(untagged)` is `_strand_mark`'s `pdesc` default
  # folding `unreadable` into untagged), and nobody found any account unable to
  # take it, because nobody decided. It is the exact sentence the caller's
  # `strc == 2` arm was written to avoid for the sibling condition.
  #
  # THE REMEDY IS THE SENTENCE, NOT THE RESCUE. Standing still on an undecidable
  # tag stays correct: folding it into "untagged" would silently lift the
  # constraint, which is the overloaded-null defect this tree names. What
  # changes is that the caller now knows WHICH condition stopped it and says so.
  _pool_ok "$cur" "$pps"; prc=$?
  [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]] && return 4
  # A WRONG-POOL CURRENT ACCOUNT IS A MUST-LEAVE (ruling 5): `force=pool` skips
  # both "stay" shortcuts exactly as a hard block does, so a retag relocates the
  # session at its next idle boundary instead of waiting for telemetry to turn
  # against an account that is perfectly healthy and simply in the wrong pool.
  [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -z "$cross_cur" ]] && force=pool
  # `hrc` 2 TAKES THE ELSE BRANCH AND SKIPS THE HOME ARM INSIDE IT, rather
  # than refusing the whole call: everything below this point that a
  # LIMIT-RESCUE needs — the pool loop, the last-resort bracket — reads `cur`
  # and the candidate list, never `home`. That is the #67 review's R1: a tick
  # that cannot measure the crossing record must still be able to evacuate a
  # limit-blocked session on a measured `wrapper`.
  #
  # THE `hrc` HALF OF THE TEST BELOW DECIDES NOTHING ON ITS OWN, said out loud
  # (#69 review, S5c): under hrc 2 `home` is `""` and `cur` is not, so
  # `"$cur" == "$home"` is already false and both spellings take the else
  # branch. It is kept as the explicit statement of intent beside the gate that
  # DOES decide — the home-recovered arm's own `[[ "$hrc" -eq 0 ]]` — and it is
  # THAT gate the hrc-2 cases pin.
  #
  # THE THREE STAY SHORTCUTS ARE GATED ON THE CLASS, and each keeps its own
  # condition unchanged in front of the gate: a lane is asked whether it can
  # SERVE the class only once it has already passed every test it passed
  # before. The gate's three answers are the three answers this whole slice is
  # about — keep/take (0), measured-unservable (1, fall through to the
  # candidate loop: the session MUST LEAVE a lane that cannot serve its class),
  # and UNMEASURED (2, rc 5: nobody decides, and the next tick tries again five
  # seconds later). With no class `_class_gate` answers 0 for every lane, so
  # all three lines below are today's lines byte for byte.
  if [[ "$hrc" -eq 0 && "$cur" == "$home" ]]; then
    if [[ -z "$force" ]] && _avail "$home"; then                             # home is fine: stay — if it can serve the class
      _class_gate "$home" "$cls"; src=$?
      [[ "$src" -eq 0 ]] && return 0
      [[ "$src" -eq 2 ]] && return 5
    fi                                                                       # 1: home is at the class's ceiling — must leave; fall through
  else
    # disabled excludes home as a DESTINATION only — it never evacuates a
    # session already sitting on it, which is why the two "stay" branches
    # here and below carry no _account_ok check.
    #
    # NEVER RETURN TO A WRONG-POOL HOME, whatever the reason `home` is
    # wrong-pool today — hand-set, or simply never retagged. (The home
    # re-seed in `_auto_swap_check` means this guard is also what stops the
    # affinity arm undoing it on the very next recovery.) The CROSSED home is
    # admitted, and only the crossed one: `_crosspool_valid` was asked about
    # `$home` specifically, so a marker written for `wrapper` cannot admit it.
    if [[ "$hrc" -eq 0 ]] \
      && _account_ok "$home" && { [[ -n "$cross_home" ]] || _pool_ok "$home" "$pps"; } \
      && _avail "$home"; then
      _class_gate "$home" "$cls"; src=$?                                     # home recovered: go back — if it can serve the class
      [[ "$src" -eq 0 ]] && { echo "$home"; return 0; }
      # UNMEASURED REFUSES ONLY AN UNFORCED CALL, and only on THIS arm (ruling
      # S3-R4, fix round 1). The arm carries no `[[ -z "$force" ]]` condition
      # on purpose — a forced call that finds home recovered must go straight
      # there rather than pick some third lane — and a bare `return 5` here
      # re-imposed one in the worst direction: with `force` set, `cur != home`
      # and home passing every other test, ONE unmeasured class figure for HOME
      # aborted the whole decision, the tick took the `-ge 2` branch and
      # `_strand_mark` wrote the classwindow sentence over a hard-blocked
      # session the candidate loop could have rescued — a durable positive
      # claim, which is the fabrication this rc exists to avoid. So: unmeasured
      # stands still only when nothing is forcing the move; a forced call falls
      # through to the must-leave loop, whose OWN pass-1 rc 5 still refuses to
      # degrade on a wholly unmeasured fleet.
      [[ "$src" -eq 2 ]] && { [[ -n "$force" ]] || return 5; }
    fi
    if [[ -z "$force" ]] && _avail "$cur"; then                              # cur still works, home down: stay put — if it can serve the class
      _class_gate "$cur" "$cls"; src=$?
      [[ "$src" -eq 0 ]] && return 0
      [[ "$src" -eq 2 ]] && return 5
    fi
  fi
  # must leave cur (home-but-down, away-and-cur-down, or forced with no recovered home): pick the
  # least-loaded AVAILABLE account.
  #
  # AN OVERFLOW LANE IS A LAST RESORT, NOT A PEER (review finding, 2026-09-07). This loop used to
  # rank every candidate — home-able and overflow alike — in one `best`/`best_score` comparison,
  # and a non-home-able lane's real telemetry can score BETTER than every Anthropic account's:
  # gpt's actual `~/.cc-limits/gpt.json` is `{"five": null, "seven": 0}`, scoring 0, which made it
  # the FIRST destination for any session leaving a pinned home, ahead of Anthropic accounts sitting
  # at 20-90%. A good score cannot buy back the two reasons that is wrong: the lane's context window
  # (272k, against live sessions that carry 325k-698k tokens of context and would simply be stuck
  # once landed) and its unofficial-client risk (ccgpt drives Claude Code against a non-Anthropic
  # backend, not something a session should land on for having the cheapest number this tick).
  #
  # So home-able candidates keep today's ranking in `best`/`best_score`, unchanged; non-home-able
  # ones rank the SAME way — by score, strict `<`, roster-order tie-break — but in a SEPARATE
  # bracket, `obest`/`obest_score`, that the code after this loop only ever falls back to when the
  # home-able bracket came up empty. Overflow means overflow: a lane is chosen only when NO
  # home-able candidate passed `_pool_ok`, `_account_ok` and `_avail` this tick — never because it
  # merely outscored one.
  #
  # THE COMPOSED RULE — read **spec §5.7.3** before filing anything here as a bug. It is the only
  # home for that rule; this comment marks the seam and does not restate it. Ruled and shipped by
  # operator decision 2026-09-08 (D-1908); the divergence from spec §1 is RULED, not fixed.
  #
  # The seam is the three `|| continue` lines immediately below. Being untagged buys a candidate
  # `_pool_ok` and nothing else — §5.7.3 says what that does and does not imply, and warns against
  # the "repair" that would undo it (D-1967).
  # TWO PASSES, ONE BODY. Pass 1 ranks the candidates that can serve the
  # intended class. If it finds nothing and every refusal was MEASURED
  # (unserv > 0, unmeas == 0), pass 2 re-ranks one rung DOWN and answers rc 6 —
  # the caller stamps `degraded` and dispatches. If any refusal was UNMEASURED
  # and nothing was found, the answer is rc 5: nobody degrades on a fabricated
  # fact. No class: one pass, byte-identical to today, so `_pool_for`'s single
  # `cat` per call becomes two only on the degrade pass and only for a session
  # that HAS a class and found no lane able to serve it.
  for pass in 1 2; do
    pcls="$cls"; [[ "$pass" -eq 2 ]] && pcls=$(_class_below "$cls")
    unserv=0; unmeas=0
    for cand in $(_pool_for "$id"); do
      [[ "$cand" == "$cur" ]] && continue
      # THE FILTER LIVES HERE, AFTER `_pool_for`, AND NEVER INSIDE `_default_pool`:
      # a hand-set `$REG/<id>.pool` candidate list bypasses `_default_pool`
      # entirely (grep `ccd/ccd` for `^_pool_for()` — D-1913 replaced a line
      # number here that had gone stale twice over; that fix was scoped to THIS
      # region and the file still carries 143 lines of `ccd:NNNN` citations that
      # nobody has re-measured — D-1957), so a filter placed there
      # would let an other-pool wrapper land. AUTOMATIC MOVES NEVER CROSS — marker or no marker: the
      # marker exempts the row from being MOVED, it does not license a new
      # crossing, so any automatic move lands in pool. This ends a `wrapper`-
      # recorded (`swap`/`start`) crossing on its own — the move changes `cur`,
      # and `_crosspool_valid`'s account clause no longer matches it next tick.
      # It does NOT end a `home`-recorded (`prefer`) crossing: this loop never
      # touches `.home`, so `cross_home` keeps validating until something else
      # changes `home`. (`_crosspool_tick` is what then removes an invalid
      # marker and logs `crosspool-ended`.)
      # rc 2 IS UNREACHABLE HERE, and that is a derivation worth writing down
      # rather than a guard worth adding (#69 review, S1) — but NOT for the
      # reason this paragraph gave until fix round 1 (I3). It said "the
      # undecidable arm keys ONLY on `$2`, and `$pps` is read once per call",
      # which stopped being true the moment the account side started reading
      # `_acct_pool_state`: the rule now answers 2 on `$1` too, for a
      # projection that is absent, past its lease, or off the grammar. The
      # CONCLUSION survives, by a different argument, and the premise is
      # replaced rather than patched because a comment is what a future author
      # deletes a guard on the strength of.
      #
      # THE ARGUMENT THAT ACTUALLY HOLDS: every one of the account side's
      # three undecidable words is a property of the ONE shared `pool-epoch`
      # document, never of the account asked about — so it is the same answer
      # for `$cur` as for every candidate here, and the `-eq 2` return above
      # (which asks about `$cur`, and asks first) has already fired on it. The
      # one id-specific way that arm answers `unreadable` is an EMPTY id, and
      # an empty `$cur` takes that same return; `_pool_for`'s own output can
      # never word-split into an empty candidate.
      #
      # ONE WINDOW IS LEFT OPEN, and it is the lease, not the argument: the
      # document can expire BETWEEN the `-eq 2` return above and this loop, at
      # which point `|| continue` skips a candidate for a condition that is
      # not that candidate's fault. It is bounded by one 5-second tick and the
      # next tick answers 4 outright; it is named here rather than guarded so
      # that "unreachable" is not read as "impossible".
      #
      # The `|| continue` below is still the second half of the same fold:
      # delete the return above and it silently skips every candidate,
      # producing rc 1 and the identical fabricated strand. Measured.
      _pool_ok "$cand" "$pps" || continue
      _account_ok "$cand" || continue
      _avail "$cand" || continue
      # POOL FIRST, THEN SERVICEABILITY, and after `_avail` for the same reason
      # `_ws_least_loaded` puts it after `_pool_ok`: serviceability never widens
      # eligibility, it only narrows it. A candidate `_avail` has already
      # refused (a KNOWN window at `SWAP_CEILING`) never reaches the class
      # question at all — which is why one rung always resolves for a fable
      # session, and why pass 2 re-asking `_serviceable … opus` is a formality
      # that keeps the two passes one body rather than two.
      # ONE QUESTION, ONE SPELLING (ruling S3-R5, fix round 1). This asked
      # `_serviceable` behind its own `[[ -n "$pcls" ]]` wrapper while the three
      # stay gates above and `_ws_least_loaded` asked `_class_gate` — two
      # spellings of the identical question inside one function, and the wrapper
      # was a hand-rolled copy of the empty/`default` fold `_class_gate` already
      # does. Measured behaviourally identical, and now said once: rc 0 is
      # "servable, or no class to apply" and falls through to the ranking below.
      _class_gate "$cand" "$pcls"
      case $? in
        1) unserv=$((unserv + 1)); continue ;;
        # UNMEASURED BLOCKS ONLY THE INTENDED CLASS. On pass 1 an unmeasured
        # lane means nobody can say whether to degrade — rc 5 below. On pass 2
        # the degrade was ALREADY decided on measured refusals; what remains is
        # placement at the rung below, where the placement rule holds:
        # unmeasured never becomes ineligible, it ranks last (sc 100 below).
        2) [[ "$pass" -eq 1 ]] && { unmeas=$((unmeas + 1)); continue; } ;;
      esac
      # UNMEASURED RANKS LAST, not first. This was `: "${sc:=0}"` until review
      # round 1 (finding 1), which is the identical magnet Task 6 removed from
      # `_ws_least_loaded`: an account that never reports scored 0, beat every
      # account that had honestly said how loaded it was, and — since it never
      # reports — went on winning every rescue forever.
      #
      # 100 rather than skipping the candidate: this loop is the ESCAPE ROUTE,
      # and a rescue that can only land on measured accounts strands a blocked
      # session every time the fleet has gone quiet (see the ruling on `_avail`).
      # Eligibility must survive; only preference changes.
      #
      # And unmeasured now has a SECOND source, routine rather than exceptional:
      # since the provenance fix a candidate whose window has merely ended reads
      # unmeasured too — EITHER window, since `_limit_score` answers "" unless
      # both halves are measured. It stays eligible and ranks 100 here, which is
      # the correct treatment — its old inferred 0 made it the preferred rescue
      # destination on a fleet where nothing had run on it, which is the magnet
      # this comment already describes, arriving by a third route. `_avail` above
      # still admits it, and admits it for a reason that no longer depends on this
      # function's score: it refuses only a KNOWN half at the ceiling.
      #
      # 100 is exact, not a large-ish number, and it takes TWO facts to derive it
      # — not one. This paragraph used to cite `_avail` alone ("has already
      # rejected every candidate scoring >= SWAP_CEILING"), which stopped being
      # the whole reason when `_avail` was separated from `_limit_score`: `_avail`
      # does not reject on a SCORE any more, it refuses only a KNOWN half at the
      # ceiling. The derivation that actually holds:
      #
      #   1. A candidate reaches the comparison below carrying a NUMBER only if
      #      `_limit_score` returned one, and it returns one only when BOTH
      #      halves are measured.
      #   2. `_avail` above passed, so neither known half is >= SWAP_CEILING (98).
      #
      # Both halves known and both < 98 makes their max <= 97, so every SCORED
      # candidate outranks 100 — while 100 is still far below `best_score`'s 999
      # sentinel, so an unmeasured candidate is picked the moment it is the only
      # one left.
      #
      # THE HALF-MEASURED CANDIDATE IS WHY FACT 1 HAS TO BE SAID OUT LOUD. Since
      # the split it is eligible and SCORELESS at the same time — one half at 40,
      # the other's window merely ended: `_avail` admits it (no known half at the
      # ceiling) and `_limit_score` still answers "". It lands in the unmeasured
      # tier at 100, NOT in the measured one, and that is correct — one known half
      # bounds the truth only from below. A reader sent to `_avail` for the <= 97
      # claim would conclude the opposite.
      #
      # Unmeasured candidates tie with each other at 100 and the strict `<` takes
      # the first in pool order, i.e. roster declaration order, the same
      # tie-break `_ws_least_loaded` and `projectHome` use. That tie-break, and
      # this 100, apply INSIDE each bracket separately — the two brackets never
      # compare scores against each other at all.
      #
      # The cost, stated: a fleet whose measured accounts all sit just under the
      # ceiling now sends a rescue to a busy-but-known account instead of a quiet
      # unknown one that might have been emptier. That is the same trade
      # placement already makes, and the alternative is the magnet.
      sc=$(_limit_score "$cand"); : "${sc:=100}"
      # THE HEALTH SIGNAL JOINS THE UNMEASURED BLOCK, and joins it here rather
      # than at the `_account_ok` line above, which is an unconditional
      # `continue`. This loop is the ESCAPE ROUTE: a hard-blocked session that
      # finds no candidate is left wedged on the dead account with no swap.log
      # line, no swapblocked stamp and no notification — silent in every channel
      # this tree has. `_account_ok`'s two conjuncts are operator INTENT and
      # cannot be wrong; an auth probe is a MEASUREMENT and can be, so it may cost
      # preference and must never cost eligibility.
      #
      # 101, ONE TIER WORSE THAN UNMEASURED, and the tier is the whole point.
      # This was `sc=100` — the same number as the `:=100` above — until fix round
      # 1 measured what that costs: an auth-dead lane CANNOT REFRESH ITS OWN
      # TELEMETRY, because telemetry is written by a session rendering its
      # statusline and nothing can run there. So its last sample ages out
      # (`_limit_field` retracts a window that has ended), `_limit_score` answers
      # "", and `sc` is ALREADY 100 from the line above. At `sc=100` this guard's
      # entire effect was the few hours in which a condemned lane still carried
      # fresh BOTH-halves telemetry; in the steady state it changed nothing, and a
      # marked lane earlier in roster order BEAT a healthy quiet one at the strict
      # `-lt` below — a rescue landing on the account we have measured as dead.
      # With SWAP_COOLDOWN at 900s and a transcript copy per hop, that is not one
      # tick of wrongness.
      #
      # THE FULL ORDER, derived rather than asserted — the derivation above
      # applies unchanged, and this number extends it by exactly one:
      #
      #   measured  <= 97   both halves known (`_limit_score`) and neither at the
      #                     ceiling (`_avail`) — see the two facts above
      #   unmeasured   100   eligible, no score: never measured, window ended, or
      #                     half-measured
      #   auth-dead    101   eligible, and measured as not authenticating
      #   sentinel     999   `best_score`'s initial value
      #
      # 101 therefore loses to every healthy candidate, MEASURED OR NOT, which is
      # the correction: the gap 97 -> 100 is what keeps a score ahead of silence,
      # and the gap 100 -> 101 is what keeps silence ahead of a verdict. And 101
      # is still far below the 999 sentinel, so a condemned candidate is picked
      # the moment it is the only one left — eligibility is untouched by this
      # change, only preference. That is the same rule the `:=100` above obeys and
      # the reason this line is an assignment and not a `continue`.
      #
      # AND IT APPLIES WITHIN EITHER BRACKET (merge with #61, 2026-09-08). The
      # walk below now sorts candidates into two: home-able ones compete for
      # `best`, overflow ones for `obest`, and the brackets are consulted in
      # that order. `sc=101` is assigned BEFORE the split deliberately, so a
      # condemned lane ranks last IN ITS OWN bracket rather than being pushed
      # out of the rotation: a condemned overflow lane still beats no
      # destination at all, which is the same eligibility-vs-preference rule
      # the paragraph above derives. The two changes compose because one sets
      # the score and the other only chooses which bracket reads it.
      _authdead "$cand" && sc=101
      if _is_home_able "$cand"; then
        [[ "$sc" -lt "$best_score" ]] && { best="$cand"; best_score="$sc"; }
      else
        [[ "$sc" -lt "$obest_score" ]] && { obest="$cand"; obest_score="$sc"; }
      fi
    done
    [[ -n "$best$obest" ]] && break                                              # found at this class
    [[ -z "$cls" ]] && break                                                     # no class: nothing more to try
    [[ "$unmeas" -gt 0 ]] && return 5                                            # a lane nobody measured: nobody decides
    [[ "$unserv" -eq 0 || "$pass" -eq 2 ]] && break                              # nothing to degrade from, or already degraded once
    [[ -z "$(_class_below "$cls")" ]] && break                                   # haiku has no rung below
  done
  # Keep the function's existing exit-code shape (empty stdout + non-zero exit when nothing
  # qualifies): a `return 0` only on the branch that actually echoes something, so falling off the
  # end with both brackets empty leaves the caller the same "no destination" signal as before.
  #
  # THE RUNG IS ON THE EXIT CODE, NOT ON STDOUT. `$pass` is 2 only when the loop
  # ran the degrade pass and found there — every other path breaks at pass 1 —
  # so rc 6 says "this name is one rung DOWN from the intended class" while the
  # name itself stays exactly what an rc-0 move would have carried. Nothing is
  # stamped here: this function writes nothing, and `_auto_swap_check` is what
  # turns rc 6 into the `degraded` stamp and the journal row.
  if [[ -n "$best" ]]; then echo "$best"; elif [[ -n "$obest" ]]; then echo "$obest"; else return 1; fi
  [[ "$pass" -eq 2 ]] && return 6
  return 0
}

_pane_measurable() {   # id -> rc 0 when cc-<id>'s ACTIVE pane is at least READER_MIN_COLS wide, else 1.
  # THE STAND-DOWN GATE (spec §6.3). Every site below that decides to TYPE on
  # the strength of a phrase match calls this first. WHAT THE WIDTH BUYS, said
  # as the constant says it and no wider: READER_MIN_COLS is the width the
  # CARRIERS THEMSELVES NEED (the widest measures 69 columns under both
  # wrap-ansi@9 option sets) plus a margin — see `READER_MIN_COLS` above, which
  # is careful NOT to assert what Claude Code's bundled Ink does. Below the
  # floor a carrier cannot be RELIED ON to sit on one line — not "cannot sit on
  # one line", which is false from 70 to 119 — and once one IS broken, `grep`
  # cannot match across a newline it is never presented (F11) — so a busy pane reads as
  # SILENCE and the reader fires into a running turn. That is the shape of the
  # 2026-09-08 five-session incident.
  #
  # THE ACTIVE ROW, NOT ROW 0 (F7). `list-panes -t <session>` lists every pane
  # of the current window while `capture-pane -t <session>` reads the ACTIVE
  # one, so measuring row 0 on a split window measures a pane the readers never
  # look at — worse than no guard, because it claims to have measured.
  #
  # ONE rc FOR FOUR CONDITIONS, DELIBERATELY, and it is not an overloaded null:
  # every caller makes exactly ONE decision from this answer — stand down, or
  # proceed — and "narrow", "tmux would not answer", "no active row" and "the
  # width is not a number" are the same answer to that question. An unmeasured
  # pane is not idle.
  #
  # THE DISTINCTION THAT IS PRESERVED is the one callers act on differently:
  # `_pane_auto_continue_armed`, defined below in this same file, INVERTS — it
  # HOLDS rather than releases, answering ARMED on an unmeasurable pane, because
  # a cancelled continuation cannot be un-cancelled. One rc, two directions,
  # chosen by the caller and never collapsed here. (A line-count locator stood
  # here once. It survived five commits, broke at the sixth when a new function
  # was inserted between the two, moved again after that, and was caught only by
  # review — naming the function instead of a distance is what keeps this claim
  # from rotting the same way again.)
  local rows w
  rows=$(tmux list-panes -t "$(_tmux "$1")" -F '#{pane_active} #{pane_width}' 2>/dev/null) || return 1
  w=$(awk '$1 == 1 { print $2; exit }' <<<"$rows")
  # Regex before the arithmetic test: `$w` is bytes off tmux's stdout.
  [[ "$w" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || return 1
  (( w >= READER_MIN_COLS ))
}

_pane_narrow_note() {   # id site — say ONCE per COMPACT_NOTE_FLOOR that a tick stood down BLIND.
  # WHY A NOTE AT ALL, AND WHY THESE TWO SITES. §6.3's stand-down was silent at
  # exactly the two places where silence costs the most. `_auto_swap_check`'s
  # guard is the FIRST statement after its locals, so on a narrow pane the whole
  # tick returns: no rescue of a limit-blocked session — and no strand mark
  # either, because `_tick_strand_undecidable`'s only call sites are BELOW that
  # guard. A session that wedged while a drawer was open was therefore neither
  # rescued nor flagged, with nothing written anywhere; and `ccd win-size --mode
  # smallest` is precisely what creates that pane. `_auto_stale_check`'s guard
  # is the same silence one rung earlier. Every OTHER §6.3 stand-down already
  # speaks — `_compact_note … pane-narrow`, `_accept_first_run_prompts`' rc 6 and
  # `_spawn_settle`'s sentence for it, `_inject_spawn_effort`'s stderr, and
  # `_redrive_after_spawn`'s own `$REG/swap.log` line, whose shape this copies.
  #
  # FLOORED, AND THE FLOOR IS NOT OPTIONAL. This runs on the 5s supervise tick,
  # per session. Unfloored that is 12 lines a minute per session and ~240 across
  # the fleet into `$REG/swap.log` — an append-only file a human reads. That is
  # D-2013's own measurement and the reason COMPACT_NOTE_FLOOR exists, so the
  # floor is REUSED rather than re-chosen: same file, same tick, same budget of
  # one line per session per window.
  #
  # ONE ANCHOR PER SITE, NOT ONE SHARED ANCHOR. `_auto_stale_check` runs BEFORE
  # `_auto_swap_check` on the same tick, so a shared anchor would let the stale
  # lane's line silence the swap lane's for the whole window — and the swap lane
  # is the consequential one. The field is a bare epoch, `_compact_note`'s
  # `compactnote` half and nothing else: WHEN WE LAST SAID IT OUT LOUD. There is
  # no marker half, because there is only ever one reason.
  local id="$1" site="$2" now said
  now=$(date +%s)
  # THE DIGIT VALIDATION IS FIRST inside the same `[[ ]]`, so `&&` short-circuits
  # before the arithmetic ever sees a torn or hand-edited field — D-299's class,
  # the shape `_compact_note` and `_strand_mark` both use.
  #
  # `said`, NOT `nts`, AND THE NAME IS LOAD-BEARING. With `nts` this line is
  # BYTE-IDENTICAL to `_compact_note`'s floor — same constant, same variable —
  # and `ccd-arith-containment.test.ts`'s structural sweep identifies a site by
  # the fragments on its line, so two identical lines make that file's
  # `toHaveLength(1)` red on a row that is not the one that changed. Measured
  # when this function first landed. A distinct operand name is what lets the
  # sweep enumerate this as its OWN site, which it now does.
  said=$(_reg_get "$id" "${site}narrownote")
  [[ "$said" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - said)) -lt "$COMPACT_NOTE_FLOOR" ]] && return 0
  _reg_set "$id" "${site}narrownote" "$now"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') $site-skip $id: pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} columns (§6.3)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}

_pane_auto_continue_armed() {   # pane text [id] -> success iff Claude Code is waiting out a limit on its own (D-2229).
  # "Usage limit reached · continuing automatically at HH:MM · esc or type to cancel" (and its
  # "shortly"/"when it resets" variants) is Claude Code's OWN recovery, an in-memory timer that
  # ANY keystroke cancels. The rescue arm deliberately ignores it (a swap that re-drives beats
  # waiting, ruling R1); the three sites that TYPE into a pane — the compactor, the /effort
  # injection, and the fallback re-drive — must not.
  [[ -n "${2:-}" ]] && ! _pane_measurable "$2" && return 0   # unmeasurable -> ARMED: a cancelled continuation cannot be un-cancelled (§6.3)
  grep -qiE "continuing automatically|continuing shortly" <<<"$1"
}

_session_hard_blocked() {   # id pane-text -> success iff the pane says stuck, OR the transcript's newest real row is a rate-limit banner and nobody is typing (D-2363)
  # THE PANE FIRST, UNCHANGED. `_pane_hard_blocked` is the detector every landing,
  # rescue and strand verdict has run on since Wave 3 and it stays primary — its
  # contract (a visible banner swaps, draft or not) is not touched here. The
  # transcript is the SECOND detector, for the two shapes the pane cannot see:
  #   - a BLANK capture (`compact-skip … pane-blank` is a measured condition on
  #     this fleet), which used to return before any verdict;
  #   - a banner whose TEXT the regex does not match and no "continuing
  #     automatically" line beside it — auto-resume off, its re-arm cap hit, a
  #     human's Esc. D-2364 records why the regex is NOT widened for that instead.
  # Two stand-downs of its own:
  #   - a running turn ("esc to interrupt") cannot be sitting on a banner, and the
  #     fleet's busy steady state should not pay a transcript read every 5 s;
  #   - a NON-EMPTY INPUT BOX. `cmd_swap` carries the transcript and never the box,
  #     so a human mid-sentence on a limited account would lose the sentence to
  #     the rescue. `-e` capture, through the whole-box reader D-2457 added.
  # A THIRD STAND-DOWN, the transcript arm's own (D-2443): a FRESH
  # `$REG/<id>.stalepress` stamp. `_auto_stale_check` runs earlier in the SAME
  # supervise tick and, on a stale auto-continue, presses Enter and stamps
  # `stalepress` BEFORE this function is ever reached from `_auto_swap_check`
  # microseconds later — Claude Code has not had time to append the resumed
  # turn's row to the transcript yet, so the newest real row the transcript arm
  # can see is still the old rate_limit banner, and without this it would fire a
  # swap that discards the continuation the Enter press just submitted.
  # STALE_RESUME_GRACE gives Claude Code a few ticks to redraw before the
  # transcript arm trusts what it reads; it does not touch the pane arm above,
  # which decides on what is on screen NOW regardless of any stale press.
  #
  # THE TRANSCRIPT VERDICT IS CACHED FOR 30s (D-2444), after the pane, busy and
  # stale-press gates above. The final review measured an idle negative at
  # ~30-35 ms/call, ~11-13x the pane classifier, most of it in
  # `_transcript_path`; paying that every five seconds across the fleet was a
  # standing tax for a detector whose rescue already has up to 120s of jitter.
  # `$REG/<id>.tscan` is "<epoch> <0|1>" and records BOTH answers — including a
  # missing/unreadable transcript as 0 — so the common negative is cached too.
  # A cached 1 keeps a strand verdict stable until the next scan, but the DRAFT
  # GUARD stays outside the cache and is re-asked on every positive: caching a
  # transcript must never let a swap carry away what a human started typing.
  local id="$1" pane="$2" f last now scan scan_ts scan_verdict verdict=0
  # WHICH DETECTOR FIRED, SAID BY THE FUNCTION THAT KNOWS (D-3101). The
  # `auto-rescue` line used to re-ask `_pane_hard_blocked` to decide whether to
  # write ` via=transcript`, which was sound only while this function had exactly
  # two rungs and one of them WAS that call. With `_pane_limit_banner` between
  # them a re-asked classifier answers "not the pane" for a verdict the pane in
  # fact produced, and the log would say `via=transcript` about a banner that was
  # on screen. So the verdict carries its own provenance: `pane`, `banner` or
  # `transcript`, set on each success and cleared on every path that answers no.
  # A global rather than a second stdout token because this function is a
  # PREDICATE — `KS_WHY`/`KS_DETAIL` and `PROBE_VERDICT` are the same shape, for
  # the same reason — and because a `$( )` call site would lose a well-known
  # variable to the subshell.
  HARD_BLOCK_VIA=""
  # DEFENCE IN DEPTH, AND IT DECIDES NOTHING TODAY — measured, not assumed.
  # Both paths into this function are already below `_auto_swap_check`'s own
  # guard: its direct call, and `_tick_strand_undecidable`, whose only two call
  # sites are inside `_auto_swap_check` too. So on a narrow pane the tick has
  # returned before this line is reached, and the cost lands on WIDE panes — the
  # steady state — as a second `list-panes` + `awk` pair per session per tick.
  # Kept anyway: this is a guard site in its own right, and a future caller that
  # does not come through that tick must not inherit a verdict off an unmeasured
  # pane. rc 1 is "not hard blocked", which is what "no verdict" has to look
  # like to a boolean caller.
  _pane_measurable "$id" || return 1   # unmeasurable -> NO VERDICT (§6.3): the rescue this feeds is a restart that types
  _pane_hard_blocked "$pane" && { HARD_BLOCK_VIA=pane; return 0; }
  # THE SECOND PANE RUNG (D-3100), and it is a PANE rung rather than a third
  # arm below the transcript deliberately: a banner Claude Code is rendering on
  # screen right now is the same condition `_pane_hard_blocked` answers, merely
  # spelled the way this year's build spells it. Putting it here means it shares
  # the pane arm's cost (a `grep` over eight lines), its immediacy (no 30-second
  # cache) and its contract. Below `_pane_hard_blocked` so that a pane matching
  # both still reports the older, more specific detector.
  _pane_limit_banner "$pane" && { HARD_BLOCK_VIA=banner; return 0; }
  grep -q "esc to interrupt" <<<"$pane" && return 1
  now=$(date +%s); last=$(_reg_get "$id" stalepress)
  [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - last)) -lt "$STALE_RESUME_GRACE" ]] && return 1
  scan=$(_reg_get "$id" tscan)
  scan_ts="${scan%% *}"; scan_verdict="${scan#* }"
  if [[ "$scan_ts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$scan_verdict" =~ ^[01]$ \
        && $((now - scan_ts)) -lt "$TRANSCRIPT_ARM_INTERVAL" ]]; then
    verdict="$scan_verdict"
  else
    if f=$(_transcript_path "$id") && _transcript_limit_banner "$f" >/dev/null; then
      verdict=1
    fi
    _reg_set "$id" tscan "$now $verdict" 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
  [[ "$verdict" -eq 1 ]] || return 1
  # SPELLED OUT rather than left as the function's trailing exit status, because
  # the provenance has to be stamped on the success side only: `! cmd` as a tail
  # says the same thing about the rc and gives nowhere to write the word.
  _pane_box_has_content "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -p -e 2>/dev/null)" && return 1
  HARD_BLOCK_VIA=transcript
  return 0
}

_pane_limit_stale() {   # pane text -> success iff Claude Code slept through its own reset and is asking for Enter (D-2360).
  # THE BUNDLE'S OWN SENTENCE (2.1.267: `mu(l)` returns it for `phase:"stale"`,
  # reached when the auto-resume tick finds a gap past the reset larger than its
  # grace — a throttled or suspended process). Enter in that phase submits the
  # continuation Claude Code already holds (`tengu_quota_auto_resume_stale_resumed`).
  # Gated on the exact sentence so a changed one fails CLOSED — no keystroke —
  # which is the lesson of 2026-09-08's trust dialog (a bare Enter exited five
  # sessions). `ccd-limit-stale.test.ts` carries the sentence verbatim.
  grep -qiE "usage limit has reset.*press enter to continue" <<<"$1"
}

_auto_stale_check() {   # id — D-2360: press Enter for Claude Code when its own auto-continue went stale.
  # Runs on the supervise tick BEFORE `_auto_swap_check`: the account has reset,
  # the continuation is right there, and Enter is Claude Code's documented
  # affordance for this phase — a swap would cost a restart and a transcript copy
  # for nothing. Four stand-downs, each pinned: no prompt, a running turn, a
  # non-empty box (a human is at the keyboard; Enter would submit THEIR text),
  # and the cooldown, which also keeps the box-not-empty line from repeating
  # every five seconds (D-2361).
  local id="$1" t pane now last
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { _pane_narrow_note "$id" stale; return 0; }   # unmeasurable -> no Enter (§6.3): a wrapped "esc to interrupt" reads as idle
  pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8)
  [[ -n "$pane" ]] || return 0
  _pane_limit_stale "$pane" || return 0
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "esc to interrupt" && return 0
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "❯" || return 0
  now=$(date +%s); last=$(_reg_get "$id" stalepress)
  [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - last)) -lt "$STALE_PRESS_COOLDOWN" ]] && return 0
  _reg_set "$id" stalepress "$now"
  if _pane_box_has_content "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p -e 2>/dev/null)"; then
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') stale-skip $id: usage limit has reset but the input box is not empty [wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    return 0
  fi
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') stale-resume $id: usage limit has reset; pressing Enter for Claude Code's own continuation [wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  return 0
}

_pane_hard_blocked() {   # pane text -> success iff the session is stuck regardless of turn state.
  # A real limit/spend banner (or lost auth) is up: waiting for a clean turn boundary is wrong,
  # the session is stuck NOW. Auth-failure strings join the same rescue lane as a 429 — a session
  # that WAS working and lost auth is exactly as stuck. "Select login method" stays OUT: that
  # screen appears during an intentional login, not a failure — evacuating out from under an
  # operator mid-login would be wrong (see _pane_login_screen, which owns that screen instead).
  grep -qiE "limit reached|reached your .*limit|out of (usage|credits)|monthly spend limit|hit your .*spend|API Error: 429|Too Many Requests|rate limit(ed| exceeded| reached)?|Invalid API key|Please run /login" <<<"$1"
}

_pane_limit_banner() {   # pane text -> success iff the pane carries Claude Code's CURRENT limit banner (D-3100).
  # THE WORDING MOVED AND THE DETECTOR DID NOT. `_pane_hard_blocked`'s alternation
  # was written against the banners of its day and matches none of the three
  # commonest ones this fleet actually renders. Measured 2026-09-19 against the
  # `isApiErrorMessage` rows on disk across every wrapper HOME — these are the
  # live texts, not remembered ones:
  #
  #   "You've hit your session limit · resets 9:10pm (UTC)"        -> NO MATCH
  #   "You've hit your weekly limit · resets Sep 21, 7am (UTC)"    -> NO MATCH
  #   "You've hit your limit · resets Sep 17, 9pm (UTC)"           -> NO MATCH
  #   "You've hit your monthly spend limit · raise it at claude.ai/..." -> matches,
  #       and only through `monthly spend limit`/`hit your .*spend`
  #
  # So the pane arm has been blind to the session, weekly and bare limits, and
  # every rescue from one of those shapes has had to come from D-2363's
  # transcript fallback instead — which is exactly what the fleet log shows: the
  # one rescue of this shape on 2026-09-19 is logged
  # `auto-rescue <row>: <lane> (blocked) -> gpt [home=…] via=transcript`, i.e. by
  # the fallback, on a pane that had been showing the answer. That fallback is cached for
  # `TRANSCRIPT_ARM_INTERVAL`, needs the banner to still be the NEWEST real row,
  # and stands down entirely over a human's draft — three ways to miss a session
  # the pane was showing the answer on the whole time.
  #
  # A SEPARATE FUNCTION, AND THAT IS D-2364 BEING KEPT RATHER THAN REVERSED.
  # D-2364 ruled that `_pane_hard_blocked` is NOT widened, because `_spawn_settle`
  # turns that same regex into rc 5 ("blocked on the new account") on every
  # landing, and whether Claude Code re-renders the previous assistant message on
  # a `--resume` landing was unmeasured. That ruling still holds and that function
  # is untouched: `_spawn_settle` and `_redrive_after_spawn` keep exactly the
  # vocabulary they had. This predicate has ONE caller —
  # `_session_hard_blocked` — whose two callers are the rescue arm and the strand
  # half, and the rescue arm sits below `SWAP_COOLDOWN`, which a landing has just
  # stamped, so a re-render cannot reach a relocation through it.
  #
  # THE SEPARATOR IS PART OF THE PATTERN, and it is what keeps this from being a
  # prose detector. Every banner in the family is `You've hit your <what> limit ·
  # <what to do>`; requiring the `·` means an assistant SAYING the words in a
  # sentence does not swap its own session out. A wording change that drops the
  # separator fails CLOSED — no keystroke, no relocation, today's behaviour — which
  # is `_pane_limit_stale`'s own rule and the lesson of 2026-09-08's trust dialog.
  #
  # WHAT IT DOES NOT PROTECT is a draft in the input box: this is a pane rung, so
  # it shares `_pane_hard_blocked`'s shipped contract that a VISIBLE banner swaps,
  # draft or not. That is deliberate rather than overlooked — one condition must
  # not have two behaviours depending on which of two equally-visible banners the
  # build happens to render — and it is the transcript arm, not this one, that
  # owes a human's unsent sentence its stand-down.
  grep -qiE "hit your ([a-z0-9]+ )*limit ·" <<<"$1"
}

# ONE MAPPING FROM THE UNDECIDABLE FIELD TO THE OPERATOR'S SENTENCE (#69 review
# round 4, B5). `_tick_undecidable` writes the WORD into `.tickstuck` and into
# swap.log; `_strand_mark` writes the SENTENCE into `.stranded` AND fires the
# SAME sentence at `notify.sh`. This merge lands the marker's second reader:
# `fieldMeasured` carries its reason through `SessionRecord.stranded` into
# `FleetSession.stranded` on the REST and WebSocket fleet payloads, while
# `notify.sh` remains the immediate banner. The future-tense split that stood
# here was true on each parent and became false on their composed tree (D-2422).
#
# The two surfaces must name the same file, or the operator is sent to two
# places and believes the louder one. MEASURED, and this is the defect it
# closes: on a row where the crossing record and the pool tag were both
# unreadable, the stamp said `crosspool` and the strand sentence said the pool
# tag. Two surfaces, two files, one tick.
#
# EVERY SENTENCE PRODUCER GOES THROUGH HERE, and round 4 shipped that as a claim
# rather than a mechanism (#69 review round 4 gate, finding 3). There was a
# SECOND producer: `_tick_strand_undecidable` built `"<word> could not be
# measured…"` by hand, and its two call sites are the tick's EARLY RETURNS — so
# one condition, an unreadable `.project`, wrote two different `.stranded`
# sentences depending on which guard caught it, and the common path was the one
# that named no file. It derives from this function now, which is what makes
# "cannot drift apart" a mechanism.
#
# THE CENSUS IS THE CALL, NOT THE SENTENCE, and the first spelling of this
# paragraph got that wrong in the way this file books twice elsewhere (#69
# review round 5, caught by its own refute pass). It cited
# `grep -n 'could not be computed' ccd/ccd`, which returns exactly ONE line —
# itself — because every arm below ends "so no destination could BE computed",
# never "could NOT be". A self-counting anchor offered as proof, in the same
# file that books the same trap at `_pool_ok`'s census and at `_reg_get`'s
# (`grep -n "counting itself" ccd/ccd`). The census that measures is
# `grep -n '_undecidable_cause "' ccd/ccd | grep -vE '^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*#'`,
# which answers TWO: the tick's late arm and `_tick_strand_undecidable`.
#
# AND THE SCOPE IS UNDECIDABLE-CONDITION SENTENCES, not every `.stranded` line.
# Two other `_strand_mark` call sites exist and neither is a drift: the tick's
# no-target arm passes NO cause and lets `_strand_why` build the pool census,
# and `cmd_swap`'s auto arm writes its own refusal sentence for a condition
# that was DECIDED rather than undecidable. Different questions, different
# answers; this function owns the one where the tick could not say.
# That fold is what the `wrapper` arm exists for — it is the word only the
# early return passes, and without an arm of its own it would have landed in
# `*` and told the operator this build does not name a condition it names.
# `home` and `crosspool` are byte-identical to the sentences that shipped before.
_undecidable_cause() {   # word id project -> the operator-facing sentence for that field
  case "$1" in
    home)      printf '%s' "the home account could not be measured, so no destination could be computed" ;;
    wrapper)   printf '%s' "the row's own account field could not be measured ($REG/$2.wrapper), so no destination could be computed" ;;
    crosspool) printf '%s' "the crossing record could not be read, so no destination could be computed" ;;
    project)   printf '%s' "the project's own registry field could not be read ($REG/$2.project), so no destination could be computed" ;;
    pool)      printf '%s' "the project's pool tag could not be read ($POOLS_DIR/$3), so no destination could be computed" ;;
    classwindow) printf '%s' "the per-class window for the session's class could not be measured on the lanes that could take it ($REG/usage/sweep/latest.json for fable, $LIMITS_DIR/<lane>.json otherwise), so no destination could be computed" ;;
    *)         printf '%s' "the tick declined to decide on a condition this build does not name ($1), so no destination could be computed" ;;
  esac
}

# ── THE MANUAL-SWAP PIN (D-3097) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE DEFECT, MEASURED. `ccd swap` moves `wrapper` and never touches `home`, and
# the affinity arm's whole job is "return home the instant home has room again".
# So every manual swap is REVERSED by the first tick past `SWAP_COOLDOWN`, and
# the operator watches their own choice evaporate 15 minutes later. From this
# fleet's `$REG/swap.log`, 2026-09-19, one session and one afternoon:
#
#   14:30:22 swap      <row>: <home-lane> -> gpt   (the operator)
#   15:25:47 auto-home <row>: gpt -> <home-lane>   (the tick)
#   15:36:54 swap      <row>: <home-lane> -> gpt   (the operator, again)
#   15:51:56 auto-home <row>: gpt -> <home-lane>   (15m 02s later — one tick
#                                                   past SWAP_COOLDOWN)
#   16:28:53 swap      <row>: <home-lane> -> gpt   (the operator, a third time)
#   17:01:11 auto-home <row>: gpt -> <home-lane>
#
# `ccd prefer` is the verb that moves `home`, and it is SHELL-ONLY — no
# exec-whitelist entry, no `CCD_ARGV` builder, so no PWA tap can reach it (its
# own header says so). An operator on a phone therefore had no way at all to make
# a swap stick.
#
# A PIN, NOT A RE-HOME (operator ruling, 2026-09-19). Rewriting `.home` to the
# target would also make the session RETURN to that account after every future
# rescue, and would destroy the home it had with no way back. The pin is the
# conservative half of that: `$REG/<id>.swappin` names the account an operator
# chose BY HAND, the affinity arm stands down while the row is still sitting on
# it, and `home` is untouched — so the moment anything else moves the row (a
# limit rescue, a pool retag, another swap) the pin stops naming the current
# account and ordinary affinity resumes from wherever the row now is.
#
# THE RESCUE ARM IS NOT GATED BY IT, and that is the whole reason the pin is
# checked where it is rather than at the top of the tick. A hard-blocked session
# must still be evacuated: a pin is a preference about a HEALTHY session's
# placement, never a reason to leave a limited one wedged. `_auto_swap_check`'s
# own header makes the same split for the hold file, which sits on the next line.
#
# NO EPOCH, SO NO EXPIRY AND NO ARITHMETIC. The file holds one account name. A
# pin does not time out, because "the operator picked this account" does not
# become less true after an hour; it ends when the row is no longer on that
# account, which is a MEASUREMENT (`_swap_pin_tick`) rather than a clock.
_swap_pin_tick() {   # id measured-wrapper — THE PIN FOLLOWS THE TRUTH (D-3099)
  # `_strand_mark`/`_compact_note`'s rule, applied to a marker that names an
  # ACCOUNT rather than a reason: a pin still on disk after the row has moved
  # off the pinned account is a claim about the present that is no longer true,
  # and the next reader would have to re-derive that for itself.
  #
  # AN UNREADABLE PIN IS NOT DELETED. `_crosspool_tick`'s lesson, booked in
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s own comments: deleting a deliberate operator marker on
  # a read that FAILED is a destructive act on a false reason. Absent is not
  # unreadable; only a pin this tick could read and that names another account
  # is removed.
  local id="$1" w="$2" pin rc
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.swappin" ]] || return 0
  pin=$(_reg_read "$id" swappin); rc=$?
  [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || return 0
  [[ "$pin" == "$w" ]] && return 0
  rm -f -- "$REG/$id.swappin"
  rm -f -- "$REG/$id.swappinnote"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') pin-ended $id: pinned to $pin, now on $w" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}

_swap_pinned() {   # id measured-wrapper -> 0 iff a manual-swap pin stands for THIS account (D-3097)
  # DOUBT READS AS PINNED, which is the opposite fail direction from most of this
  # file and is taken from the line this predicate sits beside: the hold file is
  # read with `-e` not `-f` precisely so that "an unreadable hold defers too".
  # Both answers are about the same question — may this tick relocate a healthy
  # session for cosmetic reasons — and the cost of standing down wrongly is one
  # session left where it already was, against an operator's deliberate choice
  # being undone by a file nobody could read.
  local pin rc
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.swappin" ]] || return 1
  pin=$(_reg_read "$1" swappin); rc=$?
  [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || return 0
  [[ "$pin" == "$2" ]]
}

_auto_swap_check() {   # id — called each supervise tick. HOME-account affinity:
  #   stay on your home account; leave only when home is at the rate ceiling (no headroom); return
  #   home the instant it has room again. A hard-blocked session (limit/spend banner up, or auth
  #   lost) swaps immediately; any other move waits for a clean turn boundary.
  local id="$1" wrapper home now last pane target hard_blocked="" blocked bts
  local project pps prc prjrc crossed="" ctrc wrc hrc strc stuck="" new aff_verb=auto-home
  # §6.3: a relocation is a restart that re-drives, and the only thing standing
  # between it and a running turn is the `esc to interrupt` match further down.
  #
  # AND IT LEAVES A TRACE, because this guard is the first statement after the
  # locals: everything this tick does — the rescue AND `_tick_strand_undecidable`,
  # whose only call sites are below — is skipped here, so without the note a
  # wedged session on a narrow pane is neither rescued nor marked, and nothing
  # anywhere says why. `_pane_narrow_note` carries the floor; see its header.
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { _pane_narrow_note "$id" swap; return 0; }
  # A TICK THAT CANNOT MEASURE THIS ROW'S CURRENT ACCOUNT DECIDES NOTHING
  # (C1, D-1992). `_reg_get` folds ABSENT, UNREADABLE and EMPTY into one `""`,
  # and EVERY decision below consumes this value: `_pool_ok "$wrapper"`, the
  # affinity arm's verb, `_swap_target`'s per-account questions, the strand
  # marker's account. Acting on `""` is how the crossing expirer came to log
  # `moved off <acct>` for a move nothing observed — but that was one symptom,
  # not the defect, and guarding the expirer alone left every other consumer
  # reading the same fold. So the measurement happens ONCE, here, at the only
  # point where all of them are still downstream.
  #
  # `-n` AS WELL AS rc 0, because rc alone is not enough: a zero-byte `.wrapper`
  # is READ SUCCESSFULLY and says nothing, so an rc-only guard passes it
  # straight through to the arms that treat `""` as an account. Read failure
  # and read-nothing are different conditions with the same remedy here.
  #
  # Skipping the tick costs nothing: it is idempotent and runs every 5 seconds,
  # so a transient failure is retried almost immediately, and a permanent one
  # is a broken row that an operator must fix — not one this tick should keep
  # making moves about.
  wrapper=$(_reg_read "$id" wrapper); wrc=$?
  if [[ "$wrc" -ne 0 || -z "$wrapper" ]]; then
    # THE ONE VALUE THIS TICK GENUINELY CANNOT PROCEED WITHOUT — said with the
    # measurement, as the #67 review asked, rather than assumed. `cur` is
    # `_swap_target`'s first input, the candidate loop ranks against it, and
    # `_strand_mark`/`_strand_why` name it in every sentence they write. With
    # no current account there is no rescue to compute for anyone.
    #
    # BUT IT DOES NOT LEAVE SILENTLY. `tick-undecidable` goes to `swap.log`,
    # and NOTHING in this product reads that file — measured:
    # `grep -rn 'swap\.log' server/src agent/src shared pwa/src` returns three
    # hits and all three are COMMENTS. So a row in this state would otherwise
    # look as though it had simply stopped working. `_strand_mark` writes
    # `$REG/<id>.stranded` and fires `notify.sh`: the banner is immediate, and
    # the server's fail-shut `SessionRecord.stranded` reader now carries the
    # same reason on both fleet payloads. D-1995's "standing still is SAID" is
    # only true if something says it; both current paths do.
    _tick_undecidable "$id" wrapper
    _tick_strand_undecidable "$id" wrapper
    return 0
  fi
  # HOME IS NOT FATAL, and this is the #67 review's R1 — the regression this
  # commit exists to remove. The guard that stood here returned from the WHOLE
  # tick, and everything below it — `_swap_target` at the pool loop,
  # `_strand_mark`, the `auto-rescue` dispatch — is below it. So a session
  # whose `.home` merely became unreadable was never evacuated when it hit a
  # limit: every five seconds, forever, with no marker and no banner. On main
  # `_home_for` fell back to a valid account and the rescue was entirely
  # sound, so this traded an INERT defect (C1's own, embargoed) for a LIVE one
  # on every session on the box. A fix for a dormant fault must not open a
  # live one in the most important behaviour `ccd` has.
  #
  # What an unmeasured `home` genuinely costs is the CROSSING half and only
  # that: the crossing tick below is skipped (no marker is deleted off a read
  # that failed — C1's whole point), the pool re-seed is skipped, and
  # `_swap_target` is told so explicitly through `hrc` rather than through an
  # empty string it would mistake for an account.
  home=$(_home_measured "$id"); hrc=$?
  # SAID BY THE VERDICT BELOW, NOT HERE (#69 review round 3, R1). This line
  # used to call `_tick_undecidable "$id" home` itself. Four callers each
  # writing the stamp for their own field, with a fifth clearing it in the
  # middle, is what defeated the debounce; the tick now takes ONE verdict once
  # every read has answered, and this one only records the answer.
  if [[ "$hrc" -eq 2 ]]; then home=""; else hrc=0; fi
  # THE PIN IS RECONCILED HERE (D-3099), at the first line where this row's
  # account has been MEASURED and before any gate below can return: a pin that
  # no longer names `$wrapper` is ended immediately rather than at whichever
  # later tick happens to reach the affinity arm, so `pin-ended` lands beside
  # the `auto-rescue` that caused it instead of fifteen minutes after it.
  _swap_pin_tick "$id" "$wrapper"
  # ── POOLS (§5.5.4) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # THIS READ happens once, here, and BEFORE the cooldown gates below — that
  # is not the same claim as "once per tick": `_project_pool_state` runs
  # again inside `_swap_target` below, which re-reads `project`/`pps` for its
  # own must-leave force and its two crossing checks, and twice more on a
  # strand tick (`_strand_mark` and `_strand_why` each re-read it too) — up to
  # four calls on the tick's worst path, one on its best. The re-seed is not
  # a relocation — nothing restarts, no transcript moves — so it must not
  # wait 900 s behind a gate that exists to stop swap storms, and it is
  # idempotent the moment `.home` is back in pool.
  # THE PROJECT TAG'S OWN READ IS MEASURED (#69 review round 3, R3). `_reg_get`
  # folds ABSENT, EMPTY and UNREADABLE into `""`, and `_project_pool_state`
  # answers `untagged` for `""` — deliberately, for the pre-2026-07-28 rows
  # that carry no `.project` at all. But `untagged` is the ONE state `_pool_ok`
  # admits every account under, so an UNREADABLE `.project` silently LIFTED the
  # pool constraint: measured, with `demo` tagged `pool-a` and `.project` a
  # FIFO, the next limit block wrote `auto-rescue claude-demo: claude (blocked)
  # -> claude-b` and dispatched it, where the measured tag refuses every
  # out-of-pool candidate and strands by name. Worse, and also measured: the
  # same fold reaches `_crosspool_tick`, whose guard sees the folded `untagged`
  # rather than the unreadable it is, DELETES a deliberate crossing marker and
  # logs `crosspool-ended <id>: project pool is now untagged` — a destructive
  # act on a false reason. ABSENT and EMPTY still mean untagged; only rc 2
  # stops the tick.
  #
  # GATED ON `_pool_untaggable`, AND THAT GATE IS THE WHOLE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN
  # THIS AND A REGRESSION. `$POOLS_DIR` has exactly one `mkdir` in the tree
  # (`grep -n 'mkdir -p -- "$POOLS_DIR"' ccd/ccd | grep -vE '^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*#'`
  # — the `-v` filter is what stops this citation counting itself, and naming
  # `$POOLS_DIR` is what stops the bare `mkdir -p --` spelling this comment
  # carried until round 5 from also matching the two `_LC_DIR` mkdirs, which are
  # not pools paths at all: four hits for a claim about one. Round 3's spelling
  # before that matched ONLY ITS OWN CITATION — one hit, the comment quoting it,
  # measured with GNU grep at round 3's own tree. Round 5 first wrote "matched
  # nothing at all, zero hits" here, which is a prose REGRESSION over round 4's
  # correct wording and self-refuting on this tree, because re-quoting the
  # spelling is itself a match. The zero came from a review artifact rather than
  # from a re-measurement, and that artifact's shell resolves `grep` to a shim
  # that mishandles a literal `$` mid-pattern — so this anchor has been wrong in
  # two ways, counting itself and over-matching, and the third way was an
  # inherited measurement), inside `cmd_project_pool`, so
  # on every box that has never tagged a project the directory does not exist
  # and `_project_pool_state` answers `untagged` for EVERY project name — the
  # unmeasured field cannot change the answer, no constraint is being lifted,
  # and the limit rescue is fully sound. Refusing there would kill the rescue
  # on every un-adopted box to close a hole that box does not have, which is
  # the #67 R1 shape this function's own header forbids. Measured both ways:
  # with `pools/` absent and `.project` a FIFO the rescue still fires and
  # dispatches; with `pools/` present it stands still and says so.
  #
  # ABOVE THE VERDICT, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS AN EARLY RETURN. `_tick_decided`
  # clears the stamp when the row decides; a `_tick_undecidable` reported below
  # it would have no debounce at all. Wherever the verdict moves, this guard
  # stays above it.
  project=$(_reg_read "$id" project); prjrc=$?
  if [[ "$prjrc" -eq 2 ]] && ! _pool_untaggable; then
    _tick_undecidable "$id" project
    _tick_strand_undecidable "$id" project "$wrapper"
    return 0
  fi
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  # THREE OUTCOMES, NOT TWO (C1, D-1988). `_crosspool_tick` answers rc 2 —
  # "nobody can say" — when an input it would have ended the crossing on could
  # not be measured. `&& crossed=1` reads that as "no crossing stands", which
  # is the one reading that is unsafe: the re-seed below would then clobber
  # `.home` off exactly the undecidable state the tick just refused to act on,
  # finishing by hand the destructive act the tick declined. So the code is
  # kept and the RE-SEED below is gated on the POSITIVE answers only.
  #
  # ONE DECISION, NOT TWO — corrected at review, because the sentence above
  # used to say "the two decisions below" and the second one is not gated.
  # `aff_verb` (`grep -n 'aff_verb=auto-pool' ccd/ccd`) still reads `crossed`,
  # so rc 2 leaves it on the two-way path. That is deliberate and it is not the
  # same kind of decision: the re-seed CHANGES the row, while `aff_verb` only
  # LABELS a relocation that is happening anyway because `$wrapper` failed the
  # pool rule — which is true whether or not a crossing could be read. Gating
  # it too would add a guard no case in this tree can red, and an unpinned
  # guard is what this wave is trying to stop shipping.
  # SKIPPED ENTIRELY on an unmeasured `home`: `_crosspool_tick`'s §5.7.2
  # account clause is "still on the account it crossed TO", and with no home
  # to compare it would answer "moved off" for a row it never measured —
  # deleting the marker, which is exactly the act C1 exists to prevent.
  ctrc=2
  [[ "$hrc" -eq 0 ]] && { _crosspool_tick "$id" "$wrapper" "$home" "$pps"; ctrc=$?; }
  # THE ROW'S VERDICT IS WHAT DECIDES THE VOICE, not any one read — and it is
  # taken BELOW, once the fourth answer exists. The verdict stood HERE with
  # three answers (`wrc`, `hrc`, `ctrc`) and a comment claiming it was "the
  # only place that has all three"; round 2 then added a FOURTH undecidable
  # source — the project's pool tag — 108 lines below it, and the two-way
  # `else _tick_decided` cleared the stamp every tick before that fourth source
  # re-wrote it. Measured on a fixture row with an unreadable tag and a QUIET
  # pane: 4 ticks, 4 `tick-undecidable` lines, 4 `_reg_set` cycles and 4 `rm`s,
  # into an unrotated `swap.log`. The sentence naming the invariant and the
  # line breaking it shipped in one commit (#69 review round 3, R1).
  [[ "$ctrc" -eq 0 ]] && crossed=1
  # ONE BIT, TWO DECISIONS WITH DIFFERENT SUBJECTS — DELIBERATE, review round
  # 1 (Minor 2). `_swap_target` asks `cross_cur`/`cross_home` SEPARATELY,
  # because it answers a per-account question — "may THIS account serve as
  # cur, or as home?" — and its own comment says so ("a marker written for
  # `wrapper` cannot admit it"). This tick asks a different question: "is
  # THIS ROW under a deliberate crossing AT ALL, for any reason?" — so folding
  # `wrapper` and `home` into one `crossed` flag here is right for what this
  # call site decides, not an accidental narrowing of the distinction
  # `_crosspool_valid` offers.
  #
  # THE COST, MEASURED, NOT ASSUMED: a `swap`-style marker recorded on
  # `wrapper` also suppresses the re-seed of `home` even when `home` is a
  # DIFFERENT, unrelated wrong-pool account that the marker never named —
  # `crossed` is set the moment EITHER argument matches. `_swap_target`
  # separately refuses that same wrong-pool `home` as a destination (its own
  # `cross_home` check asks about `home` alone and finds no match), so the
  # row is left with a home it can neither return to nor have fixed for as
  # long as the crossing stands — repaired on the first tick after
  # `_crosspool_tick` ends it. Splitting the flag would close this gap, but
  # it would open the hazard the crossing marker exists to prevent: an
  # automatic re-seed silently overwriting the account an operator's `swap
  # --cross-pool` deliberately put the session on. The fold stays.
  # `ccd-auto-swap-pool.test.ts`'s "DELIBERATE FOLD" case pins the cost.
  # HOME RE-SEED (ruling 5). A retag makes a pinned wrong-pool home a lie: the
  # affinity arm would keep pulling the session back onto it for ever. NOT
  # `_ws_seed_home` — that never clobbers, and clobbering is the entire point.
  # A row with no `.home` file at all (pre-2026-07-28) gets one written here
  # for the first time.
  # `-eq 1` rather than `-z "$crossed"`: re-seed only on a MEASURED "no crossing
  # stands". On rc 2 both are false, so the row is left exactly as it is until
  # the input becomes readable again — the same standing-still the undecidable
  # project tag already produces one level down.
  if [[ "$ctrc" -eq 1 ]]; then
    _pool_ok "$home" "$pps"; prc=$?
    if [[ "$prc" -eq 1 ]]; then
      new=$(_ws_least_loaded "$project")
      if [[ -n "$new" && "$new" != "$home" ]]; then
        _reg_set "$id" home "$new"
        _lc_done rehome "$id" "" meas.from "$home" meas.home "$new" \
          meas.reason pool meas.pool "${pps#named }"
        echo "$(date '+%F %T') rehome $id: home $home -> $new [pool=${pps#named }]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
        home="$new"
      fi
    fi
  fi
  # THE AFFINITY ARM'S LOG VERB, decided here because `wrapper` cannot change
  # inside a tick: when the CURRENT account is the thing that failed the pool
  # rule, the move's cause was a retag and the line must say so. `[[ "$prc"
  # -eq 1 ]]` rather than `-ne 0` is CORRECT-BY-CONSTRUCTION, not a guarded
  # branch worth a fixture of its own: `_swap_target` already refuses on rc 2
  # (`grep -n 'prc" -eq 2' ccd/ccd` — quoted by anchor, not by text, because
  # the round that changed `return 0` to `return 2` there left this comment
  # quoting the deleted line verbatim; #69 review round 3), and
  # the affinity arm below is unreachable without a non-empty `$target` — so
  # `prc=2` can never reach this line at all, and writing `-ne 0` here would
  # cover a case that cannot occur rather than change what the line does.
  _pool_ok "$wrapper" "$pps"; prc=$?
  [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -z "$crossed" ]] && aff_verb=auto-pool
  # ── THE TICK'S VERDICT (D-1995), IN ONE PLACE, ONCE EVERY READ HAS ANSWERED ─
  # Here rather than 60 lines up because `prc` — the project's pool tag against
  # THIS row's account — is the fourth answer, and a verdict that cannot see it
  # is the storm above. No `return` sits between the reads and this line, so
  # every tick that gets past the `wrapper` guard reaches it.
  #
  # SET ONLY. The CLEAR is below the `_swap_target` call, because "the row
  # decided" is not knowable until it has: `_swap_target` re-reads the crossing
  # record and can answer 2 off a marker that changed since `_crosspool_tick`
  # read it. Clearing here and letting that arm re-write is precisely the
  # defect being removed. The cooldown returns between here and there touch
  # neither — a tick that declines to act has neither decided nor failed to,
  # and leaving the stamp alone is the conservative side: the cost is a
  # suppressed line for a REPEAT of the SAME condition inside one cooldown,
  # never a suppressed new one, because the debounce compares the field.
  #
  # PRECEDENCE, NOT A SET: the deepest read that failed is the one an operator
  # must fix first, and `home` failing forces `ctrc=2` on the line above, so
  # naming `crosspool` there would send them to the wrong file.
  [[ "$hrc"  -eq 2 ]] && stuck=home
  [[ -z "$stuck" && "$ctrc" -eq 2 ]] && stuck=crosspool
  [[ -z "$stuck" && "$prc"  -eq 2 ]] && stuck=pool
  [[ -n "$stuck" ]] && _tick_undecidable "$id" "$stuck"
  # ── THE COOLDOWN GATES CLEAR, AND THE PANE GATE DOES NOT (#69 round 4, B2) ──
  # Round 2 stormed because the clear ran BEFORE the setter. Round 3 moved the
  # clear to the bottom of the tick, and so INVERTED the defect: a fault that
  # ENDS while one of these gates holds leaves a stale stamp standing, and the
  # debounce then swallows the next genuine episode of that same field for as
  # long as the gate holds. Measured: tag breaks (one line), tag fixed, row
  # swaps, tag breaks again inside `SWAP_COOLDOWN` — second episode silent.
  #
  # A ROW INSIDE THESE GATES DECIDED. A fresh `lastswap` means it swapped; a
  # fresh `swapblocked` means it tried and was refused. Both are decisions, so
  # clearing here cannot be a fabricated clear — and it cannot storm either,
  # because the verdict has already run ABOVE this line and `$stuck` is empty
  # exactly when nothing needs saying. That ordering is the invariant: a clear
  # may never precede a setter within one tick.
  #
  # THE PANE GATE IS DELIBERATELY NOT GIVEN THIS. An empty capture is a pane
  # nobody could read — nothing was measured about that row, so it neither
  # decided nor failed to, and its stamp must stand. It is also the one gate
  # here that can hold indefinitely, which is exactly why it must not be the one
  # that clears on a guess. Pinned in both directions — and that sentence was
  # itself a false claim about a mechanism until round 5 (#69 review round 4
  # gate, finding 1). Of the two clears this paragraph argues for, only the
  # `lastswap` one had a case: replacing the `swapblocked` clear with `:` left
  # the whole ccd+pools set green, on the arm that carries the REFUSAL — the one
  # `_swap_refuse` reaches after DELETING `.lastswap`, so it is the only gate
  # holding for the whole `SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN`. Both clears and the pane gate's
  # deliberate silence are cases in `ccd-crosspool.test.ts` now.
  now=$(date +%s); last=$(_reg_get "$id" lastswap)
  if [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - last)) -lt "$SWAP_COOLDOWN" ]]; then
    [[ -z "$stuck" ]] && _tick_decided "$id"
    return 0
  fi
  # A refused swap is not retried on the 5-second supervise tick. cmd_swap writes
  # $REG/<id>.swapblocked as "<epoch> <reason>" when it cannot carry the conversation
  # (spec §2.4); without this gate one refusal would produce 720 banners an hour, each
  # one a notify.sh call and a swap.log line. The epoch is VALIDATED as digits rather
  # than trusted: ccd runs under `set -u`, where `$(( now - garbage ))` on a hand-edited
  # or half-written field emits an unbound-variable line on every single tick.
  blocked=$(_reg_get "$id" swapblocked); bts="${blocked%% *}"
  if [[ "$bts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - bts)) -lt "$SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN" ]]; then
    [[ -z "$stuck" ]] && _tick_decided "$id"   # a refused swap is a decision too — see above
    return 0
  fi
  pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8)
  # THE VERDICT BEFORE THE BLANK-PANE RETURN (D-2363): a blank capture used to
  # return here, ahead of any classifier, and a limited session with a blank
  # pane was never rescued. `_session_hard_blocked` reads the transcript for
  # that case; a blank pane that is NOT blocked still stands still, as before.
  # THE VERDICT'S OWN COST, DISCLOSED (final review finding 3, 2026-09-10;
  # mitigated by D-2444). Replacing `_pane_hard_blocked` with
  # `_session_hard_blocked` here added a price. In a FIXTURE HOME (roster via
  # `shared/generate.mjs`, 200 iterations x 3 runs, box load 9-32), the old
  # uncached path measured `_pane_hard_blocked` alone at ~2.6-2.9 ms/call and
  # `_session_hard_blocked` at ~30-35 ms/call — roughly +27-32 ms, an order of
  # magnitude (~11-13x), mostly in `_transcript_path`. D-2444 now caches both
  # verdicts for 30s, so an IDLE, HEALTHY row pays that full transcript scan at
  # most once per interval rather than on every 5s tick. Intervening calls still
  # pay the registry/cache read; a positive also re-measures the draft. The
  # `_tick_strand_undecidable` block at its own call site below discloses that
  # its same-cadence call shares this cache rather than adding another scan.
  _session_hard_blocked "$id" "$pane" && hard_blocked=1
  [[ -n "$pane" || -n "$hard_blocked" ]] || return 0
  # (The Fable-unavailable override that routed any session to gpt was removed
  #  2026-07-26 and stays removed. A lane that is not home-able re-entered the
  #  rotation on 2026-09-07 through `_default_pool`, as an ordinary candidate
  #  ranked by its own telemetry — never again as a model-specific rescue path.)
  # Classify BEFORE picking a target, and pass the verdict in as `force`: a
  # hard block (limit/spend banner, OR lost auth) must win over telemetry that
  # says the wrapper is fine. Rate limits write cc-limits, so a 429 already
  # makes _avail fail on its own — but auth loss writes NO telemetry (nothing
  # marks cc-limits on a 401), so on a session sitting on its home account
  # (the fleet's steady state) `_swap_target`'s own "home is fine: stay"
  # shortcut would return empty before the auth patterns are ever reached,
  # and the rescue below would never fire. `force` skips straight to the
  # must-leave candidate loop so a hard block always finds a destination when
  # one exists, regardless of what telemetry has to say about cur/home.
  # A HEALTHY PANE IS NOT STRANDED. `_strand_clear` tests `-e` first, so the
  # steady state of a healthy session writes nothing at all.
  [[ -n "$hard_blocked" ]] || _strand_clear "$id"
  target=$(_swap_target "$id" "$wrapper" "$home" "$hard_blocked" "$hrc"); strc=$?
  # rc 6 IS A DECISION, handled BEFORE the `-ge 2` undecidable guard (routing
  # spec §5.4): a destination chosen one rung DOWN because every lane was
  # measured-unservable at the intended class. Put after that guard it would be
  # read as a condition nobody could decide, strand the row with the word
  # `rc6`, and throw away a destination that was measured. The rung is
  # re-derived here (`_class_below` is deterministic and `_swap_target` writes
  # nothing), the move then proceeds exactly as an rc 0 move would, and the
  # stamp and journal row are written by the ONE writer — AT THE DISPATCH, not
  # here (final review finding 4; `_route_degrade_at_move`'s header carries the
  # argument). All this arm does is REMEMBER the rung, because between here and
  # the two `_dispatch_swap` calls sit six gates that can still decline: the
  # `_avail "$target"` re-measurement, and on the affinity path the hold file,
  # the mid-turn test, the at-a-prompt test, the `status == idle` test and
  # `SWAP_CEIL_QUIET`. `dto` empty means there is nothing to stamp, which is
  # every non-rc-6 tick.
  #
  # AND THE STAMP IS A WRITE THAT CAN FAIL (ruling S3-R5, fix round 1), which is
  # why `_route_degrade_at_move` is a GUARD at each dispatch site rather than a
  # fire-and-forget call. The rc used to be discarded, so a registry write that
  # did not land dispatched the session anyway: it would arrive on a lane chosen
  # for the rung BELOW while the record still said the intended class, and
  # `_spawn_start` would compose `--model $dfrom` on a lane just measured unable
  # to serve it, with nothing in any channel saying why. On a failed stamp the
  # honest move is not to move: say so in one swap.log line and stand still. The
  # next tick re-measures and retries — `_route_degrade`'s standing-state
  # debounce reads the FIELD, and nothing was written, so it is unaffected.
  local dfrom="" dto=""
  if [[ "$strc" -eq 6 ]]; then
    dfrom=$(_route_peek "$id" class); dto=$(_class_below "$dfrom")
    strc=0
  fi
  if [[ "$strc" -ge 2 ]]; then
    # NOBODY CAN SAY — which is NOT "no destination exists". Stranding here
    # would be a fabricated positive claim (the #67 review's S5); returning
    # silently would be the fabricated silence R1 is about. So: say it, in the
    # one place a ccrc surface can see, with a cause that names the condition
    # rather than borrowing `_strand_why`'s "no account in pool X can take it",
    # which would be flatly false.
    #
    # WHICH undecidable — READ, not inferred, and this is #69 round 4's B5.
    # TRUE ON THE `$stuck`-EMPTY PATH, AND THE OTHER PATH IS DISCLOSED (#69
    # review round 4 gate). When the verdict above already named a condition,
    # `uword` is that word — inferred from `hrc`/`ctrc`/`prc`, measurements of
    # EARLIER moments in the same tick, not from the refusal that just happened.
    # The tail is a STALE word, never a fabricated one: the condition it names
    # was genuinely measured undecidable on this tick, which is the difference
    # from the pre-#69 behaviour that named a `.crosspool` the fixture did not
    # have. Two surfaces still agree, which is what B5 was for. Closing the
    # residue means the verdict and the refusal exchanging their words, and no
    # measured case asks for that yet.
    # The sentence that stood here said "measured, not guessed" and was false:
    # `_swap_target` refuses for FOUR conditions, not three, and NONE of those
    # refusals keys on `hrc` — it tests `hrc` only to pick the home arm
    # (`grep -n 'hrc" -eq 0' ccd/ccd`) — and the caller re-derived the word from
    # `hrc` and `prc`, two measurements of two DIFFERENT moments than the one
    # that refused. So a
    # `.project` that turned unreadable inside the tick, which is precisely the
    # race round 3 added the inner read for, stranded the row naming
    # `.crosspool` — a file that in the fixture did not even exist — and stamped
    # the debounce `crosspool`, swallowing the next genuine crossing episode too.
    # Round 3 closed that exact shape for `hrc` and opened it for `project` in
    # the same commit.
    #
    # THE EXIT CODE CARRIES IT, and the other two channels were measured and
    # rejected rather than dismissed: a well-known variable is lost because this
    # call site is a command substitution, i.e. a subshell; a second stdout
    # token puts a cause word where a destination lives and reds the four
    # existing `.toBe('rc=2')` pins. rc is already this function's vocabulary,
    # it survives the subshell, and `_swap_target` has exactly ONE call site in
    # this file — so widening it costs one `case`. The CROSSING keeps rc 2, so
    # every existing rc-2 assertion stays green by construction. The hazard it
    # buys is a future `-eq 2` written from memory; `-ge 2` above and the `*`
    # arm below make that loud instead of silent.
    local uword
    if [[ -n "$stuck" ]]; then
      # THE VERDICT ALREADY SPOKE THIS TICK. Taking its word — rather than a
      # second one — is the anti-storm gate, and it is now the same `if` that
      # picks the word, so the two cannot be separated by a later edit.
      uword="$stuck"
    else
      case "$strc" in
        2) uword=crosspool ;;   # the crossing record, asked about cur and about home
        3) uword=project ;;     # the row's own registry field, re-read at the inner seam
        4) uword=pool ;;        # the pool rule against cur — the tag under `$POOLS_DIR`
        5) uword=classwindow ;;   # the class window — the sweep's share for fable, the seven-day figure otherwise — on the lanes that could take the session
        *) uword="rc$strc" ;;   # deliberately unreachable: the next code added upstream
      esac
      _tick_undecidable "$id" "$uword"
    fi
    # ONE WORD, BOTH SURFACES. `.tickstuck`/swap.log and `.stranded`/the banner
    # now derive from the same `$uword`, so they cannot name two different files
    # for one tick — which, measured, is what they did on a double fault.
    [[ -n "$hard_blocked" ]] \
      && _strand_mark "$id" "$wrapper" "$project" "$(_undecidable_cause "$uword" "$id" "$project")"
    return 0
  fi
  # THE ROW DECIDED — the only clear, and BOTH halves of its condition are
  # load-bearing. It is here because this is the first line at which
  # `_swap_target` has answered; it is gated on `$stuck` because answering is
  # not the same as measuring. An `hrc` 2 row still reaches this line with a
  # perfectly good destination — `_swap_target` skips the home arm and ranks
  # the pool loop instead — so an unguarded clear here retracts, every tick,
  # the stamp the verdict just wrote, and the next tick says it again: five
  # lines from five ticks. MEASURED, by `ccd-crosspool.test.ts`'s own S5 case
  # ("the debounce holds THROUGH THE TICK"), which went red on exactly that
  # while this fix was being written — the storm being removed, reintroduced
  # 100 lines below its own removal. `_tick_decided` is a `rm -f` behind its
  # own `-e`, so a healthy row writes nothing at all.
  [[ -z "$stuck" ]] && _tick_decided "$id"
  # RULING 6, AND THE LINE THAT USED TO SIT HERE IS WHY. `|| return 0`
  # discarded the one fact worth keeping: a hard-blocked session with nowhere
  # to go, retried every five seconds with no marker, no log line and no
  # cooldown stamp. It fires for an UNTAGGED project too — every account at the
  # ceiling reaches exactly this state — which is the pre-existing silent
  # strand made loud, not a new behaviour for tagged projects only.
  if [[ -z "$target" || "$target" == "$wrapper" ]]; then
    [[ -z "$target" && -n "$hard_blocked" ]] && _strand_mark "$id" "$wrapper" "$project"
    return 0
  fi
  # A destination exists: whatever the pane says, this row is not stranded.
  _strand_clear "$id"
  _avail "$target" || return 0
  # Hard-blocked (a real limit/spend banner is up, or auth was lost): the session is stuck
  # anyway -> swap now. The 429/rate-limit patterns also catch an overflow lane hitting
  # its own backend's usage cap (the gpt lane's ChatGPT-sub Codex cap surfaces as "API Error:
  # 429 ..."). When the exhausted account is one that is NOT home-able, write a
  # cc-limits/<account>.json exclusion so OTHER sessions stop rotating into it until the 5h
  # window rolls over: a non-home-able lane is an external backend with no Anthropic-style
  # telemetry of its own (see the header), so this is its only "pool is full" signal. Home-able
  # accounts report for themselves and must not have a synthetic exclusion written over the
  # sample statusline-command.sh maintains.
  #
  # CAVEAT, stated rather than fixed (2026-09-07): `ccgpt-usage`'s 20-minute
  # timer rewrites `gpt.json` UNCONDITIONALLY with whatever the Codex backend
  # reports, so on this fleet the exclusion this writes holds at most until
  # that next poll overwrites it — not until the 5h window this comment
  # describes actually rolls over. `$REG/<w>-disabled`, the kill-switch, is the
  # only durable brake on a non-home-able lane; a code fix (skip the timer's
  # write while an exclusion is fresh, or fold the two writers into one) is
  # deferred.
  if [[ -n "$hard_blocked" ]]; then
    # THE DEGRADE STAMP, AT THE MOVE (see `_route_degrade_at_move`). First in
    # the block, so a stamp that cannot be written costs neither the gpt
    # exclusion, nor `lastswap`, nor a swap.log line claiming a rescue.
    _route_degrade_at_move "$id" "$dfrom" "$dto" || return 0
    # _is_valid_wrapper first, so a stale registry `wrapper` — an account since
    # dropped from the roster — cannot name the file this writes.
    # BACKEND, not placement: this row is the Codex lane's only "pool is full"
    # signal, and it must survive that lane becoming placeable.
    _is_valid_wrapper "$wrapper" && ! _is_anthropic_backend "$wrapper" \
      && printf '{"five":100,"seven":0,"ts":%s}' "$now" > "$LIMITS_DIR/$wrapper.json"
    _reg_set "$id" lastswap "$now"
    # `via=` NAMES THE DETECTOR THAT ACTUALLY FIRED (D-3101) — read off the
    # verdict rather than recomputed from a classifier that cannot see all three
    # rungs. `pane` stays silent, so every line this fleet's log already carries
    # keeps its exact shape; `via=transcript` is unchanged; `via=banner` is the
    # new word, and an operator seeing it knows the pane said so in this year's
    # wording rather than the transcript having been scanned for it.
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') auto-rescue $id: $wrapper (blocked) -> $target [home=$home]$([[ "${HARD_BLOCK_VIA:-}" == pane ]] || printf ' via=%s' "${HARD_BLOCK_VIA:-unknown}")" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    _dispatch_swap "$id" "$target"
    return 0
  fi
  # WAVE 3 §3.3, AND THE PLACEMENT IS THE WHOLE RULING. Everything from here
  # down is the AFFINITY path: returning home, or leaving because home hit
  # SWAP_CEILING. It relocates a session for reasons that are, from a running
  # wave's point of view, cosmetic — and a relocation restarts the session and
  # copies its transcript to another account. A mid-wave worker must not have
  # that happen to it because telemetry drifted.
  #
  # AHEAD OF THE RESCUE BRANCH THIS WOULD STRAND A WEDGED WAVE: a held worker
  # sitting on a rate-limited account would never be evacuated, and a hold is
  # exactly what stops a human noticing quickly. Cosmetic relocation yes,
  # rescue no. The rescue arm above is untouched and must stay untouched.
  #
  # THE MANUAL-SWAP PIN, IMMEDIATELY ABOVE THE HOLD (D-3097, D-3098). Same
  # placement argument, one sentence longer: everything below this line is the
  # cosmetic half, and a pin says an operator has already answered the question
  # this half exists to ask. The rescue arm above is untouched — a pinned session
  # that hits a limit is still evacuated, and `_swap_pin_tick` ends the pin on the
  # next tick because the row is no longer on the pinned account.
  #
  # SAID, NOT SILENT, and floored (D-3098). This runs every 5 seconds per session
  # and `$REG/swap.log` is a file a human reads, so it borrows `_route_note_floored`
  # — one line per session per `ROUTE_NOTE_FLOOR`, the anchor in the row's own
  # `swappinnote` field, which `_swap_pin_tick` clears with the pin so the NEXT
  # pin gets to speak straight away rather than inheriting this one's floor.
  # Without a line here the operator sees a session that simply never returns
  # home and nothing anywhere says why.
  if _swap_pinned "$id" "$wrapper"; then
    _route_note_floored "$id" swappinnote \
      || echo "$(date '+%F %T') pin-hold $id: pinned to $wrapper by hand; the return-home/ceiling arm stands down (a limit still evacuates)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    return 0
  fi
  # `-e` not `-f`, matching the four existing hold readers: an unreadable hold
  # defers too, because doubt reads as HELD.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]] && return 0
  # Otherwise (returning home, or leaving at the rate ceiling): relocate only at a clean turn
  # boundary — never mid-turn or mid-subagent. The idle gate reads the live process status file;
  # pane heuristics alone miss running subagents (learned 2026-07-03).
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "esc to interrupt" && return 0    # mid-turn: wait for the gap
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "❯" || return 0                    # not at a prompt yet
  local spid sf st sua
  spid=$(tmux list-panes -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  sf="$(_cfg_dir "$wrapper")/sessions/$spid.json"
  # `-f` BEFORE THE GREP, for `_authdead`'s reason (#69 review round 3, R2):
  # `grep FILE` opens by name and blocks for ever on a FIFO with no writer or on
  # an unbounded character device, inside the 5-second tick. MEASURED on the
  # shipped body with a stubbed tmux: a FIFO and a symlink to `/dev/zero` each
  # hung the whole lane at 6 s; with the guard both answer NOOP and the idle+
  # quiet positive control still swaps. Behaviour-identical for every input that
  # answers today — absent, a directory, a `chmod 000` file and `/dev/null` all
  # make `grep` produce nothing, `st` stays empty, and the line below already
  # returns 0 — true HERE, where the only answers are idle and not-idle.
  # `_auto_compact_check` carries the same rung over the same path and a
  # DIFFERENT arm, because #70 gave that lane a `status-unreadable` note this
  # one has no equivalent of; the sentence that stood here called the two reads
  # identical and the merge falsified it.
  [[ -f "$sf" ]] || return 0
  st=$(grep -oE '"status":"[a-z_-]+"' "$sf" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d'"' -f4)
  [[ "$st" == "idle" ]] || return 0
  # Quiet gate (short): don't relocate a session someone just touched from any surface —
  # statusUpdatedAt (ms) ticks on every busy<->idle transition, incl. web/mobile clients.
  sua=$(grep -oE '"statusUpdatedAt":[0-9]+' "$sf" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d: -f2)
  [[ -n "$sua" && $(( now * 1000 - sua )) -ge $(( SWAP_CEIL_QUIET * 1000 )) ]] || return 0
  # AFTER THE LAST GATE THAT CAN DECLINE, for the reason the rescue arm stamps
  # first in its own block: every `return 0` above this line is a move that did
  # not happen, and `degraded` names the class actually served.
  _route_degrade_at_move "$id" "$dfrom" "$dto" || return 0
  _reg_set "$id" lastswap "$now"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') $aff_verb $id: $wrapper -> $target [home=$home]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  _dispatch_swap "$id" "$target"
  return 0
}

_pane_ctx_pct() {   # pane text -> context % from the "▓ ctx <bar> NN%" statusline segment, or empty.
  # Anchored to "ctx": the model's "(1M context)" has no "ctx" substring, and the limits/cost %s are
  # not preceded by "ctx"; the bar glyphs between "ctx" and the number are non-digits.
  printf '%s\n' "$1" | grep -aoiE 'ctx[^0-9]*[0-9]+%' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | head -1
}

# D-2013 — WHY THE COMPACTOR DECLINED, said once rather than never or 240 times a minute.
#
# TWO REGISTRY FIELDS, and they are `_strand_mark`/`_strand_clear`'s shape for `_strand_mark`'s
# reason — there the MARKER is `$REG/<id>.stranded` and the floor's anchor is
# `$REG/<id>.strandnotify`, and `_strand_clear` removes the first while leaving the second:
#
#   • THE MARKER FOLLOWS THE TRUTH. `$REG/<id>.compactskip` is `<epoch> <reason>`, rewritten the
#     moment the reason CHANGES so the registry always names the CURRENT refusal, and REMOVED by
#     `_compact_note_clear` the moment the compactor stops declining. It is a fixed-size file
#     that is overwritten, never appended, so following the truth costs nothing that grows.
#     Its `<epoch>` is WHEN THIS REASON BECAME CURRENT — not when it was last logged, which is
#     what swap.log's line carries its own timestamp for. THE `stranded` CITATION THIS CARRIED IS
#     NOW FALSE, and in the opposite direction from a normal drift (#69 review round 3, found at
#     the merge): when this was written `_strand_mark` wrote its marker only when ABSENT, so "when
#     the episode began" and "when this reason became current" were one sentence and `stranded`
#     fairly stood for both. R4 then added the rewrite arm that replaces a CHANGED cause while
#     PRESERVING the original epoch, because that epoch is "since when" — the sense the merged
#     server reader carries as `FleetSession.stranded.at` on both REST and WebSocket fleet
#     payloads — and re-stamping it would make a four-hour strand read as new on every tick.
#     `compactskip` instead anchors when its CURRENT refusal reason became current. Two markers,
#     one shape, two epoch contracts — said here
#     because the shape is what invites a third one to copy the wrong half.
#   • THE ANCHOR OUTLIVES THE MARKER. `$REG/<id>.compactnote` is a bare epoch: WHEN WE LAST SAID
#     IT OUT LOUD in `$REG/swap.log`, and nothing else. `_compact_note_clear` must not touch it.
#
# THE SPLIT IS THE MECHANISM, NOT THE TIDINESS, and it was measured. While the epoch lived in
# `compactskip`'s first token, `_compact_note_clear` erased the floor's only anchor along with
# the marker — so a reason that FLAPS defeated the floor completely: 10 alternating ticks of
# `▓ ctx █░░░░░░░░░ 12%` (below threshold -> clear) and `? for shortcuts` (no ctx segment ->
# note) wrote 10 swap.log lines, not 1, i.e. 6 a minute per session at the 5s tick. That flap is
# the LIKELY shape, not a contrived one: it is D-2012's overlay hiding the statusline for a tick,
# and 3 of 19 live panes render no `ctx` segment at all (measured 2026-09-08).
#
# ONLY THE LOG WAITS. `$REG/swap.log` is an append-only ~4,700-line file a human reads, and the
# supervisor ticks every 5s across ~20 sessions. One line per session per COMPACT_NOTE_FLOOR is
# the whole budget, and it now holds whether the reason is steady or flapping.
#
# REASONS ARE STABLE SLUGS, and the measured numbers ride in `$3` instead, so that comparing
# "same reason?" is a string compare that does not flip every time a context percentage moves by
# one point. The slug is what a future reader greps for; the detail is what tells them how bad it
# was. Where two conditions need different remedies they get different SLUGS, not one slug with
# two details — the field is what a reader has once the log line is floored away.
_route_word_in() {   # word list — 0 iff word is one of the space-separated list
  local w="$1" x
  for x in $2; do [[ "$x" == "$w" ]] && return 0; done
  return 1
}
_route_any() {   # id — 0 iff ANY routing field file exists (spec §5.1: a session with
  # no field at all is a pre-slice-1 session and gets today's behaviour).
  local f
  for f in "${ROUTE_FIELDS[@]}"; do [[ -e "$REG/$1.$f" ]] && return 0; done
  return 1
}
_route_valid() {   # field value — THREE answers, because two of them are two different
  # facts and used to collapse into one (fix round 2, Finding 1):
  #   0  the value IS in the field's closed vocabulary
  #   1  the value is NOT in it — bad input, the caller's to refuse
  #   2  THE VOCABULARY ITSELF WAS UNAVAILABLE, so this call measured nothing
  #      about the value at all
  # Only `subagent` can answer 2, and only because its vocabulary is the one
  # that is not spelled in this file: it is `shared/models.mjs`'s
  # `SUBAGENT_CLASSES` PROJECTED into `$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh` as
  # `CCRC_SUBAGENT_CLASSES` (routing slice 1, Task 1), and a projection written
  # before that line carries no such array — a hand-installed ccd, an adopt, or
  # a half-finished deploy (the agent lane installs both in one run).
  #
  # BOTH 1 AND 2 FAIL CLOSED — neither an unrecognised value nor an unchecked
  # one reaches an argv or a keystroke — but they are not the same fact and
  # their remedies are different sentences (fix the value vs. re-run `ccrc
  # install`). So no caller here may narrow the distinction it received:
  # `cmd_route` dies with a different message for each, and `_route_get`
  # journals `route-unmeasured` rather than calling a legal `haiku` garbage.
  local f="$1" v="$2" x
  case "$f" in
    class)    _route_word_in "$v" "$ROUTE_CLASSES" ;;
    effort)   _route_word_in "$v" "$ROUTE_EFFORTS" ;;
    subagent) declare -p CCRC_SUBAGENT_CLASSES >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
              _route_word_in "$v" "${CCRC_SUBAGENT_CLASSES[*]}" ;;
    workflow) _route_word_in "$v" "$ROUTE_WORKFLOWS" ;;
    compact)  [[ "$v" =~ ^[0-9]{2,3}$ ]] && (( 10#$v >= 10 && 10#$v <= 100 )) ;;
    # `degraded` is the four CLASS names WITHOUT `default` — a session cannot
    # degrade TO "no override" — and they are DERIVED from `ROUTE_CLASSES`
    # rather than typed a second time three lines below the first (controller
    # ruling S1-R3). A SUFFIX strip, so `default` has to stay last in
    # `ROUTE_CLASSES`; it is, and this is the only reader that depends on it.
    degraded) _route_word_in "$v" "${ROUTE_CLASSES% default}" ;;
    inert)    [[ "$v" =~ ^[a-z]+(,[a-z]+)*$ ]] || return 1
              for x in ${v//,/ }; do _route_word_in "$x" "$ROUTE_INERTABLE" || return 1; done ;;
    *)        return 1 ;;
  esac
}
_route_note_floored() {   # id marker — 0 iff a note under this marker was written inside the floor
  local id="$1" m="$2" now nts
  now=$(date +%s)
  nts=$(_reg_get "$id" "$m" 2>/dev/null)
  [[ "$nts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - nts)) -lt "$ROUTE_NOTE_FLOOR" ]] && return 0
  _reg_set "$id" "$m" "$now"
  return 1
}
_route_bytes() {   # value -> its length in BYTES (controller ruling S1-R2).
  # `${#v}` counts CHARACTERS, and under this box's UTF-8 locale those are not
  # the same number: `gémini` is 6 characters and 7 bytes. Every rendering of a
  # rejected value in this file calls the number BYTES — the log line, the
  # `route` journal row and `cmd_route`'s own refusal — so bytes is what they
  # must each get.
  #
  # `LC_ALL=C` is LOCAL to this function, deliberately, and not hoisted into the
  # callers: `cmd_route`'s `[[:cntrl:]]` guard and its `[[ =~ ]]` id check are
  # locale-sensitive too, and a C locale there would quietly change WHICH bytes
  # they refuse. Bash re-reads the locale on the assignment itself, including a
  # `local` one, and restores it on return.
  local LC_ALL=C
  printf '%s' "${#1}"
}
# ── `--route` ON THE VERBS THAT MINT A SESSION (routing spec §5.3, §4) ───────
# `cmd_route` edits a record that already exists; these five helpers are what
# `ws-add`, `start` and `enable` need to write one AT THE MINT, where a refusal
# must leave the box exactly as it found it. The validation is `cmd_route`'s own,
# said a second time rather than shared, for one reason: `cmd_route` reads the
# STANDING record to refuse the haiku+effort pair across two calls, and there is
# no standing record here. Every other arm is deliberately identical — line for
# line, with ONE divergence: the two refusals here end `; nothing was touched`,
# the promise only a pre-mint refusal can make. That parity is MEASURED, not
# asked for: `ccd-route-argv.test.ts`'s last describe slices both bodies out of
# this file and reds when either side drifts, in wording or in arms.
# THE CLASS IS `opus`, AND THAT IS AN OPERATOR INSTRUCTION (2026-09-22), not a
# measurement: `fable` is NEVER reached without an explicit human choice, so it
# may not be anybody's default — and this row IS the default, the one a session
# gets when nobody typed anything. The explicit paths are untouched and still
# write it: `--route class=fable` at the mint, `ccd route --set class=fable`,
# the PWA picker's Fable row, the coordinator door's route POST. What changed is
# that no path reaches it on its own any more (the other half is
# `shared/routing-ladder.ts`'s `MAIN_CLASS_CEILING`, which stops ESCALATION on
# the rung below it, for a main loop exactly as for a subagent).
#
# NO VERSION IS PINNED HERE OR ANYWHERE IN THIS TREE. `opus` is the bare family
# alias; the harness resolves it to that family's newest (Claude Code 2.1.280
# answers `claude-opus-5-5`, released 2026-09-22). A row naming a dated id would
# be the one thing that has to be re-edited on every release — see
# `settings-aliases.test.ts`, which reds on exactly that.
#
# The other three fields are unchanged: an operator spawn still orchestrates at
# `ultracode` with `sonnet` subagents and workflows on.
ROUTE_COORDINATOR_ROW="class=opus effort=ultracode subagent=sonnet workflow=on"   # spec §3's coordinator row: the default for an OPERATOR spawn (§4)

_route_argv_check() {   # k=v... -> 0 all valid | dies naming the field and the byte count (never the bytes)
  # BEFORE ANY SIDE EFFECT. Every caller runs this above its first write — above
  # the worktree, the branch, the registry row and the pane — so a coordinator
  # that composed a bad argv gets a refusal and an untouched box, never a
  # half-made workspace nobody asked for.
  local kv f v rc cls="" eff="" seen=""
  for kv in "$@"; do
    [[ "$kv" == *=* ]] || die "bad --route '$kv' (want <field>=<value>)"
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    _route_word_in "$f" "${ROUTE_FIELDS[*]}" || die "unknown routing field '$f' (want one of: ${ROUTE_FIELDS[*]})"
    [[ "$f" == degraded || "$f" == inert ]] && die "'$f' is written by ccd only"
    # ONE VALUE PER FIELD, and this refusal has NO counterpart in `cmd_route`
    # (controller ruling S4-R4) — it is the one place the two validators are
    # allowed to differ in ARMS rather than wording, because only this side
    # feeds two consumers that read the same argv differently:
    # `_route_argv_class` answers with the FIRST `class=` pair and decides
    # PLACEMENT, `_route_argv_write` writes every pair and the LAST one wins
    # the record. `--route class=opus --route class=haiku` therefore placed by
    # `opus` and recorded `haiku` — a session whose lane was chosen for a class
    # its own record denies. Refused instead of ranked: neither answer is more
    # right than the other, and nothing was minted yet.
    _route_word_in "$f" "$seen" && die "--route $f given twice — one value per field; nothing was touched"; seen+="$f "
    # THREE ANSWERS, THREE SENTENCES — `cmd_route`'s rule, and its reasoning
    # applies here unchanged: an unreadable vocabulary is not the caller's value
    # being wrong, and folding the two tells an operator with a good `haiku` to
    # go and fix `haiku`. The `*)` arm is rc 1 and any rc a future vocabulary
    # arm invents; unknown is bad value, the fail-closed side.
    _route_valid "$f" "$v"; rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
      0) ;;
      2) die "route: this box's roster projection carries no subagent class list — re-run ccrc install" ;;
      *) die "bad value for $f ($(_route_bytes "$v") bytes) — not in its vocabulary; nothing was touched" ;;
    esac
    [[ "$f" == class ]] && cls="$v"
    [[ "$f" == effort ]] && eff="$v"
  done
  # `auto` types nothing and is the ABSENT-equivalent (controller ruling S1-R5),
  # so haiku+auto is not the refused pair — only haiku+a LEVEL is.
  [[ "$cls" == haiku && -n "$eff" && "$eff" != auto ]] && die "class haiku takes no effort level — set effort=auto or pick another class; nothing was touched"
  return 0
}

_route_argv_write() {   # id actor reason k=v... — write validated fields (the row exists), one route row + one log line each
  # THE PREVIOUS VALUE IS READ, AND RENDERED EXACTLY AS `cmd_route` RENDERS IT
  # (controller ruling S4-R3). The first cut printed `∅` unconditionally, on the
  # argument that every caller had just minted the row — true then, false as of
  # this round: `cmd_start`'s revival arm writes `--route` pairs against a
  # record that already exists, and a hard-coded `∅` there would journal a
  # first-write that did not happen and hide the value it replaced. `∅` is now
  # the ABSENT case of the same three-condition read, so the mint arm's rows are
  # byte-identical to what it wrote before.
  #
  # `_route_get`, not `_route_peek`: this read happens on a path that is about
  # to WRITE, so an unrecognised standing value is a fact the journal should
  # carry a note about — the opposite of `cmd_route`'s pair check, which may
  # `die` before touching anything (its own comment argues that side).
  # An unrecognised value is rendered as a byte COUNT, never its bytes: the file
  # is writable by any session on the box and a multi-line value would forge
  # log lines.
  #
  # AND THE `routeapplied` STAMP IS SEEDED FIRST, for the reason `cmd_route`
  # states at its own call: a write against a record with no stamp would
  # otherwise be swallowed by the applier's seed on the next tick — the wave
  # N>=2 dispatch path (`CCD_ARGV.routeSet`) and `start --route` both land here.
  local id="$1" actor="$2" reason="$3" kv f v raw old; shift 3
  _route_applied_before_write "$id"
  for kv in "$@"; do
    f="${kv%%=*}"; v="${kv#*=}"
    raw=$(_reg_get "$id" "$f" 2>/dev/null)
    old=$(_route_get "$id" "$f")
    if [[ -n "$raw" && -z "$old" ]]; then old="<$(_route_bytes "$raw") bytes, unrecognised>"; fi
    _reg_set "$id" "$f" "$v" || die "could not write $f for $id — the record is unchanged from this field on"
    _lc_done route "$id" "" dec.actor "$actor" dec.reason "$reason" detail "$f: ${old:-∅} -> $v"
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') route $id: $f ${old:-∅} -> $v [actor=$actor] ($reason)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    if [[ "$f" == class ]]; then _route_stale_degrade_clear "$id" "$v"; fi
  done
}

_route_seed_default() {   # id wrapper — the coordinator row for an OPERATOR spawn that named no field
  # ON EVERY LANE, INCLUDING A NON-ANTHROPIC ONE (controller ruling R1, on the
  # slice-4 probe that settled S1-R10). The first cut of this gated the row on
  # `_is_anthropic_backend` because nobody had run a class alias anywhere else;
  # the probe then measured `--model <class>` being HONOURED on the codex lane
  # and resolving through that lane's own `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_<CLASS>_MODEL` keys
  # (research doc §6, row `S1-R10, slice 4`), so the gate would have withheld a
  # record the lane can in fact apply. What such a lane cannot serve is caught
  # where it is MEASURED — `_serviceable`'s backend answer at the settle — not
  # guessed here.
  #
  # `_route_any` stays: a session that carries ANY field already has a record,
  # and this must never overwrite one (spec §5.1).
  _route_any "$1" && return 0
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  _route_argv_write "$1" spawn "coordinator row (default)" $ROUTE_COORDINATOR_ROW
}

_route_argv_class() {   # k=v... -> stdout the class= value among them, or nothing
  local kv
  for kv in "$@"; do [[ "$kv" == class=* ]] && { printf '%s' "${kv#class=}"; return 0; }; done
  return 0
}

_place_for_class() {   # project class strict -> stdout the lane; PLACE_DEGRADED_TO=<rung> when placed one rung down; "" when nothing places.
  # THE FIRST PRODUCTION CALLER THAT ASKS PLACEMENT WITH A CLASS. Slice 3 gave
  # `_ws_least_loaded` the class positional and left every call site passing
  # none; this is the site, and it adds exactly two things on top of it.
  #
  # THE RUNG BELOW, AND ONLY ON A MEASURED EMPTINESS. `_ws_least_loaded`'s ""
  # carries three meanings (its own header enumerates them) and only one is a
  # fact about the CLASS: at least one in-pool lane answered `_class_gate` rc 1.
  # An rc-2 lane is placeable and would have BEEN placed, so if one is present
  # here it failed an earlier filter — not a statement about the class, and
  # degrading on it would be a fabricated fact (spec §5.4).
  #
  # `strict` IS WHO ASKED. 1 is an explicit `--route class=`: a session that must
  # run at that class has no business on a rung the fleet cannot serve, so it
  # refuses rather than lands somewhere its own record forbids. 0 is the
  # coordinator row's class — nobody typed it — so when no rung places, today's
  # class-blind rule decides and a gpt-only pool keeps placing operator spawns
  # exactly where it placed them yesterday.
  local project="$1" cls="${2:-}" strict="${3:-1}" hw below w unserv=0 pps
  PLACE_DEGRADED_TO=""
  hw=$(_ws_least_loaded "$project" "$cls")
  if [[ -n "$hw" || -z "$cls" || "$cls" == default ]]; then printf '%s' "$hw"; return 0; fi
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  for w in "${CCRC_HOME_ABLE[@]}"; do
    _account_ok "$w" && _pool_ok "$w" "$pps" || continue
    _class_gate "$w" "$cls"; [[ $? -eq 1 ]] && unserv=$((unserv + 1))
  done
  if (( unserv > 0 )); then
    below=$(_class_below "$cls")
    if [[ -n "$below" ]]; then
      hw=$(_ws_least_loaded "$project" "$below")
      [[ -n "$hw" ]] && { PLACE_DEGRADED_TO="$below"; printf '%s' "$hw"; return 0; }
    fi
  fi
  (( strict )) && return 0
  # CAPTURED, THEN PRINTED WITHOUT A NEWLINE — `_ws_least_loaded` answers with
  # `echo` and the two arms above answer with `printf '%s'`. One seam, one
  # shape: a caller that does not wrap this in `$( )` (the `fresh` re-pick in
  # `cmd_ws_add`, which prints a SECOND line after it) must not be able to tell
  # which arm answered by the bytes it gets.
  hw=$(_ws_least_loaded "$project")
  printf '%s' "$hw"
}
_route_reject_note() {   # id field len — journal an unrecognised value: the FIELD and the
  # byte COUNT, never the bytes (they are the untrusted input). Floored PER FIELD
  # through `routenote<field>` (a dotless suffix, so `_reg_purge` reaps it): two
  # invalid fields read alternately each get their own line per window, which
  # a single shared marker would flap on (the `_compact_note` reason-change
  # shape ccd measured and removed).
  local id="$1" f="$2" len="$3"
  _route_note_floored "$id" "routenote$f" && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') route-reject $id: field $f holds an unrecognised value ($len bytes) — treated as absent" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}
_route_unmeasured_note() {   # id field — journal a field whose VOCABULARY could not be
  # read. NOT the same line as `_route_reject_note`'s and deliberately so: the
  # value was never measured, so this note names neither its bytes NOR their
  # count — a byte count is a claim about a value somebody looked at. Its own
  # floor marker (`routeunmeas<field>`, dotless so `_reg_purge` reaps it with
  # the row) rather than `routenote<field>`'s, because the two conditions can
  # alternate for one field across an install and a shared marker is exactly
  # the reason-flap shape `_compact_note` measured and removed.
  local id="$1" f="$2"
  _route_note_floored "$id" "routeunmeas$f" && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') route-unmeasured $id: field $f could not be checked — this box's roster projection (\$HOME/.ccrc/accounts.sh) carries no class list for it; treated as absent — re-run ccrc install" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}
_route_unread_note() {   # id field — journal a field whose FILE is on disk but whose BYTES
  # could not be read: a symlink to a device (`_reg_get` guards with `-f`), a
  # directory, a file whose mode denies this uid. NOT `_route_unmeasured_note`'s
  # condition and not `_route_reject_note`'s — those two both got a VALUE and
  # then could not judge it, or judged it bad; here nothing was ever read, so
  # the line names neither bytes nor a count, and says instead that the field is
  # PRESENT, which is the part an operator needs: `_inject_spawn_effort` asks
  # the filesystem, so an unreadable `effort` file still suppresses the box
  # default (`SPAWN_EFFORT`) while contributing no value of its own. Without
  # this line that suppression had no channel at all — the session simply came
  # up with no effort applied and nothing anywhere said why (Task 10 review,
  # fix round 2, Finding 2). Fail-closed is unchanged: the value is still
  # treated as absent.
  #
  # Its OWN floor marker (`routeunread<field>`, dotless so `_reg_purge` reaps it
  # with the row), for the reason `_route_unmeasured_note` gives about
  # `routenote<field>`: a field can alternate between these conditions (a chmod,
  # a re-pointed symlink) and a shared marker would flap on the reason change.
  local id="$1" f="$2"
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.$f" ]] || return 0   # genuinely ABSENT is silence, as it always was
  _route_note_floored "$id" "routeunread$f" && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') route-unmeasured $id: field $f is present but could not be read — treated as absent; a box default this field would suppress stays suppressed" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}
_route_pair_note() {   # id — the haiku+effort PAIR: both values are valid, the pairing is not
  _route_note_floored "$1" routenotepair && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') route-refuse $1: class haiku takes no effort level — the effort field is ignored" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}
_route_get() {   # id field -> stdout the VALIDATED value, or nothing. Always 0.
  # Three conditions in, three answers out (silence for a good value): see
  # `_route_valid`'s rc table. The `*)` arm is rc 1 and anything a future arm
  # invents — an unknown rc is a bad value here, which is the fail-closed side.
  #
  # `_reg_get`'s OWN failure is a FOURTH condition, and it is two things folded
  # into one rc: the field is absent, or its file is there and its bytes could
  # not be read. Absent is silence and always was; present-but-unreadable gets
  # its own line, because it can change behaviour (an unreadable `effort` file
  # still suppresses `SPAWN_EFFORT` — `_inject_spawn_effort` asks the
  # filesystem) with nothing anywhere to say so. `_route_unread_note` is the
  # one that tells them apart; both still answer nothing, so fail-closed and
  # every caller's contract are unchanged.
  local id="$1" f="$2" v rc
  v=$(_reg_get "$id" "$f") || { _route_unread_note "$id" "$f"; return 0; }
  _route_valid "$f" "$v"; rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) printf '%s' "$v" ;;
    2) _route_unmeasured_note "$id" "$f" ;;
    *) _route_reject_note "$id" "$f" "$(_route_bytes "$v")" ;;
  esac
  return 0
}
_route_peek() {   # id field -> stdout the VALIDATED value, or nothing. PURE: no journal
  # line, no floor marker, no registry write of any kind. For a caller that
  # reads a field only to DECIDE, and may `die` before it writes anything —
  # `cmd_route`'s haiku+effort pair check, where `_route_get` used to leave a
  # note and a floor marker behind on a call that then refused and changed
  # nothing (fix round 2, minor M2). `_route_get` stays what every APPLY path
  # uses: a value a real spawn actually dropped is what the journal is for.
  # Both conditions `_route_valid` refuses are silent here — this read makes no
  # claim about which it saw, and the caller's own `die` is not reached from
  # here anyway.
  local id="$1" f="$2" v
  v=$(_reg_get "$id" "$f") || return 0
  _route_valid "$f" "$v" && printf '%s' "$v"
  return 0
}
_route_effort_for() {   # id class [quiet] -> stdout the effort to apply. Haiku 4.5 takes no effort
  # level (the served catalogue gives it `thinking.type: none`), so the PAIR is
  # refused here with its own note and nothing is typed. `auto` is spec
  # §5.1's ABSENT-equivalent — haiku+auto types nothing and notes nothing,
  # same as haiku+absent; only haiku+a LEVEL is the refused pair (controller
  # ruling S1-R5, matched by `cmd_route`'s own pair check above).
  #
  # `quiet` IS A THIRD ARGUMENT AND NOT A SECOND FUNCTION (controller ruling
  # S4-R9). The rule this applies is one rule; a peek-shaped copy of it beside
  # this one is the drift that makes a gate and an applier disagree about the
  # same record. What `quiet` removes is the WRITING, all of it: the journal
  # read becomes `_route_peek` (no `route-reject`/`route-unmeasured` line, no
  # floor marker) and the pair refusal becomes silent. Omitting the argument
  # leaves every existing caller byte-for-byte what it was — the settle and
  # `cmd_caps`' journaling read still say everything they said.
  #
  # WHO ASKS QUIETLY: `_route_wanted`, which runs on the 5s supervise tick for
  # every live session. A tick may not write, and this used to: a haiku+level
  # record left a `routenotepair` floor marker and a `route-refuse` line the
  # first time the applier's own gate looked at it.
  local id="$1" cls="$2" quiet="${3:-}" e
  if [[ "$quiet" == quiet ]]; then e=$(_route_peek "$id" effort); else e=$(_route_get "$id" effort); fi
  if [[ "$cls" == haiku && "$e" == auto ]]; then
    e=""
  elif [[ "$cls" == haiku && -n "$e" ]]; then
    [[ "$quiet" == quiet ]] || _route_pair_note "$id"
    e=""
  fi
  printf '%s' "$e"
  return 0
}
_compact_note() {   # id reason [detail] — record why the compactor declined this tick.
  local id="$1" reason="$2" detail="${3:-}" rec prev nts now
  now=$(date +%s)
  rec=$(_reg_get "$id" compactskip)
  prev="${rec#* }"; [[ "$rec" == "$prev" ]] && prev=""   # one-token (or empty): no reason half
  # The marker, unfloored: a CHANGED reason is written this tick even when the log stays quiet.
  [[ "$prev" == "$reason" ]] || _reg_set "$id" compactskip "$now $reason"
  # THE DIGIT VALIDATION IS FIRST inside the same `[[ ]]` so `&&` short-circuits before the
  # arithmetic ever sees a torn or hand-edited field — D-299's class, the same shape as the
  # `strandnotify` floor in `_strand_mark` and the two cooldowns in `_auto_compact_check`.
  nts=$(_reg_get "$id" compactnote)
  [[ "$nts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - nts)) -lt "$COMPACT_NOTE_FLOOR" ]] && return 0
  _reg_set "$id" compactnote "$now"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') compact-skip $id: $reason${detail:+ ($detail)}" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}

_compact_note_clear() {   # id — the compactor is no longer declining, so the MARKER must not
  # outlive the condition it describes. A stale `mid-turn` read as current is the same class of
  # lie D-2012 names on the server side.
  #
  # `$REG/<id>.compactnote` — the log floor's anchor — IS DELIBERATELY LEFT STANDING, and that
  # is the whole of the fix measured in the header above: clearing it here is what let an
  # alternating reason write a line every tick. It costs one stale epoch per session, which the
  # floor reads as "we spoke recently" and nothing else reads at all.
  #
  # Silent by construction (the `-e` test, `_strand_clear`'s own reason): ~20 sessions x 12
  # ticks a minute is the real load, and a clear that logged would be the spam the floor exists
  # to prevent.
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.compactskip" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$1.compactskip"
  return 0
}

# ── THE KEYSTROKE PREDICATE, ONE COPY (routing slice 4, Task 4) ──────────────
# The compactor asked two questions before it typed anything: can this pane be
# READ at all, and is the session at a clean IDLE turn boundary? Slice 4's
# applier needs the identical two questions before it types `/effort` or
# `/model`, and a second copy of them would drift — most dangerously at
# `auto-continue`, where a stray keystroke cancels a limit wait a session is
# sitting out on its own (D-2229).
#
# So they are ONE pair of functions with `_auto_compact_check` as their first
# caller: EXTRACTED, not reimplemented. Every word, every sentence and every
# ORDER below is the compactor's own, and `ccd-auto-compact.test.ts` — written
# against the inline shape and untouched by this extraction — is the pin that
# says so.
#
# THE ANSWER IS THREE GLOBALS, not one string. `KS_PANE` carries the capture the
# caller then reads for itself (the compactor's `ctx` segment, the applier's
# picker), and `KS_WHY`/`KS_DETAIL` carry the refusal's SLUG and its SENTENCE
# apart — D-2013's split, and the reason the slug is stable enough to compare
# while the sentence stays free to carry measured numbers.
_pane_for_keystroke() {   # id — 0 with the last 8 pane rows in `KS_PANE`; 1 with `KS_WHY` one of
  # pane-unreadable / pane-blank / auto-continue and `KS_DETAIL` its sentence.
  #
  # D-2013/D-2014 — THREE CONDITIONS, THREE REASONS, AND THE FIRST TWO ARE MEASURED RATHER THAN
  # NAMED. "tmux could not be asked" (dead pane, wedged server — the context is UNMEASURED and the
  # remedy is to go look at the substrate) is a different fact from "tmux answered and the pane is
  # blank" (it is there and empty — wait, or look at the session). `_auto_compact_check` runs ONLY
  # under `PROBE_VERDICT == live` (see `_supervise_loop`), so the blank answer is the LIKELIER of
  # the two, and one slug naming a wedged tmux server would send the operator after a fault that is
  # not there. The old code recorded neither: it tested `-n "$pane"` alone and returned silently.
  #
  # THE PIPELINE ALREADY CARRIES TMUX'S STATUS — line 9 sets `pipefail`, so `$?` after
  # `tmux … | tail -8` is tmux's rc, not tail's. MEASURED: `set -uo pipefail; f(){ return 3; };
  # x=$(f | tail -8); echo $?` prints 3. An earlier cut of this fix split the capture across two
  # statements to "recover" a status the pipeline had never lost — and that reshaping silently
  # WIDENED the window every gate below reads. `$( )` strips trailing newlines, so capturing first
  # and piping second makes `tail -8` take 8 lines of CONTENT where it used to take the last 8 pane
  # ROWS: with 12 content rows and 4 trailing blank rows, 4 lines before, 8 after. That is not
  # cosmetic. `grep -q "esc to interrupt"` then matches a stale banner further up (a false mid-turn
  # refusal — the session never compacts), and `grep -q "❯"` matches an older prompt line while a
  # dialog is up, which is how `/compact` gets typed into a menu. KEEP THE PIPELINE ON ONE LINE;
  # `ccd-auto-compact.test.ts` pins the 8-ROW window against exactly that regression.
  local id="$1" prc pane
  KS_PANE=""; KS_WHY=""; KS_DETAIL=""
  pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8); prc=$?
  [[ "$prc" == 0 ]] || { KS_WHY=pane-unreadable; KS_DETAIL="tmux capture-pane rc=$prc"; return 1; }
  [[ -n "$pane" ]] || { KS_WHY=pane-blank; KS_DETAIL="tmux capture-pane rc=0, empty capture"; return 1; }
  _pane_auto_continue_armed "$pane" && { KS_WHY=auto-continue; KS_DETAIL="Claude Code is waiting out a limit on its own; a keystroke would cancel it (D-2229)"; return 1; }
  KS_PANE="$pane"
  return 0
}

_idle_for_keystroke() {   # id pane ctxnote — 0 iff this session is at a clean idle turn boundary;
  # 1 with `KS_WHY` one of mid-turn / no-prompt / no-pane-pid / status-unreadable /
  # not-idle / quiet-unmeasured / not-quiet / drafting and `KS_DETAIL` its sentence.
  #
  # `ctxnote` IS THE CALLER'S OWN MEASUREMENT, spliced into the sentences rather
  # than measured here: the compactor passes `ctx ${pct}%` and gets back byte for
  # byte the details it used to build inline, while the applier — which has no
  # context reading to offer and no business taking one — passes the empty string
  # and the `${ctxnote:+…}` arms collapse the separator with it. One template,
  # two callers, and neither has to know the other's vocabulary.
  local id="$1" pane="$2" ctxnote="${3:-}" now wrapper spid sf st sua t
  KS_WHY=""; KS_DETAIL=""
  # §6.3 STAND-DOWN, AND THE MERGE IS WHAT PUT IT HERE. Wave 2 guarded every function
  # that decided to TYPE on the strength of a phrase match; at that point the `esc to
  # interrupt` grep below lived in `_auto_compact_check` and its guard sat there. Routing
  # slice 4 EXTRACTED this function, so the phrase read moved into shared code with three
  # callers — `_auto_compact_check`, `cmd_route`'s `--apply` arm and `_route_apply_check`'s
  # tick — and only the first of them carries wave 2's guard. Without this line the other
  # two type on a wrapped phrase read as idle on a narrow pane, which is the exact defect
  # §6.3 exists to refuse, reached through code wave 2 never saw. The guard belongs in the
  # body that carries the phrase, which is also what `ccd-reader-standdown.test.ts`'s
  # POPULATION census requires: a phrase-carrying function guards itself.
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { KS_WHY=pane-narrow; KS_DETAIL="pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} cols; a wrapped phrase reads as idle (§6.3)"; return 1; }
  now=$(date +%s)
  # Clean idle turn boundary — same discipline as the swap soft-path (never mid-turn/subagent).
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "esc to interrupt" && { KS_WHY=mid-turn; KS_DETAIL="$ctxnote"; return 1; }
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "❯" || { KS_WHY=no-prompt; KS_DETAIL="$ctxnote"; return 1; }
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  spid=$(tmux list-panes -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -F '#{pane_pid}' 2>/dev/null | head -1)
  # D-2013 AT THE NARROWEST SEAM: "MEASURED BUSY" IS NOT "COULD NOT MEASURE". An earlier cut of
  # this function put `${st:-unreadable}` in the DETAIL and left one `not-idle` slug for both,
  # which is the same collapse one seam over — the detail is floored out of swap.log after the
  # first line, and the FIELD is then all a reader has. An empty `$st` is not a status: tmux may
  # have answered no pane pid, `$spid` may name another process, `_cfg_dir "$wrapper"` may be the
  # wrong config directory, and the file may be absent or carry no `status` token. Every one of
  # those means GO LOOK AT THE BOX; `status=busy` means WAIT. So each gets its own slug, and the
  # detail carries the path/status that says which flavour it is.
  [[ -n "$spid" ]] || { KS_WHY=no-pane-pid; KS_DETAIL="tmux list-panes named no pane pid${ctxnote:+, $ctxnote}"; return 1; }
  sf="$(_cfg_dir "$wrapper")/sessions/$spid.json"
  # `-f` BEFORE THE GREP — same rung and same reason as `_auto_swap_check`'s
  # copy above, but NOT the same arm, and the difference is the whole point.
  # That lane has two answers here (idle / not) and folds every failure into a
  # silent `return 0`. THIS lane has a vocabulary: #70 gave it
  # `status-unreadable`, deliberately distinct from `not-idle`, so an operator
  # can tell "I could not read the status" from "the status said busy". A bare
  # `|| return 0` here would be an ADAPTER NARROWING A DISTINCTION IT RECEIVED
  # — the ring rule this tree names as its highest-yield one — and it would do
  # it silently, for exactly the inputs the guard newly admits.
  #
  # MEASURED, and this is why the guard is written as an arm rather than a
  # return: on the pre-merge tree `|| return 0` WAS behaviour-identical, because
  # the line below only returned. #70 changed that line to record a note, and
  # `ccd-auto-compact.test.ts`'s "an UNREADABLE sessions JSON is NOT `not-idle`"
  # went red on the merge — the merge measuring a claim that had been true when
  # it was written and was not any more.
  #
  # TWO CONDITIONS, TWO SENTENCES, ONE NOTE WORD. `status-unreadable` is the
  # right FIELD for both — neither says anything about idleness — but "there is
  # no file" and "there is something here I refuse to open" send an operator to
  # different places, and folding them would be this program's own defect
  # committed inside its own fix. The absent arm is byte-identical to what #70
  # writes with no guard at all (`grep` finds nothing, `st` stays empty, its own
  # line says "no status in"), so `ccd-auto-compact.test.ts`'s case — whose
  # fixture writes NO sessions JSON — keeps its exact sentence. The first
  # spelling of this guard said "not a regular file" for that fixture, which is
  # a false sentence about an absent file, and the suite caught it.
  #
  # `-e` PAIRED WITH `-L` IN ONE STATEMENT, for D-1848's reason one lane over: a
  # DANGLING SYMLINK is not absence, and reporting it as "no status in" would
  # send the operator looking for a file that is not the problem.
  if [[ ! -e "$sf" && ! -L "$sf" ]]; then
    KS_WHY=status-unreadable; KS_DETAIL="no status in $sf${ctxnote:+, $ctxnote}"; return 1
  elif [[ ! -f "$sf" ]]; then
    KS_WHY=status-unreadable; KS_DETAIL="not a regular file: $sf${ctxnote:+, $ctxnote}"; return 1
  fi
  st=$(grep -oE '"status":"[a-z_-]+"' "$sf" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d'"' -f4)
  [[ -n "$st" ]] || { KS_WHY=status-unreadable; KS_DETAIL="no status in $sf${ctxnote:+, $ctxnote}"; return 1; }
  [[ "$st" == "idle" ]] || { KS_WHY=not-idle; KS_DETAIL="status=$st${ctxnote:+ $ctxnote}"; return 1; }
  # Quiet gate: only compact a session idle a beat — not the instant it goes idle, nor one just
  # touched from any surface (statusUpdatedAt ms ticks on every busy<->idle transition). Same
  # split for the same reason: a MISSING `statusUpdatedAt` is not "someone just touched it", and
  # `=~ ^[0-9]+$` (was `-n`) is also the D-299 guard on the operand the next line evaluates.
  sua=$(grep -oE '"statusUpdatedAt":[0-9]+' "$sf" 2>/dev/null | head -1 | cut -d: -f2)
  [[ "$sua" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { KS_WHY=quiet-unmeasured; KS_DETAIL="no statusUpdatedAt in $sf${ctxnote:+, $ctxnote}"; return 1; }
  [[ $(( now * 1000 - sua )) -ge $(( COMPACT_QUIET * 1000 )) ]] \
    || { KS_WHY=not-quiet; KS_DETAIL="$ctxnote"; return 1; }
  # Don't clobber a half-typed message in the input box. `-e`: the reader needs
  # the escape codes to tell a real draft from the dim ghost-suggestion — see
  # `_pane_box_draft`, which refused every suggestion-showing session for ever
  # when it was handed a plain capture.
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  _pane_box_has_content "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p -e 2>/dev/null)" \
    && { KS_WHY=drafting; KS_DETAIL="$ctxnote"; return 1; }
  return 0
}

_auto_compact_check() {   # id — at a clean idle turn boundary, /compact a session whose own context has
  # grown past ITS threshold (the record's `compact` field, else COMPACT_THRESHOLD) so it stays lean. Non-destructive: the SessionStart(compact) hook
  # restores git/plan/handoff context (Anthropic + gpt lanes). Kill-switch: AUTOCOMPACT_DISABLE_FILE.
  #
  # D-2013 — WHICH REFUSALS ARE NOTED, AND WHICH ARE NOT. Two of the three gates above the pane
  # read (kill-switch, lastcompact cooldown) say nothing, and so does the below-threshold return:
  # those are the ORDINARY states of a healthy fleet and noting them would drown the interesting
  # cases. Everything from the pane read down IS noted, because each one is either "we cannot see
  # this session at all" or "this session is over its threshold and we are declining anyway" —
  # the two shapes that were silent, and the second is the one that matters.
  #
  # THE THIRD GATE ABOVE THE READ DOES SPEAK, and it is the exception that proves the rule:
  # `pane-narrow` (§6.3, `_pane_measurable`) is not an ordinary state of a healthy fleet. It says
  # this function is BLIND to the session for as long as the pane stays under READER_MIN_COLS —
  # "we cannot see this session at all", the first of the two shapes above, reached one rung
  # earlier than the read that used to be the first place it could be said.
  [[ -f "$AUTOCOMPACT_DISABLE_FILE" ]] && return 0
  local id="$1" now last lastswap fromswap=0 pane pct thr wrapper t
  now=$(date +%s)
  last=$(_reg_get "$id" lastcompact)
  [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - last)) -lt "$COMPACT_COOLDOWN" ]] && return 0
  # D-2014 — MEASURE the landing compaction; do not assert it. This line used to `return 0`
  # outright on the premise that "a swap landing already compacted from summary". It records a
  # FLAG instead, and the premise is tested against the pane's own context reading below, once
  # there is a reading to test it with. The arithmetic stays exactly where it was, on one
  # `[[ ]]` line with its `=~ ^[0-9]` guard first — `ccd-arith-containment.test.ts`'s structural
  # table pins that shape, and D-299's short-circuit is the reason it must not be split.
  lastswap=$(_reg_get "$id" lastswap)
  [[ "$lastswap" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - lastswap)) -lt "$COMPACT_COOLDOWN" ]] && fromswap=1
  # THE PANE READ AND THE IDLE READ ARE `_pane_for_keystroke`/`_idle_for_keystroke` (slice 4,
  # Task 4), the two halves of this function the applier needed word for word. The refusal words,
  # their order and their sentences are unchanged — `$KS_WHY` is the slug this function used to
  # pass `_compact_note` as a literal and `$KS_DETAIL` is the sentence it used to build inline,
  # with `ctx ${pct}%` handed to the second call so every one of its details renders exactly as
  # it did. WHAT STAYS HERE is everything BETWEEN them — `no-ctx-segment`, the threshold clear
  # and `post-swap` — because those are the compactor's own arithmetic and not a keystroke
  # question at all, and because keeping them between the two calls is what makes a
  # below-threshold mid-turn pane record nothing, exactly as before.
  # §6.3 STAND-DOWN, DELIBERATELY IN THIS CALLER AND NOT IN `_pane_for_keystroke`. The merge that
  # brought routing slice 4 extracted the pane acquisition into that shared helper, which now has
  # three callers — this one, `cmd_route`'s `--apply` arm and `_route_apply_check`'s tick. Pushing
  # the width guard down into it would stand those two down as well. They ARE keystroke sites and
  # §6.3 plausibly wants them, but wave 2 never touched them and no review has seen it, so the
  # guard stays where wave 2 put it and the extension is named for wave 3 rather than taken here.
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { _compact_note "$id" pane-narrow "pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} cols; a wrapped phrase reads as idle (§6.3)"; return 0; }
  _pane_for_keystroke "$id" || { _compact_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  pane="$KS_PANE"
  # `no-ctx-segment` is the THIRD condition of the pane read and it stayed behind with the
  # arithmetic that needs it: the pane was read fine and its statusline does not publish the
  # number, so the session is permanently invisible to this function. Measured live 2026-09-08:
  # 3 of 19 panes are in that state, and nothing anywhere said so.
  pct=$(_pane_ctx_pct "$pane"); [[ -n "$pct" ]] || { _compact_note "$id" no-ctx-segment; return 0; }
  # THE THRESHOLD IS THIS SESSION'S, NOT THE BOX'S (routing spec §5.1; slice 6, Task 5). The
  # record's `compact` field is the number this comparison uses. The matrix gives a WORKER 40 so
  # a dependent chain comes back under the wall between tasks (S6-R9), while a coordinator keeps
  # the default.
  #
  # THE FIELD IS READ THROUGH `_route_get`, NOT `_reg_get` (controller ruling S6-R12). The
  # routing record's fields have ONE validated reader and this is a routing field like any
  # other: `_route_get` answers the value only when `_route_valid` accepts it — the `compact`
  # arm admits two or three digits in 10–100 and nothing else — and answers NOTHING for a bad
  # value, an unmeasurable vocabulary or a present-but-unreadable file, journalling each of the
  # three through its own routing note (`_route_reject_note` names the field and a BYTE COUNT,
  # never the bytes; `_route_unmeasured_note`; `_route_unread_note`). Reading it raw here would
  # have given the field a SECOND, looser vocabulary — `5`, `0`, `007`, `120` and `99999` are
  # all digits and none of them is a threshold this fleet admits — and would have folded
  # absent, empty and unreadable into the one `""` those three notes exist to tell apart.
  #
  # SO THE REFUSAL'S JOURNAL IS THE ROUTING NOTE, not `compactskip`. A bad value used to write
  # a `compact-skip … compact-field-invalid` line under `_compact_note`'s shared floor, where
  # the next below-threshold tick's `_compact_note_clear` erased it: a routing refusal wearing
  # the compactor's marker, cleared by the compactor's own success path. `_route_get`'s notes
  # carry their own per-field floor marker (`routenotecompact`) and nothing here clears them.
  #
  # ABSENT (and every condition `_route_get` answers nothing for) MEANS `COMPACT_THRESHOLD`,
  # which is every session that predates the field and every one the coordinator gives no
  # threshold — a bad byte must never leave a session uncompactable.
  thr=$(_route_get "$id" compact)
  thr=${thr:-$COMPACT_THRESHOLD}
  # THE DIGIT TEST IS FIRST AND INSIDE THE SAME `[[ ]]` as the arithmetic, which is the shape
  # `ccd-arith-containment.test.ts`'s SITES table pins and the only shape that short-circuits
  # before `-ge` EVALUATES its operand (D-299). `$thr` is a validated value or the default by
  # the line above, so the guard is belt and braces today — it is what refuses the future edit
  # that reads the field raw again.
  [[ "$thr" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$pct" -ge "$thr" ]] || { _compact_note_clear "$id"; return 0; }
  # D-2014, the decision the flag above deferred. Over threshold AND freshly landed: suppress only
  # below COMPACT_LANDING_WALL. At or above it the suppression no longer earns its veto — see that
  # constant, which says exactly what 80%+ does and does not establish about the landing.
  (( fromswap )) && [[ "$pct" -lt "$COMPACT_LANDING_WALL" ]] \
    && { _compact_note "$id" post-swap "ctx ${pct}% < ${COMPACT_LANDING_WALL}% within ${COMPACT_COOLDOWN}s of a swap"; return 0; }
  _idle_for_keystroke "$id" "$pane" "ctx ${pct}%" || { _compact_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  # D-3103 — NOTHING HAS HAPPENED SINCE THE LAST COMPACTION. The last gate, and it
  # is last on purpose: `_compact_no_turns` resolves a transcript path and scans
  # its tail through python, which the final D-2444 review measured at an order of
  # magnitude more than the pane classifier, so it is paid only by a session that
  # has passed EVERY cheaper test and is one line away from having `/compact`
  # typed into it. A below-threshold, mid-turn or busy session never reaches it.
  #
  # ITS OWN REASON, NOT `post-swap`'s. Both refuse an over-threshold session, and
  # an operator handles them differently: `post-swap` says "wait, the landing has
  # probably already compacted this", and this one says "this session has not
  # spoken since the last time we compacted it, so there is nothing new to
  # summarise and the compaction would only summarise a summary". The detail
  # carries the measured age of the newest real turn.
  _compact_no_turns "$id" && { _compact_note "$id" no-turns-since-compact "ctx ${pct}%; $COMPACT_TURN_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  # `wrapper` is read again here rather than carried out of the predicate: it is the log line
  # below that wants it, and a predicate that answered a second value through a global would be
  # a wider contract than "is this pane typeable" for one string.
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  # Nothing is being declined any more, so the field must go — the `auto-compact` line below is
  # this tick's record, and a surviving `compactskip` beside it would claim a refusal that did
  # not happen. Cleared BEFORE the send-keys for the same reason `_reg_set lastcompact` is: what
  # the registry says must not depend on whether tmux answered.
  _compact_note_clear "$id"
  _reg_set "$id" lastcompact "$now"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') auto-compact $id: ctx ${pct}% >= ${thr}% [wrapper=$wrapper]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "/compact"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
}

# ── APPLYING THE RECORD TO A LIVE SESSION (routing spec §5.3; slice 4, Task 4) ─
#
# ccd IS THE ONE TYPER. The record is the arbiter and nothing above ccd — not
# the server, not the PWA — sends `/effort` or `/model` into a pane; everything
# below is the whole of the fleet's live-change machinery, reached from exactly
# two places: `ccd route --apply` (the operator, once) and `_route_apply_check`
# (the supervise tick, every 5s per live session).
#
# ONLY THE SESSION-ONLY FORMS, and that is a measurement rather than a
# preference. `/effort high` typed plain PERSISTS: it writes
# `modelSettings.<model-id>.effortLevel` into the LANE's settings.json, which
# every other session on that account then inherits (D-2808, and D-2810 found
# four such pins already on the fleet). The slider + `s` writes nothing — the
# probe diffed settings.json before and after on both lanes. So the applier
# drives the pickers even though typing an argument would be one line.
#
# WHAT IS APPLIED LIVE AND WHAT WAITS FOR THE SETTLE. `class` and `effort` have
# a keystroke; `workflow`, `subagent` and `compact` do not — workflows are an
# argv `--settings` key, the subagent floor is an env var, and `compact` is read
# by the compactor itself. Those three are recorded applied the moment they are
# WRITTEN, because the next spawn composes them from the same record, and a
# field that waits for a keystroke that does not exist would keep this session
# pending for ever.
_route_note() {   # id reason [detail] — record why the applier declined this tick.
  # `_compact_note`'s shape, line for line, and the split is the same one D-2013
  # argues at length there: `$REG/<id>.routeskip` is the MARKER (`<epoch>
  # <reason>`, rewritten the moment the reason CHANGES, removed by
  # `_route_note_clear`) and `$REG/<id>.routenote` is the log floor's ANCHOR (a
  # bare epoch, never cleared). Without that split a reason that FLAPS — an
  # overlay hiding the statusline for one tick, a turn starting between two
  # ticks — defeats the floor completely and writes a swap.log line every 5s.
  #
  # ITS OWN FIELDS, NOT THE COMPACTOR'S. A refusal to apply a routing change and
  # a refusal to compact are two conditions an operator handles differently, so
  # they never share `compactskip`: a below-threshold compaction tick clears
  # `compactskip` and must leave a standing `routeskip` exactly where it was.
  local id="$1" reason="$2" detail="${3:-}" rec prev now
  now=$(date +%s)
  rec=$(_reg_get "$id" routeskip)
  prev="${rec#* }"; [[ "$rec" == "$prev" ]] && prev=""   # one-token (or empty): no reason half
  [[ "$prev" == "$reason" ]] || _reg_set "$id" routeskip "$now $reason"
  # THE FLOOR IS `_route_note_floored`, not a second copy of its arithmetic.
  # `_compact_note` spells that `[[ "$nts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - nts)) -lt … ]]`
  # guard inline because it predates this file's own floor helper; writing it out
  # a third time here would put a byte-identical line beside the helper's and
  # leave the D-299 digit guard on it unpinned by anything (the structural table
  # in `ccd-arith-containment.test.ts` anchors on the line's text and asserts the
  # match is unique). One reader of `ROUTE_NOTE_FLOOR`, already guarded.
  _route_note_floored "$id" routenote && return 0
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') route-skip $id: $reason${detail:+ ($detail)}" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}

_route_note_clear() {   # id — the applier is no longer declining, so the MARKER must go; a stale
  # `mid-turn` read as current is the same lie `_compact_note_clear` names. The
  # ANCHOR (`routenote`) is deliberately left standing — clearing it is what let
  # an alternating reason defeat the floor. Silent by construction.
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.routeskip" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$1.routeskip"
  return 0
}

# ── `routeapplied`: what this pane is ACTUALLY running ───────────────────────
# A space-separated `field=value` word list in `$REG/<id>.routeapplied`. It is
# the only thing that makes the tick cheap: the record says what is WANTED and
# this says what LANDED, so the comparison that decides whether to touch tmux at
# all is two registry reads. It is written by the settle (from what `_spawn_start`
# COMPOSED) and by the applier (on a read-back acknowledgement, never on the
# send-keys alone), and by nothing else.
_route_applied_set() {   # id field value — rewrite the list with <field> replaced or appended
  local id="$1" f="$2" v="$3" cur out="" w
  local -a words=()
  cur=$(_reg_get "$id" routeapplied 2>/dev/null)
  # `read -a`, not `for w in $cur`: the file is writable by any session on the
  # box (one UNIX user — attribution, not authentication), and an unquoted
  # expansion would GLOB a value carrying `*` against this directory.
  read -r -a words <<< "$cur"
  for w in ${words[@]+"${words[@]}"}; do
    [[ "$w" == "$f="* ]] && continue
    out="${out:+$out }$w"
  done
  _reg_set "$id" routeapplied "${out:+$out }$f=$v"
}

_route_applied_get() {   # id field -> stdout the recorded value, or nothing. Always 0.
  local id="$1" f="$2" w
  local -a words=()
  read -r -a words <<< "$(_reg_get "$id" routeapplied 2>/dev/null)"
  for w in ${words[@]+"${words[@]}"}; do
    [[ "$w" == "$f="* ]] && { printf '%s' "${w#*=}"; return 0; }
  done
  return 0
}

_route_inert_has() {   # <inert list> field -> 0 iff the comma list names the field
  # ONE READER OF THE `inert` VOCABULARY for every consumer of the stamp. The
  # list is a comma-separated set of FIELD NAMES drawn from `ROUTE_INERTABLE`
  # (`_route_valid`'s `inert` arm), so membership is a word test and never a
  # substring one: `*effort*` would also match a future `noeffort`.
  local list="$1" f="$2"
  _route_word_in "$f" "${list//,/ }"
}

_route_wanted() {   # id -> stdout "<class> <effort>": the two values the applier would type.
  # ONE SPELLING, read by both the cheap pending check and the applier itself —
  # two copies of "what does this record ask for" is exactly the drift that would
  # make the tick loop for ever on a field it then declines to type.
  #
  # AN ABSENT FIELD IS AN EMPTY WORD, AND THAT IS NOT `default`/`auto`
  # (controller ruling S4-R8). Three conditions, three answers: the field is
  # ABSENT (nothing is pending for it and nothing is ever typed for it), or it
  # carries the explicit word `default`/`auto` (the picker's `Default` row; the
  # lane decides the level), or it carries a real value. Collapsing the first two
  # is what made a live session carrying only `compact=off` get `/model` + Enter
  # + `s` on the first tick, overriding a model a human had picked by hand. The
  # settle has always drawn the same line — `[[ -n "$rclass" && "$rclass" !=
  # default ]]` composes no `--model` for either, but it composes nothing for an
  # absent class the way it composes nothing for `default`, and now says so in
  # its `routeapplied` stamp too.
  #
  # THE CLASS SERVED, NEVER THE INTENDED ONE: a `degraded` stamp means this lane
  # measured unable to serve `class`, and typing the intended class into the
  # picker would ask the pane for a model the settle already ruled out (and, on
  # the fable rung, one the picker does not even list).
  #
  # AN INERT FIELD IS NOT WANTED AT ALL (controller ruling S4-R9). `inert` is the
  # settle's own statement that this lane could not apply the field; a tick that
  # then typed it would contradict the record on disk, and the `routeapplied`
  # stamp beside it would claim a state nobody set. `class` is never inert —
  # `ROUTE_INERTABLE` has no word for it — so only the effort arm asks. `subagent`
  # IS inertable (slice 6 Task 3) and still does not ask here, for a different
  # reason: this function answers the two values THE APPLIER TYPES, and the
  # subagent is an env key on the spawn's argv with no picker behind it. Its
  # inert stamp is read where it is claimed instead — `_route_apply_seed` and
  # `_spawn_start`'s `routeapplied` line.
  #
  # IT WRITES NOTHING. Five registry READS and no write: `_route_peek` for
  # `class` and `degraded` and `inert`, and `_route_effort_for … quiet`, which is
  # a sixth read (`effort`) through the same pure reader. That is the whole
  # contract this function owes `_route_apply_pending`, which runs on every live
  # session every five seconds.
  local id="$1" cls deg eff
  cls=$(_route_peek "$id" class)
  deg=$(_route_peek "$id" degraded)
  [[ -n "$deg" ]] && cls="$deg"
  eff=$(_route_effort_for "$id" "$cls" quiet)
  _route_inert_has "$(_route_peek "$id" inert)" effort && eff=""
  printf '%s %s' "$cls" "$eff"
  return 0
}

_route_pending_word() {   # id -> "<field>=<value>" for the FIRST field the applier would type, or nothing
  # THE ONE PLACE "is this field pending" is decided, so the cheap gate, the
  # applier and the give-up note can never disagree about WHICH field is stuck.
  # Class first, then effort — `_route_apply_now`'s own order (D-2811).
  local id="$1" want cls eff
  want=$(_route_wanted "$id"); cls="${want%% *}"; eff="${want#* }"
  [[ -n "$cls" && "$cls" != "$(_route_applied_get "$id" class)" ]] && { printf 'class=%s' "$cls"; return 0; }
  [[ -n "$eff" && "$eff" != "$(_route_applied_get "$id" effort)" ]] && { printf 'effort=%s' "$eff"; return 0; }
  return 0
}

_route_apply_pending() {   # id -> 0 iff the record asks for a class or effort `routeapplied` does not record
  # REGISTRY READS ONLY, AND THAT IS NOW TRUE (controller ruling S4-R9; it was
  # not, and the comment here said it was — `_route_wanted` reached
  # `_route_effort_for`, which could leave a `routenotepair` marker and a
  # `route-refuse` line on a tick). The reads: `_route_any`'s seven `-e` stats,
  # then `_route_wanted`'s four (`class`, `degraded`, `effort`, `inert`) and one
  # or two `_route_applied_get`s. This is the tick's first act on every live
  # session — ~20 sessions x 12 ticks a minute — so the healthy answer, nothing
  # pending, has to cost a few stats and no tmux at all.
  local id="$1"
  _route_any "$id" || return 1
  [[ -n "$(_route_pending_word "$id")" ]] && return 0
  return 1
}

_route_apply_seed() {   # id [why] -> 0 iff it SEEDED `routeapplied` (the tick's caller then returns)
  # A RECORD THAT PREDATES THE APPLIER IS NOT AN UNAPPLIED RECORD (controller
  # ruling S4-R8). Every routing record on this fleet was applied by its last
  # settle's argv — that is what `_spawn_start` composes — but until slice 4
  # nothing WROTE that down, so a session whose record was set before this code
  # shipped carries no `routeapplied` file at all. Typing on first sight would
  # drive the picker on every such session at once, for no change: the pane is
  # already running what the record asks for.
  #
  # So the first tick that finds a record with no stamp WRITES THE STAMP FROM THE
  # SERVED VALUES AND TYPES NOTHING, and says so in one swap.log line. From the
  # next tick on the file exists and the ordinary comparison decides — a
  # DIVERGENT write after the seed (`ccd route --set effort=high`) is pending and
  # is typed at the next idle tick, which is the whole point of seeding rather
  # than suppressing.
  #
  # WHAT IS SEEDED IS WHAT `_route_wanted` ANSWERS, not the record's raw fields:
  # the class SERVED (a `degraded` stamp wins), the effort past the haiku filter,
  # and nothing at all for a field that is absent or named in `inert`. Written
  # any other way the seed would itself leave something pending.
  #
  # THE GATE BELOW IS A PROXY, AND EVERY WRITER MAINTAINS IT (final review
  # finding 1). "No stamp" only MEANS "predates the applier" at the deploy
  # moment: `_spawn_start` DELETES the stamp for a session with no record, so
  # every never-routed session on the fleet carries no file, and the FIRST
  # routing write to one of them — the operator's `--apply`, the coordinator's
  # plain `ccd route`, a wave N>=2 dispatch — would be SEEDED on the next tick
  # and never typed: recorded applied without a keystroke, nothing pending, no
  # retry. So `_route_applied_before_write` seeds from the values served BEFORE
  # any routing write, which is exactly what this ruling asserts about them, and
  # the write that follows is the divergence this function's third paragraph
  # promises. The two callers are therefore the tick (where the swap.log line
  # says the record predates the applier) and every writer (where it says what
  # it seeded and why); `why` is that sentence, never a fact this function
  # measured.
  #
  # AN EMPTY SEED IS SILENT. A session with NO record at all was served nothing,
  # so the stamp claims nothing and there is no line to write — the tick can
  # never reach that state (`_route_apply_pending` returned first), and a writer
  # reaching it is a session being routed for the very first time.
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.routeapplied" ]] && return 1
  local id="$1" why="${2:-record predates the applier}" want cls eff sub wf cmp inert words=""
  want=$(_route_wanted "$id"); cls="${want%% *}"; eff="${want#* }"
  [[ -n "$cls" ]] && words="class=$cls"
  [[ -n "$eff" ]] && words="${words:+$words }effort=$eff"
  # `_route_peek`, not `_route_get`: the seed runs on a supervise tick and a tick
  # writes no journal line (the rule `_route_apply_pending` states above).
  inert=$(_route_peek "$id" inert)
  sub=$(_route_peek "$id" subagent)
  _route_inert_has "$inert" subagent || { [[ -n "$sub" ]] && words="${words:+$words }subagent=$sub"; }
  wf=$(_route_peek "$id" workflow)
  _route_inert_has "$inert" workflow || { [[ -n "$wf" ]] && words="${words:+$words }workflow=$wf"; }
  cmp=$(_route_peek "$id" compact); [[ -n "$cmp" ]] && words="${words:+$words }compact=$cmp"
  _reg_set "$id" routeapplied "$words"
  [[ -n "$words" ]] && echo "$(date '+%F %T') routeapplied-seeded $id: $words ($why)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 0
}

_route_applied_before_write() {   # id — the stamp must EXIST before any routing field is written
  # ONE CALL, TWO WRITERS (`cmd_route` and `_route_argv_write`), and it is what
  # makes `_route_apply_seed`'s gate honest — that function's own header argues
  # the whole case. Called AFTER validation and BEFORE the first `_reg_set`, so
  # a refused call still touches nothing and the values it stamps are the ones
  # the last settle actually composed.
  _route_apply_seed "$1" "seeded at a route write: this is what the last settle composed"
  return 0
}

_route_stale_degrade_clear() {   # id class — drop a `degraded` stamp that cannot have been measured for THIS class
  # THE STAMP IS A STATEMENT ABOUT THE PREVIOUS CLASS (final review finding 4).
  # `_route_wanted` answers the class SERVED, never the intended one, which is
  # right while the intent is unchanged and wrong the moment it changes: with
  # `class=fable degraded=opus` already applied, an operator tapping Sonnet got
  # the record rewritten, ZERO keystrokes, no refusal word and a pane still
  # running opus — the tap silently a no-op until some later settle.
  #
  # THE TEST IS THE SETTLE'S OWN (ruling S3-R7), and this is now its one reader:
  # a stamp that is not one rung below the class now intended cannot have been
  # the measurement for it. `default` has no rung below it here — the lane
  # decides that class, so any standing stamp is about a class nobody intends
  # any more — where `_class_below` would answer `haiku` for the word's position
  # in `ROUTE_CLASSES`.
  #
  # CLEARED, NOT REFUSED: ccd re-stamps at the next settle if the new class is
  # unservable too, and `_route_restore` says so in the journal and in swap.log,
  # so nothing about the degrade is lost quietly.
  local id="$1" cls="$2" deg below=""
  deg=$(_route_get "$id" degraded)
  [[ -n "$deg" ]] || return 0
  [[ -n "$cls" && "$cls" != default ]] && below=$(_class_below "$cls")
  [[ "$deg" == "$below" ]] && return 0
  _route_restore "$id" "$deg" "$cls" "stale: the intended class is now ${cls:-∅}"
}

_route_compact_settling() {   # id -> 0 iff the compactor typed into this pane inside ROUTE_COMPACT_QUIET
  # THE ONE COLLISION THE DESIGN NEVER CONSIDERED (final review finding 5). The
  # supervise tick runs `_auto_compact_check "$id"; _route_apply_check "$id"` on
  # one line: the compactor ends by sending `/compact` + Enter and signals
  # nothing, and the applier then re-asks an idle predicate that reads a pane
  # capture and a `sessions/<pid>.json` — neither of which has moved yet — and
  # types `/model` into a pane mid-compaction. `_route_note`'s own words for
  # that hazard: a mid-turn pane takes the slash command as a message.
  #
  # `lastcompact` IS THE SIGNAL, and it is deliberately not a shell variable:
  # `_auto_compact_check` writes it immediately before its send-keys (and before
  # tmux can fail, for the reason stated there), so the interlock also holds
  # between PROCESSES — the operator's `ccd route --apply` is not the
  # supervisor's process and could collide the same way.
  #
  # ITS REFUSAL IS A NOTE, NEVER AN ATTEMPT (ruling S4-R7): nothing was typed and
  # the condition clears itself within `ROUTE_COMPACT_QUIET`, so spending the
  # retry budget on it would exhaust a session that was merely compacting.
  #
  # D-299: the digit guard is FIRST inside the `[[ ]]`, so a torn or hand-edited
  # `lastcompact` never reaches the arithmetic (`ccd-arith-containment.test.ts`
  # carries this site in its structural table).
  local id="$1" last now
  last=$(_reg_get "$id" lastcompact 2>/dev/null)
  now=$(date +%s)
  [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - last)) -lt "$ROUTE_COMPACT_QUIET" ]] || return 1
  KS_WHY=compacting
  KS_DETAIL="a /compact was typed into this pane less than ${ROUTE_COMPACT_QUIET}s ago"
  return 0
}

_route_tries_clear() {   # id — drop the bounded-retry counter (controller ruling S4-R7)
  # THREE THINGS CLEAR IT and they are the three that make a further attempt
  # MEAN something again: a `cmd_route` write to this session (the operator
  # changed what is being asked for, with or without `--apply`), the settle's own
  # `routeapplied` stamp (a fresh spawn composed the record into the argv, so
  # whatever the pane refused is moot), and the pending set becoming empty.
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.routetries" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$1.routetries"
  return 0
}

_route_try_bump() {   # id — count ONE attempt that reached the pane and was not acknowledged
  # WHAT COUNTS AS AN ATTEMPT is exactly the set of refusals that came back FROM
  # THE PANE — `apply-unconfirmed`, `no-picker-row`, `no-slider-stop`. A refusal
  # from `_pane_for_keystroke`/`_idle_for_keystroke` (mid-turn, drafting, an
  # occupied box, a session sitting out a usage limit) never gets here: nothing
  # was typed, the condition is transient by construction, and counting it would
  # exhaust the budget on a session that was simply busy.
  local id="$1" rec n now
  now=$(date +%s)
  rec=$(_reg_get "$id" routetries 2>/dev/null)
  n="${rec%% *}"
  if [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]]; then n=$((n + 1)); else n=1; fi
  _reg_set "$id" routetries "$n $now"
  return 0
}

_route_retry_ok() {   # id -> 0 iff the applier may type this tick (controller ruling S4-R7)
  # THE FLOOR BOUNDS THE LOG LINE; THIS BOUNDS THE KEYSTROKES. `_route_note`'s
  # `ROUTE_NOTE_FLOOR` makes a standing refusal quiet, which is a different thing
  # from making it cheap: before this, a permanently unapplyable field — `/effort
  # ultracode` on a lane launched `{"enableWorkflows":false}`, which Claude Code
  # refuses verbatim, or `class=fable` on a lane whose picker lists no Fable row —
  # re-entered the applier every 5s and typed a whole key sequence into a live
  # pane twelve times a minute, for ever, silently after the first note. Worse,
  # the class arm's early return starved the effort arm, so a stuck class also
  # meant an effort that never landed.
  #
  # THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO: never attempted (type), inside the backoff (wait,
  # silently — the note for this condition was already written by the attempt
  # that set the counter), and at the cap (say `apply-gave-up` once per floor
  # and type nothing until something clears the counter).
  local id="$1" rec n ts now
  rec=$(_reg_get "$id" routetries 2>/dev/null)
  [[ -n "$rec" ]] || return 0
  n="${rec%% *}"; ts="${rec##* }"
  now=$(date +%s)
  # D-299: BOTH TOKENS ARE GUARDED `=~ ^[0-9]+$` FIRST inside each `[[ ]]`, so a
  # torn or hand-edited counter never reaches an arithmetic context (`$REG` is
  # writable by any session on this box — attribution, not authentication). And
  # because neither guarded line can then fire, the third drops the unreadable
  # counter rather than wedging the applier on bytes it cannot read.
  [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$n" -ge "$ROUTE_RETRY_MAX" ]] && { _route_note "$id" apply-gave-up "$(_route_pending_word "$id") after $n attempts"; return 1; }
  [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$ts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - ts)) -ge "$ROUTE_RETRY_BACKOFF" ]] && return 0
  [[ "$n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$ts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || { _route_tries_clear "$id"; return 0; }
  return 1
}

_route_ack_wait() {   # tmuxname regex [rows] -> 0 iff the pane shows the acknowledgement inside the window
  # THE READ-BACK IS THE MEASUREMENT. A `send-keys` that returns 0 proves tmux
  # accepted the bytes and nothing more — the TUI may have been mid-repaint, a
  # dialog may have eaten them, the slash command may have been refused outright
  # (`Ultracode needs dynamic workflows enabled`, measured verbatim on a lane
  # launched with `{"enableWorkflows":false}`). So nothing is recorded applied
  # until Claude Code says so in its own words, and a silent pane is
  # `apply-unconfirmed` rather than a lie in the registry.
  #
  # `rows` BOUNDS THE READ TO THE BOTTOM OF THE PANE, and it is half of binding
  # an acknowledgement to the act that earned it (final review finding 3). A
  # whole-pane grep also matches a banner far up the scrollback — the defect
  # `_pane_for_keystroke`'s own header argues at length, one seam over — so a
  # caller that KNOWS where its acknowledgement renders says so. The default
  # `+1` is `tail`'s "from the first line", i.e. the whole visible pane, byte
  # for byte what every caller got before this parameter existed.
  local t="$1" re="$2" rows="${3:-+1}" i
  for ((i = 0; i < ROUTE_ACK_WAIT; i++)); do
    tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -n "$rows" | grep -qE "$re" && return 0
    sleep 1
  done
  return 1
}

_route_escape() {   # id — leave the pane as we found it after an unacknowledged sequence
  # THE TARGET IS DERIVED HERE, FROM THE REGISTRY ID, and that is the whole
  # reason this function takes an id rather than the tmux name its callers
  # already hold (controller ruling S4-R14). `ccd-account-pane.test.ts` reads
  # every key-sending call in this file and requires each target to be `$t` or
  # `$(_tmux "$id")`, because `_tmux` can only ever name `cc-<id>` — so no ccd
  # typer can reach an auth pane (`cc-auth-<id>`), whose whole safety property is
  # that nothing types into it. A bare `"$1"` here would be a literal target as
  # far as that scan is concerned, and a caller one refactor away could hand it
  # any pane name at all.
  #
  # CONTROLLER RULING S4-R9. A `send-keys` that is never acknowledged has one of
  # two shapes and both leave something OPEN: the slider is still up, or the
  # `Change effort level?` dialog is, or the model picker is. Returning 1 with
  # the UI standing hands the next actor a pane where a plain keystroke — the
  # compactor's `/compact`, a nudge, a human — lands inside a picker instead of
  # the prompt. And nothing downstream can tell: `_idle_for_keystroke`'s
  # `grep -q ❯` cannot distinguish a picker's cursor row from the prompt, so the
  # pane reads IDLE while it is nothing of the kind.
  #
  # ONCE, not until-clear. One `Escape` closes each of the three shapes measured
  # (D-2808, D-2811 and the slice-4 picker probe); a loop would be a second,
  # unmeasured claim, and pressing Escape at a bare prompt is a no-op anyway.
  local t; t=$(_tmux "$1")
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" Escape; sleep 1
  return 0
}

_route_type_effort() {   # id level -> 0 acknowledged | 1 unconfirmed | 2 no slider stop
  # `ultracode` HAS NO SLIDER DELTA and needs none: it is the one level whose
  # PLAIN form is session-only by construction (D-2810, measured — the ack reads
  # `(this session only)` and settings.json is byte-identical), because there is
  # no settings key to save it into. Every other level rides the slider.
  #
  # `Left`x7 THEN `Right`xN, and the 7 is not arbitrary: the slider does NOT
  # wrap (measured), so seven lefts pin the marker at `low` from any starting
  # position on a six-stop scale and the rights then count from a known origin.
  #
  # N IS THE LEVEL'S POSITION IN `ROUTE_EFFORT_STOPS`, DERIVED (controller ruling
  # S4-R9). The `case` that used to sit here — `low) n=0 ;; medium) n=1 ;; …` —
  # was a second hand-kept enumeration of the effort vocabulary in the same file
  # as the first, and the kind that rots quietly: a stop inserted in the middle
  # of the measured order would leave every level above it pointing one row low,
  # and the only symptom is a pane running the wrong effort.
  # The ID, not the tmux name (ruling S4-R14, and `_route_escape`'s own note):
  # every target this function presses a key into is `_tmux`'s answer, so the
  # auth-pane scan in `ccd-account-pane.test.ts` can see it derive.
  local id="$1" level="$2" t n i
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  local -a stops=()
  if [[ "$level" == ultracode ]]; then
    tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "/effort ultracode"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter; sleep 1
    _route_ack_wait "$t" "ultracode.*this session only" && return 0
    _route_escape "$id"; return 1
  fi
  read -r -a stops <<< "$ROUTE_EFFORT_STOPS"
  n=-1
  for ((i = 0; i < ${#stops[@]}; i++)); do [[ "${stops[i]}" == "$level" ]] && { n="$i"; break; }; done
  # A LEVEL WITH NO STOP IS ITS OWN CONDITION, not `apply-unconfirmed`. Nothing
  # is typed, so there is no pane to go and look at, and no Escape to press.
  if (( n < 0 )); then
    KS_WHY=no-slider-stop
    KS_DETAIL="effort=$level is not one of the measured slider stops ($ROUTE_EFFORT_STOPS)"
    return 2
  fi
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "/effort"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter; sleep 1
  for ((i = 0; i < 7; i++)); do tmux send-keys -t "$t" Left; sleep 1; done
  for ((i = 0; i < n; i++)); do tmux send-keys -t "$t" Right; sleep 1; done
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "s"; sleep 1
  # BOTH SHAPES ARE TOLERATED. On Opus 5 a conversation that already has turns
  # gets a second dialog — `Change effort level? … 1. Yes, switch to <level>` —
  # because the cached prefix has to be re-read; on Fable 5.1 the same two-turn
  # history showed none (measured, D-2808). Confirm it if it is there.
  if tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -q "Yes, switch"; then
    tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter; sleep 1
  fi
  _route_ack_wait "$t" "Set effort level to $level.*\(this session only\)" && return 0
  _route_escape "$id"; return 1
}

_route_picker_rows() {   # pane -> one row per line: <n>\t<cursor 0|1>\t<label>\t<description>
  # THE ONE SANITISER the picker arithmetic depends on, `_pane_ctx_pct`'s role
  # one seam over: `<n>` is a `[0-9]+` capture and nothing else from the pane
  # reaches an arithmetic context. Do not add a second guard downstream.
  #
  # The measured row shapes, both lanes: `1. Default (recommended)  Sonnet 5 ·
  # Efficient for routine tasks`, `3. Fable ✔  Fable 5.1 · Most capable…`, and on
  # the codex lane `2. gpt-5.6-sol ✔  Custom Opus model` — a LABEL, an optional
  # `✔` marking the current model, then two-or-more spaces, then a DESCRIPTION.
  # The cursor row carries `❯`. Splitting on the FIRST double space rather than a
  # greedy match is deliberate: a description may itself contain single spaces
  # and a greedy label would swallow all of it.
  local line n cur rest label desc
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [[ "$line" =~ ^[[:space:]]*(❯[[:space:]]*)?([0-9]+)\.[[:space:]]+(.*)$ ]] || continue
    cur=0; [[ -n "${BASH_REMATCH[1]}" ]] && cur=1
    n="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"; rest="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"
    label="${rest%%  *}"
    if [[ "$label" == "$rest" ]]; then desc=""; else desc="${rest#*  }"; fi
    desc="${desc#"${desc%%[![:space:]]*}"}"                 # ltrim
    label="${label%"${label##*[![:space:]]}"}"              # rtrim
    label="${label%✔}"
    label="${label%"${label##*[![:space:]]}"}"              # rtrim again, without the tick
    printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$n" "$cur" "$label" "$desc"
  done <<< "$1"
  return 0
}

_route_re_quote() {   # text -> stdout the same text as a LITERAL extended regular expression
  # THE SUBJECT COMES OFF THE PANE, not out of a closed vocabulary, so it can
  # carry regex metacharacters: `Default (recommended)` does, and an unescaped
  # `(` makes `grep -E` refuse the whole pattern — which no caller could tell
  # apart from "the pane never acknowledged it".
  printf '%s' "$1" | sed 's/[][\\.*^$(){}?+|]/\\&/g'
}

_route_ack_subject() {   # label desc want -> stdout the ERE naming the model the selected row sets
  # THE ACKNOWLEDGEMENT IS BOUND TO THE ROW `s` WAS PRESSED ON (final review
  # finding 3). `Set model to .* for this session only` binds NO subject, so any
  # earlier model acknowledgement still on the pane — a human's own `/model` tap
  # minutes ago, a previous applier run — satisfied it, and `class` was recorded
  # applied against a pane still running the old model. Nothing is pending after
  # that, so the retry budget is never spent and the next look is the settle's.
  #
  # WHICH WORD THE ACK CARRIES IS MEASURED, AND THE TWO LANES DIFFER (research
  # doc row 232, verbatim): the Anthropic lane answers `Set model to Haiku 4.5
  # for this session only` — the DESCRIPTION's model name, on a row LABELLED
  # `Haiku` — and the codex lane answers `Set model to gpt-5.6-terra …`, which
  # is that row's LABEL. Both readings of the one row we selected are offered
  # and either confirms it; neither is invented here, and a row carrying only a
  # label (the picker shapes this tree's own fixtures use) binds on that.
  #
  # THE `Default` ROW NAMES NO MODEL OF ITS OWN. Where its description carries
  # the ` · ` model name the bind is the same as any other row's; where it does
  # not (`Use the default model (currently …)`) there is nothing on the row to
  # bind to, and that ONE row keeps the loose subject rather than failing shut
  # on every `class=default` apply. An unbindable acknowledgement is stated
  # here, not silently narrowed into a refusal.
  local label="$1" desc="$2" want="$3" model=""
  [[ "$desc" == *" · "* ]] && model="${desc%% · *}"
  if [[ -n "$model" ]]; then printf '(%s|%s)' "$(_route_re_quote "$label")" "$(_route_re_quote "$model")"
  elif [[ -n "$label" && "$want" != Default ]]; then _route_re_quote "$label"
  else printf '.*'
  fi
}

_route_type_model() {   # id class wrapper -> 0 acknowledged | 1 unconfirmed | 2 no picker row
  # THE CURSOR IS READ, NEVER ASSUMED. The picker opens on the CURRENT model's
  # row, not on row 1, and `Up` on row 1 WRAPS to the last row on both lanes
  # (measured) — so "press Up×N to pin at the top" walks somewhere unpredictable
  # and then presses `s` on it. The only sound drive is to parse the rows, find
  # the cursor, find the target, and press the delta.
  #
  # WHICH ROW IS THE TARGET differs by backend, because the lanes label their
  # rows differently. An Anthropic lane labels them by CLASS (`Sonnet`, `Opus`,
  # `Haiku`, `Fable`, plus `Default (recommended)`), so the first word of the
  # label is the class's display word — derived by capitalising the class, never
  # a fifth hand-kept spelling of `ROUTE_CLASSES`. A codex lane labels them by
  # model ID and puts the class in the DESCRIPTION (`Custom Opus model`), so
  # that is what is matched there; `default` is the `Default` row on both.
  #
  # A CLASS THE PICKER DOES NOT LIST IS NOT A FAILURE OF THIS FUNCTION. `fable`
  # is offered only while it is already the current model (measured: the same
  # entitled lane launched `--model opus` lists four rows and no Fable), so a
  # live change TO fable is a RELAUNCH, not a keystroke. Escape out, answer 2,
  # and let the tick record it and the settle apply it.
  # The ID, not the tmux name — `_route_type_effort`'s note, same reason.
  local id="$1" cls="$2" w="$3" t rows n cur label desc want target=0 curs=0 i tlabel="" tdesc=""
  t=$(_tmux "$id")
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "/model"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter; sleep 1
  rows=$(_route_picker_rows "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -12)")
  if [[ "$cls" == default || -z "$cls" ]]; then want="Default"; else want="${cls^}"; fi
  while IFS=$'\t' read -r n cur label desc; do
    [[ -n "$n" ]] || continue
    [[ "$cur" == 1 ]] && curs="$n"
    # THE ROW'S OWN WORDS ARE KEPT, not just its number: `_route_ack_subject`
    # binds the acknowledgement to the model this row selects, and the loop
    # variables hold the LAST row read by the time it is asked.
    if [[ "$want" == Default ]] || _is_anthropic_backend "$w"; then
      [[ "${label%% *}" == "$want" ]] && { target="$n"; tlabel="$label"; tdesc="$desc"; }
    else
      [[ "$desc" == *"Custom $want model"* ]] && { target="$n"; tlabel="$label"; tdesc="$desc"; }
    fi
  done <<< "$rows"
  # TWO SENTENCES, ONE SLUG — `status-unreadable`'s shape one seam over. "This
  # picker lists no row for the class" and "this capture carries no cursor row at
  # all" send an operator to different places, and the remedy for both is the
  # same (leave it to the settle), so they share the word and differ in the
  # detail the swap.log line carries.
  if [[ "$target" == 0 || "$curs" == 0 ]]; then
    _route_escape "$id"
    KS_WHY=no-picker-row
    if [[ "$curs" == 0 ]]; then KS_DETAIL="class=$cls, the capture carries no cursor row"
    else KS_DETAIL="class=$cls"; fi
    return 2
  fi
  if (( target > curs )); then
    for ((i = curs; i < target; i++)); do tmux send-keys -t "$t" Down; sleep 1; done
  elif (( curs > target )); then
    for ((i = target; i < curs; i++)); do tmux send-keys -t "$t" Up; sleep 1; done
  fi
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "s"; sleep 1
  # THE SUBJECT IS THE ROW'S, AND THE READ IS THE PICKER'S OWN WINDOW — the same
  # twelve rows this function captured the picker from, because the
  # acknowledgement renders where the picker stood, above the prompt. The two
  # together are what stop a stale `Set model to …` further up the pane
  # confirming a change that never happened (final review finding 3).
  _route_ack_wait "$t" "Set model to $(_route_ack_subject "$tlabel" "$tdesc" "$want").*for this session only" 12 && return 0
  _route_escape "$id"; return 1
}

_route_apply_now() {   # id -> 0 everything wanted is applied | 1 something is still pending
  # CLASS FIRST, THEN EFFORT, and the order is a measurement: a session-only
  # effort SURVIVES a model change (D-2811 — `Sonnet 5 with medium effort` after
  # `/model sonnet`), so applying the effort first and the class second would
  # still be correct but would pay the model picker's own effort control the
  # chance to move it. Class first is the order with nothing to re-check.
  #
  # NOTHING IS RECORDED APPLIED WITHOUT AN ACKNOWLEDGEMENT, except the one case
  # where there is nothing to acknowledge: `effort=auto` is spec §5.1's
  # absent-equivalent and types NOTHING at all — the live level stands until the
  # next settle composes the argv. Recording it applied is what stops the tick
  # re-deciding that every 5 seconds.
  #
  # AN ABSENT FIELD IS NEVER TYPED (controller ruling S4-R8) and NEITHER IS AN
  # INERT ONE (S4-R9): `_route_wanted` answers an EMPTY word for both, and the
  # `-n` on each arm below is what turns that word into "nothing to do here".
  # This is not the same as `default`/`auto`, which are real values the record
  # states and which each have their own no-keystroke handling.
  #
  # EVERY REFUSAL THAT CAME BACK FROM THE PANE COUNTS AS AN ATTEMPT
  # (`_route_try_bump`, ruling S4-R7): `apply-unconfirmed`, `no-picker-row` and
  # `no-slider-stop` are the three, and they are exactly the ones that can be
  # PERMANENT. The idle predicate's refusals never reach this function.
  local id="$1" w want cls eff have rc
  w=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  want=$(_route_wanted "$id"); cls="${want%% *}"; eff="${want#* }"
  have=$(_route_applied_get "$id" class)
  if [[ -n "$cls" && "$cls" != "$have" ]]; then
    _route_type_model "$id" "$cls" "$w"; rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
      0) _route_applied_set "$id" class "$cls"; _route_note_clear "$id" ;;
      2) _route_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; _route_try_bump "$id"; return 1 ;;
      *) _route_note "$id" apply-unconfirmed "class=$cls"; _route_try_bump "$id"; return 1 ;;
    esac
  fi
  have=$(_route_applied_get "$id" effort)
  if [[ -n "$eff" && "$eff" != "$have" ]]; then
    if [[ "$eff" == auto ]]; then
      _route_applied_set "$id" effort auto
      _route_note_clear "$id"
    else
      _route_type_effort "$id" "$eff"; rc=$?
      case "$rc" in
        0) _route_applied_set "$id" effort "$eff"; _route_note_clear "$id" ;;
        # rc 2 is `no-slider-stop` — a level with no stop on the measured slider
        # (S4-R9). Its own refusal word rather than `apply-unconfirmed`, because
        # the remedy is different: nothing was typed at all and no acknowledgement
        # was ever possible, so an operator reading `apply-unconfirmed` would go
        # looking at a pane that never saw a keystroke.
        2) _route_note "$id" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; _route_try_bump "$id"; return 1 ;;
        *) _route_note "$id" apply-unconfirmed "effort=$eff"; _route_try_bump "$id"; return 1 ;;
      esac
    fi
  fi
  return 0
}

_route_apply_check() {   # id — the supervise tick's applier. Always 0: a tick never fails the loop.
  # CHEAP WHEN NOTHING IS PENDING, which is the steady state of every session on
  # the fleet. The registry comparison comes FIRST and returns before any tmux
  # call at all; only a genuinely pending field pays a pane read.
  #
  # FIVE GATES, IN THIS ORDER, and the order is the whole design:
  #   1. nothing pending — clear the marker AND the retry counter, return.
  #   2. a record with no `routeapplied` file at all — SEED it from the served
  #      values and type nothing (S4-R8): the record predates the applier and the
  #      pane is already running what its last settle composed.
  #   3. the bounded retry (S4-R7) — a field that has been refused by the pane
  #      `ROUTE_RETRY_MAX` times, or refused inside `ROUTE_RETRY_BACKOFF`, does
  #      not get typed again this tick. This is BEFORE the pane read on purpose:
  #      a session that has given up should not even be captured.
  #   4. the compactor's own keystroke (final review finding 5) — this tick may
  #      have sent `/compact` into this pane moments ago, on the line above this
  #      one, and the idle predicate's inputs both LAG that keystroke.
  #   5. the idle predicate, whose refusals are notes and never attempts.
  _route_apply_pending "$1" || { _route_note_clear "$1"; _route_tries_clear "$1"; return 0; }
  _route_apply_seed "$1" && return 0
  _route_retry_ok "$1" || return 0
  _route_compact_settling "$1" && { _route_note "$1" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  _pane_for_keystroke "$1" || { _route_note "$1" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  _idle_for_keystroke "$1" "$KS_PANE" "" || { _route_note "$1" "$KS_WHY" "$KS_DETAIL"; return 0; }
  _route_apply_now "$1"
  return 0
}

# Two readings of the input box from one normalised pane: `_pane_box_draft`
# keeps `draftOf`'s marker-row contract, while `_pane_box_has_content` asks the
# safety question every synthesized ccd action needs — whether ANY box row is
# occupied. Both share the dim/SGR normalizer, so those rules cannot drift.
#
# TWO corrections to the inline first-match, plain-space-anchored grep this
# replaces, both measured live on 2026-08-14. (The old pattern is DESCRIBED
# and not quoted: `ccd-pane-box-draft.test.ts`'s mutation guard scans this
# whole file for it, so a verbatim copy in a comment would keep that guard
# red for ever and teach the next reader that the guard is noise.)
#   • the LAST marker line, not the first. Past user turns render with the same
#     `❯` prefix in the scrollback ABOVE the box, so `-m1` returned a stale turn
#     (`❯ /compact`) while the real box was empty — failing shut.
#   • either separator. A real TYPED draft's box row is `❯` + U+00A0, not a
#     plain space (verbatim `capture-pane -e` bytes, LIVE_CU_FRAMES in
#     server/test/send.test.ts), so the plain-space pattern matched nothing on
#     a genuinely occupied box — failing OPEN, i.e. typing over a human draft.
#
# LC_ALL=C on both filters, deliberately: the marker and the separator are
# matched as BYTES (`❯` = e2 9d af, NBSP = c2 a0). A multibyte locale makes a
# pattern over raw bytes implementation-defined, and this guard is the only
# thing standing between an injector and somebody's unsent message.
#
# The separator alternation is `\(\xc2\xa0\| \)*` and NOT a bracket expression
# `[ \xc2\xa0]*`: a bracket expression matches those three bytes INDIVIDUALLY,
# so a draft beginning with any Latin-1-supplement character (`£` = c2 a3,
# `¡` = c2 a1) would have its 0xc2 LEAD BYTE eaten and the caller would be
# handed a mojibake fragment of the operator's own text. The alternation
# consumes the NBSP only as a whole.
#
# The trailing trim stays `[[:space:]]` under the same C locale — byte-for-byte
# what the two guards did before, so a pane that behaved yesterday behaves today.
#
# THE INPUT IS AN `-e` CAPTURE, and the escape strip is the third correction
# (measured on this box, 2026-08-17). Both halves are needed and either alone is
# a defect:
#   • plain `-p`, which both call sites used to pass: Claude Code's DIM
#     ghost-suggestion in an EMPTY box arrives as ordinary characters, so this
#     read `continue` off an idle session and both callers — which refuse on a
#     non-empty read and never retry — refused for ever. `draftOf` was never
#     exposed to this because it strips the dim run first.
#   • `-e` with no strip is WORSE than plain: a live box row starts with a
#     colour code (`\e[39m❯` + NBSP, verbatim in LIVE_CU_FRAMES), so the marker
#     grep below misses it and `tail -1` lands on a plain SCROLLBACK turn.
# So: dim runs first, then any remaining SGR, then the marker rules. That order
# is `draftOf`'s and it is load-bearing — stripping SGR first would erase the
# `\e[2m` that marks the ghost as a ghost and promote it to a real draft.
#
# The dim-run pattern mirrors `DIM_SPAN`'s two hard-won subtleties: OTHER SGR
# codes are allowed INSIDE the run (the queue hint renders `\e[2m\e[39mPress up
# …`), but a code whose first parameter is `0` is a reset and ENDS the run
# rather than being absorbed by it — tmux 3.4 normalises a dim-off followed by
# another attribute into a combined `\e[0;1m`, and a pattern that swallowed that
# would eat the real text after it. `[1-9][0-9;]*` is the interleaved case and
# `0[0-9;]*` the terminator, which is exactly that split. No non-greedy operator
# is needed (and ERE has none): the interleaved alternative cannot match a reset
# and `[^\e]` cannot match an ESC, so the match physically cannot run past the
# first terminator.
_pane_box_plain() {   # ANSI (`capture-pane -e`) pane-text on stdin -> dim ghosts and SGR removed
  LC_ALL=C sed -E $'s/\033\\[2m((\033\\[[1-9][0-9;]*m)|[^\033])*\033\\[0[0-9;]*m//g; s/\033\\[[0-9;]*m//g'
}

_pane_box_draft() {   # ANSI (`capture-pane -e`) pane-text -> the box's own marker row, or empty
  printf '%s\n' "$1" \
    | _pane_box_plain \
    | LC_ALL=C grep $'^\xe2\x9d\xaf' \
    | tail -1 \
    | LC_ALL=C sed -E $'s/^\xe2\x9d\xaf(\xc2\xa0| )*//; s/[[:space:]]*$//'
  # A pane with no box at all is an EMPTY BOX, not an error: `grep` exits 1 on
  # no match and this file runs under `set -o pipefail`, so without this the
  # status of the pipeline would leak out as a failure the callers (which read
  # the STDOUT, `[[ -n "$cur" ]]`) never asked about.
  return 0
}

_pane_box_has_content() {   # ANSI (`capture-pane -e`) pane-text -> 0 iff any real box row is occupied (D-2457)
  # `_pane_box_draft` deliberately answers only the marker row, matching
  # `draftOf`. That is not enough before a synthesized action: a human can press
  # Alt+Enter first, leaving an EMPTY marker row and real text on an indented
  # continuation row. Scan from the LAST marker to the closing horizontal rule,
  # exactly like server `hasContentBelowMarker`; chrome after the rule is not box
  # content. The shared normalizer keeps dim ghost suggestions empty here too.
  # Ask the marker-row reader first both to keep ONE rule for that row and so a
  # test that stubs the seam beneath an injector still controls the full guard.
  [[ -n "$(_pane_box_draft "$1")" ]] && return 0
  printf '%s\n' "$1" | _pane_box_plain | LC_ALL=C awk '
    function nonblank(s) { gsub(/[[:space:]]/, "", s); return s != "" }
    function rule(s, n) {
      gsub(/[[:space:]]/, "", s)
      n = gsub(/─/, "", s)
      return n >= 8 && s == ""
    }
    {
      line = $0
      if (index(line, "❯") == 1) {
        active = 1; found = 0
        sub(/^❯( | )*/, "", line)
        if (nonblank(line)) found = 1
        next
      }
      if (active) {
        if (rule(line)) { active = 0; next }
        if (nonblank(line)) found = 1
      }
    }
    END { exit(found ? 0 : 1) }
  '
}

_pane_external_import_gate() {   # pane text -> 0 iff Claude Code's external-CLAUDE.md-import dialog is UP (D-3108)
  # THE DIALOG, measured off the shipped bundle (Claude Code 2.1.278) rather
  # than remembered: title `Allow external CLAUDE.md file imports?`, body "This
  # project's CLAUDE.md imports files outside the current working directory.
  # Never allow this for third-party repositories.", and two options —
  # `Yes, allow external imports` / `No, disable external imports` — rendered
  # with `cancelFirst: true, focus: "cancel"`, so THE CURSOR STARTS ON NO.
  #
  # WHY IT IS AN ACCOUNT-SWITCH PROBLEM, AND WHERE THE STATE LIVES. The answer
  # is recorded as `hasClaudeMdExternalIncludesApproved` (with
  # `hasClaudeMdExternalIncludesWarningShown` beside it) on the PROJECT entry
  # inside `$CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/.claude.json` — one file per lane. `cmd_swap`
  # carries the transcript, the sidecars and `tasks/<uuid>`; it does not carry
  # this, and it must not: that file is Claude Code's, not ccrc's, and the
  # standing ruling is that a ccrc integration lives in artifacts ccrc owns
  # outright. So the destination lane has never approved this project, the
  # dialog is raised on the landing, and the session sits on it until a human
  # arrives. Measured on this fleet 2026-09-19: the lanes whose config dir
  # carries a user `CLAUDE.md` with an `@` import hold 0 approvals across 11-14
  # projects each, while two lanes that answered it long ago hold 56.
  #
  # THE SENTENCE ALONE NEVER FIRES, and that is the whole reason this is a
  # function rather than a `grep` in the loop. The two 2.1.198 gates already
  # pair their title with the `Enter to confirm` footer so the same words inside
  # a RESTORED TRANSCRIPT cannot synthesize a keystroke — and this title is
  # worse than theirs, because a session working on `ccd` has this very
  # paragraph on screen. So the gate is the LIVE SELECTOR: the title, AND a
  # cursor line sitting on one of the dialog's OWN two options. Prose cannot
  # produce that pair; only the dialog can.
  #
  # The cursor is read exactly as `_answer_two_option_dialog` reads it — the
  # LAST `❯` line, falling back to a bare `>` at line start for a terminal that
  # renders the glyph differently — because the two must agree about which line
  # is the cursor or this predicate would admit a dialog that helper then
  # answers blind.
  local pane="$1" cursor
  grep -q "Allow external CLAUDE.md file imports" <<<"$pane" || return 1
  cursor=$(printf '%s\n' "$pane" | LC_ALL=C grep -F $'\xe2\x9d\xaf' | tail -1)
  [[ -z "$cursor" ]] && cursor=$(printf '%s\n' "$pane" | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*>' | tail -1)
  [[ "$cursor" == *"allow external imports"* || "$cursor" == *"disable external imports"* ]]
}

# Auth screens must never receive synthesized keystrokes: on a numbered login
# menu a stray Enter selects whatever the cursor is on.
_pane_login_screen() {
  grep -Eq 'Select login method|Invalid API key|Please run /login' <<<"$1"
}

# Reads which line of a two-option TUI dialog CARRIES THE CURSOR and answers
# accordingly — the mechanism the trust-folder and Bypass-Permissions gates
# both need, because neither dialog's ordinal default is stable: Claude Code
# 2.1.263 (measured 2026-09-08) renders the trust prompt with "No, exit"
# FIRST, cursor on it, where an older build put "Yes, I trust this folder"
# first instead. A bare Enter keyed to either ordinal is a coin flip; reading
# the cursor line is not. $3 is the substring that identifies the dialog's
# AFFIRMATIVE option's own line (e.g. "trust this folder", "Yes, I accept") —
# matched against whichever line carries the cursor glyph `❯` (the LAST such
# line, if capture-pane's scrollback holds more than one — same "freshest
# wins" rule `_pane_box_draft` uses above), or a bare `>` at line start as a
# fallback for a terminal that renders the glyph differently. Cursor-on-
# affirmative → Enter; anything else (cursor on the other option, or no
# cursor line found at all) → Down, THEN Enter — the safe read of "can't
# tell" is "assume the negative default", exactly the failure this replaces.
_answer_two_option_dialog() {   # pane tmuxname affirmative-substring
  local pane="$1" t="$2" affirmative="$3" cursor_line
  cursor_line=$(printf '%s\n' "$pane" | LC_ALL=C grep -F $'\xe2\x9d\xaf' | tail -1)
  [[ -z "$cursor_line" ]] && cursor_line=$(printf '%s\n' "$pane" | grep -E '^[[:space:]]*>' | tail -1)
  if [[ -n "$cursor_line" && "$cursor_line" == *"$affirmative"* ]]; then
    tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  else
    tmux send-keys -t "$t" Down; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  fi
}

_accept_first_run_prompts() {   # tmuxname [fromswap] — clear startup gates, then CONFIRM the main TUI is up.
  # Returns as soon as "/rc active" (or another live marker) appears. Gates, order-agnostic,
  # each needing a DIFFERENT keystroke:
  #   • trust-folder prompt — layout order is NOT stable across Claude Code
  #       versions (2.1.263 flipped it, putting "No, exit" first); answered by
  #       `_answer_two_option_dialog`, which reads which line CARRIES THE
  #       CURSOR rather than trusting either ordinal default.
  #   • Bypass-Permissions warning — same mechanism, same helper: the cursor
  #       line decides Enter vs Down-then-Enter. (A bare Enter on the wrong
  #       line here would EXIT.)
  #   • (2.1.198+) big-transcript resume gate "Resume from summary (recommended)" → pick
  #       "2. Resume full session as-is" (Down, Enter) so nothing gets silently compacted.
  #   • (2.1.198+) per-session "Try the new fullscreen renderer?" opt-in → pick "2. Not now"
  #       (Down, Enter): ccd/ccclip scrape the pane via capture-pane; the new renderer +
  #       mouse support may break that scraping.
  # The two 2.1.198 gates are matched together with the "Enter to confirm" menu footer so the
  # same words inside a restored transcript can't false-trigger keystrokes.
  # Window is ~15 min because 700k+-token resumes take minutes between gates; healthy sessions
  # return the moment the live marker shows, so the common path stays fast.
  # rc 0 = a live marker appeared (the TUI is up); 2 = an auth screen (below); 3 = the tmux session
  # vanished mid-poll; 4 = the window expired with no marker; 5 = a hard block (limit/spend banner,
  # or lost auth): waiting longer cannot fix it; 6 = a live pane under READER_MIN_COLS, or of unreadable width, so every gate stood down. 3 and 4 both used to be "0 after ~15
  # minutes" (M6): the caller printed `started`/`ensured` over a session that was never there.
  local t="$1" fromswap="${2:-0}" bound i pane t0
  # A WALL-CLOCK bound as well as the iteration cap. The cap alone is not a
  # bound anyone can reason about: gate branches sleep 2 EXTRA seconds each, so
  # the same 450 iterations are ~900s plain and ~1350s when gates fire. The
  # bound is NOT keyed off `fromswap` — measured, the discriminator runs
  # backwards from the obvious reading: `cmd_swap` writes `lastswap` two lines
  # before the restart, so a SWAP is fromswap=1 (the fast branch) and a fresh
  # `ws-add` is fromswap=0. Keyed off it, ws-add would get the LONG window.
  #
  # THE BOUND ARRIVES IN A VARIABLE, NEVER IN ARGV, and that is a SECURITY
  # property, not a style choice. It reached this function as a positional for
  # one commit (Task 5), threaded down from a second positional on `cmd_ensure`
  # — and `ccd ensure` is granted by the agent's exec whitelist, whose own
  # docstring says "tokens after the prefix are unconstrained". `(( ))` below
  # evaluates its operand's CONTENTS as an arithmetic expression, and a command
  # substitution inside an ARRAY SUBSCRIPT runs before the arithmetic even
  # errors: `bound='REG[$(rm -rf ~)]'` executes. That made a wire-reachable
  # argv word into arbitrary code as the fleet user. `CCD_SETTLE_BOUND` is the
  # CCD_IN_UNIT idiom instead — a caller's dynamically-scoped `local`, never
  # exported, never an argv token, so nothing on the wire can reach it.
  #
  # VALIDATED ANYWAY, because a comment is a request and a red suite is a
  # mechanism: `(( ))` on any non-literal is a footgun, and the next editor
  # deserves a guard rather than a paragraph. Anything that is not 1-5 digits
  # degrades to the production default — never to zero (an instant rc 4 on
  # every spawn) and never to "no bound".
  bound="${CCD_SETTLE_BOUND:-$SPAWN_SETTLE_S}"
  [[ "$bound" =~ ^[0-9]{1,5}$ ]] || bound=$SPAWN_SETTLE_S
  t0=$(date +%s)
  for i in $(seq 1 "$SPAWN_GATE_TRIES"); do
    (( $(date +%s) - t0 >= bound )) && return 4
    # BEFORE the capture, and this order is the fix: `capture-pane` against a dead session fails into
    # an EMPTY $pane, which matches no branch below, so the loop used to poll a corpse for the full
    # window and then answer success. `has-session` is the one probe that distinguishes "not ready
    # yet" from "not there", and it costs one call — except when it just failed, in which case a
    # SECOND probe confirms it before this returns 3. A single failed `has-session` read on a box
    # running a whole fleet of sessions can be transient load, not a vanished pane; tipping a healthy
    # 15-minute resume into a false rc 3 on one flaky read would turn a spurious hiccup into a hard,
    # loudly-reported failure (this task made rc 3/4 loud specifically so it WOULD be reported — a
    # false positive is now as visible as a real one). A short debounce, not a retry loop: one
    # re-check, not a second pass at the whole window.
    if ! tmux has-session -t "$t" 2>/dev/null; then
      sleep 1
      tmux has-session -t "$t" 2>/dev/null || return 3
    fi
    # §6.3 — THE WIDTH STAND-DOWN, AND IT SITS BELOW THE LIVENESS PROBE ON
    # PURPOSE. `tmux list-panes -t cc-nope` exits 1, so the width query cannot
    # tell "too narrow to read" from "never came up": ahead of the probe it
    # answered 6 for a session that does not exist, and rc 6's sentence tells
    # the operator to CLOSE A DRAWER while rc 3's tells them the truth and names
    # the recovery (`clear $REG/<id>.started first`). Two conditions an operator
    # handles differently, collapsed onto one code and one wrong sentence — the
    # Global Constraint's own "no overloaded null at a seam". Below the probe,
    # a dead session still earns 3 and only a LIVE, unreadable pane earns 6.
    #
    # NO has-session PROBE OF ITS OWN, deliberately: the block above is already
    # the authority on liveness, and `ccd-spawn-verdict.test.ts` pins the count
    # at exactly TWO reads per verdict (one, plus the debounce) — a third here
    # would weaken the pin that kills the single-read mutant.
    #
    # MEASURED ONCE, on the first pass. The loop runs up to SPAWN_GATE_TRIES
    # times on a healthy slow resume, and re-measuring each pass would fork a
    # `list-panes` per iteration with no new decision behind it; the pane's
    # width is a property of the client, not of how far the TUI has come up.
    #
    # rc 6 is NEW and its own code — 3/4/5 are load-bearing at four call sites
    # plus `_supervised_start` and must not be renumbered. `${1#cc-}` because
    # this function knows the tmux NAME and `_pane_measurable` takes the id
    # `_tmux` built it from.
    [[ "$i" == 1 ]] && ! _pane_measurable "${1#cc-}" && return 6
    pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null)
    # THE EXTERNAL-IMPORT GATE, AND IT IS ASKED ABOVE THE READY MARKERS (D-3107).
    # Every other gate below is a full-screen startup dialog that hides the
    # footer, so the marker check cannot reach them; this one is raised while
    # `CLAUDE.md` is being loaded and a pane can carry it WITH chrome the marker
    # regex matches. Below the markers it would be unreachable exactly when it
    # fires, and the settle would report a ready TUI over a modal nobody
    # answered — the shape this fix exists to remove, with the settle's own
    # verdict now lying about it too.
    #
    # ANSWERED `YES`, DELIBERATELY, and the dialog's own warning is the reason
    # this is safe rather than in spite of it: it says "Never allow this for
    # third-party repositories", and every pane this function touches is a
    # workspace `ccd` itself created from the operator's own repo. The posture
    # is also already the fleet's — `skipDangerousModePermissionPrompt` is set
    # in every lane's settings.json, and the two arms below auto-accept the
    # trust-folder and Bypass-Permissions dialogs, both strictly more dangerous
    # than this one. NO KILL-SWITCH, for the same reason those two have none:
    # one shape for one class of gate.
    #
    # `_answer_two_option_dialog` reads WHICH LINE CARRIES THE CURSOR rather
    # than trusting an ordinal, which matters more here than anywhere else in
    # this loop: this dialog ships `cancelFirst: true, focus: "cancel"`, so a
    # bare Enter is `No, disable external imports` — the operator's own imports
    # silently switched off for that project, on that lane, permanently.
    if _pane_external_import_gate "$pane"; then
      _answer_two_option_dialog "$pane" "$t" "allow external imports"; sleep 2; continue
    fi
    # ready markers: any of these means the TUI is up. NB the RC indicator sometimes renders
    # as bare "/rc" (not "/rc active"), so the permission-mode footer is the reliable signal —
    # without it supervise sits here for the full window and never reaches its watch loop.
    echo "$pane" | grep -Eq "/rc active|\? for shortcuts|esc to interrupt|shift\+tab to cycle|← for agents" && return 0
    if echo "$pane" | grep -q "Enter to confirm" && echo "$pane" | grep -q "Resume from summary"; then
      if [[ "$fromswap" == "1" ]]; then
        # Swap landing: auto-compact — the target account pays one summary, not a full
        # 700k-token history re-read (user decision 2026-07-03). Option 1 is the default.
        tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
      else
        # Same-account resurrect: keep full fidelity — "Resume full session as-is".
        tmux send-keys -t "$t" Down; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
      fi
      sleep 2; continue
    fi
    if echo "$pane" | grep -q "Enter to confirm" && echo "$pane" | grep -q "Try the new fullscreen renderer"; then
      tmux send-keys -t "$t" Down; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter; sleep 2; continue
    fi
    if echo "$pane" | grep -q "Bypass Permissions mode"; then
      _answer_two_option_dialog "$pane" "$t" "Yes, I accept"; sleep 2; continue
    fi
    if echo "$pane" | grep -q "trust this folder"; then
      _answer_two_option_dialog "$pane" "$t" "trust this folder"; sleep 2; continue
    fi
    # An auth screen (expired key, logged out, or the login-method menu) means this spawn is
    # stuck on a human step. No keystroke here could be right, so stop polling and report —
    # the caller (_spawn) must not chase this with an /effort injection either. Checked LAST,
    # after every ready-marker and gate branch above: those match specific, narrow footers
    # ("Enter to confirm" + a gate title, "Bypass Permissions mode", "trust this folder"), so a
    # substring hit for one of THIS check's banners anywhere else in ~50 lines of scrollback — a
    # restored transcript discussing an old auth bug, ccd's own source open in the pane — can't
    # short-circuit a healthy session or an unanswered gate. Only a pane that matches none of the
    # branches above reaches here, which is what a real (short, banner-only) login screen does.
    if _pane_login_screen "$pane"; then
      # No $id/$wrapper in scope here (this function only knows the tmux
      # NAME) — the warning that names the account is _spawn's job, once this
      # returns 2. Keep this function's own job pure: a verdict, not a report.
      return 2
    fi
    # A HARD BLOCK: a real limit/spend banner, or lost auth. This earns its own
    # code rather than joining 4, because the two say opposite things — an
    # expired window means *we do not know*, a hard block means *we know
    # exactly what is wrong and waiting longer cannot fix it*. Collapsing them
    # would be an adapter narrowing a distinction it received.
    #
    # CHECKED AFTER `_pane_login_screen`, AND THAT ORDER IS THE CONTRACT, not a
    # preference: `_pane_hard_blocked`'s regex also matches "Invalid API key"
    # and "Please run /login", and the whole reason the login check sits LAST
    # is that its banners appear in restored scrollback beside live gates.
    # Ahead of the gate branches this returns 5 on a pane holding an unanswered
    # Bypass-Permissions prompt, sends no keys, and parks the session one stray
    # Enter from "1. No, exit". 5, NOT 4: renumbering 3 or 4 would silently
    # retarget four call sites plus _supervised_start.
    if _pane_hard_blocked "$pane"; then
      return 5
    fi
    sleep 2
  done
  # The window expired. An explicit verdict, because falling out of a `for` loop under `set -uo
  # pipefail` with no `-e` returns the status of the last command — `sleep 2`, i.e. 0. That is M6.
  return 4
}

# ── WS-ADD'S GIT AND DISK JUDGEMENT OF A SLUG (programme ws-slug-collision) ──
# `_ws_slug_free` reads the REGISTRY and nothing else, and stays that way: its
# contract is that every reason it refuses is one `_ws_slug_residue` can name,
# and its `ws-gc` unclaimed-census caller is about registry rows alone. But a
# slug the registry has forgotten can still be held by git — a `ws/<slug>`
# branch that outlived its workspace (measured 2026-09-23: 27 of one repo's 33
# `ws/*` branches had no row) — and `cmd_ws_add` then died AFTER the pick, at
# `git worktree add`, which the dispatch route surfaced as a bare 502. This is
# the second judgement, consulted BESIDE the first at ws-add's three slug
# sites — `_ws_slug_new`'s random loop and its `CCD_WS_SLUG` arm, and
# `cmd_ws_add`'s named-slug refusal — all of them ahead of the fetch and of
# anything minted.
#
# THREE ANSWERS, one line on stdout:
#   rc 0  `free`                no refs/heads/ws/<slug>, nothing at all at $wt
#                               (not even a dangling link), and $wt is not a
#                               registered worktree of $main;
#   rc 1  `taken <what>`        `branch ws/<slug>`, `path <wt>` or
#                               `worktree <wt>` — the first one found;
#   rc 2  `unmeasurable <why>`  git could not answer.
# The third must never fold into either of the others. Folded into `free`, it
# retries into the very late death this exists to prevent; folded into
# `taken`, it burns all sixty draws and blames the slug for a repository git
# cannot read. `show-ref --verify --quiet` exits 1 on an absent ref and
# something else on a failure to read, so 1 is the ONLY status read as absence.
#
# THE REPOSITORY IS PROVED TO BE $main FIRST. git skips a `.git` it cannot read
# and keeps discovering UPWARDS, so a broken $main inside another repository
# (a dotfiles $HOME) answers `show-ref` from the outer one — measured, rc 1,
# which would read as `free`. Its toplevel, resolved, must be $main's.
#
# PATH IS `-e || -L`: `-e` follows a link, so a dangling one reads as absent
# while `git worktree add` still refuses the path. An empty directory is
# `taken` too, deliberately: ccd did not make it, so ccd cannot say whose it is.
#
# WORKTREE COMPARES RESOLVED PATHS. git prints every worktree path with its
# symlinks collapsed ($HOME/worktrees -> /data/worktrees in production) while
# $wt is ccd's own spelling, so both sides go through `_ws_realpath`, which
# resolves the longest EXISTING prefix — the case here being a registered
# worktree whose directory is gone. A `worktree list` that fails is
# `unmeasurable`: nothing listed is not nothing registered.
_ws_slug_git_state() {   # project slug -> one line + rc: 0 `free` | 1 `taken <what>` | 2 `unmeasurable <why>`
  local main="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$1" wt="$WORKTREES_ROOT/$1/$2" top rc listing line rwt
  top=$(git -C "$main" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) \
    || { echo "unmeasurable $main is not a repository git can read"; return 2; }
  [[ "$(_ws_realpath "$top")" == "$(_ws_realpath "$main")" ]] \
    || { echo "unmeasurable git reads $main as part of $top — its own repository is unreadable"; return 2; }
  git -C "$main" show-ref --verify --quiet "refs/heads/ws/$2"; rc=$?
  case "$rc" in
    0) echo "taken branch ws/$2"; return 1 ;;
    1) ;;
    *) echo "unmeasurable git show-ref failed (rc $rc) in $main"; return 2 ;;
  esac
  [[ -e "$wt" || -L "$wt" ]] && { echo "taken path $wt"; return 1; }
  listing=$(git -C "$main" worktree list --porcelain 2>/dev/null) \
    || { echo "unmeasurable git worktree list failed in $main"; return 2; }
  rwt=$(_ws_realpath "$wt")
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [[ "$line" == "worktree "* && "${line##*/}" == "$2" ]] || continue   # same leaf first: one resolve, not one per worktree
    [[ "$(_ws_realpath "${line#worktree }")" == "$rwt" ]] && { echo "taken worktree $wt"; return 1; }
  done <<< "$listing"
  echo "free"; return 0
}

_tmux_new_session() {   # argv for `tmux new-session` -> runs it, PLACING the server if this call creates it
  # Every session on this box is a child of ONE tmux server, and that server
  # lives in whichever cgroup happened to create it — so the unit file's
  # KillMode=process is the only thing standing between the deploy's
  # `try-restart claude-session@*` sweep and a fleet kill (systemd's default
  # KillMode is control-group). This places the server deliberately instead.
  #
  # THIS REPLACES `_tmux_server_ensure`, WHICH WAS A NO-OP IN PRODUCTION, and
  # the correction is worth stating because the old shape looked right and its
  # suite was green. It ran `systemd-run --scope … tmux start-server` as its
  # own step. But A TMUX SERVER WITH NO SESSIONS EXITS IMMEDIATELY: the
  # `start-server` died, the scope collected, and the very next
  # `tmux new-session` created a FRESH server in the caller's cgroup — the flaw
  # the design existed to remove. Measured on the fleet host 2026-08-18 after
  # the planned reboot: the server sat in
  # `claude-session@ccrc-pwa-calm-mesa.service`, one unit over from where it
  # started, with `ccrc-tmux-server.scope` nowhere in `systemctl --user`.
  #
  # THE FIRST SESSION IS THE SERVER'S LIFELINE, so the scope must wrap the call
  # that creates it. Verified on an isolated socket, same box, same day:
  #   systemd-run --user --scope --collect --unit=X tmux -L probe new-session -d …
  # leaves the server in `X.scope`; the identical command with `start-server`
  # leaves no server at all. The scope outlives the `new-session` process
  # because a scope is released when its cgroup EMPTIES, and the forked server
  # stays in it.
  #
  # NO NEW UNIT FILE: nothing extra to deploy or keep in sync, and it self-heals
  # — whenever the server is next created it lands in ccrc-tmux-server.scope.
  # It only takes effect when the server is next created, i.e. at a reboot:
  # cgroup membership cannot be changed for a live process without a D-Bus
  # StartTransientUnit adoption, and that is not something to attempt against a
  # process holding 17 live sessions.
  # FAST PATH — a server is already up, so this call cannot be the one that
  # creates it. No lock, no scope, byte-for-byte the old behaviour. Every spawn
  # after the first takes this path, which is why the lock below costs nothing
  # on the hot path.
  tmux list-sessions >/dev/null 2>&1 && { tmux new-session "$@"; return $?; }

  # SLOW PATH — this call MAY create the server, and that is a race. Measured
  # on the fleet host 2026-08-18, second attempt: 17 supervisors start within
  # the same second, all seventeen see no server, and all seventeen call
  # `systemd-run --scope --unit=ccrc-tmux-server`. Exactly one wins the unit
  # name; the journal shows the other fifteen refused with "Unit
  # ccrc-tmux-server.scope was already loaded or has a fragment file" — and
  # THEY DID NOT WAIT. Each fell straight through to a bare `new-session`, one
  # of THOSE created the server (in its own `claude-session@…` cgroup), and by
  # the time the scope winner ran, its `tmux new-session` merely CONNECTED to
  # that server. The scope was left holding a client that exited immediately,
  # so `--collect` reaped it. End state: `Started ccrc-tmux-server.scope` in
  # the journal, no scope on the box, and the server in the wrong cgroup again.
  #
  # The failed assumption was that a loser's fallback would attach to a server
  # the winner had already placed. Losing the unit-name race says nothing about
  # who reaches `new-session` first. So SERIALISE the decision instead of
  # inferring an order from it.
  local lock="$REG/.tmux-server.lock" lfd="" rc=0
  if command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    exec {lfd}>>"$lock" 2>/dev/null || lfd=""
    # BLOCKING, unlike ccd's three other flock sites. They pass `-n` because a
    # loser queueing behind a worktree checkout or a destructive verb is worse
    # than a refusal. Here waiting IS the mechanism: the loser must still be
    # here when the winner returns, so that its re-check below sees the server
    # and takes the attach path. A refusal would reproduce the exact bug.
    [[ -n "$lfd" ]] && { flock -w "$TMUX_SERVER_LOCK_WAIT" "$lfd" 2>/dev/null || :; }
    # A TIMED-OUT lock is NOT fatal and deliberately so: proceeding unserialised
    # is precisely the behaviour that shipped before this lock existed, so the
    # degraded mode is "possibly misplaced server", never "no session".
  fi

  # THE SECOND CHECK, and the reason this is a double-checked lock rather than
  # one: between the fast-path check and acquiring the lock, the winner may have
  # created the server. Asking again under the lock is what turns 16 racing
  # creators into 1 creator and 16 attachers.
  local -a scope=()
  # `command -v` and not `_have_systemctl`: this asks about systemd-run
  # specifically, and the single-box OSS story is a box with neither.
  if ! tmux list-sessions >/dev/null 2>&1 && command -v systemd-run >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    # --slice IS NOT OPTIONAL, and it is the correction to this mechanism's
    # own first version (D-307 (was D-B8-11)). A user scope with no `--slice` defaults to
    # `app.slice`; the fleet's aggregate memory ceiling
    # (deploy/systemd/app-claude-session.slice.d/limits.conf, MemoryMax=24G;
    # MemoryHigh deliberately `infinity` since 2026-09-09 — an aggregate
    # MemoryHigh throttles the whole slice, while an unreclaimable MemoryMax
    # breach invokes the cgroup-local OOM killer) hangs one level in, on
    # `app-claude\x2dsession.slice`.
    # Ubuntu's tmux is built with systemd support: it mints a transient
    # `tmux-spawn-<uuid>.scope` per pane and DERIVES that scope's slice from
    # its own, so placing the server placed all seventeen panes with it. This
    # mechanism shipped to protect the deploy sweep's blast radius and, unseen,
    # carried the whole fleet's memory out of its ceiling.
    #
    # Measured on the fleet host 2026-08-19, after the reboot that verified
    # D-305 (was D-B8-9): `app-claude\x2dsession.slice` held 66 MB under a 24G cap — the
    # supervise loops and nothing else — while `app.slice` held 17.6 GB
    # uncapped. Proved causal the same day on an isolated socket: a server
    # started inside the slice mints its pane scopes in that same slice.
    #
    # Named ABSOLUTELY, never derived from the caller: `_spawn_start` is
    # reached from cmd_supervise's unit, from an interactive shell, and from a
    # transient `systemd-run` — "wherever the creator happened to be" is the
    # defect this whole function exists to remove. The slice needs no unit
    # file (systemd instantiates one on demand); an install that skipped the
    # drop-in gets an uncapped slice, which is exactly what it has today.
    scope=(systemd-run --user --scope --quiet --collect --unit=ccrc-tmux-server
           --slice='app-claude\x2dsession.slice')
  fi
  if [[ ${#scope[@]} -gt 0 ]]; then
    # systemd-run may still refuse (no D-Bus, no user manager, or a scope of
    # this name that has not collected yet). A session that does not exist is a
    # worse outcome than one in the wrong cgroup, so fall through and create it
    # bare — the same trade the old `||` made.
    "${scope[@]}" tmux new-session "$@" || tmux new-session "$@"
    rc=$?
  else
    # No scope wanted (server appeared under the lock) or none available. ONE
    # attempt: retrying a `new-session` that failed on its own merits would hit
    # a duplicate-name error and turn one failure into a confusing two.
    tmux new-session "$@"
    rc=$?
  fi
  # SINGLE EXIT for the fd, deliberately — D-300 (was D-B8-4) recorded the cost of an early
  # `return` skipping a close: ccd is SOURCED, and `flock` treats two `open()`s
  # of one file in a process as strangers, so a leaked fd is a lock nothing
  # releases. `die` is exempt only because exiting closes it.
  [[ -n "$lfd" ]] && exec {lfd}>&-
  return $rc
}

# `_spawn_start`'s ANSWER, and it is a global rather than stdout for a
# correctness reason, not a taste one — so do not "clean this up" back into an
# `echo` read through `fs=$(_spawn_start …)`. That is exactly what this file
# shipped for one commit, and here is what it cost:
#
#   `_spawn_start` ends its validation in `die` — `echo …; exit 1`. `exit`
#   inside a command substitution kills ONLY the subshell, so the fatal
#   "incomplete registry for '<id>'" arrived at the caller as rc 1. And rc 1 is
#   in NO caller's failure set: `cmd_ws_add`, `cmd_start` and `cmd_ensure` all
#   ask `[[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$rc" -eq 4 || "$rc" -eq 5 || "$rc" -eq 6 ]]` (`3 || 4` at the time
#   this was measured; rc 1 is outside the set either way). Every one of them printed its
#   SUCCESS line and returned 0 over a spawn that never happened — M6's silent
#   success, one layer below where the wave had just finished closing it.
#
# Returning through a global makes the hazard UNREPRESENTABLE instead of merely
# tested-for: with no `$( )` at any call site, `die` stays process-fatal by
# construction, and the rule Tasks 7/8's new call sites have to remember shrinks
# to `_spawn_start … || return $?` — which is the ordinary shape anyway.
# `server/test/ccd-spawn-split.test.ts` pins both halves (status of the whole
# shell; no `$(` before `_spawn_start` in `type _spawn`).
#
# Declared at file scope, and initialised, for the same reason `GC_DIRTY_STATE`
# is: `set -u` would otherwise kill the shell outright at the first read by a
# caller whose `_spawn_start` never ran (a test stub, an early return). 0 is the
# safe value — full fidelity, per the failure direction below.
SPAWN_FROMSWAP=0

_resume_env() {   # -> one line of KEY=value assignments for `env`, switching on Claude Code's interrupted-turn resume (D-2227)
  local out="CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN=$RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_PROMPT='$RESUME_PROMPT'"
  [[ -n "$RESUME_REDRIVE_MAX_AGE_MS" ]] && out="$out CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN_MAX_AGE_MS=$RESUME_REDRIVE_MAX_AGE_MS"
  printf '%s' "$out"
}

# ── A CHILD'S TEMP ROOT (child-workspace reclamation, spec §5.2) ─────────────
# A workspace minted by `ws-add --child <runId>` carries `$REG/<id>.child`, and
# ONLY such a workspace spawns with TMPDIR pointed at `$HOME/.cc-tmp/<id>` — a
# directory the reclaim tail removes, so tool scratch that follows TMPDIR
# (`cdk.out` foremost) dies with the child by construction instead of by a
# second collector chasing paths. Every other session keeps the box's own
# TMPDIR: exporting this fleet-wide would move uncollected scratch out of an
# OS-reclaimed /tmp into $HOME for sessions that have no collector, which is a
# different spec (§5.2, "Children only").
#
# DRIVEN BY THE MARKER, NEVER BY ws-add's ARGV, and read in the one function
# every spawn path funnels through — `cmd_ws_add`, `cmd_ws_restore`,
# `cmd_start`, `cmd_ensure` (so every `Restart=always` respawn), `_spawn` (swap
# and friends) and both `_supervised_start` fallbacks. A decision taken in
# `cmd_ws_add` alone would be lost at the first respawn.
#
# WHETHER CLAUDE CODE'S OWN SCRATCHPAD FOLLOWS TMPDIR is a claim about another
# program; it is measured by the wave that shipped this and recorded in the
# programme ledger, never assumed here. If it does not, that scratchpad stays
# under `/tmp/claude-<uid>/`, where `ccd-tmp-sweep` already collects it.
#
# THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO, because the caller acts on two of them differently
# and must be able to tell the third apart:
#   0  a child, and its leaf is a real directory, ours, at 0700 — path on stdout;
#   1  not a child (no marker, or one that is not a run id) — spawn exactly as before this existed;
#   2  a child whose leaf is not that — warned on stderr, and the caller spawns
#      WITHOUT TMPDIR rather than refusing (spec §5.2, a ruled arm): a refusal
#      would leave a swap or a supervisor restart with no session at all.
# THE LEAF MUST NOT BE A SYMLINK: wave 3's tail removes it, and a planted link
# would aim that removal elsewhere — so that tail unlinks a link or file leaf
# with `rm -f --`, never following it. OWNERSHIP is the `chmod`: it fails EPERM
# on a leaf another user owns. `$HOME/.cc-tmp` itself MAY be a symlink (a data
# volume, `$HOME/projects`'s shape) — the root is followed, only the leaf refused.
_child_tmpdir() {   # id -> rc 0 + path on stdout | 1 not a child | 2 child, leaf unusable (warned)
  local id="$1" root="$HOME/.cc-tmp" dir
  _child_runid_valid "$(_reg_get "$id" child)" || return 1
  dir="$root/$id"
  if [[ -L "$dir" ]] || ! mkdir -p -m 0700 -- "$root" "$dir" 2>/dev/null \
     || [[ ! -d "$dir" ]] || ! chmod 0700 -- "$dir" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "ccd: warn: $id is a child but $dir could not be made a private directory — spawning with the box's own TMPDIR, so this session's scratch is not contained" >&2
    return 2
  fi
  printf '%s' "$dir"
}

# ── `ws-add --child <runId>` (child-workspace reclamation, spec §5.1) ────────
# The dispatch path declares, at creation — the only moment it is known — which
# run minted this workspace, and `cmd_ws_add` stamps it into `$REG/<id>.child`
# before the first spawn (so `_child_tmpdir` above already contains that pane).
# That marker is what makes a workspace a CHILD, and it is never inferred from
# anything else: not `--no-rc`, not a dec string, not a run row alone.
#
# The flag is parsed in `cmd_ws_add`'s strip-then-bind loop, positionless like
# its siblings, and REFUSED above every mint — for `--route`'s reason: nothing
# exists yet, so a bad value leaves the box exactly as it found it. A marker
# that fails to LAND is the opposite case: the worktree and the row exist by
# then, so a `die` would strand both with no pane; it WARNS instead, and the
# workspace is simply not a child — the fallback every pre-policy workspace
# already takes. The marker holds the run id and nothing else (`_reg_set`
# writes no newline), because every later reader compares it string-exact.
#
# `cmd_ws_add` carries one-line pointers only: it sits above line-cited corpus
# anchors, and every comment line added there would move them.
#
# THE ONE RUN-ID GRAMMAR IN ccd, by programme ruling: the `--child-of` parse and the
# marker reads of wave 3 call THIS, never a re-spelt pattern (a test counts it),
# and the server's marker reader accepts exactly this set: a positive decimal of
# at most ten digits, no sign, no leading zero — `String(run.id)` of a rowid.
# ASCII BY `LC_ALL=C` (D-2522, see `_pool_name_valid`): `=~` bracket ranges are
# COLLATION ranges; under en_US.UTF-8 (bash 5.2.21 / glibc 2.39, measured) the
# bare pattern ACCEPTS `²`, `½`, `٣` and `१` — none a run id any server composed.
_child_runid_valid() { local LC_ALL=C; [[ "${1-}" =~ ^[1-9][0-9]{0,9}$ ]]; }   # value -> 0 iff it is a legal --child run id

_spawn_start() {   # id mode(new|resume) -> creates the tmux session, sets SPAWN_FROMSWAP. NEVER WAITS ON THE TUI.
                   # (One bounded exception, `resume` only: SPAWN_RESUME_SETTLE_S before
                   #  the --session-id retry below. The 15-minute wait stays in _spawn_settle.)
  # THE NON-BLOCKING HALF. Split out so that every caller can write the claim
  # (`_reg_claim`) and, where it has one, wire the supervision BEFORE anything
  # that can block — which is F8's whole shape: a `ws-add` killed at the
  # agent's 300 s ceiling after the pane existed and before either write,
  # leaving a live pane no registry row claimed and no unit was watching.
  #
  # Hands `$fromswap` to the caller in `SPAWN_FROMSWAP` (see the block above for
  # why that is a global and must stay one) so `_spawn_settle` need not
  # recompute it: the window is 300 seconds wide and recomputing it after a
  # settle would race it. WRITES NOTHING TO `started` — that is `_reg_claim`'s
  # single job, and the callers decide when. Writes nothing to STDOUT either:
  # callers read the variable, and a stray line here would land in whatever
  # `ws-add`/`start` was printing.
  local id="$1" mode="$2" wrapper workdir uuid tname sidflag rcflag resenv
  # Set before anything that can fail, so no path out of this function leaves a
  # PREVIOUS spawn's answer standing. The failure direction is stated at the
  # bottom of this function and is the same one: unknown degrades to 0.
  SPAWN_FROMSWAP=0
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper); workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir); uuid=$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)
  [[ -n "$wrapper" && -n "$workdir" && -n "$uuid" ]] || die "incomplete registry for '$id'"
  # A PANE IS BEING CREATED, SO THIS WORKSPACE IS NOT DORMANT. Clearing here
  # rather than REFUSING here is the measured choice: `cmd_swap` kills the pane
  # before it re-spawns, so a refusal on this path would leave a live session
  # dead with nothing left to bring it back — strictly worse than the stale
  # stamp it would be protecting against. `ws-archive` already killed the pane
  # and unsupervised the unit, so nothing reaches here by drifting; something
  # asked for this session back.
  #
  # `cmd_ws_restore` clears the stamp BEFORE it calls this function, so the
  # deliberate way back never trips this arm. It fires only on the paths that
  # had no opinion at all — start, ensure, swap, and the two unsupervised
  # fallbacks in `_supervised_start`.
  if [[ -f "$REG/$id.archived" ]]; then
    local archreason; archreason=$(_reg_get "$id" archivedreason)
    echo "ccd: warn: $id was archived${archreason:+ ($archreason)} — clearing the archive stamp, because a pane is being created for it; ws-restore is the deliberate way back" >&2
    _lc_done unarchive "$id" "" verb spawn meas.mode "$mode" \
      meas.archivedAt "$(_reg_get "$id" archived)" meas.archivedReason "$archreason"
    _ws_unarchive "$id"
  fi
  tname=$(_tmux "$id")
  # A spawn within 5 min of a swap is the swap LANDING on the new account — resume from
  # summary there (cheap) instead of full-as-is (700k re-read on the fresh limit pool).
  local fromswap=0 lastswap
  lastswap=$(_reg_get "$id" lastswap)
  [[ "$lastswap" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $(( $(date +%s) - lastswap )) -lt 300 ]] && fromswap=1
  if [[ "$mode" == "resume" ]]; then sidflag="--resume '$uuid'"; else sidflag="--session-id '$uuid'"; fi
  # WHETHER THIS BOX DRIVES ITS SESSIONS OVER THE RC SOCKET — read ONCE, here,
  # and used by BOTH spawn lines below (the primary and the --session-id retry).
  # Once, not per line, so the two can never disagree: a resume that died and
  # retried inside the same call must not come back a different KIND of pane
  # than the one it was replacing. Empty collapses out of the composed command
  # string exactly as `$sidflag`'s alternatives do — the flag is absent, not
  # passed empty, and `--remote-control` with an empty operand is not a thing
  # the wrapper accepts.
  #
  # NEVER on a lane that is not home-able (Task 2, 2026-09-07): Remote Control
  # needs a claude.ai OAuth token carrying the inference scope, and a
  # non-home-able lane is an external backend — ccgpt runs Claude Code in
  # token mode against a local proxy, and its config dir holds no claude.ai
  # OAuth at all — so the flag would be refused, or hang the spawn on a
  # handshake that can never complete. `$wrapper` is already set above (the
  # incomplete-registry guard reads it), so this costs nothing extra to check.
  # This mirrors `_spawn_settle`'s `_is_home_able` guard around
  # `_inject_spawn_effort`: the same "the external backend doesn't take
  # Claude-specific input" reasoning, gating a different line of the same spawn.
  #
  # PER-SESSION OVERRIDE (task #37, ruling 2026-08-13): a row stamped `rc=off`
  # at creation — `ccd ws-add --no-rc`, the dispatch path's spelling of "this
  # pane is a dispatched worker" — never gets the flag, whatever the box says.
  # Only the EXACT string `off` suppresses: the writer is ccd itself, so an
  # absent or garbled field follows the box (D-99's fail-safe direction, per
  # session) — never "strip RC from a session nobody marked".
  rcflag=""
  # BACKEND, not placement: Remote Control is Claude Code's own protocol and a
  # Codex lane cannot answer it.
  _is_anthropic_backend "$wrapper" && _rc_enabled && [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" rc)" != "off" ]] && rcflag="--remote-control '$id'"
  # UNCONDITIONAL, unlike `rcflag` above — deliberately, not an oversight: on a
  # non-home-able lane the vars `_resume_env` composes are simply inert (the
  # external backend never reads CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN), and
  # `_redrive_after_spawn`'s own transcript measurement (`_transcript_path`,
  # which only ever names a Claude Code JSONL) fails closed there regardless —
  # so gating this line would buy nothing and cost a second home-ability check.
  resenv=$(_resume_env)
  # THE ROUTING RECORD (routing spec 2026-09-14 §5.2, §5.4 last paragraph):
  # `--model` from `class`, and the subagent floor into the SAME `env` line
  # `_resume_env` composes. Computed ONCE, here, above BOTH spawn lines —
  # exactly as `$rcflag` is, and for the same reason: the retry may not spawn a
  # differently-shaped pane than the attempt it replaces. EMPTY when the
  # session has no record, so a pre-slice-1 session's argv is byte-identical
  # to today's (`ccd-spawn-split.test.ts` pins that string;
  # `ccd-route-spawn.test.ts` pins this one). `default` passes no flag — the
  # lane's settings.json decides. Effort IS an argv matter here (Task 8,
  # controller ruling S1-R8/D-2809): a recorded non-auto effort rides
  # `--effort`, and `--settings` carries the measured ultracode/enableWorkflows
  # keys, both composed below on the Anthropic arm. The settle's keystroke
  # (`_inject_spawn_effort`) is the NO-RECORD path only — a pre-slice-1
  # session with nothing composed here.
  local routeflags="" routeenv="" rclass rsub rwf reff inert="" rapplied
  if _route_any "$id"; then
    rclass=$(_route_get "$id" class)
    # THE CLASS SERVED (routing spec §5.4, last paragraph; §5.1 `degraded`). The
    # intended class stays in `class`; THIS settle re-measures the lane it is
    # landing on and decides what to compose: servable → clear a standing stamp
    # (the intended class is restored the moment a lane can serve it); measured
    # unservable → stamp one rung down and compose that; UNMEASURED → compose
    # what stands (a standing stamp was measured when written; no stamp means
    # the intended class) and write nothing — nobody degrades on a fabricated
    # fact. `_route_degrade`/`_route_restore` are the only writers and write
    # once: a second settle on the same lane says nothing twice.
    local rdeg="" below srv why
    if [[ -n "$rclass" && "$rclass" != default ]]; then
      # A STANDING STAMP IS STALE THE MOMENT THE INTENDED CLASS MOVES (ruling
      # S3-R7): after fable->degraded opus->reroute to `class haiku`, this
      # settle on an UNMEASURED lane would otherwise compose `--model opus` — a
      # rung ABOVE the class just asked for, from a measurement about a class
      # nobody intends anymore. The decision itself now lives in
      # `_route_stale_degrade_clear`, which the routing WRITERS also call (final
      # review finding 4); this stays the backstop for a stamp that went stale
      # by some other route, and the read below is what that call left behind.
      _route_stale_degrade_clear "$id" "$rclass"
      rdeg=$(_route_get "$id" degraded)
      # THE WORD, NOT JUST THE RC (ruling S3-R6). `_serviceable`'s rc 1 has TWO
      # causes — the figure at the ceiling, or `backend` (fable on a
      # non-Anthropic lane) — and its stdout already names which; discarding it
      # would let a `class fable` session on a codex lane journal a permanent
      # route row asserting a ceiling figure nobody read. `local why` is
      # declared above, on its own line, so this bare assignment's `$?` is
      # `_serviceable`'s own rc, not `local`'s.
      why=$(_serviceable "$wrapper" "$rclass"); srv=$?
      # WHAT A DEGRADE BUYS DIFFERS BY CLASS, and only the fable rung changes
      # ELIGIBILITY (final review finding 2, the settle's half of it).
      # `fable`'s rc 1 is the share ceiling or the roster's backend word, so
      # `opus` on that same lane really is servable where fable was not. For
      # `opus`/`sonnet`/`haiku` the ceiling IS `SWAP_CEILING` on the lane's own
      # seven-day figure, so every remaining rung asks the identical question
      # of the identical figure and answers unservable together: the rung drop
      # lands on a lane that measures exactly as unservable for the new class
      # as for the old one. Composing it is still right, for a DIFFERENT
      # reason, and the reason was nowhere in tracked text until this
      # paragraph: a cheaper class spends the shared weekly window more slowly,
      # which is the only lever left once the lane itself is already chosen.
      # A SPEND argument, not an eligibility one —
      # `ccd-route-degrade.test.ts`'s `class opus … --model sonnet` case is a
      # rung drop that buys exactly that and nothing else. At `haiku` the
      # ladder bottoms out (`below` empty), nothing is stamped, and the
      # intended class is composed unchanged.
      case "$srv" in
        0) [[ -n "$rdeg" ]] && _route_restore "$id" "$rdeg" "$rclass" "servable: $rclass on $wrapper at settle"; rdeg="" ;;
        1) below=$(_class_below "$rclass")
           if [[ -n "$below" ]]; then
             # THE STAMP IS A WRITE THAT CAN FAIL (ruling S3-R7, mirroring
             # `_auto_swap_check`'s rc-6 arm). Unlike a tick, a settle cannot
             # stand still — it must compose SOMETHING for the spawn about to
             # happen — so on an unwritable stamp it composes the INTENDED
             # class (the record, unable to say otherwise, is the arbiter) and
             # says so in one swap.log line; the next settle re-measures and
             # retries.
             if _route_degrade "$id" "$rclass" "$below" "unservable ($why): $rclass on $wrapper at settle"; then
               rdeg="$below"
             else
               echo "$(date '+%F %T') degrade-unwritable $id: $rclass -> $below at settle on $wrapper (stamp not written; composing $rclass)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
               rdeg=""
             fi
           fi ;;
      esac
      [[ -n "$rdeg" ]] && rclass="$rdeg"
    fi
    reff=$(_route_effort_for "$id" "$rclass")   # keyed on the class SERVED: a rung down to haiku takes no effort
    rsub=$(_route_get "$id" subagent); rwf=$(_route_get "$id" workflow)
    [[ "$reff" == ultracode ]] && rwf=on
    # `--model` IS COMPOSED ON EVERY BACKEND, and the reason it is not gated to
    # Anthropic lanes is that the record has no way to say otherwise: `inert` is
    # a list of FIELD NAMES drawn from `ROUTE_INERTABLE` (`effort workflow
    # subagent`) and its vocabulary has no word for `class`, so gating the flag
    # here would make a non-Anthropic session's model silently unset with no
    # channel saying so — the overloaded silence the inert stamp below exists to
    # remove.
    #
    # WHAT THE ALIAS MEANS THERE IS NOW MEASURED (controller ruling S1-R10,
    # settled by the slice-4 probe: research doc §6, row `S1-R10, slice 4`,
    # measured 2026-09-15 on Claude Code 2.1.273). On the codex lane `--model
    # <class>` is HONOURED and resolves through that lane's own
    # `ANTHROPIC_DEFAULT_<CLASS>_MODEL` keys in its config dir's settings.json —
    # so the flag names a CLASS and the lane materialises the id, exactly the
    # shape spec §5.2 assumed. `opus` is the one alias that probe cannot tell
    # apart from the lane default, by construction: both map to the same id.
    [[ -n "$rclass" && "$rclass" != default ]] && routeflags="--model $rclass"
    # BACKEND, not placement: the subagent env is Claude Code's own; on another
    # backend the lane's settings.json materialiser owns the subagent (spec
    # §5.2 "Non-Anthropic lanes"), the settle's keystroke is gated to Anthropic
    # lanes (`_spawn_settle`), and workflows have no switch — so on such a lane
    # `effort` and `workflow` are stamped INERT by field name, and the record
    # says what was not applied rather than pretending.
    if _is_anthropic_backend "$wrapper"; then
      [[ -n "$rsub" ]] && routeenv="CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL=$rsub"
      # `--settings` BEFORE `--effort` in `routeflags` — deliberately, not
      # alphabetical: `ccd-route-spawn.test.ts`'s ruled-form test asserts
      # `--model X --settings Y --` as one contiguous substring, so a flag
      # between `--model` and `--settings` would break it (measured against
      # the test, not chosen first). `auto` composes NEITHER — the lane
      # decides — and is the one word `_route_effort_for` can hand back
      # un-transformed for a non-haiku class (Task 7's --settings ruling,
      # controller ruling S1-R8).
      # `workflow=off` NOW COMPOSES `{"enableWorkflows":false}` AND STAMPS NO
      # INERT (slice 4, Task 1 decision b). Slice 1 could not compose the false
      # key because only the TRUE one had been measured, so `off` composed
      # nothing and the record said `inert=workflow` rather than claim a state it
      # had not set. Slice 4 measured it (2026-09-15, Claude Code 2.1.273, a
      # Fable-entitled lane): the flag is HONOURED and it is a real lever —
      # `/effort ultracode` is refused verbatim (`Ultracode needs dynamic
      # workflows enabled`) and `/config` matches no `workflow` row at all, one
      # row further than the true flag withdraws. Nothing is written to
      # settings.json on either arm. So the field IS applied now and the inert
      # stamp would be false. NOT reachable for ultracode: `$rwf` was forced to
      # `on` above, so an ultracode record never arrives here with `off`.
      #
      # ONE `case`, so `--settings` is composed exactly once and always BEFORE
      # `--effort` — `ccd-route-spawn.test.ts`'s ruled-form test asserts
      # `--model X --settings Y --` as one contiguous substring.
      case "$rwf" in
        on)  if [[ "$reff" == ultracode ]]; then
               routeflags="${routeflags:+$routeflags }--settings '{\"enableWorkflows\":true,\"ultracode\":true}'"
             else
               routeflags="${routeflags:+$routeflags }--settings '{\"enableWorkflows\":true}'"
             fi ;;
        off) routeflags="${routeflags:+$routeflags }--settings '{\"enableWorkflows\":false}'" ;;
      esac
      [[ -n "$reff" && "$reff" != auto ]] && routeflags="${routeflags:+$routeflags }--effort $reff"
    else
      # WHAT IS STAMPED INERT HERE IS `$reff` — the effort AFTER
      # `_route_effort_for`'s haiku filtering, which is the decision and not an
      # oversight. A haiku+effort PAIR was never going to apply on ANY backend,
      # so it is refused with its own note rather than reported as a thing this
      # lane failed to apply; `$reff` is already empty by then and no stamp is
      # written. `auto` means the lane decides on its own — S1-R6 — so it is
      # excluded here exactly as `--effort` above excludes it.
      [[ -n "$reff" && "$reff" != auto ]] && inert="effort"
      [[ "$rwf" == on ]] && inert="${inert:+$inert,}workflow"
      # AND `subagent`, WHICH THIS ARM COMPOSES NOTHING FOR EITHER (slice 6 Task 3,
      # closing slice 4's deferred minor). `routeenv` is built on the Anthropic arm
      # ALONE — `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` is Claude Code's own env key and the
      # comment above already says another backend's settings.json materialiser owns
      # the subagent there — so until now a record carrying `subagent=sonnet` had that
      # field dropped in SILENCE on this lane, the one overloaded silence left beside
      # the two the stamp had already removed. The order is `effort,workflow,subagent`
      # — the order the existing stamp already spelled its two words, with the new one
      # APPENDED — so every standing stamp keeps its bytes. It is not a composition
      # order and must not be read as one: the Anthropic arm above composes `routeenv`
      # (the subagent) FIRST, `--settings` (the workflow) second and `--effort` last.
      [[ -n "$rsub" ]] && inert="${inert:+$inert,}subagent"
    fi
    if [[ -n "$inert" ]]; then _reg_set "$id" inert "$inert"; else rm -f -- "$REG/$id.inert"; fi
    # THE SETTLE IS AN APPLICATION, and this is it saying so (slice 4, Task 4).
    # `$REG/<id>.routeapplied` records what THIS spawn composed, so the applier's
    # tick — which compares the record against this file — finds nothing pending
    # on a pane that has just come up carrying exactly what the record asked for.
    # Without it every fresh spawn would be re-typed through the pickers within
    # five seconds of settling, for no change at all.
    #
    # WHAT IS WRITTEN IS WHAT WAS COMPOSED, not what was intended: `$rclass` is
    # already the class SERVED (a degrade stamped above replaced it) and `$reff`
    # is already past `_route_effort_for`'s haiku filter. `default` and `auto` are
    # real words the record can state — the picker's `Default` row, "the lane
    # decides the level" — and they are written when the record states them.
    #
    # AN ABSENT FIELD IS OMITTED, NOT SPELLED `default`/`auto` (controller ruling
    # S4-R8). The three conditions are distinct and `_route_wanted` now answers
    # all three the same way this stamp writes them, which is what keeps the two
    # comparable: a record that never mentioned `class` composed no `--model` and
    # claims no class. `subagent` and `workflow` were already treated this way.
    #
    # AND EVERY FIELD THE `inert` STAMP NAMES IS OMITTED TOO (controller ruling
    # S4-R9). Written unconditionally, this line put `effort=high` beside
    # `inert=effort` on a non-Anthropic lane — two records of the same spawn
    # contradicting each other, because `--effort`/`--settings` are composed only
    # on the Anthropic arm above while the stamp claimed them on both. The
    # sentence this comment opens with is true again: what is written is what was
    # composed. `_route_wanted` skips the same fields, so an inert field is
    # neither claimed applied nor typed — and `subagent`, which joined the
    # vocabulary in slice 6 Task 3, is never typed by anything: its guard here
    # keeps this stamp from claiming an env key the else-branch above did not
    # compose.
    rapplied="${rclass:+class=$rclass}"
    _route_inert_has "$inert" effort   || rapplied="${rapplied:+$rapplied }${reff:+effort=$reff}"
    rapplied="${rapplied% }"
    _route_inert_has "$inert" subagent || { [[ -n "$rsub" ]] && rapplied="${rapplied:+$rapplied }subagent=$rsub"; }
    _route_inert_has "$inert" workflow || { [[ -n "$rwf" ]] && rapplied="${rapplied:+$rapplied }workflow=$rwf"; }
    _reg_set "$id" routeapplied "$rapplied"
    # THE RETRY COUNTER DIES WITH THE SPAWN IT COUNTED (ruling S4-R7): whatever
    # the old pane refused to be typed, this argv composed, so the next tick's
    # comparison is against a fresh fact and deserves fresh attempts.
    _route_tries_clear "$id"
  else
    # NO RECORD, NOTHING COMPOSED, NOTHING CLAIMED. A leftover `routeapplied`
    # from a record that has since been removed would make the applier compare
    # against a file describing a spawn nobody would make again.
    rm -f -- "$REG/$id.routeapplied"
    _route_tries_clear "$id"
  fi
  # THE CHILD'S TEMP ROOT (spec §5.2), composed ONCE, here, into the one `env`
  # string BOTH spawn lines below splice — for `$rcflag`'s reason: the resume
  # retry may not spawn a differently-contained pane than the attempt it
  # replaces. EMPTY for every non-child, so a non-child's spawn line is
  # byte-identical to what it was before (`ccd-spawn-split.test.ts` pins it).
  # `_child_tmpdir`'s rc 2 has already said on stderr why a child spawns
  # uncontained; the spawn proceeds either way.
  local tmpenv="" ctmp
  ctmp=$(_child_tmpdir "$id") && tmpenv="TMPDIR='$ctmp'"
  resenv="$resenv${routeenv:+ $routeenv}${tmpenv:+ $tmpenv}"
  local launchflags="$rcflag${routeflags:+${rcflag:+ }$routeflags}"
  # COLORTERM=truecolor so Claude Code emits 24-bit colour (it otherwise quantises
  # to 256, collapsing its palette). tmux passes it through to the terminal-drawer
  # xterm once the server has the RGB terminal-feature (see hardening/tmux.conf).
  # ── D-2605: the row's generation, read under the mutex, CLOSED BEFORE the
  # fork ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # It READS AND VALIDATES; it never MINTS. Minting is row creation's alone: a
  # mint here would hand a fresh authorization to a row that was purged or
  # REUSED in the gap, and a row with no generation is answered by the standing
  # fail-closed rule downstream instead.
  #
  # THE LOCK IS CLOSED BEFORE EITHER `_tmux_new_session` COMMAND IS BUILT OR
  # RUN, and that is not tidiness. The tmux server daemon — or anything it
  # spawns — can outlive this critical section, a held `{fd}<>` descriptor is
  # inherited across fork/exec, and no compliant path may rely on tmux calling
  # `closefrom()`. Releasing first is safe because every lifecycle arm already
  # refuses on an environment/canonical generation mismatch under its own lock,
  # so a row purged or reused in the gap is simply refused downstream.
  #
  # A CONTENDED MISS FAILS OPEN, deliberately. Failing closed here was
  # REJECTED: a rescue swap contending with the outgoing pane's own PreCompact,
  # which can hold the lock for seconds, would not come back up — and a swap
  # must never wedge on a compaction lock. The cost is stated plainly rather
  # than swallowed, because a contended hold at spawn time is itself an anomaly
  # and the resulting inertness cannot self-heal by any later event. It is
  # self-consistent with the generation gate rather than an exception to it: a
  # session with no generation cannot hold this lock, so there is no publisher
  # the missed acquisition could have raced.
  local genfd="" genenv="" genval="" genrc=0
  _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; genrc=$?
  if (( genrc == 0 )); then
    genfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
    if _reg_generation_read "$id"; then genval="$REG_GENERATION"; genenv="CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION='$genval' "
    else
      # THE COMMON CASE, AND IT USED TO BE SILENT. The `elif` below reports a
      # CONTENDED acquisition, which is rare; a row with no generation at all —
      # or one present and invalid — took this arm, read nothing, and spawned
      # without a word. Measured on this fleet 2026-09-17: 31 of 34 live rows
      # in exactly that state, and the string below appearing in no log on the
      # box. A condition that disables a whole subsystem for the life of a row
      # has to be audible, whichever of the two reasons produced it.
      echo "ccd: warn: $id: no valid $REG/$id.generation;" \
           "spawning without CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION — this session's compaction lifecycle is inert until its next respawn" >&2
    fi
    _compact_lock_release "$genfd"; genfd=""
  elif (( genrc == 1 )); then
    echo "ccd: warn: $id: could not take $REG/.$id.compactions.lock within ${COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT}s;" \
         "spawning without CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION — this session's compaction lifecycle is inert until its next respawn" >&2
  else
    # THE THIRD STATE, and the last of them to be silent. `rc 2` is MECHANISM
    # ABSENCE — no `flock`, `mktemp` or `link` resolvable — so the acquire was
    # never attempted and neither arm above can speak for it. The compaction
    # lifecycle is unavailable on such a box for the same reason, which is
    # exactly why saying nothing was the wrong answer: "no artifacts appeared"
    # is indistinguishable from "the feature is off", and a reader with no line
    # to grep has to reconstruct the cause from absence.
    echo "ccd: warn: $id: no lock mechanism on PATH (flock, mktemp, link);" \
         "spawning without CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION — this session's compaction lifecycle is inert until its next respawn" >&2
  fi
  # `_tmux_new_session`, not a bare `tmux new-session`: this is the call that
  # can create the server, and it is AFTER everything that can `die`, so a spawn
  # that is going to abort never leaves a scope behind.
  _tmux_new_session -d -s "$tname" -x 220 -y 50 \
    "cd '$workdir' && exec env ${genenv}COLORTERM=truecolor $resenv '$WRAPPER_DIR/$wrapper' $launchflags $sidflag --dangerously-skip-permissions"
  # THE PRICE OF A MONOTONE `started`, PAID AUTOMATICALLY INSTEAD OF BY HAND.
  # Nothing in ccd ever clears the field: there is no registry delete/unset
  # helper at all — `ccd-reg-claim.test.ts` greps this file for one and pins
  # zero hits, so naming the two candidate spellings HERE would red that suite —
  # and `_reg_purge` unlinks every field for an id, which destroys the identity
  # rather than un-claiming it. So once a row is claimed every later
  # revival picks mode=resume. If the session never really came up, `--resume
  # '<uuid>'` names a uuid with no transcript behind it and the wrapper exits
  # immediately — forever, on every retry AND every Restart=always cycle.
  # `_spawn_settle`'s own rc 3/4 warnings name this trap from the other side
  # ("if the session never came up, clear $REG/$id.started first"); this is that
  # escape hatch, taken once, automatically.
  #
  # ONCE, never a loop, and only on `resume`: a `new` spawn has nothing to fall
  # back to. The short settle is what makes `has-session` mean anything — the
  # wrapper's exit is not instantaneous, and `tmux new-session -d` returns 0
  # whether or not the command it launched survives.
  #
  # AND THIS IS THE ONE PLACE THIS FUNCTION WAITS. The header's "NEVER BLOCKS"
  # is about the TUI — the gate loop, up to fifteen minutes, which is F8's whole
  # window and stays in `_spawn_settle`. SPAWN_RESUME_SETTLE_S on the resume path
  # widens the unclaimed window by exactly that much and buys back a row that
  # would otherwise never start again; it cannot be moved into the settle, which
  # knows only the tmux name and neither the mode nor the uuid.
  #
  # NAMED, not a literal, and it is the only timing bound in this area that was
  # ever spelled as one — its neighbours (SPAWN_GATE_TRIES, SPAWN_SETTLE_S,
  # SPAWN_SETTLE_SUPERVISE_S, SUPERVISED_START_WAIT, CCD_SETTLE_BOUND) are all
  # greppable constants, and this one sits INSIDE the window Tasks 7/8 exist to
  # shrink, so it is precisely the number a later measurement will want to find.
  if [[ "$mode" == "resume" ]]; then
    sleep "$SPAWN_RESUME_SETTLE_S"
    if ! tmux has-session -t "$tname" 2>/dev/null; then
      echo "ccd: $id: --resume '$uuid' left no session; retrying once with --session-id" >&2
      # `$launchflags`, the SAME value the primary line above used — computed
      # once, before either spawn (it carries `$rcflag` plus the routing
      # record's `--model`), so the retry cannot spawn a differently-shaped
      # pane than the attempt it is replacing. (This is the copy that gets
      # forgotten: `ccd-spawn-split.test.ts` pins this line's argv on its own,
      # not merely the pair.)
      # ITS OWN ACQUISITION, immediately before the retry's own fork, and
      # closed before it — the primary read above happened before a `sleep` and
      # a `has-session`, so its answer is stale by now. It requires THE SAME
      # value this invocation already saw: a row purged and re-created in the
      # gap has a different generation, and exporting that one would authorize
      # this pane against a row it does not belong to.
      local rgenfd="" rgenrc=0
      genenv=""
      _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; rgenrc=$?
      if (( rgenrc == 0 )); then
        rgenfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
        if [[ -n "$genval" ]] && _reg_generation_read "$id" && [[ "$REG_GENERATION" == "$genval" ]]; then
          genenv="CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION='$genval' "
        fi
        _compact_lock_release "$rgenfd"; rgenfd=""
      fi
      _tmux_new_session -d -s "$tname" -x 220 -y 50 \
        "cd '$workdir' && exec env ${genenv}COLORTERM=truecolor $resenv '$WRAPPER_DIR/$wrapper' $launchflags --session-id '$uuid' --dangerously-skip-permissions"
    fi
  fi
  # Make this window's terminal title = the tmux session name ("cc-<id>"), so a frontmost-window-
  # aware hotkey (ccclip) can target exactly the session you're looking at, even with many open.
  tmux set-option -t "$tname" set-titles on 2>/dev/null
  tmux set-option -t "$tname" set-titles-string '#S' 2>/dev/null
  # Follow the most-recent client's size (defensive; with attach -d there's only ever one client,
  # so the window always fits the device you're on — no smaller-client "dotted filler" clash).
  tmux set-option -t "$tname" window-size latest 2>/dev/null
  # VALIDATED TO THE TWO LEGAL WORDS, and the FAILURE DIRECTION is why the check
  # survived the move off stdout: fromswap=1 picks the cheap auto-compact
  # landing, fromswap=0 keeps full fidelity, so anything unrecognised must
  # degrade to 0. It sits here, once, instead of at every call site — which is
  # the other half of what the global buys.
  [[ "$fromswap" == 1 ]] || fromswap=0
  SPAWN_FROMSWAP="$fromswap"
}

_spawn_settle() {   # id fromswap — the BLOCKING half: gate loop, fact, warnings, /effort
  # NO BOUND POSITIONAL, and none is threaded onward: the settle window is
  # `CCD_SETTLE_BOUND`, read once by `_accept_first_run_prompts` out of the
  # calling frame (see the block at its `bound=` line for the arbitrary-code
  # execution an argv-threaded bound bought). One reader, one place to validate.
  local id="$1" fromswap="$2" wrapper tname prompt_rc
  wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  tname=$(_tmux "$id")
  _accept_first_run_prompts "$tname" "$fromswap"; prompt_rc=$?
  # The verdict becomes a FACT before it becomes a return code (§3.1). This costs nothing — this
  # already runs inside the supervisor — and it is the ONLY channel from a spawn that happened
  # inside the unit to a `ccd start` running in another process, which is what _supervised_start
  # polls. KEEP THE FIELD AND ITS `<epoch-seconds> <rc>` ENCODING: the timestamp is load-bearing,
  # because _supervised_start compares `at >= since` to tell THIS attempt's failure from the
  # previous one's, and a bare word field would destroy that.
  # CHANGE-ONLY, AND IT MUST BE READ BEFORE THE WRITE BELOW. `_reg_set` is a
  # truncating writer, so this is the last instant the PREVIOUS epoch and rc
  # exist anywhere. Restart=always means this function runs on every supervisor
  # cycle for 18 sessions; a line per cycle is the whole disk budget for no
  # forensic gain, and a spawn whose rc has not changed inside five minutes is
  # not news.
  local _lc_prev _lc_prev_at _lc_prev_rc _lc_now
  _lc_prev=$(_reg_get "$id" spawn)
  _lc_prev_at="${_lc_prev%% *}"; _lc_prev_rc="${_lc_prev##* }"
  _lc_now=$(date +%s)
  # Regex before every arithmetic test: `_lc_prev_at` is bytes off disk.
  if [[ "$_lc_prev_rc" != "$prompt_rc" ]] \
     || ! [[ "$_lc_prev_at" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] \
     || (( _lc_now - _lc_prev_at > 300 )); then
    _lc_done spawn "$id" "" meas.rc "$prompt_rc" meas.wrapper "$wrapper"
  fi
  _reg_set "$id" spawn "$_lc_now $prompt_rc"
  case "$prompt_rc" in
    # A revival supersedes an earlier deliberate stop (§4.1). `rm -f` on an absent file is a no-op,
    # so this is correct whether or not the .stopped stamp itself has landed yet.
    #
    # AND THE AUTH-DEAD MARKER FOR THIS ACCOUNT, on rc 0 and only rc 0. A TUI
    # that came up on `$wrapper` is proof that account's credential
    # authenticates — the second of the three owners §A.6 gives that marker,
    # beside the probe's own 403 and an operator `rm`. Deliberately NOT the
    # `cmd_start` shape one screen down, which clears `swapblocked` on the
    # ATTEMPT: a refused swap is a stale banner a human act supersedes, while
    # this is a MEASUREMENT and erasing one without evidence is a guess. rc 2
    # ("waiting for login") and rc 5 ("hard-blocked at startup … or lost auth")
    # are the opposite of evidence and are pinned to keep the marker.
    0) rm -f "$REG/$id.stopped" "$REG/$wrapper-authdead" ;;
    # $id and $wrapper are only in scope HERE, not inside _accept_first_run_prompts (which knows
    # only the tmux name), so the operator-facing warnings — naming both, spec §5 — live on this
    # side of the return.
    2) echo "warn: $id is waiting for login on $wrapper — attach and run /login" >&2 ;;
    # `started` stays 1 after a failed spawn (cmd_start/cmd_ensure, §4.3 orphan classification), so a
    # retry resumes THIS uuid rather than minting a fresh one — correct when rc 4 means the pane lived
    # 15 minutes and may hold real history (flipping to --session-id on retry would risk clobbering
    # it), but a trap when rc 3/4 means nothing ever really started: the retry then resumes a uuid
    # with no transcript behind it, forever, on every retry AND every Restart=always cycle, because
    # nothing else ever clears $REG/$id.started. Named here so the operator has the escape hatch.
    3) echo "ccd: $id: the tmux session vanished during startup — nothing is running. A retry will --resume this uuid; if the session never came up, clear $REG/$id.started first." >&2 ;;
    4) echo "ccd: $id: startup window expired with no live TUI marker. A retry will --resume this uuid; if the session never came up, clear $REG/$id.started first." >&2 ;;
    5) echo "ccd: $id: hard-blocked at startup (limit/spend banner, or lost auth) on $wrapper — waiting will not fix it; swap the account or attach and run /login." >&2 ;;
    6) echo "ccd: $id: pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} columns (or its width could not be read), so the startup gates stood down — any narrow terminal attached to this tmux server narrows new panes: close it, then widen this one (open and close its terminal drawer) and answer any startup prompt." >&2 ;;
  esac
  # /effort ultracode is a Claude-model effort tier; skip it on any lane that is not home-able
  # (those are the external backends — gpt/Codex today — which don't take it, so the
  # injection would just error into the prompt box). Keyed on home-ability rather than on the
  # literal `gpt` since the roster became data — merged from main, whose reasoning stands
  # unchanged here.
  #
  # The RC half of the guard is this branch's, and it is STRICTER than the `!= 2` it replaces:
  # 2 used to be the only non-zero code, and with 3 and 4 now in the table (§3.1) `!= 2` would
  # type a slash command at a session that does not exist. `== 0` means inject only into a TUI
  # we watched come up — which subsumes main's "never synthesize a keystroke into an auth flow"
  # (rc 2 fails `== 0` too) rather than dropping it.
  # BACKEND, not placement: this types Claude-specific input into the TUI.
  _is_anthropic_backend "$wrapper" && [[ "$prompt_rc" == 0 ]] && _inject_spawn_effort "$tname"
  # D-2231 — after the effort injection, so its local-command chatter is already
  # in the tail the detector skips. Only on a clean landing: rc 5 is hard-blocked
  # (a keystroke there is the swap's job), rc 2 is a login screen.
  #
  # UNGATED on home-ability, unlike `_inject_spawn_effort` above — deliberately:
  # a non-home-able lane never has `_resume_env`'s flag set, so its transcript
  # never carries the resume pair `_transcript_stalled_pair` looks for, and the
  # measurement fails closed (rc 1, not-stalled) rather than needing a second
  # home-ability check here to reach the same silence.
  [[ "$prompt_rc" == 0 ]] && _redrive_after_spawn "$id" "$tname" "$fromswap"
  return "$prompt_rc"
}

_spawn() {   # id mode(new|resume) — the composition, so cmd_swap and friends are unchanged
  # NOTE: v2.1.177 does not print the claude.ai URL to the pane or debug log, so there is no
  # URL to capture. On a box whose `~/.ccrc/remote-control` says `on` we pass
  # `--remote-control '$id'` (see `_rc_enabled`), and there the session is discovered on
  # claude.ai by its name, which == the id. Where the flag is off there is no claude.ai
  # session to discover at all — the pane is an ordinary local TUI.
  #
  # A CALLER THAT WANTS THE F8-SAFE ORDERING DOES NOT USE THIS: it calls the two halves and puts
  # `_reg_claim` (and its `_ws_supervise`, where it has one) between them.
  #
  # THE CALL SHAPE EVERY CALL SITE MUST COPY, and there is exactly one of it:
  # a plain call, `|| return $?`, then read `$SPAWN_FROMSWAP`. No `$( )` — see
  # the block above `_spawn_start` for the commit where a command substitution
  # here turned its `die` into rc 1 and every caller reported success over a
  # spawn that never happened.
  _spawn_start "$1" "$2" || return $?
  _spawn_settle "$1" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"
}

_inject_spawn_effort() {   # tmuxname — apply the settle-time effort, once the TUI is up.
  # The id is the tmux name with `cc-` stripped (`_tmux`), so the signature the
  # settle calls with stays (`ccd-backend-vs-placement.test.ts` pins the call).
  # THE EFFORT FIELD DECIDES (routing spec 2026-09-14 §5.2, controller rulings
  # S1-R8 and S1-R11): a session whose record carries an `effort` file — ANY
  # value, including `auto` and including bytes no vocabulary admits — never
  # gets this keystroke, because that level was already applied on the spawn
  # argv (`_spawn_start`'s `--effort`/`--settings`) or was `auto`, which means
  # the lane decides. A session with no `effort` file gets `SPAWN_EFFORT`,
  # exactly as before, WHATEVER ELSE the record holds.
  #
  # THE FIELD, NOT `_route_any` — the distinction is the whole of S1-R11.
  # `_route_any` is true for all seven files, two of which (`degraded`, `inert`)
  # ccd writes ITSELF: slice 3's degradation stamp alone would then have read as
  # "the record carried an effort" and silently switched off the box default on
  # a session whose operator never chose an effort at all. So this asks about
  # ONE file — and it asks the FILESYSTEM, with the same `[[ -e ]]` test
  # `_route_any` uses, so an EMPTY `effort` file still counts as present: a
  # field somebody wrote is a field somebody meant.
  #
  # NOT `if _reg_get "$id" effort`, which is what stood here and is the reason
  # `ccd-crosspool.test.ts`'s "no call site can see that rc" case redded at the
  # tip. That case exists to hand the duty to the first caller that branches on
  # `_reg_get`'s exit code, and the duty is real: `_reg_get` guards with `-f`,
  # so a field that is a symlink to `/dev/null` — the one input that suite
  # measures as having MOVED — answered rc 1 with the file plainly there, and
  # this branch then typed `SPAWN_EFFORT` over a session whose operator had
  # chosen an effort. Presence is not a read's exit code.
  #
  # `_route_effort_for` is still called on the record path for its JOURNALING
  # side effect only (the haiku+effort refusal note and the unrecognised-value
  # reject note) — its return value is never typed, so nothing unrecognised
  # reaches a keystroke because nothing recognised does either.
  local t="$1" id="${1#cc-}" level
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { echo "ccd: pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} columns, skipped /effort $SPAWN_EFFORT (§6.3)" >&2; return 0; }
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.effort" ]]; then
    _route_effort_for "$id" "$(_route_get "$id" class)" >/dev/null
    return 0
  fi
  level="$SPAWN_EFFORT"
  [[ -n "$level" ]] || return 0
  # NO id, for the reason argued at `_auto_compact_check`'s copy: the guard
  # seven lines above already measured this pane, so passing it here could only
  # relabel an unmeasurable pane as an armed auto-continue.
  if _pane_auto_continue_armed "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null)"; then
    echo "ccd: auto-continue armed, skipped /effort $level (D-2229)" >&2; return 0
  fi
  # Only type into an EMPTY input box — a restored draft would swallow the slash
  # command. `-e` for the same reason the auto-compact guard passes it: without
  # the escape codes a dim ghost-suggestion reads as a draft and /effort is
  # skipped on every fresh session that shows one.
  if _pane_box_has_content "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p -e 2>/dev/null)"; then
    echo "ccd: input box not empty, skipped /effort $level" >&2; return 0
  fi
  sleep 1
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "/effort $level"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  # Sessions with cached history confirm the switch ("full history gets re-read") — accept.
  sleep 2
  if tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | grep -q "Yes, switch"; then
    tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  fi
  return 0
}

_transcript_stalled_pair() {   # transcript-path -> 0 the newest real turn is the unsubmitted resume pair | 1 not | 2 unreadable (D-2231)
  # THE MEASUREMENT BEHIND THE FLAG. Claude Code's `--resume` writes a META user
  # "Continue from where you left off." and a synthetic "No response requested."
  # and, without CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN, stops there. A tail whose
  # newest user/assistant rows are exactly that pair — local-command chatter,
  # system rows and attachments after it do not count — is a stall. Anything
  # else after the pair (a real reply, a human prompt) means someone drove it.
  # Only `"type":"user"`/`"type":"assistant"` rows are read; a false hit on that
  # substring inside quoted content can only break the pair, i.e. read "not
  # stalled" — the status quo, never a spurious keystroke.
  local f="$1" line state=0
  [[ -f "$f" && -r "$f" ]] || return 2   # D-2370: -r alone admits a FIFO (D-2347)
  while IFS= read -r line; do
    [[ "$line" == *'"type":"user"'* || "$line" == *'"type":"assistant"'* ]] || continue
    if [[ "$line" == *'"isMeta":true'* && "$line" == *'Continue from where you left off.'* ]]; then state=1; continue; fi
    if [[ "$state" -eq 1 && "$line" == *'"model":"<synthetic>"'* && "$line" == *'No response requested.'* ]]; then state=2; continue; fi
    if [[ "$state" -eq 2 ]]; then
      case "$line" in *'<local-command-caveat>'*|*'<command-name>'*|*'<local-command-stdout>'*) continue ;; esac
    fi
    state=0
  done < <(tail -n "$REDRIVE_TAIL_LINES" "$f")
  [[ "$state" -eq 2 ]]
}

_transcript_limit_banner() {   # transcript-path -> 0 the newest real row is a rate-limit banner (prints "<resetsAt>\t<rateLimitType>") | 1 not | 2 unreadable (D-2362, D-2456)
  # STRUCTURAL, NOT TEXTUAL. The row Claude Code appends on a 429 carries
  # TOP-LEVEL `isApiErrorMessage:true` and `error:"rate_limit"` (measured
  # 2026-09-10: `quotaLimits:{status:"rejected",resetsAt:<epoch s>,
  # rateLimitType:"seven_day"}`). Its TEXT is not a detector, and neither are
  # field-shaped strings nested in `message.content`: a JSON parser reads the
  # envelope so an assistant discussing this feature cannot force-swap itself.
  # Same window and the same chatter rule as `_transcript_stalled_pair`:
  # local-command rows are not turns; any other user/assistant row after the
  # banner means the session moved on (a re-drive, a human message, a new turn).
  # `-f && -r`, never `-r` alone — D-2347's class: `-r` is true for a FIFO and an
  # unbounded character device, and `tail` on either blocks for ever (D-2370).
  local f="$1" rows rc
  [[ -f "$f" && -r "$f" ]] || return 2
  rows=$(tail -n "$REDRIVE_TAIL_LINES" "$f" 2>/dev/null) || return 2
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
  printf '%s\n' "$rows" | LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import json, sys

found = None
for raw in sys.stdin:
    try:
        row = json.loads(raw)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        found = None
        continue
    if not isinstance(row, dict):
        found = None
        continue
    if row.get("type") not in ("user", "assistant"):
        continue
    message = row.get("message")
    content = message.get("content") if isinstance(message, dict) else None
    if row.get("type") == "assistant" and row.get("isApiErrorMessage") is True and row.get("error") == "rate_limit":
        found = row
        continue
    chatter = row.get("type") == "user" and isinstance(content, str) and content.startswith(
        ("<local-command-caveat>", "<command-name>", "<local-command-stdout>")
    )
    if chatter:
        continue
    found = None

if found is None:
    raise SystemExit(1)
quota = found.get("quotaLimits")
quota = quota if isinstance(quota, dict) else {}
reset = quota.get("resetsAt")
kind = quota.get("rateLimitType")
reset = str(reset) if isinstance(reset, int) and not isinstance(reset, bool) else ""
print(f"{reset}\t{kind if isinstance(kind, str) else chr(0)}".rstrip(chr(0)))
' 2>/dev/null
  rc=$?
  [[ "$rc" -le 1 ]] && return "$rc"
  return 2
}

_transcript_last_turn_ts() {   # transcript-path -> 0 + the newest REAL turn's epoch seconds | 1 no real turn in the window | 2 unreadable (D-3102)
  # WHAT A TURN IS, HERE. `_transcript_limit_banner`'s chatter rule, plus the two
  # rows a COMPACTION writes that its rule does not name — because that rule was
  # written for "did the session move on after a banner", where a compaction
  # cannot have intervened, and this one is asked EXACTLY ACROSS a compaction.
  # Measured on a live transcript (2026-09-18 02:30 -> 02:34): one `ccd`
  # `/compact` appends FOUR `type:"user"` rows and nothing else —
  #
  #   1. the summary itself, `isCompactSummary:true` ("This session is being
  #      continued from a previous conversation…"), timestamped at the moment the
  #      summary LANDED, ~4 minutes AFTER ccd typed the keys;
  #   2. `isMeta:true`, content `<local-command-caveat>…`;
  #   3. content `<command-name>/compact</command-name>…`;
  #   4. content `<local-command-stdout>[Compacted…`.
  #
  # Rows 2-4 are already the three chatter prefixes. Row 1 is not, and it is the
  # one that matters: it carries a timestamp LATER than `lastcompact`, so a
  # reader that counted it would see "a turn happened since the compaction" on
  # every single compacted session and the guard above would never fire once.
  # `isCompactSummary` is the field that names it, and `isMeta` is dropped with
  # it — an injected context row is not a turn a human or the model took.
  #
  # NEWEST WINS AND NOTHING RESETS. Unlike the banner reader, which asks "is the
  # banner still the LAST word" and therefore clears its candidate on any later
  # real row, this one asks only "when did anything real last happen", so a real
  # row simply replaces the answer. There is no state to lose.
  #
  # `-f && -r`, never `-r` alone — D-2347's class, the same one the banner reader
  # carries: `-r` is true for a FIFO and for an unbounded character device, and
  # `tail` on either blocks for ever inside the 5-second tick (D-2370).
  #
  # THE TIMESTAMP IS PARSED, NOT TRUSTED. Claude Code writes RFC3339 in UTC with
  # a literal `Z`; anything this cannot read is skipped as if the row carried no
  # timestamp at all, so a malformed row can only make the answer OLDER (fail
  # open at the caller), never newer.
  local f="$1" rows rc
  [[ -f "$f" && -r "$f" ]] || return 2
  rows=$(tail -n "$COMPACT_TURN_TAIL_LINES" "$f" 2>/dev/null) || return 2
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || return 2
  printf '%s\n' "$rows" | LC_ALL=C.UTF-8 python3 -c '
import calendar, json, sys, time

newest = None
for raw in sys.stdin:
    try:
        row = json.loads(raw)
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, UnicodeDecodeError):
        continue
    if not isinstance(row, dict):
        continue
    if row.get("type") not in ("user", "assistant"):
        continue
    if row.get("isCompactSummary") is True or row.get("isMeta") is True:
        continue
    message = row.get("message")
    content = message.get("content") if isinstance(message, dict) else None
    if row.get("type") == "user" and isinstance(content, str) and content.startswith(
        ("<local-command-caveat>", "<command-name>", "<local-command-stdout>")
    ):
        continue
    stamp = row.get("timestamp")
    if not isinstance(stamp, str) or len(stamp) < 19:
        continue
    try:
        epoch = calendar.timegm(time.strptime(stamp[:19], "%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S"))
    except ValueError:
        continue
    if newest is None or epoch > newest:
        newest = epoch

if newest is None:
    raise SystemExit(1)
print(newest)
' 2>/dev/null
  rc=$?
  [[ "$rc" -le 1 ]] && return "$rc"
  return 2
}

COMPACT_TURN_DETAIL=""
_compact_no_turns() {   # id -> 0 iff the transcript PROVES no real turn since `lastcompact` (D-3103)
  # THE DEFECT, MEASURED BEFORE IT WAS NAMED. `_auto_compact_check` decided on
  # two numbers — the `lastcompact` cooldown and `ctx%` against the threshold —
  # and neither of them is a fact about whether the CONVERSATION changed. A
  # session whose post-compaction floor sits at or above its own threshold
  # therefore re-compacts every `COMPACT_COOLDOWN` for ever, summarising a
  # summary it has already summarised. On this fleet's `$REG/swap.log`,
  # 2026-09-19: of 622 `auto-compact` lines, 163 came within 40 minutes of the
  # previous compaction OF THE SAME SESSION, and all 163 read the IDENTICAL
  # `ctx%` as the line before them — ONE workspace alone took 25 consecutive
  # compactions at exactly 1800-second spacing, every one reading `ctx 51%`, from
  # 20:39 on 2026-09-10 to 08:40 the next morning. An identical percentage is the
  # signature: nothing had changed, and compacting changed nothing.
  #
  # FAIL OPEN, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE SHAPE OF THIS GUARD. It answers 0 only on a
  # POSITIVE measurement that nothing happened; every other condition — no
  # `lastcompact` to be unchanged since, a transcript this box cannot name or
  # read, a tail window holding no real turn, python absent — answers 1 and the
  # compactor behaves exactly as it did before D-3103. A new guard that silences
  # the compactor on an unreadable transcript would trade a wasteful compaction
  # for a session that never compacts at all, which is the #67 R1 shape
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s own header forbids: a fix for one defect must not open a
  # live one beside it.
  #
  # THE EPOCH COMPARISON IS `-le`, NOT `-lt`. `lastcompact` is stamped the
  # instant BEFORE the send-keys, and a turn that began in the same second is a
  # turn this guard has no business refusing — but it is also indistinguishable
  # from the compaction's own second at one-second resolution, and the two
  # readings differ by a single wasted tick in one direction and a lost
  # compaction in the other. Equal reads as "nothing since"; the next tick
  # re-measures.
  COMPACT_TURN_DETAIL=""
  local id="$1" last f ts
  last=$(_reg_get "$id" lastcompact)
  [[ -n "$last" ]] || return 1
  f=$(_transcript_path "$id") || return 1
  ts=$(_transcript_last_turn_ts "$f") || return 1
  # THE DIGIT TESTS ARE FIRST AND INSIDE THE SAME `[[ ]]` as the arithmetic —
  # D-299's shape, and BOTH operands need one: `last` is a registry field (torn
  # or hand-edited is the threat model) and `ts` is this box's own python
  # stdout, which is a pipeline that can answer anything at all if it is ever
  # replaced. `ccd-arith-containment.test.ts`'s SITES table pins this line.
  [[ "$last" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$ts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$ts" -le "$last" ]] || return 1
  COMPACT_TURN_DETAIL="newest turn $((last - ts))s before the last compaction"
  return 0
}

_redrive_standdown_log() {   # id fromswap rc transcript-path — D-2265: the ONE lane built to
  # catch the redrive flag itself failing (rc 1 the pair is not on disk, rc 2 the transcript is
  # unreadable) used to stand `_redrive_after_spawn` down with zero swap.log evidence. Logged ONLY
  # on a swap landing (`fromswap`, threaded from `_spawn_settle`'s own positional) — an ordinary
  # new spawn also carries CLAUDE_CODE_RESUME_INTERRUPTED_TURN=1 but writes no pair, so rc 1 there
  # is the routine case, not news.
  local id="$1" fromswap="$2" rc="$3" f="$4"
  [[ "$fromswap" == 1 ]] || return 0
  case "$rc" in
    1) echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: not-stalled" >> "$REG/swap.log" ;;
    2) echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: no transcript at $f" >> "$REG/swap.log" ;;
  esac
}

_redrive_after_spawn() {   # id tmuxname fromswap — D-2231: type the resume prompt when the flag wrote the pair and drove nothing.
  # Measured on the TRANSCRIPT, not on hookstate (D-2237): `working` proves tool
  # calls, not that the re-drive took. Gives Claude Code REDRIVE_WAIT_S seconds
  # first — a running turn ("esc to interrupt") or a real row after the pair ends
  # the wait — then stands down on an armed auto-continue, a hard-blocked pane
  # or a draft in the box, each with its own swap.log line.
  #
  # PANE WINDOW IS `tail -8`, EVERYWHERE BELOW BUT `_pane_box_draft` (D-2264).
  # Every other established consumer of `esc to interrupt`/`_pane_auto_continue_armed`/
  # `_pane_hard_blocked` narrows first — `_auto_swap_check` and `_auto_compact_check` both
  # `| tail -8` their own capture — and `_auto_compact_check`'s own header (the "That is not
  # cosmetic" paragraph, just above its `pane=$(tmux capture-pane …)` line) already documents
  # why: a full ~50-row capture lets `grep -q "esc to interrupt"` match a STALE banner further
  # up the scrollback, and
  # `_pane_hard_blocked`'s `limit reached|API Error: 429|rate limit` alternation is exactly what a
  # `--resume` replay of the interrupted turn's own API-error row renders as. That row can sit
  # outside the last 8 lines while still being well inside a full capture, so widening this
  # function's window turns "redrive-skip … hard-blocked pane" into a silent, permanent stand-down
  # on precisely the sessions this fallback exists to rescue. `_pane_box_has_content` alone keeps
  # the full `-e` capture: it scopes itself from the LAST `❯` to the closing box rule.
  local id="$1" t="$2" fromswap="${3:-0}" f i rc pane
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: pane is under ${READER_MIN_COLS} columns (§6.3)" >> "$REG/swap.log"; return 0; }
  f=$(_transcript_path "$id") || return 0
  for ((i = 0; i < REDRIVE_WAIT_S; i++)); do
    _transcript_stalled_pair "$f"; rc=$?
    [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || { _redrive_standdown_log "$id" "$fromswap" "$rc" "$f"; return 0; }
    pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8)
    echo "$pane" | grep -q "esc to interrupt" && return 0
    sleep 1
  done
  # Re-measure both loop conditions once more: the loop's own last check was one
  # second ago, and Claude Code's own re-drive (or the operator) can start a turn
  # in that gap. Typing into a turn that started here would be exactly the
  # spurious keystroke the header comment promises never happens (D-2263).
  _transcript_stalled_pair "$f"; rc=$?; [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || { _redrive_standdown_log "$id" "$fromswap" "$rc" "$f"; return 0; }
  pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8)
  echo "$pane" | grep -q "esc to interrupt" && return 0
  # A FRESH MEASURE, NOT A REDUNDANT ONE, and this is the site where that is true:
  # REDRIVE_WAIT_S seconds of real `sleep 1`s sit between the top-of-function guard and
  # here, so the pane can have gone narrow in the meantime. (A comment in
  # `_auto_compact_check` used to make the contrast and name this site; routing slice 4
  # moved the call it was about into `_pane_for_keystroke`, where its premise is untrue
  # for that helper's other two callers, so it was dropped rather than relocated. The
  # argument survives here, where it is still about this function's own wait loop.) But its NEGATIVE answer is not "auto-continue armed" —
  # that would be `_pane_auto_continue_armed`'s own inverted answer to the SAME
  # unmeasurable pane, naming a cause (Claude Code's own recovery timer) this line never
  # detected. Logged as its own sentence — distinct wording from this same
  # function's TOP-OF-FUNCTION guard above the wait loop, so swap.log can tell
  # "never wide enough to redrive at all" from "went narrow during the
  # REDRIVE_WAIT_S wait" instead of collapsing both to one line.
  _pane_measurable "$id" || { echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: pane went under ${READER_MIN_COLS} columns during the redrive wait (§6.3)" >> "$REG/swap.log"; return 0; }
  _pane_auto_continue_armed "$pane" "$id" && { echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: auto-continue armed" >> "$REG/swap.log"; return 0; }
  _pane_hard_blocked "$pane" && { echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: hard-blocked pane" >> "$REG/swap.log"; return 0; }
  _pane_box_has_content "$(tmux capture-pane -t "$t" -p -e 2>/dev/null)" \
    && { echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive-skip $id: input box not empty" >> "$REG/swap.log"; return 0; }
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') redrive $id: the transcript's newest turn is the unsubmitted resume pair after ${REDRIVE_WAIT_S}s; typing the prompt [wrapper=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  tmux send-keys -t "$t" -l "$RESUME_PROMPT"; sleep 1; tmux send-keys -t "$t" Enter
  return 0
}

# Its own function for the same reason _ws_supervise and _ws_unsupervise are (ccd:212-216): a test
# harness can stub it, and `command -v` finds a shell function before it finds a binary, so nothing
# else could.
# `_have_systemctl` IS DEFINED IN THE PLATFORM BLOCK at the top of this file,
# not here, and that move is not tidying: `ccrc` and `ccrc-doctor-checks` ask
# the same question (`_box_units`, `_check_services`) and they get the block —
# they do not get this part of the file. Defined here, it was missing there,
# and both read a box with a healthy launchd as one with no service manager.

_ws_unit_state() {   # id -> enabled|loaded|absent, or EMPTY where there is no systemctl OR the probe refused
  # Three states, and the empty fourth answer is deliberately not one of them:
  # "this box has no systemd" is not "this unit is absent", and the JSON writer
  # renders the empty string as `null` rather than inventing a word.
  #
  #   enabled — a default.target.wants symlink exists: it will come back at boot.
  #   loaded  — the manager knows the unit, but nothing promises a next boot.
  #   absent  — list-units ANSWERED and does not name it at all.
  #
  # `absent` is a MEASUREMENT, not a fallthrough, and the empty answer covers a
  # probe that refused as well as a box with no systemctl. Piping list-units
  # straight into `grep -qF` collapsed the two: a down or unreachable `--user`
  # manager produces no rows, grep fails, and every workspace on the box would
  # report `absent` — "nothing is watching this session", which is the precise
  # F8 orphan signature this field exists to make visible. So the rc is read.
  #
  # THE PROBE IS `list-units`, NEVER `systemctl show`: `show` on an
  # UNINSTANTIATED TEMPLATE reports LoadState=loaded, so a naive check calls
  # every id in the roster "loaded". Measured live — list-units returned
  # exactly 18 rows, and the six ids absent from it had no unit loaded at all.
  _have_systemctl || return 0
  # THREE STATEMENTS, not one `local id=… unit="…$id…" link`: bash expands
  # every assignment word of a `local` BEFORE it performs any of them, so the
  # second word reads `$id` while it is still the freshly-declared, unset local
  # — and under `set -uo pipefail` that is `id: unbound variable`, which exits
  # the shell rather than answering. (Measured; it only LOOKED green from
  # `cmd_ws_audit`, whose own `local id` had already dynamically scoped one.)
  local id="$1" unit link
  unit="claude-session@$id.service"
  # THE DARWIN ARM, and it answers the same three words from the same two
  # observables. A plist in ~/Library/LaunchAgents IS boot persistence — it is
  # what the `default.target.wants` symlink means on the other platform — so
  # its presence is `enabled`. A label launchd holds with no plist behind it
  # was bootstrapped and then had its file removed: the manager knows it,
  # nothing promises a next boot, which is exactly `loaded`. Neither is
  # `absent`, and it is a MEASUREMENT here too: both probes answered.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    [ -e "$(_svc_plist "$unit")" ] && { echo enabled; return 0; }
    # THE EMPTY FOURTH ANSWER, kept here too. `absent` is a MEASUREMENT — the
    # manager answered and named no such job — and it must not be reachable
    # from a probe that never ran. `_svc_is_loaded` is a boolean and cannot
    # tell the two apart, so the status is read directly: 113 is launchctl's
    # "Could not find service", which IS the measurement; anything else (no
    # launchctl, the sandbox guard refusing) is a question that was not asked,
    # and the JSON writer renders that as `null` rather than inventing a word.
    local _lo _lrc
    _lo="$(_svc_launchctl print "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "$unit")" 2>/dev/null)"; _lrc=$?
    if [ "$_lrc" -eq 0 ]; then echo loaded; return 0; fi
    if [ "$_lrc" -eq 113 ]; then echo absent; return 0; fi
    return 0
  fi
  link="$HOME/.config/systemd/user/default.target.wants/$unit"
  # -L as well as -e: a wants symlink whose target is gone is still an enable.
  [[ -L "$link" || -e "$link" ]] && { echo enabled; return 0; }
  local rows
  # CAPTURED, not piped: `$(…)`'s status is systemctl's own, so a refusal
  # degrades to the documented empty answer instead of to the FACT `absent`.
  rows=$(systemctl --user list-units 'claude-session@*' --all --plain --no-legend 2>/dev/null) \
    || return 0
  grep -qF "$unit" <<<"$rows" && { echo loaded; return 0; }
  echo absent
}

_supervised_start() {   # id — ask the UNIT to spawn, then WAIT for an observable verdict.
  # `ccd start`/`ccd ensure` outside a unit become `_svc_enable_now`, and the unit's
  # ExecStart (`ccd supervise %i`) does the spawning it already does. That is what makes a restarted
  # session a SUPERVISED one — with _sync_uuid, _auto_swap_check and _auto_compact_check running and
  # something to record its death — instead of the bare pane stop-then-start used to leave.
  #
  # `local a=1 b="$a"` does NOT see `a`: local expands all its arguments before assigning any of
  # them, and under `set -u` that is a fatal unbound-variable error. Hence the second line.
  local id="$1" since i st at rc unit
  unit="claude-session@$id"
  since=$(date +%s)
  if ! _have_systemctl; then
    # TWO SPELLINGS OF ONE SENTENCE, and the Linux one is the ORIGINAL BYTES.
    # This message is asserted verbatim by `ccd-supervised-start.test.ts`, and
    # more to the point an operator greps for it: the port is not entitled to
    # reword a Linux box's output. Only the binary's name differs, because
    # only the binary differs.
    if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
      echo "ccd: warn: launchctl not found — starting $id UNSUPERVISED (no auto-swap, no uuid-sync, nothing to restart it)" >&2
    else
      echo "ccd: warn: systemctl not found — starting $id UNSUPERVISED (no auto-swap, no uuid-sync, nothing to restart it)" >&2
    fi
    # THE SPLIT FORM HERE TOO. A fallback that still spawns-first re-opens F8's
    # hole on exactly the boxes least able to recover: no systemd at all, or a
    # unit that will not enable. There is no `_ws_supervise` on this path by
    # construction — that is what "UNSUPERVISED" in the warning above means —
    # so the claim is the only write there is to get in front of the block.
    # No `$( )` around `_spawn_start`: its `die` must stay process-fatal.
    local mode=new; [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && mode=resume
    _spawn_start "$id" "$mode" || return $?
    _reg_claim "$id"
    _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
    return "$rc"
  fi
  # §3.3 deliberately creates failed units — that is the point of the start limit — and a unit
  # sitting in `failed` refuses to start again until its failure is cleared. Without this, the very
  # verb §4.4 advertises as "what revives it" would not.
  _svc_reset_failed "$unit" 2>/dev/null
  if ! _svc_enable_now "$unit" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "ccd: warn: could not enable unit $unit — starting $id UNSUPERVISED (unit installed? lingering?)" >&2
    # The same split, for the same reason as the no-systemctl fallback above:
    # this is the OTHER box that cannot recover on its own, so leaving a live
    # pane no row claims is worse here than anywhere else.
    local mode=new; [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && mode=resume
    _spawn_start "$id" "$mode" || return $?
    _reg_claim "$id"
    _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
    return "$rc"
  fi
  # `enable --now` returns when systemd has STARTED THE UNIT, not when a pane exists — but
  # `ccd attach` and `ccd menu` exec `tmux attach` on the next line, and §3.3 requires a failed
  # spawn to be reported rather than assumed. So wait on two observables: the pane (success, exactly
  # as today, since the TUI may still be working through a long resume) or a spawn stamp NEWER than
  # this call carrying a non-zero rc. The freshness test is what stops the last failure this row
  # ever had from being reported as this one's; a stale stamp written in the same second as `since`
  # can only mean an operator is retrying a session that is failing right now, where reporting the
  # failure is still the right answer.
  for i in $(seq 1 "$SUPERVISED_START_WAIT"); do
    _alive "$id" && return 0
    st=$(_reg_get "$id" spawn)
    if [[ -n "$st" ]]; then
      at="${st%% *}"; rc="${st##* }"
      # `return` truncates its argument mod 256, so an unvalidated rc from a malformed stamp
      # could come back as 0 (success) on a genuine failure — measured: "1000" returns 232, and
      # anything ≡0 mod 256 would lie outright. Unreachable today (only `_spawn` writes this
      # field, always as two fields in one `printf`), but the field is a file, not a type: accept
      # the parsed rc only if it is a plausible one-to-three-digit exit code; otherwise the stamp
      # is unusable and the loop keeps polling rather than trusting it.
      if [[ "$at" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && "$at" -ge "$since" && "$rc" =~ ^[0-9]{1,3}$ && "$rc" -le 255 && "$rc" != 0 ]]; then
        echo "ccd: $id failed to start: spawn rc $rc (see $REG/$id.spawn)" >&2
        return "$rc"
      fi
    fi
    sleep 1
  done
  echo "ccd: $id: unit started but no pane appeared within ${SUPERVISED_START_WAIT}s — $(_svc_status_hint "$unit")" >&2
  return 1
}

_resupervise_live() {   # id -> rc 0 it adopted (or tried), rc 1 there was nothing to adopt.
  # THE REVIVE PATH FOR §4.3's `unsupervised` ROW, which until now had none.
  # Every one of the three human-facing verbs returned before it could reach an
  # `enable --now`: `cmd_ensure` early-returns on `_alive`, and `cmd_start`'s
  # alive branch (which `cmd_enable` aliases through) issues `enable` WITHOUT
  # `--now` — boot persistence, not a supervisor. So the PWA rendered "running
  # unsupervised" beside a Restart button posting `/ensure`, which answered
  # success and changed nothing, on what is the whole population of D2's defect:
  # on deploy day every pane a pre-fix `ccd start` minted reads `unsupervised`.
  #
  # `enable --now` on a LIVE pane is not a second spawn. The unit runs
  # `cmd_supervise`, which stamps `supervised` and then calls `cmd_ensure` with
  # CCD_IN_UNIT set — that ensure finds the pane alive, returns, and the watch
  # loop adopts it. Measured rather than assumed; `ccd-supervised-start.test.ts`
  # pins the no-second-pane half.
  #
  # Scoped to the row that needs it, deliberately. `running` (a fresh heartbeat)
  # must stay the cheap no-op `ensure` has always been — the PWA's revive button
  # is reachable on any row, and a `_svc_enable_now` per click on
  # a healthy fleet is a round-trip nobody asked for. And a DEAD pane is not this
  # function's business at all: `_supervised_start` already owns that path.
  #
  # TWO ROW SHAPES REACH THE SAME ADOPTION, and on the `unclaimed` one the
  # repair is a CLAIM as well as a process — see the two blocks below.
  local id="$1"
  [[ "${CCD_IN_UNIT:-}" == 1 ]] && return 1   # the supervisor is the thing being asked for
  _have_systemctl || return 1
  # TWO SHAPES, ONE ADOPTION — and `unclaimed` is why this line is not a
  # single-word comparison any more. Wave 1 made `unclaimed` the FIRST rung
  # inside `_session_state`'s alive branch, so F8's residue (a live pane with a
  # fresh heartbeat and no claim) stopped answering `unsupervised` — which is
  # the population this function was written for in the first place. Narrowing
  # it back would silently delete PR #50's repair path for the exact rows this
  # build exists to name.
  local state; state=$(_session_state "$id")
  case "$state" in unsupervised|unclaimed) : ;; *) return 1 ;; esac
  # THE CLAIM IS HALF THE REPAIR, and only on this branch. `unclaimed`'s repair
  # is a CLAIM (a process is running that no row claims); `orphan`'s is a
  # PROCESS. Both `cmd_ensure` and `cmd_start` early-return on `_alive` and so
  # never reach `_spawn_start`'s `_reg_claim` — without this write, `ccd ensure`
  # on a live unclaimed pane would enable a unit and leave the row unclaimed for
  # ever, and the PWA's "Restart session" would keep promising a repair it does
  # not perform. A `running` row must NOT be claimed here: it already is.
  [[ "$state" == unclaimed ]] && _reg_claim "$id"
  # §3.3 deliberately creates failed units and a failed one refuses to start
  # until cleared — the same reset-failed `_supervised_start` carries, for the
  # same reason, on the path that reaches systemd without going through it.
  _svc_reset_failed "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null
  if _svc_enable_now "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null; then
    echo "re-supervised $id — a live pane with no supervisor; its unit has adopted it"
  else
    echo "ccd: warn: $id is alive but UNSUPERVISED and its unit would not start (unit installed? lingering?) — no auto-swap, no auto-compact, no uuid-sync" >&2
  fi
  return 0
}

cmd_start() {   # <id>  |  <wrapper> <project> [workdir]
  # THE ONE-ARGUMENT FORM IS THE CORRECT ONE FOR ANYTHING THAT ALREADY EXISTS.
  # `<wrapper> <project>` recomputes `<wrapper>-<project>`, and a session keeps
  # the id it was born with across every swap — `claude2-expoAI-assistant` is
  # still that id while its `wrapper` field reads `claude-dev0`. So an operator
  # reading the account off the board and typing it back MINTS A SECOND ID for
  # a session that already exists. `cmd_stop` grew this form first, for the
  # same reason, and its own comment records it.
  #
  # There is no id form for CREATION: a new session needs an account and a
  # project, and there is NO WORKDIR TO INVENT from an id alone. A single
  # argument with no registry row is therefore refused with the creating
  # form's usage in the message, never resolved to `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<id>`.
  #
  # `--cross-pool` IS STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE, exactly as `cmd_stop`
  # strips `--surface`: `$# -ge 2` is what distinguishes the creating form from
  # the id form, so a flag left in argv would make `ccd start --cross-pool <id>`
  # read as a creation and mint `--cross-pool-<id>`.
  #
  # `--route` IS STRIPPED HERE FOR THE SAME REASON, and it takes a VALUE, so the
  # arity check is not optional: ccd runs under `set -uo pipefail` with no `-e`,
  # and a `shift 2` past the end of argv shifts nothing (see `cmd_ws_add`'s own
  # measurement of this shape). Like `--cross-pool` it stays out of the usage
  # line, which names the two POSITIONAL forms this verb takes.
  local cross="" args=() route=()
  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
      --cross-pool) cross=1; shift ;;
      --route)      [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd start <id> | ccd start <wrapper> <project> [workdir]"
                    route+=("$2"); shift 2 ;;
      --route=*)    route+=("${1#--route=}"); shift ;;
      --)           shift; args+=("$@"); break ;;
      *)            args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"    # bash >= 4.4: an empty array here is not an unbound-
                         # variable error under `set -u`, as in cmd_swap.
  # BEFORE ANY WRITE, and before the two forms diverge: a bad value must leave
  # the registry exactly as it found it whichever form was typed (spec §5.3).
  (( ${#route[@]} )) && _route_argv_check "${route[@]}"
  local id wrapper="" project="" workdir=""
  if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then
    wrapper="$1"; project="$2"; workdir="${3:-}"
    [[ -n "$wrapper" && -n "$project" ]] \
      || die "usage: ccd start <id> | ccd start <wrapper> <project> [workdir]"
    _is_valid_wrapper "$wrapper" || die "unknown wrapper '$wrapper' (valid: ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]})"
    # SYMMETRY, not aliasing risk here: `cmd_start` mints no aliasing id of
    # its own — its id is `<wrapper>-<project>`, and roster ids cannot lead
    # with a dot. Refusing the same value at BOTH creation verbs is what
    # makes the stronger, simpler statement true — no registry row EVER
    # holds a dot-leading project — so no future reader has to re-derive
    # which verb could alias and which could not. See `_ws_project_valid`.
    _ws_project_valid "$project" \
      || die "invalid project '$project' — allowed chars: A-Za-z0-9 . _ -, and no LEADING dot"
    id=$(_id "$wrapper" "$project")
  else
    id="${1:?usage: ccd start <id> | ccd start <wrapper> <project> [workdir]}"
    # The id becomes a path under $REG, so its shape is checked exactly as
    # cmd_forget and cmd_ws_hold check theirs.
    [[ "$id" =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || die "bad session id"
    [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] \
      || die "no registry for '$id' — a new session needs its account and project: ccd start <wrapper> <project> [workdir]"
  fi

  # THE REGISTRY WINS OVER THE ARGUMENT, FOR THE ACCOUNT ONLY (spec §3.4).
  # `_reg_set "$id" wrapper "$wrapper"` used to be unconditional, so
  # `ccd start claude2 expoAI-assistant` on a session auto-swap had moved to
  # claude-dev0 rewrote the row to an account whose config dir does not hold
  # the transcript — the resumed session then finds no history under the very
  # account it was just told it lives on. `ccd swap` is the ONLY verb that
  # moves a session between accounts; a differing argument is a warning, and
  # the warning names the verb that would actually do what was asked.
  local regw; regw=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  if [[ -n "$regw" ]]; then
    [[ -z "$wrapper" || "$wrapper" == "$regw" ]] \
      || echo "ccd: warn: $id lives on $regw, not $wrapper — starting it on $regw (to move it: ccd swap $id $wrapper)" >&2
    wrapper="$regw"
  fi
  [[ -n "$wrapper" ]] || die "no wrapper recorded for '$id' and none given"
  [[ -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/$wrapper" ]] || die "wrapper missing: $WRAPPER_DIR/$wrapper"
  # AFTER the registry has won (above), so this asks about the account this verb
  # will actually write rather than the one the operator typed.
  _account_still_rostered "$wrapper" "Nothing was written."

  # The workdir stays the creating form's third argument to set. An id alone
  # carries no directory, so the id form READS what the row already has and
  # writes nothing back over it.
  #
  # B3'S THIRD VERB READER, MEASURED (#69 review round 4 gate, finding 4). This
  # read was `_reg_get`, which folds ABSENT, UNREADABLE and EMPTY into one `""`
  # — and `_project_pool_state ""` answers `untagged` at its first line, which
  # permits every account. So an unreadable `.project` did not merely go
  # unreported: it made the pool block below decide `in pool` and say nothing.
  # Round 4 closed the same fold at `cmd_swap` and `cmd_prefer` and reported the
  # verb readers as done; this was the third, and one of three left is worse
  # than three, because the next reader inherits "done" as the baseline.
  #
  # WHICH HALF IS ACTUALLY REACHABLE, and the gate's own finding overstates it
  # by half — corrected here rather than carried. The gate says "both the die
  # and the warn go silent". The die sits on the `-z "$regw"` arm, and `regw` is
  # NON-EMPTY on every path that reaches this read: this read runs only on the
  # ID form, where an empty `regw` has already died four lines up at `no wrapper
  # recorded for '$id' and none given`. So the die is unreachable from here and
  # what the fold silenced is the WARNING. The remedy below is a warning for the
  # same reason ruling 5 gives the mismatch one — a revival must not be refused
  # for a placement the auto-swapper moves at the next idle boundary.
  #
  # AND `-z "$regw"` IS NOT A FORM TEST, which this paragraph and the one below
  # both used to call it (both corrected in the same edit — #69 review round 5,
  # its own refute pass). `regw` comes from `_reg_get`, so it is EMPTY for a
  # row whose `.wrapper` is absent,
  # unreadable OR blank — the overloaded null this whole PR is about. Saying
  # "creation-only" turns a measured claim about ONE path into a false claim
  # about the branch. The narrow statement above is the one that holds, and
  # `.wrapper`'s own fold is B3's FOURTH verb reader, pre-existing on
  # `origin/main` and booked as its own follow-up rather than fixed here.
  local prjrc=0
  if [[ -n "$project" ]]; then
    [[ -z "$workdir" ]] && workdir="$PROJECTS_ROOT/$project"
  else
    project=$(_reg_read "$id" project); prjrc=$?
    workdir=$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)
  fi
  [[ "$workdir" != *\'* ]] || die "workdir must not contain a single quote: $workdir"
  [[ -d "$workdir" ]] || die "workdir missing: $workdir"

  # ── POOL POLICY (§5.5.5), CREATION ONLY ──────────────────────────────────
  # Placed here rather than at the registry-wins block above because `project`
  # is only resolved on BOTH forms by the block above it; `regw` non-empty is
  # what selects the warn arm, and that is the whole correctness argument. It is
  # a proxy for "this row already exists" and NOT a form test — see the
  # correction above; an unreadable `.wrapper` empties it on an existing row. With a
  # registry row the registry's account has already WON (the block above), and
  # `wrapper` is no longer the caller's argument, so a refusal here would
  # refuse a REVIVAL of a session a retag put in the wrong pool — which
  # ruling 5 moves at the next idle boundary instead. At most a warning.
  local pps prc pside pstate
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  _pool_ok "$wrapper" "$pps"; prc=$?
  # SNAPSHOT THE TWO FACTS IMMEDIATELY (fix round 1, I1/I2) — see the header at
  # the rule itself. Nothing runs between here and the two `die`s below today,
  # so reading the globals directly would be correct today and one inserted
  # call away from being silently wrong; the snapshot is what makes "which side
  # was undecidable" a fact about THIS call rather than about the last one.
  local pside="$POOL_OK_SIDE" pstate="$POOL_OK_STATE"
  if [[ -z "$regw" ]]; then
    [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]] \
      && die "$(_pool_undecidable_why "$pside" "$pstate" "$project" "$wrapper"); nothing was touched"
    [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -z "$cross" ]] \
      && die "pool-mismatch: $(_pool_mismatch_why "$wrapper" "$pstate" "$project" "$pps") — to cross on purpose: ccd start --cross-pool $wrapper $project"
  elif [[ "$prjrc" -eq 2 ]] && ! _pool_untaggable; then
    # SAID, NOT DECIDED. `$pps` is `untagged` here because the field could not be
    # read, not because the project carries no tag, so the mismatch arm below
    # cannot fire and its silence would read as "checked, and fine".
    echo "ccd: warn: the project field for $id could not be read ($REG/$id.project), so its pool was not checked — fix or clear it" >&2
  elif [[ "$prc" -eq 1 ]]; then
    echo "ccd: warn: $id lives on $wrapper, which is not in project '$project''s pool '${pps#named }' — the auto-swapper relocates it at the next idle boundary" >&2
  fi

  if _alive "$id"; then
    # M5's shape, reachable from a keyboard: a live pane whose unit was never enabled, or was
    # disabled without `--now` some other way. A bare `ccd start` staying a pure no-op here is
    # correct — a fresh spawn is not what an alive session needs — but `cmd_enable` aliases
    # straight through this branch, and its whole contract is "make this boot-persistent"; review
    # found it printing `enabled boot-persistence for <id>` here while issuing zero systemctl
    # calls. Reconcile unconditionally: idempotent, no `--now` (the same line cmd_supervise's own
    # self-heal uses), and never reached from the fresh-start branch below, which already ran
    # `enable --now` moments ago — so this never duplicates that call.
    #
    # …EXCEPT that a bare `enable` promises a start at NEXT BOOT and supervises
    # nothing now, so on the row §4.3 calls `unsupervised` — a live pane with no
    # heartbeat, which is every pane a pre-fix `ccd start` minted — this branch
    # was reconciling the symlink and leaving the actual defect in place.
    # `_resupervise_live` is the `--now` for exactly that row and nothing else;
    # when it declines (a `running` row, or no systemctl) the cheap boot-
    # persistence line below is still the right and only act.
    if _have_systemctl && ! _resupervise_live "$id"; then
      _svc_enable "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null \
        || echo "ccd: warn: could not enable boot-persistence for $id" >&2
    fi
    # THE NAMED PAIRS ARE WRITTEN ON THIS ARM TOO (controller ruling S4-R3,
    # final review finding 2). S4-R3's fix sat BELOW this return, so the silent
    # drop it was raised to close was still live for the commonest case of all:
    # `ccd enable --route class=opus claude demo` against a LIVE session printed
    # `already running`, wrote nothing, journaled nothing and exited 0 — and the
    # PWA renders that exit 0 as `{ok:true}` under a "Starting …" toast, so an
    # operator who sets Class/Effort in the new-session sheet for a wrapper and
    # project already running watched a write that never happened succeed.
    #
    # The pairs were validated at the top of this verb, before either positional
    # form was resolved, and the record exists by construction here — a live
    # session has a row — so this is `ccd route`'s write reached through the
    # verb the operator was already typing, exactly as the minting arm below
    # describes it. Nothing is TYPED: `--route` is not `--apply`, and the
    # supervise tick applies what this writes at the next idle pane.
    if (( ${#route[@]} )); then
      _route_argv_write "$id" start argv "${route[@]}"
    fi
    echo "already running: $id (find on claude.ai by name: $id)"; return 0
  fi
  # ── D-2605: ROW GENERATION IS MINTED HERE, BEFORE ANY ROW FIELD ─────────
  # The acquire comes BEFORE the first `_reg_set`, and a MISS FAILS CLOSED:
  # this verb dies with a retryable message naming the lock and leaves NOTHING
  # — no `.uuid`, no `.generation`, no partial row — so nothing irreversible
  # has happened and re-running is the whole remedy. That is the opposite
  # disposition from `_spawn_start`'s contended miss, and deliberately: there,
  # failing closed would wedge a rescue swap on a compaction lock; here, there
  # is nothing to wedge and a half-written row is the worse outcome.
  #
  # The lock is CLOSED before the setup hook and before `_spawn_start`, which
  # takes its own — it guards the residue check and the generation mint, and
  # nothing else.
  #
  # …AND A MISS IS TWO CONDITIONS, not one. `_compact_lock_acquire` answers rc 2
  # for MECHANISM ABSENCE and rc 1 for a refusal, deliberately, because §3.4's
  # platform outcome gives them opposite dispositions here: row creation is NOT
  # gated on the mechanism at all — it skips generation initialization and
  # CONTINUES — since gating pre-existing registry work on a mechanism it never
  # required would import a new failure mode into unrelated code. `cmd_start`
  # has no `command -v flock` probe of its own, so a bare `||` caught both and
  # `ccd start` was impossible on a flock-less userland, told to retry a 5 s
  # timeout that had not happened. The retryable message belongs to rc 1 alone;
  # no binary arrives because you ran the verb again. `_spawn_start` and
  # `_reg_purge` already branch this way; `cmd_ws_add` is the deliberate
  # opposite and dies, because it MINTS the generation.
  local gfd="" _cs_rc=0
  _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; _cs_rc=$?
  if (( _cs_rc == 1 )); then
    # Same conditional remedy as `cmd_ws_add`'s, and for the same reason.
    die "could not take $REG/.$id.compactions.lock within ${COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT}s — nothing was written; retry${COMPACT_LOCK_WHY:+ — EXCEPT that a retry cannot help here: the acquire measured $COMPACT_LOCK_WHY, a state of $REG that no wait changes. Inspect $REG/.$id.compactions.lock and $REG by hand: ccd will not mint a second lock inode over a live holder, will not write through an object occupying that pathname, and cannot mint at all into a $REG that refuses the write}"
  elif (( _cs_rc == 0 )); then
    gfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
    if ! _reg_generation_init "$id"; then
      _compact_lock_release "$gfd"
      die "$REG/$id.generation is present and invalid — refusing to start on it; inspect it by hand"
    fi
    _compact_lock_release "$gfd"; gfd=""
  fi
  local uuid; uuid=$(_reg_get "$id" uuid); [[ -z "$uuid" ]] && uuid=$(_plat_uuid)
  _reg_set "$id" wrapper "$wrapper"
  [[ -n "$project" ]] && _reg_set "$id" project "$project"
  _reg_set "$id" workdir "$workdir"; _reg_set "$id" uuid "$uuid"
  _ws_seed_home "$id" "$wrapper"
  # ── THE ROUTING RECORD: THE ARGV ALWAYS, THE DEFAULT ONLY AT A MINT ─────────
  # TWO CONDITIONS, TWO ARMS (controller ruling S4-R3). The first cut wrote
  # NEITHER on a revival, so `ccd enable --route class=opus claude demo` on a
  # session that already had a row validated the pair, wrote nothing, journaled
  # nothing, printed nothing and exited 0 — a request the operator watched
  # succeed and that changed no byte. That is the overloaded seam this tree
  # forbids: "you named fields" and "you named none" are not the same act.
  #
  # NAMED PAIRS ARE WRITTEN WHETHER THE ROW IS NEW OR OLD. They were validated
  # at the top of this verb, before either positional form was resolved, and
  # `_route_argv_write` reads the standing value so the row it journals says
  # what it replaced. This is `ccd route`'s write reached through the verb the
  # operator was already typing; the record still belongs to the session.
  #
  # THERE ARE TWO SUCH WRITE SITES, and this is the one a REVIVAL takes. The
  # other is the already-running arm above, which returns before this line ever
  # runs — the half S4-R3's first cut missed (final review finding 2).
  #
  # THE DEFAULT IS STILL MINT-ONLY. `$regw` empty is this verb's own "this row
  # did not exist" proxy — the same one the pool policy above uses, with the
  # same caveat stated there — and re-seeding the coordinator row over a
  # coordinator's considered fields at every restart would make the record mean
  # nothing.
  #
  # `start` is the actor on both arms, including the one `cmd_enable` reaches:
  # that verb forwards the pairs here rather than parsing them twice, and its own
  # `enable` lifecycle row already records that boot-persistence was asked for.
  if (( ${#route[@]} )); then
    _route_argv_write "$id" start argv "${route[@]}"
  elif [[ -z "$regw" ]]; then
    _route_seed_default "$id" "$wrapper"
  fi
  # The crossing marker for a creating `--cross-pool`. `_ws_seed_home` above
  # already pinned home to this wrapper, so the marker's account clause is
  # satisfied by BOTH `wrapper` and `home` from the first tick onward.
  [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -n "$cross" && -z "$regw" ]] && _crosspool_mark "$id" "${pps#named }" "$wrapper"
  local mode=new; [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && mode=resume
  # A deliberate revival supersedes a refused swap's banner: `ccd start <id>`
  # is the human act §4.4 names as "what would bring this back" (whether typed
  # directly or reached via the two-argument form once the registry has
  # reasserted the real account above), so a stale refusal must not go on
  # shadowing it.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.swapblocked"
  # …and a stale strand, at the same altitude and for the same reason: `ccd
  # start <id>` is the human act §4.4 names as "what would bring this back".
  _strand_clear "$id"
  # And a stale deliberate-stop stamp, at the same altitude and for the same
  # reason (§4.1: "cleared by ccd start, ccd enable and any successful
  # spawn"). `_spawn`'s own clear only fires on prompt_rc==0, and on THIS path
  # it runs in a DIFFERENT PROCESS entirely — `_supervised_start` below polls a
  # unit whose ExecStart is a separate `ccd supervise` invocation. When that
  # unit never comes up at all (a crash-looped FAILED unit, `enable --now`
  # itself failing, `_supervised_start`'s own 30s timeout with no spawn stamp
  # yet), `_spawn` never runs in THIS process and never gets the chance to
  # clear it — the brief's original "clear inside _spawn" fix could not have
  # reached this case. Cleared here on ATTEMPT, unconditionally, exactly like
  # swapblocked above: `started` stays 1 regardless of the outcome (restated
  # below), so a failed revival still reads `orphan` — the honest signal that
  # someone tried and it didn't come up — instead of a stale `stopped` that
  # hides the attempt and tells the operator to do the thing they just did.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.stopped"
  # ONE EMIT, AT THE TOP, because this verb has FIVE exits — the supervised
  # arm's (ccd:11916 `return "$rc"`, ccd:11918 `return 0`), the in-unit arm's
  # (ccd:11927's `|| return $?`, ccd:11935 `return "$rc"`, and falling off the end
  # at ccd:11937) — plus `_supervised_start`'s own unsupervised fallbacks. A line
  # per exit misses one. The OUTCOME is not asserted here: `_reg_claim`'s
  # `claim` line and `_spawn_settle`'s `spawn` line (with its rc) are the
  # measured downstream facts, and composing the record from independent
  # measurements is the point.
  _lc_done start "$id" "" meas.mode "$mode" meas.wrapper "$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)"
  if [[ "${CCD_IN_UNIT:-}" != 1 ]]; then
    local rc; _supervised_start "$id"; rc=$?
    # `started` stays unconditional here too (Task 4's own invariant, restated below): a
    # supervised start that never even reaches the in-unit cmd_ensure — the crash-looped FAILED
    # unit this task's own StartLimitBurst is designed to produce, or `_supervised_start`'s own
    # 30s timeout with no spawn stamp yet — must still classify `orphan` once a later task reads
    # this field, not `never-started`. `($mode)` now appears here too (Task 6): the id form exists
    # precisely so an operator can tell whether they resumed the row they named or minted a fresh
    # one, and the supervised path is the one a human at a keyboard actually takes.
    _reg_claim "$id"
    [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || return "$rc"
    echo "started $id ($mode) — discover on claude.ai by name: $id"
    return 0
  fi
  # SPLIT, so the claim precedes the blocking wait. IT MUST NOT SUPERVISE, and
  # that must stay: `cmd_supervise` IS the unit's ExecStart and reaches this
  # branch (and `cmd_ensure`'s) with CCD_IN_UNIT=1, so supervising here would
  # have the unit `enable --now` itself on every restart — ccd records that as
  # an explicit decision ("ccd ensure does NOT re-supervise … boot persistence
  # would be silently lost") and this build does not overturn it.
  local rc
  _spawn_start "$id" "$mode" || return $?
  # `started` stays unconditional: it records that this row has HAD a session, which is what §4.3's
  # never-started rung reads. A spawn that failed is still a row that was started — it classifies
  # `orphan`, which is the honest answer, not `never-started`.
  _reg_claim "$id"
  _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
  # rc 6 too — argued at `cmd_ws_add`'s copy of this `if`.
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$rc" -eq 4 || "$rc" -eq 5 || "$rc" -eq 6 ]]; then
    echo "ccd: start failed for $id (spawn rc $rc) — see $REG/$id.spawn" >&2
    return "$rc"
  fi
  echo "started $id ($mode) — discover on claude.ai by name: $id"
}

cmd_ensure() {   # id — idempotent; used by systemd. arg is the id directly.
  # ONE POSITIONAL, AND THAT IS A SECURITY BOUNDARY. `ccd ensure` is granted by
  # the agent's exec whitelist (agent/src/whitelist.ts), which matches a PREFIX:
  # "tokens after the prefix are unconstrained". A second positional here was
  # threaded down to `(( … >= bound ))` in `_accept_first_run_prompts`, where
  # bash evaluates a variable's CONTENTS as arithmetic and a command
  # substitution inside an array subscript EXECUTES — i.e. any extra argv word
  # this verb accepts is a candidate for arbitrary code as the fleet user. The
  # settle bound travels in `CCD_SETTLE_BOUND` instead (cmd_supervise sets it as
  # a `local`, exactly like CCD_IN_UNIT below). Do not give this verb another
  # argument without asking what a hostile string does once it lands.
  local id="${1:?usage: ccd ensure <id>}"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "no registry for '$id' (run ccd start first)"
  if _alive "$id"; then
    # A LIVE PANE IS NOT NECESSARILY A SUPERVISED ONE, and this early return used
    # to treat the two as the same thing. `_resupervise_live` is the one place
    # that discriminates; on a `running` row it does nothing at all, so `ensure`
    # stays the cheap no-op it has always been for the ordinary case.
    _resupervise_live "$id"
    echo "alive: $id"; return 0
  fi
  # A deliberate revival supersedes a refused swap's banner (§2.4, mirroring
  # cmd_start's matching line): `POST /api/sessions/:id/ensure` is the PWA's
  # actual revive route, so a refusal banner left standing on the row `ensure`
  # just revived would teach the operator to ignore banners. Placed AFTER the
  # already-alive no-op return above, so it fires only on an actual (re)start
  # attempt, exactly as cmd_start's own clear does.
  #
  # TWO GUARDS, one per process that reaches this line, because a refusal's
  # marker was measured surviving neither.
  #   - CCD_IN_UNIT: `cmd_supervise` calls this function on entry, and on the
  #     restart `_swap_refuse` itself performs the pane is still dead when it
  #     does — so the supervisor coming back erased the very field the refusal
  #     had just written, in a DIFFERENT process, seconds later. A supervisor
  #     re-entering its own unit is not a human act, and §2.4's "a deliberate
  #     revival supersedes" is about a human act. (`.stopped` below is NOT
  #     guarded and must not be: it says "this session is not running because
  #     somebody stopped it", which the session being alive again falsifies
  #     whoever caused it. `swapblocked` says "this conversation could not be
  #     moved", which a restart onto the SAME account leaves exactly as true.)
  #   - CCD_KEEP_SWAPBLOCK: `_swap_refuse`'s `|| cmd_ensure` fallback reaches
  #     this in-process, where dynamic scoping makes the caller's `local`
  #     visible — the same idiom, and the same argument, as CCD_IN_UNIT itself.
  if [[ "${CCD_IN_UNIT:-}" != 1 && "${CCD_KEEP_SWAPBLOCK:-}" != 1 ]]; then
    rm -f "$REG/$id.swapblocked"
    # THE STRAND SHARES `swapblocked`'s ARGUMENT VERBATIM, both guards
    # included. `_auto_swap_check` writes `.stranded` and, seconds later in a
    # DIFFERENT process, `cmd_supervise` re-enters this function — clearing
    # there would erase the marker and the next tick would re-mark and
    # re-banner. Outside this block it would also be erased by `_swap_refuse`'s
    # in-process `|| cmd_ensure` fallback.
    _strand_clear "$id"
  fi
  # And the mirror of cmd_start's stopped-clear (see its comment for the full
  # argument): `_spawn`'s success-only clear cannot reach the supervised path
  # when the unit itself never starts, since `_spawn` then runs in a different
  # process altogether. The verb clears it here instead, on attempt,
  # unconditionally — `ensure` is `POST /api/sessions/:id/ensure`'s actual
  # revive route, so this is the case the spec sentence was written for.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.stopped"
  # The Restart=always hot path, which is exactly why `_spawn_settle`'s
  # change-only rule exists one function up. This line is cheap — no fork beyond
  # the emitter's own — and it is what tells a respawn from a human's
  # `ccd ensure`, through `obs.cg`: the systemd unit names the id in the cgroup
  # path, which is respawn provenance nothing on this box has today.
  #
  # NOT CHANGE-GATED, AND THAT IS A KNOWN, ACCEPTED DEPENDENCY ON A LIMIT THIS
  # FILE DOES NOT SET (FIX ROUND 1 (d)). Unlike `_spawn_settle` one function up,
  # this emit fires on EVERY invocation, in-unit or not — there is no rc/300s
  # gate here. The volume that leaves unbounded is therefore bounded
  # EXTERNALLY, by systemd's own `StartLimitIntervalSec`/`StartLimitBurst` on
  # `claude-session@.service` (a crash-looped unit stops retrying, so
  # `cmd_ensure` stops being re-entered), never by anything journal-side. A
  # future reader retuning those systemd limits is also retuning this
  # journal's write rate, and nothing here will warn them of that.
  # ── D-2605 TRANSITION: THE ROW'S GENERATION, MINTED HERE IF IT IS MISSING ─
  # `_reg_generation_init` mints ONLY on genuine absence and is idempotent, so
  # on an ordinary row this costs one acquisition and a read. It exists for the
  # population row creation cannot reach: a row that predates the generation
  # mechanism carries no `.generation` at all, and BOTH minting sites —
  # `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start` — are row CREATION. The systemd unit reaches
  # neither. It runs `ccd supervise`, which calls THIS function, so without
  # this block a pre-mechanism row can never gain a generation: its compaction
  # lifecycle is inert for the life of the row, and `_spawn_start`'s own
  # warning ("inert until its next respawn") promises a repair that no respawn
  # performs. Measured on this fleet 2026-09-17, hours after the card shipped:
  # 31 of 34 live rows had none, and no path could have given them one.
  #
  # BEST EFFORT, NEVER FATAL — the one way it differs from `cmd_start`'s
  # otherwise identical block. That verb DIES on a contended lock because it
  # has written nothing yet and a half-built row is the worse outcome; this is
  # the SUPERVISOR's path, where a die leaves the session down and the unit
  # retrying into its own start limit. The disposition is `_spawn_start`'s
  # instead: fail OPEN, spawn without a generation, and let the standing
  # fail-closed rule answer for the lifecycle. Every rc is non-fatal here —
  # rc 1 contended, rc 2 no `flock` mechanism, and a present-but-INVALID
  # generation, which `_reg_generation_init` refuses to repair by design and
  # which is reported rather than silently carried.
  #
  # IT CANNOT AUTHORIZE A PANE THAT SHOULD NOT PUBLISH, and that is the whole
  # safety argument. `_hook_generation_ok` requires a NON-EMPTY
  # `CCRC_SESSION_GENERATION` in the pane's environment AND byte equality with
  # the row's file. A pane spawned before this mint carries no value at all, so
  # it is refused whatever the file says afterwards; a pane spawned after it
  # carries exactly what was minted. The mint is strictly forward-looking: it
  # can only reach the spawn this call is about to make.
  local _ce_gfd="" _ce_grc=0
  _compact_lock_acquire "$id" "$COMPACT_LOCK_WAIT"; _ce_grc=$?
  if (( _ce_grc == 0 )); then
    _ce_gfd="$COMPACT_LOCK_FD"
    _reg_generation_init "$id" \
      || echo "ccd: warn: $id: $REG/$id.generation is present and invalid — spawning without it; inspect it by hand" >&2
    _compact_lock_release "$_ce_gfd"; _ce_gfd=""
  fi
  _lc_done ensure "$id" "" meas.inUnit "${CCD_IN_UNIT:-0}"
  # Inside the unit (cmd_supervise sets CCD_IN_UNIT in its own process) this IS the spawn; anywhere
  # else it is a REQUEST for one, and the request goes through the unit so the session comes back
  # supervised. `cmd_swap`'s tail (ccd:12599) reaches this from inside a transient systemd-run unit
  # and correctly takes the supervised branch — which is why the guard is not INVOCATION_ID.
  if [[ "${CCD_IN_UNIT:-}" != 1 ]]; then
    local rc; _supervised_start "$id"; rc=$?
    # `started` stays unconditional (Task 4's invariant, ccd:11908) on this path too — see cmd_start's
    # matching comment for why the in-unit cmd_ensure setting it is not enough on its own.
    _reg_claim "$id"
    [[ "$rc" -eq 0 ]] || return "$rc"
    echo "ensured $id"
    return 0
  fi
  # WRONG-MODE RESURRECTION IS FIXED BY THE MOVE, not by a new check: `ensure`
  # picks mode=new when `started` is empty, handing `--session-id '<uuid>'` to a
  # wrapper for a uuid whose session-env directory already exists (measured on
  # the live orphan). With `started` written at session-creation time — which is
  # what the split below finally makes true on every path — it picks `resume`.
  local mode=new; [[ "$(_reg_get "$id" started)" == "1" ]] && mode=resume
  # SPLIT, and NO `_ws_supervise` on this branch: see cmd_start's matching
  # comment. The settle takes two positionals and no more — the bound rides
  # `CCD_SETTLE_BOUND`, because a third one here would be `cmd_ensure`'s second
  # argv word and this verb is whitelisted by prefix.
  local rc
  _spawn_start "$id" "$mode" || return $?
  _reg_claim "$id"
  _spawn_settle "$id" "$SPAWN_FROMSWAP"; rc=$?
  # rc 6 too — argued at `cmd_ws_add`'s copy of this `if`.
  if [[ "$rc" -eq 3 || "$rc" -eq 4 || "$rc" -eq 5 || "$rc" -eq 6 ]]; then
    echo "ccd: ensure failed for $id (spawn rc $rc) — see $REG/$id.spawn" >&2
    return "$rc"
  fi
  echo "ensured $id ($mode)"
}

cmd_supervise() {   # systemd ExecStart: ensure then block until the session dies
  local id="${1:?usage: ccd supervise <id>}"
  # ── THE START LIMIT, ON A MANAGER THAT HAS NONE ────────────────────────
  # `claude-session@.service` carries StartLimitIntervalSec=120 and
  # StartLimitBurst=5, and that pair is not tuning — it is what makes §4.3's
  # `orphan` classification true. A pane that dies instantly must become a
  # FAILED unit, because a failed unit heartbeats nothing and the row then
  # reads "nothing is bringing this back". launchd has `ThrottleInterval`
  # (how long to WAIT before respawning) and no burst limit at all: left
  # alone it would restart a crash-looping session every 3 seconds forever,
  # and the row would heartbeat `supervised` the whole time — a session
  # visibly alive and permanently broken, which is the one state this fleet's
  # display has no word for.
  #
  # SO THE LIMIT IS COUNTED HERE, by the process the manager keeps starting.
  # Each entry appends its timestamp; the window is the last
  # SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST entries, and if the oldest of them is within
  # SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_S the manager is looping. `bootout` removes the job
  # rather than merely exiting, because exiting is what got us here.
  #
  # THE STAMP IS THE `failed` STATE. `_svc_is_active` reads it, and
  # `_svc_reset_failed` removes it — so `ccd start <id>`, whose whole promise
  # is "this is what revives it", clears the refusal exactly as
  # `systemctl reset-failed` does on the other platform.
  if [ "$CCD_OS" = darwin ]; then
    local _sl_file="$REG/.$id.starts" _sl_now _sl_oldest _sl_n
    _sl_now=$(date +%s)
    printf '%s\n' "$_sl_now" >> "$_sl_file" 2>/dev/null
    # Keep only the window's worth of entries, so the file cannot grow without
    # bound on a session that restarts legitimately for months.
    _sl_n=$(wc -l < "$_sl_file" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' '); [[ "$_sl_n" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || _sl_n=0
    if [ "$_sl_n" -gt "$SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST" ]; then
      tail -n "$SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST" "$_sl_file" > "$_sl_file.tmp" 2>/dev/null \
        && _plat_mv_notdir "$_sl_file.tmp" "$_sl_file"
      _sl_n="$SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST"
    fi
    if [ "$_sl_n" -ge "$SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST" ]; then
      _sl_oldest=$(head -1 "$_sl_file" 2>/dev/null); [[ "$_sl_oldest" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || _sl_oldest=0
      if [ "$_sl_oldest" -gt 0 ] && [ $(( _sl_now - _sl_oldest )) -le "$SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_S" ]; then
        printf '%s' "$_sl_now" > "$REG/$id.svcfailed" 2>/dev/null
        rm -f -- "$_sl_file" 2>/dev/null
        echo "ccd: $id: start limit reached ($SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_BURST starts in ${SUPERVISE_START_LIMIT_S}s) — giving up, this session is FAILED. Revive it with: ccd start $id" >&2
        _svc_launchctl bootout "$(_svc_domain)/$(_svc_label "claude-session@$id")" 2>/dev/null
        return 1
      fi
    fi
  fi
  # THE FIRST STAMP GOES BEFORE cmd_ensure, NOT AFTER IT. `cmd_ensure` can sit
  # inside `_accept_first_run_prompts` for up to fifteen minutes while a 700k-
  # token resume works through its gates, and a heartbeat that started with the
  # watch loop would leave every large resume reading `unsupervised` for those
  # fifteen minutes — the loudest possible false alarm, fired precisely when
  # the fleet is doing the most work (§4.2).
  #
  # The stamp is how the SERVER learns this at all: the agent's read whitelist
  # permits ~/.cc-sessions and ~/.claude*, and nothing under ~/.config/systemd,
  # so rather than widen a security boundary to learn a fact, the supervisor
  # publishes it. And it is strictly better than an enable symlink: the symlink
  # promises a start at next boot, this proves auto-swap, uuid-sync and
  # auto-compact are running for this row right now.
  _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"
  # §3.2's self-heal: creating the enable symlink is idempotent and safe from inside the unit
  # (`enable` without `--now` asks systemd for nothing else), and this one line heals M5's three
  # boot-persistence-less rows the next time each supervisor restarts — which the deploy's
  # `try-restart "claude-session@*"` sweep makes fleet-wide and automatic.
  _svc_enable "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null \
    || echo "ccd: warn: could not enable boot-persistence for $id" >&2
  # The recursion guard, in-process. `local` is dynamically scoped in bash, so cmd_ensure sees it
  # and the caller does not; it is never exported and never an argv token, so — unlike an added
  # argv word, which the agent's prefix-matching exec whitelist would permit — it is not
  # addressable from the wire at all.
  local CCD_IN_UNIT=1
  # THE NO-CEILING CALLER, and the only one that raises the settle bound: this
  # runs as the unit's ExecStart with nothing timing it out, and a 700k-token
  # resume legitimately takes minutes between gates. A global SPAWN_SETTLE_S
  # here would make every systemd restart of a large session settle
  # unconfirmed — suppressing _inject_spawn_effort and lighting a warning on a
  # healthy row.
  #
  # SIGNALLED THE SAME WAY CCD_IN_UNIT IS, one line up, and for the same reason
  # that comment gives: dynamically scoped, so `_accept_first_run_prompts` sees
  # it several frames down without any function in between naming it; never
  # exported and never an argv token, so — unlike an added argv word, which the
  # agent's prefix-matching exec whitelist would permit — it is not addressable
  # from the wire. It reached `cmd_ensure` as a POSITIONAL for one commit, and
  # the value lands in `(( ))`, which executes a command substitution written
  # into an array subscript. That is why this is a variable.
  local CCD_SETTLE_BOUND=$SPAWN_SETTLE_SUPERVISE_S
  # PRE-FLIGHT PROBE, before ensure (branch review, confirmed major): a supervisor
  # (re)started MID-FAULT must not walk into cmd_ensure — under the hang-shaped wedge its
  # `_alive` reads unknown->false after the deadline and it falls into `_spawn_start` ->
  # `_tmux_new_session`, whose `tmux list-sessions`/`new-session` carry NO deadline and
  # block forever on the wedged socket: a hang wearing an active unit, stuck BEFORE the
  # loop below could mark or back off. On `unknown` the loop is entered directly — it
  # marks, stamps, and probes its way to live (pane survived the fault) or gone (exit 1;
  # the NEXT supervise run ensures normally against a healed substrate). `live`/`gone`
  # take ensure exactly as before: alive short-circuits it, gone is the cold start.
  #
  # AND THE COLD-START ESCAPE (D-514, incident 2026-08-26). `unknown` alone held the whole fleet
  # down for 4.5 hours with all 18 units reporting `active`: after an OOM killed the tmux
  # server, every probe answered `no server running`, which is `unknown`, so ensure was
  # skipped and the loop below — whose `unknown` arm has no exit — spun on backoff
  # forever. Recovery then required a human running a BARE `tmux new-session`, which
  # bypasses `_tmux_new_session` and so births the server in the caller's cgroup instead
  # of ccrc-tmux-server.scope, leaving every pane uncapped: the aggregate ceiling covered
  # nothing, and the next OOM killed the supervision layer rather than the offenders.
  # Trap -> manual bootstrap -> uncapped panes -> OOM -> trap.
  #
  # `_tmux_new_session`'s own comment assumed this could not happen — "it self-heals
  # whenever the server is next created" holds only while ccd is the creator, and the
  # trap is exactly what made a human the creator.
  #
  # An ABSENT server is safe to ensure into and a SILENT one is not, which is the whole
  # reason `PROBE_SUBSTRATE` exists: absence is fast and definitive, and there is no
  # socket for `_tmux_new_session`'s deadline-less `new-session` to block on — it creates
  # one, in the right scope. The wedge stays excluded, so the hang this pre-flight was
  # written for is still refused. Nothing else in the tree reads PROBE_SUBSTRATE, so
  # `ws-reap`/`ws-archive`/`cmd_forget` are untouched: for them `no server running` is
  # still `unknown`, still refuses.
  _session_probe "$id"
  # D-515 — `${PROBE_SUBSTRATE:-}`, NOT `$PROBE_SUBSTRATE`: `_session_probe` is STUBBED WHOLESALE
  # by several suites (ccd-substrate.test.ts's `seq` double drives this loop through a
  # scripted verdict sequence), and those stubs set PROBE_VERDICT/PROBE_DETAIL only. Under
  # `set -u` a bare reference aborts the supervisor; unset must simply mean "no claim
  # about the substrate", which falls back to the pre-2026-08-26 behaviour. A pre-flight
  # that dies on an older double is a worse failure than the trap it is fixing.
  if [[ "$PROBE_VERDICT" != unknown || "${PROBE_SUBSTRATE:-}" == absent ]]; then
    cmd_ensure "$id"
  fi
  # Re-stamped every 30s from the 5-second tick this loop already runs, so the
  # 120-second freshness window never expires under a live supervisor.
  #
  # VERDICT-DRIVEN, not boolean (D-310): the old `while _alive` collapsed
  # `gone` and `unknown` into one exit, so a wedged or version-skewed tmux
  # read as seventeen simultaneous deaths — every supervisor exited, systemd
  # restarted each one into `cmd_ensure`, and the fleet churned against a
  # substrate that had refused to answer, not pronounced anything dead.
  #
  # THE BEAT COUNTS REAL SECONDS — the sleep it is ABOUT to take plus the work
  # this tick actually spent. The tick helpers BLOCK for far longer than the
  # tick: `_route_apply_check` types a session-only `/effort` or `/model` one
  # key at a time with a `sleep 1` between keystrokes (the pacing the TUI was
  # measured to need) and then polls `ROUTE_ACK_WAIT` seconds for the
  # acknowledgement — ~20s for a slider, ~30s when class and effort are both
  # pending — and `_auto_compact_check` sends `/compact` the same way. Against
  # the old `beat=$((beat + tick))`, five ASSUMED seconds a tick, six such
  # ticks are 120-150 REAL seconds between stamps: at or past the 120-second
  # freshness window `_session_state` classifies by, so a perfectly healthy
  # session reads `unsupervised` on the fleet board because its supervisor was
  # busy moving a slider. It is the `unknown)` arm's failure (below) reached by
  # a second route, and the remedy is the same one stated honestly: measure the
  # seconds instead of assuming them. The worst gap is then 30s plus one tick's
  # own duration.
  local beat=0 unknown_run=0 tick=5 t0 spent
  while :; do
    t0=$(date +%s)
    _session_probe "$id"
    case "$PROBE_VERDICT" in
      gone) break ;;   # the ONLY exit — a session tmux itself pronounced dead (D-310)
      live)
        [[ -e "$REG/$id.substrate" ]] && _substrate_clear "$id"
        unknown_run=0; tick=5
        _sync_uuid "$id"; _auto_stale_check "$id"; _auto_swap_check "$id"; _auto_compact_check "$id"; _route_apply_check "$id"
        spent=$(date +%s); spent=$((spent - t0)); (( spent < 0 )) && spent=0   # a backwards clock is not negative work
        beat=$((beat + tick + spent))
        (( beat >= 30 )) && { _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"; beat=0; }
        ;;
      unknown)
        # Not death — refusal to guess (spec §1). Mark, keep the heartbeat honest EVERY
        # tick — this arm accrues no beat at all, because a 30s backoff sleep counted
        # through a 30-second beat as a 5-second tick stamps every 180 real seconds and
        # ages all 17 rows into orphan mid-fault (the false dead by a slower route) —
        # skip the tick helpers (each shells into the tmux that just did not answer),
        # and back off: 17 supervisors hammering a wedged server every 5s is a
        # thundering herd against a component already unwell.
        unknown_run=$((unknown_run + 1))
        _substrate_mark "$id"
        _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"; beat=0
        tick=5; (( unknown_run >= SUBSTRATE_BACKOFF_AFTER )) && tick=$SUBSTRATE_BACKOFF_S
        ;;
    esac
    sleep "$tick"
  done
  echo "session $id ended; exiting for systemd restart" >&2
  exit 1
}

_sanitize_anthropic() {   # transcript-path — fill EMPTY assistant text blocks (a gpt/Codex-lane
  # artifact) with a placeholder so the Anthropic API accepts the resumed history.
  local f="$1"; [[ -f "$f" ]] || return 0
  python3 - "$f" <<'PY' 2>/dev/null || echo "ccd: warn: transcript sanitize skipped (python3?)" >&2
import json,sys,os
f=sys.argv[1]; out=[]; n=0
for ln in open(f):
    s=ln.rstrip("\n")
    try: o=json.loads(s)
    except: out.append(s); continue
    c=o.get("message",{}).get("content")
    if isinstance(c,list):
        ch=False
        for b in c:
            if isinstance(b,dict) and b.get("type")=="text" and b.get("text","").strip()=="":
                b["text"]="..."; ch=True; n+=1
        if ch: s=json.dumps(o,ensure_ascii=False)
    out.append(s)
if n:
    open(f+".tmp","w").write("\n".join(out)+"\n"); os.replace(f+".tmp",f)
    sys.stderr.write(f"ccd: sanitized {n} empty text blocks for Anthropic\n")
PY
}

_transcript_matches() {   # cfgdir uuid -> every <cfgdir>/projects/*/<uuid>.jsonl, one per line.
  # A session uuid is globally UNIQUE, so the transcript can be found instead of
  # guessed. cmd_swap used to munge one directory and copy exactly that path;
  # Claude Code writes under the munge of ITS cwd, which diverges the moment the
  # session moves (a /cd relocates the transcript, a worktree tool does not), and
  # on 2026-08-11 the guess missed and the swap completed with no history.
  # Deliberately scoped to ONE config dir: the same uuid exists under five
  # accounts at five different sizes, and a cross-account sweep would carry
  # whichever stale copy sorted first.
  # The glob runs in a subshell so `nullglob` — which is what makes "no matches"
  # an empty list rather than a literal `*`-bearing path that `[[ -f ]]` quietly
  # answers no to — cannot leak into the caller's shell.
  # rc is ALWAYS 0, found or not: "no matches" is empty output, not a nonzero
  # rc. A caller deciding whether to refuse (Task 3) must count output lines —
  # `_transcript_matches … || die` never fires.
  local cfg="$1" uuid="$2" f
  [[ -n "$cfg" && -n "$uuid" ]] || return 0
  ( shopt -s nullglob
    for f in "$cfg"/projects/*/"$uuid".jsonl; do
      [[ -f "$f" ]] || continue
      printf '%s\n' "$f"
    done )
}

_sidecar_matches() {   # cfgdir uuid -> every <cfgdir>/projects/*/<uuid>/ sidecar dir, no trailing slash.
  # Globbed in its OWN right, not "beside each jsonl": two of the incident's
  # sidecars sit in project dirs that hold no .jsonl for that uuid at all. The
  # trailing slash in the glob is what restricts it to directories. The
  # stripped slash on the way OUT gives every caller the same bare `<uuid>`
  # path — it is NOT what stops `cp -a src/ dst` nesting: measured, `cp -al
  # src/<uuid> dst/<uuid>` onto an ALREADY-EXISTING destination nests
  # regardless of either side's trailing slash. The real guard is "the
  # destination must not already exist before the copy runs", and that is the
  # copying caller's job (Task 2), not this locator's.
  local cfg="$1" uuid="$2" d
  [[ -n "$cfg" && -n "$uuid" ]] || return 0
  ( shopt -s nullglob
    for d in "$cfg"/projects/*/"$uuid"/; do
      [[ -d "$d" ]] || continue
      printf '%s\n' "${d%/}"
    done )
}

_swap_carry_jsonl() {   # srccfg dstcfg uuid mdir sanitize(0|1)
  # rc 0 = carried, including the `mdir` slot; rc 1 = nothing found, or every
  # copy failed; rc 2 = copies landed but NOT at `mdir`, the one slot the
  # resumed session actually reads (see the verdict at the foot of this
  # function). Every non-zero rc means the same thing to the caller — do not
  # proceed with this swap — and they differ only in the sentence the refusal
  # puts in front of a human.
  # Destination slots = each match's MIRRORED relative dir (so the target account
  # looks like where Claude Code actually had the file) plus `mdir`, the munge of
  # the resolved registry workdir — the directory the resumed process starts in,
  # and the first address it reads. Where two matches want one slot the newest
  # source mtime wins, which is the rule that makes the mdir slot safe: a session
  # that moved has a STALE transcript still sitting at its old startup munge,
  # which for many rows IS mdir, and copying both while preserving directories
  # would land the stale one at exactly the address that matters.
  #
  # One copy per distinct INODE (one inode wore three names in production, at
  # 70MB); every other slot for that inode is a hardlink to the copy. Every
  # destination write unlinks first (`cp -p --remove-destination`, `ln -f`): a
  # previous swap onto this account left its names hardlinked to EACH OTHER, so a
  # plain cp writes through the shared inode and rewrites siblings this swap
  # never claimed, and a plain ln fails EEXIST — which, with no `set -e`, this
  # script would sail straight past.
  #
  # A cp across config dirs, never a link from source to destination: the
  # transcript is the one file that gets APPENDED to, so an aliased source would
  # grow a conversation that account never had. `-p` because two of this design's
  # rules read mtimes and a plain cp stamps every copy with the time of the swap.
  local srccfg="$1" dstcfg="$2" uuid="$3" mdir="$4" sanitize="${5:-0}"
  local -a matches=()
  # C-collated: the glob itself carries no order guarantee, and ccd runs from
  # an interactive shell, from `systemd-run --user`, and from the auto-swap
  # dispatcher — three environments, three possible locales. Left unpinned,
  # which match wins an equal-mtime tie (below) would depend on who triggered
  # the swap. Pinning here is what makes that tie-break reproducible; the
  # `LC_ALL=C sort` on the slot list further down is the same reasoning
  # applied one step later.
  mapfile -t matches < <(_transcript_matches "$srccfg" "$uuid" | LC_ALL=C sort)
  (( ${#matches[@]} )) || return 1

  local -A mtime_of=() ino_of=() winner=()
  local f pdir mt s cur
  for f in "${matches[@]}"; do
    # Parameter expansion, not `basename "$(dirname …)"`: every munged project
    # dir starts with `-`, and GNU basename would read `-data-projects-x` as
    # options.
    pdir="${f%/*}"; pdir="${pdir##*/}"
    mt=$(_plat_mtime "$f" 2>/dev/null); [[ "$mt" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || mt=0
    mtime_of["$f"]="$mt"
    # device:inode, not inode alone — an inode number is unique only WITHIN a
    # filesystem, and two distinct transcripts on different devices could
    # otherwise collide and get treated as the same content.
    ino_of["$f"]=$(_plat_devino "$f" 2>/dev/null)
    [[ -n "${ino_of[$f]}" ]] || ino_of["$f"]="path:$f"
    # Its own mirrored dir, and — for every match — the mdir slot, UNLESS mdir
    # is empty: `_reg_get`'s `cat … 2>/dev/null` yields "" for a missing
    # `.workdir` file, and `winner[""]=...` on an empty subscript is a FATAL
    # bash error under `set -u` that kills the interpreter mid-loop, not a
    # recoverable one — no rc, nothing after it runs. `${mdir:+"$mdir"}` drops
    # the slot instead of ever writing to it. Strictly greater, so an
    # equal-mtime tie keeps the first match in (C-sorted, see above) order,
    # and the answer stays deterministic.
    for s in "$pdir" ${mdir:+"$mdir"}; do
      cur="${winner[$s]:-}"
      if [[ -z "$cur" ]] || (( mt > ${mtime_of[$cur]} )); then winner["$s"]="$f"; fi
    done
  done

  # Sorted, and C-collated, so which slot holds the physical copy is reproducible
  # rather than a function of bash's hash order.
  local -a slots=()
  mapfile -t slots < <(printf '%s\n' "${!winner[@]}" | LC_ALL=C sort)

  local -A copied=() placed=()
  local carried=0 slot src ino dst
  for slot in "${slots[@]}"; do
    src="${winner[$slot]}"; ino="${ino_of[$src]}"
    dst="$dstcfg/projects/$slot/$uuid.jsonl"
    mkdir -p "$dstcfg/projects/$slot"
    if [[ -n "${copied[$ino]:-}" ]]; then
      ln -f "${copied[$ino]}" "$dst" 2>/dev/null \
        || _plat_cp_replace "${copied[$ino]}" "$dst" 2>/dev/null \
        || { echo "ccd: warn: could not place transcript at $dst" >&2; continue; }
    else
      _plat_cp_replace "$src" "$dst" 2>/dev/null \
        || { echo "ccd: warn: could not carry transcript $src -> $dst" >&2; continue; }
      # Order is load-bearing: _sanitize_anthropic rewrites through os.replace,
      # which BREAKS hardlinks — the replaced name gets a new inode and every
      # sibling keeps the unsanitized one. Copy -> sanitize -> link.
      [[ "$sanitize" == 1 ]] && _sanitize_anthropic "$dst"
      copied["$ino"]="$dst"; carried=$((carried + 1))
    fi
    # Recorded on BOTH arms, because a hardlink into a slot is a landing as
    # surely as a copy is — `carried` counts distinct INODES copied and can
    # therefore be 1 while N slots are correctly filled. This records SLOTS.
    placed["$slot"]=1
  done
  (( carried > 0 )) || return 1
  # Logged before the mdir verdict below, not after: a partial carry is exactly
  # the case whose forensic trail matters, and these three numbers are what say
  # which part landed. (This line has always recorded fewer copies than
  # destinations for the failure the verdict now catches — the arithmetic that
  # exposes it was written all along and simply never branched on.)
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') carry $uuid: ${#matches[@]} match(es), $carried copy/copies, ${#slots[@]} destination(s)" \
    >> "$REG/swap.log"
  # THE mdir SLOT IS NOT ONE DESTINATION AMONG SEVERAL. It is the munge of the
  # resolved registry workdir — the directory the resumed process starts in and
  # the first address it reads — so "at least one copy landed" is the wrong bar
  # for it: measured, two distinct source inodes with an unwritable mdir
  # destination gave a warning on stderr, carried=1, rc 0, and `cmd_swap` went
  # on to flip `wrapper` and print `swapped …`. The resumed session then munges
  # its cwd to mdir, finds nothing, and reports no history — D1's exact symptom,
  # behind a success report. rc 2 rather than 1 so the caller can name the real
  # cause: "every copy failed" and "the copy that mattered failed" want different
  # sentences in the banner an operator reads at 2am.
  [[ -z "$mdir" || -n "${placed[$mdir]:-}" ]] || return 2
  return 0
}

_swap_carry_sidecars() {   # srccfg dstcfg uuid — carry every sidecar as a hardlink tree. Always rc 0.
  # <projects dir>/<uuid>/ holds subagents, tool-results and workflows, and was
  # copied by NOTHING before this build: 188MB of the incident session's state,
  # left behind on the account it was swapped off. Globbed in its own right
  # because sidecars exist in project dirs with no .jsonl for the same uuid, and
  # each lands at the mirror of its OWN source dir.
  #
  # `cp -al` — a hardlink tree, the one place a source inode is shared across
  # accounts, and the asymmetry with §2.2 is deliberate: the transcript is
  # appended to, a sidecar's contents are write-once artifacts read after they
  # are written. `cp -a` without `-l` is the fallback the moment linking fails
  # (a cross-device destination), so the degradation is a slow correct copy
  # rather than nothing — and it is the whole remedy if the write-once property
  # is ever disproved: delete one flag, pay the bytes, change nothing else. The
  # mode is logged so the evidence exists if a future defect implicates a shared
  # checkpoint.
  #
  # An existing destination sidecar is LEFT ALONE rather than merged — the
  # opposite of the transcript's unlink-first rule, because a tree can be
  # half-merged where a single file is replaced in one step. `_sidecar_matches`
  # strips the source's trailing slash; the anti-nesting guard is that `$dst`
  # (the `<uuid>` directory itself, not just its parent) must not already
  # exist before a copy attempt runs — measured: `cp -al src/<uuid> dst/<uuid>`
  # (and `cp -a`, identically) onto an ALREADY-EXISTING destination nests the
  # tree inside itself regardless of either path's trailing slash. `mkdir -p`
  # below only creates the PARENT (`$dstcfg/projects/$pdir`), never `$dst`
  # itself, so the FIRST attempt (`cp -al`) always starts clean.
  #
  # The SECOND attempt does not start clean on its own, and this is the
  # subtle half of the guard: `cp -al` builds the destination directory
  # skeleton before it fails on the first unlinkable file — measured on this
  # box, a cross-device `$dst` gets every subdirectory created and every file
  # missing, `cp` exits nonzero, and `$dst` now EXISTS where a moment ago it
  # did not. Falling back to `cp -a "$src" "$dst"` against that survivor nests
  # the whole tree one level down (`$dst/$uuid/…`) exactly as the top-of-loop
  # case does, leaving the real path full of empty directories — and because
  # `$dst` now exists, EVERY later swap onto this account hits the top-of-loop
  # guard and logs `(kept)`, permanently, never repairing it. The fallback
  # therefore clears any partial `$dst` immediately before it copies.
  #
  # Success is read from `cp`'s own exit status, not `[[ -d "$dst" ]]`: a
  # same-device partial failure (one unreadable member, ENOSPC, EMLINK) can
  # leave `$dst` populated as a directory before `cp -al` reports failure, so
  # a directory-existence check would read a half-linked tree as a clean
  # success — and log `(link)` for a tree that is only partly shared. Clearing
  # `$dst` before the fallback and always doing a full `cp -a` on ANY first-
  # attempt failure means the logged mode never overstates what is shared:
  # `(link)` only when the whole tree is hardlinks to `$src`, `(copy)`
  # whenever any part of it needed a real copy.
  #
  # C-collated sort: no destination is ever contested (each sidecar's `pdir`
  # comes from a distinct source project directory), so ordering never changes
  # WHAT is carried — only the order of the swap.log lines a human reads
  # afterward. Sorted anyway so that order is reproducible rather than a
  # function of glob/readdir order, which is locale- and filesystem-dependent.
  local srccfg="$1" dstcfg="$2" uuid="$3" src pdir dst mode rc
  local -a sidecars=()
  mapfile -t sidecars < <(_sidecar_matches "$srccfg" "$uuid" | LC_ALL=C sort)
  (( ${#sidecars[@]} )) || return 0
  for src in "${sidecars[@]}"; do
    # Parameter expansion, not `basename "$(dirname …)"`: every munged project
    # dir starts with `-`, and GNU basename would read it as options.
    pdir="${src%/*}"; pdir="${pdir##*/}"
    dst="$dstcfg/projects/$pdir/$uuid"
    if [[ -e "$dst" ]]; then
      echo "ccd: sidecar already present at $dst — left alone (not merged)" >&2
      echo "$(date '+%F %T') sidecar $uuid -> $dst (kept)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
      continue
    fi
    mkdir -p "$dstcfg/projects/$pdir"
    mode=link
    cp -al "$src" "$dst" 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
    if (( rc != 0 )); then
      mode=copy
      # $dst may already exist here — a partial skeleton the failed `cp -al`
      # left behind — and `cp -a` onto an existing $dst nests instead of
      # landing at the real path. Clear it first every time, unconditionally.
      [[ -e "$dst" ]] && rm -rf "$dst"
      cp -a "$src" "$dst" 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
    fi
    if (( rc == 0 )); then
      echo "$(date '+%F %T') sidecar $uuid -> $dst ($mode)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
    else
      echo "ccd: warn: could not carry sidecar $src -> $dst" >&2
      # Do not leave a half-built $dst behind on total failure either: the
      # top-of-loop existence check would read it as "already carried" and
      # this sidecar would never be retried by a later swap.
      [[ -e "$dst" ]] && rm -rf "$dst" 2>/dev/null
    fi
  done
  return 0
}

_swap_beat() {   # id parent-pid — re-stamp `supervised` every SWAP_BEAT_INTERVAL while the carry runs.
  # §4.2 says "the swap re-stamps AS IT GOES, and the window classifies as
  # `restarting`", and it says so for a measured reason: §2.3's `cp -a` fallback
  # over a 188MB sidecar can exceed the 120s freshness window. What shipped was
  # two stamps, one before the carry and one after it, and nothing between —
  # bracketing, not covering. Measured against the shipped ladder with a 200s
  # carry: `restarting` while the window held, then `orphan` at 125s and again
  # at 180s. So on exactly the carry the spec names, the row flipped to
  # "orphan — nothing is watching it" MID-SWAP, with the PWA's revive
  # affordance beside it — and a revive there
  # runs `ccd ensure` -> `systemctl start` while `cmd_swap` is still copying and
  # the registry `wrapper` still names the SOURCE account.
  #
  # A background stamper rather than a stamp inside the carry helpers, because
  # the long pole is ONE `cp -a` call, not a loop: a per-file stamp would leave
  # the 188MB copy exactly as uncovered as it is today.
  #
  # A LEAKED STAMPER WOULD BE A WORSE LIE THAN THE ONE IT FIXES — a row that
  # heartbeats forever with nobody watching — so it is bounded twice over: it
  # dies with its parent (`kill -0`, checked every tick BEFORE the stamp, so an
  # orphan never claims a supervisor even once; it covers a `cmd_swap` killed
  # mid-carry where the explicit `_swap_beat_stop` never runs) and it gives up
  # after SWAP_BEAT_MAX ticks regardless. Both bounds are the same argument as
  # the `120` in `_session_state`: nothing may claim to be watching for longer
  # than it plausibly is.
  #
  # Both bounds, and the `_swap_beat_stop` on cmd_swap's REFUSAL arm, are pinned
  # by server/test/ccd-swap.test.ts — named here because they were not: each of
  # the three lines was deleted in turn during the final review and the whole
  # 2585-test suite stayed green, which made this paragraph a safety claim
  # nothing defended, the finding this branch has now made four times.
  local id="$1" parent="$2" i=0
  while (( i < SWAP_BEAT_MAX )); do
    sleep "$SWAP_BEAT_INTERVAL"
    kill -0 "$parent" 2>/dev/null || return 0
    _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"
    i=$((i + 1))
  done
}

_swap_beat_stop() {   # pid — end the carry heartbeat. Safe on an empty/dead pid.
  # `wait` after the kill so the shell reaps it rather than announcing
  # "Terminated" into the middle of a swap's own output; both are swallowed
  # because a stamper that already exited on its own bound is the normal case,
  # not an error.
  local pid="${1:-}"
  [[ -n "$pid" ]] || return 0
  kill "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  wait "$pid" 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}

_swap_refuse() {   # id cur target reason restart(0|1) — a swap that cannot carry the
  # conversation is refused, not completed (spec §2.4). A swap exists to MOVE a
  # conversation; completing one with nothing found trades a session's entire history
  # for a rate-limit reprieve, which is exactly the trade that made the 2026-08-11
  # incident. Refusing keeps the session on an account that may be throttled but still
  # holds its file, and puts a human in the loop.
  #
  # THREE channels, because the loudest one is the one nobody hears. The common
  # invocation is from inside the session being swapped, where cmd_swap detaches a
  # transient unit and returns 0 to its caller (see the systemd-run arm below) — the
  # caller is dead, killed by this very swap, long before this function runs. So:
  #   - $REG/<id>.swapblocked = "<epoch> <reason>" — the DURABLE one. It lands on the fleet
  #     wire within one 2s watcher tick and renders on the row (spec M9): `readSession`
  #     reads this field and `SessionRecord.swapBlocked` carries it (server/src/registry.ts).
  #     Read back on this side too, by `_auto_swap_check`'s own back-off gate below.
  #     Survives nobody watching, which /api/notify does not. (An earlier draft of this
  #     comment said nothing under `server/` or `shared/` read the field — true when the
  #     ccd half shipped first, false from the moment the server half landed on this same
  #     branch, and flagged by two reviewers.)
  #   - $REG/notify.sh — the banner for whoever IS watching; the same hook the successful
  #     swap already fires, so it rides a pipeline that is deployed and working.
  #   - $REG/swap.log — the forensic trail beside every other swap decision.
  #
  # This refusal is deliberately NOT written in any of the four literal emission shapes
  # server/test/wsaudit.test.ts harvests out of this file (its header lists them). Those
  # belong to the workspace audit/reap protocol, which answers a MACHINE over a closed
  # vocabulary that must have a matching sentence in wsaudit.ts, in both directions. A
  # swap refusal answers a human. Pinned by server/test/ccd-swap-refuse.test.ts so the
  # choice survives whoever edits this next.
  local id="$1" cur="$2" target="$3" reason="$4" restart="${5:-0}"
  _reg_set "$id" swapblocked "$(date +%s) $reason"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') swap-refused $id: $cur -> $target ($reason)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  [[ -x "$REG/notify.sh" ]] && "$REG/notify.sh" "cc swap BLOCKED: $id stays on $cur — $reason" >/dev/null 2>&1
  # `lastswap` is CLEARED here, not merely left unwritten. On the AUTO-swap path
  # (_auto_swap_check, both the hard-blocked rescue arm and the return-home arm) it is
  # already stamped BEFORE `_dispatch_swap` ever runs — seconds before this function is
  # reached — so "never write it" would not be enough on that path; a refusal has to
  # retract a stamp that already landed. A refusal means no landing happened, so a
  # `lastswap` claiming one is false for every one of its readers, not only `_spawn`
  # (which would answer the big-transcript resume gate with "resume from summary" — an
  # auto-compaction of exactly the history this refusal exists to protect): the same
  # false stamp makes `_auto_compact_check` skip a compaction on the grounds that "a
  # swap landing already compacted from summary" (untrue after a refusal), and it would
  # also re-arm `_auto_swap_check`'s own 900s SWAP_COOLDOWN gate — moot regardless,
  # since the 1800s swapblocked gate this task added is strictly longer, but a stale
  # `lastswap` is still a lie sitting in the registry. Plain removal, not back-dating:
  # there is no old value worth preserving once a swap has been refused, and every
  # window that reads `lastswap` is minutes wide, so deleting a genuinely old one costs
  # nothing either.
  rm -f "$REG/$id.lastswap"
  if [[ "$restart" == 1 ]]; then
    # Registry `wrapper` was never touched, so this puts the session back exactly where
    # it was: same account, same unit, still holding its file. Same `|| cmd_ensure`
    # fallback the successful swap's own tail uses for a box with no unit installed.
    #
    # AND IT MUST NOT ERASE THE MARKER IT JUST WROTE. The pane is already dead —
    # `cmd_swap` killed it two lines before calling this — so `cmd_ensure` sails
    # past its own `_alive` early return straight into the `swapblocked` clear.
    # Measured before this line existed: the field above was gone by the time
    # this function returned, `lastswap` was deliberately cleared, BOTH of
    # `_auto_swap_check`'s gates were therefore open, and the next supervise tick
    # re-dispatched. Run against the real dispatcher rather than a logging stub,
    # ten ticks gave ten refusals, ten banners, ten swap.log lines and ten rounds
    # of `systemctl stop` + `tmux kill-session` on a live session — the exact
    # storm SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN exists to prevent, plus a kill loop. Dynamically
    # scoped, so the in-process `cmd_ensure` below sees it and nothing else does;
    # the unit's own `cmd_supervise` -> `cmd_ensure`, a different process
    # entirely, is covered by cmd_ensure's CCD_IN_UNIT guard instead.
    local CCD_KEEP_SWAPBLOCK=1
    # TWO realistic reasons that `start` fails, and the second is this build's own
    # doing: (a) no unit installed — what the `|| cmd_ensure` fallback is for; and
    # (b) the unit is `failed` and rate-limited by §3.3's StartLimitIntervalSec/
    # StartLimitBurst, which is precisely why `_supervised_start` carries a
    # reset-failed. A refusal's restart is a revival too, and needs the same key.
    _svc_reset_failed "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null
    _svc_start "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null || cmd_ensure "$id"
  fi
  echo "ccd: refusing to swap $id: $reason — it stays on $cur. If there is genuinely nothing to carry: ccd swap --force $id $target" >&2
  return 1
}

_strand_clear() {   # id — the strand is over; say so exactly once per strand.
  # THE `-e` TEST IS LOAD-BEARING, and it is not a micro-optimisation.
  # `_auto_swap_check` calls this on EVERY healthy tick of EVERY session.
  # Without this guard the 5-second supervise tick would mean ONE
  # `unstranded` line per session per tick — twelve lines a minute per
  # session, times however many sessions the box is actually running
  # (measure it; it drifts) — and swap.log, the forensic trail beside every
  # swap decision, would stop being readable by the human it exists for.
  [[ -e "$REG/$1.stranded" ]] || return 0
  rm -f -- "$REG/$1.stranded"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') unstranded $1" >> "$REG/swap.log"
}

_strand_why() {   # id project -> one line naming why no candidate could take this session
  # WALKS `_pool_for`, NOT `CCRC_ACCOUNTS`. The candidate set is the thing the
  # decision was actually about: a reason naming an account a hand-set registry
  # `pool` list excluded sends the operator to enable something that would not
  # have been used anyway.
  #
  # AN OVERFLOW LANE IS NOW PART OF THAT SET, AND NAMING IT IS THE POINT.
  # REVERSED at the wave-2b merge (D-1910). This header used to put "a non-home-able
  # lane" alongside the excluded-by-registry case as a reason NOT to name — and
  # since PR #61 (operator decision 2026-09-07) that is exactly backwards: an
  # installed, non-kill-switched overflow lane IS in `_pool_for`, `_swap_target`
  # will rank it in its last-resort bracket, and a census that hid it would omit
  # the one candidate the rotation was still willing to use. `_default_pool`
  # already applied installation and the kill-switch before this walk sees the
  # list, so a lane that reaches here is a real destination and its annotation
  # is a real remedy. This is one claim with #61's own, not two: see its
  # sentence in the placement-parity block, `grep -n '_strand_why. (account
  # pools, wave 2b) mirrors' ccd/ccd` — "mirrors `_swap_target`'s walk, so it
  # names an overflow lane too". If either sentence is ever changed, change both.
  #
  # THIS FUNCTION'S OWN ORDER is pool, then _account_ok, then _avail, so the
  # annotation is the FIRST thing that failed, which is the thing to fix — an
  # account in the wrong pool reads `pool=`, never `limit`, even when both are
  # true. This now MIRRORS `_swap_target`'s own candidate loop (Task 2), which
  # checks `_pool_ok` before `_account_ok` before `_avail` for the identical
  # reason — the two orders were independent choices until Task 2 inserted
  # `_pool_ok` into that loop, and they agree now on purpose, not by mirror
  # maintenance: a future change to either order should re-check the other.
  # THIS AGREEMENT IS ABOUT PREDICATE ORDER; #61's "THE FOUR CANDIDATE WALKS DO
  # NOT AGREE" block is about candidate SET. Neither refutes the other, and they
  # are stated in two places because they are two different questions: which
  # accounts a walk considers, and in which order it rejects them.
  local id="$1" p="$2" pps cand ap cur out="" prc
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$p")
  # Undecidable is NOT a per-candidate fact: nobody decides, so annotating five
  # accounts would invent five reasons for one condition. One token, with the
  # path, because the remedy is a permission or a rewrite and not an account.
  [[ "$pps" == untagged || "$pps" == "named "* ]] \
    || { echo "tag:$pps $POOLS_DIR/$p"; return 0; }
  cur=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  for cand in $(_pool_for "$id"); do
    [[ "$cand" == "$cur" ]] && continue
    _pool_ok "$cand" "$pps"; prc=$?
    if [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]]; then
      # THE SAME RULE THE PROJECT ARM ABOVE OBEYS, now that the ACCOUNT side
      # can answer undecidable too (fix round 1, I2). The paragraph five lines
      # up — "Undecidable is NOT a per-candidate fact … annotating five
      # accounts would invent five reasons for one condition" — was written
      # about the project tag and was being VIOLATED by this loop the moment
      # the account arm gained its own undecidable states: `! [the rule]`
      # takes rc 2 as true, so all five candidates fell into the branch below
      # and each printed a `pool=` token for one shared condition.
      #
      # ONE CONDITION, because every one of the account side's undecidable
      # words is a property of the ONE shared document, never of the
      # candidate: absent or unreadable, past its lease, or off the grammar.
      # So this hoists exactly as the project arm does — one token, with the
      # path, and the remedy is that file or the control-plane link, not an
      # account. (`cmd_ws_add`'s reason builder makes the opposite choice for
      # the opposite contract: its list is one token per account and it says
      # so.) The project tag is READABLE here, which is why this token names
      # the projection instead: the arm above already returned for the other
      # case, so `$pps` cannot be the cause.
      echo "projection:$POOL_OK_STATE $REG/pool-epoch"; return 0
    elif [[ "$prc" -eq 1 ]]; then
      # THE PROJECTED NAME, NOT THE DECLARED ONE (fix round 1, I2). This read
      # `_acct_pool` — spec §5.6's LOWEST-precedence carrier — while the
      # projection is what returned rc 1, so a roster/projection skew named
      # the pool the account is NOT being judged by.
      #
      # `:-none` STILL CANNOT PRINT, and the derivation is new, because the
      # one this replaces cited a body that no longer exists (`[[ -z "$ap" ||
      # "$ap" == "$pp" ]]`). rc 1 is now the fall-through of the account
      # `case`, reachable only on its `"named "*` arm, and `_pool_name_valid`
      # refuses an empty name — so `${POOL_OK_STATE#named }` is non-empty
      # whenever this branch runs. Kept as a belt on that guarantee, not
      # because the fallback is reachable: a future contract change here
      # should degrade to an honest `:pool=none` rather than an empty,
      # easy-to-miss `:pool=` in an operator-facing banner.
      ap=${POOL_OK_STATE#named }; out+=" $cand:pool=${ap:-none}"
    elif ! _account_ok "$cand"; then
      if [[ -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/$cand" ]]; then out+=" $cand:disabled"; else out+=" $cand:missing"; fi
    elif ! _avail "$cand"; then
      out+=" $cand:limit"
    fi
  done
  [[ -n "$out" ]] || out=" no candidate"
  echo "${out# }"
}

_strand_mark() {   # id wrapper project [cause] — a hard-blocked session with nowhere to go.
  # RULING 6: an empty pool STAYS IN POOL and says so. Never cross.
  #
  # TWO FLOORS, and they are floors on different things. The MARKER is the
  # debounce for the log line — presence means "this strand has already been
  # announced" — so the 5-second tick cannot spam swap.log. The BANNER carries
  # its own floor on top, because `_pane_hard_blocked` greps the last eight
  # pane lines and a scrolling limit banner can flip the verdict every tick:
  # each flip is a legitimate mark -> clear -> mark, and the marker must follow
  # the truth, but 720 banners an hour is the storm SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN was
  # introduced for on the refusal channel (see _auto_swap_check's gate).
  # Suppressing the MARKER instead would hide the truth; only the banner waits.
  #
  # `$4` (CAUSE) IS ADDITIVE, and this is Task 5's fix for Important 4:
  # `_strand_why` describes THIS ccd's pool-aware world ("$cand:pool=…"), which
  # is the right question for "no candidate is free" but the WRONG one for
  # "cmd_swap's own guard refused this exact target as an undeclared crossing"
  # (§5.8.4) — `cmd_swap`'s guard already knows precisely what happened and
  # `_strand_why` would have to reverse-engineer it, badly: a wrong-pool
  # candidate not in `_pool_for`'s list degrades to " no candidate", making
  # the stock sentence ("no account in pool $pdesc can take it") flatly false
  # whenever another in-pool account genuinely could. When `$4` is given it
  # REPLACES the computed reason everywhere a reason is shown — the marker,
  # swap.log's line, and the banner — so the deploy-window caller can say what
  # actually happened instead of a misleading pool census. Omitted, this
  # function is byte-identical to before: `why` still computes from
  # `_strand_why`, and the banner still reads its original sentence.
  #
  # WHAT "$pdesc" NAMES, AND WHAT `$why` NAMES, ARE NOT THE SAME SET — D-1917,
  # no text changed. The sentence says "no account in pool $pdesc"; `$why` is
  # the `_pool_for` census, which is wider than the pool, for the reason spec
  # §5.7.3 gives. Pre-existing looseness rather than a regression, and the
  # banner text is asserted VERBATIM by `ccd-auto-swap-pool.test.ts`, so
  # rewording it is a test change in the same commit, not a comment.
  local id="$1" w="$2" p="$3" cause="${4:-}" now pps pool pdesc why nts banner=1 msg prev prc pat
  now=$(date +%s)
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$p")
  pool='-'; pdesc='(untagged)'
  [[ "$pps" == "named "* ]] && { pool="${pps#named }"; pdesc="$pool"; }
  if [[ -n "$cause" ]]; then
    why="$cause"
    msg="cc swap STRANDED: $id is blocked on $w — $cause"
  else
    why=$(_strand_why "$id" "$p")
    msg="cc swap STRANDED: $id is blocked on $w and no account in pool $pdesc can take it — $why"
  fi
  # THE MARKER IS A CURRENT-STATE CLAIM; THE LOG LINE IS A HISTORICAL ONE (#69
  # review round 3, R4). The `-e` test debounces the LOG LINE — correctly, an
  # append storms — and until this branch it froze the MARKER'S CAUSE with it.
  # Measured: one tick of registry trouble stranded a row with "wrapper could
  # not be measured"; the genuine census that stood for the rest of the limit
  # window never replaced it, because the pane stays blocked and none of the
  # tick's three `_strand_clear` sites is reachable while it does.
  #
  # WHAT THAT COSTS NOW. A stale cause corrupts the marker file and omits the
  # replacement `swap.log` line, which is the box's own forensic trail. The
  # banner recomputes its sentence from a freshly measured reason on every
  # firing, so it already tells the truth; this merge also carries the marker
  # VERBATIM through `SessionRecord.stranded.reason` into `FleetSession.stranded`
  # on the REST and WebSocket fleet payloads. The old future-tense split became
  # false when the two parent trees were composed; D-2422 records why neither
  # parent's prose was enough.
  #
  # A MARKER REWRITE CANNOT STORM, which is why it takes no floor of its own:
  # `_reg_set` is one tmp-plus-rename over one file. The reason string CAN
  # change tick to tick — a candidate crossing `SWAP_CEILING` adds or drops a
  # `:limit` token — which is exactly why the APPEND is not keyed on it.
  #
  # READ THROUGH `_reg_read`, AND THE DISTINCTION IS LOAD-BEARING. `_reg_get`
  # would fold a marker that is a directory, a FIFO or mode 000 into the same
  # `""` an EMPTY one gives, and this branch would then decide a WRITE off a
  # path it had proved only `-e` for: measured, one unsuppressed `mv: cannot
  # overwrite directory` per stranded row per tick, for ever, from the two
  # `_strand_mark` call sites in `_auto_swap_check` that carry no redirect
  # group. rc != 0 stands still and writes nothing — the same rule the caller
  # states about `.wrapper` — and the SERVER-SIDE reader fails shut an
  # unreadable marker as `STRANDED_UNREADABLE` rather than believing it, so
  # nothing is lost by not repairing it. The reader is now a measurement, not
  # the future requirement this paragraph originally placed on the wave-3
  # branch: `fieldMeasured` distinguishes an unreadable listed marker and
  # `buildRecord` preserves that distinction in `SessionRecord.stranded`.
  #
  # THE EPISODE'S OWN EPOCH SURVIVES. The epoch is "since when", not "last
  # touched"; re-stamping it would make a four-hour strand read as new on every
  # tick now that `fleet.ts` ships it on the fleet wire as `stranded.at`. A torn
  # stamp degrades to `$now` rather than writing a non-numeric field the wire would
  # have to fail shut on.
  prev=$(_reg_read "$id" stranded); prc=$?
  if [[ ! -e "$REG/$id.stranded" ]]; then
    _reg_set "$id" stranded "$now $why"
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') stranded $id: $w (blocked) -> nowhere [pool=$pool] ($why)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  elif [[ "$prc" -eq 0 && "${prev#* }" != "$why" ]]; then
    pat="${prev%% *}"; [[ "$pat" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] || pat="$now"
    _reg_set "$id" stranded "$pat $why"
  fi
  # THE DIGIT VALIDATION IS FIRST inside the same `[[ ]]`, so `&&`
  # short-circuits before the arithmetic ever sees a torn or hand-edited field
  # (D-299's class; the same shape as the `swapblocked` gate above).
  nts=$(_reg_get "$id" strandnotify)
  [[ "$nts" =~ ^[0-9]+$ && $((now - nts)) -lt "$SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN" ]] && banner=""
  if [[ -n "$banner" ]]; then
    _reg_set "$id" strandnotify "$now"
    # DELIBERATELY NOT `cc swap: <id> moved …`: `grep -n 'cc swap: ' server/src/server.ts` parses
    # that exact prefix into a per-session chat line about an account CHANGE,
    # and nothing changed here.
    [[ -x "$REG/notify.sh" ]] && "$REG/notify.sh" "$msg" >/dev/null 2>&1
  fi
  return 0
}

_crosspool_valid() {   # id pool-state account... -> 0 valid / 1 none / 2 the record cannot be read. A VALID crossing marker names one of them
  # RULING 8 (spec §5.7.2). `$REG/<id>.crosspool` records a DELIBERATE crossing
  # and is meant to switch off the pool machinery's reaction to it — the
  # must-leave force, the home re-seed, the home guard, and `cmd_swap`'s
  # in-unit refusal — for exactly as long as two things hold:
  #   - the project is still in the pool it was in when the operator crossed
  #     (a retag or an untag is a new instruction and outranks the old one), and
  #   - the session is still on the account it crossed TO: `wrapper` for a
  #     `swap`/`start` crossing, `home` for a `prefer` one. Callers pass both
  #     and either satisfies it.
  # ADDED (final whole-branch review, M-2): `wrapper`/`home` are alternatives
  # this predicate CHECKS, but the record itself is ONE `_reg_set` field
  # (`_crosspool_mark` holds exactly one `_reg_set`, `grep -n
  # '^_crosspool_mark()' ccd/ccd`; it has THREE call sites, which is a
  # different count and not the one this sentence is about) —
  # single-valued and last-write-wins, not one slot per act. So `swap
  # --cross-pool` then `prefer --cross-pool` on the SAME row silently
  # OVERWRITES the first mark with the second rather than recording both:
  # the swap crossing's own account stops being what the marker names, and
  # this predicate's account clause fails for it on the very next tick —
  # ending a crossing the operator never asked to end.
  # Deliberately NOT switched off: the pre-existing home affinity (§2, O1) —
  # but only for a `wrapper`-recorded (`swap`/`start`) crossing. There, once a
  # crossed session returns home, it is `cur` that moves off the crossed
  # account (onto the recovered `home`), so the very next tick's check
  # against `cur` fails the account clause below on its own — no expiry
  # mechanism needed for that arm. A `home`-recorded (`prefer`) crossing is
  # different: the crossed account already IS `home`, so an affinity return
  # does not apply to it the same way, and nothing here ends that crossing by
  # itself — it takes a new `.home` write (another `prefer`) to change what
  # the `home` argument is checked against.
  #
  # `_swap_target` (Task 2) asks about `cur` and `home` to gate the
  # must-leave force and the home guard — two of the four reactions listed
  # above. The home re-seed in `_auto_swap_check` (Task 3) is this
  # predicate's second reader: it asks about `wrapper` and `home` before ever
  # touching `.home`, one tick before `_swap_target`'s own read of the same
  # marker runs. `_crosspool_tick` (Task 4) is this field's EXPIRER — the
  # only remover that judges the marker's OWN validity; `_reg_purge` removes
  # it too, but unconditionally, as one of every field wiped when the whole
  # row is purged, which says nothing about whether the crossing was still
  # good. `_crosspool_tick` runs immediately after that same read, before
  # the re-seed acts on it, and removes an invalid marker and logs
  # `crosspool-ended` — see its own header for the three ways a marker goes
  # invalid. The fourth reaction is `cmd_swap`'s in-unit refusal (Task 5,
  # above the detach arm, gated on this same predicate). THREE WRITERS now
  # (Task 6 landed the remaining two): `cmd_swap --cross-pool`'s success
  # tail (`_crosspool_mark`, Task 5, the marker's first writer),
  # `cmd_start --cross-pool`'s creation-only marker, and
  # `cmd_prefer --cross-pool`'s marker — plus a test that hand-writes it
  # with `_reg_set` for fixture setup that does not depend on any one verb's
  # shape.
  #
  # The `-e` test is FIRST so the steady state of an uncrossed row costs two
  # stats and never a `cat` — `_swap_target` calls this once for `cur` and
  # once for `home` per decision, every row, every 5-second tick, and each
  # call returns at the `-e` line.
  local id="$1" pps="$2" raw rrc ts pool acct a
  shift 2
  # `|| -L` (D-1994). A bare `-e` is a FOURTH read of this path, and it kept
  # exactly the fold `_reg_read` was added to remove: a dangling symlink or a
  # symlink loop at the marker answered "no crossing stands" here while
  # `_reg_read` calls the same path unreadable. Under the caller added by C1
  # that answer is no longer merely permissive — rc 1 is the one value that
  # positively ENABLES the home re-seed, so the cheap gate could authorise the
  # clobber the expensive one refuses.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.crosspool" || -L "$REG/$id.crosspool" ]] || return 1
  raw=$(_reg_read "$id" crosspool); rrc=$?
  # C1, AND THE REASON THE DISTINGUISHING READ BELONGS HERE RATHER THAN IN THE
  # EXPIRER. This is the ONLY place in this file that reads the marker's own
  # bytes, so a third answer put here is inherited by every consumer at once:
  # `_crosspool_tick` (the expirer), `_swap_target`'s two per-account
  # questions, and `cmd_swap`'s in-unit refusal. rc 2 means "the record could
  # not be read", which is NOT "no crossing stands" — and answering 1 there was
  # enough, on its own, to undo a deliberate crossing from a call site that
  # never touches the marker: `_swap_target` read `cross_cur=""`, set
  # `force=pool`, and relocated the session while the expirer was correctly
  # leaving the marker alone (D-1991).
  [[ "$rrc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  # rc 1 — it went away between the `-e` above and this line. Nothing stands.
  [[ "$rrc" -eq 1 ]] && return 1
  read -r ts pool acct <<<"$raw" || :
  [[ -n "${pool:-}" && "$pps" == "named $pool" ]] || return 1
  for a in "$@"; do [[ -n "$a" && "$a" == "${acct:-}" ]] && return 0; done
  return 1
}

_crosspool_tick() {   # id wrapper home pool-state -> 0 a crossing stands / 1 none does / 2 nobody can say
  # THE MARKER MUST EXPIRE, or a stale crossing is indistinguishable from a
  # live one and the pool machinery stays switched off on that row for ever.
  # Evaluated once per tick, BEFORE the re-seed consults it.
  #
  # `cur` and `home` are folded into ONE `_crosspool_valid` call, exactly as
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s own call site folds them into `crossed` — this
  # helper answers the same per-row question ("is this row under a
  # deliberate crossing AT ALL?"), not `_swap_target`'s per-account one, so
  # it inherits the identical cost: an unrelated wrong-pool `home` sitting
  # alongside a wrapper-arm marker is not distinguished from one the marker
  # actually names (see the DELIBERATE FOLD comment at the
  # `_auto_swap_check` call site for what that costs).
  #
  # THREE REASONS, not a boolean, because the remedies differ: a retag is
  # the operator's own newer instruction; a move off the account is the
  # affinity arm doing what §2's ruling-8 interpretation says it should; a
  # marker whose own record cannot be read is neither, and must say so
  # rather than guess at one.
  #
  # UNDECIDABLE IS NOT A DECISION — FOR THE PROJECT TAG. That is the only
  # input this paragraph covers and the only one a mutation confirms; the
  # scope sentence you are reading was added at merge review (M8), because
  # without it the assurance reads as covering every input to the same
  # decision, and it does not. The same rule `_swap_target` states in its own
  # words (`grep -n 'UNDECIDABLE IS NOT A DECISION' ccd/ccd` finds both
  # sites). An unreadable or malformed PROJECT tag means nobody can say
  # whether the crossing still holds, so the marker survives untouched — the
  # decided-state gate below (`[[ "$pps" == named* || "$pps" == untagged ]]`)
  # is what enforces that, and deleting it reds a measured case. Removing the
  # marker on an undecidable tag would invent a FOURTH way for a crossing to
  # end, beyond the three §5.7.2 names (a retag, an untag, a move off the
  # account) — and a transient permission hiccup on `$POOLS_DIR` would then
  # end every live crossing on the fleet box inside one tick, irreversibly,
  # with no writer able to put the marker back.
  #
  # THE ASSURANCE NOW REACHES THE OTHER INPUTS TOO — C1, LANDED 2026-09-08.
  # THE EMBARGO IT CARRIED IS LIFTED BY A SUCCESSFUL AGENT DEPLOY INCLUDING
  # ITS SUPERVISOR SWEEP, NOT BY THE MERGE (D-1999). This function runs inside
  # the long-lived `ccd supervise` processes, which hold the pre-deploy inode
  # until `deploy.sh`'s `try-restart claude-session@*` restarts them — so
  # between a merge and a completed sweep this file says one thing and ~20
  # running supervisors do another. The sentence that stood here said "and the
  # EMBARGO IT CARRIED IS LIFTED", unconditionally, contradicting D-1999 in
  # the same PR that defined it, and it is the artifact an operator greps at
  # exactly the moment they are deciding whether to cross (#67 review, S3).
  # What stood below was prose describing
  # a gap: `_reg_get` is `cat … 2>/dev/null` (`grep -n '^_reg_get()' ccd/ccd`),
  # folding ABSENT, UNREADABLE and EMPTY into one `""`, and this function acted
  # on that fold twice — deleting a marker whose record it merely could not
  # read, and logging `moved off <acct>` for a current account it had never
  # measured. `_reg_read` (rc 0 read / 1 absent / 2 unreadable) is the
  # distinguishing read the carry asked for, and the three guards below spend
  # it. The rule they enforce is the SAME one the project tag already had, one
  # paragraph up: undecidable is not a decision, so the marker survives and
  # this function answers rc 2 rather than picking an arm.
  #
  # ONE MEASURED CORRECTION (D-1986) to what this comment used to say, because
  # the old sentence made the `home` arm look like the `cur` arm and it is not:
  # "a failed read of either presents as `""`" was true of `cur` and FALSE of
  # `home`. `home` reaches this function through `_home_for`
  # (`grep -n '^_home_for()' ccd/ccd`), which is `[[ -n "$h" ]] && echo "$h" ||
  # _id_wrapper "$1"` — so an unreadable `.home` presents not as `""` but as
  # the account encoded in the id prefix, a real account name that no read
  # produced. That is worse than an empty string in BOTH directions: a
  # substituted name cannot match the marker when it should, and it CAN match
  # when it should not — and the second direction is the one a guard inside
  # this function could never have closed, because `_crosspool_valid` above
  # consumes `home` four lines before any guard here would run. Both directions
  # are closed by measuring `home` at the top of `_auto_swap_check` instead
  # (`grep -n '^_home_measured()' ccd/ccd`), which is why no `.home` guard
  # appears in this function at all. `ccd-crosspool.test.ts` pins the
  # substitution itself, and pins that an ABSENT `.home` still decides.
  #
  # STILL FOLDED, DISCLOSED NOT CLOSED (D-1990) — AND THE FIRST VERSION OF THIS
  # PARAGRAPH WAS WRONG, which is why it is spelled out rather than summarised.
  # It said the residual was "the same two fields" and that every other read of
  # them "refuses or ranks, it does not delete a marker". Both halves were
  # false: the marker RECORD was a third folded input, and `_swap_target`'s use
  # of it set `force=pool` — a RELOCATION, not a refusal. Those are closed now
  # (D-1991), and `.wrapper`/`.home` are measured before any consumer sees them
  # (D-1992).
  #
  # WHAT IS ACTUALLY LEFT is `.project`. `project=$(_reg_get "$id" project)`
  # feeds `_project_pool_state` at four sites (`grep -n 'pps=$(_project_pool_state'
  # ccd/ccd`), and that function answers `untagged` for an empty argument — on
  # purpose, for the pre-2026-07-28 rows that have no `.project` at all. So an
  # UNREADABLE `.project` reads as "this project is in no pool" and silently
  # lifts the pool constraint for that row. Same class, different field, wider
  # than this carry: REPORTED to the coordinator, not folded in here (D-2000).
  local id="$1" cur="$2" home="$3" pps="$4" raw rrc cvrc ts pool acct reason
  # `|| -L` for the same reason `_crosspool_valid`'s own pre-gate carries it
  # (D-1994) — the two must agree about which paths exist.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.crosspool" || -L "$REG/$id.crosspool" ]] || return 1
  _crosspool_valid "$id" "$pps" "$cur" "$home"; cvrc=$?
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 0 ]] && return 0
  # UNDECIDABLE, decided by the one reader of the record (C1). The marker
  # survives untouched and this function says so, rather than picking an arm.
  [[ "$cvrc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  [[ "$pps" == named* || "$pps" == untagged ]] || return 1
  raw=$(_reg_read "$id" crosspool); rrc=$?
  # A SECOND READ, FOR THE REASON ONLY — the verdict above was
  # `_crosspool_valid`'s. It is measured too, because the record can turn
  # unreadable between the two reads and an arm below would then name a
  # condition nothing observed. rc 1 is the same race one line up: the record
  # went away, so there is nothing left to end and nothing to log.
  [[ "$rrc" -eq 2 ]] && return 2
  [[ "$rrc" -eq 1 ]] && return 1
  read -r ts pool acct <<<"$raw" || :
  if [[ -z "$raw" ]]; then
    # The MARKER's own record is what is broken here — not the project tag:
    # `$pps` is already known to be a DECIDED state by this line (the gate
    # above). Falling into "project pool is now $pps" would be a LIE whenever
    # `$pps` happens to be exactly the pool this crossing was made under —
    # the operator would go hunting for a retag that never happened.
    #
    # EMPTY AND MALFORMED BOTH END IT (D-1987), and both are correct to end
    # on, for the reason the first paragraph of this function gives: a record
    # that names no pool can never be validated by any later tick, so keeping
    # it would switch the pool machinery off on this row for ever. They get
    # SEPARATE reasons because they send an operator to different places —
    # a zero-byte write versus bytes that do not parse.
    reason="marker record is empty"
  elif [[ -z "${pool:-}" ]]; then
    reason="marker record is malformed"
  elif [[ "$pps" != "named $pool" ]]; then
    reason="project pool is now $pps"
  else
    reason="moved off ${acct:-<unrecorded>}"
  fi
  rm -f -- "$REG/$id.crosspool"
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') crosspool-ended $id: $reason" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  return 1
}

# THE VOICE rc 2 WOULD OTHERWISE NOT HAVE (D-1995). C1 turned a destructive
# answer into a standing-still, which is the right trade only if the standing
# still is OBSERVABLE — and every `return 2` path writes nothing at all. The
# code before C1 was wrong and LOUD: it emitted one `crosspool-ended <id>:
# marker record is unreadable` line, misnamed but naming the row and putting an
# operator on the file. Silence is a worse failure than a wrong reason, because
# nothing in ccd ever re-chmods a registry file: a permanently unmeasurable
# field parks the row for ever, its crossing never expiring and its home never
# re-seeded, with no artifact anywhere on the box. `$REG/swap.log` is the only
# window onto this machinery.
#
# FIRST-WRITE-WINS, NOT A COOLDOWN. The condition repeats every 5 seconds, so a
# plain `echo` is 720 lines an hour per stuck row. The stamp field makes it one
# line per EPISODE: `_tick_decided` removes it the moment the row decides
# again, so a transient hiccup logs once and a permanent one logs once, and the
# next real episode logs again. Same shape as `_strand_mark`'s notify debounce,
# and the `-e` test is first so a healthy row writes nothing at all.
_tick_undecidable() {   # id field — one swap.log line per episode of standing still
  # THE STAMP IS BEST-EFFORT AND THE LINE IS NOT CONDITIONAL ON IT (#67
  # review). `_reg_set`'s result used to be discarded, so in the one condition
  # this function most needs to report — `$REG` itself unsearchable, which is
  # what makes every field unreadable at once — the stamp could never be
  # written, `-e` was false on every tick, and the debounce never engaged: one
  # FAILED write and one failed append per row per five seconds, forever,
  # with the errors going to the supervise journal instead of the operator's
  # stated window. Writing the line whether or not the stamp lands keeps the
  # BEHAVIOUR (say it once per episode when the registry works) and refuses to
  # make the report depend on the thing being reported.
  #
  # THE DEBOUNCE FOLLOWS THE TRUTH; IT DOES NOT MERELY COUNT (#69 review round
  # 3, R1). The test here was `[[ -e … ]]` — bare EXISTENCE — and an episode is
  # not a file, it is a CONDITION. With four callers naming four different
  # fields, existence alone made the FIRST condition of a row silence every
  # later one for as long as the stamp stood, and `ccd` never re-chmods a
  # registry file, so "for as long" can be forever: measured, a row whose
  # `.home` went unreadable and then recovered onto an unreadable POOL TAG
  # logged the home episode once and the pool episode NEVER, while the stamp
  # went on naming a condition that had ended. That is the same shape as the
  # stale `.stranded` marker this round's S2 fix retracts one function down,
  # and it gets the same answer: compare the stamp to the condition, and let a
  # CHANGED condition through.
  #
  # READ THROUGH `_reg_read`, NOT `_reg_get`, AND THE DISTINCTION IS THE POINT:
  # an UNREADABLE stamp must not be read as "no stamp". Folding those would put
  # this function back to writing one line and one `_reg_set` per row per five
  # seconds in exactly the condition it exists to report — the storm, arrived
  # at through the fold instead of through the clear. rc 2 therefore answers
  # ALREADY-SAID, which keeps the best-effort contract above unchanged: a
  # registry too broken to read the stamp is also too broken to have written
  # it, and the line is already being written unconditionally below.
  #
  # THE RESIDUAL, DISCLOSED RATHER THAN CAPPED: a row whose undecidable field
  # FLAPS between two values on successive ticks now logs on every flip. That
  # needs two faults alternating at 5 s, and the result is LOUD rather than
  # silent — the failure direction this file chooses everywhere else. A floor
  # would have to sit on the re-stamp path only, never on an episode's first
  # line, and no measured case asks for one yet.
  local _tu_prev _tu_rc
  _tu_prev=$(_reg_read "$1" tickstuck); _tu_rc=$?
  [[ "$_tu_rc" -eq 2 ]] && return 0
  [[ "$_tu_rc" -eq 0 && "${_tu_prev#* }" == "$2" ]] && return 0
  _reg_set "$1" tickstuck "$(date +%s) $2" 2>/dev/null || true
  # THE GROUP CARRIES THE REDIRECT, and the order is the whole point: in
  # `cmd >> file 2>/dev/null` the redirections are applied left to right, so
  # when `>>` fails bash reports "Is a directory" on a stderr that has not been
  # silenced yet. Measured — the first spelling of this line printed exactly
  # that. `{ …; } 2>/dev/null` silences the group before the inner redirect is
  # attempted, which is the only spelling that keeps a failed append out of the
  # supervise journal.
  { echo "$(date '+%F %T') tick-undecidable $1: $2 could not be measured — deciding nothing" \
      >> "$REG/swap.log"; } 2>/dev/null || true
  return 0
}
_tick_strand_undecidable() {   # id field [wrapper] — the LOUD half of standing still
  # `swap.log` is not a surface. Measured, not assumed:
  # `grep -rn 'swap\.log' server/src agent/src shared pwa/src` finds three
  # hits and all three are comments — no reader anywhere in the product.
  # Both observable paths exist now: `notify.sh` is the immediate banner, and
  # the `.stranded` marker is read fail-shut through `SessionRecord.stranded`
  # into both fleet payloads. The future-tense split inherited from both parents
  # was true on neither one's composed tree (D-2422).
  #
  # ONLY WHEN THE PANE IS REALLY STUCK, and that is the whole discipline here.
  # A session standing still on an unmeasurable field is not by itself
  # stranded — it may be idle, mid-turn, or perfectly happy — and marking it
  # would be the same fabricated positive claim this commit removes one seam
  # up. `_session_hard_blocked` is the same classifier the ordinary rescue path
  # uses, asked pane-first with D-2363's transcript fallback; it needs only
  # `_tmux "$1"`, never the field that could not be read.
  # THE CAPTURE'S COST, DISCLOSED (#69 review round 1 S7, ruled NO FIX as
  # D-2162, re-measured in round 3, and re-measured AGAIN on the #70 merge —
  # which moved it). This `capture-pane` is unconditional and sits above
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s two cooldown gates, so on an affected row it is a fork
  # the gates cannot stop. Measured with a counting `tmux` first on PATH, over
  # every trip shape crossed with the gate and pane states below.
  #
  # THE GRID ITSELF WAS NEVER RECORDED, AND SAYING SO IS THE CORRECTION (#69
  # review round 4 gate). Three different designs have been written for one set
  # of results: this line said "both cooldown states and six pane states" (12
  # cells), the plan says "3 `lastcompact` x 4 `lastswap` x 6 pane" (72), and
  # every figure either states is "of 36". The RESULTS are internally coherent
  # (18 + 18 - 6 = 30, and remedy 3 gates on the swap cooldowns) and were
  # measured; the sentence describing how they were produced was reconstructed
  # afterwards, three times, and cannot be reproduced from any of the three. It
  # is deleted rather than guessed at a fourth time. Nothing below depends on
  # it: the NO-FIX ruling rests on REACHABILITY, which is measured on its own
  # line at the end of this block and was re-measured independently at the gate.
  # The cardinals below are quotes from that unrecorded run — read them as an
  # order of magnitude, and re-derive the grid before re-deriving a fraction:
  #
  #   • THE DELTA IS +1 per tick per affected row, in every affected cell on
  #     both trees. Never a doubling as such — that framing died twice.
  #   • A WRAPPER-GUARD row (the call at the tick's `wrapper` guard) tops out at
  #     2, which is what a HEALTHY row costs in its ordinary steady state and
  #     one below the 3 a compacting tick costs. It cannot reach a third: an
  #     unreadable `.wrapper` empties `_cfg_dir`, so `$sf` fails its `-e`/`-L`
  #     pair and the compact lane returns at `status-unreadable` two gates
  #     earlier.
  #   • THE PROJECT-GUARD CALL SITE IS NOT THAT SHAPE, and this is the half the
  #     round-3 measurement missed by measuring one call site and generalising.
  #     That row's `.wrapper` reads FINE, so `_cfg_dir` resolves, `$sf` exists,
  #     the quiet gate passes, and the compact lane reaches its SECOND capture.
  #     Measured 3 captures per tick in 7 of 36 gate/pane states — EQUAL to the
  #     healthy ceiling, not below it — and those ticks compact for real. This
  #     class is also the one place the trip table's independence claims fail:
  #     it is neither pane-independent nor `lastswap`-independent.
  #     SAY THE CALL SITE WHENEVER YOU SAY THE CEILING; they are different
  #     numbers, and the sentence that stood here said only the smaller one.
  #   • IT CANNOT TRIP ON A BOX WITH NO POOLS. `$POOLS_DIR` has one `mkdir` in
  #     the tree, and the project guard is gated on `_pool_untaggable`, so the 3
  #     is one `ccd project-pool` away rather than live.
  #
  # WHAT THE #70 MERGE MOVED: its `_auto_compact_check` `lastswap` arm went from
  # `return 0` to `fromswap=1` — the lane no longer ends there, it falls through
  # to its own capture. So the states where THIS capture is the tick's only one
  # (0 -> 1) shrank from 30 of 36 to 18 of 36, and the gate for them is now
  # `lastcompact` fresh ALONE rather than either cooldown. Every cost figure
  # taken before that merge understates the in-cooldown compact tick.
  # Three remedies were measured and all three are defects; the nearest one,
  # replicating the cooldown tests here, now removes only 6 of those 18 cells —
  # 12 survive — while un-stranding a hard-blocked row and stranding a healthy
  # one, with every case green.
  local pane blocked=""
  pane=$(tmux capture-pane -t "$(_tmux "$1")" -p 2>/dev/null | tail -8)
  # AN UNMEASURABLE PANE WITH NO TRANSCRIPT VERDICT CLEARS NOTHING (#69 review,
  # S2, amended by D-2363). An empty capture is normally a pane nobody could
  # read; only `_session_hard_blocked`'s transcript fallback may make a positive
  # claim from it. Without either measurement, the return below must stay ahead
  # of the clear or it would fabricate recovery.
  # THIS CALL'S OWN COST, DISCLOSED SEPARATELY FROM THE CAPTURE ABOVE (final
  # review finding 3, 2026-09-10; mitigated by D-2444) — the capture-pane essay
  # above this function is about the `tmux capture-pane` fork alone. This
  # `_session_hard_blocked` call runs on the same 5s cadence as
  # `_auto_swap_check`, but both calls share `$REG/<id>.tscan`: whichever misses
  # first may pay the ~+27-32 ms uncached transcript path measured at the other
  # call site, while the later same-tick call normally pays only cache lookup.
  # An idle negative gets at most one full scan per 30s interval; a positive
  # additionally re-measures the draft on every call.
  _session_hard_blocked "$1" "$pane" && blocked=1
  [[ -n "$pane" || -n "$blocked" ]] || return 0
  # THE MARKER MUST FOLLOW THE TRUTH — `_strand_mark`'s own docstring says so,
  # and until this line it could not, from here. The caller returns at the
  # `wrapper` guard ABOVE the tick's only automatic healthy-pane clear, so a
  # strand set on this path survived every later tick on which the pane had
  # recovered, and only a human verb could retract it. Worse, and measured: the
  # `[[ ! -e … ]]` debounce inside `_strand_mark` then SWALLOWED every later
  # genuine strand on that row — the commit whose thesis is that standing still
  # must be SAID had made a class of standing-still permanently unsayable.
  #
  # This is not a new rule. It is `_auto_swap_check`'s existing rule ("a healthy
  # pane is not stranded") said at the second exit: same capture, same
  # classifier, same refusal to act on an empty one. `_strand_clear`'s own `-e`
  # guard keeps the steady state silent, so a healthy row with an unreadable
  # field writes nothing at all.
  [[ -n "$blocked" ]] || { _strand_clear "$1"; return 0; }
  # THE GROUP CARRIES THE REDIRECT (#69 review, S6), same spelling and same
  # reason as `_tick_undecidable` above: `_strand_mark`'s two `_reg_set` writes
  # and its own `swap.log` append are unwrapped, and this caller runs every five
  # seconds on a row whose registry may itself be the broken thing. Measured
  # with `$REG` at mode 000: 5 shell errors per row per tick without this group,
  # 0 with it. `cmd_swap`'s `_strand_mark` call stays LOUD on purpose — an
  # operator running a verb by hand should see a failed registry write.
  #
  # WHY IT REPEATS, which is the half that makes it a storm and which the
  # sentence above did not say (#69 review round 3, R6). `_strand_mark`'s own
  # `[[ ! -e … ]]` debounce cannot stat anything at mode 000, so it is TRUE on
  # every tick: the burst is not 5 lines once per episode, it is 5 lines per row
  # per tick, for ever. Measured 15 over 3 ticks, not 5 then 0.
  #
  # TWO CORRECTIONS TO THE SENTENCE THIS REPLACES, both measured. (1) It said
  # "the only caller of it that runs every five seconds". `_auto_swap_check`
  # holds TWO `_strand_mark` CALL SITES (`grep -n '_strand_mark "\$id"'
  # ccd/ccd`) — MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE, so at most one fires per tick, which
  # corrects round 3's own "TWICE on the same tick"; neither carries a group —
  # so a narrower fault, `$REG`
  # searchable but `.stranded`/`swap.log` unwritable, still reaches those two
  # unwrapped. That is disclosed here rather than fixed: wrapping them is a
  # separate decision about the ORDINARY strand path, not about this one.
  # (2) It said "against origin/main's 3" as though the 3 were this code's.
  # They are `_tick_undecidable`'s — `main` carries no strand arm at all
  # (`grep -c _tick_strand_undecidable` on main is 0), so the baseline is a
  # different function's cost, not a smaller version of this one.
  #
  # `_strand_clear` STAYS OUTSIDE, and that is measured rather than conceded.
  # At mode 000 its own `-e` test is FALSE, so it writes nothing this group
  # could silence. The only shape where it does write is `$REG` READ-ONLY with
  # a `.stranded` it cannot remove — and there the SAME two lines come out of
  # `_auto_swap_check`'s ordinary healthy-pane clear, on every tick of every
  # healthy session, which `origin/main` does too. Moving the rarest of the six
  # `_strand_clear` call sites inside would silence two lines nobody is seeing
  # and leave the common one untouched.
  # `$3` IS THE ACCOUNT WHEN THE CALLER MEASURED ONE (#69 review round 3, R3).
  # The `wrapper` guard above this function's own caller has no account to give
  # — that is the field it could not read — so `<unmeasured>` stays the default
  # and that call site is unchanged. The project guard, which sits BELOW the
  # wrapper read, does have one, and a banner reading "blocked on <unmeasured>"
  # for an account that WAS measured is a fabricated claim of the same kind this
  # round keeps removing. Nothing in `server/test` or `ccd/` matches the
  # `<unmeasured>` literal (grepped), so no assertion moves.
  # THE SENTENCE COMES FROM `_undecidable_cause`, NOT FROM HERE (#69 review
  # round 4 gate, finding 3). This line built its own string — `"$2 could not be
  # measured, …"` — while the LATE path — `_auto_swap_check`'s rc>=2 arm,
  # `grep -n '_undecidable_cause "' ccd/ccd` — rendered the same condition
  # through `_undecidable_cause`. Measured: an unreadable `.project`
  # caught here said "project could not be measured" and caught there said "the
  # project's own registry field could not be read ($REG/<id>.project)", and
  # these two call sites are the EARLY RETURNS, so the common case was the one
  # that named no file. `_undecidable_cause`'s own header claimed both surfaces
  # "cannot drift apart" while this line was the drift.
  local _tsu_prj; _tsu_prj=$(_reg_get "$1" project)
  { _strand_mark "$1" "${3:-<unmeasured>}" "$_tsu_prj" \
      "$(_undecidable_cause "$2" "$1" "$_tsu_prj")"; } 2>/dev/null
  return 0
}
_tick_decided() { [[ -e "$REG/$1.tickstuck" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$1.tickstuck"; return 0; }

_crosspool_mark() {   # id project-pool account — record a DELIBERATE crossing
  # One dot-free registry field, so `_reg_purge` (`grep -n '^_reg_purge()'
  # ccd/ccd`; its one-dot rule is stated in that same function's own header
  # comment, "one dot, no more" — grepped, not line-numbered, because Task 7
  # edits that comment block) purges it with the row. `cmd_swap --cross-pool`'s
  # success tail is this field's first writer; `cmd_start --cross-pool` and
  # `cmd_prefer --cross-pool` are Task 6's and HAVE LANDED — three call sites
  # in all, `grep -n '_crosspool_mark "' ccd/ccd`. CORRECTED at merge review
  # (M7): this said "will call", in the future tense, while the sibling
  # header 78 lines up already said "THREE WRITERS now (Task 6 landed the
  # remaining two)" — two comments in one file disagreeing about the same
  # fact, which is worse than either being wrong alone.
  _reg_set "$1" crosspool "$(date +%s) $2 $3"
}

cmd_swap() {   # [--force] [--cross-pool] id target-wrapper — move a session to another account, conversation intact.
  # Transcripts are plain JSONL with no account binding (validated 2026-07-03): carry the
  # session's transcripts into the target config dir, flip the registry wrapper, restart.
  # A manual swap is now pool-constrained exactly like an automatic one — see
  # the POOL POLICY block below — and `--cross-pool` is the operator's one way
  # past it. Only `--force` is unconstrained by pools: it overrides the
  # transcript pre-flight, a completely separate question.
  #
  # Flags are stripped BEFORE the positional parse, so `ccd swap <id> <target> --force`
  # and `ccd swap --force <id> <target>` mean the same thing and neither lets "--force"
  # land in $1 or $2 — the same rule §4.1 needs for `ccd stop --surface`, applied here for
  # the same reason. `--force` is an operator's word and stays one: _auto_swap_check never
  # passes it and no CCD_ARGV entry builds it, so no server route can reach it; and
  # _is_valid_wrapper rejects the literal, which is the second lock on the target slot.
  #
  # `--cross-pool` IS A SECOND, SEPARATE TOKEN and never an alias of --force.
  # --force means "accept transcript loss"; overloading it would make a
  # transcript-loss acceptance also a pool crossing, or the reverse. It is
  # NOT caught by a downstream lock the way the sentence above reads for
  # --force: on an unpatched build the flag drifts into the `id` slot, which
  # no lock validates at all, and what refuses it there is only an
  # INCIDENTAL collision further down (`ccd-project-pool.test.ts`'s D-1798
  # describe measures both shapes), never the flag itself. What this loop
  # actually buys is the real invariant: once it has run, NOTHING downstream
  # on this verb ever sees "--cross-pool" sitting in $1 or $2. That makes
  # this loop the ONLY guard against the flag landing in a positional slot —
  # it must stay ABOVE the `local id=… target=…` reads below, never move
  # beneath them.
  local force="" cross="" args=()
  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
      --force)      force=1; shift ;;
      --cross-pool) cross=1; shift ;;
      --)      shift; args+=("$@"); break ;;
      *)       args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"    # bash >= 4.4 (the fleet host runs 5.2): an empty array here is
                         # not an unbound-variable error under `set -u`.
  local id="${1:?usage: ccd swap [--force] [--cross-pool] <id> <target-wrapper>}" target="${2:?usage: ccd swap [--force] [--cross-pool] <id> <target-wrapper>}"
  _is_valid_wrapper "$target" || die "unknown wrapper '$target' (valid: ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]})"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "no registry for '$id'"
  local cur; cur=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
  [[ "$target" != "$cur" ]] || die "'$id' is already on $cur"
  [[ -x "$WRAPPER_DIR/$target" ]] || die "wrapper missing: $WRAPPER_DIR/$target"
  # WITH THE PRE-FLIGHT, ABOVE THE TEARDOWN. Same reason the transcript
  # pre-flight sits where it does: a refusal here costs nothing and leaves the
  # session alive on the account that still holds its file, while the same
  # refusal after the `systemctl stop` leaves a session that is dead AND still
  # on its old lane. The matching check at the far end of the carry is at the
  # registry flip below; this one is not redundant with it — it is the one that
  # keeps a doomed swap from killing a pane.
  _account_still_rostered "$target" "The session is untouched and stays on $cur."
  # ── POOL POLICY (§5.5.5) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
  # HERE, above the detach arm, so this dies SYNCHRONOUSLY: the PWA gets a 502
  # carrying this sentence, a shell gets stderr, and the transient unit's
  # stderr lands in swap.log. Below the detach arm it would refuse in a process
  # nobody is waiting for.
  local project pps prc prjrc crossing="" pside pstate
  # THE PROJECT FIELD IS MEASURED HERE TOO (#69 review round 4, B3). Round 3
  # converted the tick's two reads and left the VERBS, and the verbs are the half
  # a PWA tap reaches. `_reg_get` folds ABSENT, EMPTY and UNREADABLE into `""`,
  # `_project_pool_state ""` short-circuits to `untagged`, and `untagged` is the
  # ONE state `_pool_ok` admits every account under — so neither `die` below is
  # reached and the relocation happens silently, out of pool, with no crossing
  # marker. MEASURED on five shapes with `demo` tagged `pool-a`: a FIFO, a
  # directory, a character device, a dangling symlink and `chmod 000` each
  # printed the move and wrote the new wrapper, where the readable control
  # refuses `pool-mismatch`.
  #
  # THREE OF THOSE FIVE PREDATE THIS BRANCH; TWO DO NOT — measured on
  # `origin/main` itself, same fixture, rather than assumed. A DIRECTORY, a
  # DANGLING SYMLINK and `chmod 000` already relocated silently there: those were
  # never a hang, they are the round-2 constraint lift reaching the verb, and
  # nobody closed it. What THIS branch changed is the other two: a FIFO blocked
  # in `open(2)` on main and a symlink to `/dev/zero` in `read(2)`, and the `-f`
  # added to `_reg_get` turned both hangs into the same silent move. The branch
  # widened the hole from three shapes to five; it did not open it. Said that way
  # because the first draft of this comment claimed the branch created the whole
  # defect, and that is false for three of the five inputs.
  #
  # A VERB MAY DIE WHERE THE TICK MUST STAND STILL. The tick answers an
  # unmeasurable field by deciding nothing and saying so; a human or a PWA tap is
  # WAITING for an answer, and the same two lines below already `die` on an
  # undecidable pool TAG. This is the same condition one level down, so it gets
  # the same voice.
  project=$(_reg_read "$id" project); prjrc=$?
  [[ "$prjrc" -eq 2 ]] && ! _pool_untaggable \
    && die "the project field for $id could not be read: $REG/$id.project — fix or clear it; nothing was touched"
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  _pool_ok "$target" "$pps"; prc=$?
  # THE SNAPSHOT IS LOAD-BEARING HERE, not a convention (fix round 1, I1/I2).
  # `_crosspool_valid` and `_strand_mark` both run between this call and the
  # mismatch `die` below; neither consults the pool rule today, and "today" is
  # exactly the word that makes reading the globals down there a latent
  # stale-fact defect. Taken here, one line after the call that filled them.
  pside="$POOL_OK_SIDE"; pstate="$POOL_OK_STATE"
  # UNDECIDABLE REFUSES EVEN WITH THE FLAG. `--cross-pool` says "cross from
  # pool X to pool Y on purpose"; when the tag cannot be read there is no X to
  # name, so there is no decision to override.
  [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]] \
    && die "$(_pool_undecidable_why "$pside" "$pstate" "$project" "$target"); nothing was touched"
  if [[ "$prc" -eq 1 ]]; then
    # `if` TAKES rc 2 AS FALSE, AND THAT IS THE RIGHT ANSWER HERE (C1) — but it
    # is right for a reason, not by luck, so it is said out loud. This is the
    # only one of `_crosspool_valid`'s three consumers where "the marker cannot
    # be read" must FAIL SHUT rather than stand still: an operator is asking to
    # move a session out of pool right now, a standing crossing is the only
    # thing that would let it through unflagged, and a crossing nobody can read
    # is not one this verb may act on. Same rule as the undecidable TAG four
    # lines up, same outcome — refuse, and say so.
    if _crosspool_valid "$id" "$pps" "$target"; then
      : # the operator already crossed to this account and the crossing stands
    elif [[ -n "$cross" ]]; then
      crossing=1
    else
      # §5.8.4: inside `_dispatch_swap`'s transient unit nobody reads this exit
      # code, so when this gate fires with the variable SET, mark and banner
      # (both floor-debounced) instead of leaving a silent strand for days.
      # CORRECTED (final whole-branch review, I-2): this line is a sound
      # FILTER on a dispatch that arrives with `CCD_SWAP_AUTO=1` — it does
      # NOT by itself make the classic pre-deploy window audible, because an
      # old supervisor's OWN pre-deploy `_dispatch_swap` predates the
      # variable and never sets it, so that dispatch reaches this line with
      # the variable unset and refuses silently instead (see
      # `_dispatch_swap`'s own comment for the full account and the
      # supervisor-sweep closer). The CAUSE is passed explicitly (Important
      # 4): `_strand_why`'s pool census would be misleading here — an in-pool
      # account can often genuinely take the session; what actually happened
      # is this exact refusal, reachable today chiefly via the
      # retag-races-a-dispatched-unit's-jitter window spec §5.8.4 accepts as
      # P-8, not a stale supervisor.
      [[ "${CCD_SWAP_AUTO:-}" == 1 ]] && _strand_mark "$id" "$cur" "$project" \
        "$target refused as a cross-pool crossing nobody asked for — likely a supervisor still running a pre-deploy ccd; the remedy is the claude-session@* unit sweep"
      die "pool-mismatch: $(_pool_mismatch_why "$target" "$pstate" "$project" "$pps") — to cross on purpose: ccd swap --cross-pool $id $target"
    fi
  fi
  # Invoked from inside the session being swapped (Claude's own Bash tool)? The swap kills
  # its caller mid-flight — detach an orphan to finish the job after we return. The
  # detached process re-enters this function and runs the pre-flight itself; its refusal
  # reaches the operator through swapblocked + notify.sh, never through this return code,
  # which nothing is waiting for by then. --force rides along or the detached retry would
  # silently drop the operator's decision.
  if [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" && "$(tmux display-message -p '#S' 2>/dev/null)" == "$(_tmux "$id")" && -z "${CCD_SWAP_DETACHED:-}" ]]; then
    # THE systemd-run's OWN EXIT STATUS IS THE ONLY EVIDENCE THIS ARM EVER GETS,
    # and it used to be discarded: measured, `systemd-run` returning 1 printed
    # the confident `detached: …` line below, returned 0, and recorded NOTHING —
    # no swap.log line, no swapblocked, no banner, `wrapper` unchanged. This is
    # the COMMON invocation (a session swapping itself from its own Bash tool),
    # and `_dispatch_swap`, the auto-path twin, has carried the matching
    # `|| echo "… dispatch FAILED …"` guard all along.
    #
    # Not routed through `_swap_refuse`, deliberately. Nothing was torn down —
    # the pane is alive and the caller is READING THIS — so the strongest
    # channel here is exactly the one the successful-detach case cannot use: a
    # non-zero rc and a line on stderr. A `swapblocked` stamp would also be a
    # lie about the cause (the conversation was never even looked for) and would
    # gate a legitimate auto-rescue for the next SWAPBLOCK_COOLDOWN seconds over
    # a failure that has nothing to do with carrying it.
    if ! _svc_run_detached bash -c "sleep 2; CCD_SWAP_DETACHED=1 exec '$HOME/.local/bin/ccd' swap ${force:+--force} ${cross:+--cross-pool} '$id' '$target' >>'$REG/swap.log' 2>&1"; then
      echo "$(date '+%F %T') detach FAILED for $id: $cur -> $target (systemd-run)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
      echo "ccd: could not detach the swap of $id ($cur -> $target): systemd-run failed. Nothing was moved and this session is untouched — retry from OUTSIDE the session: ccd swap ${force:+--force }${cross:+--cross-pool }$id $target" >&2
      return 1
    fi
    echo "detached: $id will restart under $target in a few seconds (this session dies with the swap — that's expected)"
    return 0
  fi
  local uuid wd srccfg dstcfg mdir
  uuid=$(_reg_get "$id" uuid); wd=$(readlink -f "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)")
  srccfg=$(_cfg_dir "$cur"); dstcfg=$(_cfg_dir "$target")
  [[ -n "$srccfg" && -n "$dstcfg" ]] || die "no config-dir mapping for '$cur' -> '$target'"
  mdir=$(echo "$wd" | tr '/._' '---')
  # PRE-FLIGHT — advisory, and it runs BEFORE the teardown. The glob is a read, so a
  # refusal here costs nothing and leaves the session alive on the account that still
  # holds its file; the pre-fix order looked only after stopping the unit and killing the
  # pane, so a miss produced a session that was dead AND historyless. Advisory rather than
  # authoritative because the file the swap actually carries is the one that exists after
  # Claude Code has flushed below.
  if [[ -z "$force" && -z "$(_transcript_matches "$srccfg" "$uuid")" ]]; then
    _swap_refuse "$id" "$cur" "$target" "no transcript found for $uuid under $cur" 0
    return 1
  fi
  _svc_stop "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null   # first, or Restart=always resurrects under the old wrapper
  tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null
  sleep 1                                                  # let claude flush its final transcript entries
  # A SWAP IS NOT AN ABANDONMENT. Between the stop and the start this row is
  # not alive and, after 120 seconds, would also stop looking watched — and
  # §2.3's `cp -a` fallback over a 188MB sidecar can take that long. Stamped
  # here, re-stamped every SWAP_BEAT_INTERVAL by `_swap_beat` for as long as the
  # carry runs, and stamped once more below, so the window classifies
  # `restarting` throughout — which is exactly what it is. Note the swap does
  # NOT write `.stopped`: it is not a deliberate stop, which is why it goes
  # round `_ws_unsupervise` rather than through it.
  _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"
  # Started BEFORE the first byte moves and stopped on every path out of the
  # carry — the success tail below, and the refusal arm inside the `if`, which
  # stops it before `_swap_refuse` runs so a refused swap leaves nothing behind
  # claiming to watch the row it just gave up on.
  local beat=""
  _swap_beat "$id" "$$" & beat=$!
  # AUTHORITATIVE. Two things changed across that sleep and both matter. The transcript
  # gained the session's final entries — for a session being moved mid-turn, the most
  # valuable lines in it, which is the whole reason the sleep exists. And the uuid may
  # have ROTATED: a /clear does not delete the file, it mints a new uuid, and _sync_uuid
  # writes that into the registry — so a swap that pre-flighted uuid-A can arrive here
  # owning uuid-B. Re-read it; uuid-B is the one the resumed session will ask for.
  #
  # An external lane -> an Anthropic one: the Codex lane (via LiteLLM) writes
  # assistant messages with EMPTY text blocks, which the Anthropic API rejects on
  # the next turn ("text content blocks must be non-empty" -> every turn 400s).
  # Fill them so the resumed session is valid. Anthropic -> Anthropic needs no
  # fixup, and neither does a move ONTO an external lane. Keyed on home-ability
  # rather than on the name `gpt` (merged from main, whose roster-becomes-data
  # refactor made the literal wrong): a non-home-able account is the
  # external backend by definition — and sanitizing is safe in the over-eager
  # direction anyway, since it only rewrites text blocks that are already empty.
  #
  # The carry runs it on the COPY and BEFORE the hardlinks, because
  # _sanitize_anthropic rewrites through os.replace and that breaks links. That
  # ordering is this branch's (§2.2) and is why the decision is passed DOWN as a
  # flag rather than applied here: main applied it to one guessed path, and there
  # is no longer one path — there is every match the uuid has under the source
  # account, which is the defect this task exists to close.
  uuid=$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)
  local sanitize=0
  # `if` rather than the old `[[ … ]] && sanitize=1` one-liner: `!` binds to the
  # PIPELINE it precedes, not to the `&&` list, so the inline form's precedence
  # is a thing a reader has to know rather than see. Behaviour verified below.
  # BACKEND, not placement: the sanitiser exists for the CROSS-BACKEND hop, so
  # it must keep firing on Codex -> Anthropic even once the Codex lane is placeable.
  if ! _is_anthropic_backend "$cur" && _is_anthropic_backend "$target"; then sanitize=1; fi
  local carry_rc=0
  _swap_carry_jsonl "$srccfg" "$dstcfg" "$uuid" "$mdir" "$sanitize" || carry_rc=$?
  if (( carry_rc != 0 )); then
    local reason
    # rc 1 covers two different causes it does not distinguish in its own return code:
    # nothing matched this uuid at all, OR matches existed and every copy failed (a full
    # disk, an EACCES). Ask _transcript_matches again — cheap, a read, and nothing on disk
    # has changed since the carry attempt — so the operator reading swap.log/the banner is
    # pointed at the right cause instead of always being told "not found" when the real
    # problem was a copy that failed. rc 2 needs no such probe: the carry already knows
    # exactly what happened and says so.
    if (( carry_rc == 2 )); then
      reason="transcript found for $uuid under $cur but the copy to $mdir failed — the directory the resumed session reads first"
    elif [[ -n "$(_transcript_matches "$srccfg" "$uuid")" ]]; then
      reason="transcript found for $uuid under $cur but every copy failed"
    else
      reason="no transcript found for $uuid under $cur after flush"
    fi
    if [[ -z "$force" ]]; then
      # Registry `wrapper` is still $cur — nothing has been flipped — so _swap_refuse's
      # restart puts the session back on the account that still holds its history.
      _swap_beat_stop "$beat"
      _swap_refuse "$id" "$cur" "$target" "$reason" 1
      return 1
    fi
    # The forced warning carries the SAME sentence, for the same reason the refusal does:
    # `--force` means the operator accepted a swap they were told about, and "no transcript
    # for $uuid" was the wrong thing to tell them whenever a copy had in fact been found.
    echo "ccd: warn: $reason; --force: $id resumes anyway" >&2
  fi
  # Unconditional, and separate: sidecars are globbed in their own right and
  # exist in project dirs that hold no .jsonl for this uuid at all.
  _swap_carry_sidecars "$srccfg" "$dstcfg" "$uuid"
  # The task list lives at <configdir>/tasks/<uuid>/ and is keyed by config dir,
  # so a swap orphans it under the OLD account: the resumed session forgets its
  # plan entirely (seen 2026-07-26 — custom-tools kept 8 task files under
  # .claude-gpt while running on claude-corp with an empty list). Carry it over
  # with the transcript. Copy, don't move: if the swap is reverted the old lane
  # still has its copy, and a stale dir costs a few KB.
  if [[ -d "$srccfg/tasks/$uuid" ]]; then
    mkdir -p "$dstcfg/tasks"
    cp -r "$srccfg/tasks/$uuid" "$dstcfg/tasks/" 2>/dev/null \
      || echo "ccd: warn: could not carry the task list across to $target" >&2
  fi
  # THE CARRY IS OVER — every file that was going to move has moved — so the
  # heartbeat stops here, before the registry is flipped. What follows is
  # milliseconds of `_reg_set`, and the final stamp below covers the restart.
  _swap_beat_stop "$beat"
  # RE-ASKED AT THE FLIP, because the carry between the pre-flight and this line
  # is the longest window ccd has — `cp -a` over a sidecar can take minutes — and
  # a removal fits inside it comfortably. Routed through `_swap_refuse` rather
  # than `die`: by this line the pane is dead and the unit is stopped, and that
  # helper is what restarts the session on the account that still holds its
  # history. The transcripts copied into the departed lane's config directory are
  # left where they are — inert bytes in a directory `remove` keeps by contract.
  local _swap_rc=0
  _wrapper_rostered_now "$target" || _swap_rc=$?
  if [[ "$_swap_rc" -ne 0 ]]; then
    local why="account $target left the roster while this swap was carrying its transcripts"
    [[ "$_swap_rc" -eq 2 ]] && why="$CCRC_ACCOUNTS_SH could not be re-read while this swap was carrying its transcripts, so $target cannot be proved to still exist"
    _swap_refuse "$id" "$cur" "$target" "$why" 1
    return 1
  fi
  _reg_set "$id" wrapper "$target"
  _reg_set "$id" lastswap "$(date +%s)"
  # THE PIN, WRITTEN BY THE OPERATOR PATH ONLY (D-3097). `CCD_SWAP_AUTO=1` is
  # `_dispatch_swap`'s own discriminator — the one thing that tells the tick's
  # automatic rescue from a human, a `swap-self` or a PWA tap — and it is already
  # read once in this function, at the cross-pool refusal above. An automatic
  # move must not mint a pin (the tick would then refuse to bring the session
  # home, which is the opposite of what a rescue means) and it CLEARS one,
  # immediately, because the row has just left the account the pin named. The
  # tick's `_swap_pin_tick` would reach the same conclusion on its next pass;
  # doing it here means the two surfaces never disagree even for one tick.
  if [[ "${CCD_SWAP_AUTO:-}" == 1 ]]; then
    [[ -e "$REG/$id.swappin" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$id.swappin" "$REG/$id.swappinnote"
  else
    _reg_set "$id" swappin "$target"
  fi
  # `cmd_ensure` runs in this same process at ccd:12815, but only when
  # `systemctl --user start` FAILS — the `||` fallback — and it writes its own
  # line WHEN IT FIRES. Two acts, two events, two tx-free records: the same
  # ruling as `cmd_enable`. Without this one a reader would see an `ensure` it
  # could not attribute.
  # `dec.*`, not `meas.*` (§14 O6): the dec namespace is for a DECLARED
  # operator choice, and a crossing is declared — the flag is the declaration.
  # The array form is the `cmd_ws_add` idiom (`grep -n 'local lc_dec=()'
  # ccd/ccd`): an empty array under `set -u` needs bash >= 4.4, which this
  # file already requires.
  local lc_cross=()
  [[ -n "$crossing" ]] && lc_cross=(dec.crosspool 1)
  _lc_done swap "$id" "" meas.from "$cur" meas.wrapper "$target" meas.uuid "$uuid" "${lc_cross[@]}"
  rm -f "$REG/$id.swapblocked"   # a completed swap supersedes an earlier refusal: a
                                 # banner left standing on a row that just worked teaches
                                 # the operator to ignore banners.
  # …and it supersedes a STRAND for the same reason: "no account can take it"
  # is falsified by the session having just moved.
  _strand_clear "$id"
  # ONCE, AT THE TAIL. The guard above runs a SECOND time when the swap
  # re-execs itself detached, so a line written there would tell the operator
  # they crossed twice.
  if [[ -n "$crossing" ]]; then
    _crosspool_mark "$id" "${pps#named }" "$target"
    # `target-pool` IS THE SNAPSHOT, NOT A FRESH DECLARED READ (fix round 1 —
    # the same carrier correction as the refusals above, applied to the one
    # renderer of this fact the review did not enumerate). Two reasons the
    # `$(_acct_pool "$target")` that stood here was wrong: it named spec
    # §5.6's lowest-precedence carrier while the projection is what ruled this
    # a crossing at all, and it re-read minutes later — the carry can take
    # them — so the line recorded a pool that need not be the one the
    # decision was made against. `$pstate` is that decision's own word.
    echo "$(date '+%F %T') cross-pool $id: $cur -> $target [project=$project pool=${pps#named } target-pool=${pstate#named }]" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  fi
  echo "$(date '+%F %T') swap $id: $cur -> $target (uuid $uuid)" >> "$REG/swap.log"
  _reg_set "$id" supervised "$(date +%s)"   # the carry may have taken minutes
  _svc_start "claude-session@$id" 2>/dev/null || cmd_ensure "$id"
  # Optional user notification (swap kills any attached remote session — give them a ping).
  # Drop an executable ~/.cc-sessions/notify.sh taking the message as $1 (curl to a Discord
  # webhook / ntfy topic / anything); absent hook = silent.
  [[ -x "$REG/notify.sh" ]] && "$REG/notify.sh" "cc swap: $id moved $cur -> $target (limits) — reopen it on claude.ai under the $target account" >/dev/null 2>&1
  echo "swapped $id: $cur -> $target (discover on claude.ai under the $target account)"
}

cmd_swap_self() {   # target — run from INSIDE a cc session (terminal, web, or mobile:
  # just tell Claude "run ~/.local/bin/ccd swap-self <wrapper>"). Swaps the calling session.
  local target="${1:?usage: ccd swap-self <target-wrapper>}"
  [[ -n "${TMUX:-}" ]] || die "swap-self only works from inside a cc tmux session"
  local id; id=$(tmux display-message -p '#S' 2>/dev/null); id="${id#cc-}"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "current tmux session '$id' is not ccd-managed"
  cmd_swap "$id" "$target"
}

cmd_prefer() {   # [--cross-pool] id wrapper — set a session's HOME account (auto-swap keeps it
  # here, and returns here whenever home is free again). Default home = the
  # wrapper in the id prefix.
  local cross="" args=()
  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
      --cross-pool) cross=1; shift ;;
      --)           shift; args+=("$@"); break ;;
      *)            args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"
  local id="${1:?usage: ccd prefer <id> <wrapper>}" w="${2:?usage: ccd prefer <id> <wrapper>}"
  _is_valid_wrapper "$w" || die "unknown wrapper '$w' (valid: ${CCRC_ACCOUNTS[*]})"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || die "no registry for '$id'"
  _account_still_rostered "$w" "The home for '$id' is unchanged."
  # Shell-only: no whitelist entry and no CCD_ARGV builder, so no route can
  # reach this. The pool rule still applies — the auto-swapper would otherwise
  # re-seed this home away on the next tick and the operator would watch their
  # own choice evaporate.
  local project pps prc prjrc old
  # THE SAME GUARD AS `cmd_swap`'s, for the same measured reason — `ccd prefer`
  # rewrites `.home`, so an unreadable `.project` pins a session's HOME out of
  # pool rather than merely moving it once. Measured: with `.project` a FIFO it
  # printed `home for <id> set to claude-b` where the readable control refuses.
  # THE CENSUS, SPELLED SO IT DOES NOT COUNT ITSELF:
  # `grep -nF '_reg_read "$id" project' ccd/ccd | grep -vE '^[0-9]+:[[:space:]]*#'`
  # answers FIVE — the two tick reads and the three verbs (`cmd_start`,
  # `cmd_swap`, this one). The bare grep answers six, because this sentence
  # quotes the pattern; that trap is booked twice already in this file
  # (`_pool_ok`'s census and `_reg_get`'s), and writing the self-counting
  # spelling here would have been the third.
  #
  # IT SAID FOUR AND TWO VERBS UNTIL ROUND 5, WHICH IS THE POINT (#69 review
  # round 5, its own refute pass): the same commit that converted `cmd_start`'s
  # reader left this later enumeration asserting the old count — a census
  # falsified by its own round, exactly the shape that round books
  # against the `_reg_get` header and against `CCD_BLOCKS`. It is pinned now,
  # by `ccd-reg-get-census.test.ts`, for the reason a comment cannot be: a
  # request goes stale silently and a red suite does not. `-F` because the
  # pattern is a literal — an unescaped `$id` is a regex quantifier's worth of
  # trouble under any grep that anchors `$` mid-pattern.
  project=$(_reg_read "$id" project); prjrc=$?
  [[ "$prjrc" -eq 2 ]] && ! _pool_untaggable \
    && die "the project field for $id could not be read: $REG/$id.project — fix or clear it; nothing was touched"
  pps=$(_project_pool_state "$project")
  _pool_ok "$w" "$pps"; prc=$?
  # SNAPSHOT, for the reason `cmd_start`'s own snapshot states.
  local pside="$POOL_OK_SIDE" pstate="$POOL_OK_STATE"
  [[ "$prc" -eq 2 ]] \
    && die "$(_pool_undecidable_why "$pside" "$pstate" "$project" "$w"); nothing was touched"
  [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -z "$cross" ]] \
    && die "pool-mismatch: $(_pool_mismatch_why "$w" "$pstate" "$project" "$pps") — to cross on purpose: ccd prefer --cross-pool $id $w"
  old=$(_home_for "$id")
  _reg_set "$id" home "$w"
  # AN EXPLICIT HOME SUPERSEDES A PIN (D-3099). The pin exists because `swap`
  # could not say "and keep it there"; `prefer` says exactly that, in the field
  # the affinity arm actually reads, so leaving a pin standing beside it would
  # give one session two answers to the same question — and the pin's would win,
  # silently suppressing the return-home the operator just asked for.
  [[ -e "$REG/$id.swappin" ]] && rm -f -- "$REG/$id.swappin" "$REG/$id.swappinnote"
  local lc_cross=()
  [[ "$prc" -eq 1 && -n "$cross" ]] \
    && { lc_cross=(dec.crosspool 1); _crosspool_mark "$id" "${pps#named }" "$w"; }
  # THE ONE UNCONDITIONAL `.home` WRITER IN THIS FILE, and until now the only
  # account decision that left no record at all — so the one move an operator
  # makes by hand was the one the journal could not show.
  _lc_done rehome "$id" "" meas.from "$old" meas.home "$w" meas.reason prefer "${lc_cross[@]}"
  echo "home for $id set to $w"
}

cmd_enable() {   # <id>  |  <wrapper> <project> [workdir]
  # Behaviorally an ALIAS of cmd_start since §3.1 — the start path itself is
  # `_svc_enable_now`, so there is no second act left for this verb to perform beyond
  # the boot-persistence confirmation line below. It keeps its name because the agent whitelist and
  # CCD_ARGV grant `enable` and `start` separately, and whitelist-subset.test.ts layer 3 fails on a
  # grant no route builds. §3.4 gives it the same one-argument id form as `start` — it takes the
  # SAME two shapes, so it needs its own usage line rather than borrowing start's, or a mistyped
  # `ccd enable` would answer as a verb the operator did not type.
  # STRIPPED BEFORE `_id`, and that ordering is the whole point: `_id "$1" "$2"`
  # on an unstripped argv mints `--cross-pool-<wrapper>` and this verb journals
  # `enable` against a session id that does not exist.
  #
  # `--route` IS FORWARDED, NOT PARSED. This loop strips it only so the arity
  # rule below counts POSITIONALS, and hands the pairs back to `cmd_start`
  # unchanged — one parser, one validation, one set of journal rows saying
  # `start`. A second parse here would be a second vocabulary to keep in step.
  local cross="" args=() route=()
  while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
      --cross-pool) cross=1; shift ;;
      --route)      [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd enable <id> | ccd enable <wrapper> <project> [workdir]"
                    route+=(--route "$2"); shift 2 ;;
      --route=*)    route+=("$1"); shift ;;
      --)           shift; args+=("$@"); break ;;
      *)            args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"
  [[ $# -ge 1 ]] || die "usage: ccd enable <id> | ccd enable <wrapper> <project> [workdir]"
  # The id is computed the same way cmd_start computes it — never trusted from
  # a raw argument — so this line names the row cmd_start actually touched,
  # even on the two-argument form once the registry's wrapper has won.
  local id; if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then id=$(_id "$1" "$2"); else id="$1"; fi
  # TWO EVENTS, NOT ONE. `cmd_start` runs in THIS process and writes its own
  # `start` line; this one records that boot-persistence was asked for. No
  # shared tx: tx is for one intent/outcome PAIR, never for a call tree, and
  # folding these would report one act where two happened.
  _lc_done enable "$id" ""
  cmd_start ${cross:+--cross-pool} "${route[@]}" "$@" || return $?
  echo "enabled boot-persistence for $id"
}

cmd_stop() {   # [--surface <word>] <id>  |  [--surface <word>] <wrapper> <project>
  # FLAGS ARE STRIPPED BEFORE THE ARITY RULE, and that order is the whole
  # correctness argument. `$# -ge 2` means wrapper-plus-project, so with the
  # flag left in argv `ccd stop <id> --surface pwa` reads as a two-argument
  # stop and `_id` mints `<id>---surface` — aiming the stop at a session that
  # does not exist while the real one keeps running.
  #
  # The surface arrives as an argv flag because nothing else can carry it: the
  # exec seam is `(cmd, args) => Promise<ExecResult>` with no env, and the
  # agent's wire ExecReq carries {cmd, args, timeoutMs} and nothing else — a
  # CCD_SURFACE variable would report the server process's own environment,
  # identically for every caller in the fleet.
  #
  # cmd_stop's OWN default is `cli`, not _ws_unsupervise's `ccd`: a session
  # shelling `ccd stop` from its own Bash tool passes no flag, and `cli` is
  # exactly what that looks like from the box.
  local surface=cli declared="" args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # An explicit arity check, not a bare `shift 2`: ccd runs under
      # `set -uo pipefail` with NO `-e`, so a shift past the end of argv fails,
      # shifts nothing, and this loop never terminates.
      --surface)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd stop [--surface <word>] <id> | ccd stop [--surface <word>] <wrapper> <project>"
                   surface="$2"; declared="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --surface=*) surface="${1#--surface=}"; declared="$surface"; shift ;;
      *)           args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"

  # The two-argument form is the legacy one and recomputes `<wrapper>-<project>`.
  # That only works for ids that ENCODE a wrapper; a workspace id is
  # `<project>-<slug>`, so a caller forced to reverse one guesses a wrapper and
  # aims the stop at a different, live session. The one-argument form takes the
  # id directly, exactly as `ccd ensure` does.
  local id
  if [[ $# -ge 2 ]]; then
    local wrapper="${1:?}" project="${2:?}"
    id=$(_id "$wrapper" "$project")
  else
    id="${1:?usage: ccd stop [--surface <word>] <id> | ccd stop [--surface <word>] <wrapper> <project>}"
  fi
  # Through the choke point, never around it: the inlined `systemctl --user
  # disable --now` this replaces was the one stop path in the file that left
  # no record, which is how a deliberate stop became indistinguishable from a
  # crash (§4.1). The pane kill stays HERE rather than moving inward, because
  # every other call site already runs these two as a pair.
  # The ONLY call site with a DECLARATION to carry, which is why it is the only
  # one that passes a third argument. `$surface` (defaulted to `cli`) is still
  # the stamp's word; `$declared` is empty unless a flag actually arrived, and
  # the journal keeps the two apart.
  _lc_done stop "$id" "" dec.surface "$(_lc_surface_norm "$declared")"
  _ws_unsupervise "$id" "$surface" "$declared"
  tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null || true
  echo "stopped $id"
}

cmd_forget() {   # <id> — remove a DEAD non-workspace session's registry entry.
  # The end-of-life a plain session never had. `stop` deliberately leaves the
  # registry (the row and its record survive a restart), archive/reap are
  # workspace-only by contract, and ws-gc's dead-reg arm walks only
  # `.workspace` entries — so a dead wrapper session on a project's main
  # checkout was an immortal fleet row, removable by nothing.
  #
  # What this touches is EXACTLY the registry entry: no worktree, no branch,
  # no git anything — a non-workspace session has none that are ccd's — and
  # deliberately NOT the transcript or the pasted images. Those exist nowhere
  # else; a verb whose confirmation surface is one line of stdout has no
  # business destroying anything it would first have to enumerate. The row
  # can be re-created any time by starting the session again; what cannot be
  # re-created is kept.
  # Same order, same argument, same cap as `cmd_ws_rm` above. The exact-arity
  # guard is RE-ASSERTED on the residue rather than removed: this verb is
  # granted to the agent as the bare `['forget']` prefix (agent/src/whitelist.ts),
  # where "tokens after the prefix are unconstrained", so the arity rule is the
  # only thing bounding what a caller can put here.
  local reason="" args=()
  while (( $# )); do
    case "$1" in
      # SANCTIONED bare `die` — no `$id` is bound yet. See cmd_ws_rm's copy.
      --reason)   [[ $# -ge 2 ]] || die "usage: ccd forget [--reason <text>] <id>"
                  reason="$2"; shift 2 ;;
      --reason=*) reason="${1#--reason=}"; shift ;;
      # F2 FIX (review round 1): same shape as cmd_ws_rm's copy above — an
      # unrecognised `--`-prefixed token refuses BY NAME instead of falling
      # into the positional catch-all and being misrecorded as an arity
      # side-effect or a `no-such-session` for the flag's own text.
      --*)        _lc_refuse forget "$1" bad-args \
                    "unrecognised flag: $1" verb forget dec.reason "$reason" ;;
      *)          args+=("$1"); shift ;;
    esac
  done
  set -- "${args[@]}"
  # F1 FIX (review round 1): same shape as cmd_ws_rm's copy above — the arity
  # guard fires before `id` is bound, so zero args refuses through the journal
  # instead of dying on an unbound parameter before any `_lc_*` runs.
  [[ $# -eq 1 ]] || _lc_refuse forget "${1-}" bad-args \
    "usage: ccd forget [--reason <text>] <id>" verb forget dec.reason "$reason"
  local id="${1-}"
  _lc_dec_ok "$reason" || _lc_refuse forget "$id" bad-args \
    "--reason is longer than $_LC_DEC_MAX bytes — nothing was removed"
  # The id is globbed against $REG by _reg_purge, so its shape is checked
  # here the same way cmd_ws_hold checks its own.
  [[ $id =~ ^[A-Za-z0-9._-]+$ ]] || _lc_refuse forget "$id" bad-session-id "bad session id" dec.reason "$reason"
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || _lc_refuse forget "$id" no-such-session "no such session: $id" dec.reason "$reason"
  local ws; ws=$(_reg_get "$id" workspace)
  [[ -z "$ws" ]] \
    || _lc_refuse forget "$id" is-a-workspace \
         "$id is a workspace — its removal is audited and confirmed: use the workspace sheet (ws-audit/ws-reap), or ccd ws-rm" dec.reason "$reason"
  # A hold is a program's declaration that it is mid-flight here. Same rung,
  # same polarity as ws-rm: `-e` not `-f`, present-but-unreadable refuses.
  if [[ -e "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
    # D-2378: bounded exactly as `cmd_ws_rm`'s copy of this rung — `-f`+`-r`
    # checked before the open, so a FIFO/chardev at the hold path cannot
    # block this read, never touching the `-e` gate itself.
    local _hold_reason
    if [[ -f "$REG/$id.hold" && -r "$REG/$id.hold" ]]; then
      _hold_reason=$(cat "$REG/$id.hold" 2>/dev/null || echo '<unreadable — treat as held>')
    else
      _hold_reason='<unreadable — treat as held>'
    fi
    _lc_refuse forget "$id" held \
      "held: $_hold_reason — release first: ccd ws-release --session $id" dec.reason "$reason"
  fi
  # Deadness is a precondition, not an effect: forget is for a row whose
  # session is already gone. Stopping a live one is `ccd stop`'s job, and
  # collapsing the two into one verb turns a cleanup into a kill.
  # Proven, not assumed. `! _alive` was true both when the session was gone and
  # when the tmux server never answered, so an unreachable substrate let this
  # purge the row of a RUNNING session — the "cleanup into a kill" the comment
  # above warns about, by a route it did not consider. Same polarity as the
  # hold check: what cannot be read refuses (D-308).
  case "$(_session_verdict "$id")" in
    live)    _lc_refuse forget "$id" session-live "$id is still running — stop it first: ccd stop $id" dec.reason "$reason" ;;
    unknown) _lc_refuse forget "$id" session-verdict-unknown "$id: cannot tell whether it is still running — the tmux server did not answer. Nothing was removed. Check with: tmux ls" dec.reason "$reason" ;;
  esac
  # The unit outlives the tmux session: enabled + dead is exactly the state
  # systemd exists to reverse, and a purge that left it enabled would race a
  # resurrection writing fresh registry files behind it.
  local lctx; lctx=$(_lc_tx)
  _lc_intent forget "$id" "$lctx" verb forget dec.reason "$reason" meas.wrapper "$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)" \
    meas.project "$(_reg_get "$id" project)" meas.uuid "$(_reg_get "$id" uuid)"
  _ws_unsupervise "$id"
  tmux kill-session -t "$(_tmux "$id")" 2>/dev/null || true
  # ── D-2605: THIS CALLER READS `_reg_purge`'s STATUS ─────────────────────
  # It reached here AFTER irreversible action, so a refusal is a FAILURE, not a
  # refusal — that is `_lc_fail`'s documented distinction from `_lc_refuse`: a
  # refusal precedes anything irreversible, a failure follows it. Reporting it
  # as a suppressed success is the exact false success `_reg_purge`'s own
  # "never claims success" rule forbids, and reporting it as a bare decline
  # would say nothing was touched, which is false. The message names WHAT
  # COMPLETED and that the registry row and its generation still stand.
  # THE VALUE, not the boolean (D-2605) — see `cmd_ws_rm`'s note.
  local _fg_prc=0
  _reg_purge "$id" || _fg_prc=$?
  if (( _fg_prc == 3 )); then
    _lc_fail forget "$id" "$lctx" purge-incomplete \
      "the session was unsupervised, its pane killed and the registry row purged — this journal's own purge fact is the record of it — but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed and still stands; re-running \`ccd forget $id\` will not take it, remove it by hand" \
      verb forget dec.reason "$reason" meas.unremoved "$REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED"
    echo "ccd: $id: unsupervised, killed and purged, but $REG_PURGE_UNREMOVED could not be removed — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _fg_prc == 2 )); then
    # STATUS 2 IS NOT STATUS 1 — see `cmd_ws_rm`'s note (r3 A-I3). The REMEDY
    # differs from ws-rm's, which is why it is written out rather than shared:
    # `forget`'s own liveness precondition is `_session_verdict`, whose
    # `unknown` arm is an `_lc_refuse` that EMITS THEN DIES, so a re-run from a
    # flock-capable PATH still refuses unless a tmux server is answering.
    _lc_fail forget "$id" "$lctx" purge-mechanism-absent \
      "the session was unsupervised and its pane killed, but the registry row could not be purged: the compaction lock's MECHANISM is absent on this box — flock, mktemp or link could not be resolved on the PATH ccd ran with, so the lock was never consulted and the lock file may not even exist — while $REG/$id.generation is present, so the purge refused rather than race a hook that may hold it. $REG/$id.* and $REG/$id.generation still stand; waiting changes nothing — re-run \`ccd forget $id\` from a PATH that resolves flock, mktemp and link, and only while a tmux server is answering, because forget refuses outright on an unknown session verdict" \
      verb forget dec.reason "$reason"
    echo "ccd: $id: unsupervised and killed, registry NOT purged — the compaction lock mechanism is absent; see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  elif (( _fg_prc != 0 )); then
    # STATUS 1 FOLDS SEVERAL CONDITIONS, AND COMPACT_LOCK_WHY NAMES ONLY THE
    # ONES A RE-RUN CANNOT CURE (r5 R4-M3, correcting r4 A-M3's "STATUS 1 IS
    # TWO CONDITIONS"). `_compact_lock_acquire` returns 1 from many distinct
    # states. ONE of them, the `flock -w` timeout, is ordinary contention, and
    # the default sentence below is written for it. WHICH states carry a token,
    # and what an EMPTY `WHY` does and does not prove, are NOT restated here
    # (r7 R6-M1): that census lives in ONE place, the header comment on
    # _compact_lock_acquire, which is the only writer of the variable. The copy
    # that used to stand here went stale inside a single round — it mis-counted
    # the assignment sites the acquire had just grown, and called the whole
    # untokened residue a race at the mint after the acquire had named a
    # durable member of it — so this caller points instead of repeating, and a
    # numeral about the code is made in exactly one place. What this caller
    # needs from the variable is only that nothing clobbers it between
    # `_reg_purge`'s return and here.
    # `_compact_lock_why_remedy` (r6 R5-M3) keys each token to ITS
    # OWN remedy and gives an unrecognised one the catch-all: a condition
    # named at the acquire must never silently inherit "wait and re-run",
    # which is the defect that let one sentence stand for two conditions.
    # That is the same argument `cmd_ws_add` and `cmd_start` make at their own
    # acquire sites, one level up; this is it one level down, where the
    # record is DURABLE — and it is the ONE renderer all four durable callers
    # consult, so this caller's text cannot drift from theirs.
    local _fg_why; _fg_why=$(_compact_lock_why_remedy "$id" forget)
    _lc_fail forget "$id" "$lctx" purge-refused \
      "the session was unsupervised and its pane killed, but the registry row could not be purged: $_fg_why" \
      verb forget dec.reason "$reason"
    echo "ccd: $id: unsupervised and killed, registry NOT purged — see the lifecycle journal" >&2
    return 1
  fi
  _lc_done forget "$id" "$lctx" verb forget dec.reason "$reason"
  echo "forgot $id — registry entry removed; the transcript and any pasted images are kept"
}

cmd_ls() {
  # STATE, not ALIVE: `no` was one word for three different facts — a session
  # somebody deliberately stopped, one that died with nothing watching it, and
  # a row that never had a session at all (§4.4). The column is 13 wide because
  # `never-started` is 13 characters. Nothing parses this output — no server,
  # agent or pwa caller shells `ccd ls`, and `cmd_menu` formats its own list —
  # so the rename costs no parser. The Codex trailer below is a different
  # matter: its exact strings are pinned by ccd-limits.test.ts and
  # ccd-session-state.test.ts, which move with it when it changes.
  printf '%-28s %-12s %-13s %s\n' "ID" "WRAPPER" "STATE" "WORKDIR"
  local f id; shopt -s nullglob
  local files=("$REG"/*.uuid)
  [[ ${#files[@]} -eq 0 ]] && { echo "(no sessions)"; return 0; }
  local -A codex_here=()
  for f in "${files[@]}"; do
    id=$(basename "$f" .uuid); local state; state=$(_session_state "$id")
    local w; w=$(_reg_get "$id" wrapper)
    if _is_codex_backend "$w"; then codex_here["$w"]=$(( ${codex_here["$w"]:-0} + 1 )); fi
    printf '%-28s %-12s %-13s %s\n' "$id" "$w" "$state" "$(_reg_get "$id" workdir)"
  done
  # ONE LINE PER CODEX LANE, driven by the roster rather than by a name in this
  # file. The previous version printed a single trailer for `gpt` and called it
  # "the gpt overflow lane", and by the time it was changed BOTH halves of that
  # were wrong: `gpt2` had existed since 2026-09-10 and was carrying the
  # sessions, and on 2026-09-11 both lanes became home-able, so neither is an
  # overflow lane any more — they are placement targets that a session can be
  # BORN on, ranked on usage beside every Anthropic lane.
  #
  # WHAT IS AND IS NOT SKIPPED, stated as the code behaves rather than as the
  # first version of this comment wished (corrected 2026-09-14). The trailer is
  # skipped ENTIRELY only when `_codex_lanes` is empty — a roster with no
  # `telemetry: "codex"` account. A rostered Codex lane whose wrapper is NOT
  # installed still gets a line, reading `not installed`; that is
  # `_codex_lane_status`'s first branch, not a skip, and the old "skip rather
  # than describe what you cannot" rule did not survive the change.
  #
  # `_is_valid_wrapper` below is therefore NOT a wrapper-installation check —
  # it reads CCRC_ACCOUNTS membership. On the generated path it can never fire,
  # because CCRC_CODEX_BACKEND is by construction a subset of CCRC_ACCOUNTS. It
  # earns its place on the FALLBACK path alone: `_codex_lanes` emits `gpt`
  # unconditionally for a pre-CCRC_CODEX_BACKEND accounts.sh, and without this
  # line a box whose roster has no gpt account would get a trailer naming one.
  #
  # The wording is deliberately the roster's own vocabulary — "codex lane" — and
  # not a claim about placement: `ccd ls` lists sessions, and whether a lane is
  # home-able is `_default_pool`'s question, visible in `ccrc account`.
  local lane
  while read -r lane; do
    _is_valid_wrapper "$lane" || continue
    echo "codex lane $lane: $(_codex_lane_status "$lane")  —  ${codex_here["$lane"]:-0} session(s) currently on it"
  done < <(_codex_lanes)
  echo "(open $CLAUDE_AI_BASE in the matching account and pick the session whose name == ID)"
}

cmd_attach() {   # create-on-demand, then exec tmux attach
  local wrapper="${1:?usage: ccd attach <wrapper> <project>}" project="${2:?}"
  local id; id=$(_id "$wrapper" "$project")
  [[ -f "$REG/$id.uuid" ]] || cmd_start "$wrapper" "$project" >/dev/null || die "start failed"
  _alive "$id" || cmd_ensure "$id" >/dev/null || die "ensure failed for $id"
  exec tmux attach -t "$(_tmux "$id")"
}

cmd_menu() {   # interactive picker → attach. The universal entry point for any SSH/terminal app:
  # set the app's on-connect command to `~/.local/bin/ccd menu` and you get this list, pick, attach.
  shopt -s nullglob
  local files=("$REG"/*.uuid) ids=() f id i alive
  for f in "${files[@]}"; do ids+=("$(basename "$f" .uuid)"); done
  if [[ ${#ids[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then echo "no sessions yet — create one with: ccd start <wrapper> <project>"; return 1; fi
  echo "── ccd sessions ──"
  for i in "${!ids[@]}"; do
    id="${ids[$i]}"; alive="dead"; _alive "$id" && alive="live"
    printf "  %2d) %-30s [%s]\n" "$((i + 1))" "$id" "$alive"
  done
  printf "pick a number (q to quit): "
  local choice; read -r choice
  [[ "$choice" == "q" || -z "$choice" ]] && return 0
  [[ "$choice" =~ ^[0-9]+$ ]] && (( choice >= 1 && choice <= ${#ids[@]} )) || { echo "invalid choice"; return 1; }
  id="${ids[$((choice - 1))]}"
  _alive "$id" || cmd_ensure "$id" >/dev/null || die "ensure failed for $id"
  exec tmux attach -t "$(_tmux "$id")"
}

# <dir> <src> — destination for a filed clip. Split out of cmd_clip so it can be
# sourced and asserted: cmd_clip dies at `_alive` long before it gets here.
# Keeps the SOURCE extension (this used to hardcode .png, so a JPEG lied about
# its format) and adds a random suffix (the 1s stamp let two clips in the same
# second mv -f over each other).
_clip_dest() {
  local dir="$1" src="$2" ext
  ext="${src##*.}"
  ext="$(printf '%s' "$ext" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')"
  case "$ext" in png|jpg|jpeg|webp) ;; *) ext=png ;; esac
  printf '%s/clip-%s-%08x.%s\n' "$dir" "$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)" "$(( (RANDOM << 15) | RANDOM ))" "$ext"
}

cmd_clip() {   # <png-path> [id] — file an image into a session and inject its path into the prompt.
  # Default target is the session of the most-recently-active ATTACHED client (i.e. the one you're
  # looking at). Pass an explicit id to override. The path is typed (no Enter) so you add your text.
  local png="${1:?usage: ccd clip <png-path> [id]}" id="${2:-}"
  [[ -f "$png" ]] || die "no such image: $png"
  if [[ -z "$id" ]]; then
    id=$(tmux list-clients -F '#{client_activity} #{client_session}' 2>/dev/null \
           | sort -rn | awk 'NR==1{print $2}' | sed 's/^cc-//')
    [[ -n "$id" ]] || die "no attached cc session (attach with 'cc <wrapper> <project>', or pass an id)"
  fi
  _alive "$id" || die "session not alive: $id"
  local dir="$HOME/.cc-clips/$id"; mkdir -p "$dir"
  local dest; dest="$(_clip_dest "$dir" "$png")"
  mv -f "$png" "$dest"
  tmux send-keys -t "$(_tmux "$id")" -l "$dest "
  echo "clip -> $dest (injected into $id)"
}

